window.QUIZ_DATA = {"exams":[{"examNumber":1,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":27,"title":"Innate & Adaptive Immune Cells","questions":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cells involved in the innate immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A virologist studying cellular immunity notes that certain lymphocytes kill virus-infected host cells without prior sensitization to viral antigens. These cells are found to downregulate their cytotoxic activity when target cells express high surface levels of MHC class I. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which these cells distinguish infected cells from healthy host cells?","choices":{"A":"These cells bear inhibitory receptors that bind MHC class I on healthy cells; virus-infected cells that downregulate MHC class I lose this inhibitory signal, tipping the balance toward activation and perforin-mediated lysis of the target cell","B":"These cells express a somatically recombined T-cell receptor that binds viral peptides displayed on MHC class I, triggering cytotoxic granule release only when the antigen-specific receptor engages the correct peptide-MHC complex","C":"These cells are activated exclusively through antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity and cannot kill target cells unless IgG antibody has already opsonized the surface of the infected cell via Fc receptor binding","D":"These cells use toll-like receptors located on their plasma membrane to detect intracellular viral RNA, which then triggers granzyme B release in a process that requires co-stimulation by IL-4 from helper T cells","E":"These cells require prior activation by CD4+ helper T cells secreting IL-2 before mounting any cytotoxic response, and they cannot kill target cells during the initial innate phase of an immune response"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Natural killer (NK) cells bear inhibitory receptors, such as KIRs, that recognize MHC class I molecules constitutively expressed on healthy cells. This inhibitory signal normally prevents NK-mediated killing. Viruses often downregulate MHC class I expression on infected cells to evade CD8+ cytotoxic T cells; paradoxically, this removes the inhibitory brake on NK cells, shifting the balance toward activating receptor signaling and triggering perforin/granzyme B release. NK cells do not require prior sensitization and lack antigen-specific TCRs, distinguishing them from adaptive CTLs. ADCC via CD16 is a separate NK killing mechanism that does require IgG.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic board trap — NK cells kill cells with ABSENT MHC I (\"missing self\"), while CD8+ CTLs kill cells with PRESENT MHC I-peptide complex. NK activity is enhanced by IL-2, IL-12, IFN-alpha, and IFN-beta. COMLEX: NK cell dysfunction is relevant to understanding impaired innate immune surveillance; OMM-induced cytokine modulation (IFN-gamma) can influence NK cell activity in chronic inflammatory states.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Innate cells: neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, NK cells, and ILCs form the immediate, germline-encoded first line of defense"},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cells involved in the innate immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pathology professor asks students to identify the tissue-resident macrophage populations derived from yolk sac or fetal liver progenitors that establish themselves in specific organs early in life. Which of the following correctly pairs the specialized macrophage population name with its primary tissue of residence?","choices":{"A":"Kupffer cells populate the brain parenchyma as the primary CNS resident phagocytes; microglia reside in the hepatic sinusoids filtering portal blood; and alveolar macrophages are found in the renal cortex surveilling the glomerular filtration barrier","B":"Microglia reside in the pulmonary alveoli and ingest inhaled particulates; Kupffer cells populate the intestinal lamina propria as the primary gut-resident phagocytes; and alveolar macrophages are located in the splenic red pulp filtering the blood","C":"Alveolar macrophages populate the hepatic sinusoids filtering blood-borne microbes from portal circulation; Kupffer cells reside in the pulmonary alveoli; and microglia are located in the renal mesangium performing local immune surveillance","D":"Langerhans cells reside in the hepatic sinusoids filtering portal blood; microglia populate the pulmonary alveoli; and Kupffer cells are the primary resident macrophages of the brain parenchyma performing CNS immune surveillance","E":"Kupffer cells reside in the hepatic sinusoids and filter blood-borne bacteria and antigens from portal circulation; microglia populate the brain parenchyma as the primary CNS immune sentinels; alveolar macrophages patrol the lung airways and ingest inhaled particles"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tissue-resident macrophages are long-lived cells that populate specific organs early in development from yolk sac or fetal liver precursors. Kupffer cells line the hepatic sinusoids and phagocytose bacteria, debris, and antigens arriving from portal circulation. Microglia are the brain-resident macrophages and serve as the primary immune surveillance cells of the CNS. Alveolar macrophages patrol the pulmonary airways and ingest inhaled particulates and pathogens. Langerhans cells are dendritic cells (not macrophages) that reside in the epidermis. Mesangial cells in the kidney are a separate myeloid population.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize the organ-macrophage pairs — Kupffer (liver), microglia (brain), alveolar macrophages (lung), osteoclasts (bone), mesangial cells (kidney). Kupffer cells are activated in alcoholic hepatitis and certain bacterial infections. COMLEX: Tissue macrophages are key mediators of chronic inflammation relevant to granulomatous and autoimmune disease pathogenesis tested on COMLEX.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Innate cells: neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, NK cells, and ILCs form the immediate, germline-encoded first line of defense"},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cells involved in the innate immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"During an immunology lecture, a professor describes a specialized antigen-presenting cell population that resides within the epidermal layer of the skin, expresses abundant pattern recognition receptors, and migrates to regional lymph nodes following antigen capture. Which of the following correctly identifies these cells and describes their primary immunologic role?","choices":{"A":"These cells are called Kupffer cells; they reside in the epidermal basal layer, phagocytose dermal pathogens directly, and signal resident mast cells to release histamine without trafficking to lymph nodes or activating T lymphocytes","B":"These cells are called innate lymphoid cells; they populate the epidermis and respond to IL-25 and IL-33 from damaged keratinocytes, directly lysing bacteria through perforin release before adaptive immunity is initiated by T lymphocytes","C":"These cells are called plasmacytoid dendritic cells; they reside in the epidermis and specialize in secreting large amounts of type I interferons after detecting viral nucleic acids via TLR-7 and TLR-9 located in endosomes","D":"These cells are called Langerhans cells; they are immature dendritic cells that capture protein antigens in the epidermis and transport them via afferent lymphatics to draining lymph nodes, where they mature, upregulate MHC class II and costimulatory molecules, and activate naive T lymphocytes","E":"These cells are called follicular dendritic cells; they populate the epidermal layer, trap antigen-antibody complexes via Fc receptors, and present antigens directly to B lymphocytes to drive germinal center affinity maturation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Langerhans cells are immature dendritic cells located in the epidermis. As sentinel cells, they capture antigens at this common portal of pathogen entry using pattern recognition receptors including TLRs and lectins. After capturing antigen, Langerhans cells migrate via afferent lymphatics to draining lymph nodes, where they mature into interdigitating dendritic cells with high levels of MHC class II and B7 costimulatory molecules (CD80/CD86), enabling them to activate naive T cells. Follicular dendritic cells are specialized fibroblasts in germinal centers that trap antigen for B cells and do not present to T cells. Plasmacytoid DCs are a distinct subset that specializes in IFN-alpha production.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dendritic cells are the most potent APCs for initiating T-cell responses — they are the bridge between innate (TLR sensing) and adaptive (T-cell activation) immunity. Langerhans cells specifically test the concept of how skin pathogens are detected. COMLEX: Dendritic cell migration to lymph nodes parallels the osteopathic concept of lymphatic flow optimization; OMM may facilitate antigen trafficking via improved lymphatic pump function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Innate cells: neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, NK cells, and ILCs form the immediate, germline-encoded first line of defense"},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cells involved in the adaptive immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An immunology instructor asks students to match T-lymphocyte subsets with their correct surface marker expression, the MHC class they recognize, and their primary effector function in the adaptive immune response. Which of the following correctly pairs all three elements for both major T-cell subsets?","choices":{"A":"CD4+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes express CD4, recognize peptide-MHC class I complexes on all nucleated cells, and kill infected cells; CD8+ helper T cells express CD8, recognize peptide-MHC class II complexes on APCs, and stimulate B-cell antibody production","B":"CD8+ helper T lymphocytes express CD8, recognize exogenous peptide-MHC class II complexes on dendritic cells, and stimulate B cells and macrophages; CD4+ cytotoxic cells express CD4, recognize MHC class I, and kill virus-infected cells via Fas ligand","C":"CD4+ and CD8+ T lymphocytes both recognize peptide-MHC class I complexes, with CD4+ cells killing infected cells and CD8+ cells stimulating B-cell class switching and affinity maturation in germinal centers of secondary lymphoid organs","D":"CD8+ regulatory T cells express CD8, CD25, and FOXP3, and recognize self-peptide-MHC class II complexes to suppress autoimmune responses; CD4+ cytotoxic T cells express CD4 and kill tumor cells via granzyme B release","E":"CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes express CD8, recognize peptide-MHC class I complexes on all nucleated cells, and kill virus-infected and tumor cells via perforin and granzyme B; CD4+ helper T cells express CD4, recognize peptide-MHC class II complexes on APCs, and coordinate adaptive responses by secreting cytokines"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The CD4-MHC II and CD8-MHC I pairings are fundamental. A useful mnemonic: CD4 x MHC II = 8, and CD8 x MHC I = 8, both equal 8. CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes (CTLs) recognize endogenous or cross-presented antigen on MHC class I, which is expressed on all nucleated cells, allowing surveillance of any infected cell. CD4+ helper T cells recognize exogenous antigen on MHC class II, expressed mainly on professional APCs (dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells). Regulatory T cells are CD4+, CD25+, FOXP3+ — not CD8+. The wrong choices systematically reverse these pairings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The CD4/CD8-MHC class pairing is guaranteed boards material. Mnemonic: CD4 x 2 = 8 (MHC II); CD8 x 1 = 8 (MHC I). MHC I is on all nucleated cells plus platelets but NOT RBCs. MHC II is on professional APCs only. COMLEX: T-cell subsets are foundational for understanding autoimmune disease pathogenesis (Th1/Th17 in RA, Th2 in allergy) tested on COMLEX clinical presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — Adaptive cells: T lymphocytes (CD4+ helper, CD8+ cytotoxic, Tregs) and B lymphocytes mediate specific, memory-capable immunity"},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cells involved in the adaptive immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A hematology fellow reviews the origin, location, and function of B lymphocytes as part of a teaching session on humoral immunity. Which of the following most accurately describes the development, anatomic distribution, and primary effector function of B lymphocytes in the adaptive immune response?","choices":{"A":"B lymphocytes develop in the thymus via positive and negative selection and migrate to lymph node paracortex zones, where they recognize antigen via MHC class I-restricted T-cell receptors and kill opsonized bacteria through direct cytotoxic granule release","B":"B lymphocytes develop from NK cell precursors in the spleen and circulate exclusively in secondary lymphoid organs; they are activated by direct contact with dendritic cells via CD28-B7 interactions and produce only IgM antibodies without class switching","C":"B lymphocytes develop and mature in the bone marrow, where they undergo VDJ recombination to generate diverse surface immunoglobulin receptors; upon antigen stimulation and T-cell help, they differentiate into plasma cells that secrete large quantities of specific antibodies and into long-lived memory B cells","D":"B lymphocytes develop in the bone marrow but require thymic education for final maturation; once activated, they differentiate exclusively into memory cells without a plasma cell intermediate and do not secrete free immunoglobulin into the circulation","E":"B lymphocytes reside exclusively in the T-cell zones of lymph nodes and require CD8+ T-cell signals via MHC class I before they can be activated to produce antibodies against protein antigens in a T-dependent immune response"},"correct":"C","explanation":"B lymphocytes develop in the bone marrow, where V(D)J recombination generates the enormous diversity of surface immunoglobulin (IgM and IgD) receptors — approximately 10^11 different specificities. Mature naive B cells circulate and reside in follicles of secondary lymphoid organs (lymph nodes, spleen, MALT). Protein antigen recognition requires T-cell help: CD40L on activated Tfh cells binds CD40 on B cells, and cytokines drive isotype class switching and affinity maturation. Activated B cells differentiate into plasma cells (the antibody factories) and memory B cells. B cells do NOT use TCRs and are NOT thymus-derived.","boardPrep":"Step 1: B cells = Bone marrow origin; T cells = Thymus. B cells express surface IgM and IgD as the BCR complex; Igα/Igβ heterodimer handles signal transduction. EBV uses CD21 (CR2 complement receptor) to enter B cells — classic boards association. COMLEX: B-cell deficiencies (X-linked agammaglobulinemia) present with recurrent encapsulated bacterial infections; important COMLEX clinical presentation to recognize.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — Adaptive cells: T lymphocytes (CD4+ helper, CD8+ cytotoxic, Tregs) and B lymphocytes mediate specific, memory-capable immunity"},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cells involved in the adaptive immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student studying immune tolerance learns about a specialized CD4+ T-cell subset that actively suppresses self-reactive T lymphocytes and prevents autoimmune responses. This subset is identified by expression of a specific transcription factor whose deficiency causes a multisystem autoimmune syndrome. Which of the following correctly identifies this T-cell population, its defining markers, and its mechanism of immune suppression?","choices":{"A":"Th17 cells express CD4 and the transcription factor RORgammaT; they suppress autoimmunity by secreting IL-17 and IL-22, which activate neutrophilic infiltration and restore epithelial barrier function, and their deficiency results in chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis","B":"Regulatory T cells express CD3, CD4, CD25, and the transcription factor FOXP3; they suppress CD4+ and CD8+ effector T-cell responses by secreting anti-inflammatory cytokines including IL-10 and TGF-beta, and FOXP3 deficiency causes the IPEX syndrome of immune dysregulation and polyendocrinopathy","C":"Cytotoxic T lymphocytes express CD8 and the transcription factor Tbet; they suppress self-reactive B cells by releasing perforin and granzyme B, eliminating autoreactive B-cell clones before they enter germinal centers to undergo affinity maturation","D":"Follicular helper T cells express CD4 and the transcription factor Bcl-6; they suppress immune hyperactivation by inhibiting germinal center B-cell survival signals, preventing excessive affinity maturation and blocking IgE isotype class switching","E":"Th1 cells express CD4 and the transcription factor Tbet; they suppress excessive Th2 responses by secreting IFN-gamma and IL-2, maintaining immune balance, and their deficiency results in FOXP3 upregulation and systemic autoimmunity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Regulatory T cells (Tregs) are defined by co-expression of CD3, CD4, CD25 (IL-2 receptor alpha chain), and the master transcription factor FOXP3. They suppress effector T-cell responses through secretion of IL-10 and TGF-beta, preventing inflammation against self-antigens. Loss-of-function FOXP3 mutations cause IPEX syndrome (Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked), which manifests as severe autoimmunity including type 1 diabetes, enteropathy, and dermatitis in male infants. Negative selection in the thymic medulla is driven by the AIRE protein, distinct from Tregs. Th17 cells promote inflammation and are not suppressive.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tregs = CD3, CD4, CD25, FOXP3. FOXP3 deficiency = IPEX syndrome (mnemonic: \"Without FOXP3, you are IPEX-ed\"). AIRE deficiency causes APS-1/APECED (Addison, hypoparathyroidism, candidiasis). Both involve failed tolerance but at different levels — thymic (AIRE) vs peripheral (FOXP3). COMLEX: Tregs are central to the osteopathic concept of homeostasis; understanding their role in preventing autoimmunity is foundational for rheumatology clinical presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — Adaptive cells: T lymphocytes (CD4+ helper, CD8+ cytotoxic, Tregs) and B lymphocytes mediate specific, memory-capable immunity"},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanistic differences between innate, cell-mediated, and humoral immunity.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pharmacologist studying immunomodulatory drugs asks a student to explain why innate immune responses occur within hours of infection while adaptive immune responses take days to weeks to develop. The student's explanation should also address why re-exposure to a pathogen elicits a faster, more vigorous adaptive response. Which of the following best explains these observations?","choices":{"A":"Innate immunity uses germline-encoded pattern recognition receptors that are pre-formed and immediately available, enabling rapid responses without specificity or memory; adaptive immunity requires somatic VDJ recombination to generate diverse antigen-specific lymphocyte receptors, but subsequent antigen encounters activate expanded memory cell pools for faster, amplified responses","B":"Innate immunity uses somatically recombined antigen-specific receptors that provide immediate and highly specific responses to pathogens, while adaptive immunity uses germline-encoded complement proteins and defensins that cannot generate memory and must be reconstituted after each infection","C":"Innate immunity is slower than adaptive immunity because it requires cytokine-driven lymphocyte proliferation before mounting a response, while adaptive immunity is faster because pre-formed IgM antibodies and complement proteins neutralize pathogens within minutes of exposure","D":"Innate immunity provides a faster initial response because macrophages constitutively secrete IgG antibodies that opsonize common pathogens, while adaptive immunity is slower because B cells must first undergo somatic hypermutation to generate the first antigen-specific antibody","E":"Innate immunity requires prior sensitization to a pathogen before mounting a response, which is why first exposures are slow, while adaptive immunity is immediately active at first encounter because lymphocytes constitutively express pre-formed antigen-specific IgM on their surface"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The speed difference between innate and adaptive immunity stems from receptor generation strategy. Innate immune cells (macrophages, DCs, NK cells, neutrophils) use germline-encoded pattern recognition receptors such as toll-like receptors (TLRs) that recognize broadly shared pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) and are constitutively expressed — ready immediately. Adaptive immunity requires VDJ recombination during lymphocyte development to generate diverse antigen-specific TCRs and BCRs; only rare cells (1 in 100,000 lymphocytes) bear receptors for any given antigen, requiring clonal expansion before an effective response. Memory cells are the survivors of prior responses — more numerous and more readily activated, explaining faster secondary responses and the principle behind vaccination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Innate = germline-encoded, nonspecific, no memory, rapid (minutes to hours); Adaptive = VDJ recombined, highly specific, memory-forming, slow (days to weeks). TLRs recognize PAMPs — LPS via TLR-4, flagellin via TLR-5, dsRNA via TLR-3, ssRNA via TLR-7, CpG DNA via TLR-9. COMLEX: The innate-adaptive distinction underlies the rationale for immunizations and is directly testable on COMLEX clinical vignettes involving vaccine response timing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — Innate vs cell-mediated vs humoral: speed of response, receptor diversity, memory capacity, and effector mechanism distinguish each arm"},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanistic differences between innate, cell-mediated, and humoral immunity.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A microbiology professor presents two patients: one with a Mycobacterium tuberculosis lung infection (an obligate intracellular pathogen) and one with Streptococcus pneumoniae bacteremia (an extracellular pathogen). She asks which arm of adaptive immunity is primarily responsible for clearing each infection and why the effector mechanisms differ. Which of the following best explains the distinction?","choices":{"A":"Humoral immunity, mediated by IgM antibodies from plasma cells, is required for intracellular pathogens because IgM can cross cell membranes to reach cytosolic bacteria; cell-mediated immunity via NK cells eliminates extracellular bacteria using perforin and granzyme B without requiring prior antigen sensitization","B":"Cell-mediated immunity is the primary defense against extracellular bacteria by activating macrophage phagocytosis through CD40-CD40L interactions; humoral immunity is responsible for intracellular viral infections because IgG antibodies neutralize viral replication enzymes inside infected cells","C":"Cell-mediated immunity, carried out by CD4+ Th1 cells and CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes, is required for intracellular pathogens because antibodies cannot access cytosolic microbes; humoral immunity, mediated by antibodies secreted by plasma cells, is the primary defense against extracellular pathogens by neutralization, opsonization, and complement activation","D":"Both intracellular and extracellular pathogens are primarily cleared by the same adaptive immune mechanism — CD4+ helper T cells — which coordinate all effector responses; the difference lies only in which secondary cytokine profile the Th cells produce and not in the fundamental effector mechanism used","E":"Humoral immunity eliminates intracellular bacteria via complement-mediated lysis after IgG crosses the plasma membrane; cell-mediated immunity eliminates extracellular pathogens through Th2-mediated mast cell degranulation and eosinophil activation at mucosal surfaces"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fundamental logic of cell-mediated versus humoral immunity reflects the location of the pathogen. Intracellular pathogens like M. tuberculosis reside inside macrophage phagosomes or cytosol, where circulating antibodies cannot reach. Cell-mediated immunity addresses this: Th1 CD4+ cells activate macrophages via IFN-gamma and CD40L-CD40 interactions, enhancing intracellular killing; CD8+ CTLs kill cells harboring cytosolic microbes (viruses, some bacteria) by releasing perforin and granzyme B. Extracellular bacteria in the bloodstream are accessible to antibodies, which neutralize them, opsonize them for Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis, or activate complement via the classical pathway to lyse them. Antibodies do NOT cross cell membranes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key distinction — antibodies work OUTSIDE cells; T cells work INSIDE (via killing infected cells). Classic intracellular pathogens requiring cell-mediated immunity: Mycobacteria, Listeria, Salmonella, viruses, fungi. Classic extracellular pathogens requiring humoral immunity: encapsulated bacteria (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, N. meningitidis). COMLEX: This distinction is central to understanding which immunodeficiencies predispose to which infections — tested heavily on COMLEX.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — Innate vs cell-mediated vs humoral: speed of response, receptor diversity, memory capacity, and effector mechanism distinguish each arm"},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanistic differences between innate, cell-mediated, and humoral immunity.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A clinical pharmacologist describes several mechanisms by which antibodies eliminate pathogens during humoral immune responses. She lists neutralization, opsonization, complement activation, and transplacental transfer as distinct antibody effector functions. Which of the following most accurately summarizes all four of these mechanisms and their molecular basis?","choices":{"A":"Antibodies neutralize pathogens by activating mast cell degranulation via IgE-Fc epsilon receptor interactions; IgA opsonizes bacteria in the bloodstream for Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis; IgG activates the alternative complement pathway by binding C3b directly; and IgM is transported across the placenta to provide neonatal immunity against common pathogens","B":"Antibodies neutralize pathogens by binding and blocking surface structures required for host cell attachment; IgG opsonizes bacteria for phagocytosis via Fc receptor recognition on neutrophils and macrophages; IgM and IgG activate complement via the classical pathway to lyse microbes and enhance phagocytosis; and IgG is actively transported across the placenta via neonatal Fc receptors to confer passive immunity","C":"Antibodies neutralize pathogens by directly lysing bacterial cell walls through muramidase activity; IgM opsonizes bacteria for phagocytosis by binding Fc receptors on NK cells; IgA activates complement via the lectin pathway at mucosal surfaces; and IgE is transported across the placenta providing long-term neonatal humoral immunity","D":"Antibodies neutralize pathogens exclusively by activating the membrane attack complex of complement; IgM opsonizes all pathogens for phagocytosis via high-affinity Fc receptor binding; IgA provides systemic complement activation; and IgG activates Fc epsilon receptors on mast cells to trigger degranulation and pathogen clearance","E":"Antibodies neutralize pathogens by secreting perforin and granzyme B directly from plasma cells; IgE opsonizes bacteria via complement receptor 1 on macrophages; IgM provides transplacental immunity; and IgG activates the alternative complement pathway to generate C5a and C3b on bacterial surfaces"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Antibody effector functions are diverse and isotype-specific. Neutralization: antibodies bind pathogen surface molecules, physically blocking receptor engagement with host cells — does not require other effectors. Opsonization: IgG coats bacteria, and phagocytes (neutrophils, macrophages) bearing Fc-gamma receptors recognize the IgG Fc tail, enhancing phagocytosis by approximately 1000-fold. Complement activation: IgM and IgG activate the classical complement pathway; C3b is a potent opsonin and C5a is a chemoattractant; the membrane attack complex lyses gram-negative bacteria. Transplacental transfer: IgG (not IgM or IgA) is actively transported across the placenta via neonatal Fc receptors (FcRn), providing passive immunity to the neonate for the first months of life. IgE binds mast cells — relevant to allergy, not bacterial neutralization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgG = opsonization, complement, transplacental transfer, ADCC; IgA = secretory immunity at mucosa (gut, respiratory, breast milk); IgM = first antibody produced, best complement activator; IgE = mast cell/basophil degranulation, eosinophil activation. Neonates receive IgG transplacentally — protects for roughly 6 months. COMLEX: Antibody effector mechanisms are testable in the context of selective IgA deficiency (most common antibody deficiency) causing recurrent sinopulmonary infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — Innate vs cell-mediated vs humoral: speed of response, receptor diversity, memory capacity, and effector mechanism distinguish each arm"},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cells involved in the innate immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the pediatric immunology clinic for evaluation of recurrent severe herpesvirus infections and persistent Epstein-Barr virus viremia. His parents report that he has had four hospitalizations for herpes encephalitis and disseminated EBV disease over the past two years. Laboratory testing reveals markedly reduced numbers of a specific lymphocyte population that comprises 5 to 10 percent of peripheral blood lymphocytes in healthy individuals. Flow cytometry shows the remaining lymphocytes lack T-cell receptors and do not express CD3, but do express CD16 and CD56. Which of the following best explains the mechanism responsible for this patient's susceptibility to herpesvirus infections?","choices":{"A":"The patient has a deficiency of dendritic cells, which are required to present herpesvirus antigens to naive CD4+ T cells via MHC class II; without dendritic cell antigen presentation, no adaptive immune response is initiated and herpesviruses replicate unchecked in neural and lymphoid tissues","B":"The patient has a deficiency of innate lymphoid cells type 1, which normally produce IFN-gamma in response to IL-12 from macrophages; without ILC1-derived IFN-gamma, macrophages cannot undergo classical activation and are unable to kill phagocytosed herpesvirus particles in endolysosomes","C":"The patient has a deficiency of natural killer cells, which normally eliminate virus-infected cells by recognizing the downregulation of MHC class I on infected cells; without this innate surveillance, cells harboring herpesviruses that have evaded CD8+ CTL recognition by reducing MHC class I expression are not cleared, allowing viral persistence and dissemination","D":"The patient has a complement deficiency affecting the membrane attack complex, which normally lyses enveloped viruses including herpesviruses directly in the bloodstream; without MAC formation, viremia persists and herpesviruses gain access to neural tissues causing recurrent encephalitis","E":"The patient has a deficiency of neutrophils, which are the primary innate responders to viral infections and normally release neutrophil extracellular traps that capture and destroy herpesvirus particles before they can infect epithelial cells and establish latency in neural ganglia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This vignette describes NK cell deficiency. NK cells are the innate lymphoid cells that express CD16 and CD56 (not CD3 or TCR) and comprise 5 to 10 percent of peripheral blood lymphocytes. Their hallmark function is surveillance for cells that have downregulated MHC class I — a strategy used by many herpesviruses (including EBV, HSV, CMV) to evade CD8+ CTL recognition. NK cells express inhibitory KIR receptors that bind MHC class I; when MHC class I is absent (\"missing self\"), inhibitory signaling is lost and activating receptors dominate, triggering perforin and granzyme B release. Without NK cells, herpesvirus-infected cells that downregulate MHC class I are not cleared by innate immunity, and the adaptive CTL response is insufficient to control disseminated infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NK cell markers = CD16, CD56, CD57 (no CD3, no TCR). NK cells are the primary innate defense against herpesviruses and tumors via \"missing self\" recognition. Activity enhanced by IL-2, IL-12, IFN-alpha, IFN-beta. NK cells also kill via ADCC (CD16 binds IgG Fc region). COMLEX: NK cell deficiency is rare but clinically important; similar presentation to CTL defects but distinguished by flow cytometry — COMLEX tests pattern recognition of immunodeficiency presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Innate immune cells: NK cell missing-self mechanism and role in viral immune surveillance"},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cells involved in the innate immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with known HIV infection presents to her primary care physician for a routine follow-up visit. Her CD4+ count is 410 cells per microliter and viral load is undetectable on antiretroviral therapy. She asks why she recently developed a painful shingles outbreak despite being on treatment. The physician explains that even with controlled HIV, a specific innate immune cell population that is critical for containing reactivation of latent varicella-zoster virus in dorsal root ganglia remains functionally impaired. This cell population patrols peripheral tissues, does not express antigen-specific receptors, and kills infected cells through perforin and granzyme B release. Which of the following best describes the additional mechanism by which this cell type contributes to antiviral defense beyond direct cytotoxic killing?","choices":{"A":"These cells secrete large quantities of IL-4 and IL-13, which drive B-cell isotype class switching from IgM to IgE and activate mast cells and eosinophils to mount an allergic-type response that clears varicella-zoster virus from infected neurons in the dorsal root ganglia","B":"These cells produce IL-17 and IL-22, which induce neutrophilic infiltration into dorsal root ganglia and promote epithelial barrier repair; IL-17 also stimulates hepatocyte production of acute-phase proteins that opsonize varicella-zoster viral particles for complement-mediated lysis","C":"These cells secrete TGF-beta and IL-10, which suppress CD4+ T-cell activation and prevent excessive inflammation in the dorsal root ganglia; this regulatory cytokine profile allows controlled viral clearance without immunopathologic damage to surrounding neural structures","D":"These cells produce IL-5 and IL-25 to activate eosinophils, which then release major basic protein and eosinophil cationic protein that directly neutralize varicella-zoster viral glycoproteins required for fusion with the neural cell plasma membrane","E":"These cells secrete IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages to enhance their intracellular microbicidal capacity, and produce IL-12-stimulated cytotoxic activity that creates an antiviral state in surrounding tissue; their secreted IFN-gamma also promotes CD8+ T-cell differentiation into effector cytotoxic T lymphocytes to amplify adaptive antiviral responses"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This vignette describes NK cells. NK cells not only kill via direct cytotoxicity (perforin/granzyme B) but also serve a critical regulatory function by secreting IFN-gamma, a potent macrophage-activating cytokine that enhances intracellular killing of phagocytosed microbes (classical macrophage activation). NK cell IFN-gamma also promotes Th1 polarization of CD4+ T cells and enhances CD8+ CTL differentiation — linking innate and adaptive responses. NK cell activity is stimulated by IL-12 from macrophages/DCs encountering virus. In HIV, even with controlled viremia, NK cell functional impairment persists, contributing to susceptibility to VZV reactivation. IL-4/IL-13 are Th2 cytokines; IL-17 is from Th17; TGF-beta/IL-10 are from Tregs; IL-5 is from Th2 cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NK cells produce IFN-gamma (activates macrophages) — same cytokine as Th1 cells. NK cells are stimulated by IL-12, IL-15, IFN-alpha, IFN-beta. NK cell activity is the reason tumor cells often upregulate MHC class I as an escape mechanism (reversed from the viral strategy). COMLEX: IFN-gamma is the key macrophage-activating cytokine in granuloma formation (TB, sarcoidosis) — directly relevant to COMLEX rheumatology and infectious disease presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Innate immune cells: NK cell cytokine secretion and IFN-gamma role in macrophage activation and antiviral defense"},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cells involved in the innate immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old man presents to the emergency department with fever (38.9 degrees Celsius), erythema, and purulent discharge from a wound on his right forearm after a construction injury two days ago. Culture of the wound grows Staphylococcus aureus. A peripheral blood smear reveals increased numbers of cells with multilobed nuclei and granular cytoplasm. Microscopy of a biopsy from the wound margin shows these cells actively undergoing phagocytosis of bacteria. These cells are known to be the first leukocytes recruited from postcapillary venules to sites of infection. Which of the following correctly describes the mechanism by which these cells eliminate the bacterial pathogen?","choices":{"A":"These neutrophils engulf bacteria into phagosomes that fuse with lysosomes to form phagolysosomes; within this compartment, NADPH oxidase generates reactive oxygen species via oxidative burst, myeloperoxidase catalyzes hypochlorous acid formation from hydrogen peroxide and chloride, and lysosomal enzymes including elastase and defensins directly degrade bacterial structures","B":"These neutrophils kill bacteria extracellularly by secreting large quantities of IgG antibodies that opsonize the bacteria and activate the classical complement pathway, generating membrane attack complex insertion into the gram-positive bacterial membrane to achieve osmotic lysis without phagocytosis","C":"These neutrophils eliminate bacteria by presenting bacterial antigens on MHC class II to CD4+ helper T cells in the wound tissue, inducing Th1 differentiation and IFN-gamma secretion that activates tissue-resident macrophages to kill phagocytosed bacteria via reactive nitrogen species","D":"These neutrophils kill bacteria by secreting IL-12 and IL-18 into the wound microenvironment, which activate local NK cells to recognize and lyse bacteria-coated host cells via perforin and granzyme B release in an antibody-independent cytotoxic mechanism","E":"These neutrophils eliminate bacteria by releasing large extracellular webs of chromatin-histone complexes decorated with antimicrobial peptides that trap and immobilize bacteria, after which complement opsonization and adaptive antibody responses are required for final bacterial clearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Neutrophils are the first recruited phagocytes at sites of infection, characterized by multilobed nuclei and a lifespan of only hours to days in tissues. They eliminate bacteria primarily through phagocytosis: bacteria are engulfed into phagosomes, which fuse with lysosomes to form phagolysosomes. Within this compartment, the NADPH oxidase complex generates superoxide and hydrogen peroxide (oxidative burst), myeloperoxidase converts H2O2 and chloride to hypochlorous acid (bleach), and lysosomal proteases (elastase, cathepsins) plus defensins degrade bacterial components. Neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs) are a separate mechanism (option D partially describes this) but require cell death. Neutrophils do not present antigen via MHC class II and are not the primary APC for T cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) = NADPH oxidase deficiency; recurrent catalase-positive organisms (S. aureus, Aspergillus, Nocardia, Serratia). NBT test is negative (cannot reduce nitroblue tetrazolium). Chediak-Higashi = giant granules in neutrophils, impaired chemotaxis and degranulation. MPO deficiency = usually mild, impaired hypochlorous acid production. COMLEX: Neutrophil dysfunction leads to recurrent pyogenic bacterial infections — important COMLEX immunodeficiency pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Innate immune cells: neutrophil phagocytosis, oxidative burst, and NADPH oxidase mechanism of bacterial killing"},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cells involved in the adaptive immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old college student presents with a three-day history of fever, sore throat, and malaise. Physical examination reveals exudative pharyngitis, bilateral cervical lymphadenopathy, and mild splenomegaly. A monospot test is positive. Peripheral blood smear shows atypical lymphocytes with abundant pale blue cytoplasm. Serology confirms Epstein-Barr virus infection. A virologist explains that a specific subset of CD4+ T helper cells is the dominant adaptive responder in acute EBV mononucleosis, secreting IFN-gamma to activate macrophages and IL-2 to drive cytotoxic T-lymphocyte expansion. Which of the following cytokines induces the differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells into this specific helper T-cell subset?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 secreted by mast cells and basophils at sites of epithelial injury is the primary inducing cytokine for differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells into the Th1 subset, which characteristically produces IFN-gamma and drives eosinophil activation and IgE class switching in humoral immunity","B":"IL-12 secreted by macrophages and dendritic cells that have encountered intracellular pathogens is the primary inducing cytokine for differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells into the Th1 subset, which characteristically produces IFN-gamma and activates macrophage classical activation and CD8+ cytotoxic T-lymphocyte expansion","C":"IL-6 and TGF-beta secreted by inflammatory macrophages at sites of EBV infection are the primary inducing cytokines for the Th1 subset, which characteristically produces IL-17 and activates neutrophilic infiltration and mucosal barrier repair in response to the viral infection","D":"IL-25 and IL-33 secreted by stressed epithelial cells are the primary inducing cytokines for differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells into the Th1 subset, which characteristically produces IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 to activate eosinophils and drive IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation","E":"TGF-beta and IL-10 secreted by resident tissue macrophages are the primary inducing cytokines for differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells into the Th1 subset, which characteristically suppresses immune responses by producing FOXP3 and activating tolerance to EBV-infected B cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Th1 cells are induced by IL-12 (from macrophages and dendritic cells that have detected intracellular pathogens) in combination with IFN-gamma. Th1 cells are characterized by production of IFN-gamma and IL-2: IFN-gamma classically activates macrophages to enhance killing of intracellular organisms (via CD40L-CD40 and IFN-gamma pathways), and IL-2 drives CTL expansion. EBV infects B cells (via CD21) and causes CD8+ T-cell hyperactivation (the atypical lymphocytes on smear are reactive CD8+ CTLs). Th2 cells are induced by IL-4 (from mast cells, basophils, ILC2s) and produce IL-4, IL-5, IL-13 — appropriate for helminths and allergy. Th17 cells are induced by IL-6 + TGF-beta and produce IL-17. Tregs are induced by TGF-beta + IL-2 and produce IL-10 and TGF-beta.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Th subset inducing cytokines — Th1: IL-12 + IFN-gamma; Th2: IL-4; Th17: IL-6 + TGF-beta; Treg: TGF-beta + IL-2. Effector cytokines — Th1: IFN-gamma, IL-2; Th2: IL-4, IL-5, IL-6, IL-10, IL-13; Th17: IL-17, IL-21, IL-22; Treg: IL-10, TGF-beta, IL-35. COMLEX: EBV mononucleosis is a classic clinical presentation requiring understanding of atypical lymphocytes (reactive CD8+ CTLs, not malignant), hepatosplenomegaly risk, and avoidance of amoxicillin (causes rash).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Adaptive immune cells: Th1 subset differentiation, IL-12 induction, and IFN-gamma effector function in intracellular infection"},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cells involved in the adaptive immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with multiple myeloma is admitted to the hospital for pneumonia caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae. Her serum protein electrophoresis shows a monoclonal spike, and her nephelometry reveals markedly reduced levels of normal polyclonal immunoglobulins. Her oncologist explains that myeloma involves the clonal proliferation of a specific adaptive immune cell that has terminally differentiated and no longer expresses surface immunoglobulin or MHC class II. The patient's susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria is directly related to deficiency of the normal functional products of this terminally differentiated cell type. Which of the following correctly identifies this terminally differentiated cell type and its functional products?","choices":{"A":"Memory B cells are terminally differentiated lymphocytes that have lost surface IgM expression and MHC class I; they circulate between blood and peripheral tissues and constitutively secrete IgG antibodies into the bloodstream at basal rates to maintain protective antibody titers against previously encountered pathogens","B":"Follicular helper T cells are terminally differentiated CD4+ lymphocytes that lose CD28 and TCR expression after antigen stimulation; they reside in lymph node germinal centers and constitutively secrete IL-4 and IL-21 to sustain ongoing antibody production against encapsulated bacteria","C":"Regulatory T cells are terminally differentiated CD4+ lymphocytes that lose FOXP3 expression after chronic antigen stimulation; they reside in the bone marrow and continuously produce IL-10 and TGF-beta to suppress normal B-cell maturation, explaining the reduced polyclonal immunoglobulin levels","D":"Plasma cells are terminally differentiated B lymphocytes that have lost surface immunoglobulin and MHC class II expression; they reside in the bone marrow and lymphoid organs and constitutively secrete large quantities of specific antibodies, with a single plasma cell capable of producing hundreds to thousands of antibody molecules per second","E":"Cytotoxic T lymphocytes are terminally differentiated CD8+ lymphocytes that lose their T-cell receptor expression in the bone marrow; they produce perforin and granzyme B constitutively, and myeloma involves their clonal expansion, reducing normal polyclonal antibody levels against encapsulated bacteria"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Plasma cells are the terminal effectors of humoral immunity and the cells involved in multiple myeloma. They are derived from antigen-activated B cells that have undergone class switching and affinity maturation in germinal centers. Plasma cells characteristically lose surface immunoglobulin (replaced by secretory forms) and MHC class II expression, differentiating them from naive B cells and memory B cells on flow cytometry. A single plasma cell can secrete hundreds to thousands of antibody molecules per second. In myeloma, clonal plasma cell expansion produces a monoclonal paraprotein spike while suppressing normal polyclonal immunoglobulin synthesis, leaving patients susceptible to encapsulated bacteria (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, N. meningitidis) that require opsonizing IgG for phagocytic clearance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Plasma cells = terminally differentiated B cells; lost surface Ig and MHC II; express CD138 (syndecan-1); found in bone marrow. Multiple myeloma = clonal plasma cell proliferation; CRAB criteria (Calcium elevation, Renal disease, Anemia, Bone lesions); Bence-Jones proteins = free light chains in urine. COMLEX: Myeloma-associated immunodeficiency leading to encapsulated bacterial infections is a classic COMLEX clinical presentation requiring pattern recognition.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Adaptive immune cells: plasma cell terminal differentiation, loss of surface markers, and constitutive antibody secretion function"},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cells involved in the adaptive immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old man presents to his physician with a three-week history of fatigue, night sweats, and a five-pound weight loss. CT scan reveals mediastinal lymphadenopathy and a pulmonary nodule. Bronchoscopy with transbronchial biopsy shows necrotizing granulomas, and acid-fast stain reveals mycobacteria. An immunologist explains that effective clearance of Mycobacterium tuberculosis requires a specific subset of CD4+ T cells that recognize mycobacterial peptides on MHC class II of macrophages and secrete a cytokine that greatly amplifies macrophage intracellular bactericidal activity. Which combination of receptor-ligand interactions between the T cell and macrophage is required for full macrophage activation in this response?","choices":{"A":"CD28 expressed on Th2 CD4+ T cells binds B7 proteins on macrophages, and IL-4 secreted by Th2 cells engages IL-4 receptor on macrophages; together these signals drive alternative macrophage activation characterized by arginase expression and anti-inflammatory cytokine production that clears mycobacteria from granulomas","B":"TCR expressed on CD8+ cytotoxic T cells binds mycobacterial peptide-MHC class I on macrophages, and perforin secreted by the CTLs directly punctures the macrophage membrane; this releases mycobacteria into the extracellular space where complement and antibodies provide final clearance","C":"CD40 ligand expressed on activated Th1 CD4+ T cells binds CD40 on macrophages, and IFN-gamma secreted by the same Th1 cells engages IFN-gamma receptor on macrophages; together these two signals drive classical macrophage activation, inducing NADPH oxidase activity and reactive nitrogen species generation to kill intracellular mycobacteria","D":"PD-1 expressed on exhausted CD4+ T cells binds PD-L1 on macrophages, and IL-10 secreted by regulatory T cells engages IL-10 receptor on macrophages; this suppressive interaction is paradoxically required to achieve full macrophage bactericidal activation against intracellular mycobacteria in granulomas","E":"FasL expressed on activated Th17 CD4+ T cells binds Fas on macrophages, triggering macrophage apoptosis and release of mycobacteria into the extracellular space; complement then opsonizes the released bacteria and neutrophils phagocytose and kill them via oxidative burst"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Classical macrophage activation — the key effector mechanism against intracellular pathogens like M. tuberculosis — requires two coordinated signals from Th1 CD4+ T cells. Signal 1: CD40L (CD154) on the T cell binds CD40 on the macrophage, providing direct cell-contact activation. Signal 2: IFN-gamma secreted by the Th1 cell binds IFN-gamma receptor on the macrophage. Together, these signals activate the macrophage to produce NADPH oxidase-derived reactive oxygen species, nitric oxide synthase-derived NO (reactive nitrogen species), and enhance lysosomal killing capacity — a state of heightened bactericidal activity. Without adequate Th1 responses (as in HIV-induced CD4+ depletion), mycobacteria persist and disseminate. Alternative macrophage activation (Th2-induced via IL-4/IL-13) promotes tissue repair, not killing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classical macrophage activation = IFN-gamma (from Th1 and NK cells) + CD40L. Alternative macrophage activation = IL-4, IL-13 (from Th2). Granuloma = central epithelioid macrophages surrounded by lymphocytes; caused by persistent intracellular organisms (TB, leprosy, fungi) or foreign material. IFN-gamma can also cause hypercalcemia in granulomatous disease via 1-alpha-hydroxylase activation. COMLEX: Macrophage-T cell interactions are central to understanding granuloma formation — key for COMLEX rheumatology and infectious disease vignettes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Adaptive immune cells: Th1 CD4+ T cell activation of macrophages via CD40L-CD40 and IFN-gamma in intracellular pathogen clearance"},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanistic differences between innate, cell-mediated, and humoral immunity.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old organ transplant immunologist is explaining to a medical student why a patient who received a kidney transplant four years ago developed acute rejection when his immunosuppressive therapy was inadvertently held for two weeks. The patient presents with rising creatinine, graft tenderness, and oliguria. Biopsy of the graft shows a dense infiltrate of mononuclear cells and tubulitis. The immunologist notes that this rejection is mediated by lymphocytes that had formed memory during the initial sensitization phase shortly after transplantation. She contrasts this with the innate response that occurred immediately after reperfusion injury. Which of the following best explains why the adaptive memory response to the allograft is both faster and more vigorous than the original sensitization response, while the innate response to reperfusion injury was identical in character to any other inflammatory stimulus?","choices":{"A":"Adaptive immunity generates long-lived memory lymphocytes with expanded clone sizes and lower activation thresholds after first antigen exposure; upon re-exposure, these memory cells respond rapidly with minimal co-stimulatory requirements, explaining the accelerated rejection; in contrast, innate immunity relies on germline-encoded receptors that recognize conserved damage signals identically at first and subsequent exposures without generating memory","B":"Adaptive immunity generates secreted complement proteins that are stored in the graft vasculature after initial sensitization and immediately activate the membrane attack complex upon re-exposure; innate immunity is slower because it requires de novo synthesis of pattern recognition receptors each time a new inflammatory stimulus is encountered at the graft site","C":"Adaptive memory responses are faster because plasma cells continuously secrete MHC-specific antibodies into the circulation during the quiescent period, reaching concentrations sufficient to immediately lyse the graft endothelium upon reinstatement of normal blood flow after immunosuppression withdrawal","D":"Innate immunity generates pathogen-specific memory through epigenetic reprogramming of macrophages after the initial reperfusion injury, explaining why the innate response is faster on re-exposure; adaptive memory is slower because lymphocytes must re-enter the thymus for re-education before mounting a secondary response","E":"Adaptive memory responses are faster because memory T cells constitutively circulate through the graft tissue monitoring for antigen and do not require lymph node migration or dendritic cell presentation to initiate rejection; innate reperfusion injury responses are slower because TLRs must be de novo synthesized by macrophages after each inflammatory episode"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Memory lymphocytes are the mechanistic basis of faster secondary adaptive immune responses. After initial antigen exposure and clonal expansion, most effector cells undergo apoptosis but a subset survives as memory cells: they are more numerous than the original naive pool, have lower activation thresholds (less co-stimulation required), respond faster (hours rather than days), and produce greater quantities of effector molecules. This explains why allograft rejection accelerates with each subsequent rejection episode. Innate immunity, in contrast, uses germline-encoded PRRs (TLRs, NLRs) that do not undergo somatic change between exposures — there is no classical innate memory (though trained immunity via epigenetic reprogramming is a real phenomenon, it is distinct from adaptive memory and not the primary tested concept). Innate responses to reperfusion injury (via DAMPs) are quantitatively similar each time.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memory cell characteristics — lower activation threshold, faster kinetics, greater magnitude, less co-stimulation required. Acute cellular rejection = T-cell mediated (tubulitis and interstitial infiltrate on biopsy); treated with high-dose steroids and anti-thymocyte globulin. Hyperacute rejection = preformed antibodies against donor HLA (Type II hypersensitivity), occurs within minutes. COMLEX: Transplant rejection types are directly testable and map to hypersensitivity classification — a COMLEX favorite topic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — Innate vs adaptive immunity: memory cell generation and accelerated secondary response mechanism versus germline-encoded innate receptor constancy"},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanistic differences between innate, cell-mediated, and humoral immunity.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old man who recently emigrated from a country with high TB prevalence presents to the infectious disease clinic. A tuberculin skin test placed 48 hours ago shows a 22-mm induration. He has no symptoms and his chest X-ray is normal, consistent with latent TB infection. A resident explains to a student that the induration reflects a specific type of immune reaction that accounts for the delayed 48-to-72-hour time course, the mononuclear cell-rich infiltrate, and the absence of antibody involvement. This reaction is mediated by sensitized T lymphocytes that release cytokines upon re-encountering tuberculin antigen, recruiting and activating macrophages. Which of the following best explains why the peak induration occurs at 48 to 72 hours rather than within minutes as seen in immediate hypersensitivity?","choices":{"A":"The 48-to-72-hour delay occurs because IgE antibodies must be produced de novo by plasma cells upon re-exposure to tuberculin antigen; once synthesized, IgE binds Fc epsilon receptors on mast cells, triggering degranulation and the release of vasoactive amines that cause the observed induration at the injection site","B":"Type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity is mediated by previously sensitized Th1 CD4+ T cells and CD8+ CTLs that must recognize antigen via MHC-TCR interactions, clonally activate, produce cytokines, and recruit and activate macrophages — a multi-step cellular process requiring 24 to 72 hours; unlike type I hypersensitivity, no preformed IgE or mast cell mediators are available for immediate release","C":"The 48-to-72-hour delay occurs because complement must be activated via the lectin pathway after tuberculin-specific IgM antibodies form immune complexes that deposit in the dermis; complement-derived C5a then recruits neutrophils that produce the fibrinoid necrosis and induration characteristic of the tuberculin reaction","D":"The 48-to-72-hour delay occurs because NK cells require IL-12 priming from tissue-resident macrophages before recognizing PPD-coated dermal cells; once activated, NK cells release IFN-gamma and perforin, causing keratinocyte apoptosis that produces the characteristic induration and central clearing seen at the injection site","E":"The 48-to-72-hour delay occurs because B lymphocytes must undergo somatic hypermutation and affinity maturation in regional lymph node germinal centers before producing high-affinity IgG antibodies that opsonize tuberculin antigen and initiate Fc receptor-mediated complement activation in the dermis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The tuberculin (PPD) reaction is the classic example of type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity, a T-cell-mediated reaction that does not involve antibodies or preformed mediators. In previously sensitized individuals, tuberculin antigen is taken up and presented on MHC class II by dermal APCs to memory Th1 CD4+ T cells. These cells become activated, secrete IFN-gamma (activating macrophages to produce cytokines and lysosomal enzymes) and TNF (promoting local inflammation and vascular changes), recruit monocytes and lymphocytes, and generate the characteristic indurated, erythematous lesion. This multi-step process of T-cell recognition, cytokine secretion, and macrophage recruitment takes 24 to 72 hours. Type I hypersensitivity (IgE-mast cell mediated) occurs within minutes because preformed IgE is already bound to mast cells, and antigen cross-linking triggers immediate degranulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type IV DTH is the ONLY hypersensitivity type that does NOT involve antibodies — purely T-cell and macrophage mediated. The 4 hypersensitivity types: I (IgE, mast cells, immediate), II (IgG/IgM against cell surface), III (immune complexes), IV (T-cells, delayed 48-72h). Contact dermatitis, poison ivy, graft rejection, and MS are all Type IV. COMLEX: DTH reactions are relevant to understanding somatic dysfunction and chronic inflammatory states evaluated and treated with OMM.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — Mechanistic differences: cell-mediated (Type IV DTH) versus immediate (Type I IgE-mast cell) hypersensitivity and time course explanation"},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanistic differences between innate, cell-mediated, and humoral immunity.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the pediatric infectious disease clinic after his third episode of Streptococcus pneumoniae bacteremia in 18 months. Previous episodes were also caused by encapsulated bacteria including Haemophilus influenzae type b and Neisseria meningitidis. His spleen is structurally intact on imaging. Flow cytometry shows markedly reduced CD19+ B lymphocytes, absent circulating immunoglobulins of all isotypes, and normal T-cell counts and function. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in a tyrosine kinase critical for B-cell development in the bone marrow. The infectious disease fellow explains that this patient's susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria results from the specific arm of adaptive immunity that is absent. Which of the following best explains why deficiency of this immune arm particularly predisposes to encapsulated bacterial infections rather than to viral or fungal infections?","choices":{"A":"Cell-mediated immunity carried out by CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes is the primary defense against encapsulated bacteria because CTLs directly recognize polysaccharide capsule components on MHC class I and kill bacteria via perforin injection; without CTLs, encapsulated bacteria evade cytotoxic killing and disseminate hematogenously","B":"Innate NK cell activity is the primary defense against encapsulated bacteria because NK cells recognize reduced MHC class I expression on bacteria and activate complement-mediated lysis; without NK cells, encapsulated organisms use the missing-self mechanism to evade NK surveillance and persist in the bloodstream","C":"Th17-mediated cell-mediated immunity is the primary defense against encapsulated bacteria because IL-17 induces neutrophil-rich mucosal responses that trap encapsulated bacteria in neutrophil extracellular traps before bacteremia occurs; without Th17 function, bacteria cross mucosal barriers and enter the bloodstream causing recurrent bacteremia","D":"Humoral immunity mediated by antibodies is the primary defense against encapsulated bacteria because the polysaccharide capsule resists phagocytosis without opsonization; IgG antibodies opsonize the bacteria for phagocytic uptake via Fc receptor binding, and IgM and IgG activate classical complement to generate C3b opsonin and C5a chemoattractant; without antibodies, encapsulated bacteria evade innate phagocytosis and cause invasive disease","E":"Regulatory T-cell activity is the primary defense against encapsulated bacteria because Tregs suppress excessive IFN-gamma production that would otherwise impair phagocyte function; without Tregs, unchecked IFN-gamma paralyzes neutrophil phagocytosis and allows encapsulated bacteria to escape innate immune clearance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This vignette describes X-linked agammaglobulinemia (Bruton disease), caused by mutations in Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK), which is required for B-cell maturation beyond the pro-B stage. The result is absent mature B cells, absent plasma cells, and absent circulating immunoglobulins of all isotypes. The susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, N. meningitidis) directly reflects the loss of humoral immunity: encapsulated bacteria use their polysaccharide capsule to resist phagocytosis by blocking complement-mediated and direct receptor-mediated uptake. Opsonizing IgG and complement activation (via classical pathway) are required to breach this defense. Without IgG to opsonize and without complement C3b generated by IgM/IgG-mediated classical activation, phagocytes cannot efficiently engulf these pathogens. Viral infections are controlled by cell-mediated immunity (CD8+ CTLs), which is intact in this patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: X-linked agammaglobulinemia = BTK mutation; absent B cells, absent Ig, normal T cells; presents after 6 months when maternal IgG wanes; recurrent encapsulated bacterial infections. Most common antibody deficiency = selective IgA deficiency. Common variable immunodeficiency = acquired pan-hypogammaglobulinemia in adults. All antibody deficiencies share susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria. COMLEX: Recognizing which organisms require humoral immunity (encapsulated bacteria) vs cell-mediated immunity (intracellular pathogens, viruses, fungi) is a high-yield COMLEX pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — Mechanistic differences: humoral immunity (antibody-dependent opsonization) as the critical defense against encapsulated bacteria versus cell-mediated immunity for intracellular pathogens"}]},{"sdlNumber":28,"title":"Lymphocyte Development & Immune Activation","questions":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the process of lymphocyte development.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student studying hematopoiesis notes that common lymphoid progenitors (CLPs) can give rise to multiple cell types. Which of the following correctly identifies the primary anatomical site where B cells complete their early (antigen-independent) development?","choices":{"A":"Thymus, where progenitor cells differentiate into mature, antigen-competent B cells through positive and negative selection","B":"Spleen, where lymphoid progenitors first commit to the B cell lineage and undergo initial VDJ recombination","C":"Lymph nodes, where VDJ recombination generates the initial B cell receptor before antigen encounter","D":"Peripheral blood, where naive B cells complete their maturation after exiting the bone marrow progenitor niche","E":"Bone marrow, where pro-B cells undergo VDJ recombination to generate surface IgM-positive immature B cells before exiting as naive mature B cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"B cell development occurs entirely in the bone marrow through antigen-independent stages: pro-B → pre-B → immature B → transitional B, with cells exiting as naive mature B cells co-expressing IgM and IgD. The thymus is exclusively the site of T cell development. The spleen is a secondary lymphoid organ where transitional B cells undergo further maturation but not initial commitment or VDJ recombination. Lymph nodes are sites of antigen-dependent activation, not primary lymphocyte development. Peripheral blood is a transit compartment, not a developmental site.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic trap — DiGeorge (22q11 deletion → thymic aplasia) → absent T cells, normal B cells; X-linked agammaglobulinemia (BTK mutation) → absent B cells, normal T cells. COMLEX: Absent T cells vs absent B cells produce distinct clinical phenotypes with different infection susceptibilities.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — Lymphocyte development: CLPs give rise to B cells in bone marrow and T cells in thymus via ordered developmental stages"},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the process of lymphocyte development.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the sequence of T cell developmental stages as lymphocyte progenitors mature in the thymus?","choices":{"A":"Double-negative (DN) → double-positive (DP) → CD4+ or CD8+ single-positive (SP) naive T cell","B":"Double-positive (DP) → double-negative (DN) → CD4+ or CD8+ single-positive (SP) naive T cell","C":"Single-positive (SP) → double-positive (DP) → double-negative (DN) T cell exiting to periphery","D":"Pro-T cell → single-positive (SP) → double-positive (DP) T cell undergoing receptor editing","E":"Double-negative (DN) → single-positive (SP) → double-positive (DP) T cell entering lymph nodes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"T cell precursors enter the thymus as double-negative (DN) cells lacking both CD4 and CD8. DN cells rearrange the TCR β-chain and become double-positive (DP) cells expressing both CD4 and CD8. DP cells undergo positive selection in the thymic cortex (survival if TCR engages self-MHC) and negative selection in the medulla (deletion if TCR binds self-antigen too strongly), then emerge as single-positive (SP) CD4+ or CD8+ naive T cells. None of the other sequences reflect actual thymic biology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DN→DP→SP is heavily tested; remember TCRβ rearrangement and pre-TCR checkpoint occur at DN stage; positive selection occurs at DP stage in cortex. COMLEX: Clinical correlation — thymic aplasia (DiGeorge) blocks all T cell development; CD4/CD8 SP fate is determined by which MHC class each TCR recognizes during positive selection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — Lymphocyte development: CLPs give rise to B cells in bone marrow and T cells in thymus via ordered developmental stages"},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the process of lymphocyte development.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher studying B cell development finds that a specific developmental checkpoint tests whether the immunoglobulin heavy chain is functional before light chain rearrangement begins. Which of the following best describes what must occur for a developing B cell to pass this pre-BCR checkpoint?","choices":{"A":"The κ or λ light chain must rearrange successfully and pair with a surrogate heavy chain to form a pre-BCR on the cell surface","B":"The μ heavy chain protein must pair successfully with surrogate light chains (λ5 and VpreB) to form a pre-BCR that signals survival and proliferation of the pre-B cell","C":"The VDJ-rearranged heavy chain gene must be transcribed and translated without undergoing allelic exclusion at the second allele","D":"The TCRβ chain must pair with pTα to form a pre-TCR that confirms commitment to the B cell lineage in bone marrow","E":"The mature IgM molecule must be assembled and expressed on the surface before light chain rearrangement is permitted"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At the pro-B to pre-B cell transition, the newly rearranged μ heavy chain is tested by pairing with surrogate light chains — λ5 and VpreB — to form the pre-BCR complex. Successful pre-BCR signaling (via Igα/Igβ) indicates a functional heavy chain, providing survival and proliferative signals and imposing allelic exclusion on the second heavy chain allele. Cells that fail to form a functional pre-BCR undergo apoptosis. Light chain rearrangement (κ first, then λ) begins only after the pre-BCR checkpoint is passed. The pre-TCR (TCRβ/pTα) is the analogous checkpoint in T cell development, not B cell development. Full IgM assembly requires light chains and comes after this checkpoint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pre-BCR uses surrogate light chain (λ5 + VpreB); BTK (Bruton's tyrosine kinase) is required for pre-BCR signaling — BTK mutation blocks development at pro-B to pre-B transition causing XLA. COMLEX: Allelic exclusion at the heavy chain locus ensures each B cell has one BCR specificity; enforced by pre-BCR signaling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — Lymphocyte development: CLPs give rise to B cells in bone marrow and T cells in thymus via ordered developmental stages"},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the formation and diversity of B cell receptors (BCRs) and T cell receptors (TCRs).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes the gene segments used to generate diversity in the immunoglobulin heavy chain versus the light chain during B cell development?","choices":{"A":"Both heavy and light chains use V, D, and J segments, with N-nucleotide addition occurring at all junctions equally","B":"Heavy chains use only V and J segments; light chains use V, D, and J segments to maximize diversity at the Fc region","C":"Heavy chains use V, D, and J segments; light chains (κ and λ) use only V and J segments without a D-segment contribution","D":"Light chains use V, D, and J segments; heavy chains use only V and J segments because the D-region is absent from the IgH locus","E":"Both heavy and light chains use only V and J segments, with D-segments exclusive to TCR αβ diversity generation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The immunoglobulin heavy chain locus undergoes V-D-J recombination, incorporating V, D, and J gene segments. Light chains (both κ and λ) undergo only V-J recombination — there is no D segment in light chain loci. This distinction is significant: the extra D segment in heavy chains greatly increases CDR3 diversity, and TdT-mediated N-nucleotide addition at D-J and V-D junctions is more prominent in heavy chains. For TCRs, α and γ chains use VJ recombination; β and δ chains use VDJ recombination — analogous to immunoglobulin heavy chains.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heavy chain = VDJ; Light chain = VJ only — classic board fact. TCR β and δ chains also use VDJ. COMLEX: RAG1/RAG2 catalyze all V(D)J recombination; D-segment presence in heavy chains allows 3 junctional sites vs 1 in light chains, dramatically expanding diversity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — BCR/TCR diversity arises from VDJ recombination (RAG1/2) and TdT-mediated junctional N-nucleotide addition"},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the formation and diversity of B cell receptors (BCRs) and T cell receptors (TCRs).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following enzymes is responsible for adding non-templated (N) nucleotides at V(D)J junctions during lymphocyte receptor development, and what is its primary clinical significance?","choices":{"A":"RAG1 adds N-nucleotides at VDJ junctions after introducing double-strand breaks at recombination signal sequences; elevated RAG1 distinguishes ALL from AML","B":"Activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID) adds N-nucleotides at VDJ junctions in pro-B cells; AID expression distinguishes lymphoblastic from myeloid precursors","C":"DNA ligase IV adds N-nucleotides at VDJ junctions during NHEJ repair; its expression identifies cells undergoing active V(D)J recombination","D":"Artemis nuclease adds N-nucleotides at VDJ junctions by hairpin processing; Artemis mutations cause SCID with absent T and B cells but preserved NK cells","E":"Terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase (TdT) adds random N-nucleotides at junctions, generating junctional diversity; TdT expression in blast cells is a diagnostic marker for acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"TdT (terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase) is a template-independent DNA polymerase that adds random nucleotides at the junctions between V, D, and J segments. These N-nucleotides contribute significantly to BCR and TCR diversity at CDR3. TdT is expressed only in immature lymphoid precursors actively undergoing V(D)J recombination (pro-B/pre-B cells and pro-T/pre-T cells), making it a valuable immunohistochemical marker for ALL. RAG1/RAG2 introduce double-strand breaks but do not add nucleotides. AID mediates somatic hypermutation and class switching in mature germinal center B cells, not V(D)J junctions. Artemis processes hairpin intermediates but does not add nucleotides.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TdT+ + MPO- = ALL; TdT- + MPO+ = AML; ALL is the most common pediatric malignancy. COMLEX: TdT positivity confirms immature lymphoid origin; B-ALL commonly t(12;21) TEL-AML1; T-ALL commonly presents with mediastinal mass.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — BCR/TCR diversity arises from VDJ recombination (RAG1/2) and TdT-mediated junctional N-nucleotide addition"},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the formation and diversity of B cell receptors (BCRs) and T cell receptors (TCRs).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient is found to have a homozygous loss-of-function mutation in the RAG2 gene. Which of the following best describes the expected immunologic phenotype?","choices":{"A":"Absence of B cells only, with normal T cell counts, because RAG2 is exclusively expressed in bone marrow B cell progenitors","B":"Absence of T cells only, with normal B cell counts, because RAG2 is exclusively expressed in thymic T cell progenitors","C":"Normal B and T cell counts with severely impaired somatic hypermutation and absent affinity maturation in germinal centers","D":"Absence of both mature B and T lymphocytes due to failure of V(D)J recombination in all lymphoid progenitors, resulting in severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID)","E":"Normal lymphocyte counts but absent NK cells, because RAG2 is required for NK cell receptor diversification through gene rearrangement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"RAG1 and RAG2 together form the RAG recombinase, which is essential for initiating V(D)J recombination in all lymphoid precursors — both B cell progenitors in the bone marrow and T cell progenitors in the thymus. Loss of RAG2 function completely abolishes the ability to generate BCRs and TCRs, resulting in absence of both mature B and T cells (T-B-NK+ SCID). NK cells do not undergo V(D)J recombination and are therefore unaffected. Somatic hypermutation is mediated by AID in germinal centers, not RAG. Partial/hypomorphic RAG mutations cause Omenn syndrome (oligoclonal T cells, eosinophilia, erythroderma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RAG1/RAG2 deficiency → T-B-NK+ SCID; distinguish from γc mutation (T-B+NK- SCID) and ADA deficiency (T-B-NK- SCID). Omenn syndrome = hypomorphic RAG mutations. COMLEX: RAG deficiency is autosomal recessive; definitive treatment is hematopoietic stem cell transplant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — BCR/TCR diversity arises from VDJ recombination (RAG1/2) and TdT-mediated junctional N-nucleotide addition"},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A double-positive (DP) thymocyte's T cell receptor is unable to engage any self-MHC molecule on cortical thymic epithelial cells during positive selection. Which of the following best describes the fate of this thymocyte?","choices":{"A":"The thymocyte undergoes clonal deletion because failure to bind MHC indicates it may be self-reactive in the periphery","B":"The thymocyte undergoes apoptosis via \"death by neglect\" because it receives no MHC-dependent survival signal in the thymic cortex","C":"The thymocyte differentiates into a regulatory T cell (Treg) because non-MHC-restricted cells are redirected to suppressive lineages","D":"The thymocyte exits the thymus as an anergic naive T cell that cannot respond to antigen but persists in the periphery","E":"The thymocyte re-expresses RAG genes and undergoes TCR receptor editing to generate a new receptor with MHC reactivity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Positive selection occurs in the thymic cortex where DP thymocytes test their TCRs against self-MHC molecules displayed on cortical thymic epithelial cells (cTECs). Only thymocytes whose TCRs can engage self-MHC with appropriate (low-to-intermediate) affinity receive survival signals via ERK and Bcl-2 upregulation. Thymocytes that fail to engage any self-MHC receive no survival signal and die by apoptosis — a process called \"death by neglect.\" This ensures that only MHC-restricted T cells (capable of recognizing antigen presented on self-MHC) survive to enter the periphery. Negative selection (clonal deletion) occurs in the medulla for T cells with too-high affinity for self-antigen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Positive selection in cortex (cTECs) → survival if TCR binds self-MHC (low affinity); death by neglect if no MHC binding. Negative selection in medulla (mTECs, dendritic cells) → deletion if TCR binds self-antigen too strongly. COMLEX: MHC restriction established during positive selection determines CD4 vs CD8 fate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Central tolerance: thymic positive/negative selection and AIRE-driven self-antigen expression eliminate autoreactive lymphocytes"},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl presents with chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis since age 2, hypoparathyroidism, and newly diagnosed primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease). Her serum anti-adrenal cortex antibodies are positive. Genetic testing reveals a homozygous loss-of-function mutation in AIRE. Which of the following best explains the pathogenesis of her autoimmune endocrine disease?","choices":{"A":"Loss of AIRE disrupts positive selection by preventing MHC class II expression on cortical epithelial cells, resulting in absent CD4+ T cells that cannot suppress autoreactive B cells","B":"Loss of AIRE impairs VDJ recombination efficiency, leading to a globally reduced T cell repertoire unable to mount anti-fungal Th17 responses","C":"Loss of AIRE prevents FOXP3 upregulation in developing thymocytes, eliminating natural Treg formation and peripheral B cell suppression","D":"Loss of AIRE disrupts thymic stromal lymphopoietin (TSLP) production, impairing dendritic cell-mediated negative selection of B cell progenitors in bone marrow","E":"Loss of AIRE prevents ectopic expression of peripheral tissue antigens (adrenal, parathyroid, and other organ antigens) by medullary thymic epithelial cells, allowing autoreactive T cells specific for these antigens to escape negative selection and attack peripheral organs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"AIRE (autoimmune regulator) is expressed by medullary thymic epithelial cells (mTECs) and drives ectopic transcription of peripheral tissue-specific antigens — including insulin, adrenal cortex antigens, parathyroid antigens, and many others. This ectopic expression allows T cells with high-affinity TCRs for these self-antigens to undergo negative selection (clonal deletion) in the medulla. Loss of AIRE means these antigens are never displayed in the thymus, so reactive T cells escape and cause autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1 (APS-1/APECED): mucocutaneous candidiasis + hypoparathyroidism + Addison's disease (classic triad). AIRE does not affect VDJ recombination, FOXP3 expression, or MHC class II expression directly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AIRE deficiency → APS-1/APECED (autosomal recessive); triad of mucocutaneous candidiasis + hypoparathyroidism + adrenal insufficiency. COMLEX: AIRE-driven ectopic antigen expression is the key mechanism of central T cell tolerance to peripheral tissues; AIRE mutations clinically manifest as multi-endocrine autoimmunity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Central tolerance: thymic positive/negative selection and AIRE-driven self-antigen expression eliminate autoreactive lymphocytes"},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An immature B cell in the bone marrow expresses a surface IgM that strongly crosslinks upon encountering a self-antigen abundantly present in the bone marrow environment. Which of the following is the PRIMARY mechanism used to prevent this self-reactive B cell from entering the periphery?","choices":{"A":"Clonal anergy, in which the B cell downregulates surface IgM and exits as a functionally unresponsive cell unable to be activated in the periphery","B":"Clonal deletion, in which immediate strong BCR crosslinking triggers apoptosis as the primary and first-line response to self-antigen","C":"Receptor editing, in which RAG genes are reactivated and the light chain locus undergoes additional rearrangements to replace the self-reactive BCR with one that is no longer autoreactive","D":"Isotype class switching, in which the B cell switches from IgM to IgD to reduce its avidity for the bone marrow self-antigen","E":"BCR internalization, in which the self-reactive receptor is endocytosed and the B cell proceeds through development with no surface BCR"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When an immature B cell encounters a bone marrow self-antigen that strongly crosslinks its IgM BCR, the primary rescue mechanism is receptor editing: RAG1 and RAG2 are reactivated, and additional light chain VJ rearrangements replace the self-reactive light chain with a new one that creates a non-autoreactive BCR. This allows the B cell to survive with a new, non-self-reactive specificity. If receptor editing fails after multiple attempts, the cell then undergoes clonal deletion (apoptosis) as a secondary mechanism. Clonal anergy is predominantly a peripheral tolerance mechanism for B cells that encounter antigen without T cell help. Isotype class switching (AID-dependent) does not occur in immature bone marrow B cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central B cell tolerance: receptor editing (primary) → clonal deletion (backup); occurs in bone marrow. Peripheral B cell tolerance: anergy (self-antigen without T cell help). COMLEX: Receptor editing is unique to B cells — T cells do not perform TCR receptor editing; T cell rescue from autoreactivity occurs differently (some become Tregs).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Central tolerance: thymic positive/negative selection and AIRE-driven self-antigen expression eliminate autoreactive lymphocytes"},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A naive CD4+ T cell encounters an antigen-presenting cell in the lymph node. Signal 1 is delivered by TCR/CD3 engagement with a peptide-MHC class II complex. Which molecule provides the critical co-stimulatory Signal 2, and what is the consequence if Signal 2 is absent when Signal 1 is received?","choices":{"A":"CD28 on the T cell binds B7 (CD80/CD86) on the APC; absence of Signal 2 leads to T cell anergy — a state of functional unresponsiveness in which the cell cannot produce IL-2 or proliferate upon antigen re-exposure","B":"CD40 on the T cell binds CD40L on the APC; absence of Signal 2 causes immediate activation-induced cell death of the T cell via the Fas-FasL pathway","C":"CTLA-4 on the T cell binds B7 on the APC; absence of Signal 2 permits T cell hyperactivation and causes autoimmune tissue destruction","D":"LFA-1 on the T cell binds ICAM-1 on the APC; absence of Signal 2 redirects the T cell to the splenic marginal zone for antigen-independent activation","E":"PD-1 on the T cell binds PD-L1 on the APC; absence of Signal 2 enhances effector T cell function and accelerates the immune response"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Effective T cell activation requires two distinct signals. Signal 1 (antigen-specific) is provided by TCR/CD3 binding to peptide-MHC class II complex on the APC. Signal 2 (co-stimulation, antigen-nonspecific) is provided by CD28 on the T cell binding B7-1 (CD80) or B7-2 (CD86) on the APC. CD28 engagement activates PI3K, promotes IL-2 production, and supports T cell survival and proliferation. When Signal 1 is received without Signal 2 — as occurs when resting parenchymal cells present self-antigen but lack B7 — the T cell becomes anergic: unable to produce IL-2 or mount a response even on subsequent re-exposure to antigen. CTLA-4 is an inhibitory receptor that competes with CD28 for B7, not a co-activator. PD-1 is also inhibitory. CD40/CD40L interaction is between activated T helper cells and B cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two-signal model: Signal 1 (TCR/MHC) + Signal 2 (CD28/B7) → activation; Signal 1 alone → anergy. Abatacept (CTLA-4-Ig) blocks Signal 2 therapeutically in RA. COMLEX: Anergy is a key peripheral tolerance mechanism protecting against autoimmunity at sites lacking professional APCs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — T/B cell activation requires two signals; CD28/B7 co-stimulation and CD40L/CD40 interaction are required for class switching and memory"},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-year-old child receives a 13-valent conjugate pneumococcal vaccine (PCV13) rather than a plain 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine (PPSV23). Which of the following best explains why the conjugate vaccine produces superior immunologic memory in children under 2 years of age compared to the polysaccharide vaccine?","choices":{"A":"Conjugate vaccines directly activate innate immune NK cells and macrophages to destroy encapsulated bacteria, bypassing the need for adaptive B cell responses entirely","B":"Plain polysaccharide vaccines are degraded by complement before reaching B cells, while conjugate vaccines are protected from complement lysis by the carrier protein","C":"Conjugate vaccines provide stronger TLR4 stimulation via the carrier protein, generating a superior innate cytokine environment that drives non-specific IgG production","D":"Plain polysaccharide vaccines activate B cells via T-dependent mechanisms in young children, but the T cell help provided is qualitatively inferior to that in adults","E":"Conjugating polysaccharide to a carrier protein converts a T-independent antigen into a T-dependent antigen, enabling germinal center formation, affinity maturation, class switching to IgG, and long-lived memory B cell generation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bacterial polysaccharides are T-independent antigens: they crosslink multiple BCRs simultaneously but cannot be processed and presented on MHC class II to T helper cells, resulting in primarily short-lived IgM production without germinal center reactions, affinity maturation, or memory B cells. Children under 2 years have immature marginal zone B cells incapable of effective T-independent responses. Conjugating polysaccharide to a protein carrier (e.g., diphtheria toxoid) creates a T-dependent antigen — the carrier protein is processed and presented on MHC class II, activating carrier-specific Th cells, which then provide CD40L/CD40 and cytokine signals to polysaccharide-specific B cells. This enables IgG class switching, affinity maturation, and durable immunologic memory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T-independent antigens → IgM only, no class switching, no memory; T-dependent antigens → class switching to IgG/IgA/IgE, affinity maturation, memory B cells. Hyper-IgM syndrome (CD40L deficiency): stuck at IgM, cannot switch. COMLEX: Conjugate vaccines overcome the inability of T-independent antigens to generate memory — critical for infant vaccination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — T/B cell activation requires two signals; CD28/B7 co-stimulation and CD40L/CD40 interaction are required for class switching and memory"},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following molecular interactions is required for B cells to undergo immunoglobulin class switch recombination from IgM to IgG, IgA, or IgE?","choices":{"A":"BCR crosslinking by multivalent antigen alone is sufficient to trigger AID expression and initiate class switch recombination without T cell involvement","B":"CD40L (CD154) on activated CD4+ T helper cells binds CD40 on B cells, delivering the critical licensing signal that induces AID (activation-induced cytidine deaminase) expression required for class switch recombination","C":"IL-4 signaling through the common gamma chain is sufficient to drive class switch recombination to IgE without requiring CD40/CD40L interaction","D":"CTLA-4 on T cells binding CD86 on B cells provides the necessary co-stimulatory signal to activate B cell AID expression and isotype switching","E":"MHC class II on B cells presenting antigen to cytotoxic CD8+ T cells provides the switch signal directing B cells to produce IgG subclasses"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Immunoglobulin class switch recombination (CSR) requires two coordinated signals: (1) the CD40/CD40L interaction between activated CD4+ T helper cells (expressing CD40L) and B cells (expressing CD40), which induces AID (activation-induced cytidine deaminase) expression in B cells; and (2) cytokine signals that determine the specific isotype — IL-4 directs IgE and IgG1 switching; IFN-γ directs IgG2a/2c; TGF-β directs IgA. AID is the enzyme that catalyzes CSR by deaminating cytosines in switch regions. BCR crosslinking alone or cytokines alone are insufficient — both signals are required. CD40L deficiency (X-linked Hyper-IgM syndrome) confirms this: patients have normal B cell counts and elevated IgM but absent IgG, IgA, and IgE due to failed CSR. CTLA-4 on T cells is inhibitory, not a B cell activation signal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CD40L deficiency → X-linked Hyper-IgM syndrome; elevated IgM + absent IgG/IgA/IgE; susceptible to Pneumocystis and Cryptosporidium. AID deficiency → autosomal recessive Hyper-IgM type 2. COMLEX: CD40L-CD40 also required for macrophage activation via T cells — explains unusual intracellular pathogen susceptibility in Hyper-IgM despite normal T cell numbers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — T/B cell activation requires two signals; CD28/B7 co-stimulation and CD40L/CD40 interaction are required for class switching and memory"},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A self-reactive T cell escapes thymic deletion and enters the periphery. This T cell encounters its cognate self-antigen presented on a resting hepatocyte that expresses MHC class II but lacks B7 co-stimulatory molecules. Which of the following best describes the primary outcome for this T cell?","choices":{"A":"The T cell undergoes activation-induced cell death (AICD) via Fas-FasL signaling immediately upon encountering self-antigen without co-stimulation","B":"The T cell is suppressed by a bystander regulatory T cell that secretes IL-10 and TGF-β specifically at the site of self-antigen presentation","C":"The T cell is eliminated by NK cells that recognize its activated surface phenotype as aberrant and deliver cytotoxic granules","D":"The T cell becomes anergic: receiving TCR signal (Signal 1) without co-stimulation (Signal 2) induces a long-lived state of functional unresponsiveness, preventing IL-2 production and proliferation upon subsequent antigen encounter","E":"The T cell upregulates CTLA-4 after Signal 1, and CTLA-4 delivers a terminal apoptotic signal that permanently removes the autoreactive clone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Peripheral anergy is the primary intrinsic tolerance mechanism that prevents self-reactive T cells from causing autoimmunity when they encounter self-antigen on cells that lack B7 co-stimulatory molecules. Signal 1 (TCR/MHC) alone activates intracellular inhibitory pathways (including GRAIL E3 ubiquitin ligase and Cbl-b), degrading signaling intermediates and epigenetically silencing IL-2 transcription. The anergic T cell persists but is non-functional. AICD (Fas/FasL-mediated apoptosis) is a separate peripheral tolerance mechanism triggered by repeated strong antigen stimulation of already-activated T cells — not the initial anergy-inducing encounter. Treg suppression and NK elimination are independent mechanisms. CTLA-4 inhibits activation but does not directly induce apoptosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peripheral anergy = Signal 1 without Signal 2 on non-professional APCs (lack B7); distinct from AICD (Fas-FasL, repeated stimulation of activated cells). Both protect against autoimmunity. COMLEX: Peripheral tolerance mechanisms are redundant layers — central tolerance is not perfect, so peripheral mechanisms provide secondary protection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — Peripheral tolerance: anergy, CTLA-4, PD-1/PD-L1, Tregs (FOXP3), and AICD prevent autoreactive lymphocytes from causing tissue damage"},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"CTLA-4 and CD28 are both expressed on T cells and bind the same ligands (B7-1/CD80 and B7-2/CD86). Which of the following best describes the key functional distinction between these two receptors?","choices":{"A":"CTLA-4 is constitutively expressed on all naive T cells and promotes activation, while CD28 is upregulated after activation to terminate the response","B":"Both CD28 and CTLA-4 promote T cell activation, but CTLA-4 has lower affinity for B7, so it activates T cells only when antigen load is very high","C":"CD28 is expressed exclusively on CD8+ cytotoxic T cells, while CTLA-4 is expressed exclusively on CD4+ T helper cells and regulatory T cells","D":"Both CD28 and CTLA-4 deliver inhibitory signals; the difference is that CTLA-4 acts on naive T cells while CD28 inhibits previously activated T cells","E":"CD28 is constitutively expressed on naive T cells and promotes activation by lowering the threshold for TCR signaling; CTLA-4 is upregulated only after T cell activation, has higher affinity for B7, and delivers an inhibitory signal that terminates the T cell response"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CD28 is constitutively expressed on naive and memory T cells and serves as the primary co-stimulatory receptor; CD28/B7 engagement promotes PI3K activation, IL-2 production, cell cycle entry, and survival. CTLA-4 is upregulated on T cells hours to days after activation and is constitutively expressed on regulatory T cells. CTLA-4 has 10- to 100-fold higher affinity for B7 than CD28, enabling it to outcompete CD28 for B7 binding and deliver inhibitory signals (via phosphatases PP2A and SHP-2) that limit T cell activation amplitude. This provides critical negative feedback. Therapeutically, ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4) blocks CTLA-4 inhibitory signaling to enhance anti-tumor T cell activity, while abatacept (CTLA-4-Ig) exploits high B7 affinity to block CD28 co-stimulation in autoimmune disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CTLA-4 > CD28 affinity for B7; CTLA-4-Ig (abatacept) = RA treatment; ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4) = melanoma immunotherapy causing irAEs. COMLEX: CTLA-4 deficiency causes lymphoproliferation and multi-organ autoimmunity; CTLA-4 is most highly expressed on regulatory T cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — Peripheral tolerance: anergy, CTLA-4, PD-1/PD-L1, Tregs (FOXP3), and AICD prevent autoreactive lymphocytes from causing tissue damage"},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A male infant presents at 6 weeks of age with intractable watery diarrhea, a scaly erythematous rash, and blood glucose of 42 mg/dL consistent with neonatal diabetes mellitus. Laboratory testing reveals lymphocytic infiltration of the intestinal mucosa and pancreatic islets. Genetic testing identifies a hemizygous pathogenic variant in FOXP3. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of disease?","choices":{"A":"Loss of FOXP3 activates constitutive JAK-STAT3 signaling in T cells, driving uncontrolled Th17 differentiation that mediates inflammatory destruction of epithelial surfaces","B":"Loss of FOXP3 prevents CTLA-4 expression on all T cell subsets, allowing excessive CD28 co-stimulation to drive autoimmune tissue destruction","C":"Loss of FOXP3 eliminates regulatory T cell (Treg) development and function, removing the peripheral suppressive checkpoint that prevents autoreactive effector T cells from attacking self-tissues including intestinal epithelium and pancreatic beta cells","D":"Loss of FOXP3 impairs thymic positive selection of CD4+ T cells, resulting in severe lymphopenia paradoxically accompanied by multi-organ autoimmunity","E":"Loss of FOXP3 constitutively activates Fas-FasL signaling, causing both T cell accumulation through defective AICD and concurrent autoimmune tissue damage"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FOXP3 is the master transcription factor required for the development and function of CD4+CD25+FOXP3+ regulatory T cells (Tregs). Without FOXP3, Tregs fail to develop, eliminating the peripheral suppressive mechanisms they provide: secretion of IL-10 and TGF-β, competition for CD28 co-stimulation via CTLA-4, IL-2 consumption, and direct cytotoxic mechanisms. Autoreactive T cells that escaped thymic deletion then attack the intestinal mucosa (causing enteropathy/diarrhea), pancreatic beta cells (type 1 diabetes), and skin (eczematous dermatitis). This is IPEX syndrome (Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked), an X-linked condition because FOXP3 is on the X chromosome — affecting males. While CTLA-4 is expressed on Tregs, the primary defect in IPEX is Treg absence, not isolated CTLA-4 loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FOXP3 mutation → IPEX syndrome (X-linked recessive); triad of enteropathy + endocrinopathy (T1DM) + eczema; presents in infancy in males. COMLEX: Treatment is HSCT (curative) or rapamycin (bridge); IPEX is the prototypic Treg deficiency disease — fundamental to understanding peripheral tolerance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — Peripheral tolerance: anergy, CTLA-4, PD-1/PD-L1, Tregs (FOXP3), and AICD prevent autoreactive lymphocytes from causing tissue damage"},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the process of lymphocyte development.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-day-old male neonate born at term is found to have hypocalcemia-induced tetany, a conotruncal cardiac defect (truncus arteriosus), and subtle dysmorphic facial features including low-set ears and a small jaw. Immunologic workup at 6 weeks shows markedly reduced CD3+ T cells with normal B cell counts and normal serum IgM. Which of the following best explains the immunologic findings?","choices":{"A":"Deletion of chromosome 22q11.2 leads to failure of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches to develop, resulting in thymic hypoplasia or aplasia and absent T lymphocyte development while B cell development in the bone marrow remains intact","B":"A BTK mutation blocks B cell development at the pro-B to pre-B transition, resulting in absent B cells with preserved T cell counts and elevated susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria","C":"A RAG2 mutation prevents VDJ recombination in all lymphoid progenitors, resulting in absent B and T cells with preserved NK cell function","D":"A common gamma chain (γc) mutation prevents IL-7 and IL-15 signaling, abolishing T and NK cell development while B cell progenitors remain intact but non-functional","E":"An ADA mutation causes toxic metabolite accumulation selectively toxic to T cell progenitors, sparing B cell development in the bone marrow"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The constellation of conotruncal cardiac defect, hypocalcemia (due to absent parathyroid glands), absent/low T cells with intact B cells, and characteristic dysmorphic facial features is diagnostic of DiGeorge syndrome caused by 22q11.2 deletion. The third and fourth pharyngeal pouches give rise to the thymus and inferior parathyroid glands; their failure to form explains both the T cell deficiency and the hypocalcemia. B cell development in the bone marrow is independent of the thymus and is therefore unaffected. BTK mutations cause X-linked agammaglobulinemia (absent B cells, normal T cells). RAG2 deficiency causes T-B-NK+ SCID. γc mutations cause T-B+NK- SCID without cardiac defects. ADA deficiency causes T-B-NK- SCID.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DiGeorge = 22q11.2 deletion; CATCH-22 mnemonic (Cardiac defects, Abnormal facies, Thymic aplasia, Cleft palate, Hypocalcemia, 22q11). Absent thymus → absent T cells; normal B cells. COMLEX: Treatment depends on severity; complete DiGeorge requires thymic transplant; partial DiGeorge may spontaneously improve.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Lymphocyte development"},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the process of lymphocyte development.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-month-old male infant presents with his third episode of bacterial pneumonia (now Streptococcus pneumoniae) in 5 months. He was well until 6 months of age. Physical examination reveals absent tonsils and very small lymph nodes. Flow cytometry shows markedly reduced circulating B cells (< 1%) with normal T cell counts. Serum IgG is 42 mg/dL, IgM is undetectable, and IgA is undetectable. Which of the following best explains the underlying defect?","choices":{"A":"A mutation in AID prevents immunoglobulin class switch recombination, resulting in normal B cell counts with elevated IgM but absent IgG and IgA","B":"A mutation in the common gamma chain (γc) prevents IL-7 signaling, abolishing both T and B cell development and presenting as T-B+NK- SCID","C":"A mutation in CD40L prevents T helper cell co-stimulation of B cells, resulting in elevated IgM with absent IgG due to failure of class switching","D":"A mutation in Bruton's tyrosine kinase (BTK) blocks pre-BCR signaling at the pro-B to pre-B cell transition, arresting B cell development in the bone marrow and resulting in absent mature B cells and absent immunoglobulins of all isotypes","E":"A mutation in the IL-7 receptor α-chain blocks T cell development, secondarily impairing T cell-dependent B cell activation and class switching to IgG"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The presentation — recurrent bacterial infections beginning after 6 months (when maternal IgG wanes), absent tonsils (which contain B cell follicles), absent circulating B cells, and undetectable all immunoglobulin isotypes in a male infant — is classic for X-linked agammaglobulinemia (XLA/Bruton's disease) caused by BTK mutations. BTK is required for pre-BCR signaling; without it, development arrests at the pro-B cell stage. Absent B cells and absent all Ig (IgM, IgG, IgA) distinguish XLA from Hyper-IgM syndrome (AID or CD40L mutations), which has present B cells and elevated IgM. The onset at 6 months reflects protection by transplacentally acquired maternal IgG. T cells are normal in XLA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: X-linked agammaglobulinemia (XLA): BTK mutation; males; absent B cells + absent all Ig; recurrent bacterial infections post-6 months; absent tonsils on exam; treatment = IVIG. COMLEX: Susceptible to Giardia, enteroviruses (esp. echovirus encephalitis), and encapsulated bacteria; distinguish from Hyper-IgM (normal B cells, elevated IgM only).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Lymphocyte development"},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the process of lymphocyte development.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-month-old female infant presents with failure to thrive, persistent oral thrush unresponsive to antifungal therapy, and Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia. She has no palpable lymph nodes and her chest X-ray shows absence of the thymic shadow. Flow cytometry reveals profoundly reduced T cells and NK cells with normal or elevated B cell counts; serum immunoglobulins are low but IgM is detectable. Which of the following mutations best explains this phenotype?","choices":{"A":"Mutation in RAG1 prevents VDJ recombination in all lymphocyte progenitors, resulting in absent T, B, and NK cells (T-B-NK+ SCID)","B":"Mutation in the IL-2 receptor common gamma chain (γc/CD132) abrogates signaling through receptors for IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, IL-15, and IL-21, selectively eliminating T and NK cell development while leaving B cell progenitors in the bone marrow intact","C":"Mutation in AIRE prevents ectopic self-antigen expression in the thymic medulla, causing autoreactive T cells to escape and destroy the thymus, resulting in lymphopenia","D":"Mutation in Artemis prevents V(D)J hairpin resolution, causing radio-sensitive SCID with absent T and B cells but preserved NK cells and sensitivity to ionizing radiation","E":"Mutation in the CD3ζ chain prevents TCR surface expression, selectively abolishing T cell development while NK cells and B cells develop normally"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The T-B+NK- SCID phenotype (absent T and NK cells, present but non-functional B cells) is characteristic of X-linked SCID caused by mutations in the IL-2 receptor common gamma chain (γc, CD132). The γc chain is shared by receptors for IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, IL-15, and IL-21. IL-7 signaling is essential for T cell development; IL-15 is essential for NK cell development. Without γc, both T and NK cells fail to develop. B cell progenitors do not depend on γc-utilizing cytokines for initial development, so B cells are present but non-functional due to absent T cell help. RAG1/2 deficiency causes T-B-NK+ SCID. Artemis deficiency causes radiosensitive T-B-NK+ SCID. AIRE mutations cause APS-1, not SCID.","boardPrep":"Step 1: X-linked SCID (γc mutation) = T-B+NK- SCID; absent thymic shadow on CXR; P. jirovecii, Candida, CMV infections; treatment = HSCT or gene therapy. COMLEX: Distinguish SCID phenotypes — T-B+NK- (γc), T-B-NK+ (RAG), T-B-NK- (ADA); the NK cell status distinguishes γc from RAG deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Lymphocyte development"},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the formation and diversity of B cell receptors (BCRs) and T cell receptors (TCRs).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher studying B cell development examines the order of immunoglobulin gene rearrangements. She identifies a B cell that has just undergone its first successful gene recombination event, producing an in-frame transcript but no completed antigen receptor on the surface. Which rearrangement event has most likely just occurred, and what is the next step?","choices":{"A":"VJ joining at the κ light chain locus has occurred first; the next step is VDJ joining at the heavy chain locus to complete receptor assembly","B":"V-DJ joining at the heavy chain locus has already produced a complete VDJ rearrangement; the next step is D-J joining to finalize light chain assembly","C":"VJ joining at the λ light chain locus has occurred first; the next step is κ light chain rearrangement, followed by heavy chain VDJ recombination","D":"Both heavy and light chain loci have rearranged simultaneously; the cell is simply awaiting post-translational assembly of the complete IgM molecule","E":"D-J joining at the heavy chain locus has occurred first; the next step is V-to-DJ joining to complete heavy chain VDJ recombination, after which a productive μ heavy chain enables pre-BCR formation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Immunoglobulin gene rearrangement follows a strict ordered sequence governed by regulated chromatin accessibility. At the pro-B cell stage, D-J rearrangement occurs first at both heavy chain alleles, followed by V-to-DJ joining to complete VDJ recombination. If the first allele produces a productive (in-frame) rearrangement, allelic exclusion blocks rearrangement of the second allele. The μ heavy chain protein pairs with surrogate light chains to form the pre-BCR, signaling survival and proliferation. Only after successful heavy chain rearrangement and pre-BCR formation does light chain rearrangement begin — first attempting κ, then λ if κ fails. This ordered sequence (D-J → V-DJ → light chain VJ) ensures that each B cell has a unique, functional, monospecific receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Order of Ig gene rearrangement: heavy chain D-J → V-DJ first, then light chain VJ (κ before λ); allelic exclusion enforced by pre-BCR signaling after productive heavy chain. COMLEX: Understanding this order explains why each B cell expresses BCRs of only one specificity — allelic exclusion at both heavy and light chain loci.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — BCR/TCR formation and diversity"},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the formation and diversity of B cell receptors (BCRs) and T cell receptors (TCRs).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pathologist examines bone marrow from a 5-year-old child with peripheral lymphocytosis and circulating blast cells. The blasts are TdT-positive, CD19-positive, CD10-positive, and myeloperoxidase-negative. Which of the following best explains why TdT expression confirms lymphoid origin and identifies the developmental arrest point?","choices":{"A":"TdT is expressed exclusively in immature lymphoid precursors (pro-B/pre-B and pro-T/pre-T cells) during active VDJ recombination; its expression indicates that these malignant cells are arrested at an early B-lymphoid developmental stage before completing receptor assembly","B":"TdT is expressed in germinal center B cells during somatic hypermutation, identifying this as a mature B cell malignancy that has undergone clonal expansion following antigen activation","C":"TdT expression marks cells producing large amounts of immunoglobulin heavy chain, indicating a plasma cell malignancy with active VDJ-region transcription","D":"TdT is expressed in myeloid progenitors undergoing T cell lineage conversion; its presence distinguishes a mixed-lineage leukemia from purely myeloid or lymphoid disease","E":"TdT expression in NK cell precursors identifies malignant cells that have failed to commit to the NK cell lineage and are instead arrested in a lymphoid progenitor state"},"correct":"A","explanation":"TdT (terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase) is a template-independent DNA polymerase expressed during V(D)J recombination specifically in lymphoid precursors — pro-B and pre-B cells (in B-ALL) and pro-T and pre-T cells (in T-ALL). TdT expression is extinguished once VDJ recombination is completed in mature lymphocytes. Therefore, TdT+ blast cells are definitively arrested at an immature lymphoid stage. In this case, TdT+ + CD19+ + CD10+ (CALLA) + MPO- confirms B-cell ALL (the most common childhood malignancy). TdT is not expressed in germinal center B cells (which use AID for somatic hypermutation), plasma cells, myeloid cells, or NK cells. TdT negativity distinguishes AML (MPO+) from ALL.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TdT+/MPO- = ALL; TdT-/MPO+ = AML; B-ALL peaks at ages 2-5; CD10 (CALLA) + TdT = classic pre-B ALL phenotype; most common pediatric cancer. COMLEX: T-ALL commonly presents with anterior mediastinal mass (thymic origin); B-ALL more often presents with bone pain, lymphadenopathy, and splenomegaly.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — BCR/TCR formation and diversity"},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the formation and diversity of B cell receptors (BCRs) and T cell receptors (TCRs).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman receives her second influenza vaccination. Compared to her primary response 1 year earlier, her secondary immune response generates serum antibodies with significantly higher binding affinity for viral hemagglutinin antigens. Which of the following cellular and molecular processes best explains this increase in antibody affinity?","choices":{"A":"VDJ recombination occurs a second time in antigen-stimulated memory B cells, generating new BCR sequences with intrinsically higher affinity for influenza antigens","B":"Class switch recombination from IgM to IgG during the primary response permanently increases antibody binding affinity by altering the Fc region and the antigen-binding cleft","C":"IL-4 secreted by Th2 cells during the secondary response directly upregulates immunoglobulin transcription, increasing antibody quantity but not individual antibody affinity","D":"Somatic hypermutation in germinal center B cells introduces point mutations into immunoglobulin variable region genes; B cells whose mutations increase antigen-binding affinity are selectively rescued from apoptosis by follicular dendritic cells presenting limiting antigen, generating clones with progressively higher affinity","E":"Memory B cells express significantly more surface BCR molecules per cell, increasing avidity through multivalent interactions that mimic higher individual antibody affinity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Affinity maturation occurs in germinal centers (GCs) of secondary lymphoid organs during the T-dependent antibody response. GC B cells undergo rapid somatic hypermutation (SHM) of their immunoglobulin variable region genes, catalyzed by activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID). Mutations are random, generating clones with higher or lower antigen affinity. Follicular dendritic cells (FDCs) retain antigen as immune complexes in limiting amounts; only B cells with higher-affinity BCRs can successfully compete for this antigen and receive Tfh (follicular T helper) cell survival signals. Lower-affinity clones undergo apoptosis. This iterative positive selection process — somatic hypermutation followed by affinity-based selection — results in progressively higher-affinity antibodies in secondary and subsequent responses. V(D)J recombination does not recur in mature B cells. Class switching alters Fc effector function, not binding affinity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Somatic hypermutation + affinity-based selection in germinal centers = affinity maturation; requires AID; explains why secondary immune responses have higher-affinity antibodies. COMLEX: Germinal center reactions also generate long-lived plasma cells (migrate to bone marrow) and memory B cells — the basis of durable immunologic memory.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — BCR/TCR formation and diversity"},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl presents with recurrent oral and vaginal candidiasis since infancy, primary hypoparathyroidism, and newly diagnosed Addison's disease. Her younger brother has similar history, and a maternal cousin died of adrenal crisis. Serum anti-21-hydroxylase and anti-NALP5 autoantibodies are positive. Genetic testing reveals a homozygous loss-of-function mutation. Which of the following best explains the disease mechanism?","choices":{"A":"An autosomal dominant gain-of-function mutation in FOXP3 causes Treg hyperactivity, impairing anti-fungal Th17 immunity and creating a paradoxically immunosuppressed state that enables candidiasis","B":"An autosomal dominant mutation in STAT3 causes Hyper-IgE syndrome (Job syndrome), impairing Th17 development and anti-fungal immunity while allowing eosinophil-mediated adrenal destruction","C":"Autosomal recessive loss-of-function mutation in AIRE prevents medullary thymic epithelial cells from expressing peripheral self-antigens (adrenal, parathyroid, and gonadal antigens), allowing autoreactive T cells specific for these antigens to escape thymic negative selection and cause multi-organ autoimmunity","D":"An X-linked recessive mutation in WASp (Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome) impairs cytoskeletal function in T cells, reducing anti-fungal immunity and secondarily causing adrenal autoimmunity via Th1 dysregulation","E":"An autosomal recessive mutation in RAG2 causes a hypomorphic SCID phenotype with a restricted T cell repertoire, enabling candidiasis while paradoxically generating oligoclonal autoreactive T cell clones"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The classic triad of mucocutaneous candidiasis (appearing first, often in infancy), hypoparathyroidism, and Addison's disease with autosomal recessive inheritance, positive family history, and organ-specific autoantibodies is diagnostic of Autoimmune Polyendocrinopathy-Candidiasis-Ectodermal Dystrophy (APECED/APS-1) caused by AIRE mutations. AIRE drives ectopic expression of peripheral self-antigens (including 21-hydroxylase, CYP11A1, NALP5) in medullary thymic epithelial cells for central T cell tolerance. Loss of AIRE allows T cells reactive against these antigens to escape and cause autoimmune destruction of adrenal glands, parathyroids, gonads, and other organs. Susceptibility to Candida occurs because Th17 cells needed for anti-fungal mucosal immunity are simultaneously destroyed by autoreactive immune responses. FOXP3 loss-of-function causes IPEX (not gain-of-function). Hyper-IgE (STAT3) has different features.","boardPrep":"Step 1: APS-1/APECED = AIRE mutation; autosomal recessive; triad (CMC + hypoparathyroidism + Addison's); anti-21-hydroxylase antibodies. Contrast APS-2 (Schmidt syndrome): HLA-associated, includes Hashimoto's + T1DM + Addison's, no candidiasis. COMLEX: AIRE is essential for establishing central tolerance to peripheral organ antigens; its loss is the definitive cause of APS-1.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Central immune tolerance"},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Researchers create a transgenic mouse in which all T cells express a TCR specific for a pancreatic islet antigen (insulin B chain peptide presented on I-A^b MHC class II). In a wild-type mouse with normal AIRE function, which of the following outcomes is most likely for the majority of these islet-specific T cells during thymic development?","choices":{"A":"The majority of islet-specific T cells exit the thymus as naive T cells because AIRE-driven ectopic insulin expression only affects B cell tolerance in the periphery, not thymic negative selection","B":"The majority of islet-specific T cells undergo clonal deletion (apoptosis) in the thymic medulla after encountering insulin peptide ectopically expressed by AIRE-driven medullary thymic epithelial cells, because their high-affinity TCR-self-antigen interaction triggers negative selection","C":"The majority of islet-specific T cells differentiate into natural regulatory T cells (nTregs) and migrate to the pancreas to suppress future autoimmune responses against beta cells","D":"The majority of islet-specific T cells fail positive selection and undergo death by neglect because insulin peptide-MHC II complexes are only expressed on dendritic cells, not thymic cortical epithelium","E":"The majority of islet-specific T cells undergo receptor editing, replacing their autoreactive TCR with a new receptor that recognizes foreign antigens rather than self-antigens"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In TCR transgenic mice where all T cells express an islet-specific TCR, normal AIRE function ensures that insulin antigens are ectopically expressed by medullary thymic epithelial cells (mTECs). Thymocytes expressing this TCR encounter insulin peptide-MHC II complexes in the medulla and, because the affinity is high (insulin-specific TCR engineered for strong recognition), the vast majority undergo negative selection (clonal deletion via Bim-mediated apoptosis). This experiment validates the concept that AIRE-driven ectopic antigen expression is the primary mechanism preventing anti-islet T cells from reaching the periphery. A small proportion may alternatively become natural Tregs (when affinity is intermediate), but clonal deletion predominates for high-affinity interactions. Receptor editing does not occur in T cells. Death by neglect occurs when TCR cannot engage any MHC at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AIRE drives ectopic expression of peripheral antigens in mTECs → negative selection (clonal deletion) of high-affinity autoreactive T cells in thymic medulla. COMLEX: Some autoreactive T cells with intermediate affinity become nTregs rather than being deleted — explaining how nTregs arise with self-antigen specificity to patrol peripheral tissues.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Central immune tolerance"},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman presents with progressive bilateral ptosis, diplopia, and fatigable proximal muscle weakness that worsens with repetitive activity and improves with rest. Chest CT reveals an anterior mediastinal mass. Serum anti-acetylcholine receptor (AChR) IgG antibodies are markedly elevated. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology linking the mediastinal mass to the autoimmune neuromuscular disease?","choices":{"A":"The thymoma secretes thymosin hormones that directly stimulate B cells to produce anti-AChR antibodies via a T-independent activation mechanism","B":"The thymoma compresses the phrenic nerve, causing diaphragmatic weakness that triggers a compensatory autoimmune response against neuromuscular junction proteins","C":"The thymoma undergoes inflammatory necrosis, releasing intracellular AChR protein that acts as a foreign antigen to stimulate a primary immune response against self","D":"The thymoma contains neoplastic thymic epithelial cells that fail to properly perform negative selection against T cells recognizing AChR epitopes, allowing these autoreactive T cells to escape and provide cognate help for AChR-specific B cells to produce pathogenic IgG autoantibodies","E":"The thymoma induces aberrant MHC class II upregulation on muscle cells, allowing direct CD4+ T cell cytotoxicity against neuromuscular junction components"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Myasthenia gravis (MG) is a type II hypersensitivity disease in which IgG autoantibodies against nicotinic AChR at the neuromuscular junction cause receptor internalization and complement-mediated destruction, resulting in fatigable weakness (ptosis, diplopia, limb and respiratory muscle weakness worse with activity). Approximately 15% of MG patients have a thymoma. The thymic tumor contains aberrant epithelial cells that express AChR subunit peptides but fail to properly present them to developing thymocytes for negative selection. Autoreactive CD4+ T cells specific for AChR escape the thymoma and enter the periphery, where they provide CD40L/CD40 and cytokine signals to AChR-specific B cells, driving pathogenic IgG autoantibody production. Thymectomy is a cornerstone of MG treatment because it removes the source of these autoreactive T cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thymoma most commonly associated with myasthenia gravis (anti-AChR IgG); also associated with pure red cell aplasia and hypogammaglobulinemia. MG = Type II hypersensitivity; ptosis + diplopia + fatigable weakness + positive Tensilon test. COMLEX: Thymectomy improves MG even without visible thymoma; edrophonium (Tensilon) test: anticholinesterase transiently reverses weakness.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Central immune tolerance"},{"id":"sdl28_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with seropositive rheumatoid arthritis has an inadequate response to methotrexate after 6 months of therapy. Her rheumatologist adds abatacept (CTLA-4-Ig) to her regimen. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which abatacept reduces synovial inflammation?","choices":{"A":"Abatacept is a monoclonal antibody that neutralizes soluble TNF-α, preventing it from binding TNFR1 and TNFR2 on synoviocytes and reducing NF-κB-driven inflammatory gene transcription","B":"Abatacept is a fusion protein targeting the IL-6 receptor that blocks STAT3 phosphorylation, reducing Th17 differentiation and the production of IL-17-driven synovial matrix metalloproteinase activity","C":"Abatacept is a CTLA-4/IgG1-Fc fusion protein that binds B7 (CD80/CD86) on antigen-presenting cells with high affinity, preventing CD28/B7 co-stimulatory engagement and thereby inducing T cell anergy instead of full activation","D":"Abatacept is a monoclonal antibody targeting CD20 on B cells that depletes mature B cells via ADCC and CDC, reducing synovial plasma cell density and autoantibody titers","E":"Abatacept is a small molecule inhibitor of JAK1/JAK3 that blocks cytokine receptor signaling downstream of IL-2, IL-6, and IL-15, broadly suppressing T and B cell proliferation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Abatacept (CTLA-4-Ig) is a selective T cell co-stimulation blocker consisting of the extracellular domain of CTLA-4 fused to the Fc region of IgG1. CTLA-4 has approximately 10-100× higher affinity for B7 (CD80/CD86) than CD28; therefore, abatacept competes with CD28 for B7 binding on APCs, blocking the co-stimulatory Signal 2 required for full T cell activation. Without Signal 2, T cells receiving Signal 1 alone become anergic, suppressing the T cell-driven synovial inflammation in RA. This mechanism directly targets the upstream T cell activation step rather than downstream cytokines. TNF-α blockade is the mechanism of adalimumab, etanercept, and certolizumab. IL-6R blockade is tocilizumab and sarilumab. CD20 B cell depletion is rituximab. JAK inhibition is tofacitinib and baricitinib.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Abatacept = CTLA-4-Ig = blocks CD28/B7 co-stimulation (Signal 2); used in RA when methotrexate inadequate. Each biologic class targets a different step. COMLEX: Mechanism of abatacept is unique among biologics — it targets the T cell activation step upstream of all cytokines; can be combined with methotrexate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — T and B cell activation mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl28_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old male presents with recurrent bacterial pneumonias, Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, and Cryptosporidium parvum cholangitis. Laboratory testing reveals markedly elevated serum IgM (890 mg/dL), absent IgG, absent IgA, and absent IgE. Flow cytometry shows normal T cell counts with normal mitogen proliferative responses. B cell counts are normal. Which of the following best identifies the molecular defect?","choices":{"A":"Deficiency of CD40 ligand (CD40L/CD154) on activated CD4+ T helper cells prevents CD40/CD40L signaling to B cells, blocking AID-dependent class switch recombination and also impairing macrophage activation, explaining both the isotype deficiency and the intracellular pathogen susceptibility","B":"Deficiency of BTK prevents pre-BCR signaling, arresting B cell development at the pro-B stage and resulting in absent B cells and absent immunoglobulins across all isotypes including IgM","C":"Deficiency of AID in B cells prevents class switch recombination, resulting in normal B cells and elevated IgM with absent IgG/IgA/IgE via autosomal recessive inheritance without intracellular pathogen susceptibility","D":"Deficiency of IL-7 receptor prevents T cell development, secondarily eliminating all T cell help for B cell activation and producing a clinical picture similar to DiGeorge syndrome","E":"Deficiency of RAG1 prevents VDJ recombination, resulting in absent B and T cells with absent all immunoglobulin isotypes including IgM"},"correct":"A","explanation":"X-linked Hyper-IgM syndrome (HIGM1) is caused by CD40LG mutations (encoding CD40L/CD154 on CD4+ T helper cells). CD40L/CD40 signaling has two critical functions: (1) it delivers the licensing signal required for B cell AID expression and class switch recombination from IgM to IgG, IgA, and IgE; and (2) it activates macrophages via T cell-macrophage interaction — this second function explains why patients are susceptible to intracellular pathogens (Pneumocystis, Cryptosporidium, Toxoplasma) despite having normal T cell counts and function. BTK deficiency causes absent all immunoglobulins including IgM (XLA). AID deficiency causes HIGM2 (elevated IgM, absent class-switched Ig) without Pneumocystis susceptibility because macrophage activation remains intact. RAG deficiency causes SCID with absent all lymphocytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: X-linked Hyper-IgM = CD40L deficiency; elevated IgM + absent IgG/IgA/IgE + normal B cells + Pneumocystis/Cryptosporidium susceptibility; CD40L also needed for macrophage activation. COMLEX: Treatment = IVIG + TMP-SMX prophylaxis; males affected (X-linked); distinguish from AID deficiency (HIGM2, autosomal recessive, no intracellular pathogen susceptibility).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — T and B cell activation mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl28_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-month-old infant of consanguineous North African parents presents with recurrent bacterial and viral infections, persistent diarrhea, and failure to thrive. Flow cytometry reveals profoundly reduced CD4+ T cells with normal CD8+ T cell counts. HLA-DR staining on peripheral blood monocytes is absent. Serum IgM is present but IgG and IgA are markedly reduced. Which of the following best explains this immunodeficiency?","choices":{"A":"Deficiency of TAP1 or TAP2 prevents peptide loading onto MHC class I molecules, leading to absent CD8+ T cells with normal CD4+ T cell counts and normal surface HLA-DR expression","B":"Deficiency of the common gamma chain (γc) prevents IL-7 and IL-15 signaling, eliminating both T and NK cells while leaving B cell progenitors and MHC class II expression intact","C":"Deficiency of CD3ζ prevents TCR surface assembly and signaling, selectively abolishing all T cell subsets equally while preserving normal MHC class II expression on APCs","D":"Deficiency of an MHC class II transcription factor (CIITA, RFX5, RFXANK, or RFXAP) abolishes surface MHC class II expression, preventing thymic positive selection of CD4+ T cells and impairing peripheral antigen presentation to T helper cells, secondarily reducing class-switched antibody production","E":"Deficiency of β2-microglobulin prevents MHC class I surface expression, eliminating CD8+ T cells through failed positive selection with preserved CD4+ T cell counts and MHC class II"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bare Lymphocyte Syndrome type II (BLS-II) is caused by mutations in transcription factors required for constitutive and inducible MHC class II gene expression: CIITA (most common), RFX5, RFXANK, or RFXAP. Absent MHC class II has two consequences: (1) failure of thymic positive selection of CD4+ T cells (which require MHC class II recognition for positive selection) → dramatically reduced CD4+ T cells; and (2) impaired peripheral antigen presentation to CD4+ T helper cells → reduced T cell help for B cells → absent class-switched IgG and IgA. CD8+ T cells are relatively preserved because they depend on MHC class I (unaffected). The absent HLA-DR on monocytes (demonstrable by flow cytometry) is pathognomonic. This is common in North African families due to founder effects. TAP deficiency (BLS-I) affects MHC class I → absent CD8+ T cells with normal CD4+ T cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: BLS-II (MHC class II deficiency) → absent HLA-DR on monocytes → ↓ CD4+ T cells, normal CD8+ T cells; ↓ IgG/IgA; presents as SCID-like; North African ancestry. COMLEX: Contrast BLS-I (TAP deficiency → absent MHC class I → absent CD8+ cells) with BLS-II (absent MHC class II → absent CD4+ cells).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — T and B cell activation mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl28_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with metastatic non-small cell lung cancer achieves partial remission with pembrolizumab (anti-PD-1). After cycle 5, he develops fatigue, polyuria, and polydipsia. His glucose is 498 mg/dL, pH 7.19, and C-peptide is undetectable. Anti-GAD65 antibodies are positive. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of his new endocrine disorder?","choices":{"A":"Pembrolizumab blocks PD-1 on T cells, removing the inhibitory checkpoint that peripheral tissues use to silence autoreactive T cells at the organ site; unleashed pancreatic islet-specific T cells that were previously held in check by PD-1/PD-L1 signaling now destroy beta cells, causing autoimmune T1DM","B":"Pembrolizumab activates NK cells via ADCC, causing non-specific cytotoxic killing of insulin-secreting cells as bystander destruction during the anti-tumor response","C":"Pembrolizumab cross-reacts with the insulin receptor on beta cells due to structural homology between PD-1 and the insulin receptor extracellular domain, blocking insulin signaling","D":"PD-1 blockade prevents B cell tolerance checkpoints in the bone marrow, directly enabling anti-insulin IgG autoantibody production that destroys beta cells through complement","E":"Pembrolizumab triggers macrophage polarization to M1 phenotype in the pancreas, causing IL-1β and TNF-α-mediated non-immune-mediated beta cell cytotoxicity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PD-1 (programmed death-1) is an inhibitory checkpoint receptor expressed on activated T cells. When PD-1 binds its ligands PD-L1 (CD274) and PD-L2 on peripheral tissues and APCs, it delivers an inhibitory signal (via SHP-2 phosphatase) that silences T cell effector function. Pancreatic beta cells, thyroid follicular cells, and many other peripheral tissues express PD-L1 as a mechanism of local peripheral tolerance. Pembrolizumab blocks PD-1, releasing this inhibitory brake globally. While this enhances anti-tumor T cell activity, it simultaneously unleashes autoreactive T cells that were previously suppressed by PD-1/PD-L1 signaling at self-tissue sites. The resulting immune-related adverse events (irAEs) include autoimmune diabetes, thyroiditis, adrenalitis, colitis, hepatitis, and pneumonitis. The new-onset T1DM with positive anti-GAD65 and absent C-peptide confirms immune-mediated beta cell destruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PD-1/PD-L1 checkpoint blockade irAEs: endocrinopathies (thyroiditis, adrenal insufficiency, T1DM), colitis, hepatitis, pneumonitis; treat with steroids; endocrine irAEs often permanent. COMLEX: PD-L1 is expressed on peripheral tissues as a self-tolerance mechanism; tumors co-opt this to evade immunity — rationale for checkpoint blockade in cancer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — Peripheral immune tolerance"},{"id":"sdl28_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-week-old male infant presents with severe watery diarrhea, failure to thrive, and a scaly erythematous skin rash present since birth. He is found to have type 1 diabetes mellitus and autoimmune enteropathy with villous blunting on intestinal biopsy showing dense lymphocytic infiltration. His older maternal uncle died in early infancy of a similar illness. Genetic testing identifies a hemizygous pathogenic variant in the FOXP3 gene on chromosome Xp11.23. Which of the following best describes the primary mechanism of disease?","choices":{"A":"Loss of FOXP3 constitutively activates JAK1/STAT5 signaling in all T cell lineages, causing unchecked Th2 polarization that drives eosinophilic destruction of the intestinal mucosa and IgE-mediated beta cell apoptosis","B":"Loss of FOXP3 prevents the development and suppressive function of CD4+CD25+ regulatory T cells (Tregs), removing peripheral inhibitory checkpoints that prevent autoreactive effector T cells — which escaped thymic deletion — from mounting destructive immune responses against intestinal mucosa, pancreatic beta cells, and skin","C":"Loss of FOXP3 eliminates all CTLA-4 expression from T cell surfaces, removing the inhibitory signal that restrains CD28-driven T cell hyperactivation at mucosal surfaces and the pancreatic islet microenvironment","D":"Loss of FOXP3 upregulates Fas ligand expression on all T cells, triggering indiscriminate fratricidal T cell killing that paradoxically spares autoreactive clones while depleting regulatory populations","E":"Loss of FOXP3 impairs thymic positive selection of CD4+ T cells, generating a severely reduced T cell repertoire that creates gaps in anti-microbial immunity while concentrating autoreactive specificities"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IPEX syndrome (Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked) is caused by hemizygous loss-of-function mutations in FOXP3, the master transcription factor for regulatory T cells (Tregs). FOXP3+ Tregs suppress autoreactive effector T cells via multiple mechanisms: IL-10 and TGF-β secretion, competition for CD28 co-stimulation via CTLA-4, IL-2 consumption, and potentially direct cytotoxicity. Without functional Tregs, autoreactive T cells that escaped thymic negative selection are unopposed, leading to lymphocytic destruction of target organs. The X-linked inheritance pattern (males affected, transmitted through carrier mothers — the maternal uncle history) and early-onset severe multi-organ autoimmunity (enteropathy, T1DM, eczema) are pathognomonic. While CTLA-4 is expressed on Tregs, the primary defect is Treg absence, not isolated CTLA-4 loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IPEX = FOXP3 mutation (X-linked recessive); classic triad — enteropathy + endocrinopathy (T1DM) + eczema; presents in male infants; maternal uncle history classic X-linked inheritance. COMLEX: Curative treatment is HSCT; mTOR inhibitor (rapamycin) used as bridge; IPEX is the definitive disease of peripheral Treg deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — Peripheral immune tolerance"},{"id":"sdl28_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with triple-negative breast cancer has tumor biopsy showing strong PD-L1 expression (TPS 80%). Despite dense tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes (TILs) present within the tumor, these TILs demonstrate poor cytotoxic function with diminished IFN-γ production and reduced cytotoxic granule release. Which of the following best explains why the anti-tumor T cells fail to eradicate her tumor despite being physically present within it?","choices":{"A":"Tumor cells downregulate MHC class II expression, preventing CD4+ T helper cells from recognizing tumor antigens and providing cytokine support for CD8+ cytotoxic T cell killing at the tumor site","B":"Regulatory T cells recruited to the tumor microenvironment secrete IL-12 and IFN-γ, which paradoxically drive T cell exhaustion through excessive STAT4 signaling rather than providing suppressive signals","C":"Tumor PD-L1 binds PD-1 on tumor-infiltrating T cells, delivering inhibitory signals via SHP-2 phosphatase that exhaust and inactivate cytotoxic T cells at the tumor site — a co-option of normal peripheral tolerance mechanisms that prevents collateral tissue damage from being exploited by the tumor to evade immune destruction","D":"Tumor cells secrete large amounts of IL-10, which directly activates the tumor cells through autocrine signaling while simultaneously inducing apoptosis of tumor-infiltrating T cells via caspase activation","E":"Tumor cells upregulate FasL, which binds Fas on infiltrating T cells and triggers apoptosis through the intrinsic mitochondrial pathway, physically depleting the anti-tumor T cell infiltrate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adaptive immune resistance via PD-L1 upregulation is a major mechanism of tumor immune evasion. Tumor cells exposed to IFN-γ from infiltrating T cells upregulate PD-L1 (CD274) expression. Tumor PD-L1 then binds PD-1 on those same cytotoxic T cells, activating SHP-2 phosphatase that dephosphorylates TCR and CD28 signaling intermediates. This creates a state of T cell exhaustion — a progressive loss of cytokine production, proliferative capacity, and cytotoxic granule release, with upregulation of inhibitory receptors (PD-1, LAG-3, TIM-3, TIGIT). This mechanism directly co-opts the normal peripheral tolerance mechanism that PD-1/PD-L1 signaling provides to prevent collateral tissue damage during infections. Tumor downregulation of MHC class I (not class II) impairs CD8+ T cell recognition. Tregs suppress via TGF-β and IL-10 (not IL-12/IFN-γ). Tumor FasL-induced T cell apoptosis uses the extrinsic pathway (not intrinsic mitochondrial pathway), and is a separate but real immune evasion mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tumor PD-L1 upregulation → T cell exhaustion via PD-1; rationale for checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab, nivolumab target PD-1; atezolizumab, durvalumab target PD-L1); PD-L1 IHC used to select patients. COMLEX: T cell exhaustion = dysfunctional state with upregulation of multiple co-inhibitory receptors; reversible with checkpoint blockade.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — Peripheral immune tolerance"}]},{"sdlNumber":29,"title":"Arthrocentesis & Joint Effusions","questions":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the PRIMARY indication for performing arthrocentesis in clinical practice?","choices":{"A":"To deliver a therapeutic corticosteroid injection in any patient with established rheumatoid arthritis who requests joint-specific anti-inflammatory treatment during a disease flare","B":"To decompress an acutely swollen joint following trauma and thereby relieve pain and restore range of motion as the primary initial management of hemarthrosis","C":"To confirm the diagnosis of osteoarthritis in a patient with bilateral knee pain and radiographic joint space narrowing before initiating physical therapy","D":"To provide intra-articular hyaluronic acid viscosupplementation as first-line therapy for symptomatic degenerative joint disease in patients who have failed oral analgesics","E":"To obtain synovial fluid for diagnostic analysis in a patient with monoarticular or polyarticular joint pain or swelling of unclear etiology, allowing fluid classification and workup for infection, crystal disease, or inflammatory arthritis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The primary indication for arthrocentesis is to gain diagnostic information through synovial fluid analysis in patients with joint pain or swelling of unclear etiology. While arthrocentesis can also be used therapeutically to deliver corticosteroids or viscosupplementation, or to decompress a tense effusion, these are secondary uses. Confirming osteoarthritis does not require arthrocentesis. The critical clinical principle is that the purpose of the procedure must always be clarified upfront — and when infection is a concern, therapeutic injection must wait until infection is excluded.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary indication for arthrocentesis = diagnostic workup of unclear joint effusion; always rule out infection before any therapeutic injection. COMLEX: Arthrocentesis is a testable clinical skill; the key question before injecting any joint is whether infection has been excluded.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 1 — Indications for arthrocentesis include diagnostic workup of unclear joint effusions; absolute contraindications are cellulitis overlying the joint and bacteremia"},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with a large knee effusion requires arthrocentesis for diagnostic purposes. Physical examination reveals overlying skin erythema and warmth consistent with cellulitis of the skin surface above the joint. Which of the following correctly classifies this clinical finding in relation to arthrocentesis?","choices":{"A":"Cellulitis overlying the joint is an absolute contraindication to arthrocentesis because inserting a needle through infected skin creates a direct route for bacteria to enter the joint space and cause iatrogenic septic arthritis","B":"Therapeutic anticoagulation with warfarin at a supratherapeutic INR of 4.0 is an absolute contraindication that requires full reversal before any attempt at joint aspiration under any circumstances","C":"Prior hemarthrosis following a previous arthrocentesis represents an absolute contraindication preventing all future joint aspirations regardless of clinical urgency or diagnostic necessity","D":"Severe thrombocytopenia with platelet count of 60,000 per microliter is an absolute contraindication that cannot be overridden even with platelet transfusion and specific bleeding precautions","E":"A documented latex allergy is an absolute contraindication to arthrocentesis because all arthrocentesis equipment contains latex components that cannot be substituted with alternative materials"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cellulitis overlying the joint is an absolute contraindication to arthrocentesis because passing a needle through infected skin creates a direct inoculation pathway for bacteria into the joint space, risking conversion of a superficial infection to septic arthritis. Bacteremia is also an absolute contraindication. Anticoagulation is a RELATIVE contraindication — no hemarthrosis has been reported in patients with INR below 4.5 when the smallest available needle is used with prolonged post-procedure pressure. Thrombocytopenia, hemophilia, and bleeding diatheses are also relative contraindications. Latex allergy requires equipment substitution but does not absolutely prevent the procedure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Absolute contraindications = cellulitis over the joint + bacteremia; relative = anticoagulation (no hemarthrosis if INR <4.5), bleeding diathesis, thrombocytopenia. COMLEX: Never inject a steroid into a joint that may be infected — parallel principle to contraindications for aspiration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 1 — Indications for arthrocentesis include diagnostic workup of unclear joint effusions; absolute contraindications are cellulitis overlying the joint and bacteremia"},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies the most COMMON complication of arthrocentesis when performed with standard aseptic technique?","choices":{"A":"Iatrogenic septic arthritis with post-procedure joint infection is the most common complication, occurring in approximately 1 in 100 procedures even when strict sterile technique is maintained throughout","B":"Vasovagal syncope with transient bradycardia and hypotension is the most common complication, occurring frequently enough that all patients must be positioned supine regardless of which joint is accessed","C":"Hemarthrosis with clinically significant post-procedure joint swelling is the most common complication, requiring re-aspiration in the majority of procedures performed on anticoagulated patients","D":"Pain at the procedure site is the most common complication and is expected in most patients undergoing arthrocentesis as an anticipated procedural consequence regardless of technique quality","E":"Cartilage damage and direct nerve injury are the most common complications, occurring with high frequency in all arthrocentesis attempts performed without real-time ultrasound guidance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pain is the most common complication of arthrocentesis and should be discussed during informed consent as an anticipated experience. Infection is a rare but serious complication with a risk of less than 1 in 10,000 procedures when proper aseptic technique is used. Vasovagal syncope can occur but is not the most common complication. Hemarthrosis is uncommon even in anticoagulated patients when INR is below 4.5. Cartilage damage and nerve injury are rare potential complications. The relative frequency hierarchy is: pain (most common) > vasovagal reaction > hemarthrosis/bleeding > infection (least common at <1:10,000).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arthrocentesis complications in order of frequency: pain (most common), vasovagal syncope, hemarthrosis, infection (<1:10,000). COMLEX: Post-procedure patient education: expect pain, apply ice, avoid strenuous activity 24-48 hours, return for increasing warmth/swelling suggesting infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 1 — Indications for arthrocentesis include diagnostic workup of unclear joint effusions; absolute contraindications are cellulitis overlying the joint and bacteremia"},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A synovial fluid analysis returns WBC 18,000 per cubic millimeter with 65% polymorphonuclear cells, slightly turbid appearance, and poor viscosity on the string test. Which of the following effusion classifications and clinical interpretations best describes this fluid?","choices":{"A":"Group 1 non-inflammatory fluid; a WBC below 20,000 with turbid appearance indicates a mechanical or degenerative process without active leukocyte recruitment into the synovial space","B":"Group 2 inflammatory fluid; WBC 2,000-50,000 with PMN greater than 25% indicates an inflammatory process with a broad differential including rheumatoid arthritis, crystal arthropathies, or infectious arthritis requiring gram stain and culture","C":"Group 3 purulent fluid; the turbid appearance and elevated PMN percentage exceeding 60% indicate pyarthrosis requiring immediate hospitalization and intravenous antibiotics","D":"Normal synovial fluid with reactive leukocytosis; the WBC elevation is a normal variant seen transiently after vigorous exercise or minor trauma and does not reflect pathologic inflammation","E":"Hemarthrosis with inflammatory overlap; the turbid appearance results from red blood cell contamination during aspiration that artificially elevates the measured WBC count into an apparent inflammatory range"},"correct":"B","explanation":"WBC 18,000/mm³ with 65% PMNs represents Group 2 inflammatory fluid (WBC 2,000-50,000, PMN 25-75%), with turbid appearance and poor viscosity caused by inflammatory cell-derived enzymes degrading hyaluronic acid. Group 2 fluid has a broad differential: RA, gout, pseudogout, psoriatic arthritis, reactive arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, SLE, and infectious arthritis (viral, gonococcal, bacterial, fungal, spirochetal). Gram stain and culture must always accompany crystal analysis because infection and crystal disease can coexist. Group 1 requires WBC <2,000 and PMN <20%. Group 3 requires WBC >50,000 and PMN >75%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Group 2 inflammatory (WBC 2,000-50,000, PMN 25-75%): RA, gout, CPPD, infectious arthritis; gram stain + culture + polarized microscopy always required. COMLEX: Even in Group 2 fluid with crystals present, gram stain and culture must be sent — crystals do not exclude infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Effusions are classified as Group 1 non-inflammatory (WBC 200-2,000), Group 2 inflammatory (WBC 2,000-50,000), and Group 3 purulent (WBC >50,000)"},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with bilateral knee pain and radiographic osteophytes with joint space narrowing undergoes arthrocentesis. The aspirated fluid is clear to pale yellow with good viscosity, and the laboratory reports WBC 750 per cubic millimeter with 14% polymorphonuclear cells. Which diagnosis is most consistent with this synovial fluid classification?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid arthritis, which typically produces Group 2 inflammatory fluid with WBC 2,000-50,000 and PMN percentage exceeding 25% due to synovial pannus formation and cytokine-driven neutrophil recruitment","B":"Septic arthritis caused by Staphylococcus aureus, which produces Group 3 purulent fluid with WBC greater than 50,000 and PMN greater than 75% due to direct bacterial invasion of the synovial space","C":"Osteoarthritis, which characteristically produces Group 1 non-inflammatory synovial fluid with WBC 200-2,000 and fewer than 20% polymorphonuclear cells due to minimal synovial membrane inflammatory infiltration","D":"Gout, which characteristically produces Group 2 inflammatory fluid with WBC 2,000-50,000 and needle-shaped monosodium urate crystals identified by negative birefringence on polarized light microscopy","E":"Reactive arthritis following a genitourinary Chlamydia trachomatis infection, which produces Group 2 inflammatory fluid with elevated PMN percentage and sterile bacterial culture results"},"correct":"C","explanation":"WBC 750/mm³ with 14% PMNs represents Group 1 non-inflammatory fluid (WBC 200-2,000, PMN <20%), most consistent with osteoarthritis in the clinical context of bilateral degenerative radiographic findings. Other Group 1 causes include joint trauma, mechanical derangement, pigmented villonodular synovitis (PVNS — produces bloody fluid with very few WBCs), prosthetic joint wear, and avascular necrosis. Rheumatoid arthritis, gout, and reactive arthritis all produce Group 2 inflammatory fluid. Septic arthritis produces Group 3 purulent fluid. The low WBC and low PMN percentage reflect minimal synovial inflammation characteristic of degenerative joint disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Group 1 non-inflammatory (WBC 200-2,000, PMN <20%): OA, trauma, AVN, PVNS; PVNS is the classic exception — produces bloody fluid but few WBCs. COMLEX: In an older patient with typical degenerative X-ray findings and Group 1 fluid, OA is the leading diagnosis; no further diagnostic workup needed if presentation is straightforward.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Effusions are classified as Group 1 non-inflammatory (WBC 200-2,000), Group 2 inflammatory (WBC 2,000-50,000), and Group 3 purulent (WBC >50,000)"},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes presents with an acutely hot and swollen right knee. Arthrocentesis yields 55 mL of turbid yellow-green fluid. Laboratory results show WBC 85,000 per cubic millimeter with 90% polymorphonuclear cells and gram-positive cocci in clusters on gram stain. Which effusion classification and management approach are most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Group 2 inflammatory fluid because the WBC is below 100,000 per cubic millimeter; outpatient oral antibiotics with close rheumatology follow-up are appropriate for non-emergent bacterial arthritis","B":"Group 2 inflammatory fluid suggesting possible crystal arthropathy; polarized microscopy should be completed before initiating antibiotics to confirm whether gout or pseudogout is the primary diagnosis","C":"Normal fluid with a leukocyte contamination artifact; the WBC elevation is attributable to peripheral blood leukocytosis at the time of aspiration rather than true synovial inflammatory infiltration","D":"Group 3 purulent fluid indicating pyarthrosis; emergent hospitalization with serial joint drainage and intravenous antibiotics is required because this WBC and PMN profile with positive gram stain indicates bacterial septic arthritis","E":"Group 1 non-inflammatory fluid with a superimposed hemorrhagic component; the turbid appearance results from red blood cell lysis rather than leukocyte infiltration of the synovial membrane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"WBC 85,000/mm³ with 90% PMNs is Group 3 purulent fluid (WBC >50,000, PMN >75%), indicating pyarthrosis. Gram stain showing gram-positive cocci in clusters identifies Staphylococcus aureus — the most common cause of septic arthritis in adults, particularly in those with diabetes, immunosuppression, or prior joint disease. This is a medical emergency requiring hospitalization, intravenous antibiotics, and serial joint drainage. Group 3 causes include septic joint, crystal arthropathies (gout > pseudogout), reactive arthritis, and RA. The critical principle is that gram stain and culture must always be sent alongside crystal analysis when Group 3 fluid is encountered, since infection and crystal disease can coexist. Outpatient management is never appropriate for septic arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Group 3 purulent (WBC >50,000, PMN >75%): septic arthritis most feared; S. aureus most common in adults; N. gonorrhoeae in sexually active young adults; also gout, pseudogout, reactive arthritis, RA. COMLEX: Never inject steroids into a potentially septic joint; joint destruction can begin within hours of untreated bacterial infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Effusions are classified as Group 1 non-inflammatory (WBC 200-2,000), Group 2 inflammatory (WBC 2,000-50,000), and Group 3 purulent (WBC >50,000)"},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A clinician performs the string test on freshly aspirated synovial fluid by placing a drop between the thumb and forefinger and slowly separating them. The fluid forms a string that breaks at approximately 1.5 centimeters. Which of the following best interprets this finding?","choices":{"A":"The short string indicates normal viscosity because any measurable string formation confirms that adequate hyaluronic acid content is present and functional within the joint space","B":"The short string indicates poor viscosity, consistent with inflammatory synovial fluid in which inflammatory cell-derived degradative enzymes have broken down hyaluronic acid molecules and reduced normal fluid cohesion","C":"The short string indicates hemarthrosis with red blood cell contamination that physically disrupts the protein matrix required for formation of a normal-length viscosity string","D":"The short string is an artifact caused by specimen processing delay exceeding 6 hours, during which CPPD crystals degraded the hyaluronic acid component and reduced the fluid string-forming capacity","E":"The short string is pathognomonic for Group 3 purulent fluid exclusively and indicates bacterial pyarthrosis with WBC greater than 50,000 per cubic millimeter in all cases"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Normal synovial fluid forms a string of 4-6 centimeters in the string test due to its high hyaluronic acid content, which is produced by type B fibroblast-derived synoviocytes. In inflammatory conditions, neutrophils and other inflammatory cells release proteolytic enzymes that degrade hyaluronic acid polymers, reducing fluid viscosity. A string of only 1.5 cm indicates poor viscosity consistent with inflammatory fluid (Group 2 or above). The string test is a bedside qualitative assessment performed before laboratory results return. Poor viscosity is not exclusive to Group 3 — it is seen throughout all inflammatory states. Hemarthrosis produces bloody fluid but is not specifically characterized by poor string formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: String test: normal = 4-6 cm (high viscosity, intact hyaluronic acid); short string = poor viscosity = inflammatory fluid; correlates with elevated WBC and enzyme-mediated hyaluronan degradation. COMLEX: The string test is a rapid bedside tool; combine with the newsprint test (read through normal fluid, cannot read through inflammatory fluid) for initial qualitative assessment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Synovial fluid analysis includes WBC count with differential, gram stain, culture, polarized microscopy, and string test for viscosity; specimens must be processed within 6 hours"},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A clinician aspirates synovial fluid from a patient with an acutely swollen knee and prepares to send the specimen for complete diagnostic workup including cell count with differential, crystal analysis, and microbiologic culture. Which of the following correctly matches the tube type with its intended analysis?","choices":{"A":"Red top tube with no additive for cell count and differential; lavender top tube with EDTA for crystal analysis; blood culture bottles exclusively for gram stain and culture sensitivity","B":"Lavender top tube with EDTA for cell count only; red top tube with no additive for gram stain; sterile container for crystal analysis and polarized light microscopy only","C":"Lavender top tube with EDTA anticoagulant for cell count and differential; red top tube with no additive for crystal analysis; sterile container for gram stain and culture sensitivity","D":"Green top tube with lithium heparin for cell count and differential; yellow top tube with serum separator for crystal analysis; sterile container for gram stain and culture","E":"Any tube type is equally appropriate for all three analyses because EDTA anticoagulant does not affect crystal morphology, cell viability, or microbiologic culture results in standard clinical laboratories"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct tube selection is essential for accurate synovial fluid analysis: the lavender top tube (EDTA anticoagulant) is used for WBC count and differential because EDTA prevents clotting that would trap and sequester cells from counting; the red top tube (no additive) is used for crystal analysis because EDTA can form calcium-containing crystals that mimic CPPD under polarized microscopy, producing false-positive results; a sterile container is used for gram stain and culture to avoid anticoagulant interference with bacterial growth or staining. Using a lavender tube for crystal analysis is a recognized pre-analytical error. Green top heparin tubes can substitute for EDTA for cell counts but are not the standard choice.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Synovial fluid tubes: Lavender (EDTA) = cell count and differential; Red top = crystal analysis and chemistry; Sterile container = gram stain and culture. EDTA forms calcium artifacts mimicking CPPD — a classic lab pitfall that makes red top mandatory for crystal analysis. COMLEX: Always order all three specimen types when joint effusion etiology is unclear; proper tube selection prevents diagnostic error.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Synovial fluid analysis includes WBC count with differential, gram stain, culture, polarized microscopy, and string test for viscosity; specimens must be processed within 6 hours"},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A synovial fluid specimen is collected at 8:30 AM and transported to the laboratory, but it is not processed until 4:00 PM — approximately 7.5 hours later. Which of the following best describes the expected effects of this processing delay on the laboratory results?","choices":{"A":"Leukocyte count increases due to in vitro proliferation of white blood cells at room temperature, which can falsely elevate the WBC count into the Group 3 purulent range","B":"No significant change occurs in any laboratory parameters because EDTA anticoagulant in the lavender tube preserves both cellular and crystalline components for up to 24 hours at room temperature","C":"Monosodium urate crystals dissolve selectively because uric acid is highly soluble at room temperature, while CPPD crystals remain entirely stable in aqueous synovial specimens","D":"Gram stain results deteriorate due to bacterial overgrowth altering the microbial profile, while crystal morphology and leukocyte counts remain entirely unchanged during the delay period","E":"Leukocyte count decreases due to cell degradation, crystal count decreases (especially CPPD crystals which are inherently unstable), and artifactual crystals may appear that were not present in the original in vivo specimen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Synovial fluid specimens should be analyzed within 6 hours of collection to prevent pre-analytical errors. After this threshold, three key changes occur: (1) leukocyte count decreases as cells undergo apoptosis and fragmentation; (2) crystal count decreases — CPPD crystals are particularly unstable and dissolve with time, reducing detection sensitivity for pseudogout; (3) artifactual crystals can form in the specimen that do not reflect the in vivo state, creating false-positive results. White blood cells do not proliferate in vitro in synovial fluid. Monosodium urate crystals are more stable than CPPD but can also decrease in number with prolonged delay. Processing delay most commonly leads to underestimation of inflammatory severity and missed CPPD diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Process synovial fluid within 6 hours; delay causes decreased WBC (cell lysis), decreased crystal count (CPPD especially), and artifactual crystals; CPPD more unstable than urate. COMLEX: In specialty rheumatology clinics, synovial fluid is analyzed at the bedside immediately after aspiration to avoid processing delay and maximize crystal yield.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Synovial fluid analysis includes WBC count with differential, gram stain, culture, polarized microscopy, and string test for viscosity; specimens must be processed within 6 hours"},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the difference in crystals analyzed under a polarizing microscope.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Under compensated polarized light microscopy, intracellular needle-shaped crystals are identified in synovial fluid from a patient with an acutely swollen first metatarsophalangeal joint. When the long axis of the crystals is oriented parallel to the slow axis of the red compensator, the crystals appear bright yellow. What is the correct identification of these crystals and their birefringence pattern?","choices":{"A":"Monosodium urate crystals with negative birefringence; they appear yellow when their long axis is parallel to the compensator slow axis and blue when perpendicular, consistent with acute gouty arthritis","B":"Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals with positive birefringence; they appear blue when parallel to the compensator slow axis and yellow when perpendicular, consistent with pseudogout of the first metatarsophalangeal joint","C":"Calcium oxalate crystals with negative birefringence; they appear yellow when parallel to the compensator and are associated with primary hyperoxaluria and end-stage renal disease rather than hyperuricemia","D":"Cholesterol crystals with negative birefringence; they appear yellow when parallel to the compensator and are most commonly found in chronic rheumatoid arthritis effusions rather than acute crystal arthropathy","E":"Calcium hydroxyapatite crystals with strong negative birefringence; they appear yellow when parallel and are the cause of acute calcific periarthritis affecting the supraspinatus tendon most commonly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Monosodium urate (MSU) crystals are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent: when oriented parallel to the slow axis of the compensator, they appear bright yellow; when perpendicular, they appear blue. This is the hallmark finding of gout. Podagra — acute inflammation of the first metatarsophalangeal joint — is the classic initial presentation of gout. CPPD crystals have the opposite pattern: rhomboid or rectangular shape with positive birefringence (blue when parallel, yellow when perpendicular). Calcium oxalate crystals are associated with renal failure and are bipyramidal. Cholesterol crystals have a different flat, notched appearance. Hydroxyapatite crystals (Milwaukee shoulder) are not visible by standard polarized light microscopy and require electron microscopy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MSU = needle-shaped, NEGATIVE birefringence, YELLOW when parallel; classic in podagra (first MTP). Mnemonic: \"Yellow-Negative-Needles = gout.\" Serum uric acid may be normal during acute attack. COMLEX: Gout crystals are intracellular within PMNs in acute attacks and extracellular in chronic tophaceous gout; intracellular localization confirms active acute inflammation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Monosodium urate crystals are needle-shaped with negative birefringence (yellow when parallel); CPPD crystals are rhomboid with weak positive birefringence (blue when parallel)"},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the difference in crystals analyzed under a polarizing microscope.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman with a history of hyperparathyroidism presents with an acutely painful and swollen left knee. Knee X-ray shows calcification within the meniscal cartilage consistent with chondrocalcinosis. Polarized microscopy of the synovial fluid reveals intracellular rhomboid-shaped crystals appearing blue when oriented parallel to the axis of the polarized light compensator. Which of the following correctly identifies these crystals?","choices":{"A":"Monosodium urate crystals with negative birefringence; they appear yellow when parallel to the compensator and are needle-shaped rather than rhomboid, primarily associated with elevated serum uric acid levels","B":"Calcium oxalate crystals with positive birefringence; they appear blue when parallel to the compensator but are bipyramidal rather than rhomboid and are associated with end-stage renal disease rather than hyperparathyroidism","C":"Calcium apatite crystals with strong positive birefringence; they are rhomboid in shape and cause calcific tendinitis and Milwaukee shoulder syndrome in association with hyperparathyroidism and advanced age","D":"Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals with weak positive birefringence; they appear blue when parallel to the compensator axis and yellow when perpendicular, and are associated with pseudogout and metabolic conditions including hyperparathyroidism","E":"Corticosteroid crystals from a prior joint injection with weak positive birefringence; they appear blue when parallel and can transiently mimic CPPD crystals in post-injection arthropathy within 24-48 hours"},"correct":"D","explanation":"CPPD crystals are rhomboid or rectangular in shape and weakly positively birefringent: they appear blue when parallel to the compensator slow axis and yellow when perpendicular — the opposite optical pattern from monosodium urate. The clinical syndrome of pseudogout most commonly affects the knee in older adults. CPPD deposition is associated with metabolic conditions including hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, and hypothyroidism, as well as aging. Chondrocalcinosis on X-ray supports the diagnosis. MSU crystals are needle-shaped with negative birefringence. Calcium oxalate crystals are bipyramidal and associated with dialysis-related arthropathy. Hydroxyapatite crystals are not visible by standard polarized light microscopy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CPPD = rhomboid, WEAKLY POSITIVE birefringence, BLUE when parallel; metabolic associations: hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, hypothyroidism; chondrocalcinosis on X-ray. COMLEX: Mnemonic: \"CPPD = Chunky (rhomboid), Blue, Positive\" vs \"Urate = Needle, Yellow, Negative.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Monosodium urate crystals are needle-shaped with negative birefringence (yellow when parallel); CPPD crystals are rhomboid with weak positive birefringence (blue when parallel)"},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the difference in crystals analyzed under a polarizing microscope.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A rheumatologist examining synovial fluid under compensated polarized light microscopy wants to definitively distinguish monosodium urate crystals from calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals. Which of the following correctly identifies the distinguishing features between the two crystal types?","choices":{"A":"Monosodium urate crystals are rhomboid-shaped with positive birefringence; CPPD crystals are needle-shaped with negative birefringence; the two types share the same color under the polarizer but differ in morphology and clinical syndrome","B":"Both crystal types are needle-shaped and distinguished exclusively by birefringence color: MSU appears blue when parallel and CPPD appears yellow when parallel under compensated polarized light microscopy","C":"Monosodium urate crystals are needle-shaped with negative birefringence (yellow when parallel to the compensator); CPPD crystals are rhomboid or rectangular with weak positive birefringence (blue when parallel to the compensator)","D":"Monosodium urate crystals are negatively birefringent and appear blue when parallel to the compensator; CPPD crystals are positively birefringent and appear yellow when parallel; shape is identical for both types","E":"Both crystal types appear yellow under polarized light regardless of orientation relative to the compensator; they are distinguished only by shape, with needle morphology for urate and rhomboid morphology for CPPD"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Definitive crystal identification requires both morphologic and optical criteria. MSU crystals: needle-shaped (long, thin, pointed ends) + negatively birefringent (yellow when parallel to compensator slow axis, blue when perpendicular). CPPD crystals: rhomboid or rectangular shape + weakly positively birefringent (blue when parallel, yellow when perpendicular). These two distinguishing features together allow confident identification. Option D reverses the color assignments, which is a classic board-tested trap. Relying on shape alone is insufficient — both types can vary somewhat, and clinical context is also important. The birefringence direction is the definitive optical criterion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic board trap — MSU is YELLOW when PARALLEL (negative birefringence); CPPD is BLUE when PARALLEL (positive birefringence). Mnemonic: \"MSU = Yellow Needles (Negative); CPPD = Blue Rhomboids (Positive).\" COMLEX: Both crystals can produce Group 3 purulent-range WBC; MSU typically causes higher WBC counts in acute flares than CPPD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Monosodium urate crystals are needle-shaped with negative birefringence (yellow when parallel); CPPD crystals are rhomboid with weak positive birefringence (blue when parallel)"},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with a well-documented history of recurrent gout presents with a severely swollen, warm, and erythematous right knee. He reports a prior gout flare in this joint 4 months ago and requests a corticosteroid injection for immediate relief. You aspirate 35 mL of turbid fluid and are preparing your corticosteroid preparation. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step regarding joint injection?","choices":{"A":"Inject the corticosteroid preparation immediately after aspiration because the history of documented prior gout flares and the absence of systemic fever makes bacterial septic arthritis highly unlikely in this clinical context","B":"Inject hyaluronic acid viscosupplementation instead of corticosteroid to treat the probable gout flare while avoiding local immunosuppressive effects of steroids in a potentially infected joint space","C":"Inject a combination of corticosteroid and lidocaine to provide immediate anti-inflammatory and analgesic relief while awaiting culture results over the next 48-72 hours of inpatient observation","D":"Perform a second aspiration to confirm complete fluid removal before injecting corticosteroid, since complete joint decompression is required as a prerequisite to any corticosteroid injection","E":"Withhold the corticosteroid injection until gram stain and culture results are available to definitively exclude infectious arthritis; injecting corticosteroid into a potentially infected joint can worsen infection and cause rapid irreversible joint destruction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The cardinal rule of arthrocentesis is to never inject a corticosteroid preparation into a joint that may be infected. While this patient has a documented history of gout and the presentation could represent a crystal flare, septic arthritis cannot be excluded on clinical grounds alone — infection can present without systemic fever, and crystal disease and septic arthritis can coexist in the same joint. Injecting corticosteroid into a septic joint suppresses the local immune response, allows bacterial proliferation, and can cause rapid cartilage and subchondral bone destruction. The correct approach is to aspirate for diagnosis, send fluid for WBC count, gram stain, culture, and crystal analysis, and withhold all therapeutic injection until infection is definitively excluded.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Never inject steroid into a potentially infected joint — a cardinal clinical principle tested on boards. Gout and septic arthritis can coexist; history alone does not exclude infection. COMLEX: Corticosteroids are locally immunosuppressive; injecting into a septic joint accelerates joint destruction and represents a significant medicolegal risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — Indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis"},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman on warfarin for chronic atrial fibrillation presents with an acutely swollen and febrile right knee (temperature 38.6°C). Her INR measured this morning is 3.2. You suspect infectious arthritis and need to perform arthrocentesis urgently. Which of the following best describes the appropriate management of her anticoagulation status in relation to arthrocentesis?","choices":{"A":"Withhold arthrocentesis until warfarin is reversed with vitamin K and the INR returns below 1.5, because any supranormal INR represents an absolute contraindication to joint aspiration regardless of clinical urgency","B":"Proceed with arthrocentesis using the smallest gauge needle available and apply prolonged pressure post-procedure; anticoagulation with warfarin is a relative contraindication and arthrocentesis is safe when INR is below 4.5","C":"Administer fresh frozen plasma to immediately correct the INR before performing arthrocentesis, because an INR of 3.2 exceeds the safe threshold for invasive joint procedures in febrile patients with suspected infection","D":"Perform arthrocentesis only under ultrasound guidance with intraoperative coagulation monitoring in a procedural suite, because any therapeutic anticoagulation mandates real-time imaging for all joint aspiration procedures","E":"Discontinue warfarin for 5 days and bridge with low molecular weight heparin before performing arthrocentesis, because anticoagulation therapy is an absolute contraindication to all elective and urgent joint procedures"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anticoagulation with warfarin is a relative contraindication to arthrocentesis, not an absolute contraindication. Clinical studies have demonstrated no episodes of hemarthrosis in anticoagulated patients with INR below 4.5 when the smallest available needle is used and prolonged post-procedure pressure is applied. An INR of 3.2 is well below this threshold, and the urgent clinical indication — suspected septic arthritis with fever — mandates prompt joint aspiration. Delaying diagnosis of infectious arthritis while reversing anticoagulation could result in irreversible joint destruction. The absolute contraindications to arthrocentesis are cellulitis overlying the joint and bacteremia, not anticoagulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anticoagulation = RELATIVE contraindication; safe if INR <4.5 with small needle and prolonged pressure; do not delay urgent arthrocentesis. Absolute contraindications = cellulitis and bacteremia. COMLEX: Clinical urgency overrides relative contraindications; always balance procedural risk against the consequences of diagnostic delay for septic arthritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — Indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis"},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman undergoes knee arthrocentesis in the outpatient office for evaluation of a new joint effusion. Immediately after the needle is withdrawn, she becomes pale and diaphoretic, develops bradycardia (heart rate 46 bpm) and hypotension (BP 80/52 mmHg), and transiently loses consciousness for approximately 60 seconds. She is placed supine and recovers fully without intervention. Which of the following best describes this complication and its management?","choices":{"A":"Vasovagal syncope triggered by procedural pain or anxiety; managed with Trendelenburg positioning and vital sign monitoring until full recovery, with no antibiotic or emergency medication required and no indication for joint re-aspiration","B":"Septic shock from intraoperative introduction of bacteria into the bloodstream during arthrocentesis; requires immediate blood cultures, broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics, and transfer to the emergency department for resuscitation","C":"Hemarthrosis with internal hemorrhage causing hypovolemic shock; requires urgent ultrasound of the joint space to quantify the hemarthrosis and orthopaedic surgical consultation for operative drainage","D":"Anaphylaxis to the local anesthetic agent lidocaine; requires immediate intramuscular epinephrine, intravenous antihistamines, and transfer to emergency care for observation and anaphylaxis protocol management","E":"Cardiogenic shock from a procedure-induced myocardial infarction triggered by intense vagal stimulation; requires immediate 12-lead ECG, troponin measurement, and urgent cardiology consultation for percutaneous intervention"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vasovagal syncope is a recognized complication of arthrocentesis caused by a neurocardiogenic reflex response to procedural pain, anxiety, or witnessing blood or fluid. It presents with bradycardia, hypotension, pallor, diaphoresis, and transient loss of consciousness. Recovery is spontaneous within minutes in the supine or Trendelenburg position without the need for emergency medications. Septic shock from a single properly performed arthrocentesis would not manifest immediately post-procedure. Hemarthrosis does not cause hemodynamic instability within seconds of the procedure. True lidocaine anaphylaxis is extremely rare (reported reactions are usually to epinephrine in dental preparations) and would present with urticaria, bronchospasm, and persistent hypotension not responsive to positioning alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vasovagal syncope after arthrocentesis presents with bradycardia, hypotension, pallor, and transient LOC; managed with supine or Trendelenburg positioning. Distinguish from anaphylaxis (rash, bronchospasm, persistent hypotension despite positioning). COMLEX: Patients should be positioned supine during arthrocentesis to reduce vasovagal risk; always warn patients of this potential complication during informed consent.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — Indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis"},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with a 12-year history of seropositive rheumatoid arthritis presents with increased right knee swelling over 3 days without fever. Arthrocentesis yields 28 mL of yellow-cloudy fluid. Laboratory results: WBC 24,000 per cubic millimeter, 61% PMNs, gram stain negative, no crystals on polarized microscopy. Which of the following effusion classification and pathophysiologic explanation best describes this fluid?","choices":{"A":"Group 3 purulent fluid consistent with septic arthritis; the high PMN percentage and turbid appearance require immediate hospitalization and intravenous antibiotics regardless of negative gram stain findings","B":"Group 1 non-inflammatory fluid consistent with degenerative joint disease; elevated WBC in RA joints reflects synovial membrane thickening rather than active leukocyte recruitment into the fluid phase","C":"Hemarthrosis with secondary inflammatory response; the yellow color reflects bilirubin breakdown products from prior hemorrhage rather than active PMN infiltration of the synovial space","D":"Normal synovial fluid with hyperplastic synoviocytes; apparent turbidity is an artifact of pannus tissue fragments released during aspiration rather than true elevation of leukocyte concentration","E":"Group 2 inflammatory fluid due to active rheumatoid arthritis; synovial pannus tissue releases pro-inflammatory cytokines including TNF-alpha, IL-1 beta, and IL-6 that recruit neutrophils into the joint space, producing elevated WBC with turbid appearance and poor viscosity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In established RA, synovial fluid is classically Group 2 inflammatory: WBC 2,000-50,000/mm³ with 25-75% PMNs, turbid appearance, and poor viscosity. The pathophysiology involves activated synovial fibroblasts and macrophages releasing TNF-α, IL-1β, and IL-6, which upregulate adhesion molecules and recruit neutrophils. Negative gram stain and absence of crystals support RA flare as the diagnosis. However, RA patients are at increased risk for septic arthritis due to immune dysfunction and immunosuppressive therapy — a negative gram stain does not fully exclude infection, and cultures must still be sent. The WBC of 24,000 does not meet the Group 3 threshold (>50,000); Group 3 would mandate hospitalization and antibiotics pending culture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RA synovial fluid = Group 2; WBC 2,000-50,000; turbid; gram stain negative; no crystals; but always send culture — RA patients have elevated septic arthritis risk from immunosuppression. COMLEX: RA is driven by TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6 — the targets of biologic DMARDs (anti-TNF, tocilizumab, anakinra).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Synovial effusion classification and differential"},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old male soccer player presents to the emergency department with acute right knee swelling that developed during a game after a hyperextension injury 3 hours ago. On aspiration, the clinician obtains frank blood that does not clear with continued aspiration. Which of the following is the most common cause of hemarthrosis in a young, otherwise healthy athlete?","choices":{"A":"Pigmented villonodular synovitis, which produces recurrent bloody joint effusions due to hemosiderin-laden macrophage infiltration of the synovium and is the most common structural cause of hemarthrosis in young adults under 30 years of age","B":"Hemophilia A with underlying factor VIII deficiency, which causes recurrent hemarthrosis in young males and is the most common cause of bloody joint effusion in all age groups when trauma history is present","C":"Scurvy from severe vitamin C deficiency, which causes hemarthrosis due to perivascular collagen deficiency and is common in young male athletes who follow restrictive dietary regimens for weight management","D":"Trauma is the most common cause of hemarthrosis; in a young athlete with an acute hyperextension mechanism, anterior cruciate ligament rupture and intra-articular fracture are the most frequently identified causes following bloody joint aspiration","E":"Arteriovenous fistula of the popliteal vessels, which causes hemarthrosis through direct high-pressure arterial bleeding into the joint space and is the leading non-traumatic cause in athletic young men"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Trauma is by far the most common cause of hemarthrosis. In a young athlete with an acute hyperextension knee injury, the differential includes anterior cruciate ligament rupture (accounts for approximately 70% of acute hemarthrosis in young athletes), posterior cruciate ligament tear, patellar dislocation, and intra-articular fracture. Lipohemarthrosis (fat droplets in bloody aspirate) suggests intra-articular fracture and should prompt X-ray and consideration of CT. Other causes of hemarthrosis include bleeding disorders, pigmented villonodular synovitis (PVNS — important non-traumatic cause producing bloody fluid with few WBCs), tumors, hemangioma, scurvy, iatrogenic, arteriovenous fistula, intense inflammation, and Charcot joint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acute hemarthrosis in young athlete = ACL tear or intra-articular fracture until proven otherwise; bloody fluid with fat droplets (lipohemarthrosis) = intra-articular fracture. PVNS = recurrent atraumatic hemarthrosis with few WBCs. COMLEX: Hemarthrosis in a child should raise suspicion for bleeding disorder (hemophilia) or non-accidental trauma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Synovial effusion classification and differential"},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with known gout presents with a severely swollen right knee. Arthrocentesis yields turbid fluid with WBC 68,000 per cubic millimeter and 85% PMNs. Polarized microscopy reveals negatively birefringent needle-shaped intracellular crystals. Which of the following statements best describes the appropriate interpretation of these findings?","choices":{"A":"The finding of monosodium urate crystals definitively excludes bacterial septic arthritis; infection and crystal deposition are mutually exclusive processes, and antibiotics should be withheld to avoid unnecessary adverse effects","B":"A WBC count above 50,000 in the setting of crystal arthropathy is pathognomonic for tophaceous gout with synovial invasion; no further diagnostic testing is required and urate-lowering therapy with allopurinol should be initiated immediately","C":"Crystals alone do not exclude concurrent septic arthritis; gram stain and culture must still be performed because infection and crystal arthropathy can coexist in the same joint, and misidentifying a septic joint as crystal-only disease can result in irreversible joint destruction","D":"The high PMN percentage above 80% definitively excludes crystal arthritis as the cause; WBC above 50,000 with PMN greater than 75% is exclusively seen in bacterial septic arthritis and never occurs with gout or pseudogout flares","E":"Immediate surgical joint washout is mandatory whenever synovial WBC exceeds 50,000 regardless of crystal findings, because crystal-containing joints with purulent-range WBC have equivalent 30-day mortality to untreated bacterial septic arthritis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is a critical clinical principle from the SDL: crystal arthropathies — especially gout — can produce Group 3 purulent-appearing fluid with WBC >50,000 and PMN >75%. Finding crystals does NOT exclude concurrent bacterial infection. Gram stain and culture must always be sent when Group 3 fluid is encountered, regardless of crystal findings. RA patients and patients on immunosuppressive medications have elevated risk for septic arthritis in already inflamed joints. The clinically correct approach is to send gram stain, culture, and sensitivity alongside crystal analysis, and to treat empirically for septic arthritis if clinical suspicion is high pending culture results.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crystals do NOT exclude infection — always send gram stain and culture with Group 3 fluid even when crystals are found. Gout can produce WBC >50,000. COMLEX: Patients with crystal arthropathies are not protected from septic arthritis; increased susceptibility exists due to chronic joint inflammation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Synovial effusion classification and differential"},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A rheumatologist aspirates synovial fluid from a 79-year-old woman with an acutely swollen knee at 9:00 AM. Due to laboratory delays, the specimen is not processed until 4:45 PM. Polarized microscopy shows only rare crystals and an unexpectedly low WBC of 3,400 per cubic millimeter, despite the clinical presentation suggesting acute inflammatory arthritis. Which of the following best explains the discrepancy between the clinical picture and the laboratory findings?","choices":{"A":"The 7-hour processing delay caused leukocyte degradation reducing the measured WBC count and dissolution of unstable crystals (particularly CPPD), leading to underestimation of the true in vivo inflammatory and crystalline burden","B":"The low WBC count reflects spontaneous clinical resolution of joint inflammation during the 7-hour interval between aspiration and analysis, which can occur rapidly and completely in self-limiting crystal arthropathy episodes","C":"CPPD crystals are inherently rare in knee joint fluid and are typically found in large quantities only in the first metatarsophalangeal joint; the rarity of crystals in this specimen is anatomically expected regardless of processing time","D":"The laboratory used an incorrect compensator orientation during polarized microscopy, causing CPPD crystals to appear negatively birefringent and be misidentified as cellular debris rather than true crystals","E":"EDTA anticoagulant from the lavender top tube dissolved the CPPD crystals during transport, which is why red top tubes without anticoagulant should always be used for both crystal analysis and cell count simultaneously"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Synovial fluid should be analyzed within 6 hours of collection. After this threshold, two key degradation events occur: (1) leukocytes undergo apoptosis and fragmentation, falsely lowering the measured WBC count and potentially misclassifying Group 2 inflammatory fluid as Group 1; and (2) CPPD crystals, which are inherently less stable than monosodium urate crystals, dissolve and decrease in number. The 7-hour delay in this case most likely caused both a decrease in WBC count from true Group 2-3 levels down to an apparent 3,400, and dissolution of crystals that were present at the time of aspiration. This explains why the laboratory findings appear inconsistent with the clinical presentation of acute inflammatory arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Synovial fluid within 6 hours; delay causes ↓ WBC (cell death), ↓ crystals (CPPD especially), artifactual crystals; CPPD more unstable than MSU. COMLEX: When lab results do not match clinical presentation, always consider pre-analytical errors such as processing delay as a cause of discordance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"During arthrocentesis of an acutely inflamed wrist joint, the aspirated fluid appears cloudy yellow and opaque. When the clinician holds the tube up to a printed page, the text cannot be read through the fluid. The string test produces a string that breaks at 1 cm. Which of the following laboratory results is MOST expected for this synovial fluid specimen?","choices":{"A":"WBC 350 per cubic millimeter with 9% polymorphonuclear cells, consistent with Group 1 non-inflammatory fluid from degenerative joint disease with intact synoviocyte hyaluronic acid production","B":"WBC 15,000 per cubic millimeter with 58% polymorphonuclear cells, consistent with Group 2 inflammatory fluid in which enzyme-mediated hyaluronic acid degradation has reduced viscosity and increased turbidity","C":"WBC 2 per cubic millimeter with 4% polymorphonuclear cells, consistent with completely normal synovial fluid from a healthy joint with no inflammatory infiltrate or synoviocyte dysfunction","D":"WBC 92,000 per cubic millimeter with 91% polymorphonuclear cells, consistent with Group 3 purulent pyarthrosis from bacterial septic arthritis with complete synovial lysis","E":"WBC 1,100 per cubic millimeter with 18% polymorphonuclear cells, consistent with Group 1 non-inflammatory fluid from mild reactive synovitis with limited leukocyte recruitment into the joint space"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The clinical description — cloudy yellow color, inability to read a printed page through the fluid (failed newsprint test), and a 1 cm string (poor viscosity) — is characteristic of Group 2 inflammatory synovial fluid. Normal fluid is clear and viscous (4-6 cm string) and allows the newsprint to be read through the specimen. The inability to read newsprint through the fluid indicates significant WBC-mediated turbidity. The poor viscosity indicates enzymatic hyaluronic acid degradation by inflammatory cells. The most consistent expected result is Group 2 inflammatory fluid (WBC 2,000-50,000, PMN 25-75%). The fluid is too opaque and viscous-poor for Group 1 or normal, and was not described as the extreme turbidity of pyarthrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Newsprint test — can read through normal/Group 1 fluid; cannot read through Group 2/3. String test — normal 4-6 cm; short = inflammatory. COMLEX: Bedside qualitative assessment of synovial fluid provides immediate diagnostic direction before laboratory results return; integrates with full clinical picture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A clinician aspirating synovial fluid from a patient with an acutely swollen knee wants to specifically test for crystal arthropathy using polarized light microscopy. Which of the following tube selections is most appropriate for this analysis, and what is the reason for this specific choice?","choices":{"A":"Lavender top tube with EDTA is the correct choice for crystal analysis because anticoagulation prevents fibrin clot formation that would otherwise trap and sequester crystals before polarized microscopy can be performed","B":"Sterile container is the correct choice for crystal analysis because preservative-free conditions prevent crystal dissolution during transport and are required for accurate polarized light microscopy of all synovial specimens","C":"Green top tube with lithium heparin is the correct choice for crystal analysis because heparin prevents platelet activation that otherwise binds and degrades CPPD crystals during transport to the laboratory","D":"Red top tube with no additive is the correct choice for crystal analysis because EDTA anticoagulant in lavender top tubes can form calcium-containing precipitates that mimic CPPD crystals under polarized microscopy and produce false-positive results","E":"Any tube type is equally acceptable for crystal analysis because the polarizing microscope reliably distinguishes true pathologic crystals from anticoagulant-induced precipitates based on crystal morphology and size alone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Synovial fluid for crystal analysis must be sent in a red top tube (no additive) because EDTA — the anticoagulant in lavender top tubes — can form calcium-containing precipitates that appear as weakly birefringent rhomboid structures under polarized microscopy, mimicking CPPD crystals and producing false-positive pseudogout diagnoses. Red top tubes lack any additive that could interfere with crystal identification. The lavender top tube (EDTA) is the correct choice for WBC count and differential because anticoagulation prevents clot formation that would trap cells. A sterile container is reserved for gram stain and culture. Distinguishing true crystals from EDTA precipitates by morphology alone is unreliable in clinical practice.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crystal analysis = red top tube; WBC count = lavender tube (EDTA); culture = sterile container. EDTA calcium precipitates mimic CPPD — a critical pre-analytical pitfall. COMLEX: Proper specimen tube selection is testable procedural knowledge; always use the correct tube for each analysis to avoid diagnostic error.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the difference in crystals analyzed under a polarizing microscope.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with hyperuricemia presents with severe pain and exquisite swelling of his right first metatarsophalangeal joint consistent with podagra. Arthrocentesis yields a small amount of turbid fluid. The laboratory reports intracellular crystals within polymorphonuclear cells that appear bright yellow when their long axis is oriented parallel to the red compensator under polarized light microscopy. Which of the following correctly identifies the crystal type and its birefringence pattern?","choices":{"A":"Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals with positive birefringence; blue when parallel to the compensator and yellow when perpendicular, consistent with pseudogout affecting the first metatarsophalangeal joint in elderly patients","B":"Calcium hydroxyapatite crystals with no birefringence; these crystals are not visualized under standard polarized light microscopy and require specialized electron microscopy for definitive identification in acute calcific arthritis","C":"Monosodium urate crystals with negative birefringence; yellow when oriented parallel to the compensator slow axis and blue when perpendicular, definitively confirming acute gouty arthritis of the first metatarsophalangeal joint","D":"Cholesterol crystals with negative birefringence; bright yellow when parallel to the compensator, associated with chronic inflammatory effusions in longstanding rheumatoid arthritis rather than acute crystal arthropathy","E":"Corticosteroid crystals from a prior intra-articular injection with variable birefringence; they mimic monosodium urate under polarized microscopy and require correlation with injection history to exclude in the differential"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intracellular needle-shaped crystals that are bright yellow when parallel to the compensator represent monosodium urate (MSU) with negative birefringence — definitively confirming gout. Intracellular localization within PMNs confirms that the crystals are driving the acute inflammatory attack. Podagra, acute first MTP joint inflammation, is the classic initial presentation of gout and accounts for approximately 50% of first gout attacks. MSU crystals are yellow when parallel and blue when perpendicular. CPPD crystals have the opposite optical pattern (blue when parallel) and are rhomboid rather than needle-shaped; pseudogout most commonly affects the knee, not the first MTP. Hydroxyapatite crystals cannot be seen by standard polarized microscopy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gout = intracellular needle-shaped MSU, NEGATIVE birefringence, YELLOW when parallel; podagra (first MTP) is classic initial presentation; serum uric acid may be normal during acute attack. COMLEX: Do not initiate allopurinol or urate-lowering therapy during acute gout flare — abrupt uric acid changes can prolong or worsen the attack.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal analysis under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the difference in crystals analyzed under a polarizing microscope.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with known primary hyperparathyroidism presents with acute monoarthritis of the left knee. Knee X-ray shows linear calcification within the meniscal fibrocartilage. Polarized microscopy of the synovial fluid reveals intracellular rhomboid-shaped crystals that appear blue when their long axis is oriented parallel to the axis of the polarized light compensator. Which of the following best describes these crystals and their disease association?","choices":{"A":"Monosodium urate crystals with negative birefringence; they appear yellow when parallel to the compensator axis and are needle-shaped rather than rhomboid, with primary association with elevated serum uric acid levels","B":"Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals with weak positive birefringence; blue when parallel to the compensator and yellow when perpendicular; associated with pseudogout, chondrocalcinosis on X-ray, and metabolic conditions including hyperparathyroidism","C":"Calcium oxalate crystals with positive birefringence; they appear blue when parallel to the compensator but are bipyramidal rather than rhomboid and are associated with end-stage renal disease and dialysis-related arthropathy","D":"Calcium apatite crystals with strong positive birefringence; they are rhomboid-shaped and are associated with hyperparathyroidism-related calcific tendinitis and Milwaukee shoulder syndrome in elderly patients","E":"Corticosteroid crystals from a prior joint injection with weak positive birefringence; they appear blue when parallel to the compensator and transiently mimic CPPD crystals in the post-injection period within 24-48 hours"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CPPD crystals are rhomboid or rectangular and weakly positively birefringent: blue when parallel to the compensator slow axis, yellow when perpendicular. The clinical syndrome of pseudogout most commonly affects the knee in older adults, and chondrocalcinosis (calcification of fibrocartilage, best seen in the menisci and triangular fibrocartilage of the wrist) on X-ray supports the diagnosis. CPPD deposition is associated with hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, and hypothyroidism. MSU crystals are needle-shaped with negative birefringence (yellow when parallel). Calcium oxalate crystals are bipyramidal and associated with renal failure. Hydroxyapatite crystals are not visible by standard polarized microscopy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CPPD = rhomboid, WEAKLY POSITIVE, BLUE when parallel; chondrocalcinosis on X-ray; metabolic associations: hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, hypothyroidism. Mnemonic: \"Pseudo-gout: Positive, Blue, rHomBoid.\" COMLEX: The knee is the most common joint affected by pseudogout; first MTP is the classic gout joint.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal analysis under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the difference in crystals analyzed under a polarizing microscope.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman presents with acute monoarthritis of the right knee without prior gout history. Synovial fluid analysis shows WBC 36,000 per cubic millimeter with 70% PMNs. Polarized microscopy reveals weakly birefringent rhomboid-shaped crystals appearing blue when oriented parallel to the compensator. Which of the following correctly identifies the diagnosis and the crystal features that confirm it?","choices":{"A":"Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate deposition disease (pseudogout); crystals are rhomboid-shaped with weak positive birefringence (blue when parallel to the compensator), distinguishing them from the negatively birefringent needle-shaped monosodium urate crystals of gout","B":"Gout; rhomboid crystal morphology is an emerging recognized variant of monosodium urate deposition occurring in postmenopausal women, and the apparent color difference reflects compensator calibration error rather than true birefringence difference","C":"Septic arthritis; birefringent crystals in synovial fluid are always secondary artifacts of bacterial enzyme activity on synovial proteins rather than true crystal deposition within the joint space","D":"Milwaukee shoulder syndrome from calcium hydroxyapatite deposition; these crystals appear as weakly birefringent rhomboids in standard polarized light microscopy of large joint effusions in elderly women","E":"Pseudogout confirmed by crystal color alone; crystal shape (rhomboid vs needle) is an unreliable distinguishing feature because both monosodium urate and CPPD crystals can appear rhomboid depending on the angle of microscopic visualization"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The combination of rhomboid crystal morphology and weak positive birefringence (blue when parallel to the compensator) definitively identifies CPPD crystals, confirming pseudogout. Gout produces needle-shaped crystals with negative birefringence (yellow when parallel). Pseudogout most commonly affects the knee in elderly women, while gout is more common in middle-aged men. Calcium hydroxyapatite crystals are not visible by standard polarized light microscopy and require electron microscopy. True birefringent crystals in synovial fluid represent genuine crystal deposition, not bacterial artifacts. Both morphology and optical properties together provide the most reliable crystal identification — relying on either feature alone can lead to diagnostic error.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Use BOTH shape and birefringence to identify crystals; MSU = needle + negative = yellow when parallel; CPPD = rhomboid + positive = blue when parallel. Mnemonic: \"Negative-Yellow-Needles = gout; Positive-Blue-Rhomboids = pseudogout.\" COMLEX: Demographics matter: first MTP gout in middle-aged men vs knee pseudogout in elderly women with metabolic disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal analysis under polarizing microscopy"}]},{"sdlNumber":32,"title":"Osteoarthritis & Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis","questions":[{"id":"sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which primary event initiates the cartilage degradation cascade in osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Autoimmune T lymphocyte activation directed against type II collagen in articular cartilage","B":"Synovial B cell proliferation producing anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies against joint antigens","C":"Biomechanical stress causing chondrocyte injury and subsequent matrix metalloproteinase secretion","D":"Hematogenous seeding of the joint space by Staphylococcus aureus during an episode of bacteremia","E":"Type III hypersensitivity immune complex deposition throughout the synovial membrane and capsule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"OA is initiated by biomechanical stress — repetitive loading, obesity, or prior joint injury — that damages chondrocytes and triggers MMP secretion, degrading type II collagen and proteoglycans. Autoimmune T cell attack (A) and ACPA production (B) are mechanisms of RA, not OA. Hematogenous seeding (D) describes septic arthritis. Immune complex deposition (E) is a Type III hypersensitivity reaction seen in serum sickness and lupus nephritis, not OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = mechanical NOT autoimmune; risk factors are obesity, age, prior joint trauma. COMLEX: distinguish from RA by absence of systemic inflammation and normal inflammatory markers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines IL-1 and TNF overlap in OA and RA cartilage destruction"},{"id":"sdl32_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokines most directly drive matrix metalloproteinase upregulation and cartilage breakdown in osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"IL-1 and TNF-alpha stimulate chondrocytes to secrete matrix metalloproteinases and aggrecanases","B":"IL-4 and IL-10 suppress osteoclast differentiation and reduce proteoglycan matrix turnover rates","C":"IL-17 and IFN-gamma promote Th17-driven pannus formation and synovial membrane thickening","D":"IL-6 and CXCL8 recruit neutrophils that release elastase, degrading the subchondral bone trabeculae","E":"TGF-beta and BMP-2 promote chondrocyte anabolic activity and type II collagen synthesis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-1 and TNF-alpha are the key pro-degradative cytokines in OA — they signal chondrocytes to upregulate MMPs, aggrecanases, PGE2, and nitric oxide while downregulating anabolic genes. IL-4 and IL-10 (B) are anti-inflammatory; they do not drive cartilage breakdown. IL-17/IFN-gamma (C) and pannus formation are features of RA. IL-6/CXCL8 (D) are more relevant to neutrophil-driven septic or crystal arthritis. TGF-beta and BMP-2 (E) are pro-anabolic and contribute to osteophyte formation rather than cartilage degradation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-1 and TNF are the classic cytokine drivers of OA matrix breakdown — same mediators targeted in RA therapy. COMLEX: chronic low-level inflammation in OA differs from the acute inflammatory flares of crystal arthritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines IL-1 and TNF overlap in OA and RA cartilage destruction"},{"id":"sdl32_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of radiographic findings constitutes the classic triad of osteoarthritis on plain X-ray?","choices":{"A":"Juxta-articular osteopenia, periarticular bony erosions, and uniform bilateral joint space loss","B":"Soft tissue swelling, subperiosteal bone resorption, and symmetric bilateral joint narrowing","C":"Chondrocalcinosis, periarticular cyst formation, and fibrocartilage calcification throughout","D":"Joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and marginal osteophyte formation","E":"Diffuse osteopenia, cortical thinning, and pathologic fracture occurring at the epiphysis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The radiographic triad of OA is joint space narrowing from cartilage loss, subchondral sclerosis from reactive bone hardening, and marginal osteophytes from bony overgrowth at joint margins. Juxta-articular osteopenia and periarticular erosions (A) are features of RA. Subperiosteal resorption (B) is seen in hyperparathyroidism. Chondrocalcinosis (C) characterizes calcium pyrophosphate crystal disease. Diffuse osteopenia and cortical thinning (E) indicate osteoporosis rather than OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA triad on X-ray — narrowing, sclerosis, osteophytes; contrast with RA showing osteopenia and erosions. COMLEX: asymmetric joint space narrowing in weight-bearing joints points to OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines IL-1 and TNF overlap in OA and RA cartilage destruction"},{"id":"sdl32_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify the types of joints affected by osteoarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which joints are MOST commonly affected in primary osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Weight-bearing joints including the knees and hips, the lumbar spine, and the DIP and PIP finger joints","B":"Small joints of the hands and wrists bilaterally with symmetrical metacarpophalangeal joint involvement","C":"Sacroiliac joints, vertebral apophyseal joints, and entheses with predominantly axial spine distribution","D":"Elbow, shoulder, and ankle joints primarily due to repetitive overhead and distal extremity loading","E":"Temporomandibular joints, sternoclavicular joints, and acromioclavicular joints with bilateral symmetry"},"correct":"A","explanation":"OA predominantly involves weight-bearing joints — knees, hips, lumbar and cervical spine — as well as the DIP/PIP joints and first CMC joint of the hands. Small joint symmetric involvement including the MCPs (B) is characteristic of RA. Sacroiliac and apophyseal joint involvement (C) defines spondyloarthropathies. Shoulder, elbow, and ankle OA (D) may occur secondary to trauma but are not primary OA sites. Temporomandibular and sternoclavicular joints (E) are rarely primary OA sites.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = knees, hips, lumbar spine, DIP/PIP/CMC1; RA = MCP, PIP, wrist. The joint distribution is a classic differentiator on vignette questions. COMLEX: also know that the ankle and elbow are typically spared in primary OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Joint effusion class patterns differ between weight-bearing OA joints and inflammatory arthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify the types of joints affected by osteoarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Heberden nodes in osteoarthritis represent osteophytes located at which specific joint?","choices":{"A":"Proximal interphalangeal joints of the hands, producing dorsal bony swellings called Bouchard nodes","B":"Distal interphalangeal joints of the hands, forming visible bony nodular outgrowths at the joint margins","C":"Metacarpophalangeal joints producing ulnar deviation and swan neck deformity of the fingers","D":"First carpometacarpal joints causing squaring of the thumb base in advanced osteoarthritis","E":"Wrist joints producing dorsal ganglion cysts from herniation of synovial fluid through capsule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Heberden nodes are osteophytes at the DIP joints, most common in women with hand OA. They are palpable hard nodules and are a hallmark physical exam finding. Bouchard nodes (A) are osteophytes at the PIP joints, not DIP — this is the key distinction. MCP involvement with ulnar deviation (C) is the hallmark of RA, not OA. First CMC OA (D) causes thumb base pain but is distinct from Heberden nodes. Dorsal ganglion cysts (E) arise from tendon sheaths or joint capsules and are not the same as Heberden nodes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heberden = DIP; Bouchard = PIP. OA involves DIP; RA involves MCP and PIP but characteristically spares DIP. COMLEX: note that Heberden nodes are more common in women and have a familial component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Joint effusion class patterns differ between weight-bearing OA joints and inflammatory arthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify the types of joints affected by osteoarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The first carpometacarpal joint, which is commonly affected in osteoarthritis, is located between which two bones?","choices":{"A":"The capitate and the base of the second metacarpal, positioned centrally under high axial load","B":"The distal radius and the proximal carpal row, forming the radiocarpal wrist joint surface","C":"The trapezium and the base of the first metacarpal, forming the joint at the root of the thumb","D":"The scaphoid and lunate bones, which articulate in the proximal carpal row under gripping stress","E":"The hamate and the bases of the fourth and fifth metacarpals under loading during grip strength"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The first CMC joint is the trapeziometacarpal joint, articulating between the trapezium and the base of the first metacarpal. It is a saddle joint that enables thumb opposition and is subject to high pinch forces, making it a common OA site. The capitate-second metacarpal articulation (A) is not a mobile joint and rarely affected. The radiocarpal joint (B) is the primary wrist joint and is not a classic OA site. The scapholunate articulation (D) is commonly involved in ligamentous injury. The hamate-fourth/fifth metacarpal joints (E) are semicircular gliding joints not commonly affected by OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First CMC OA causes thumb base pain aggravated by pinching — classic presentation. Positive grind test confirms involvement. COMLEX: distinguish from De Quervain tenosynovitis, which causes pain over the radial styloid with Finkelstein test.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Joint effusion class patterns differ between weight-bearing OA joints and inflammatory arthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the clinical manifestations and classic symptoms patients with osteoarthritis exhibit.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which feature of joint pain most reliably distinguishes osteoarthritis from rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Pain that remains constant regardless of activity level and does not improve with rest at any time","B":"Pain that is most severe in the morning with prolonged joint stiffness lasting more than one hour","C":"Pain accompanied by systemic symptoms including fever, malaise, unintentional weight loss, and fatigue","D":"Pain that worsens with use and weight bearing, improves with rest, and morning stiffness lasting less than 30 minutes","E":"Pain that follows a migratory pattern affecting different joints sequentially over several days"},"correct":"D","explanation":"OA pain is mechanical — it worsens with use, loading, and activity, and improves with rest. Morning stiffness is brief, typically lasting under 30 minutes and resolving with movement. RA pain is inflammatory — worse with inactivity and prolonged morning stiffness exceeding 1 hour (B). Constant pain at rest (A) may suggest inflammatory arthritis, infection, or malignancy. Systemic symptoms (C) are features of RA, SLE, or infectious arthritis. Migratory arthritis (D) characterizes reactive arthritis or rheumatic fever.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic vignette cue — OA pain improves with rest; RA stiffness improves with activity. Morning stiffness greater than 1 hour is the hallmark RA discriminator. COMLEX: OA has no systemic inflammation; ESR and CRP are typically normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — ESR and CRP are typically normal in OA, distinguishing it from inflammatory arthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the clinical manifestations and classic symptoms patients with osteoarthritis exhibit.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the pathologic term for the ivory-white, dense, hardened surface of exposed subchondral bone seen in advanced osteoarthritis after complete cartilage loss?","choices":{"A":"Fibrillation, which refers to the frayed and granular softening of the articular cartilage surface in early OA","B":"Chondrocalcinosis, which refers to calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition within fibrocartilage tissue","C":"Eburnation, which describes the polished, bone-on-bone sclerotic surface that results from complete cartilage loss","D":"Pannus, which refers to proliferative inflammatory synovial tissue that invades and erodes articular cartilage","E":"Osteolysis, which refers to focal bone resorption producing geographic lucent areas on radiograph"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Eburnation is the ivory-like, polished, sclerotic appearance of exposed subchondral bone after complete cartilage loss — the bone-on-bone contact hardens the surface. Fibrillation (A) is an earlier finding, when cartilage becomes rough and granular but is still present. Chondrocalcinosis (B) is a distinct entity involving CPPD crystal deposits in fibrocartilage, unrelated to OA severity. Pannus (C) is the defining pathologic lesion of RA synovitis. Osteolysis (E) is a feature of aggressive bone destruction seen in malignancy or septic arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA pathology sequence — fibrillation early, then cartilage loss, then eburnation and osteophytes. Subchondral cysts form from synovial fluid entering bone through exposed fissures. COMLEX: know the morphologic sequence and what each term means on gross pathology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — ESR and CRP are typically normal in OA, distinguishing it from inflammatory arthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the clinical manifestations and classic symptoms patients with osteoarthritis exhibit.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old woman with a 10-year history of knee osteoarthritis reports a crunching sensation felt and heard each time she flexes and extends her knee while climbing stairs. She denies locking, giving way, or acute effusion. Radiograph shows osteophytes and asymmetric joint space narrowing. What is the best term for the sensation she is experiencing?","choices":{"A":"Clicking from intraarticular loose bodies shifting and dislodging as she flexes the joint","B":"Snapping from the iliotibial band moving across the lateral femoral epicondyle during flexion","C":"Popping from gas bubble collapse within synovial fluid during rapid capsular distraction","D":"Grating from a fibrocartilage meniscal tear creating mechanical impingement with knee motion","E":"Crepitus from irregular articular cartilage surfaces grinding against each other during joint motion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Crepitus is the audible and palpable grating or crunching sensation produced by irregular, fibrillated articular surfaces moving against each other — a hallmark of OA. Clicking from loose bodies (A) is also possible in OA but typically associated with intermittent locking. Iliotibial band snapping (B) occurs at the lateral knee or hip and is not caused by articular surface changes. Synovial gas bubble popping (C) describes joint cracking in normal joints. Meniscal tear grating (D) can co-exist with OA but represents a distinct structural lesion causing mechanical symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crepitus is a classic physical exam finding in OA — present over the knee during range of motion. COMLEX: combine with limited ROM, bony enlargement, and absence of warmth to build the full OA exam picture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — ESR and CRP are typically normal in OA, distinguishing it from inflammatory arthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the demographics of juvenile idiopathic arthritis and how it is similar/different from adult onset rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which feature of the antibody profile best distinguishes juvenile idiopathic arthritis from adult-onset rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"JIA is more commonly positive for rheumatoid factor than adult RA at the time of initial diagnosis","B":"JIA commonly shows ANA seropositivity but is typically seronegative for rheumatoid factor","C":"JIA exclusively produces anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies while adult RA produces rheumatoid factor","D":"JIA shows no detectable autoantibodies at all while adult RA requires RF positivity for classification","E":"JIA and adult RA have identical antibody profiles with equivalent rates of both RF and ANA seropositivity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"JIA is more commonly ANA seropositive but rheumatoid factor negative, in contrast to adult RA where RF and ACPA seropositivity are characteristic features. RF positivity is less common in JIA and, when present, may define a specific RF-positive polyarticular subtype. Stating that JIA has more RF positivity than adult RA (A) reverses the relationship. JIA does not exclusively produce ACPA (C), and ANA is typically the more relevant autoantibody. Saying JIA has no autoantibodies (D) is incorrect — ANA seropositivity is a defining serologic feature. The two diseases have distinct, not identical, antibody profiles (E).","boardPrep":"Step 1: JIA = ANA positive, RF negative (usually); Adult RA = RF and ACPA positive. COMLEX: ANA positivity in JIA is especially important because it increases the risk of uveitis — a key screening indication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — HLA associations and PTPN22 variants link JIA pathogenesis to adult RA genetic risk"},{"id":"sdl32_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the demographics of juvenile idiopathic arthritis and how it is similar/different from adult onset rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What are the minimum duration and age of onset required to diagnose juvenile idiopathic arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Arthritis present continuously for at least two weeks with onset prior to 18 years of age","B":"Arthritis present intermittently over three months with two separately documented disease flares","C":"Arthritis present for at least six weeks with onset before 16 years of age","D":"Arthritis present for at least three months with onset before 18 years of age per current US criteria","E":"Arthritis present for at least one year with radiographic joint damage confirmed on plain imaging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"JIA is defined as arthritis with onset before age 16 that persists for at least 6 weeks, with no identifiable cause. The 6-week duration criterion distinguishes persistent JIA from transient viral or reactive arthritis. A 2-week or 2-month threshold (A, B) is insufficient for diagnosis. Adult RA uses an 18-year age cutoff (C, D), but JIA criteria specify 16 years. Radiographic confirmation (E) is not required for JIA diagnosis, which is made clinically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: JIA = onset before 16 years, duration at least 6 weeks. Know this definition cold for pediatric rheumatology vignettes. COMLEX: subclassification into oligoarticular, polyarticular, and systemic forms has treatment and prognosis implications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — HLA associations and PTPN22 variants link JIA pathogenesis to adult RA genetic risk"},{"id":"sdl32_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the demographics of juvenile idiopathic arthritis and how it is similar/different from adult onset rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which shared pathogenic feature most directly links juvenile idiopathic arthritis to adult-onset rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Production of anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies driving complement-mediated cartilage destruction","B":"Immune complex deposition in glomerular capillaries causing concurrent nephritis in both conditions","C":"Rheumatoid factor driven B cell activation producing high-affinity IgG antibodies targeting Fc regions","D":"Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody production causing small vessel vasculitis in both conditions","E":"HLA and PTPN22 gene variant associations with Th1 and Th17 cell-mediated joint inflammation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"JIA and adult RA share genetic risk via HLA allele associations and PTPN22 polymorphisms, and both involve Th1 and Th17 effector cells with cytokines including IL-1, IL-17, TNF, and IFN-gamma as key pathogenic mediators. ACPA (A) is characteristic of adult RA but less prominent in JIA. Nephritis and immune complex deposition (B) are features of SLE, not JIA or RA. RF-driven B cell activation (C) is more specific to seropositive adult RA. ANCA vasculitis (D) characterizes granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis, not JIA or RA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: JIA and RA share HLA associations, T cell-mediated pathogenesis, and response to similar biologic therapies. COMLEX: PTPN22 encodes a phosphatase that regulates T cell receptor signaling — its variants lower the activation threshold for autoreactive T cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — HLA associations and PTPN22 variants link JIA pathogenesis to adult RA genetic risk"},{"id":"sdl32_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with a BMI of 34 presents with progressive right knee pain over three years that worsens going down stairs and after prolonged standing. Knee radiograph reveals asymmetric medial compartment joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and marginal osteophytes. Arthroscopy shows softened, frayed articular cartilage. Which sequence most accurately describes the cellular and molecular events underlying this patient's joint pathology?","choices":{"A":"Synovial B cell activation produces rheumatoid factor, which fixes complement, recruits neutrophils, and drives lysosomal enzyme release that progressively erodes articular cartilage","B":"Autoimmune CD4 T cell infiltration into synovium promotes pannus formation, and IL-17 and TNF are secreted, causing enzymatic cartilage degradation and periarticular osteopenia","C":"Crystal deposition within articular cartilage activates the NLRP3 inflammasome, releasing IL-1beta in bursts that recruit neutrophils and dissolve the cartilage matrix over time","D":"Mechanical stress causes chondrocyte injury, triggering IL-1 and TNF secretion, which upregulate MMPs and aggrecanases, degrading type II collagen and proteoglycans and producing fibrillation","E":"Infection seeds the joint via hematogenous spread, bacterial proteases degrade the cartilage matrix, and a neutrophilic inflammatory infiltrate accelerates progressive joint space destruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"OA pathogenesis begins with mechanical stress — amplified by obesity and abnormal joint loading — which injures chondrocytes. Injured chondrocytes secrete IL-1 and TNF, upregulating MMPs and aggrecanases that degrade type II collagen and proteoglycans. The result is progressive cartilage fibrillation, then full-thickness loss with eburnation. RF and complement-driven neutrophil arthritis (A) describes RA. Pannus and periarticular osteopenia (B) are RA findings. NLRP3 and crystal arthritis (C) describes gout or CPPD. Hematogenous seeding (E) describes septic arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The OA cascade is mechanical stress → chondrocyte injury → IL-1/TNF → MMPs → cartilage loss. COMLEX: obesity is the most modifiable risk factor — each additional kg multiplies knee joint reaction forces by 3-5x.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in rheumatoid inflammation"},{"id":"sdl32_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old obese man presents with bilateral knee pain worsening after walking one block. Arthrocentesis yields clear, viscous synovial fluid with a white blood cell count of 800 cells per microliter with predominantly mononuclear cells. Analysis of the synovial fluid reveals elevated MMP-13 and aggrecanase-2 levels. Which cytokine combination most directly triggered the upregulation of these cartilage-degrading enzymes in this patient?","choices":{"A":"IL-1 and TNF-alpha acting on chondrocytes to induce transcription of MMP and aggrecanase genes","B":"IL-17 and IFN-gamma driving Th17 and Th1 cell-mediated synovial fibroblast MMP secretion","C":"IL-4 and IL-10 providing negative feedback on chondrocyte proteoglycan synthesis and repair","D":"TGF-beta and BMP-2 stimulating osteophyte formation and chondrocyte hypertrophy at joint margins","E":"IL-6 and leukemia inhibitory factor transducing JAK-STAT3 signals that promote chondrocyte autophagy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-1 and TNF-alpha are the master regulators of cartilage catabolism in OA — they signal chondrocytes through NF-kB to upregulate MMP-1, MMP-3, MMP-13, and aggrecanases (ADAMTS-4 and ADAMTS-5) while downregulating type II collagen synthesis. The low WBC count with mononuclear predominance confirms a non-inflammatory effusion consistent with OA rather than septic or crystal arthritis. IL-17 and IFN-gamma (B) are Th17 and Th1 products primarily driving RA pannus. IL-4 and IL-10 (C) are anti-inflammatory. TGF-beta and BMP-2 (D) promote osteophyte growth. IL-6 and LIF (E) signal through JAK-STAT but are not the primary MMP inducers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA synovial fluid WBC is typically under 2000 cells per microliter, classifying it as non-inflammatory. This differentiates OA from inflammatory arthritis where WBC exceeds 2000 and from septic arthritis where WBC exceeds 50,000. COMLEX: in a vignette, clear viscous fluid plus low WBC = OA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in rheumatoid inflammation"},{"id":"sdl32_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman who underwent medial meniscectomy five years ago now presents with ipsilateral medial knee pain. MRI shows medial joint space narrowing and articular cartilage thinning. A research biopsy of the affected chondrocytes reveals elevated MMP-13 and prostaglandin E2 production with downregulated type II collagen synthesis. Which upstream molecular event most directly initiates this pro-degradative chondrocyte phenotype?","choices":{"A":"Complement C3b opsonization of exposed collagen residues leading to Mac-1 receptor mediated chondrocyte phagocytosis and lysis","B":"Mechanical stress from altered joint biomechanics activates NF-kB signaling in chondrocytes, inducing IL-1 expression and subsequent MMP upregulation","C":"NLRP3 inflammasome activation triggered by calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposits releasing IL-18 from chondrocytes","D":"HLA-DR antigen presentation of citrullinated aggrecan peptides to CD4 T cells recruited to the joint synovium","E":"Type I interferon signaling downstream of innate RNA sensor RIG-I activation in synovial fibroblasts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Post-meniscectomy OA is a classic example of secondary OA caused by altered joint mechanics. Without the meniscus, contact stress on the articular cartilage increases markedly, injuring chondrocytes and activating NF-kB, the master transcription factor for inflammatory gene expression, including IL-1 and MMP-13. This is the upstream driver of the pro-degradative phenotype. Complement opsonization and Mac-1 phagocytosis (A) are relevant to synovial macrophage activation, not primary chondrocyte biology. NLRP3 and CPPD crystals (C) are relevant to pseudogout. Citrullinated peptide presentation (D) drives RA. Type I interferon via RIG-I (E) is a feature of viral arthritis and SLE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Post-meniscectomy OA demonstrates how mechanical injury is the primary OA trigger — the same mechanism as obesity-induced OA. COMLEX: counseling patients about weight loss and joint protection is a key OMM-aligned management principle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in rheumatoid inflammation"},{"id":"sdl32_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify the types of joints affected by osteoarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old right-handed carpenter presents with a 2-year history of aching pain at the base of his right thumb, aggravated by pinching, gripping, and turning a key. Examination reveals point tenderness over the radial base of the thumb with a positive grind test eliciting pain and crepitus on axial compression and rotation. Radiograph shows joint space narrowing and subchondral sclerosis. Which joint is most likely the primary site of disease?","choices":{"A":"The trapeziometacarpal joint between the trapezium and the base of the first metacarpal","B":"The scaphotrapeziotrapezoid joint formed by the scaphoid, trapezium, and trapezoid articulating together","C":"The radiocarpal joint between the articular surface of the distal radius and the proximal carpal row","D":"The first metacarpophalangeal joint between the head of the first metacarpal and the proximal phalanx","E":"The thumb interphalangeal joint between the proximal and distal phalanges of the first digit"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The trapeziometacarpal joint — the first CMC joint — is the classic site of thumb base OA. It is a saddle joint that enables thumb opposition and is subject to repeated compressive and shear forces during pinching and gripping, making it the most common site of hand OA in manual laborers. A positive grind test specifically identifies this joint. The scaphotrapeziotrapezoid joint (B) can also develop OA but does not produce a positive grind test at the thumb base. The radiocarpal joint (C) is the primary wrist joint. The first MCP joint (D) and thumb IP joint (E) are not the typical sites of first CMC OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First CMC OA is tested frequently — thumb base pain with pinch aggravation and positive grind test is the classic vignette. COMLEX: differentiate from De Quervain tenosynovitis using Finkelstein test and localization to the radial styloid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Classes of joint effusions and differential diagnosis"},{"id":"sdl32_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify the types of joints affected by osteoarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman presents with three years of progressive right knee pain. She has had bilateral hard nodular swellings at the distal interphalangeal joints of her hands for over 10 years. On examination, no warmth or synovitis is noted at the DIP joints. Knee radiograph demonstrates joint changes. Which additional finding would be MOST consistent with osteoarthritis at this new joint?","choices":{"A":"Symmetric swelling of the metacarpophalangeal joints with prolonged morning stiffness exceeding 60 minutes","B":"Sacroiliac joint sclerosis with vertebral syndesmophytes bridging adjacent vertebral body margins","C":"Erosive changes at the ulnar styloid with subluxation of the extensor carpi ulnaris tendon sheath","D":"Medial tibiofemoral compartment joint space narrowing with progressive varus alignment of the knee","E":"Bony ankylosis of the distal interphalangeal joints with bridging bone across the joint space"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Medial tibiofemoral compartment narrowing with varus deformity is the classic OA pattern in the knee — the medial compartment bears the greatest load. Together with her bilateral Heberden nodes, this fits a pattern of generalized primary OA. Symmetric MCP swelling with prolonged morning stiffness (A) characterizes RA. Sacroiliac sclerosis with syndesmophytes (B) defines ankylosing spondylitis. Ulnar styloid erosions (C) are seen in RA. DIP joint ankylosis (E) can occur in psoriatic arthritis but not typically in OA, where osteophytes enlarge but joint space is preserved until late.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the association between Heberden nodes and generalized OA — patients with hand OA are at higher risk for knee and hip OA. COMLEX: varus knee alignment concentrates force on the medial compartment, accelerating OA progression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Classes of joint effusions and differential diagnosis"},{"id":"sdl32_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify the types of joints affected by osteoarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student is reviewing joint distribution patterns before a rheumatology shelf examination. She notes that osteoarthritis characteristically involves certain joints while sparing others. Which joint is most characteristically SPARED in primary osteoarthritis, in contrast to its frequent involvement in rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Distal interphalangeal joints of the hands, which develop Heberden nodes in established OA","B":"Hip joints, which are major weight-bearing joints commonly affected in primary osteoarthritis","C":"Metacarpophalangeal joints, which are classic sites of symmetric early involvement in rheumatoid arthritis","D":"Knee joints, including the medial and lateral tibiofemoral and patellofemoral compartments","E":"Lumbar facet joints and intervertebral disc spaces of the lower spine commonly affected in OA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The MCPs are characteristically spared in OA and are a hallmark site of RA. This joint distribution difference — OA at DIP/PIP/CMC1 versus RA at MCP/PIP/wrist — is one of the most tested clinical differentiators. DIP joints (A) are classic OA sites with Heberden nodes. Hip joints (B) are major OA sites. Knee joints (C) are primary OA sites, particularly the medial tibiofemoral compartment. Lumbar facet joints (E) are common OA sites in older patients, especially with degenerative disc disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA joint distribution — DIP, PIP, CMC1, hips, knees, spine. MCP joints are a red flag away from OA toward RA. COMLEX: \"Spare the MCP\" is the key rule for distinguishing OA from RA on the boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Classes of joint effusions and differential diagnosis"},{"id":"sdl32_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the clinical manifestations and classic symptoms patients with osteoarthritis exhibit.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old retired postal worker presents with right knee pain for four years. He states the pain is worst after long walks and stair climbing, and improves after 15 to 20 minutes of rest. He notices brief stiffness when rising from a chair that resolves within 5 minutes of walking. Morning stiffness upon waking lasts about 10 minutes. Laboratory studies show normal ESR, normal CRP, and no autoantibodies. Which feature MOST reliably distinguishes this presentation from rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Morning stiffness lasting less than 30 minutes that resolves promptly with activity and pain that worsens with use","B":"Occupational history of prolonged standing and heavy physical labor as a risk factor for knee joint loading","C":"Absence of fever and constitutional symptoms including night sweats and unintentional weight loss","D":"Unilateral single-joint involvement in contrast to the symmetric polyarticular pattern of active RA","E":"Radiographic joint space narrowing and osteophytes confirming degenerative rather than erosive disease"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The defining clinical discriminator is the duration and character of morning stiffness: OA stiffness lasts under 30 minutes and resolves with movement, while RA stiffness exceeds 1 hour. Combined with use-related worsening and rest-related improvement, this pattern is the hallmark of mechanical OA. Occupational history (B) identifies risk but does not distinguish OA from RA. Absence of systemic symptoms (C) helps but RA can also be relatively \"quiet\" systemically. Unilateral involvement (D) can occur in both OA and RA, particularly early RA. Radiographic findings (E) are supportive but do not replace the clinical pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Morning stiffness under 30 minutes = OA. Morning stiffness over 1 hour = RA. This is the single most reliable clinical discriminator on vignette-based questions. COMLEX: OA management starts with physical activity, weight reduction, and acetaminophen before NSAIDs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Laboratory testing for evaluation of rheumatic disease"},{"id":"sdl32_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the clinical manifestations and classic symptoms patients with osteoarthritis exhibit.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with a 15-year history of severe knee osteoarthritis presents with new episodes of knee locking during ambulation. Knee arthroscopy reveals multiple small fragments of cartilage and bone debris floating freely within the joint cavity. The articular surface shows pitting and irregularity, and the medial compartment has denuded subchondral bone. What is the correct clinical and pathologic term for the freely floating fragments observed in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Pannus, which refers to proliferative synovial tissue that invades and erodes articular cartilage in RA","B":"Tophi, which refer to monosodium urate crystal deposits that accumulate in soft tissues in gout","C":"Enthesophytes, which refer to bony spurs formed at ligamentous insertion sites into periarticular bone","D":"Chondroblastomas, which are benign cartilage tumors arising from epiphyseal chondrocytes in young patients","E":"Joint mice, which are loose bodies of detached cartilage and bone debris floating freely within the joint cavity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Joint mice are loose bodies — fragments of cartilage and bone that have broken off from the articular surface and float freely within the joint cavity. They can cause intermittent mechanical locking. They are a late complication of severe OA. Pannus (A) is the proliferative inflammatory synovium of RA that invades cartilage from the margins. Tophi (B) are chalk-white MSU crystal deposits in periarticular soft tissue and tendons in gout. Enthesophytes (C) are bony spurs at tendon and ligament insertions, seen in spondyloarthropathies. Chondroblastomas (D) are rare benign tumors in young patients, unrelated to OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Joint mice are a classic OA complication that can cause intermittent joint locking — distinguished from meniscal tears by the context of long-standing OA with loose body visualization. COMLEX: know the gross pathology sequence in OA: fibrillation → eburnation → osteophytes → loose bodies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Laboratory testing for evaluation of rheumatic disease"},{"id":"sdl32_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the clinical manifestations and classic symptoms patients with osteoarthritis exhibit.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman undergoes total knee arthroplasty for severe osteoarthritis. Gross pathologic examination of the resected tibial plateau reveals a dense, ivory-white, highly polished articular surface in the medial compartment where articular cartilage is completely absent. The surface has a smooth, burnished appearance. The lateral compartment retains some residual cartilage. Which pathologic process best explains the ivory-white polished appearance of the medial compartment?","choices":{"A":"Dystrophic calcification of necrotic cartilage remnants embedded within the subchondral bone trabeculae","B":"Eburnation from direct bone-on-bone contact, which progressively hardens and polishes the exposed subchondral bone surface","C":"Amyloid deposition within subchondral trabeculae producing a waxy ivory-colored bone marrow appearance","D":"Cortical bone hypertrophy from stress-shielding induced by years of adjacent ligament-mediated loading","E":"Avascular necrosis causing segmental collapse and reactive sclerosis of the medial tibial plateau"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eburnation is the pathologic term for the ivory-like, burnished, hardened appearance of exposed subchondral bone after complete cartilage loss. Direct bone-on-bone contact compresses and polishes the subchondral bone surface. It is a late-stage finding of severe OA and is a classic gross pathology descriptor. Dystrophic calcification (A) appears as chalk-white deposits within dead tissue, not as a polished smooth surface. Amyloid deposition (B) is a systemic finding in amyloidosis, not related to mechanical joint disease. Stress-shielding cortical hypertrophy (C) is an implant-related phenomenon. Avascular necrosis (E) produces a segmental crescent-shaped collapse rather than a diffuse polished surface.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Eburnation on gross pathology = end-stage OA. The term comes from the Latin for \"ivory.\" COMLEX: the pathologic sequence in OA is fibrillation → surface ulceration → full thickness loss → eburnation and subchondral cyst formation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Laboratory testing for evaluation of rheumatic disease"},{"id":"sdl32_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the demographics of juvenile idiopathic arthritis and how it is similar/different from adult onset rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 8-year-old girl is brought in for evaluation of joint swelling present for 3 months. She has swelling at the left knee and right wrist, with morning stiffness lasting approximately 45 minutes. Physical examination confirms effusions at both joints without active synovitis elsewhere. Laboratory studies show ANA positive at 1:160, rheumatoid factor negative, and ESR mildly elevated. No rheumatoid nodules are present. Which of the following best describes this patient's diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Adult-onset rheumatoid arthritis presenting unusually early with bilateral asymmetric joint involvement","B":"Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis characterized by high daily fevers and hepatosplenomegaly","C":"Reactive arthritis following a recent streptococcal infection causing oligoarticular joint inflammation","D":"Oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis, characterized by four or fewer joints involved with ANA seropositivity and absent rheumatoid factor","E":"Psoriatic arthritis in a child with distal interphalangeal joint disease preceding skin manifestations"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This presentation — arthritis onset before 16 years, duration over 6 weeks, four or fewer joints involved (oligoarticular), ANA positive, RF negative, no rheumatoid nodules — defines oligoarticular JIA, the most common JIA subtype. The ANA seropositivity also increases her risk for uveitis. Adult RA (A) requires onset after 16 and is typically RF and ACPA positive. Systemic JIA (B) requires high quotidian fevers, rash, and systemic features not present here. Reactive arthritis (C) follows infection and is typically transient and HLA-B27 associated. Psoriatic arthritis (D) requires either psoriatic skin/nail changes or a first-degree relative with psoriasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oligoarticular JIA is the most common subtype and classically presents with ANA+, RF-, in girls, with risk of uveitis. COMLEX: ophthalmology screening for uveitis is mandatory in ANA-positive oligoarticular JIA regardless of symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Genetic and environmental factors in rheumatoid arthritis development"},{"id":"sdl32_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the demographics of juvenile idiopathic arthritis and how it is similar/different from adult onset rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pediatric rheumatologist is leading a case conference comparing the clinical and laboratory differences between juvenile idiopathic arthritis and adult-onset rheumatoid arthritis. The audience is asked to identify one feature that is found MORE commonly in JIA than in adult-onset RA based on the known differences in disease presentation and demographics.","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid nodules forming subcutaneously at pressure sites such as the olecranon and Achilles tendon","B":"High seropositivity rates for both rheumatoid factor and anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies at diagnosis","C":"Predominant symmetric small joint involvement beginning at the metacarpophalangeal joints bilaterally","D":"Extra-articular organ involvement including interstitial lung disease and systemic vasculitis skin manifestations","E":"Oligoarticular joint distribution affecting four or fewer large joints with ANA seropositivity and absent rheumatoid factor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Oligoarticular presentation with large joint involvement and ANA seropositivity is more characteristic of JIA than adult RA. In contrast, adult RA more commonly presents with symmetric small joint polyarthritis, RF and ACPA seropositivity, and rheumatoid nodules. Rheumatoid nodules (A) are more common in seropositive adult RA and rare in JIA. RF and ACPA seropositivity (B) are hallmarks of adult RA, not JIA. Symmetric small joint involvement at MCPs (C) defines adult RA. Extra-articular organ involvement including ILD and vasculitis (D) occurs in adult RA but not specifically in pediatric JIA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: JIA is relatively RF-negative and ANA-positive; RA is relatively RF/ACPA-positive. Large joint oligoarthritis in a child with normal RF = think JIA. COMLEX: systemic JIA resembles adult Still disease and is the most dangerous subtype with risk of macrophage activation syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Genetic and environmental factors in rheumatoid arthritis development"},{"id":"sdl32_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the demographics of juvenile idiopathic arthritis and how it is similar/different from adult onset rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy with a 14-month history of daily fevers reaching 39.5 degrees Celsius, a salmon-colored evanescent rash, and hepatosplenomegaly is diagnosed with systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis. He has not responded adequately to NSAIDs and corticosteroids. His rheumatologist initiates tocilizumab. Which inflammatory mediator is most directly targeted by tocilizumab, and which adult rheumatic disease also depends on this same mediator as a primary driver of systemic inflammation and acute phase response?","choices":{"A":"Tumor necrosis factor-alpha, which is a primary therapeutic target in ankylosing spondylitis and psoriatic arthritis","B":"Interleukin-6, which drives hepatic acute phase reactant production and synovial inflammation in rheumatoid arthritis","C":"Interleukin-17, which promotes neutrophil recruitment and is targeted by secukinumab in psoriasis and spondyloarthritis","D":"Interleukin-1, which activates the NLRP3 inflammasome and is targeted by anakinra in autoinflammatory fever syndromes","E":"Janus kinase pathway signaling, which transduces multiple cytokine signals and is inhibited by tofacitinib in adult RA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tocilizumab is an IL-6 receptor antibody that blocks IL-6 signaling. IL-6 drives hepatic acute phase reactant production (ESR, CRP, ferritin), promotes B cell differentiation, and contributes to synovial inflammation — it is a key pathogenic mediator in both systemic JIA and adult RA. TNF-alpha (A) is targeted by infliximab, etanercept, and adalimumab — valid in RA and spondyloarthropathies but not the target of tocilizumab. IL-17 (B) is targeted by secukinumab for psoriatic disease. IL-1 (C) is targeted by anakinra and canakinumab, most relevant in systemic JIA and autoinflammatory syndromes. JAK inhibitors (E) target multiple downstream cytokine pathways but are distinct from biologic antibodies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tocilizumab = anti-IL-6 receptor; used in systemic JIA, RA, and COVID-19 cytokine storm. IL-6 is the principal driver of the acute phase response. COMLEX: systemic JIA is analogous to adult Still disease — high ferritin, quotidian fever, salmon rash are the classic triad.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Genetic and environmental factors in rheumatoid arthritis development"}]},{"sdlNumber":33,"title":"Gout & Crystal Arthropathies","questions":[{"id":"sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the mechanism of gout arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the primary mechanism by which monosodium urate crystals initiate gout arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Precipitation of monosodium urate crystals into joints due to hyperuricemia triggers innate immune activation and acute arthritis","B":"Deposition of calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals in hyaline cartilage triggers complement-mediated joint inflammation","C":"Autoimmune T cell attack on articular chondrocytes is mediated by anti-citrullinated protein antibodies in hyperuricemic patients","D":"Bacterial seeding of the synovial space during bacteremia produces suppurative joint destruction and crystal byproducts","E":"Immune complex deposition in synovium activates complement via the classical pathway causing crystal precipitation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gout occurs when serum uric acid exceeds its solubility threshold (approximately 6.8 mg/dL), allowing monosodium urate to crystallize and precipitate in and around joints, triggering innate immune activation. CPPD crystals (B) cause pseudogout, a distinct entity. Autoimmune T cell pathology (C) describes rheumatoid arthritis. Bacterial seeding (D) describes septic arthritis. Immune complex deposition (E) is a Type III hypersensitivity reaction seen in lupus and serum sickness, not gout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gout = MSU crystal precipitation from hyperuricemia; uric acid is the end product of purine catabolism. Classic vignette: obese middle-aged male, nocturnal first MTP joint attack. COMLEX: know both the mechanism and the classic patient demographics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Crystal microscopy: MSU crystals identified by needle shape and negative birefringence under polarized light"},{"id":"sdl33_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the mechanism of gout arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What serum uric acid level represents the physiologic saturation threshold above which monosodium urate crystals may precipitate in tissues?","choices":{"A":"Greater than 5.5 mg/dL in any patient regardless of symptoms or history of prior crystal documentation","B":"Greater than 6.0 mg/dL confirmed on two separate measurements taken at least four weeks apart","C":"Greater than 7.0 mg/dL in women and greater than 8.5 mg/dL in men at the time of initial diagnosis","D":"Greater than 5.0 mg/dL combined with at least one documented acute attack of monoarticular arthritis","E":"Greater than 6.8 mg/dL, which is the saturation point of monosodium urate in physiologic conditions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Uric acid has a saturation threshold of approximately 6.8 mg/dL in physiologic conditions — above this level, monosodium urate may begin to crystallize in tissues. Hyperuricemia is defined as serum uric acid above this threshold. However, only about 10% of hyperuricemic individuals develop clinical gout, indicating that additional factors including duration of hyperuricemia, temperature, and genetic predisposition determine who develops symptomatic disease. The other options represent incorrect thresholds or criteria not used in the definition of hyperuricemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 6.8 mg/dL is the urate saturation threshold — a key number for boards. Hyperuricemia is necessary but not sufficient for gout. COMLEX: urate-lowering therapy targets serum uric acid below 6.0 mg/dL to keep crystals dissolved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Crystal microscopy: MSU crystals identified by needle shape and negative birefringence under polarized light"},{"id":"sdl33_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the mechanism of gout arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Fifty percent of first gout attacks involve the first metatarsophalangeal joint. What is the specific clinical term for acute gouty arthritis at this site?","choices":{"A":"Pseudopodagra, which describes acute CPPD arthritis mimicking gout at the first metatarsophalangeal joint","B":"Podagra, which refers specifically to acute gouty arthritis affecting the first metatarsophalangeal joint","C":"Enthesitis, which refers to inflammation at the insertion of the plantar fascia into the calcaneal apophysis","D":"Dactylitis, which refers to diffuse sausage-like swelling of an entire digit from tenosynovitis in psoriatic arthritis","E":"Bursitis, which refers to inflammation of the retrocalcaneal bursa causing posterior heel swelling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Podagra is the classic term for acute gout specifically affecting the first MTP joint. It presents as sudden-onset, exquisitely painful monoarthritis with erythema and warmth, classically waking the patient from sleep. The first MTP is the most common site because urate solubility decreases at cooler peripheral temperatures. Pseudopodagra (A) is a term for CPPD mimicking podagra at the first MTP, but the classic term remains podagra for the gout-specific entity. Enthesitis (C) affects tendon/ligament insertions (spondyloarthropathy). Dactylitis (D) is psoriatic arthritis. Bursitis (E) involves bursal sacs, not joint spaces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Podagra = acute gout of the first MTP joint = the classic board presentation. The joint is cool, peripheral, and at lower temperature — ideal for MSU precipitation. COMLEX: nocturnal onset, exquisite tenderness, and rapid resolution without treatment are the hallmarks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Crystal microscopy: MSU crystals identified by needle shape and negative birefringence under polarized light"},{"id":"sdl33_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the metabolic pathways which produce purine excess.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Uric acid is the final catabolic product of which metabolic pathway?","choices":{"A":"Beta-oxidation of long-chain fatty acids, releasing acetyl-CoA and ketone bodies that accumulate in metabolic acidosis","B":"Anaerobic glycolysis, which produces pyruvate converted to lactate when oxygen delivery to tissues is insufficient","C":"Purine catabolism, in which hypoxanthine and xanthine are sequentially oxidized to uric acid by xanthine oxidase","D":"Pyrimidine degradation, where cytosine and uracil are catabolized to beta-alanine and excreted by the kidney","E":"Amino acid catabolism, where phenylalanine is hydroxylated to tyrosine and then degraded to fumarate and acetoacetate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Uric acid is the end product of purine catabolism in humans. Adenine and guanine nucleotides are degraded through a series of steps: AMP → IMP → hypoxanthine → xanthine → uric acid; and GMP → guanosine → guanine → xanthine → uric acid. Xanthine oxidase catalyzes both the conversion of hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid. This is why allopurinol and febuxostat — xanthine oxidase inhibitors — are the cornerstones of urate-lowering therapy. The other pathways (fatty acid oxidation, glycolysis, pyrimidine catabolism, amino acid degradation) do not produce uric acid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Uric acid = end product of purine (not pyrimidine) catabolism. Xanthine oxidase is the drug target for allopurinol and febuxostat. COMLEX: pyrimidines are degraded to beta-alanine and beta-aminoisobutyrate, not uric acid — a common distractor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Serum uric acid is the key diagnostic lab test in crystal arthropathy workup"},{"id":"sdl33_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the metabolic pathways which produce purine excess.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Deficiency of which enzyme in the purine salvage pathway leads to Lesch-Nyhan syndrome and is inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern?","choices":{"A":"Adenosine deaminase (ADA), whose deficiency causes severe combined immunodeficiency from toxic deoxyadenosine accumulation","B":"Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT), which recycles free purine bases into nucleotides via the salvage pathway","C":"PRPP synthetase, whose superactivity leads to overproduction of purine nucleotides via the de novo synthesis pathway","D":"Xanthine oxidase, whose deficiency causes xanthinuria with reduced uric acid production and xanthine kidney stones","E":"Glucose-6-phosphatase, whose deficiency causes glycogen storage disease type I with secondary hyperuricemia from lactic acidosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HGPRT catalyzes the salvage of hypoxanthine and guanine back to IMP and GMP using PRPP. When HGPRT is deficient, free purines cannot be recycled and are instead catabolized to uric acid, causing severe hyperuricemia. Complete HGPRT deficiency causes Lesch-Nyhan syndrome (hyperuricemia, gout, neurologic features, self-mutilation); partial deficiency causes gout alone. HGPRT is encoded on the X chromosome, making Lesch-Nyhan X-linked recessive. ADA deficiency (A) causes SCID, not gout. PRPP synthetase superactivity (C) causes gout from overproduction, not a salvage defect. Xanthine oxidase deficiency (D) causes xanthinuria with reduced uric acid. G6Pase deficiency (E) is a glycogen storage disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HGPRT deficiency = Lesch-Nyhan syndrome = X-linked recessive = hyperuricemia + neurologic features. Partial deficiency = gout only. COMLEX: HGPRT is the salvage pathway enzyme; deficiency forces purines into degradation, producing excess uric acid.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Serum uric acid is the key diagnostic lab test in crystal arthropathy workup"},{"id":"sdl33_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the metabolic pathways which produce purine excess.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with acute myeloid leukemia develops excruciating right first toe pain three days after starting induction chemotherapy. Serum uric acid is 14.2 mg/dL. Which mechanism best explains this secondary hyperuricemia?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin-induced proximal tubular nephrotoxicity decreasing glomerular filtration and reducing uric acid excretion","B":"Corticosteroid use during induction therapy reducing renal tubular secretion of urate into the collecting duct","C":"Methotrexate-induced folate depletion increasing de novo purine synthesis and channeling more purines to uric acid","D":"Rapid tumor cell lysis releases massive quantities of intracellular nucleic acids catabolized to uric acid, causing tumor lysis syndrome","E":"Cyclophosphamide-induced urothelial injury impairing renal uric acid clearance across the proximal tubule"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tumor lysis syndrome occurs when chemotherapy destroys a large tumor cell burden rapidly, releasing massive amounts of intracellular nucleic acids. These are catabolized through purine degradation pathways to produce enormous quantities of uric acid — a classic cause of secondary gout and acute urate nephropathy. This is why allopurinol or rasburicase is started prophylactically before chemotherapy. Cisplatin causes nephrotoxicity (A) and may reduce excretion but is not the primary mechanism here. Corticosteroids (B) can promote hyperuricemia but not through direct tubular secretion reduction. Methotrexate (C) inhibits DHFR and folate metabolism but is not the mechanism in this case. Cyclophosphamide (D) causes hemorrhagic cystitis, not uric acid renal disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tumor lysis syndrome is a classic cause of secondary gout — high cell turnover releases nucleic acids that are degraded to uric acid. Other high-turnover causes: polycythemia vera, psoriasis. COMLEX: rasburicase converts uric acid to more soluble allantoin and is used for tumor lysis prophylaxis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Serum uric acid is the key diagnostic lab test in crystal arthropathy workup"},{"id":"sdl33_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanism of inflammation occurring with acute gout arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cellular mechanism is responsible for initiating the acute inflammatory cascade when monosodium urate crystals are deposited in a joint?","choices":{"A":"Macrophages phagocytose MSU crystals, activating the NLRP3 inflammasome and generating active IL-1beta via caspase-1 cleavage","B":"Autoantibodies bind to MSU crystal surfaces activating complement via the classical pathway with MAC formation","C":"Th17 lymphocytes infiltrate the joint and release IL-17, which stimulates synovial fibroblasts to recruit neutrophils","D":"MSU crystals directly activate mast cells to degranulate, releasing histamine, tryptase, and prostaglandins into the joint","E":"MSU crystals activate the coagulation cascade generating thrombin and bradykinin that increase vascular permeability"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The central mechanism of acute gouty inflammation is crystal phagocytosis by resident synovial macrophages. Intracellular MSU crystals are sensed by the NLRP3 inflammasome, which oligomerizes and activates caspase-1. Active caspase-1 cleaves pro-IL-1beta to generate mature IL-1beta, which is secreted and drives neutrophil recruitment, vasodilation, and acute joint inflammation. The autoantibody/complement pathway (B) is relevant to RA and lupus. Th17 cells and IL-17 (C) drive RA and psoriatic arthritis. Mast cell degranulation (D) is a Type I hypersensitivity reaction. Coagulation cascade activation and bradykinin (E) are not the primary mechanisms in gout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MSU crystals → NLRP3 inflammasome → caspase-1 → IL-1beta → neutrophil influx → acute arthritis. The NLRP3 inflammasome pathway is the highest-yield mechanistic concept in gout. COMLEX: anakinra (IL-1 receptor antagonist) can be used for refractory acute gout, demonstrating the centrality of IL-1.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Macrophages and neutrophils (innate immune cells) are the primary effectors of acute gouty inflammation"},{"id":"sdl33_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanism of inflammation occurring with acute gout arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What happens to neutrophils that phagocytose monosodium urate crystals within the inflamed joint, and how does this amplify the inflammatory response?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophils present crystal-derived peptide antigens on MHC class II molecules to activate CD4 T helper lymphocytes","B":"Neutrophils release anti-inflammatory IL-4 and IL-10 in a negative feedback loop to terminate the acute attack","C":"Crystals rupture phagolysosomes, releasing proteases and reactive oxygen species that amplify inflammation and recruit additional leukocytes","D":"Crystals are safely sequestered within phagolysosomes and transported to regional lymph nodes for permanent clearance","E":"Crystal phagocytosis immediately triggers neutrophil apoptosis via caspase-3 activation, resolving inflammation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MSU crystals are hard, needle-shaped structures that rupture the phagolysosomal membrane after being engulfed by neutrophils. This releases lysosomal enzymes (proteases, elastase), reactive oxygen species, and additional pro-inflammatory mediators into the cytoplasm and extracellular space, amplifying the inflammatory vicious cycle. More neutrophils are recruited by these mediators, further driving crystal phagocytosis and phagolysosomal rupture. Antigen presentation to CD4 T cells (A) is a function of dendritic cells and macrophages, not the primary neutrophil response in gout. IL-4/IL-10 release (B) would terminate, not amplify, inflammation. Safe crystal sequestration and lymph node transport (D) does not occur. Immediate apoptosis (E) does not describe the mechanism in acute gout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MSU crystals rupture phagolysosomes → protease and ROS release → amplification of inflammation. This is why acute gout attacks are so severe and self-propagating. COMLEX: colchicine prevents neutrophil microtubule polymerization, blocking chemotaxis to the joint and crystal phagocytosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Macrophages and neutrophils (innate immune cells) are the primary effectors of acute gouty inflammation"},{"id":"sdl33_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanism of inflammation occurring with acute gout arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokine plays the most central role in amplifying neutrophil recruitment and sustaining the acute inflammatory response in gouty arthritis?","choices":{"A":"IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages and promotes Th1 cell expansion in chronically inflamed joints","B":"TGF-beta, which promotes fibrosis around urate crystal deposits in chronic tophaceous gout lesions","C":"IL-17, which is secreted by Th17 cells and drives neutrophil chemokine production in the joint space","D":"IL-1beta, activated by inflammasome-mediated caspase-1 cleavage, which drives neutrophil influx and vascular permeability","E":"IL-6, which drives hepatic acute phase protein synthesis and systemic fever but is downstream of IL-1"},"correct":"D","explanation":"IL-1beta is the master cytokine of acute gout. It is generated by NLRP3 inflammasome activation in macrophages and drives neutrophil recruitment, endothelial activation, and prostaglandin production — producing the cardinal features of acute inflammation: pain, warmth, redness, and swelling. The centrality of IL-1beta is validated by the effectiveness of anakinra (IL-1 receptor antagonist) and canakinumab (anti-IL-1beta antibody) in refractory acute gout. IFN-gamma (A) is a Th1/NK cell product driving macrophage activation, not the primary acute gout mediator. TGF-beta (B) drives fibrosis in chronic disease. IL-17 (C) is primarily from Th17 cells and is central to RA/spondyloarthropathies. IL-6 (D) is a downstream mediator elevated in gout but not the initiating cytokine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-1beta is the key cytokine in acute gout — produced by NLRP3 inflammasome activation in macrophages. Targeting IL-1 with anakinra or canakinumab is a therapeutic option. COMLEX: colchicine and NSAIDs work upstream of IL-1; biologics targeting IL-1 work downstream.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Macrophages and neutrophils (innate immune cells) are the primary effectors of acute gouty inflammation"},{"id":"sdl33_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the composition of tophi and typical distribution of these lesions.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the composition of a gouty tophus on histologic examination?","choices":{"A":"Deposits of calcium pyrophosphate crystals surrounded by fibrous tissue and multinucleated macrophages","B":"Collections of hydroxyapatite crystals within tendon sheaths causing periarticular calcifications and pain","C":"Immune complexes of IgG antibodies and complement factors deposited in periarticular soft tissue","D":"Aggregates of amyloid fibrils surrounded by plasma cells in chronically inflamed periarticular tissue","E":"Aggregates of monosodium urate crystals surrounded by foreign-body giant cells, fibroblasts, and chronic inflammatory tissue"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tophi are the pathognomonic hallmark of chronic gout. Histologically, they consist of large aggregates of monosodium urate crystals surrounded by a foreign-body giant cell reaction, reactive fibroblasts, and chronic mononuclear inflammatory cells. The urate crystals appear as needle-shaped structures, and the surrounding inflammatory reaction includes multinucleated giant cells attempting to phagocytose the crystals. Calcium pyrophosphate deposits (A) describe CPPD disease, not gout. Hydroxyapatite deposits (B) cause calcific tendinitis (e.g., rotator cuff). Immune complex deposition (C) is a feature of serum sickness and lupus. Amyloid deposits (D) are seen in AA or AL amyloidosis, not in tophi.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tophus = MSU crystals + giant cell foreign body reaction + chronic inflammation. On gross exam, tophi appear as chalk-white nodules. COMLEX: tophi are pathognomonic for gout — no other crystal arthropathy produces tophi at classic sites.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Tophi contain MSU crystals identifiable as needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals under polarized light"},{"id":"sdl33_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the composition of tophi and typical distribution of these lesions.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which anatomical locations are MOST characteristic for extraarticular tophus formation in patients with chronic gout?","choices":{"A":"Liver sinusoids, splenic follicles, and mesenteric lymph nodes as urate is cleared by the reticuloendothelial system","B":"The helix of the ear, Achilles tendon, olecranon bursa, and finger tendons are classic sites of tophus deposition","C":"Lung parenchyma and pleural surfaces as MSU crystals disseminate hematogenously to serosal surfaces","D":"Vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs producing spondylitic changes in chronic tophaceous gout disease","E":"Coronary arteries and cardiac valve leaflets as MSU crystals deposit along high-flow vascular endothelium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tophi form in cool, avascular, or poorly vascularized tissues where urate solubility is lowest. Classic extraarticular sites include the ear helix (pinna), Achilles tendon, olecranon bursa, and the fingers and toes. Less commonly, tophi form in the articular cartilage, ligaments, and bursae. The ear helix tophus is the classic board examination physical finding — a whitish nodule on the antihelix or helix. Tophi do not form in the liver or lymph nodes (A). Hematogenous dissemination to serosal surfaces (B) does not occur with urate crystals. Vertebral involvement (C) is not a feature of gout. Vascular deposition (D) is not a recognized tophus site.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ear helix tophi are pathognomonic for gout on physical exam — chalky white nodules on the antihelix. COMLEX: tophi at the olecranon bursa and Achilles tendon are also classic exam findings; note that tophi are painless unless inflamed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Tophi contain MSU crystals identifiable as needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals under polarized light"},{"id":"sdl33_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the composition of tophi and typical distribution of these lesions.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In chronic tophaceous gout, what severe joint complication can develop after years of repeated urate crystal deposition and inflammatory attacks?","choices":{"A":"Osteonecrosis from vascular compromise caused by urate crystal emboli in periarticular feeding arteries","B":"Periarticular amyloidosis from deposition of amyloid fibrils around chronic crystal-laden inflammatory tissue","C":"Septic arthritis from secondary bacterial seeding of chronically inflamed and structurally damaged synovial tissue","D":"Fibrous or bony ankylosis from urate pannus formation and progressive cartilage and juxtaarticular bone erosion","E":"Avascular necrosis of the femoral head from urate crystal deposition within the hip joint capsule and vessels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chronic tophaceous gout develops after approximately 10 years of untreated or undertreated disease. Urate crystals encrust articular surfaces and invade the synovium, which becomes hyperplastic and fibrotic. The resulting inflammatory pannus destroys underlying cartilage and leads to juxtaarticular bone erosions with overhanging edges — a classic radiographic finding. In severe cases, fibrous or bony ankylosis ensues with complete loss of joint function. Osteonecrosis from crystal emboli (A) is not a recognized complication. Periarticular amyloidosis (B) is associated with chronic inflammatory diseases but not specifically tophaceous gout. Secondary septic arthritis (C) is possible but not the defining complication. Femoral head avascular necrosis (E) is caused by corticosteroid use or traumatic disruption of blood supply, not gout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chronic tophaceous gout causes bone erosions with overhanging edges on X-ray — a classic radiographic finding that distinguishes chronic gout from acute gout and RA. COMLEX: chronic gout does not cause periarticular osteopenia (unlike RA), helping distinguish the two on imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Tophi contain MSU crystals identifiable as needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals under polarized light"},{"id":"sdl33_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the mechanism of calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals form in which tissue, and what pathologic change allows this crystallization to occur?","choices":{"A":"CPP crystals form within the synovial lining membrane where CPP transporter proteins concentrate inorganic pyrophosphate","B":"CPP crystals form in subchondral bone marrow where elevated calcium concentrations drive precipitation near osteoblasts","C":"CPP crystals form in hyaline cartilage and fibrocartilage, where proteoglycan degradation removes inhibitors of mineralization","D":"CPP crystals form directly in synovial fluid where elevated pyrophosphate concentrations exceed physiologic solubility","E":"CPP crystals form in periarticular bursae where alkaline pH and high calcium concentrations promote CPP precipitation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unlike monosodium urate crystals which precipitate in synovial fluid and periarticular tissues, CPP crystals preferentially form within hyaline cartilage and fibrocartilage. Proteoglycans in normal articular cartilage inhibit mineralization by chelating calcium and pyrophosphate ions. When cartilage proteoglycans are degraded (due to aging, metabolic disease, or prior joint damage), this inhibitory mechanism is lost and CPP crystals can form around chondrocytes. The crystals may then rupture into the joint space, seeding the synovial fluid and triggering inflammation. CPP crystals do not primarily form in the synovial lining (A), subchondral bone (B), synovial fluid directly (C), or bursae (D).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CPPD = crystals form IN the cartilage (vs gout where MSU precipitates in the joint fluid/tissue). This distinction in crystal formation site is a classic exam discriminator. COMLEX: chondrocalcinosis on X-ray reflects CPP crystal deposits within cartilage — pathognomonic for CPPD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — CPPD crystals appear rhomboid with weakly positive birefringence, distinguishing pseudogout from gout"},{"id":"sdl33_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the mechanism of calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Mutations in which gene have been linked to hereditary calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease by disrupting inorganic pyrophosphate transport?","choices":{"A":"HGPRT gene encoding hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase in the purine nucleotide salvage pathway","B":"UMOD gene encoding uromodulin causing renal tubular dysfunction and secondary urate crystal retention","C":"COL2A1 gene encoding type II collagen causing premature cartilage matrix degeneration and CPPD formation","D":"PRPP gene encoding phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate synthetase causing purine nucleotide overproduction","E":"ANKH gene, which encodes an inorganic pyrophosphate transporter, and mutations increase pyrophosphate in the extracellular joint space"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ANKH encodes a transmembrane pyrophosphate transporter that moves inorganic pyrophosphate from intracellular to extracellular compartments. Gain-of-function mutations in ANKH increase extracellular pyrophosphate levels around chondrocytes, promoting CPP crystal nucleation and growth within articular cartilage. The OPG gene (encoding a RANKL decoy receptor) has also been linked to some CPPD forms. HGPRT mutations (A) cause Lesch-Nyhan syndrome and gout, not CPPD. UMOD mutations (B) cause familial hyperuricemic nephropathy. COL2A1 mutations (C) cause skeletal dysplasias. PRPP synthetase superactivity (D) causes hyperuricemia and gout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ANKH mutations → increased extracellular pyrophosphate → hereditary CPPD. COMLEX: secondary CPPD occurs with hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, hypothyroidism, and previous joint damage — these metabolic conditions alter cartilage proteoglycan composition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — CPPD crystals appear rhomboid with weakly positive birefringence, distinguishing pseudogout from gout"},{"id":"sdl33_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the mechanism of calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Under compensated polarized light microscopy, which features distinguish calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals from monosodium urate crystals?","choices":{"A":"CPPD crystals are rhomboid-shaped and show weakly positive birefringence, appearing blue when oriented parallel to the compensator axis","B":"CPPD crystals are needle-shaped and show strongly negative birefringence, appearing yellow when parallel to the compensator","C":"CPPD crystals are spherical and show no detectable birefringence under standard compensated polarized light","D":"CPPD crystals are plate-shaped and show strongly positive birefringence regardless of crystal orientation to the axis","E":"CPPD crystals are monoclinic and rotate plane-polarized light identically to monosodium urate crystals in all orientations"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Under compensated polarized light: MSU crystals are needle-shaped with NEGATIVE birefringence — yellow when parallel to the compensator axis. CPPD crystals are rhomboid (short, brick-like) with WEAKLY POSITIVE birefringence — blue when parallel to the compensator axis. The shape and birefringence sign are the two key discriminators. \"Negative birefringence, yellow parallel\" for MSU can be remembered as NYPD (Negative, Yellow, Parallel → gout). CPPD are Blue when Parallel (BP). Needle-shaped negative birefringence (B) describes MSU, not CPPD. Spherical with no birefringence (C) does not describe either crystal type. Plate-shaped strongly positive birefringence (D) is incorrect — CPPD is weakly, not strongly, positive. Identical optical behavior to MSU (E) is incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MSU = needle-shaped, NEGATIVE birefringence, YELLOW when parallel. CPPD = rhomboid, WEAKLY POSITIVE birefringence, BLUE when parallel. This is nearly always tested on boards. COMLEX: know that compensated polarized light is needed — plain polarized light alone cannot distinguish the two.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — CPPD crystals appear rhomboid with weakly positive birefringence, distinguishing pseudogout from gout"},{"id":"sdl33_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the mechanism of gout arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old obese man with hypertension is awakened from sleep at 3 AM with excruciating pain and swelling of his right great toe. He reports a similar episode two years ago that resolved spontaneously after one week. Serum uric acid is 9.4 mg/dL. Arthrocentesis reveals needle-shaped crystals with negative birefringence under polarized light. Which sequence most accurately describes the events that led to this acute attack?","choices":{"A":"A recent Chlamydia trachomatis urogenital infection triggered reactive arthritis via molecular mimicry of joint antigens","B":"Autoimmune B cell activation produced IgM rheumatoid factor causing immune complex deposition in the first MTP joint","C":"CPPD crystals formed within the fibrocartilage of the first MTP joint seeding the joint space during sleep and nocturnal cooling","D":"Chronic hyperuricemia exceeded the MSU saturation threshold, causing crystal precipitation in the cooler first MTP joint and triggering innate immune activation","E":"Hematogenous spread of Neisseria gonorrhoeae seeded the first MTP joint causing suppurative monoarthritis with crystal-like debris"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is a classic gout presentation: middle-aged obese hypertensive man, nocturnal onset, first MTP joint, recurrent episodes, negative birefringent needle crystals. Chronic hyperuricemia (serum uric acid above 6.8 mg/dL) causes MSU to crystallize preferentially in cooler peripheral joints, particularly the first MTP. The NLRP3 inflammasome is activated by phagocytosed crystals, producing IL-1beta and driving the acute attack. Reactive arthritis following Chlamydia (A) does not produce intracellular negative birefringent crystals. RF-mediated immune complexes (B) are RA pathology. CPPD crystals (C) are rhomboid with positive birefringence. Gonococcal arthritis (D) typically affects the knee and is confirmed by culture, not crystal microscopy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic gout vignette — obese, hypertensive, nocturnal first MTP attack, negative birefringent needles. The crystal finding is diagnostic and distinguishes gout from all other causes. COMLEX: alcohol intake, red meat, and thiazide diuretics are the classic precipitants of acute attacks.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the mechanism of gout arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old man is found to have a serum uric acid of 8.2 mg/dL on routine blood work. He has no joint pain, no tophi, and no history of kidney stones. His physician explains that only approximately 10% of hyperuricemic individuals ever develop clinical gout. Which additional factors would most increase this patient's individual risk of progression to symptomatic gout?","choices":{"A":"Seronegative status for rheumatoid factor and anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies in the absence of ANA","B":"Elevated ESR, CRP, and ferritin consistent with systemic inflammation that predisposes to crystal precipitation","C":"Prolonged duration of hyperuricemia, genetic polymorphisms in urate transport genes, obesity, alcohol use, and thiazide diuretic therapy","D":"Positive HLA-B27 allele combined with inflammatory back pain suggesting an underlying spondyloarthropathy","E":"Recent streptococcal pharyngitis producing anti-streptolysin O antibodies that cross-react with synovial antigen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Although hyperuricemia is necessary for gout, only about 10% of hyperuricemic individuals develop clinical disease. Risk factors that increase progression include prolonged duration of hyperuricemia (gout typically appears after 20–30 years of elevated uric acid), genetic predisposition through polymorphisms in URAT1, GLUT9, and KCNQ1, obesity, alcohol consumption (especially beer, which contains guanosine), and thiazide diuretics (which reduce renal urate excretion). RF and ACPA (A) are markers of RA, not gout risk. ESR, CRP, and ferritin elevation (B) are nonspecific inflammatory markers unrelated to gout precipitation risk. HLA-B27 (C) is the spondyloarthropathy risk allele, not a gout risk factor. Anti-streptolysin O antibodies (E) are markers of prior streptococcal infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gout risk factors — duration of hyperuricemia, obesity, alcohol (especially beer), purine-rich diet, thiazides, CsA, low-dose aspirin. COMLEX: age is also a factor; gout typically appears after 20–30 years of asymptomatic hyperuricemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the mechanism of gout arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with stage 4 chronic kidney disease presents with her first episode of acute monoarthritis of the right knee. Serum uric acid is 11.0 mg/dL. She takes no uricosuric medications. Which mechanism most directly explains her hyperuricemia and secondary gout?","choices":{"A":"Reduced glomerular filtration and decreased proximal tubular secretion of urate due to chronic renal disease leads to urate retention","B":"Increased de novo purine synthesis from loss of negative feedback due to absent glomerular filtration of uric acid","C":"Upregulation of HGPRT activity in response to renal insufficiency drives increased uric acid production from purine salvage","D":"Autoimmune destruction of renal tubular cells causes URAT1 transporter upregulation increasing tubular urate reabsorption","E":"Accumulation of uremic toxins activates xanthine oxidase in peripheral tissues increasing end-stage uric acid production"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In chronic renal disease, both glomerular filtration of uric acid and tubular secretion are reduced, leading to uric acid retention and hyperuricemia — the most common cause of secondary gout. Normal renal handling of urate involves: glomerular filtration, nearly complete proximal tubular reabsorption, partial secretion by the distal nephron, and final net excretion of about 10% of filtered load. In CKD, this secretory capacity is markedly reduced. Increased de novo purine synthesis (B) is not the mechanism in CKD. HGPRT upregulation (C) does not occur in renal failure. Autoimmune tubular destruction (D) describes a different entity. Uremic toxin activation of xanthine oxidase (E) is not the established mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CKD is a classic cause of secondary gout — reduced urate excretion leads to accumulation. Other secondary causes: thiazides, cyclosporine, lead nephropathy. COMLEX: febuxostat is preferred over allopurinol in CKD because it is hepatically metabolized.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the metabolic pathways which produce purine excess.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy presents with severe hyperuricemia, gout, renal calculi, choreoathetosis, spasticity, intellectual disability, and compulsive self-mutilating behavior. His mother states that he frequently bites his fingers and lips. Laboratory studies reveal undetectable HGPRT activity. Which metabolic consequence most directly explains his hyperuricemia?","choices":{"A":"HGPRT deficiency increases de novo purine synthesis by removing product feedback inhibition on PRPP amidotransferase","B":"Absent HGPRT causes accumulation of PRPP, which is converted by alternative enzymes directly to uric acid without forming purines","C":"Absence of HGPRT prevents purine base recycling via the salvage pathway, forcing all free hypoxanthine and guanine into oxidative degradation to uric acid","D":"HGPRT deficiency impairs AMP synthesis, diverting all adenosine through adenosine deaminase to inosine and then to uric acid","E":"Loss of HGPRT destabilizes the purine ring structure of existing nucleotides, accelerating spontaneous degradation to uric acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HGPRT catalyzes the salvage pathway: hypoxanthine + PRPP → IMP, and guanine + PRPP → GMP. Without HGPRT, free hypoxanthine and guanine generated from nucleotide turnover cannot be recycled and are instead catabolized: hypoxanthine → xanthine → uric acid by xanthine oxidase. This massive increase in uric acid production causes severe hyperuricemia and gout. In the severe (Lesch-Nyhan) form, neurologic manifestations including self-mutilation dominate. HGPRT deficiency does increase de novo synthesis somewhat (A) by raising PRPP levels due to reduced salvage consumption of PRPP — this is a secondary contributing factor but not the primary mechanism. PRPP does not convert directly to uric acid (B). HGPRT deficiency does not selectively impair AMP synthesis (C). Nucleotide ring destabilization (D) is not a mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lesch-Nyhan syndrome = HGPRT deficiency + X-linked recessive + hyperuricemia + self-mutilation + choreoathetosis. Partial HGPRT deficiency = gout without neurologic features (Kelley-Seegmiller syndrome). COMLEX: HGPRT is the key salvage pathway enzyme; its absence forces purines into degradation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl33_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the metabolic pathways which produce purine excess.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with chronic tophaceous gout is started on allopurinol. After 6 weeks his serum uric acid falls from 10.8 to 5.4 mg/dL. Which enzymatic step does allopurinol inhibit to achieve this reduction in uric acid production?","choices":{"A":"HGPRT, which catalyzes the salvage of hypoxanthine and guanine back to IMP and GMP respectively using PRPP as cofactor","B":"Xanthine oxidase, which normally catalyzes the conversion of hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid in the purine degradation pathway","C":"PRPP synthetase, which catalyzes the rate-limiting step of de novo purine synthesis by generating PRPP from ribose-5-phosphate","D":"Adenosine deaminase, which catalyzes the deamination of adenosine to inosine in the purine degradation cascade","E":"Purine nucleoside phosphorylase, which catalyzes the phosphorylation of hypoxanthine and guanine nucleosides"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Allopurinol is a purine analogue that is a suicide inhibitor of xanthine oxidase — the enzyme that catalyzes the final two oxidation steps in uric acid production: hypoxanthine → xanthine, and xanthine → uric acid. Allopurinol is converted by xanthine oxidase to oxypurinol (alloxanthine), which binds tightly and irreversibly to the enzyme. The result is buildup of the more soluble substrates hypoxanthine and xanthine and a marked decrease in uric acid production. HGPRT (A) is the salvage enzyme; inhibiting it would increase uric acid, opposite to the desired effect. PRPP synthetase (B) is not inhibited by allopurinol; febuxostat selectively inhibits xanthine oxidase. ADA (C) and purine nucleoside phosphorylase (D) are upstream enzymes not targeted by allopurinol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Allopurinol = xanthine oxidase inhibitor = reduces uric acid production. Febuxostat is a non-purine xanthine oxidase inhibitor. Both cause buildup of the more soluble xanthine and hypoxanthine. COMLEX: allopurinol can trigger acute gout flares when starting therapy due to crystal mobilization — co-prescribe colchicine or NSAIDs prophylactically.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl33_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the metabolic pathways which produce purine excess.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with hypertension and stage 2 CKD develops his first acute gout attack one month after his antihypertensive regimen is changed to include hydrochlorothiazide. Which mechanism most directly explains the new-onset gout?","choices":{"A":"Thiazide diuretics reduce uric acid secretion in the proximal tubule and increase reabsorption, raising serum uric acid levels","B":"Thiazide diuretics increase hepatic xanthine oxidase activity, accelerating purine catabolism and uric acid production","C":"Volume depletion from thiazide diuresis concentrates serum urate, raising effective MSU concentration above solubility threshold","D":"Thiazide-induced hypokalemia impairs proximal tubular Na-K-ATPase, reducing the electrochemical gradient for urate secretion","E":"Thiazide-induced metabolic alkalosis shifts the uric acid equilibrium toward MSU crystal precipitation in acidic synovial fluid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Thiazide diuretics are a classic precipitant of gout because they compete with uric acid for secretion in the proximal tubule via the OAT1/OAT3 transporters and also enhance proximal tubular urate reabsorption via URAT1 — both effects reduce urate excretion and raise serum levels. This is why thiazides are relatively contraindicated in patients with gout. Thiazides do not increase hepatic xanthine oxidase activity (B). While volume depletion (C) does concentrate serum solutes, this is a minor secondary contributor compared to the direct tubular transport effect. Hypokalemia and Na-K-ATPase impairment (D) are not the established mechanism. Thiazides cause metabolic alkalosis (E), not acidosis, making this option mechanistically incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thiazides, low-dose aspirin, cyclosporine, and loop diuretics all raise uric acid by reducing renal excretion — classic precipitants of secondary gout. COMLEX: when managing hypertension in a gout patient, avoid thiazides and prefer amlodipine or losartan (which is mildly uricosuric).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl33_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanism of inflammation occurring with acute gout arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old man presents to the ED with a hot, swollen right knee that began 18 hours ago. Arthrocentesis yields turbid fluid with 48,000 WBC per microliter and 90% neutrophils. Polarized light microscopy reveals needle-shaped negatively birefringent crystals. A researcher studying this patient's synovial fluid finds markedly elevated IL-1beta. Which molecular pathway most directly generated this IL-1beta?","choices":{"A":"T follicular helper cells in the synovium stimulated plasma cells to produce IL-1beta through B cell receptor cross-linking","B":"Synovial fibroblasts expressed Toll-like receptor 4 in response to MSU opsonization, triggering MyD88-NF-kB-IL-1beta transcription","C":"MSU crystals phagocytosed by synovial macrophages activated the NLRP3 inflammasome, which cleaved pro-IL-1beta via caspase-1","D":"Natural killer cells recognized MSU crystals as danger signals and secreted pre-formed IL-1beta stored in cytoplasmic granules","E":"Mast cells degranulated in response to IgE antibodies targeting MSU crystal surfaces, releasing IL-1beta from granule stores"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MSU crystals are phagocytosed by synovial macrophages, where they activate the NLRP3 (NOD-like receptor pyrin domain-containing 3) inflammasome. Crystal-induced phagolysosomal rupture with cathepsin B release provides the activating signal for NLRP3 oligomerization. The assembled inflammasome complex activates caspase-1, which cleaves pro-IL-1beta into the mature, secreted form. The high WBC with neutrophil predominance and negative birefringent needles confirm acute gout. T follicular helper cells and plasma cells (A) do not produce IL-1beta through B cell receptor signaling. Synovial fibroblasts with TLR4 (B) can produce cytokines but IL-1beta maturation specifically requires inflammasome caspase-1 cleavage. NK cells (C) do not secrete pre-formed IL-1beta. Mast cell IgE degranulation (D) is a Type I hypersensitivity mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NLRP3 inflammasome → caspase-1 → IL-1beta is the gold-standard mechanism of acute gouty inflammation. This pathway is now targeted therapeutically with IL-1 inhibitors (anakinra, canakinumab) for refractory gout. COMLEX: colchicine prevents neutrophil chemotaxis and NLRP3 activation, explaining its efficacy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cells of the innate immune response"},{"id":"sdl33_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanism of inflammation occurring with acute gout arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with his second acute gout attack is treated with colchicine within 12 hours of symptom onset. His pain and swelling resolve within 48 hours. Which mechanism best explains colchicine's anti-inflammatory effect in acute gout?","choices":{"A":"Colchicine inhibits xanthine oxidase activity, reducing uric acid production and lowering the MSU crystal burden in the joint","B":"Colchicine binds tubulin and prevents microtubule polymerization, inhibiting neutrophil chemotaxis and migration into the inflamed joint","C":"Colchicine blocks the IL-1 receptor on synoviocytes, preventing IL-1beta signaling and downstream neutrophil recruitment","D":"Colchicine inhibits COX-1 and COX-2, reducing prostaglandin synthesis and limiting vascular permeability in the joint","E":"Colchicine activates regulatory T cells in the synovial membrane, suppressing innate immune activation by crystal phagocytosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Colchicine binds to the tubulin dimer and prevents its assembly into microtubules. Neutrophils depend on microtubule polymerization for cytoskeletal dynamics including pseudopod formation, chemotaxis, and phagocytosis. By disrupting these functions, colchicine prevents neutrophils from migrating to the joint in response to IL-1beta and other chemoattractants. Colchicine also inhibits NLRP3 inflammasome assembly and IL-1beta secretion at higher concentrations. Colchicine does NOT inhibit xanthine oxidase (A) — that is allopurinol and febuxostat. Colchicine does not block the IL-1 receptor (B) — that is anakinra. Colchicine does not inhibit COX (C) — that is NSAIDs. Colchicine does not activate Tregs (D).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colchicine = microtubule inhibitor = blocks neutrophil chemotaxis. It does not lower uric acid. COMLEX: colchicine is most effective when taken within 24 hours of attack onset; it is also used prophylactically when initiating urate-lowering therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cells of the innate immune response"},{"id":"sdl33_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanism of inflammation occurring with acute gout arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with untreated gout has experienced four acute attacks over the past two years, each more severe and longer-lasting than the previous one. He is now developing a chronic, persistently painful arthritis between attacks. Which pathologic mechanism best explains the transition from episodic acute arthritis to chronic persistent joint inflammation?","choices":{"A":"Autoantibodies against citrullinated urate crystal proteins develop over time, converting gout into an autoimmune synovitis","B":"Progressive joint destruction allows Staphylococcus aureus colonization of the damaged synovium causing low-grade infection","C":"Repetitive crystal attacks exhaust synovial macrophage populations, causing unopposed neutrophil-driven inflammatory cascades","D":"Long-standing hyperuricemia causes urate deposition in subchondral bone marrow, displacing hematopoietic cells with crystal debris","E":"Repeated crystal deposition triggers recurring NLRP3 inflammasome cycles, and progressive urate crystal accumulation in synovium sustains chronic low-grade inflammation between attacks"},"correct":"E","explanation":"With untreated hyperuricemia, MSU crystals accumulate progressively in synovium, cartilage, and periarticular tissues. Repeated acute attacks deposit more urate, and between attacks the persistent crystal burden triggers chronic low-grade NLRP3 inflammasome activation. The result is chronic tophaceous arthritis with persistent synovial inflammation, pannus formation, cartilage erosion, and eventual joint destruction. Acute attacks become more frequent and polyarticular. Autoantibodies against citrullinated urate proteins (A) do not develop in gout — this would convert the disease to RA pathology, which does not occur. Staphylococcal colonization (B) is not a recognized complication. Macrophage exhaustion (C) is not an established mechanism. Urate displacement of bone marrow hematopoiesis (D) is not a gout complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The four clinical stages of gout are: asymptomatic hyperuricemia → acute gouty arthritis → intercritical period → chronic tophaceous gout. Untreated, attacks recur at shorter intervals and become polyarticular. COMLEX: chronic tophaceous gout requires long-term urate-lowering therapy to dissolve existing tophi and prevent further crystal accumulation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cells of the innate immune response"},{"id":"sdl33_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the composition of tophi and typical distribution of these lesions.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with a 15-year history of gout presents for a routine rheumatology visit. On examination, you note firm, whitish nodules on his right ear helix, a nodular swelling over the olecranon bursa, and thickened nodules along the Achilles tendon bilaterally. Serum uric acid is 10.1 mg/dL. Aspiration of an ear nodule reveals chalky white material. Microscopy of this material would most likely reveal which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Rhomboid-shaped calcium pyrophosphate crystals surrounded by fibrous capsule with no giant cell response present","B":"Needle-shaped monosodium urate crystals surrounded by multinucleated foreign-body giant cells and chronic inflammatory cells","C":"Hydroxyapatite crystal deposits embedded in calcified fibrous tissue without surrounding inflammatory cell infiltrate","D":"Amyloid fibrils in a beta-pleated sheet configuration with Congo red staining and apple-green birefringence","E":"Needle-shaped cholesterol crystals from hyperlipidemia-related xanthoma formation in periarticular tissue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tophi from chronic gout contain aggregates of MSU (monosodium urate) crystals — needle-shaped under polarized light — surrounded by a vigorous foreign-body giant cell reaction and mononuclear inflammatory cells including fibroblasts, macrophages, and lymphocytes. The chalky white material is dissolved urate seen macroscopically. On histology, the characteristic multinucleated giant cells attempting to engulf the crystals are pathognomonic. CPPD rhomboid crystals (A) cause CPPD disease, not tophi. Hydroxyapatite (B) causes calcific tendinitis with minimal giant cell response. Amyloid fibrils with Congo red birefringence (C) are diagnostic of amyloidosis, not gout. Cholesterol cleft crystals (D) are found in xanthomas and atherosclerotic plaques, not tophi.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tophus histology = MSU crystals + foreign-body giant cells. Tophi on the ear helix, olecranon, and Achilles are pathognomonic for gout. COMLEX: clinical recognition of tophi on exam is the highest-yield physical finding in gout — soft, chalky, whitish, painless nodules at characteristic sites.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the composition of tophi and typical distribution of these lesions.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with longstanding severe tophaceous gout presents with flank pain and hematuria. Urinalysis shows an acidic pH with amorphous urate crystals and no casts. Renal ultrasound reveals bilateral echogenic foci in the medullary pyramids. Which best describes the renal complication of chronic gout in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Membranous nephropathy from urate immune complex deposition in glomerular basement membrane causing proteinuria","B":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis from ANCA-associated vasculitis triggered by chronic hyperuricemia","C":"Renal papillary necrosis from analgesic nephropathy caused by chronic NSAID use for repeated gout attacks","D":"Light chain cast nephropathy from plasma cell dyscrasia associated with chronic gouty inflammatory stimulus","E":"Urate nephropathy from MSU crystal deposition in the renal medullary interstitium and tubules, causing nephrolithiasis and interstitial nephritis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Urate nephropathy refers to renal damage caused by MSU crystal deposition in the renal medullary interstitium and collecting tubules. This produces interstitial nephritis, tubular obstruction, and uric acid kidney stones (nephrolithiasis). The acidic urine pH in this patient promotes uric acid crystallization in the kidney (pKa of uric acid is 5.35). The echogenic foci in the medullary pyramids represent urate crystal deposits. This is a recognized complication of chronic severe gout. Membranous nephropathy (A) and ANCA vasculitis (B) are not gout complications. Renal papillary necrosis from NSAID overuse (C) is possible in gout patients but is not caused by urate crystals directly. Light chain cast nephropathy (D) is a plasma cell dyscrasia complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gout causes urate nephropathy — crystals in the medullary interstitium cause interstitial nephritis and nephrolithiasis. Uric acid stones are radiolucent on plain X-ray. COMLEX: alkalinization of urine with potassium citrate or sodium bicarbonate prevents uric acid stone recurrence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the composition of tophi and typical distribution of these lesions.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pathologist examines an excised soft tissue nodule from the olecranon of a 70-year-old man with longstanding gout. Histology reveals amorphous deposits surrounded by large multinucleated cells with abundant pale cytoplasm arranged radially around the deposits. Under polarized light, the material within the nodule shows needle-shaped negative birefringent crystals. What is the correct pathologic description of the cellular reaction surrounding these deposits?","choices":{"A":"Caseating granuloma with central necrosis, peripheral epithelioid macrophages, and Langhans-type giant cells from mycobacterial infection","B":"Non-caseating granuloma with tight clusters of epithelioid macrophages and CD4 T cells consistent with sarcoidosis","C":"Palisading neutrophilic granuloma with central fibrin deposits and nuclear dust consistent with rheumatoid nodule pathology","D":"Foreign-body granulomatous reaction with multinucleated giant cells attempting to phagocytose the insoluble MSU crystal deposits","E":"Suppurative granuloma with central abscess formation and surrounding neutrophilic infiltrate consistent with bacterial infection"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gouty tophi elicit a classic foreign-body granulomatous reaction — MSU crystals are too large to be fully phagocytosed by a single macrophage, so multiple macrophages fuse to form multinucleated foreign-body giant cells that attempt to engulf the crystals. These giant cells surround the crystal aggregates and are accompanied by fibroblasts and chronic inflammatory cells. This is a non-caseating, non-infectious foreign-body reaction. Caseating granulomas with Langhans-type giant cells (A) are characteristic of mycobacterial infection (TB) — Langhans giants have peripheral nuclei. Non-caseating granulomas with tight epithelioid clusters (B) are the hallmark of sarcoidosis. Palisading neutrophilic granuloma with fibrin (C) describes rheumatoid nodule histology. Suppurative granulomas (D) indicate bacterial infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tophi = foreign-body giant cell reaction around MSU crystals. Distinguish from rheumatoid nodules (palisading histiocytes around fibrinoid necrosis) — both occur at the olecranon. COMLEX: the negative birefringent needle crystals under polarized light confirm gout, while rheumatoid nodules do not contain crystals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the mechanism of calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman presents with acute onset of knee pain and effusion. Arthrocentesis yields mildly turbid fluid with 12,000 WBC per microliter. Polarized light microscopy reveals short, rhomboid-shaped crystals with weak positive birefringence. Plain knee radiograph shows calcification within the menisci and articular cartilage. Which mechanism best explains the origin of these crystals and the radiographic findings in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Monosodium urate crystals precipitate from synovial fluid in the knee joint as the local temperature decreases during sleep","B":"Hydroxyapatite crystals deposit in the periarticular soft tissues from dystrophic calcification of necrotic chondrocytes","C":"Calcium oxalate crystals form in the joint cartilage in patients with renal failure from oxalate retention and supersaturation","D":"Basic calcium phosphate crystals deposit in the joint lining membrane from hypercalcemia of primary hyperparathyroidism","E":"CPP crystals form within hyaline cartilage and fibrocartilage as proteoglycan degradation removes inhibitors of mineralization, producing chondrocalcinosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This is a classic CPPD (pseudogout) presentation: elderly woman, acute knee effusion, rhomboid crystals with weakly positive birefringence, and chondrocalcinosis on radiograph. CPP crystals form within articular cartilage and fibrocartilage (not synovial fluid) when proteoglycan degradation removes the normal inhibitors of mineralization around chondrocytes. The crystals enlarge and may rupture into the joint space, seeding synovial fluid and triggering inflammasome-mediated inflammation. Radiographic chondrocalcinosis — calcification visible within the cartilage — is a hallmark finding. MSU crystals (A) are needle-shaped with negative birefringence and do not cause chondrocalcinosis. Hydroxyapatite (B) causes calcific tendinitis. Calcium oxalate (C) occurs in severe renal failure. Basic calcium phosphate (D) is associated with Milwaukee shoulder, not the knee with chondrocalcinosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CPPD = rhomboid crystals + weakly positive birefringence + chondrocalcinosis on X-ray + elderly patient + knee most common. COMLEX: age over 50 is the strongest risk factor for CPPD; secondary causes include hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, and hypothyroidism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the mechanism of calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher compares the inflammatory pathogenesis of CPPD with acute gout. Despite the different crystal compositions, both diseases produce similar acute inflammatory responses. Which molecular mechanism is shared between MSU and CPP crystal-induced joint inflammation?","choices":{"A":"Both MSU and CPP crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome in synovial macrophages, generating IL-1beta via caspase-1 cleavage","B":"Both crystal types stimulate mast cell degranulation through IgE-mediated crosslinking of crystal-specific surface antibodies","C":"Both crystal types activate the classical complement pathway via C1q recognition of crystal-associated IgG complexes","D":"Both crystal types induce Th17 cell differentiation in regional lymph nodes, providing memory-based recurrent inflammation","E":"Both crystal types trigger bradykinin release through contact activation of the intrinsic coagulation pathway in synovial fluid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Despite their different chemical compositions, both MSU and CPP crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome in phagocytic cells. Crystal phagocytosis causes phagolysosomal rupture with cathepsin B release, which activates NLRP3 oligomerization. The assembled complex activates caspase-1, generating IL-1beta, which drives neutrophil recruitment and acute arthritis. This shared pathway explains why both diseases respond similarly to colchicine, NSAIDs, corticosteroids, and IL-1 inhibitors. Neither crystal type triggers mast cell IgE-mediated degranulation (A). Classical complement via C1q-IgG (B) is the mechanism of RA and SLE. Th17-mediated memory inflammation (C) is the mechanism of autoimmune arthritis. Contact activation-bradykinin (D) is not established for crystal-induced joint inflammation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Both gout (MSU) and pseudogout (CPPD) share the NLRP3 inflammasome-IL-1beta pathway — this is why both respond to colchicine and IL-1 inhibitors. COMLEX: the shared pathway explains why the two diseases are clinically difficult to distinguish without crystal analysis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the mechanism of calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with known hereditary hemochromatosis presents with acute wrist pain and swelling. Arthrocentesis reveals rhomboid crystals with positive birefringence. Plain radiograph shows calcification in the triangular fibrocartilage of the wrist. He is diagnosed with secondary CPPD. Which mechanism best explains why hemochromatosis predisposes to calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition?","choices":{"A":"Iron deposits displace calcium from hydroxyapatite in subchondral bone, releasing free calcium that combines with pyrophosphate","B":"Excess iron catalyzes oxidative damage to MSU crystals, converting their shape from needle to rhomboid and reversing birefringence","C":"Iron deposition in synovial lining cells stimulates RANKL expression, driving osteoclast activation and calcium release from cartilage","D":"Iron deposition in chondrocytes inhibits pyrophosphatase enzymes, causing inorganic pyrophosphate to accumulate and precipitate with calcium as CPP crystals","E":"Ferritin released from hemochromatotic chondrocytes directly nucleates CPP crystal formation by providing a mineralization scaffold"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hemochromatosis causes iron deposition in multiple tissues including articular chondrocytes. Within chondrocytes, excess iron inhibits inorganic pyrophosphatase (the enzyme that breaks down inorganic pyrophosphate to orthophosphate), leading to accumulation of inorganic pyrophosphate. This accumulated pyrophosphate combines with calcium to form CPP crystals within the cartilage. Hemochromatosis-associated CPPD classically affects the wrists and metacarpophalangeal joints — a distribution that should prompt iron studies. Iron displacing calcium from subchondral bone (A) is not the established mechanism. Iron does not convert MSU crystals (B) — the crystal types are chemically distinct. Iron-RANKL-osteoclast activation (C) is not the CPPD mechanism. Ferritin as a nucleation scaffold (D) has not been established as the primary mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary CPPD causes include hemochromatosis, hyperparathyroidism, hypomagnesemia, hypothyroidism, and ochronosis. The mechanism in hemochromatosis is pyrophosphatase inhibition by iron → PPi accumulation → CPP crystallization. COMLEX: hemochromatosis-associated CPPD at the wrist and MCP joints should prompt serum ferritin and transferrin saturation testing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"}]},{"sdlNumber":35,"title":"Cytokines & Antibody Class Switching","questions":[{"id":"sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the different immune cells that produce cytokines and the specific cytokines each cell type releases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which set of cytokines is most characteristically produced by activated macrophages and dendritic cells during an innate immune response?","choices":{"A":"IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13, which are signature Th2 cytokines that promote IgE class switching and eosinophil activation","B":"IL-17 and IL-22, which are signature Th17 cytokines that recruit neutrophils to combat extracellular pathogens","C":"IFN-gamma and IL-2, which are produced by Th1 and NK cells to activate macrophages and drive T cell proliferation","D":"IL-10 and TGF-beta, which are anti-inflammatory cytokines produced by regulatory T cells to suppress immune activation","E":"IL-1, IL-6, IL-12, and TNF-alpha, which mediate fever, acute phase responses, and innate immune activation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Activated macrophages and dendritic cells are the primary innate cytokine producers. They secrete IL-1 (fever, leukocyte recruitment via inflammasome), IL-6 (acute phase response, B cell differentiation, Th17 induction), IL-12 (Th1 polarization, NK activation), and TNF-alpha (fever, cachexia, apoptosis, NF-kB activation). IL-4/IL-5/IL-13 (A) are Th2 cytokines. IL-17/IL-22 (B) are Th17 cytokines. IFN-gamma and IL-2 (C) are T cell and NK products. IL-10 and TGF-beta (D) are Treg products.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Macrophages produce IL-1, IL-6, IL-12, TNF — the innate cytokine quartet. These are the targets for biologics in RA (anti-TNF, anti-IL-6) and gout (anti-IL-1). COMLEX: sepsis is driven by massive macrophage release of TNF and IL-1 causing systemic inflammatory response syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Macrophages and NK cells of the innate immune system are major cytokine producers alongside T helper subsets"},{"id":"sdl35_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the different immune cells that produce cytokines and the specific cytokines each cell type releases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokines are produced by Th2 helper T cells, and what immune functions do they collectively promote?","choices":{"A":"IFN-gamma and IL-2, which activate macrophages and drive T cell proliferation for intracellular pathogen defense","B":"IL-17, IL-22, and IL-21, which recruit neutrophils and promote extracellular bacterial and fungal clearance","C":"IL-10 and TGF-beta, which suppress T cell and macrophage responses to maintain immune homeostasis","D":"IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13, which promote IgE class switching, eosinophil activation, and alternative macrophage activation","E":"IL-12 and IL-23, which induce Th1 and Th17 differentiation from naive CD4 T cells in lymph nodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Th2 cells produce IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 as their signature cytokines. IL-4 induces Th2 differentiation (autocrine), drives B cell class switching to IgE and IgG1, and promotes alternative macrophage activation. IL-5 is the principal eosinophil growth, activation, and survival factor, critical for anti-helminth immunity. IL-13 acts on B cells and epithelial cells similarly to IL-4 and promotes mucus secretion. Excess Th2 activity drives atopy and allergic disease. IFN-gamma/IL-2 (A) are Th1. IL-17/IL-22 (B) are Th17. IL-10/TGF-beta (C) are Treg. IL-12/IL-23 (D) are macrophage/DC products that induce Th1/Th17.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Th2 = IL-4, IL-5, IL-13 = allergy, atopy, helminths, IgE, eosinophils. Anti-IL-5 (mepolizumab) and anti-IL-4R (dupilumab) are therapeutic targets in severe asthma and atopic dermatitis. COMLEX: Th2 excess in atopic patients creates the atopic march — eczema → food allergy → asthma → allergic rhinitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Macrophages and NK cells of the innate immune system are major cytokine producers alongside T helper subsets"},{"id":"sdl35_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the different immune cells that produce cytokines and the specific cytokines each cell type releases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokine is the signature product of Th17 cells, and what is its primary role in innate-adaptive immune coordination?","choices":{"A":"IL-17, which recruits neutrophils and monocytes to mucosal barriers to combat extracellular bacteria and fungi","B":"IL-4, which drives Th2 differentiation and class switching to IgE in response to extracellular parasites and allergens","C":"IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages to destroy intracellular bacteria through classical macrophage activation","D":"IL-10, which inhibits macrophage cytokine production and downregulates Th1-mediated inflammatory responses","E":"IL-2, which serves as the primary T cell autocrine growth factor driving clonal expansion after antigen recognition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-17 is the signature cytokine of Th17 cells — it acts on epithelial cells, endothelial cells, and fibroblasts to induce chemokine production, most importantly CXCL8 (IL-8), which recruits neutrophils. Th17 cells are critical for defense against extracellular bacteria (Staphylococcus, Klebsiella) and fungi (Candida). Patients with IL-17 pathway defects (e.g., STAT3 mutations in hyper-IgE syndrome) suffer from recurrent Candida and Staphylococcal infections. IL-17 also plays a pathogenic role in RA, psoriasis, and ankylosing spondylitis. IL-4 (B) is Th2. IFN-gamma (C) is Th1 and NK. IL-10 (D) is Treg. IL-2 (E) is the T cell autocrine growth signal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Th17 = IL-17 = neutrophil recruitment = extracellular pathogens and fungi. IL-17 inhibitors (secukinumab, ixekizumab) are used for psoriasis and ankylosing spondylitis. COMLEX: Th17 vs Th1 distinction is critical — Th17 protects mucosal barriers, Th1 activates macrophages against intracellular pathogens.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Macrophages and NK cells of the innate immune system are major cytokine producers alongside T helper subsets"},{"id":"sdl35_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the primary function of IL-12, and which cell types produce and respond to this cytokine?","choices":{"A":"IL-12 is produced by Th1 cells and acts on B cells to induce class switching from IgM to IgG3 and IgG1 subclasses","B":"IL-12 is produced by Th2 cells and drives eosinophil activation and survival in response to helminthic parasites","C":"IL-12 is produced by regulatory T cells and acts on macrophages to inhibit classical macrophage activation","D":"IL-12 is produced by macrophages and DCs to drive Th1 differentiation and activate NK cells to produce IFN-gamma","E":"IL-12 is produced by mast cells and promotes IgE synthesis by B cells to sensitize mast cells for future allergen responses"},"correct":"D","explanation":"IL-12 is a key innate-to-adaptive bridge cytokine produced by macrophages and dendritic cells upon encountering intracellular pathogens and viruses. It acts on naive CD4 T cells to drive Th1 differentiation and on NK cells to stimulate IFN-gamma production and enhance killing of virus-infected cells. This is why IL-12 deficiency causes susceptibility to mycobacterial infections and Salmonella. IL-12 is not produced by Th1 cells (A) — Th1 cells secrete IFN-gamma and IL-2. IL-12 is not a Th2 or Treg or mast cell product (B, C, D).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-12 = macrophage/DC product = drives Th1 differentiation = activates NK cells. IL-12 deficiency causes Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease. COMLEX: the IL-12/IFN-gamma axis is essential for granuloma formation in TB and other intracellular infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Cytokine profiles underlie hypersensitivity classification: IL-4/IgE drives Type I, IFN-gamma drives Type IV"},{"id":"sdl35_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokine produced by Th2 cells drives B cell class switching to IgE, and what pathologic consequence results from excess production of this cytokine?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 drives class switching to IgE, and excess IL-4 production by Th2 cells promotes allergic disease and atopy","B":"IL-5 drives class switching to IgE, and elevated IL-5 levels cause systemic eosinophilia and anaphylaxis risk","C":"IL-13 drives class switching to IgE, and elevated IL-13 causes goblet cell hyperplasia and airway hyperreactivity","D":"IFN-gamma drives class switching to IgE, and elevated IFN-gamma promotes mast cell sensitization at mucosal surfaces","E":"TGF-beta drives class switching to IgE, and excess TGF-beta causes both fibrosis and allergic mast cell sensitization"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-4 is the principal inducer of class switching to IgE in B cells. It is produced by Th2 cells, mast cells, and basophils. IL-4 also acts autocrinally on Th2 cells to reinforce their differentiation and promotes B cell proliferation and differentiation. Excess IL-4 in atopic individuals drives chronic IgE production, mast cell and basophil sensitization, and the clinical manifestations of allergy. Dupilumab, which blocks the shared IL-4/IL-13 receptor chain (IL-4Ralpha), is used for severe asthma and atopic dermatitis. IL-5 (B) promotes eosinophilia but its primary class switching effect is IgA, not IgE. IL-13 (C) shares many IL-4 functions but class switching to IgE is primarily IL-4 driven. IFN-gamma (D) drives IgG3 class switching. TGF-beta (E) drives IgA, not IgE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-4 = IgE class switching = atopy and allergy. Dupilumab blocks IL-4Ralpha (shared by IL-4 and IL-13 receptors) and is a major biologic for atopic disease. COMLEX: IgE sensitizes mast cells via FcεRI; re-exposure to allergen cross-links IgE and triggers degranulation (Type I hypersensitivity).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Cytokine profiles underlie hypersensitivity classification: IL-4/IgE drives Type I, IFN-gamma drives Type IV"},{"id":"sdl35_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the primary function of IL-10, and which immune cell type is its principal producer in the context of immune regulation?","choices":{"A":"IL-10 is a pro-inflammatory cytokine produced by Th17 cells that recruits neutrophils to sites of extracellular bacterial infection","B":"IL-10 is produced by NK cells and activates macrophage killing of intracellular bacteria through classical activation pathways","C":"IL-10 is a potent anti-inflammatory cytokine produced by Tregs and macrophages that suppresses Th1 responses and limits macrophage activation","D":"IL-10 is produced by mast cells and drives isotype class switching to IgA at mucosal surfaces in response to TI antigens","E":"IL-10 is produced by dendritic cells and drives Th1 polarization of naive CD4 T cells responding to intracellular pathogens"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IL-10 is the major anti-inflammatory regulatory cytokine. It is produced primarily by Tregs, macrophages (as an autocrine feedback), Th2 cells, and B regulatory cells. IL-10 inhibits macrophage and DC function by downregulating MHC class II, co-stimulatory molecules, and pro-inflammatory cytokine production. It also suppresses Th1 responses by inhibiting IL-12 production. IL-10 knockout mice develop severe inflammatory bowel disease due to uncontrolled intestinal immune activation. Mutations in IL-10 or its receptor in humans cause very-early-onset inflammatory bowel disease. IL-10 is not pro-inflammatory or a Th17, NK, mast cell, or DC product that drives Th1 (A, B, C, D).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-10 = anti-inflammatory = Treg and macrophage product = suppresses Th1 and macrophage activation. IL-10 deficiency → autoimmune colitis. COMLEX: IL-10 and TGF-beta are the two major Treg effector cytokines maintaining peripheral tolerance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Cytokine profiles underlie hypersensitivity classification: IL-4/IgE drives Type I, IFN-gamma drives Type IV"},{"id":"sdl35_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain antibody class switching.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which two signals are required for B cells to undergo class switch recombination in a T-dependent antibody response?","choices":{"A":"CD40 on the B cell must engage CD40L on an activated T follicular helper cell, and a cytokine signal must direct the specific isotype switch","B":"B cell receptor cross-linking by multivalent antigen alone provides sufficient signal for class switching without T cell help","C":"Toll-like receptor activation on B cells by pathogen-associated molecular patterns provides the class switching signal independently","D":"IgM binding to FcRn on B cells sends a negative feedback signal that triggers recombination to IgG or IgA isotypes","E":"Complement C3d deposition on antigen lowers the B cell activation threshold and directly triggers switch recombination"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Class switch recombination requires two distinct signals: Signal 1 is the physical contact between CD40L (expressed on activated Tfh/T helper cells) and CD40 (constitutively expressed on B cells) — this is the co-stimulatory signal that licenses the B cell for switching and is absent in T-independent responses. Signal 2 is a cytokine signal that determines which isotype is selected: IL-4 → IgE/IgG4; IFN-gamma → IgG3/IgG1; TGF-beta → IgA. BCR cross-linking alone (B) does not trigger class switching — it only provides Signal 1 for initial B cell activation. TLR activation (C) can contribute to T-independent responses but does not cause class switching. FcRn is a neonatal Fc receptor for IgG transport, not a B cell class switch receptor (D). Complement C3d (E) lowers BCR activation threshold via CD21 but does not trigger class switch recombination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two signals for class switching: CD40-CD40L (contact) + cytokine (direction). Hyper-IgM syndrome — CD40L deficiency — cannot class switch → stuck with IgM. COMLEX: T-independent antigens stimulate only IgM; T-dependent protein antigens allow class switching via Tfh help.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — CD40L-CD40 interaction between activated T cells and B cells is the co-stimulatory signal required for class switching"},{"id":"sdl35_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain antibody class switching.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which enzyme mediates the DNA recombination event during class switch recombination in germinal center B cells?","choices":{"A":"RAG1 and RAG2 recombinases, which mediate V(D)J recombination of immunoglobulin variable region genes in pro-B cells","B":"Activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID), which introduces cytosine-to-uracil mutations in switch region DNA enabling recombination","C":"Terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase (TdT), which adds non-templated nucleotides to coding joints during V(D)J recombination","D":"DNA polymerase eta, which repairs AID-induced mutations during somatic hypermutation without enabling isotype switching","E":"Recombination-activating protein 23, which processes double-strand breaks in switch region DNA during isotype recombination"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AID (activation-induced cytidine deaminase, encoded by AICDA) is the key enzyme for both class switch recombination and somatic hypermutation. In class switching, AID deaminates cytosines to uracils in the switch (S) regions upstream of the CH gene segments, generating DNA lesions that are processed into double-strand breaks, allowing recombination between S regions and deletion of the intervening CH genes while retaining the same V(D)J region. AID deficiency causes Hyper-IgM syndrome type 2 — patients cannot class switch or undergo affinity maturation. RAG1/2 (A) mediate V(D)J recombination in pro/pre-B cells, not class switching. TdT (C) adds N-nucleotides during V(D)J joining. DNA polymerase eta (D) is involved in somatic hypermutation repair. Option E is fictitious.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AID = enzyme for class switch recombination AND somatic hypermutation. AID deficiency = Hyper-IgM syndrome type 2 = no class switching or affinity maturation. COMLEX: AID works on switch regions for CSR but on variable regions for somatic hypermutation — same enzyme, different genomic targets.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — CD40L-CD40 interaction between activated T cells and B cells is the co-stimulatory signal required for class switching"},{"id":"sdl35_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain antibody class switching.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokine produced by T regulatory cells drives B cell class switching to IgA, and what anatomical compartment is most dependent on this isotype?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 produced by Th2 cells drives class switching to IgA, which is critical for complement activation at mucosal surfaces","B":"IFN-gamma produced by Th1 cells drives class switching to IgA, which is responsible for opsonizing intestinal pathogens","C":"IL-5 produced by Th2 cells drives class switching to IgA, which protects mucosal barriers by neutralizing luminal antigens","D":"IL-12 produced by macrophages drives class switching to IgA, which is secreted across epithelium as polymeric dIgA","E":"TGF-beta drives class switching to IgA, which is the dominant antibody at mucosal surfaces including the gut, respiratory tract, and secretions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"TGF-beta is the primary cytokine that drives B cell class switching to IgA. It is produced by T regulatory cells and also by stromal cells in gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT) including Peyer patches. IgA is the most abundantly produced antibody overall (though its serum levels are lower than IgG) due to the enormous surface area of mucosal tissues. Secretory IgA (sIgA) is the dimeric form found in mucosal secretions — it contains a J chain and secretory component acquired during transcytosis. IL-4 (A) drives IgE and IgG4 class switching. IFN-gamma (B) drives IgG3 and IgG1. IL-5 (C) can contribute to IgA switching but TGF-beta is the primary driver. IL-12 (D) drives Th1 differentiation, not IgA class switching.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TGF-beta → IgA class switch. IgA = mucosal immunity = secretory IgA (dimer with J chain + secretory component). Selective IgA deficiency is the most common primary immunodeficiency. COMLEX: sIgA in breast milk provides passive mucosal immunity to neonates.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — CD40L-CD40 interaction between activated T cells and B cells is the co-stimulatory signal required for class switching"},{"id":"sdl35_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major antibody classes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which antibody class is the first to be produced during a primary immune response, and what structural feature allows it to be the most efficient activator of complement via the classical pathway?","choices":{"A":"IgG is produced first and its ability to cross the placenta makes it the dominant antibody in fetal circulation during infection","B":"IgA is produced first at mucosal sites and its dimeric secretory form most efficiently activates complement on mucosal pathogens","C":"IgD is produced first on naive B cells and its high avidity for antigen initiates B cell activation and complement fixation","D":"IgM is produced first and its pentameric structure provides up to 10 antigen-binding sites and multiple Fc regions that efficiently fix C1q","E":"IgE is produced first in primary responses to parasites and its high-affinity binding to FcεRI initiates complement activation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"IgM is the first antibody class secreted in a primary immune response and is the major component of T-independent responses to polysaccharide antigens. Serum IgM is a pentamer of five basic four-chain units joined by a J chain and disulfide bonds, creating a structure with 10 antigen-binding sites (high avidity) and 5 Fc regions that are very efficient at activating complement via C1q binding (requires only one IgM molecule vs. two IgG molecules). IgG (A) is produced later but in greater quantities in secondary responses. IgA (B) does not activate complement efficiently and is not the first antibody produced. IgD (C) is expressed on naive B cells as a receptor but is not secreted in significant quantities. IgE (D) does not activate complement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgM = first produced = pentamer = best complement activator = elevated in primary infection. Elevated IgM with low IgG/IgA = Hyper-IgM syndrome (CD40L deficiency or AID deficiency). COMLEX: IgM is not transported across the placenta due to its pentameric size — only IgG crosses via FcRn.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Antibody isotypes link to hypersensitivity mechanisms: IgE Type I, IgG/IgM Type II and III, T cells Type IV"},{"id":"sdl35_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major antibody classes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which antibody class is actively transported across the placenta to provide passive immunity to the neonate, and which receptor mediates this transport?","choices":{"A":"IgA is transported across the placenta via the poly-Ig receptor expressed on placental trophoblasts throughout gestation","B":"IgG is transported across the placenta via the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), providing passive immunity during the first months of life","C":"IgM is transported across the placenta via complement receptors on syncytiotrophoblasts during the third trimester","D":"IgE is selectively transported across the placenta to sensitize fetal mast cells against maternal allergens in utero","E":"IgD is transported across the placenta via a novel receptor expressed on fetal endothelium during late gestation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IgG is the only antibody class that crosses the placenta, mediated by FcRn (neonatal Fc receptor), which is expressed on syncytiotrophoblasts. FcRn binds IgG Fc at the slightly acidic endosomal pH (6.0) and releases it at physiologic pH (7.4) on the fetal side — a pH-dependent transcytosis. Maternal IgG protects the neonate until its own immune system matures. This passive immunity wanes by 6 months as maternal IgG is catabolized. IgA (A) is transported across mucosal epithelia by the poly-Ig receptor (not the placenta). IgM (B) is too large (pentamer) to cross. IgE (C) does not cross the placenta. IgD (D) does not have a recognized placental transport mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgG crosses placenta via FcRn. Maternal IgG → neonatal protection for 6 months → then physiologic hypogammaglobulinemia. COMLEX: FcRn also extends IgG half-life in serum by rescuing it from lysosomal degradation — the basis for the 21-day IgG half-life.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Antibody isotypes link to hypersensitivity mechanisms: IgE Type I, IgG/IgM Type II and III, T cells Type IV"},{"id":"sdl35_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major antibody classes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which antibody class is found in the highest concentrations in mucosal secretions such as saliva, colostrum, tears, and intestinal fluid, and what structural modification enables its secretion?","choices":{"A":"IgM, which is a pentamer secreted into mucosal fluids where it neutralizes luminal pathogens via its 10 antigen-binding sites","B":"IgG, which is the most abundant serum antibody and diffuses passively from serum into mucosal secretions via tight junctions","C":"Secretory IgA, which is a dimer stabilized by a J chain and associated with a secretory component acquired during transcytosis across mucosal epithelium","D":"IgE, which is actively transported to mucosal surfaces via FcεRI on mast cells to provide first-line neutralization of pathogens","E":"IgD, which is secreted in large amounts by submucosal plasma cells and neutralizes bacterial colonization of the airway"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Secretory IgA (sIgA) is the dominant antibody in mucosal secretions. Plasma cells in the lamina propria secrete dimeric IgA (two IgA monomers joined by a J chain). This dimer binds to the poly-Ig receptor (pIgR) on the basolateral surface of epithelial cells, is endocytosed, transcytosed, and cleaved to release sIgA with the secretory component (a fragment of pIgR) attached. The secretory component protects sIgA from proteolytic degradation in the harsh mucosal environment. IgM (A) is a pentamer present in serum and is not the dominant mucosal antibody, though it is secreted in small amounts. IgG (B) diffuses into secretions but in low concentrations. IgE (C) is on mast cell surfaces, not secreted into lumen. IgD (D) has no established role in mucosal secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sIgA = mucosal immunity = dimeric + J chain + secretory component. Selective IgA deficiency (most common primary immunodeficiency) causes recurrent sinopulmonary infections and increased susceptibility to Giardia. COMLEX: sIgA in breast milk provides passive mucosal immunity to the infant gut and respiratory tract.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Antibody isotypes link to hypersensitivity mechanisms: IgE Type I, IgG/IgM Type II and III, T cells Type IV"},{"id":"sdl35_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the different immune cells that produce cytokines and the specific cytokines each cell type releases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old college student presents with recurrent abdominal pain, diarrhea, and significant eosinophilia on CBC. Stool examination reveals Ascaris lumbricoides eggs. The student was raised in a rural area and has a personal history of eczema. Which T helper subset and cytokine profile most directly mediates the immune response against this helminthic parasite?","choices":{"A":"Th1 cells producing IFN-gamma and IL-2 activate macrophages and cytotoxic T cells to eliminate the helminthic organism","B":"Th2 cells producing IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 drive IgE class switching, mast cell sensitization, and eosinophil activation against helminths","C":"Th17 cells producing IL-17 and IL-22 recruit neutrophils and promote epithelial barrier integrity against luminal parasites","D":"Regulatory T cells producing IL-10 and TGF-beta suppress the inflammatory response against the helminth to prevent tissue damage","E":"NK cells producing IFN-gamma and cytolytic granules directly kill helminth-infected intestinal epithelial cells via ADCC"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anti-helminth immunity is prototypically Th2-mediated. Epithelial cells damaged by helminths release IL-25 (IL-17E), IL-33, and TSLP, which activate innate lymphoid cells type 2 (ILC2s) and promote Th2 differentiation. Th2-derived IL-4 drives IgE class switching (facilitating mast cell sensitization), IL-5 activates and expands eosinophils (which are key effectors against helminths), and IL-13 promotes smooth muscle contraction and goblet cell hyperplasia to facilitate expulsion. This also explains why helminth infections are associated with elevated IgE and eosinophilia. Th1 cytokines (A) are not effective against helminths. Th17 neutrophil recruitment (B) is primarily for bacteria and fungi. Tregs (C) may be activated by helminths to modulate immunity but do not mediate killing. NK cell ADCC (D) targets virus-infected cells, not helminths.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Helminths = Th2 = IL-4 (IgE) + IL-5 (eosinophils) + IL-13. Elevated IgE + eosinophilia = classic helminth infection pattern. COMLEX: the same Th2 cytokine profile drives atopic disease — this is why individuals with heavy parasite burdens paradoxically have lower rates of atopy (hygiene hypothesis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cells of the innate immune response"},{"id":"sdl35_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the different immune cells that produce cytokines and the specific cytokines each cell type releases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex infection is found to have a genetic deficiency affecting her capacity to produce IFN-gamma. A researcher investigates which cell types normally produce this cytokine and finds two primary sources. Which cells are the primary producers of IFN-gamma in this patient's immune system?","choices":{"A":"Mast cells during early innate responses and Th2 CD4 T cells during adaptive responses secrete IFN-gamma at inflamed sites","B":"Macrophages during classical activation and Th17 CD4 T cells during fungal infections are the primary IFN-gamma sources","C":"NK cells during innate responses and Th1 CD4 T cells during adaptive responses are the primary IFN-gamma producers","D":"Dendritic cells during antigen presentation and regulatory T cells during immune suppression secrete IFN-gamma at lymph nodes","E":"Neutrophils during early bacterial infection and Th2 CD4 T cells during helminthic responses produce large amounts of IFN-gamma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IFN-gamma has two primary cellular sources: NK cells during innate immune responses (stimulated by IL-12 and IL-18 from macrophages) and Th1 CD4 T cells during adaptive responses (differentiated by IL-12 from DCs). IFN-gamma is the key macrophage-activating cytokine — it induces classical activation, upregulates MHC class I and II, enhances antigen presentation, and drives production of reactive oxygen species and nitrogen species for intracellular pathogen killing. Its deficiency causes susceptibility to mycobacteria, Salmonella, Histoplasma, and other intracellular pathogens. Mast cells and Th2 cells (A) do not produce IFN-gamma — they produce Th2 cytokines. Th17 cells (B) produce IL-17, not IFN-gamma. Tregs (C) produce IL-10 and TGF-beta. Th2 cells (D) never produce IFN-gamma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IFN-gamma sources = NK cells (innate, stimulated by IL-12) + Th1 cells (adaptive). IFN-gamma deficiency = susceptibility to mycobacteria and intracellular pathogens. COMLEX: IL-12/IFN-gamma axis is the central pathway for granuloma formation — both are required for TB containment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cells of the innate immune response"},{"id":"sdl35_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the different immune cells that produce cytokines and the specific cytokines each cell type releases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy presents with recurrent infections with Salmonella typhi and Mycobacterium bovis following BCG vaccination. His parents are consanguineous. Immunologic workup reveals normal lymphocyte counts and normal B cell function, but impaired macrophage killing of intracellular bacteria. Genetic testing reveals a homozygous loss-of-function mutation in the IL-12 receptor beta-1 chain. Which downstream immune defect most directly explains his susceptibility to intracellular pathogens?","choices":{"A":"Failure of IL-12 signaling prevents Th1 differentiation and NK cell IFN-gamma production, leaving macrophages unable to kill intracellular bacteria","B":"Loss of IL-12 receptor signaling activates STAT6 instead, diverting T helper cells into Th2 differentiation and promoting IgE production","C":"Absent IL-12 receptor prevents T cell migration to lymph nodes, blocking antigen presentation and naive T cell activation entirely","D":"Defective IL-12 signaling reduces CXCL8 production, preventing neutrophil recruitment to sites of mycobacterial infection","E":"Loss of IL-12 receptor signaling impairs B cell somatic hypermutation, producing low-affinity antibodies against intracellular antigens"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-12 receptor deficiency is the most common cause of Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease (MSMD). Without IL-12 signaling, naive CD4 T cells cannot differentiate into Th1 cells, and NK cells cannot be activated to produce IFN-gamma. Without IFN-gamma, macrophages remain in a resting state and cannot mount effective killing of intracellular bacteria — they cannot upregulate iNOS, reactive oxygen species, or lysosomal enzymes needed to destroy mycobacteria. IL-12 receptor deficiency does not activate STAT6 (A) — STAT6 is downstream of IL-4/IL-13 receptors. IL-12 signaling is not required for T cell migration to lymph nodes (B). CXCL8 production and neutrophil recruitment (C) are independent of IL-12. B cell somatic hypermutation (D) depends on AID and Tfh help, not IL-12.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-12 receptor deficiency = Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease = recurrent non-tuberculous mycobacteria and Salmonella. The IL-12/IFN-gamma axis is essential for macrophage activation. COMLEX: this same axis is disrupted by anti-TNF therapy, which is why TB screening before biologics is mandatory.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cells of the innate immune response"},{"id":"sdl35_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman is admitted to the ICU with septic shock from Gram-negative bacteremia. Her temperature is 39.8 degrees Celsius, blood pressure is 78/42 mmHg, and she is in multiorgan failure. Which cytokines most directly mediate the fever, hypotension, and vascular leak seen in this patient?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 and IL-13, released by activated Th2 cells, cause vascular smooth muscle relaxation and widespread tissue edema","B":"IFN-gamma and IL-2, released by NK and Th1 cells, cause macrophage hyperactivation and secondary cytokine storm in sepsis","C":"TNF-alpha and IL-1, released by macrophages stimulated by LPS, cause fever via PGE2 induction, vasodilation, and increased vascular permeability","D":"IL-10 and TGF-beta, released by Tregs, paradoxically induce fever and vascular leak through prostaglandin-independent pathways","E":"IL-17 and IL-22, released by Th17 cells, cause neutrophil-mediated endothelial damage and subsequent hypotensive shock"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In Gram-negative sepsis, LPS (lipopolysaccharide) binds TLR4 on macrophages, triggering NF-kB activation and massive release of TNF-alpha and IL-1. TNF-alpha and IL-1 induce fever by stimulating PGE2 production in hypothalamic endothelium, cause systemic vasodilation by inducing endothelial nitric oxide synthase and prostacyclin, increase vascular permeability causing fluid leak and hypotension, and activate the coagulation cascade. High-dose TNF can cause DIC and hemorrhagic necrosis of tissues (Shwartzman reaction). Anti-TNF therapy was tested in sepsis but did not improve outcomes. IL-4 and IL-13 (A) are Th2 anti-inflammatory cytokines. IFN-gamma and IL-2 (B) drive cell-mediated immunity. IL-10/TGF-beta (C) are anti-inflammatory. IL-17 (D) recruits neutrophils but does not cause the systemic sepsis pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sepsis cytokines = TNF-alpha + IL-1 (from macrophages, LPS-stimulated) → fever via PGE2, hypotension via NO, DIC. COMLEX: ibuprofen works in sepsis fever by blocking PGE2 synthesis downstream of TNF/IL-1 signaling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity type classification and pathologic mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl35_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with severe eosinophilic asthma has an absolute eosinophil count of 2,400 per microliter. She is started on mepolizumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting a specific cytokine. After 3 months her eosinophil count falls to 180 per microliter and her asthma exacerbations decrease dramatically. Which cytokine does mepolizumab target, and what is the primary function of this cytokine in eosinophil biology?","choices":{"A":"Mepolizumab targets IL-4, which drives B cell class switching to IgE and sensitizes eosinophil precursors to mature more rapidly","B":"Mepolizumab targets IL-5, which is the most potent eosinophil growth, differentiation, activation, and survival factor produced primarily by Th2 cells","C":"Mepolizumab targets IL-13, which promotes goblet cell hyperplasia, mucus secretion, and eosinophil tissue recruitment in asthma","D":"Mepolizumab targets IL-33, which is an epithelial alarmin that activates ILC2s and promotes eosinophil accumulation in airways","E":"Mepolizumab targets TSLP, which is produced by damaged airway epithelium and drives Th2 differentiation and eosinophilia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mepolizumab is a humanized anti-IL-5 monoclonal antibody. IL-5 is the critical cytokine for eosinophil biology: it promotes eosinophil differentiation in bone marrow, release into circulation, tissue recruitment, activation, and survival. IL-5 is produced primarily by Th2 cells, ILC2s, and mast cells. In eosinophilic asthma, elevated IL-5 sustains peripheral and airway eosinophilia, contributing to airway inflammation and remodeling. Anti-IL-5 therapy dramatically reduces eosinophil counts and asthma exacerbation rates. Anti-IL-4R (dupilumab) targets IL-4 (B), not mepolizumab. Anti-IL-13 alone (C) is not mepolizumab. IL-33 is targeted by tezepelumab (an anti-TSLP, not anti-IL-33). TSLP is targeted by tezepelumab, not mepolizumab.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mepolizumab = anti-IL-5 = treats eosinophilic asthma and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis. IL-5 is the master eosinophil cytokine. COMLEX: know the biologic targets: anti-IL-5 (mepolizumab), anti-IL-4Ralpha (dupilumab), anti-IgE (omalizumab), anti-TSLP (tezepelumab) for severe asthma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity type classification and pathologic mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl35_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher studies mice with targeted deletion of the IL-10 gene. These mice develop spontaneous severe colitis with bloody diarrhea, weight loss, and diffuse intestinal inflammation beginning at 3 weeks of age. Which mechanism best explains why IL-10 deficiency leads to intestinal autoimmunity in these mice?","choices":{"A":"IL-10 deficiency causes B cell hyperactivation and excess IgE production, which sensitizes intestinal mast cells to commensal antigens","B":"Without IL-10, Th17 cells overproduce IL-17 in the gut, causing excessive neutrophil recruitment and mucosal barrier destruction","C":"IL-10 deficiency leads to excess AID expression in gut plasma cells, causing uncontrolled somatic hypermutation and autoantibody production","D":"Without IL-10, IL-12 production is not suppressed in Peyer patches, resulting in excessive NK cell cytotoxicity against epithelial cells","E":"Without IL-10, macrophages and Th1 cells in the gut are not suppressed, leading to unchecked inflammatory responses against commensal gut bacteria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IL-10 is the critical brake on mucosal immune responses. In the gut, macrophages and DCs are continuously exposed to commensal bacteria and their products (LPS, flagellin, peptidoglycans). IL-10 produced by regulatory T cells, macrophages, and Tr1 cells continuously suppresses innate and adaptive responses in the intestinal lamina propria to maintain tolerance to commensal flora. Without IL-10, macrophages and Th1 cells mount uncontrolled inflammatory responses to normal gut bacteria — producing IL-12, IFN-gamma, and TNF — causing the severe transmural colitis seen in IL-10 knockout mice. This phenotype resembles Crohn disease. Mutations in IL-10 or IL-10R in humans cause very-early-onset IBD. IgE mast cell sensitization (A) is not the primary mechanism. IL-17 excess (B) may contribute but is secondary. AID excess (C) is not related. NK cell cytotoxicity (D) is not the primary mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-10 = anti-inflammatory Treg cytokine = critical for gut immune homeostasis. IL-10 or IL-10R mutations cause very-early-onset IBD in infants. COMLEX: IL-10 also prevents macrophage-mediated tissue damage in RA and is being explored as a therapy for IBD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity type classification and pathologic mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl35_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain antibody class switching.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy presents with recurrent sinopulmonary infections, Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, and Cryptosporidium cholangiopathy. Serum immunoglobulins show very elevated IgM (320 mg/dL) with absent IgG, IgA, and IgE. Flow cytometry shows normal T cell and B cell counts. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in the CD40LG gene encoding CD40L. Which mechanism explains this boy's antibody profile?","choices":{"A":"CD40L deficiency prevents B cell receptor signaling, so B cells cannot be activated and cannot produce any antibody including IgM","B":"Absent CD40L impairs AID expression only partially, allowing IgM production but reducing the efficiency of IgG and IgA synthesis","C":"CD40L deficiency prevents IL-4 receptor expression on B cells, blocking cytokine-driven class switching while preserving baseline IgM secretion","D":"Without CD40L-CD40 interaction, B cells cannot receive the contact signal required for class switch recombination and remain stuck producing only IgM","E":"Without CD40L, T regulatory cells are overactivated and suppress IgG and IgA class switching by secreting excess TGF-beta"},"correct":"D","explanation":"X-linked Hyper-IgM syndrome (HIGM type 1) is caused by mutations in CD40LG encoding CD40L, which is expressed on activated CD4 T cells. Without CD40L, B cells cannot receive Signal 1 for class switch recombination (CD40-CD40L interaction). They continue to receive BCR-mediated activation signals and can produce T-independent IgM, but they cannot undergo class switching to IgG, IgA, or IgE. They also cannot form germinal centers or undergo somatic hypermutation. This explains the elevated IgM with absent other isotypes. These patients also suffer from opportunistic infections (PCP, Cryptosporidium) because CD40L-mediated macrophage activation is impaired. BCR signaling (A) is intact — B cells can be activated; they just cannot class switch. AID expression (B) may be reduced secondarily but the primary defect is CD40L-CD40 interaction. IL-4 receptor expression (C) is normal. Treg overactivation (D) is not the mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyper-IgM syndrome = X-linked = CD40L deficiency = cannot class switch = high IgM, absent IgG/IgA/IgE. Also causes PCP and Cryptosporidium due to impaired macrophage activation. COMLEX: distinguish from AID deficiency (HIGM type 2) — both show Hyper-IgM but AID deficiency also causes enlarged lymph nodes from follicular hyperplasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Mechanisms of T cell and B cell activation"},{"id":"sdl35_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain antibody class switching.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher is studying B cell responses in a tuberculosis patient. She finds that after 6 weeks of infection, the dominant serum antibody against mycobacterial antigens has shifted from IgM to IgG3, which is highly efficient at opsonizing bacteria. Which cytokine and T helper subset most directly drove this IgG3 class switch in the germinal center?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 produced by Th2 cells drives class switching to IgG3, because IL-4 promotes all IgG subclass production nonselectively","B":"IL-5 produced by Th2 cells specifically drives the IgM to IgG3 switch during T-dependent responses against intracellular pathogens","C":"IFN-gamma produced by Th1 cells provides the cytokine signal that directs class switch recombination toward IgG3 and IgG1 subclasses","D":"TGF-beta produced by regulatory T cells drives IgG3 class switching as a secondary effect of suppressing IgA production","E":"IL-10 produced by regulatory B cells acts on germinal center B cells to promote switching to IgG3 during chronic infection"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IFN-gamma, the signature Th1 cytokine, drives class switching to IgG1 and IgG3 in humans (and IgG2a/IgG3 in mice). These IgG subclasses are highly efficient at opsonization and complement activation, making them particularly effective against intracellular bacteria like mycobacteria. The Th1 response against TB generates IFN-gamma from both NK cells (early) and Th1 cells (sustained), which in germinal centers acts as the cytokine signal for IgG3/IgG1 switching alongside CD40L-CD40 interaction. IL-4 (A) drives IgE and IgG4 switching, not IgG3. IL-5 (B) primarily drives IgA switching, not IgG3. TGF-beta (C) drives IgA, not IgG3. IL-10 (D) promotes plasma cell differentiation generally but does not specifically drive IgG3 switching.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cytokine to isotype map: IL-4 → IgE, IgG4; IFN-gamma → IgG1, IgG3 (opsonizing); TGF-beta → IgA; IL-5 → IgA (intestinal). COMLEX: knowing cytokine-to-isotype relationships helps predict which deficiencies produce which antibody patterns.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Mechanisms of T cell and B cell activation"},{"id":"sdl35_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain antibody class switching.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman is found on routine screening to have absent serum IgG, IgA, and IgE, with markedly elevated IgM. Unlike X-linked Hyper-IgM syndrome, her lymph nodes are significantly enlarged due to follicular hyperplasia. CD40L expression on her T cells is normal. Genetic testing reveals a loss-of-function mutation in the AICDA gene. Why are IgG, IgA, and IgE absent despite intact T cell function?","choices":{"A":"AICDA encodes a transcription factor that activates promoters of the IgG, IgA, and IgE heavy chain constant region genes in plasma cells","B":"AID encoded by AICDA is required to introduce switch region DNA mutations enabling class switch recombination, so its absence prevents all non-IgM isotype production","C":"AID is required for somatic hypermutation only; its deficiency impairs affinity maturation but a separate mechanism blocks class switching","D":"AICDA encodes the J chain required for IgA dimerization, and its absence prevents both IgA secretion and the associated class switch","E":"AID acts as a co-receptor for IL-4 signaling on B cells, and its absence specifically blocks IL-4-induced IgE and IgG4 production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AID (activation-induced cytidine deaminase, encoded by AICDA) is essential for both class switch recombination and somatic hypermutation. In class switching, AID deaminates cytosines to uracils in switch (S) regions of the heavy chain locus, generating DNA lesions processed into double-strand breaks that allow recombination between S regions and deletion of intervening CH genes. Without AID, B cells cannot perform the DNA recombination required to switch from IgM to any other isotype. They also cannot undergo affinity maturation since somatic hypermutation in variable regions also requires AID. The enlarged lymph nodes reflect follicular hyperplasia from B cells that are activated but cannot complete class switching. AID is not a transcription factor for Ig genes (A). AID is required for BOTH class switching and SHM — they cannot be separated (B). AID does not encode the J chain (C). AID is not an IL-4 co-receptor (D).","boardPrep":"Step 1: AID deficiency = Hyper-IgM syndrome type 2 = autosomal recessive = enlarged lymph nodes (unlike CD40L deficiency which has small nodes). AID mediates both CSR and somatic hypermutation. COMLEX: enlarged lymph nodes distinguish HIGM type 2 (AID deficiency) from HIGM type 1 (CD40L deficiency).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Mechanisms of T cell and B cell activation"},{"id":"sdl35_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major antibody classes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman presents with recurrent sinusitis, otitis media, and two episodes of Giardia lamblia gastroenteritis in the past year. Serum IgG and IgM levels are normal. IgA is undetectable on repeat testing. She is otherwise healthy and has no autoimmune history. Which immunologic defect best explains her susceptibility to these specific infectious presentations?","choices":{"A":"Selective IgM deficiency prevents complement activation at mucosal surfaces, reducing bactericidal killing in the respiratory tract","B":"Selective IgG deficiency prevents opsonization of encapsulated bacteria in the sinuses, causing recurrent sinopulmonary infections","C":"Combined IgG and IgA deficiency prevents both opsonization and mucosal immunity, causing susceptibility to all extracellular pathogens","D":"Selective IgE deficiency prevents mast cell sensitization in the GI tract, reducing mucosal immunity against luminal parasites","E":"Selective IgA deficiency prevents mucosal protection in the respiratory and gastrointestinal tracts, causing recurrent sinopulmonary infections and Giardia susceptibility"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Selective IgA deficiency (serum IgA below 7 mg/dL with normal IgG and IgM) is the most common primary immunodeficiency, occurring in approximately 1 in 600 individuals. IgA is the dominant antibody at mucosal surfaces, where it neutralizes pathogens in the lumen before tissue invasion. Without secretory IgA, the respiratory mucosa and GI mucosa lack first-line neutralizing protection, leading to recurrent sinopulmonary infections and susceptibility to Giardia (which colonizes the intestinal lumen). Normal IgG and IgM explain why systemic infections are not a major problem. Selective IgM deficiency (A) is rare and would cause susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria. Selective IgG deficiency (B) causes susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria but IgG is normal here. Combined IgG and IgA deficiency (C) describes CVID. Selective IgE deficiency (D) would not explain Giardia susceptibility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Selective IgA deficiency = most common primary immunodeficiency = recurrent sinopulmonary infections + Giardia + normal IgG/IgM. Also associated with increased risk of autoimmune disease and anaphylaxis to IgA-containing blood products. COMLEX: always screen for selective IgA deficiency before giving IVIG — anti-IgA antibodies can cause anaphylaxis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity type classification and pathologic mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl35_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major antibody classes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 5-month-old boy presents with his third episode of Haemophilus influenzae pneumonia. His mother reports he was healthy at birth and had no infections until 4 months of age. Physical examination reveals no palpable lymph nodes and no tonsillar tissue. Serum immunoglobulins show absent IgG, IgA, IgM, and IgE. Which mechanism best explains why this infant was healthy for the first 4 months of life despite having agammaglobulinemia since birth?","choices":{"A":"Maternal IgA was present in breast milk and provided mucosal immunity until weaning, protecting the infant from systemic infection","B":"Innate immune cells including macrophages and NK cells provided sufficient defense against bacterial pathogens for the first 4 months","C":"Residual fetal B cell IgM production from primordial germinal centers provides partial protection during the first trimester after birth","D":"Maternal IgG was transported transplacentally via FcRn and provided passive humoral immunity for the first 4 to 6 months of life","E":"Maternal IgM was transported across the placenta via FcRn and provided complement-activating antibody coverage early in life"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is X-linked agammaglobulinemia (XLA, Bruton agammaglobulinemia) due to BTK deficiency. The key clinical teaching point is that affected boys are healthy for the first 4 to 6 months because maternal IgG is transported transplacentally via the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), which recognizes IgG at slightly acidic pH and releases it at physiologic pH on the fetal side of the placenta. After birth, maternal IgG is catabolized over 21-day half-life cycles and is largely gone by 6 months, exposing the underlying agammaglobulinemia. Maternal IgA in breast milk (A) protects mucosal surfaces but does not prevent systemic bacterial infections — and most serious infections in XLA are systemic. Innate immunity alone (B) is insufficient against encapsulated bacteria. Fetal B cells in XLA produce no antibody due to the BTK mutation. IgM is too large (pentamer) to cross the placenta via FcRn.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maternal IgG via FcRn → protects neonate for 6 months → then physiologic hypogammaglobulinemia → reveals XLA. This timeline is the classic board vignette clue. COMLEX: FcRn also extends IgG serum half-life by rescuing internalized IgG from lysosomal degradation in adult tissues.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity type classification and pathologic mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl35_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major antibody classes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with severe peanut allergy presents to the allergy clinic. Serum testing reveals markedly elevated peanut-specific IgE. She is started on omalizumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting total IgE. Which mechanism explains why targeting IgE specifically can prevent anaphylaxis from allergen exposure?","choices":{"A":"IgE binds to FcεRI on mast cells and basophils, and cross-linking of surface IgE by allergen triggers degranulation; omalizumab neutralizes free IgE and prevents this sensitization","B":"IgE activates the complement classical pathway to generate C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins, and omalizumab prevents complement fixation at allergic sites","C":"IgE mediates opsonization of peanut proteins for phagocytosis by eosinophils, and omalizumab prevents allergen uptake by antigen-presenting cells","D":"IgE acts as an antigen receptor on Th2 cells to present allergen to CD4 T cells, and omalizumab blocks this antigen presentation step","E":"IgE activates NK cells via FcεRIII to kill peanut-sensitized cells, and omalizumab prevents IgE-dependent natural killer cell cytotoxicity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IgE-mediated (Type I) hypersensitivity operates in two phases. Sensitization phase: allergen-specific IgE, produced after Th2-driven class switching, binds with high affinity to FcεRI on mast cells and basophils via its Fc region, sensitizing these cells. Elicitation phase: re-exposure to allergen cross-links adjacent IgE molecules on the mast cell surface, triggering calcium influx and degranulation with release of histamine, tryptase, PGD2, and leukotrienes — causing anaphylaxis. Omalizumab is a humanized anti-IgE antibody that binds free IgE in serum, reducing the availability of IgE to bind FcεRI on mast cells. This prevents sensitization and reduces the mast cell IgE-receptor burden over months. IgE does not activate the classical complement pathway (A) — that requires IgG and IgM. IgE does not mediate opsonization (B) — that is IgG. IgE is not a Th2 antigen receptor (C). IgE acts via FcεRI on mast cells, not FcεRIII on NK cells (D).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgE + FcεRI on mast cells = Type I hypersensitivity. Omalizumab = anti-IgE = used for severe allergic asthma and chronic urticaria. COMLEX: the sensitization → elicitation two-step model is essential for understanding anaphylaxis, atopy, and food allergies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity type classification and pathologic mechanisms"}]},{"sdlNumber":36,"title":"Autoimmunity & Hypersensitivity","questions":[{"id":"sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the mechanisms underlying autoimmunity.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient develops migratory polyarthritis and carditis three weeks after an untreated streptococcal pharyngitis. ASO titers are markedly elevated. Cardiac biopsy reveals lymphocytic infiltrates targeting host tissue despite bacterial clearance. Which mechanism best explains how the prior infection triggered autoimmune cardiac injury?","choices":{"A":"Bystander activation, in which inflammatory cytokines released during pharyngitis nonspecifically activate pre-existing self-reactive lymphocytes in cervical lymph nodes without requiring antigen-specific cross-reactivity","B":"AIRE gene deficiency, in which medullary thymic epithelial cells fail to express cardiac myosin during thymic development, allowing autoreactive T cell clones to escape negative selection and enter the periphery","C":"Molecular mimicry, in which Group A Streptococcus M-protein epitopes share structural homology with cardiac myosin, activating cross-reactive T and B cell clones that persist and attack host tissue after bacterial clearance","D":"Complement deficiency, in which impaired clearance of streptococcal antigen-antibody complexes allows persistent immune stimulation and polyclonal generation of anti-myosin IgG autoantibodies over time","E":"Epitope spreading, in which initial immune responses to streptococcal antigens sequentially expand to target additional cardiac self-epitopes released during early complement-mediated tissue injury at the valve"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rheumatic fever is the canonical example of molecular mimicry: Group A Streptococcus M-protein epitopes structurally resemble cardiac myosin, valve glycoproteins, and other host proteins, activating cross-reactive T and B cells that persist after bacterial clearance. Bystander activation involves nonspecific inflammatory milieu rather than antigen-specific cross-reactivity and would not explain selective cardiac targeting. AIRE deficiency causes APS-1, not rheumatic fever. Complement deficiency predisposes to SLE-like disease. Epitope spreading is a secondary phenomenon that can amplify established autoimmunity, not the initiating mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rheumatic fever is the canonical molecular mimicry example — not rheumatoid arthritis. Jones criteria: carditis, polyarthritis, Sydenham chorea, erythema marginatum, subcutaneous nodules. Elevated ASO titer confirms prior GAS infection. COMLEX: thoracic somatic dysfunction T1-T5 may accompany carditis via viscerosomatic reflexes; OMT is supportive after acute phase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Immune tolerance failure is the root cause of autoimmunity; central deletion and peripheral suppression prevent self-reactivity"},{"id":"sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the mechanisms underlying autoimmunity.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Mutations in a gene encoding a transcriptional regulator expressed in medullary thymic epithelial cells cause autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1, characterized by Addison disease, hypoparathyroidism, and type I diabetes mellitus. Deficiency in this gene prevents ectopic expression of organ-specific antigens such as glutamic acid decarboxylase in the thymus. Which gene is mutated?","choices":{"A":"FOXP3 (forkhead box P3 transcription factor), which is required for development and maintenance of CD25+CD4+ regulatory T cells; mutations cause IPEX syndrome with polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, and eczema","B":"AIRE (autoimmune regulator gene), which drives ectopic expression of peripheral tissue antigens in the thymic medulla, enabling clonal deletion of autoreactive T cells that would otherwise escape into the periphery","C":"PTPN22 (protein tyrosine phosphatase non-receptor type 22), which encodes a phosphatase that modulates T cell and B cell receptor signaling thresholds; variants associate with RA, T1D, SLE, and autoimmune thyroiditis","D":"CTLA-4 (cytotoxic T-lymphocyte antigen 4), which encodes a co-inhibitory receptor that competes with CD28 for B7 ligands on antigen-presenting cells and dampens effector T cell activation in peripheral tissues","E":"RAG1 (recombination activating gene 1), which mediates V(D)J recombination required to generate a diverse T cell receptor repertoire; deficiency causes severe combined immunodeficiency rather than organ-specific autoimmunity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AIRE drives ectopic expression of hundreds of peripheral tissue antigens in medullary thymic epithelial cells, enabling negative selection of autoreactive T cells. AIRE deficiency causes APS-1: Addison disease + hypoparathyroidism + T1DM + mucocutaneous candidiasis (autosomal recessive). FOXP3 mutations cause IPEX (X-linked): enteropathy + eczema + endocrinopathy. PTPN22 variants confer broad multi-disease susceptibility but do not cause APS-1. CTLA-4 mutations cause a separate autoimmune lymphoproliferative phenotype. RAG1 deficiency impairs V(D)J recombination causing SCID, not autoimmunity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AIRE = central tolerance enforcer in thymic medulla. APS-1 mnemonic: ADD (Addison) + HYPO (hypoparathyroidism) + DM1 + candidiasis. Autosomal recessive. IPEX (FOXP3) = X-linked. COMLEX: APS-1 patients may also develop alopecia, primary hypogonadism, and chronic active hepatitis; full screening is needed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Immune tolerance failure is the root cause of autoimmunity; central deletion and peripheral suppression prevent self-reactivity"},{"id":"sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the mechanisms underlying autoimmunity.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A co-inhibitory receptor expressed on regulatory T cells binds B7 ligands on antigen-presenting cells with higher affinity than CD28, transmitting inhibitory signals that prevent unchecked T cell proliferation. A polymorphism linked to this gene is associated with rheumatoid arthritis, type I diabetes, and autoimmune thyroiditis. Which molecule is described?","choices":{"A":"PD-1, which binds PD-L1 or PD-L2 expressed on tumor cells and peripheral tissues to induce T cell exhaustion and suppress antitumor immunity during chronic antigen stimulation in malignancy","B":"LAG-3, which binds MHC class II molecules on dendritic cells with high affinity and suppresses CD4+ T cell activation by disrupting dendritic cell maturation signals in peripheral lymphoid organs","C":"TIM-3, which recognizes galectin-9 and phosphatidylserine on apoptotic cells and promotes exhaustion of CD4+ and CD8+ T cells during chronic viral infection and tumor microenvironments","D":"BTLA, which binds herpesvirus entry mediator on activated lymphocytes and delivers co-inhibitory signals that suppress effector T cell responses during acute viral infection and transplant rejection","E":"CTLA-4, which outcompetes CD28 for B7 ligands and delivers inhibitory signals via SHP phosphatase recruitment, and whose variants impair peripheral tolerance by reducing regulatory T cell suppressive function"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CTLA-4 is expressed on activated T cells and Tregs and binds CD80/CD86 with 10- to 20-fold higher affinity than CD28, competitively inhibiting costimulation. Polymorphisms reduce inhibitory signaling, associating with RA, T1DM, and thyroid autoimmunity. Abatacept exploits this pathway as a biologic DMARD for RA. PD-1 (targeted by nivolumab/pembrolizumab) mainly operates in tumors and peripheral tissues. LAG-3, TIM-3, and BTLA are other checkpoint molecules associated with T cell exhaustion in cancer/infection but not classically linked to the same autoimmune diseases as CTLA-4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CTLA-4 = brake on T cells. Abatacept = CTLA-4-Ig fusion = blocks B7-CD28 = used in RA. Trap: CTLA-4 inhibitor ipilimumab used in cancer can cause autoimmune adverse effects including colitis, hepatitis, and endocrinopathy. COMLEX: patients on checkpoint inhibitors presenting with new inflammatory arthritis should be evaluated for immune-related adverse events mimicking RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Immune tolerance failure is the root cause of autoimmunity; central deletion and peripheral suppression prevent self-reactivity"},{"id":"sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Among all known HLA-disease associations in autoimmune disease, one specific allele confers a relative risk exceeding 150 — the highest reported for any single HLA allele-autoimmune disease pair. This allele is present in over 90% of patients with a seronegative spondyloarthropathy characterized by sacroiliitis, enthesitis, and ascending spinal ankylosis. Which allele is this?","choices":{"A":"HLA-DR4, which encodes the shared epitope in the DRB1 chain between positions 70 and 74 and confers a relative risk of 6-9 for seropositive rheumatoid arthritis in most studied populations","B":"HLA-DQ2/DQ8, which are class II alleles that present gluten-derived peptides in celiac disease and confer elevated risk for type I diabetes, with relative risks well below 50 in each disease","C":"HLA-B27, the MHC class I allele strongly associated with ankylosing spondylitis, conferring the highest single-allele relative risk in autoimmune disease and present in more than 90% of affected patients","D":"HLA-DR3, which confers susceptibility to SLE, Graves disease, Addison disease, and Hashimoto thyroiditis with relative risks typically in the range of 2-7, and is often in linkage disequilibrium with HLA-B8","E":"HLA-DQ6.02, which confers susceptibility to narcolepsy with a relative risk exceeding 40 and is associated with reduced rather than elevated risk for type I diabetes mellitus in most populations studied"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HLA-B27 has a relative risk exceeding 150 for ankylosing spondylitis — the strongest single HLA allele-disease association known. Mechanisms proposed include aberrant peptide presentation, HLA-B27 heavy chain misfolding triggering UPR, and arthritogenic peptide hypothesis. HLA-DR4 (RR 6-9) is the RA shared epitope allele. DQ2/DQ8 are celiac/T1D alleles with much lower RR. HLA-DR3 predisposes to several autoimmune conditions but with RR of 2-7. DQ6.02 has RR >40 for narcolepsy but is protective for T1DM.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HLA-B27 mnemonic PAIR — Psoriatic arthritis, Ankylosing spondylitis, IBD-associated arthritis, Reactive arthritis. RR >150 for AS is a high-yield board fact. COMLEX: AS causes rib cage rigidity and thoracolumbar facet joint ankylosis; OMT is generally contraindicated in advanced ankylosis due to fracture risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — HLA shared epitope and PTPN22 are critical genetic contributors to rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Rheumatoid arthritis shows no HLA-A or HLA-B locus associations but is associated with a specific amino acid sequence in the DRbeta chain shared by DR1 and major DR4 subtypes. The MHC association with RA is strongest in patients who are serologically positive for a specific antibody. Which serologic finding is most strongly associated with this HLA risk factor?","choices":{"A":"Antinuclear antibodies (ANA) at a titer exceeding 1:160, which are present in over 95% of SLE patients and are associated with HLA-DR2 and HLA-DR3 haplotypes rather than the shared epitope in DR4","B":"Anti-Smith (anti-Sm) antibodies, which are highly specific for SLE at greater than 99% specificity and are associated with HLA-DR2/DR3 haplotypes rather than the shared epitope found in DR1/DR4","C":"Rheumatoid factor (RF) IgM against Fc portions of IgG, which is found in 70-80% of RA patients but is also seen in Sjogren syndrome, SLE, and infection and is not uniquely linked to the shared epitope","D":"Anti-citrullinated protein antibodies (ACPA), which are the serologic marker that specifically co-associates with the HLA shared epitope in DR1/DR4-positive patients and indicate more erosive, aggressive RA disease","E":"Elevated serum ferritin greater than 10,000 ng/mL, which indicates macrophage activation syndrome or adult-onset Still disease rather than a seronegative or seropositive shared-epitope RA phenotype"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The HLA shared epitope encodes amino acids 70-74 of the DRbeta chain found in DR1 and DR4 subtypes, directly shaping the peptide-binding groove to present citrullinated peptides to CD4+ T cells. The SE association with RA is restricted to ACPA-positive patients, suggesting citrullinated antigens drive HLA-mediated pathology. The SE sequence is also found in EBV gp110 and bacterial dnaJ proteins, supporting molecular mimicry as a trigger. RF is 70-80% sensitive for RA but lacks SE-specific association. Anti-Sm and high-titer ANA are SLE markers. Ferritin >10,000 suggests macrophage activation syndrome or Still disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RA antibody pairs: RF (70-80% sensitive, not specific) + ACPA/anti-CCP (60-70% sensitive, 95%+ specific). ACPA = most specific RA antibody. ACPA+ RA = more erosive, higher risk of extra-articular manifestations. Shared epitope = HLA-DRB1 alleles encoding positions 70-74. COMLEX: citrullination is catalyzed by PAD enzymes (PAD4); enhanced by smoking, which is also an environmental RA risk factor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — HLA shared epitope and PTPN22 are critical genetic contributors to rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A protein tyrosine phosphatase expressed in lymphocytes has a minor allele that causes gain-of-function inhibition of B cell and T cell receptor signaling. This variant is associated with type I diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis, SLE, and autoimmune thyroiditis, but inversely associated with Crohn disease. Which gene encodes this phosphatase?","choices":{"A":"AIRE, located at chromosome 21q22, which encodes the autoimmune regulator transcription factor required for ectopic self-antigen expression in medullary thymic epithelial cells to enforce central tolerance","B":"PTPN22, located at chromosome 1p13, the most common non-HLA gene associated with multiple autoimmune diseases; the Trp620 allele impairs lymphocyte receptor signaling and likely reduces thymic deletion of autoreactive T cells","C":"CTLA-4, located at chromosome 2q33, which encodes the inhibitory T cell costimulatory receptor that competes with CD28 for B7 ligands and whose polymorphisms are linked to RA, T1DM, and thyroid autoimmunity","D":"IL-23R, located at chromosome 1p31, which encodes the IL-23 receptor subunit that drives Th17 differentiation and is associated with IBD, psoriasis, and ankylosing spondylitis rather than RA or SLE","E":"FCGR3B, located at chromosome 1q23, which encodes an Fc gamma receptor on neutrophils mediating immune complex clearance; null alleles predispose to SLE and ANCA-associated vasculitis rather than RA or T1DM"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PTPN22 encodes lymphoid tyrosine phosphatase, which negatively regulates TCR and BCR signaling. The Trp620 minor allele gains function to further inhibit receptor signaling, paradoxically predisposing to autoimmunity — likely by impairing thymic deletion and Treg function. The common Arg620 allele associates with Crohn disease, illustrating how opposite effects on receptor signaling associate with distinct immunopathology. PTPN22 is the single most replicated non-HLA autoimmune susceptibility locus. It is on chromosome 1p13, distinct from CTLA-4 (2q33), AIRE (21q22), IL-23R (1p31), and FCGR3B (1q23).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-HLA autoimmune genes: PTPN22 (multiple autoimmune), CTLA-4 (RA/T1D/thyroid), IL-23R (IBD/spondylitis/psoriasis), AIRE (APS-1), FOXP3 (IPEX), FCGR genes (SLE). COMLEX: PTPN22 Trp620 often appears in vignettes with positive family history of multiple unrelated autoimmune conditions across generations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — HLA shared epitope and PTPN22 are critical genetic contributors to rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl36_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis of autoimmune diseases and other immunological diseases as it pertains to their hypersensitivity type classification (I-IV).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman presents with pallor, jaundice, and exertional dyspnea. Laboratory results show hemoglobin 7.2 g/dL, elevated indirect bilirubin, low haptoglobin, and reticulocytosis. A direct Coombs test is positive for IgG coating of erythrocytes. Which hypersensitivity type and mechanism is responsible for the anemia?","choices":{"A":"Type I hypersensitivity, in which IgE anti-erythrocyte antibodies bound to FcεRI receptors on mast cells degranulate upon RBC antigen cross-linking, releasing histamine that causes hemolytic jaundice and pruritus","B":"Type III hypersensitivity, in which circulating IgG-antigen immune complexes form soluble aggregates that coincidentally deposit onto circulating erythrocytes and activate complement through the classical pathway","C":"Type IV hypersensitivity, in which sensitized CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes recognize altered peptide-MHC I complexes on RBC precursors in the bone marrow and directly destroy erythroid progenitor cells","D":"Type II hypersensitivity, in which IgG autoantibodies bind erythrocyte surface antigens and promote opsonization and Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis by splenic macrophages, causing extravascular hemolysis","E":"Type II hypersensitivity mediated by IgM cold agglutinins that fix complement on erythrocytes at cold temperatures and cause agglutination in acral vessels, producing a positive direct Coombs test for complement rather than IgG"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia is the prototype Type II hypersensitivity disorder. IgG anti-RBC antibodies detected by direct Coombs test opsonize erythrocytes, which are then phagocytosed by splenic macrophages via Fc receptors, producing extravascular hemolysis with elevated indirect bilirubin and low haptoglobin. The direct Coombs positive for IgG distinguishes warm AIHA (Type II, IgG, extravascular) from cold agglutinin disease (also Type II, but IgM complement-positive Coombs, intravascular hemolysis). Type I requires IgE and mast cells. Type III involves soluble complexes. Type IV has no antibody component.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type II = antibody against cell surface or fixed tissue antigen. Direct Coombs = IgG on RBCs (warm AIHA) or complement on RBCs (cold agglutinin). Warm AIHA: IgG, 37 degrees C, extravascular. Cold agglutinin: IgM, cold temps, intravascular complement-mediated. COMLEX: splenomegaly is expected; splenectomy treats refractory warm AIHA. Associated with CLL, lymphoma, infections (Mycoplasma, EBV).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — Antibody isotypes map directly to hypersensitivity type: IgE drives Type I; IgG/IgM drive Types II and III"},{"id":"sdl36_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis of autoimmune diseases and other immunological diseases as it pertains to their hypersensitivity type classification (I-IV).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with SLE presents with new hematuria and proteinuria. Kidney biopsy shows granular immune deposits along the glomerular basement membrane with C3 and IgG staining. Serum complement levels C3 and C4 are both low. Which hypersensitivity mechanism is the primary driver of renal injury in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Type III hypersensitivity, in which circulating IgG-anti-dsDNA immune complexes deposit in glomerular capillaries, activate complement via the classical pathway, and recruit neutrophils that release lysosomal enzymes causing nephritis","B":"Type II hypersensitivity, in which IgG anti-GBM antibodies bind linearly along the glomerular basement membrane and activate complement, producing the same granular immunofluorescence pattern found in Goodpasture syndrome","C":"Type I hypersensitivity, in which renal mast cells sensitized by IgE anti-dsDNA antibodies degranulate upon antigen re-exposure, releasing histamine and prostaglandins that increase glomerular vascular permeability","D":"Type IV hypersensitivity, in which Th17 cells infiltrating the glomerulus produce IL-17A and recruit neutrophils to produce fibrinoid necrosis without immunoglobulin or complement deposition in glomerular capillaries","E":"Type II hypersensitivity, in which anti-C1q autoantibodies bind complement fixed along the GBM and produce a granular pattern on immunofluorescence indistinguishable from immune complex nephritis by light microscopy alone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lupus nephritis is the prototypical Type III immune complex disease. Circulating anti-dsDNA IgG forms complexes with nucleosomal antigens that deposit in glomeruli, activating complement classically (explaining low C3 and C4). The granular IF pattern distinguishes it from Goodpasture syndrome (Type II = linear IgG). Low complement is the key clinical clue for active Type III. Type I requires IgE. Type IV involves T cells without immunoglobulin deposition and would not produce low serum complement or granular IF. Anti-C1q antibodies are seen in SLE but cause Type III pathology through immune complex formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Granular IF + low complement = Type III immune complex. Linear IF = Type II anti-GBM. Pauci-immune (no Ig) = ANCA vasculitis. Low C3 + low C4 = classical pathway activation = SLE/MPGN type I/post-infectious. Low C3 only = alternative pathway = MPGN type II/dense deposit disease. COMLEX: SLE patients should be monitored for nephritis with serial C3/C4 and urinalysis; drop in complement + proteinuria = flare.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — Antibody isotypes map directly to hypersensitivity type: IgE drives Type I; IgG/IgM drive Types II and III"},{"id":"sdl36_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis of autoimmune diseases and other immunological diseases as it pertains to their hypersensitivity type classification (I-IV).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old healthcare worker develops pruritus, erythema, and vesicles on her hands 48 to 72 hours after wearing latex gloves. A patch test applied for 48 hours reproduces the reaction with maximal induration at 72 hours. Skin biopsy shows perivascular lymphocytic infiltrates with few eosinophils. Which hypersensitivity mechanism is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Type I (immediate) hypersensitivity, in which IgE anti-latex antibodies bound to dermal mast cells cross-link upon re-exposure and release preformed histamine within 15-30 minutes, producing immediate urticaria and potential anaphylaxis","B":"Type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity, in which sensitized CD4+ Th1 cells recognize latex hapten-protein complexes on MHC II and produce IFN-gamma, activating macrophages and driving lymphocytic dermal inflammation with 48-72 hour kinetics","C":"Type II (antibody-mediated) hypersensitivity, in which IgG anti-latex antibodies bind to latex-modified keratinocyte surface proteins and activate complement, generating C5a that recruits neutrophils into the dermis within hours","D":"Type III (immune complex) hypersensitivity, in which soluble latex-IgG antibody complexes deposit in dermal capillaries, activate complement via the classical pathway, and trigger fibrinoid vasculitis and neutrophilic infiltrates","E":"Type II (antibody-mediated) hypersensitivity, in which IgM anti-latex antibodies activate the classical complement pathway on epidermal cells, generating C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins that recruit eosinophils to the dermis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Allergic contact dermatitis is the prototype Type IV reaction. Sensitization: latex haptens bind host proteins, are processed by Langerhans cells, and presented to CD4+ T cells that differentiate into Th1 memory cells. Elicitation: re-exposure triggers Th1 IFN-gamma production, macrophage activation, and perivascular lymphocytic inflammation with 48-72 hour peak. Type I latex allergy (IgE-mediated) causes immediate urticaria/anaphylaxis within minutes — a distinct presentation. Patch testing positive at 48-72 h is the diagnostic test for Type IV. All three wrong choices misidentify the mechanism as antibody-mediated rather than T cell-mediated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type IV = DTH = Delayed (48-72 h) + T cells + no antibody. Classic examples: TB skin test, poison ivy/oak, nickel, contact dermatitis. Type I latex = immediate urticaria (minutes); Type IV latex = delayed vesicular dermatitis (48-72 h). Both can coexist. COMLEX: patch testing is performed at 48-72 hours for Type IV; intradermal skin testing is used for Type I evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — Antibody isotypes map directly to hypersensitivity type: IgE drives Type I; IgG/IgM drive Types II and III"},{"id":"sdl36_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the mechanisms underlying autoimmunity.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 15-month-old male presents with severe watery diarrhea, failure to thrive, eczematous rash, and type I diabetes mellitus diagnosed at 8 months of age. Immunologic workup reveals absent CD4+CD25+ regulatory T cells in peripheral blood. Gene sequencing confirms a frameshift mutation in a transcription factor located on the X chromosome. Which gene is mutated, and what is the best mechanistic explanation for the multi-organ autoimmunity?","choices":{"A":"AIRE; loss of ectopic self-antigen expression in medullary thymic epithelial cells permits autoreactive T cells targeting gut and skin antigens to escape negative selection and enter the periphery unchecked","B":"PTPN22; a gain-of-function variant in this non-HLA phosphatase gene impairs thymic deletion of autoreactive T cells and reduces regulatory T cell function across the gut, skin, and endocrine pancreas","C":"FOXP3; loss of functional regulatory T cells eliminates peripheral suppression of autoreactive effector T cells in the gut, skin, and pancreatic islets, allowing unchecked tissue destruction across multiple organ systems","D":"IL-2RA; a null mutation in the high-affinity IL-2 receptor alpha chain abolishes IL-2-driven expansion and survival of FOXP3+ regulatory T cells, mimicking FOXP3 deficiency through a distinct signaling mechanism","E":"RAG2; biallelic loss of function prevents V(D)J recombination entirely, producing a SCID phenotype with absent T and B cells rather than autoimmune polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, and eczema in a male infant"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is IPEX syndrome caused by FOXP3 loss-of-function mutations. FOXP3 is the master transcription factor for CD4+CD25+ Tregs, which suppress autoreactive effector T cells via IL-10, TGF-beta, and contact-dependent mechanisms. Without Tregs, effector T cells attack gut (diarrhea), skin (eczema), and pancreatic islets (T1DM). It is X-linked and presents in infancy. AIRE mutation causes APS-1 (Addison + hypoparathyroidism + T1DM + candidiasis, autosomal recessive, onset in childhood). IL-2RA deficiency phenotypically resembles IPEX but is caused by a distinct mechanism. RAG2 deficiency causes SCID with absent lymphocytes rather than autoimmunity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IPEX = FOXP3 + X-linked + triad of enteropathy + eczema + endocrinopathy in infancy. Fatal without HSCT. Distinguishing from APS-1: IPEX has enteropathy and eczema; APS-1 has Addison and candidiasis. Both include T1DM. COMLEX: male infants with chronic diarrhea + eczema + elevated glucose warrant genetic workup; IPEX is rapidly progressive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system"},{"id":"sdl36_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the mechanisms underlying autoimmunity.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with a 6-year history of SLE is found to have monozygotic twin sister who has remained unaffected. Their physician notes that concordance for SLE in identical twins is approximately 25%. A systemic viral infection two months ago preceded worsening anti-dsDNA titers and declining complement levels. Which statement best integrates the genetic and environmental factors in SLE pathogenesis?","choices":{"A":"The 25% monozygotic twin concordance indicates SLE is primarily a single-gene disorder caused by a complement C4 null allele, with environmental triggers playing only a minor role in disease initiation","B":"SLE has no significant genetic component because concordance in identical twins below 50% indicates predominantly environmental causation with genetic factors providing minimal predisposition to disease","C":"SLE is caused exclusively by type I interferon overproduction triggered by viral infection; the 25% concordance reflects variation in antiviral immunity rather than shared genetic autoimmune susceptibility","D":"Identical twins with SLE always develop the same clinical phenotype since they share identical HLA haplotypes, and phenotypic variability within twin pairs would disprove a genetic contribution to SLE","E":"SLE requires both polygenic susceptibility including HLA-DR2/DR3, complement null alleles, and type I IFN pathway variants, and environmental triggers such as viral infections and UV radiation, explaining why 75% of genetically identical individuals never develop disease"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SLE is a complex polygenic disorder. Monozygotic twin concordance of 25% proves strong genetic predisposition compared with DZ concordance of 2-3%, while the 75% non-concordance demonstrates that genetic susceptibility alone is insufficient. SLE-predisposing genes include HLA-DR2/DR3, C4 null alleles (impaired immune complex clearance), type I IFN pathway genes (IRF5, IRF7, TYK2), TLR7, and FCGR genes. Environmental triggers include UV radiation, certain infections, estrogen, and drugs. The distinction between 25% concordance (strong genetic component, incomplete penetrance) and 100% concordance (monogenic) is a key board concept.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MZ concordance for SLE 25% vs DZ 2-3% = polygenic with strong HLA component. C4 null alleles (most common in SLE) impair apoptotic debris clearance. Type I IFN signature = hallmark of active SLE, activated by TLR7/9 sensing of nucleic acids. Environmental triggers: UV light, EBV, estrogen, drugs (procainamide, hydralazine, isoniazid). COMLEX: drug-induced lupus = positive anti-histone antibodies; resolves with drug discontinuation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system"},{"id":"sdl36_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the mechanisms underlying autoimmunity.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with multiple sclerosis asks her neurologist why her immune cells attack myelin basic protein when AIRE should have deleted such clones in the thymus. The physician explains that AIRE expression does not cover all self-antigens, and that escapee self-reactive T cells are normally suppressed by additional mechanisms. Which combination best describes the peripheral tolerance mechanisms that suppress these escaped autoreactive T cells?","choices":{"A":"Clonal anergy from absence of costimulatory signal during self-antigen presentation, Treg suppression via IL-10 and TGF-beta, and activation-induced cell death via Fas-FasL interactions together maintain tolerance to self-antigens not covered by AIRE","B":"Somatic hypermutation in peripheral B cells generates new BCR specificities that cross-react with self-antigens, but affinity maturation in germinal centers eliminates all self-reactive B cell clones before autoantibody production occurs","C":"Negative selection in the thymic cortex under supervision of cortical thymic epithelial cells provides complete elimination of all T cell clones reactive to tissue-specific antigens such as myelin basic protein","D":"Class switch recombination from IgM to IgG requires CD40-CD40L interaction, which is blocked in peripheral lymph nodes when self-reactive B cells encounter self-antigen without adequate T helper cell activation","E":"Receptor editing in peripheral B cells replaces autoreactive BCR light chains with non-self-reactive sequences during secondary V(D)J recombination, rescuing self-reactive clones from activation-induced apoptosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Central tolerance (AIRE-driven negative selection) is incomplete — AIRE covers many but not all peripheral tissue antigens. Peripheral tolerance provides the second line of defense via three main mechanisms: (1) Clonal anergy: T cells recognizing self-antigen without CD28 costimulation become functionally unresponsive. (2) Treg suppression: FOXP3+ Tregs secrete IL-10 and TGF-beta and provide contact-dependent suppression. (3) AICD: Fas-FasL interaction triggers apoptosis of chronically activated T cells. In MS, peripheral tolerance breakdown to myelin antigens (MBP, PLP, MOG) leads to demyelination. The wrong choices describe B cell biology rather than T cell peripheral tolerance mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central tolerance = thymus (clonal deletion via AIRE). Peripheral tolerance = anergy + Treg suppression + AICD. Failure = autoimmune disease. MS = Type IV hypersensitivity, CD4+ Th1/Th17-mediated demyelination. Oligoclonal bands on CSF = intrathecal IgG from B cells. COMLEX: MS patients may have concurrent other autoimmune diseases; MS is 3x more common in women and in higher latitudes with less UV light exposure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system"},{"id":"sdl36_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with ACPA-positive rheumatoid arthritis and HLA-DRB1*04:01 reports that her sister has autoimmune thyroiditis and her mother has SLE. Genetic counseling reveals the family likely carries PTPN22 Trp620, HLA-DR3, and HLA-DR4 variants distributed across members. Which statement about the familial autoimmune clustering is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"HLA-DR4 is co-inherited exclusively with PTPN22 Trp620 in a conserved haplotype on chromosome 1, so family members carrying DR4 automatically also carry the Trp620 variant and share identical overall disease risk","B":"The presence of multiple autoimmune conditions within the family indicates a shared C4 null allele that impairs immune complex clearance and predisposes all members equally to SLE, RA, and thyroid autoimmunity","C":"HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4 within the same family indicate that only HLA genes contribute to autoimmune susceptibility, since non-HLA genes like PTPN22 have been shown to be statistically insignificant in GWAS studies","D":"Familial clustering of autoimmune diseases reflects exclusively environmental exposures shared by family members rather than genetic factors, since no autoimmune disease exceeds 50% concordance in identical twins","E":"PTPN22 Trp620 confers risk for multiple autoimmune diseases including RA, T1DM, SLE, and autoimmune thyroiditis in the same family because it broadly impairs lymphocyte receptor signaling, while specific HLA alleles additionally confer organ-specific disease susceptibility"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Non-HLA genes, especially PTPN22, confer risk across multiple autoimmune diseases simultaneously, explaining why different autoimmune conditions cluster in the same family. PTPN22 is on chromosome 1p13 and HLA is on chromosome 6p21 — they are on different chromosomes and segregate independently. HLA alleles provide organ-specific disease targeting (DR4 for RA, DR3 for SLE/thyroid), while PTPN22 provides a shared background of lymphocyte dysregulation. GWAS studies confirm that over 50% of autoimmune risk alleles are shared between at least two diseases. The 25% MZ concordance for SLE and similar values for other diseases prove genetic, not purely environmental, causation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTPN22 and HLA are on different chromosomes and not co-inherited. Familial autoimmune clustering = PTPN22 + HLA diversity. One patient with one autoimmune disease should be screened for others. COMLEX: RA patients have elevated rates of Sjogren overlap, thyroid disease, and interstitial lung disease; these co-occurrences are explained by shared genetic susceptibility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis"},{"id":"sdl36_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with progressive ankylosing spondylitis carries HLA-B27 and reports his father and brother are similarly affected. His 20-year-old son carries HLA-B27 but has no symptoms. Genetic counseling reveals that some HLA alleles are protective. Which HLA allele confers a dramatically reduced risk for type I diabetes mellitus, with a relative risk far below 1.0, making it one of the best-characterized protective alleles in autoimmune disease?","choices":{"A":"HLA-B27, which confers a relative risk exceeding 150 for ankylosing spondylitis and is not considered protective against any major autoimmune condition studied in large population cohorts","B":"HLA-DR4, which confers a relative risk of 6-9 for rheumatoid arthritis and is associated with more aggressive, erosive, seropositive disease rather than any protective effect against autoimmunity","C":"HLA-DQ8, which confers a relative risk of approximately 14 for type I diabetes and is further amplified when DQ2/DQ8 compound heterozygosity is present, conferring the highest known risk for T1DM","D":"HLA-DQ6.02, which is associated with a dramatically reduced risk for type I diabetes mellitus, relative risk approximately 0.02, making it one of the best-characterized protective HLA alleles known in autoimmune disease","E":"HLA-DR3, which confers susceptibility to SLE, Graves disease, and Addison disease with relative risks of 2-7, and is associated with elevated rather than reduced risk for type I diabetes mellitus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"HLA-DQ6.02 (also DQ0602) has a relative risk of approximately 0.02 for T1DM — a dramatic protective effect. The mechanism likely involves DQ6.02 binding insulin-derived peptides in a configuration that promotes tolerance rather than autoreactivity. This illustrates the key concept that HLA alleles can be protective (RR less than 1) as well as predisposing (RR greater than 1). Notably, the same DQ6.02 allele confers susceptibility to narcolepsy with RR exceeding 40, illustrating allele-specific bidirectional effects across diseases. All four wrong choices have RR greater than 1 for their associated diseases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Protective HLA = DQ6.02 for T1DM (RR approximately 0.02). Board trap: same DQ6.02 is susceptibility allele for narcolepsy (RR >40). Highest T1DM risk: DQ2/DQ8 compound heterozygosity. DQ8 alone: RR approximately 14. Board tip: protective alleles appear on USMLE to test whether students understand relative risk can be below 1. COMLEX: T1DM patients should receive annual thyroid screening given shared genetic risk with Hashimoto thyroiditis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis"},{"id":"sdl36_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman newly diagnosed with SLE is found to have a null allele for complement component C4A, reducing C4A expression by 50%. Her rheumatologist explains that early complement deficiencies strongly predispose to lupus-like disease. What is the mechanism by which early complement component deficiency contributes to SLE pathogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Deficiency of early complement components impairs clearance of apoptotic cellular debris and circulating immune complexes; accumulation of apoptotic nuclear material provides persistent antigen stimulation that drives anti-dsDNA and antinuclear antibody production","B":"Complement deficiency reduces terminal membrane attack complex formation, which directly increases susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria and prevents the normal lysis of autoreactive B cells in secondary lymphoid organs","C":"C4A null alleles reduce complement-dependent opsonization of bacteria and viruses, leading to recurrent infections that trigger molecular mimicry and generation of cross-reactive antibodies against nuclear antigens","D":"Loss of C4A reduces total serum complement levels, activating a compensatory feedback loop in which B cells upregulate anti-dsDNA IgG production that further depletes residual C3 and C4 via ongoing classical pathway activation","E":"C4A null alleles impair complement-mediated B cell tolerance checkpoints in the bone marrow, preventing the elimination of newly formed autoreactive B cell clones before they reach peripheral lymphoid organs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Early complement components C1q, C2, and C4 play a critical role in opsonization and clearance of apoptotic cells and immune complexes via complement receptor-mediated phagocytosis. When early complement is deficient, apoptotic cells and immune complexes accumulate. Nucleosomal antigens released from poorly cleared apoptotic cells persistently stimulate autoreactive B cells, driving anti-dsDNA and ANA production. C1q null alleles confer the highest SLE risk (greater than 90%). C4A null alleles are found in approximately 50% of SLE cases. This is distinct from terminal complement deficiency (C5-C9), which increases susceptibility to Neisseria rather than causing autoimmunity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C1q null = strongest single-gene SLE risk (>90%). C2 deficiency = most common complement deficiency overall; also predisposes to SLE + encapsulated organisms. Mechanism: impaired apoptotic debris clearance = persistent nuclear antigen = anti-dsDNA. Low C3/C4 during SLE flare = complement consumption by active immune complexes. COMLEX: patients with recurrent Neisseria infections should be screened for terminal complement deficiency (C5-C9), while those with lupus phenotype and recurrent infections may have early complement deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis"},{"id":"sdl36_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis of autoimmune diseases and other immunological diseases as it pertains to their hypersensitivity type classification (I-IV).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male with known peanut allergy develops generalized urticaria, laryngeal edema, bronchospasm, and hypotension within 10 minutes of eating a cookie. His roommate, who has never been exposed to peanuts, eats the same cookie with no adverse reaction. Intramuscular epinephrine is administered with rapid resolution. Which sequence of events best explains the anaphylaxis in this patient but not his roommate?","choices":{"A":"Prior sensitization activated peanut-specific CD4+ Th1 memory cells; re-exposure triggers rapid IFN-gamma production and macrophage activation causing systemic inflammation with onset within 10 minutes of antigen contact","B":"Prior sensitization generated peanut-specific IgE antibodies now bound to FcεRI receptors on mast cells; re-exposure cross-links adjacent IgE-FcεRI complexes, triggering mast cell degranulation with immediate release of histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins","C":"Peanut lectins directly activate the mannose-binding lectin complement pathway, generating C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins that stimulate systemic basophil and mast cell degranulation without requiring prior IgE antibody formation","D":"Peanut-specific IgG antibodies formed during prior sensitization bind peanut antigens and form immune complexes that deposit in dermal vessels and activate complement, causing systemic urticaria and hypotension","E":"Peanut glycoproteins activate plasmacytoid dendritic cells to produce type I interferons that trigger systemic NK cell activation and vascular permeability, causing urticaria and bronchospasm within minutes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Type I hypersensitivity requires two exposures: (1) Sensitization: first exposure drives Th2 differentiation and IL-4 signaling, inducing IgE class switching by B cells; peanut-specific IgE binds FcεRI on mast cells and basophils. (2) Elicitation: re-exposure cross-links adjacent IgE-FcεRI complexes, triggering immediate degranulation. Preformed mediators (histamine, tryptase) cause immediate reactions within minutes; newly synthesized mediators (leukotrienes, prostaglandins, PAF) cause late-phase reactions. No prior exposure = no sensitization = no anaphylaxis in the roommate. Epinephrine acts via alpha-1 (vasoconstriction) and beta-2 (bronchodilation) to reverse shock.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anaphylaxis = Type I = IgE-FcεRI-mast cell. First-line = IM epinephrine. Preformed: histamine, tryptase, heparin. Newly synthesized: LTC4, LTD4, PGD2, PAF. Sensitization required — first exposure is silent. COMLEX: omalizumab (anti-IgE) prevents IgE from binding FcεRI and reduces anaphylaxis risk and asthma exacerbations; used in chronic refractory asthma and food allergy prevention protocols.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 4 — Describe the major antibody classes"},{"id":"sdl36_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis of autoimmune diseases and other immunological diseases as it pertains to their hypersensitivity type classification (I-IV).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old male smoker presents with hemoptysis, hematuria, and rapidly progressive renal failure. Kidney biopsy shows linear IgG deposits along the glomerular basement membrane with C3 and crescentic glomerulonephritis. Serum anti-GBM antibodies targeting the NC1 domain of type IV collagen are positive. Which statement best distinguishes this condition from SLE nephritis by immunofluorescence pattern and hypersensitivity mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Goodpasture syndrome is Type II hypersensitivity with linear IgG along the GBM from antibodies against a fixed tissue antigen; SLE nephritis is Type III with granular IgG from circulating immune complex deposition — both activate complement but via distinct mechanisms","B":"Goodpasture syndrome is Type III hypersensitivity with granular immune complex deposits because anti-GBM antibodies form circulating complexes first before depositing in the glomerulus in a subendothelial distribution","C":"SLE nephritis is Type II hypersensitivity with linear IgG deposition because anti-dsDNA antibodies directly bind fixed DNA antigens on glomerular epithelial cell surfaces in a continuous linear pattern","D":"Both Goodpasture syndrome and SLE nephritis are Type IV hypersensitivity with predominant CD8+ cytotoxic T cell infiltrates; the immunofluorescence pattern difference reflects only the degree of non-specific IgG accumulation","E":"Goodpasture syndrome is Type IV hypersensitivity; the linear IgG along the GBM represents non-specific immunoglobulin deposition, while the actual injury is mediated by CD8+ cytotoxic T cells destroying type IV collagen directly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Key IF distinction: Goodpasture disease = LINEAR IgG = Type II (antibody against fixed tissue antigen — NC1 domain of type IV collagen alpha-3 chain). SLE nephritis = GRANULAR (\"lumpy-bumpy\") IF = Type III (immune complex deposition). Both activate classical complement pathway. Anti-GBM targets both kidney GBM and lung alveolar basement membrane, producing the pulmonary-renal syndrome. Smoking increases risk by exposing alveolar antigens to the immune system. Pauci-immune glomerulonephritis (no IF deposits) with ANCA is the third major pattern for rapidly progressive GN.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Linear IF = anti-GBM (Goodpasture), Type II. Granular IF = SLE/MPGN/post-strep, Type III. Pauci-immune = ANCA vasculitis (GPA, MPA). Pulmonary-renal syndrome: Goodpasture (anti-GBM) vs ANCA vasculitis (cANCA/pANCA) vs SLE (anti-dsDNA). Treatment: plasmapheresis + cyclophosphamide + steroids. COMLEX: young male smoker + hemoptysis + hematuria = Goodpasture until proven otherwise; smoking is thought to expose alveolar antigens to circulating anti-GBM antibodies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 4 — Describe the major antibody classes"},{"id":"sdl36_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis of autoimmune diseases and other immunological diseases as it pertains to their hypersensitivity type classification (I-IV).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old nursing student has a tuberculin skin test placed for employment. At 48 hours, a 17 mm area of induration is present at the injection site. The same student recently received a subcutaneous injection of rabbit serum protein for research; 4-6 hours later she developed erythema and palpable swelling at the injection site. Which statement correctly identifies the hypersensitivity type for each reaction and distinguishes their effector mechanisms?","choices":{"A":"The TST is Type III (immune complex) hypersensitivity because it requires prior sensitization and involves IgG anti-tuberculin antibodies depositing in dermal vessels; the rabbit serum reaction is Type IV (delayed-type) because it recruits Th17 cells and neutrophils at 4-8 hours","B":"Both reactions are Type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity because they both require prior sensitization, both peak within hours to days, and both involve T lymphocyte-mediated inflammation without requiring pre-formed antibody","C":"The TST is Type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity mediated by sensitized CD4+ Th1 cells producing IFN-gamma at 48-72 hours; the rabbit serum reaction is a Type III (immune complex) hypersensitivity Arthus reaction mediated by local IgG-antigen complex deposition with complement activation at 4-8 hours","D":"The TST is Type I (immediate) hypersensitivity mediated by IgE anti-tuberculin antibodies on dermal mast cells; the rabbit serum reaction is Type II (antibody-mediated) mediated by IgG opsonizing rabbit protein-coated host cells for phagocytic destruction","E":"Both reactions are Type II (antibody-mediated) hypersensitivity; the TST involves IgG anti-mycobacterial antibodies opsonizing macrophages, while the Arthus reaction involves IgG opsonizing injected antigen-protein conjugates at the injection site"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The TST is the classic Type IV DTH reaction: tuberculin-sensitized CD4+ Th1 memory cells recognize tuberculin-MHC II complexes on APCs, produce IFN-gamma, and activate macrophages. Induration (not just redness) from mononuclear infiltrate peaks at 48-72 hours. The Arthus reaction is a local Type III reaction: preformed IgG antibodies in tissue react with injected antigen to form in-situ immune complexes, activate complement (C3a/C5a), and recruit neutrophils. Peaks at 4-8 hours. Fibrinoid necrosis of vessel walls is characteristic. Neutrophils = key effectors in Type III; T cells = key effectors in Type IV. Both require prior sensitization but differ in effector mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arthus reaction = local Type III; serum sickness = systemic Type III. DTH = local Type IV; systemic = transplant rejection/MS/T1DM. Timing: Type I = minutes; Type II and III = hours (4-8 h); Type IV = days (48-72 h). PPD induration thresholds: >5 mm in immunocompromised, >10 mm in healthcare workers, >15 mm in low-risk individuals. COMLEX: induration = key physical finding; erythema alone does not count as positive TST.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 4 — Describe the major antibody classes"}]},{"sdlNumber":37,"title":"Rheumatoid Arthritis Pathogenesis","questions":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Among environmental exposures associated with rheumatoid arthritis, one factor is thought to promote disease initiation by activating peptidyl-arginine deiminase enzymes in lung tissue, driving citrullination of self-proteins and generating neoantigens that trigger ACPA formation years before joint symptoms appear. Which exposure best fits this description?","choices":{"A":"UV radiation exposure, which activates PAD4 in keratinocytes to citrullinate histones released during apoptosis, creating circulating citrullinated nucleosomal antigens that trigger ACPA before joint symptoms develop","B":"Gluten ingestion, which activates tissue transglutaminase in the synovial lining to convert arginine to citrulline on joint fibronectin and type II collagen, initiating ACPA formation in HLA-DR4 positive individuals","C":"Oral contraceptive use, which drives PAD2 expression in endometrial stromal cells and promotes systemic citrullination of fibrinogen, allowing ACPA to form through a hormonal mechanism distinct from smoking","D":"Low-dose aspirin therapy, which inhibits COX-2 in neutrophils and promotes extracellular PAD enzyme release into synovial fluid, initiating citrullination of joint proteins before clinical arthritis is detectable","E":"Cigarette smoking, which activates PAD enzymes in the lungs and promotes citrullination of proteins such as fibrinogen and type II collagen, generating neoantigens that drive ACPA production in genetically susceptible individuals"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cigarette smoking is the best-established environmental risk factor for RA. Smoking promotes PAD enzyme activation in the lungs, leading to citrullination of proteins including fibrinogen, vimentin, and type II collagen. These citrullinated neoantigens can trigger ACPA formation years before joint symptoms appear — a concept called the pre-clinical window of RA. Periodontitis via Porphyromonas gingivalis (which expresses its own PAD enzyme) is the other well-established environmental trigger. UV radiation predisposes to SLE rather than RA. Gluten activates tissue transglutaminase (relevant in celiac disease) which converts glutamine to glutamate, not arginine to citrulline.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Smoking + HLA-DR4 + PTPN22 = highest RA risk triad. Smoking drives citrullination via PAD in lungs. ACPA precedes joint symptoms by years. Periodontitis (P. gingivalis) is second major environmental trigger. COMLEX: smoking cessation is a therapeutic priority in RA; continued smoking worsens disease activity, reduces biologic efficacy, and increases cardiovascular risk already elevated in RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA-DR4 shared epitope and PTPN22 are the key genetic risk factors for RA identified in the autoimmunity SDL"},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The HLA-DR4 allele is associated with ACPA-positive RA. Evidence suggests that an epitope on citrullinated proteins can mimic epitopes on certain microbes and be presented by HLA-DR4 to CD4+ T cells. Which post-translational modification creates the citrullinated neoantigens recognized by ACPA?","choices":{"A":"Post-translational phosphorylation of serine residues on synovial fibronectin by protein kinases activated during macrophage NF-kB signaling, creating phosphopeptide neoantigens presented by HLA-DR4","B":"Post-translational conversion of arginine residues to citrulline by peptidyl-arginine deiminase enzymes on joint proteins including fibrinogen, type II collagen, alpha-enolase, and vimentin","C":"Post-translational deamidation of glutamine to glutamic acid on synovial type II collagen by tissue transglutaminase, producing deamidated collagen peptides structurally similar to citrullinated peptides","D":"Post-translational ubiquitination of lysine residues on joint structural proteins by E3 ubiquitin ligase complexes activated during synovial NF-kB signaling, creating ubiquitinated neoepitopes on collagen","E":"Post-translational acetylation of histone H3 lysine 27 by CBP/p300 acetyltransferases in synovial fibroblasts undergoing epigenetic remodeling during sustained inflammatory conditions in the synovium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Citrullination is the post-translational conversion of arginine to citrulline catalyzed by PAD enzymes (primarily PAD2 and PAD4). This removes a positive charge from arginine (positively charged guanidinium group converted to neutral citrulline), altering the shape and charge of proteins. Citrullinated forms of fibrinogen, type II collagen, alpha-enolase, and vimentin are the primary ACPA targets. HLA-DR4 binds citrullinated peptides with high affinity, driving CD4+ T cell activation. Deamidation by transglutaminase is relevant to celiac disease (gliadin). Phosphorylation and ubiquitination do not generate ACPA targets. Histone acetylation is an epigenetic modification with no known ACPA-generating role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACPA targets citrullinated proteins. Citrullination = arginine to citrulline via PAD enzymes. Key ACPA targets: fibrinogen, vimentin, alpha-enolase, type II collagen. HLA-DR4 shared epitope presents citrullinated peptides to CD4+ T cells. Molecular mimicry: citrullinated protein epitopes resemble certain microbial antigens. COMLEX: ACPA can be positive years before clinical RA; use anti-CCP test (second-generation ACPA assay) for diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA-DR4 shared epitope and PTPN22 are the key genetic risk factors for RA identified in the autoimmunity SDL"},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Rheumatoid arthritis has a prevalence of 0.25% to 1% in the United States and peaks in the third to fourth decades, with approximately 50% of disease risk attributed to genetic factors. It is three times more common in females than males. Which statement best characterizes the genetic and environmental interaction in RA pathogenesis?","choices":{"A":"RA is a single-gene disorder caused by HLA-DR4 homozygosity alone, which is sufficient to cause the disease without any environmental cofactors given that genetic factors account for 50% of disease risk","B":"The female predominance in RA reflects X-chromosome inactivation skewing that silences the FOXP3 locus preferentially in females, leading to Treg deficiency that unmasks citrullinated joint antigens","C":"RA is a polygenic, multifactorial disorder in which genetic susceptibility from HLA-DR4 shared epitope and PTPN22 variants interacts with environmental triggers such as smoking and periodontal infection to initiate autoimmunity against citrullinated self-proteins","D":"The 50% genetic contribution to RA risk is accounted for entirely by HLA-DR4, making non-HLA genes such as PTPN22 statistically insignificant compared to the dominant MHC class II contribution","E":"RA develops only in individuals who are compound heterozygous for HLA-DR4 and HLA-DR1 in the shared epitope alleles; single HLA-DR4 homozygosity alone has lower RA risk than compound heterozygosity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"RA is a polygenic disorder. Genetic factors (primarily HLA-DR4 shared epitope, PTPN22, and other loci) account for approximately 50% of RA risk. Environmental factors — especially cigarette smoking (activates PAD, promotes citrullination), periodontal infection (P. gingivalis PAD enzyme), and possibly other infections — account for the remainder. Neither genetics nor environment alone is sufficient; both are required. The female predominance is real (3:1) but reflects hormonal influences on immunity (estrogen drives B cell activation, Th2 responses) rather than FOXP3 silencing. HLA-DR4 is the dominant genetic risk factor but non-HLA genes including PTPN22 make statistically significant independent contributions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 50% RA risk = genetic (HLA-DR4 + PTPN22 + other loci). 50% = environmental (smoking + periodontitis + hormones). Female predominance 3:1. Peak onset 3rd-4th decade. COMLEX: RA increases cardiovascular risk 2-3x beyond traditional risk factors; this excess risk is partially mediated by systemic inflammation and should be screened and treated aggressively.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA-DR4 shared epitope and PTPN22 are the key genetic risk factors for RA identified in the autoimmunity SDL"},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — List the pro-inflammatory cytokines involved in rheumatoid inflammation and their contribution to the disease process.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Based on the dramatic clinical response to TNF antagonists, TNF is considered a key mediator of inflammation and joint damage in RA. Which best describes the primary cellular source of TNF in the inflamed RA synovium and its primary mechanism of articular cartilage destruction?","choices":{"A":"Synovial macrophages are the primary TNF source; TNF recruits activated leukocytes and stimulates synovial fibroblasts to secrete matrix metalloproteinases that degrade hyaline cartilage and destroy the joint","B":"CD4+ Th17 cells are the primary TNF producers in RA synovium; TNF recruits neutrophils and monocytes through CXCR1 and CXCR2 receptor signaling on vascular endothelium within the synovial membrane","C":"Regulatory T cells in synovial germinal centers produce TNF as a pro-survival signal for ACPA-secreting plasma cells; TNF acts directly on chondrocytes to induce autophagy-mediated cartilage matrix degradation","D":"Endothelial cells lining synovial capillaries secrete TNF in response to mechanical shear stress; TNF signals NK cells to upregulate RANKL expression, driving subchondral osteoclast activation and bone resorption","E":"B cells in subsynovial germinal centers are the predominant producers of TNF; TNF acts on dendritic cells to upregulate MHC II and CD80/CD86 co-stimulatory molecule expression for T cell activation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"TNF is produced primarily by synovial macrophages in RA and acts via multiple downstream mechanisms: (1) recruiting activated leukocytes to the joint through upregulation of adhesion molecules on endothelium; (2) stimulating synovial fibroblasts (type B synoviocytes) to secrete MMPs including MMP-1, MMP-3, and MMP-13 that degrade type II collagen and aggrecan; (3) inducing IL-6 production from fibroblasts, amplifying the inflammatory cascade. IL-17 from Th17 cells (not TNF) primarily recruits neutrophils. RANKL from T cells (not TNF) drives osteoclast activation. The therapeutic efficacy of etanercept, infliximab, adalimumab, and similar agents confirms TNF centrality.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF from macrophages = central RA cytokine. Mechanism: recruits leukocytes + stimulates MMP secretion from fibroblasts + cartilage destruction. Biologics: etanercept (fusion protein), infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab, golimumab. TNF antagonism risk: TB reactivation (screen with TST/IGRA before starting). COMLEX: TB reactivation risk with anti-TNF therapy — must treat latent TB before initiating.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 cytokines covered in SDL 35 drive rheumatoid synovial inflammation and cartilage destruction"},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — List the pro-inflammatory cytokines involved in rheumatoid inflammation and their contribution to the disease process.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Periarticular bone erosion is a radiographic hallmark of RA and results from osteoclast activation by a ligand expressed on activated T cells in the inflamed synovium. Which molecule is this ligand, and what receptor on osteoclast precursors does it engage?","choices":{"A":"IFN-gamma expressed on activated Th1 cells binds IFN-gamma receptor on pre-osteoclasts and inhibits osteoclast differentiation; this explains why RA patients on IFN-gamma therapy paradoxically have worse bone erosion","B":"IL-17A expressed on activated Th17 cells binds IL-17RA on osteoclast precursors and directly drives their differentiation into mature bone-resorbing osteoclasts through STAT3-dependent NF-kB activation","C":"TRAIL (TNF-related apoptosis-inducing ligand) expressed on activated NK cells in the synovium binds DR4 and DR5 on osteoclast precursors and triggers their apoptosis, paradoxically reducing bone erosion","D":"RANKL (receptor activator of NF-kB ligand) expressed on activated T cells binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, driving their differentiation into mature multinucleated osteoclasts that cause periarticular erosions","E":"M-CSF (macrophage colony-stimulating factor) expressed on activated B cells in synovial germinal centers binds c-Fms on osteoclast precursors, driving their proliferation before RANKL-independent differentiation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"RANKL (also called TRANCE, OPGL, or ODF) is expressed on activated T cells and synovial fibroblasts in the RA pannus. It binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, activating NF-kB and c-Fos signaling that drives osteoclastogenesis. OPG (osteoprotegerin) acts as a decoy receptor that normally prevents osteoclast overactivation, but this balance is disrupted in RA. IFN-gamma actually inhibits osteoclast differentiation (paradoxical finding). IL-17 promotes neutrophil/monocyte recruitment rather than directly driving osteoclast differentiation. Denosumab (anti-RANKL antibody) is approved for osteoporosis and reduces bone erosions in RA when added to methotrexate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RANKL on T cells/fibroblasts + RANK on osteoclast precursors = bone erosion in RA. OPG (osteoprotegerin) = decoy receptor that blocks this interaction. Denosumab = anti-RANKL = approved for osteoporosis + reduces RA erosions. Board trap: IFN-gamma inhibits osteoclast differentiation despite being pro-inflammatory overall. COMLEX: osteoporosis is a major comorbidity in RA due to both systemic inflammation and corticosteroid use.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 cytokines covered in SDL 35 drive rheumatoid synovial inflammation and cartilage destruction"},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — List the pro-inflammatory cytokines involved in rheumatoid inflammation and their contribution to the disease process.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"CD4+ T helper cells in the RA synovium are polarized into distinct subsets that produce different cytokines with different inflammatory consequences. Which cell type is the primary source of IL-17 in RA, and what is the primary cellular target and functional result of IL-17 signaling in joint inflammation?","choices":{"A":"CD4+ Th1 cells are the primary IL-17 producers in RA synovium; IL-17 acts on chondrocytes to upregulate aggrecanase and MMP-13 expression, producing direct articular cartilage degradation without leukocyte recruitment","B":"CD4+ Th17 cells are the primary IL-17 producers; IL-17 recruits neutrophils and monocytes into the joint by upregulating CXCL8 and CCL2 on synovial fibroblasts and endothelial cells, amplifying leukocyte influx","C":"Synovial mast cells produce IL-17 after FcεRI cross-linking by IgE anti-citrullinated protein antibodies; IL-17 then activates synovial macrophages to produce TNF and IL-1 in a mast cell-dependent cascade","D":"FOXP3+ regulatory T cells paradoxically produce IL-17 in the inflamed RA synovium due to phenotypic conversion driven by elevated IL-6 and TGF-beta, switching Tregs into pathogenic Th17-like effector cells","E":"NK cells are the dominant source of IL-17 in RA synovium; IL-17 acts on subsynovial B cells to promote class switching from IgM to IgG4, reducing complement-fixing capacity and immune complex pathology"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Th17 cells are a distinct CD4+ T cell subset that produces IL-17A and IL-17F in response to IL-6 + TGF-beta + IL-23 (for maintenance). In RA synovium, IL-17 acts on synovial fibroblasts and endothelial cells to upregulate CXCL8 (IL-8, neutrophil chemokine) and CCL2 (monocyte chemoattractant), amplifying leukocyte recruitment. IL-17 also promotes osteoclastogenesis and MMP production. Th1 cells are not the primary IL-17 source — they produce IFN-gamma. Tregs can lose FOXP3 expression in inflamed environments with high IL-6, but this is a minor contributor. Mast cells are not the primary IL-17 producers in RA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Th17 + IL-17 = recruit neutrophils and monocytes to RA joint. Th1 + IFN-gamma = activate macrophages. Macrophages + TNF/IL-1/IL-6 = MMP secretion and cartilage destruction. RANKL on T cells = osteoclast activation. Secukinumab/ixekizumab = anti-IL-17A biologics (used more in psoriatic arthritis/AS than RA). COMLEX: IL-17 plays an even larger role in psoriasis and spondyloarthritis than in RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 cytokines covered in SDL 35 drive rheumatoid synovial inflammation and cartilage destruction"},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the structure of the antibodies found in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"IgM and IgA autoantibodies present in approximately 80% of RA patients collectively referred to as rheumatoid factor target a specific structural region of IgG. Which statement best describes the molecular target and structural properties of IgM rheumatoid factor?","choices":{"A":"IgM rheumatoid factor is a pentameric antibody that binds the Fc region of IgG molecules, forming high-molecular-weight immune complexes that can fix complement and deposit in synovial tissue","B":"IgM rheumatoid factor is a dimeric antibody that binds the variable region (Fab) of IgG, blocking antigen recognition and preventing ACPA from binding citrullinated peptides in the joint space","C":"IgM rheumatoid factor is a monomeric antibody that binds the J chain of secretory IgA in mucosal tissues, forming complexes that activate toll-like receptors on synovial macrophages via HMGB1 signaling","D":"IgM rheumatoid factor is a pentameric antibody that targets the variable domain of IgE molecules, blocking FcεRI binding on mast cells and reducing histamine-mediated synovial vascular permeability in RA joints","E":"IgM rheumatoid factor binds the hinge region of IgM molecules rather than IgG, forming layered immune complexes that uniquely deposit in periarticular subchondral bone rather than the synovial membrane"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Rheumatoid factor is defined as an IgM (or IgA or IgG) antibody that binds the Fc region of IgG. IgM RF forms pentameric structures due to the intrinsic pentameric organization of IgM, making it highly effective at forming large immune complexes. These RF-IgG complexes can fix complement via the classical pathway (C1q binds the Fc region of complexed IgG) and deposit in the synovium, triggering inflammation. The Fc region of IgG (not the Fab, hinge, IgM J chain, or IgE) is the specific target. RF was historically defined as the antibody detected by the latex fixation test, which relies on RF-IgG interaction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RF = IgM (primarily) anti-Fc of IgG. IgM = pentamer = 10 antigen-binding sites = very efficient at forming immune complexes. Detected by latex fixation test or nephelometry. Normal RF titer < 14 IU/mL. Present in 70-80% of RA but also in Sjogren, SLE, healthy elderly, SBE, TB. COMLEX: high-titer RF is associated with extra-articular manifestations of RA including rheumatoid nodules, vasculitis, and Felty syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — IgM rheumatoid factor targets the Fc region of IgG; antibody class structure determines RF pathogenicity and immune complex formation"},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the structure of the antibodies found in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies are detected in up to 70% of RA patients and target arginine-to-citrulline modified epitopes on several joint proteins. Which list best identifies the proteins whose citrullinated forms serve as the primary ACPA targets in the RA synovium?","choices":{"A":"Hyaluronic acid, type IV collagen, laminin, and fibronectin, which are citrullinated by PAD2 on the surface of endothelial cells during angiogenesis in the inflamed synovium of RA patients","B":"Glycosaminoglycans, aggrecan, versican, and decorin, which are citrullinated by PAD4 secreted by activated neutrophils as part of neutrophil extracellular trap formation in the joint space","C":"Synapsin-1, tau protein, myelin basic protein, and neurofilament light chain, which are citrullinated by PAD6 in perisynovial nerve endings during nociceptive sensitization in chronic RA pain","D":"IgG1 Fc region, complement C3b, fibrin, and MMP-13, which are citrullinated by PAD1 released during lysosomal exocytosis in activated macrophages during the initial innate immune response to joint injury","E":"Fibrinogen, type II collagen, alpha-enolase, and vimentin, which are citrullinated by PAD enzymes and serve as the primary protein targets of ACPA; these are all abundant in inflamed synovial tissue"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The four canonical ACPA targets are fibrinogen, type II collagen, alpha-enolase, and vimentin — all proteins abundant in synovial tissue that become citrullinated by PAD2 and PAD4 during inflammation. Fibrinogen citrullination is particularly important because fibrin deposits are a major component of the synovial exudate in RA. Type II collagen is the main structural protein of articular cartilage. Alpha-enolase (an intracellular glycolytic enzyme) becomes accessible when cells undergo apoptosis. Vimentin is an intermediate filament citrullinated during cell death. None of the other options lists the canonical ACPA targets — glycosaminoglycans, myelin proteins, and complement components are not primary ACPA targets.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACPA targets citrullinated: fibrinogen, vimentin, alpha-enolase, type II collagen. PAD2 and PAD4 are the key enzymes. Anti-CCP (anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide) is the clinical ACPA test using a synthetic cyclic citrullinated peptide as the antigen. Sensitivity 60-70%, specificity >95% for RA. COMLEX: ACPA is positive years before joint symptoms in high-risk individuals, making it useful for early identification.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — IgM rheumatoid factor targets the Fc region of IgG; antibody class structure determines RF pathogenicity and immune complex formation"},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the structure of the antibodies found in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Rheumatoid factor is present in approximately 80% of individuals with RA. Which statement best describes the diagnostic limitations of rheumatoid factor as a test for RA?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid factor has greater specificity than ACPA for RA because RF targets joint-specific citrullinated peptides on fibrinogen, making it highly positive only when synovial PAD enzymes are actively producing citrullinated antigens","B":"Rheumatoid factor is present exclusively in seropositive RA and is never detected in healthy individuals, healthy elderly, or patients with other autoimmune diseases or systemic infections","C":"Rheumatoid factor lacks specificity for RA because it can also be detected in Sjogren syndrome, SLE, chronic infections including subacute bacterial endocarditis and tuberculosis, and in healthy elderly individuals","D":"Rheumatoid factor is more specific than ACPA for RA because IgM RF targets only the Fc region of IgG found in inflamed joint tissue rather than ubiquitous citrullinated proteins distributed throughout the body","E":"A negative rheumatoid factor definitively excludes RA because the 80% sensitivity means that only a trivial minority of patients with RA are seronegative, making seronegative RA a misclassification rather than a true phenotype"},"correct":"C","explanation":"RF lacks specificity for RA — it targets the Fc region of IgG, which is a non-organ-specific structure. RF is found in approximately 5% of healthy adults (rising to 10-25% in elderly), 70-90% of Sjogren syndrome patients, 40% of SLE patients, and patients with subacute bacterial endocarditis, hepatitis B/C, tuberculosis, and other chronic infections. RF detection in conditions without RA is explained by the fact that B cells can generate anti-IgG responses during any chronic immune activation that produces large amounts of IgG. ACPA (>95% specific) is far more specific than RF (70-80% sensitive but only 60-80% specific) for RA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RF = 70-80% sensitive, 60-80% specific for RA. Non-RA conditions with positive RF: Sjogren (70-90%), SLE (40%), SBE, viral hepatitis, TB, healthy elderly (5-25%). ACPA (anti-CCP) = 60-70% sensitive, >95% specific. High-titer RF (>1:160) is more specific. Remember: 20% of RA is seronegative (RF and ACPA negative). COMLEX: seronegative RA diagnosis requires clinical and radiographic criteria; joint aspiration with synovial fluid analysis may help confirm inflammatory arthritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — IgM rheumatoid factor targets the Fc region of IgG; antibody class structure determines RF pathogenicity and immune complex formation"},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between the non-specific antibodies (seen in those with and without RA) and more specific antibodies which may drive disease persistence.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Among the autoantibodies associated with RA, rheumatoid factor is considered a non-specific marker. Which best illustrates the non-specificity of RF as a diagnostic test for RA?","choices":{"A":"RF is detected only in RA patients who are HLA-DR4 positive with the shared epitope, making it specific for the seropositive erosive RA subtype and confirming joint-specific antigen targeting by autoreactive B cells","B":"RF can be detected in approximately 5-10% of healthy adults, rising to 10-25% of adults over 65, and is also present in patients with Sjogren syndrome, SLE, subacute bacterial endocarditis, and tuberculosis","C":"RF is a universal marker of any joint inflammation regardless of etiology, being detected at equal titers in both RA and osteoarthritis as a general quantitative indicator of synovitis severity in inflammatory joint disease","D":"RF specificity for RA is undermined only by its detection in fibromyalgia and chronic fatigue syndrome, while it otherwise remains a highly specific marker of true inflammatory arthritis with synovial pannus formation","E":"RF is specific for seropositive RA because its formation requires the HLA-DR4 shared epitope to present IgG Fc-derived peptides to CD4+ T cells in synovial germinal centers during active disease flares"},"correct":"B","explanation":"RF is non-specific because IgG Fc regions are structurally conserved and any B cell can be driven to produce anti-IgG antibodies during chronic immune activation. RF is found in: ~5-10% of healthy adults (25% in elderly), 70-90% of Sjogren syndrome patients, 40% of SLE patients, and various infectious conditions (SBE, hepatitis B/C, TB, leprosy). In contrast, ACPA (>95% specific) is rarely positive in non-RA conditions because citrullinated proteins in the specific configuration recognized by ACPA are predominantly produced in the RA synovium. RF is NOT restricted to HLA-DR4 positive patients and is found at equal titers in OA and healthy individuals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RF non-specific > ACPA specific. Non-RA RF conditions: SASHES = Sjogren, AIDS, SBE, Hepatitis, Elderly, SLE. Mnemonic for ACPA: specific for RA, predicts erosive disease, associated with HLA-DR4 shared epitope. COMLEX: in a patient with positive RF but ambiguous joint symptoms, order ACPA (anti-CCP) to improve diagnostic specificity before confirming RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — B cell activation and germinal center formation in the synovium produce the RA-specific autoantibodies covered in SDL 28"},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between the non-specific antibodies (seen in those with and without RA) and more specific antibodies which may drive disease persistence.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement best describes the clinical significance of ACPA compared with RF in terms of diagnostic specificity and potential role in driving disease persistence in RA?","choices":{"A":"ACPA and RF have equivalent diagnostic sensitivity and specificity for RA; neither contributes to disease persistence because both are epiphenomena of B cell activation rather than serving as pathogenic mediators","B":"ACPA has lower specificity than RF for RA because ACPA targets ubiquitous citrullinated proteins found in muscle, skin, and lung, producing false positives in interstitial lung disease and dermatomyositis patients","C":"RF has greater specificity than ACPA for RA diagnosis and has a stronger association with HLA-DR4 shared epitope, predicting erosive disease and extra-articular complications better than ACPA in seropositive patients","D":"ACPA has greater specificity than RF for RA and, in combination with T cells reactive to citrullinated proteins, ACPA may actively contribute to disease severity and persistence rather than serving only as a passive biomarker","E":"ACPA and RF together have combined specificity approaching 100% for RA, making the simultaneous order of both tests sufficient to diagnose RA without clinical examination or radiographic evaluation of joints"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ACPA has greater than 95% specificity for RA, compared with RF at 60-80% specificity. More importantly, ACPA is not merely a biomarker — data suggest that in combination with T cells reactive to citrullinated proteins, ACPA may contribute to disease severity and persistence through FcgammaR engagement on macrophages, complement activation, and possibly direct effects on osteoclasts. This pathogenic role of ACPA distinguishes it from RF, which deposits in joints as immune complexes but has less evidence for independent pathogenicity. ACPA positivity also predicts more erosive disease course, extra-articular manifestations, and reduced likelihood of spontaneous remission.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACPA (anti-CCP) = most specific RA antibody (>95%). RF = more sensitive (70-80%) but less specific (60-80%). ACPA precedes symptoms by years. ACPA+ = worse prognosis (more erosive). Both positive together = highest diagnostic certainty. COMLEX: ACPA is measured as anti-CCP in clinical practice; the cyclic citrullinated peptide substrate increases sensitivity vs. linear peptides. High-titer ACPA predicts need for early aggressive DMARD therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — B cell activation and germinal center formation in the synovium produce the RA-specific autoantibodies covered in SDL 28"},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between the non-specific antibodies (seen in those with and without RA) and more specific antibodies which may drive disease persistence.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Approximately 20% of RA patients are seronegative for both RF and ACPA. Which statement best describes the clinical characteristics and prognosis of seronegative RA compared to seropositive RA?","choices":{"A":"Seronegative RA is uniformly more severe than seropositive RA because the absence of autoantibodies indicates a more aggressive T cell-dominant pathogenesis with higher rates of joint ankylosis and disability","B":"Seronegative RA is characterized by markedly elevated complement levels because the absence of immune complex formation prevents classical pathway complement consumption in the joint and synovial tissue","C":"Seronegative RA patients are more likely to have disease that stabilizes or regresses spontaneously, and in these patients the diagnosis is based on clinical and radiographic findings rather than serologic testing","D":"Seronegative RA is always associated with HLA-B27 positivity because the absence of ACPA and RF indicates the disease is actually a spondyloarthropathy rather than true primary inflammatory arthritis","E":"Seronegative RA is defined by the absence of synovial inflammation on biopsy and represents a non-destructive arthralgia without pannus formation or the periarticular erosions characteristic of seropositive disease"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Seronegative RA (negative for both RF and ACPA) represents approximately 20% of RA cases and has a generally more favorable prognosis — these patients are more likely to have disease that stabilizes or even regresses spontaneously compared with seropositive patients. Diagnosis relies on clinical criteria: symmetric inflammatory arthritis with morning stiffness, appropriate joint distribution (MCP, PIP, wrist, ankle), elevated inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP), and radiographic evidence of erosive joint disease. Seronegative RA is NOT the same as spondyloarthropathy (which has distinct features: axial involvement, HLA-B27, enthesitis). Seronegative RA still has synovial inflammation and pannus histologically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Seronegative RA = 20% of cases, RF negative + ACPA negative. Prognosis: better chance of spontaneous remission/stabilization. Diagnosis: clinical + radiographic (not serologic). Seronegative does NOT mean HLA-B27 positive or spondyloarthropathy. COMLEX: seronegative inflammatory arthritis with axial involvement, sacroiliitis, or enthesitis should prompt workup for spondyloarthritis rather than RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — B cell activation and germinal center formation in the synovium produce the RA-specific autoantibodies covered in SDL 28"},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old male smoker is evaluated for a 6-month history of symmetric MCP and PIP joint swelling with 90-minute morning stiffness. He has a 10-year history of chronic periodontitis. ACPA is positive at 480 U/mL. HLA-DR4 genotyping is positive. Which statement best explains how smoking and periodontitis synergize to initiate RA in this genetically susceptible individual?","choices":{"A":"Both smoking and periodontitis activate the alternative complement pathway in synovial tissue, generating C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins that recruit mast cells and initiate synovitis independent of ACPA or T cell activation","B":"Both smoking and periodontitis promote post-translational citrullination via PAD enzyme activation — smoking in lung tissue and P. gingivalis PAD in oral tissue — generating citrullinated neoantigens that are presented by HLA-DR4 to drive ACPA production","C":"Cigarette smoking directly inhibits regulatory T cells in cervical lymph nodes draining periodontal tissue, allowing autoreactive B cells specific for synovial type II collagen to escape peripheral tolerance checkpoints","D":"Periodontitis generates IgM antibodies against P. gingivalis that cross-react with the Fc region of synovial IgG through molecular mimicry, generating a secondary rheumatoid factor in susceptible HLA-DR4 positive individuals","E":"Both smoking and periodontitis upregulate TLR4 signaling in synovial macrophages via circulating LPS, inducing MyD88-NF-kB activation and constitutive TNF production that is entirely independent of T cell or B cell involvement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Smoking activates PAD enzymes in bronchial and alveolar cells, promoting widespread protein citrullination and ACPA formation years before joint symptoms. Porphyromonas gingivalis, the dominant organism in chronic periodontitis, is unique among human-associated bacteria in expressing its own PAD enzyme (PPAD) that can citrullinate both bacterial and host proteins. Both exposures thus generate citrullinated protein neoantigens. When these neoantigens are presented by HLA-DR4 (which has a peptide-binding groove particularly suited to citrullinated peptides), they activate CD4+ T cells and drive B cell ACPA class switching. The synergy between multiple PAD-activating environmental exposures plus HLA-DR4 explains this patient's high ACPA titer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two canonical environmental triggers for RA citrullination: (1) cigarette smoking (PAD in lung), (2) P. gingivalis periodontitis (PPAD bacterial PAD). Both generate citrullinated neoantigens presented by HLA-DR4. ACPA formation precedes arthritis by 3-10 years (pre-clinical window). COMLEX: periodontal disease screening and treatment is recommended in RA patients; treatment of periodontitis may reduce RA disease activity in some studies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases"},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with 8-year RA history presents for follow-up. Wrist radiographs show periarticular erosions, narrowed joint space, and juxtaarticular osteopenia. Synovial biopsy of the wrist joint shows edematous, hyperplastic tissue with lymphoid follicles, plasma cells, fibrin-rich exudate with neutrophils on the surface, and abundant new vessel formation. What is the term for the pathologic structure formed by these combined changes?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid nodule, a subcutaneous mass with central fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by a rim of palisaded macrophages and lymphocytes, most commonly found over extensor surfaces of the forearm and elbows","B":"Tophus, a mass composed of monosodium urate crystals surrounded by a giant cell inflammatory reaction found in periarticular soft tissue, bursae, and tendon sheaths in patients with chronic gout","C":"Osteophyte, a bony outgrowth at joint margins formed by endochondral ossification driven by TGF-beta and BMP signaling from subchondral bone during mechanical stress in osteoarthritis","D":"Pannus, a mass of edematous synovium, inflammatory cells, granulation tissue, and fibroblasts that grows over articular cartilage causing progressive erosion and eventually leading to fibrous or bony ankylosis","E":"Fibrous ankylosis, the end-stage fibrotic bridge between apposing bone surfaces after complete cartilage destruction in late RA, representing the terminal consequence of pannus erosion rather than its active form"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pannus is the pathognomonic lesion of RA: a proliferative mass of edematous synovium, inflammatory cells (CD4+ T cells, B cells with germinal centers, plasma cells, macrophages), granulation tissue, and synovial fibroblasts. It grows over and erodes articular cartilage through MMP secretion and direct cellular invasion. Neutrophilic fibrin exudate on the surface and angiogenesis are also hallmarks. The pannus eventually bridges the joint space to form fibrous ankylosis, which ossifies into bony ankylosis. Rheumatoid nodules are separate subcutaneous lesions (central fibrinoid necrosis + palisaded macrophages). Tophi are urate deposits in gout. Osteophytes are OA-specific bony outgrowths.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pannus = proliferative synovium + inflammatory cells + granulation tissue + fibroblasts that erodes cartilage. RA histology: (1) synovial cell hyperplasia, (2) dense CD4+ T cell/B cell/plasma cell infiltrate with germinal centers, (3) angiogenesis, (4) neutrophilic fibrin exudate on surface, (5) osteoclastic subchondral bone erosion. Board distinction: pannus erodes from the joint edge inward in RA; OA degenerates from the weight-bearing surface downward. COMLEX: pannus formation correlates with elevated ESR/CRP and positive ACPA titer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases"},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the genetic and environmental factors contributing to the development of rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman presents with symmetric swelling and tenderness of multiple joints of both hands, 90-minute morning stiffness, elevated ESR, and positive ACPA. Radiographs show periarticular erosions at the wrists. Which joint distribution in the hand characteristically distinguishes RA from osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"RA primarily affects the distal interphalangeal joints and first carpometacarpal joint with Bouchard nodes, while OA affects the MCP joints and wrists with morning stiffness lasting over 60 minutes","B":"RA produces asymmetric monoarticular or oligoarticular involvement of the knee and hip, while OA produces symmetric polyarticular involvement of all finger joints including MCP, PIP, and DIP joints equally","C":"RA preferentially involves the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints while sparing the distal interphalangeal joints, while OA preferentially affects the DIP joints and the first carpometacarpal joint","D":"RA and OA have identical joint distribution in the hand; the two conditions are distinguished only by duration of morning stiffness (greater than 60 minutes in RA versus less than 30 minutes in OA) and inflammatory markers","E":"RA characteristically begins in the large joints of the axial skeleton including the spine and sacroiliac joints before progressing distally to the small joints of the hands and feet over a period of years"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The joint distribution in the hand is a classic board-tested distinguishing feature: RA involves MCP and PIP joints (and spares DIP joints) + wrists, ankles, and elbows in a symmetric pattern. OA involves DIP joints (producing Heberden nodes) and PIP joints (Bouchard nodes) + first CMC (base of thumb) + first MTP. Both can involve the knee and hip. In RA, the MCP involvement leads to characteristic ulnar deviation; PIP involvement contributes to swan-neck and boutonniere deformities. The DIP sparing in RA is a distinguishing board feature. Morning stiffness >60 minutes supports RA (OA morning stiffness typically <30 min and improves with use).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RA = MCP + PIP + wrist (DIP spared). OA = DIP + PIP + first CMC (MCP less affected). Both can affect knee and hip but by different mechanisms. Ulnar deviation = RA. Heberden nodes (DIP) + Bouchard nodes (PIP) = OA. COMLEX: morning stiffness in RA improves with use (activity reduces synovial viscosity); in OA it worsens with use. This symptom pattern helps differentiate inflammatory from non-inflammatory arthritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Describe the roles of specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases"},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — List the pro-inflammatory cytokines involved in rheumatoid inflammation and their contribution to the disease process.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with moderate-to-severe RA is starting etanercept therapy. Pre-treatment tuberculin skin test shows 18 mm induration. Her rheumatologist explains the dual role of TNF in RA pathogenesis and TB immunity. Which best explains why TNF blockade both treats RA and increases risk of TB reactivation?","choices":{"A":"TNF is essential both for driving synovial macrophage activation and cartilage destruction in RA and for maintaining granuloma integrity around latent Mycobacterium tuberculosis; TNF blockade reduces joint inflammation and simultaneously impairs macrophage containment of mycobacteria","B":"TNF antagonists reduce RANKL expression on T cells in the synovium; this also reduces macrophage differentiation from monocyte precursors in the bone marrow, eliminating the cellular basis of granuloma formation in lymph nodes","C":"TNF blockade directly inhibits IL-12 production by macrophages, preventing Th1 differentiation; without Th1 responses, IFN-gamma production is abolished and mycobacterial killing by activated macrophages is eliminated","D":"Etanercept binds both TNF and lymphotoxin-alpha; lymphotoxin-alpha is required for secondary lymphoid organ formation, so its blockade eliminates germinal centers and all memory B cell responses to M. tuberculosis antigens","E":"TNF antagonism eliminates NK cell cytotoxicity against M. tuberculosis-infected macrophages because NK cells require TNF as a survival signal to maintain perforin, granzyme B, and NKG2D receptor expression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"TNF plays a dual physiologic role that explains this paradox. In RA synovium: TNF from macrophages recruits leukocytes, activates synovial fibroblasts to secrete MMPs, and drives cartilage destruction — therefore blocking TNF reduces joint inflammation effectively. In latent TB granulomas: TNF is required to maintain the structural integrity of the macrophage granuloma that contains dormant M. tuberculosis. TNF blockade dissolves granuloma structure, allowing mycobacterial reactivation, dissemination, and development of active TB. This is why all patients must be screened for latent TB (with TST or IGRA) and treated with isoniazid prophylaxis for at least one month before starting anti-TNF therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Screen for latent TB before all TNF antagonists. Treat latent TB with isoniazid x 9 months (or rifampin x 4 months). Start biologic at least 1 month after beginning TB prophylaxis. Risk is highest with infliximab/adalimumab (monoclonal antibodies that fully block TNF) compared to etanercept (which also neutralizes lymphotoxin-alpha). COMLEX: disseminated TB from anti-TNF reactivation can present with extrapulmonary disease (miliary TB, TB meningitis) rather than classic pulmonary presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses"},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — List the pro-inflammatory cytokines involved in rheumatoid inflammation and their contribution to the disease process.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old male with RA has a synovial biopsy showing dense lymphoid follicles with CD4+ T cells, plasma cells, and activated macrophages with high MHC II and co-stimulatory molecule expression. Which cytokine produced by CD4+ T cells is primarily responsible for activating these macrophages and resident synovial cells in the biopsy?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 produced by CD4+ Th2 cells, which binds IL-4R on synovial macrophages and drives their differentiation toward an anti-inflammatory M2 phenotype that suppresses germinal center responses and reduces synovial inflammation","B":"IL-17A produced by CD4+ Th17 cells, which binds IL-17RA on synovial macrophages to upregulate CXCL8 and G-CSF expression, predominantly recruiting neutrophils rather than classically activating resident tissue macrophages","C":"IL-10 produced by CD4+ Treg cells, which binds IL-10R on synovial macrophages to suppress MHC II expression and co-stimulatory molecule upregulation, reducing rather than increasing T cell activation in the joint","D":"IFN-gamma produced by CD4+ Th1 cells, which activates synovial macrophages through the JAK1-STAT1 pathway, upregulating MHC II expression, co-stimulatory molecules CD80/CD86, and pro-inflammatory cytokine production","E":"TGF-beta produced by CD4+ Treg cells, which acts on synovial macrophages to induce M2 polarization and fibroblast activation, contributing to pannus fibrosis but suppressing rather than promoting the M1 inflammatory phenotype"},"correct":"D","explanation":"IFN-gamma is the prototypical macrophage-activating cytokine, produced predominantly by CD4+ Th1 cells (and CD8+ T cells and NK cells). In the RA synovium, IFN-gamma binds IFN-gamma receptor on macrophages, activating JAK1/JAK2-STAT1 signaling. This upregulates MHC II expression (amplifying antigen presentation), CD80/CD86 co-stimulatory molecules (amplifying T cell activation), and pro-inflammatory cytokine production (TNF, IL-1, IL-12). IL-4 drives M2 anti-inflammatory polarization (anti-RA direction). IL-17 recruits neutrophils/monocytes but does not classically activate macrophages. IL-10 suppresses macrophages. TGF-beta drives M2 polarization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IFN-gamma from Th1 = classical macrophage activator (M1 polarization). In RA synovium: Th1 (IFN-gamma activates macrophages) + Th17 (IL-17 recruits neutrophils) + macrophage TNF/IL-1/IL-6 (destroys cartilage) + T cell RANKL (erodes bone). IFN-gamma upregulates MHC II and co-stimulatory molecules, amplifying the antigen presentation cycle. COMLEX: IFN-gamma drives granuloma formation in TB and sarcoidosis, linking innate and adaptive immunity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses"},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — List the pro-inflammatory cytokines involved in rheumatoid inflammation and their contribution to the disease process.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with active RA has elevated ESR 85 mm/h, CRP 42 mg/L, thrombocytosis, elevated fibrinogen, and normocytic normochromic anemia. She is afebrile. Which cytokine is the primary driver of the systemic acute phase response including the elevated CRP, fibrinogen, and thrombocytosis in this patient?","choices":{"A":"IFN-gamma from Th1 cells, which acts on hepatocytes via STAT1 to upregulate fibrinogen, CRP, serum amyloid A, and hepcidin, and which also directly stimulates megakaryocytes to produce excess platelets causing thrombocytosis","B":"IL-17A from Th17 cells, which acts on hepatocytes via the STAT3 pathway to upregulate all acute phase reactant production including CRP, fibrinogen, complement C3, and thrombopoietin in RA patients with active disease","C":"RANKL expressed on activated T cells, which acts on hepatocytes via NF-kB to stimulate acute phase protein synthesis while simultaneously driving osteoclast activation and periarticular bone erosion in the inflamed joint","D":"TNF-alpha from synovial macrophages, which is the primary driver of the systemic acute phase response in RA by acting on hepatocytes via NF-kB to upregulate CRP, fibrinogen, serum amyloid A, and thrombopoietin directly","E":"IL-6 from synovial macrophages and fibroblasts, which acts on hepatocytes via the JAK1-STAT3 pathway to drive synthesis of acute phase proteins including CRP, fibrinogen, serum amyloid A, and hepcidin responsible for the anemia of chronic inflammation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IL-6 is the primary cytokine driving the systemic acute phase response in RA. Synovial macrophages and fibroblasts produce large amounts of IL-6 in the inflamed joint, which enters the circulation and acts on hepatocytes via JAK1/TYK2-STAT3 signaling to upregulate CRP (the most sensitive acute phase reactant), fibrinogen, serum amyloid A, complement C3, and hepcidin. Hepcidin decreases ferroportin expression on enterocytes and macrophages, trapping iron intracellularly and causing the normocytic normochromic anemia of chronic inflammation. IL-6 also acts on megakaryocytes to promote thrombopoietin production, explaining thrombocytosis. Tocilizumab (anti-IL-6R) normalizes CRP and ESR within days of infusion, confirming IL-6 as the primary acute phase driver.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-6 = main driver of acute phase response. IL-6 hepatic effects: CRP, fibrinogen, SAA, complement, hepcidin (anemia of chronic disease). Tocilizumab = anti-IL-6 receptor. CRP drops rapidly with tocilizumab (within 24-72 h). Anemia of chronic disease: hepcidin blocks iron release from macrophages and absorption from gut; treat by treating underlying RA. COMLEX: tocilizumab is also approved for cytokine release syndrome (CRS) in CAR-T therapy and COVID-19 pneumonia, reflecting IL-6 centrality across inflammatory conditions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Explain the functions of cytokines and their roles in regulating immune responses"},{"id":"sdl37_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the structure of the antibodies found in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman with long-standing seropositive RA has an RF titer of 1:640, bilateral subcutaneous nodules over the elbows, and mononeuritis multiplex. How do IgM RF pentamers mechanistically contribute to joint damage in RA?","choices":{"A":"IgM RF binds the variable (Fab) domain of IgG in synovial fluid, sterically blocking ACPA from accessing citrullinated fibrinogen, which paradoxically reduces joint inflammation by preventing ACPA-driven complement activation","B":"IgM RF is a monomeric antibody that binds the J chain of secretory IgA dimers in synovial fluid, activating the lectin complement pathway selectively at the synovial membrane rather than in the joint space","C":"IgM RF binds the hinge region of IgM molecules in synovial germinal center B cells, disrupting B cell receptor signaling and paradoxically reducing ACPA production by competing with BCR-bound IgG for B cell surface receptors","D":"IgM RF pentamers bind the Fc region of IgG, forming large immune complexes that activate the classical complement pathway via C1q binding, generating C3a, C5a, and membrane attack complex that promote leukocyte recruitment and lysosomal enzyme release in the joint","E":"IgM RF binds the CH1 domain of IgG heavy chains in circulation, disrupting FcRn-mediated IgG recycling and markedly reducing IgG half-life in patients with high RF titers and active RA disease"},"correct":"D","explanation":"IgM RF pentamers have 10 binding sites for IgG Fc regions, making them highly efficient at forming immune complexes. These RF-IgG complexes activate the classical complement pathway because C1q binds the Fc regions of aggregated IgG, generating C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins (which increase vascular permeability and recruit neutrophils/monocytes) and C5b-9 membrane attack complex. The deposited complexes in synovium trigger leukocyte activation and release of lysosomal enzymes including cathepsins and MMPs, amplifying joint destruction. High RF titers (>1:160) correlate with extra-articular RA manifestations including rheumatoid nodules (fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by macrophages), vasculitis, and Felty syndrome (RA + splenomegaly + neutropenia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgM RF pentamers + IgG Fc = large immune complexes + C1q binding + classical complement activation. Extra-articular RA with high-titer RF: rheumatoid nodules, vasculitis, Felty syndrome (RA + splenomegaly + neutropenia), pleuritis/pericarditis. Board association: high-titer RF + extra-articular disease = more aggressive RA needing earlier biologic therapy. COMLEX: rheumatoid nodules have central fibrinoid necrosis + palisaded macrophages; must distinguish from gouty tophi (urate crystals + giant cells).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 4 — Describe the major antibody classes"},{"id":"sdl37_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the structure of the antibodies found in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with RA has ACPA at 480 U/mL and elevated PAD4 enzyme levels in synovial fluid. Her rheumatologist explains that citrullination creates the neoantigens targeted by ACPA. Which best describes the biochemical reaction and enzyme responsible for generating the ACPA target antigen in the RA joint?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinogen lysine residues are converted to hydroxylysine by prolyl hydroxylase in the endoplasmic reticulum; the hydroxylated fibrinogen is then secreted into synovial fluid where it activates complement via C1q binding","B":"Peptidyl-arginine deiminase enzymes convert arginine residues to citrulline by hydrolyzing the guanidinium side chain, generating citrullinated neoepitopes on proteins such as fibrinogen and vimentin in inflamed joint tissue","C":"Matrix metalloproteinase-3 cleaves the fibrinogen D-domain into a 50 kDa fragment that exposes a cryptic arginine epitope recognized by ACPA without requiring any post-translational amino acid modification or PAD activity","D":"Transglutaminase 2 converts glutamine residues to glutamic acid on synovial type II collagen, producing deamidated collagen peptides structurally distinct from citrullinated peptides but recognized by the same ACPA B cell clones","E":"Cathepsin B in synovial macrophage lysosomes cleaves type II collagen at the MMP-cleavage site to expose a buried internal arginine sequence in the triple helix that is recognized by ACPA without citrullination of that residue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PAD enzymes (primarily PAD2 and PAD4) catalyze the hydrolysis of the guanidinium group of arginine residues to form citrulline, in a calcium-dependent reaction. This removes the positive charge of arginine and alters the protein structure, creating neoantigens not present in the normal protein. PAD4 is found in the nucleus and cytoplasm of neutrophils and monocytes; PAD2 is found in many tissues. The altered charge and shape of citrullinated residues allows them to fit differently into the HLA-DR4 peptide-binding groove compared to arginine-containing peptides. Prolyl hydroxylase converts proline to hydroxyproline (not lysine to hydroxylysine). MMP-3 degrades proteoglycans and fibronectin. Transglutaminase 2 is relevant to celiac disease (gliadin deamidation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Citrullination: PAD enzyme + arginine = citrulline (lost positive charge). PAD4 = found in neutrophil nuclei (involved in NETosis). Calcium required for PAD activation. ACPA binds citrullinated fibrinogen, vimentin, enolase, collagen. Anti-CCP test = ACPA against synthetic cyclic citrullinated peptide. COMLEX: elevated PAD4 in synovial fluid may eventually serve as a biomarker for RA disease activity and PAD4 inhibitors are in development as potential RA therapeutics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 4 — Describe the major antibody classes"},{"id":"sdl37_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the structure of the antibodies found in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with 15-year RA history presents with bilateral finger deformities. Examination of her right hand shows hyperextension at the PIP joints and flexion at the DIP joints. Which deformity is this, and what is the primary mechanism responsible for its development in RA?","choices":{"A":"Swan-neck deformity, characterized by PIP hyperextension and DIP flexion, resulting from FDS rupture or volar plate laxity at the PIP combined with extensor tendon tightening caused by chronic synovitis at the MCP and PIP joints","B":"Boutonniere deformity, characterized by PIP flexion and DIP hyperextension, caused by central slip rupture of the extensor tendon with volar displacement of lateral bands at the PIP joint after chronic synovitis","C":"Mallet deformity, characterized by terminal DIP flexion and inability to actively extend the fingertip, caused by rupture or avulsion of the terminal extensor tendon at the DIP joint insertion in chronic RA","D":"Trigger finger deformity, characterized by locking of the finger in flexion due to nodular thickening of the flexor tendon as it catches on the A1 pulley during finger flexion and extension in chronic RA","E":"Ulnar deviation deformity, in which the fingers deviate toward the ulnar side at the MCP joints due to erosion of the radial-sided collateral ligaments and joint capsule after chronic synovitis at the MCP joint"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Swan-neck deformity = PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion. Mechanism: RA-related synovitis causes FDS (flexor digitorum superficialis) rupture or volar plate laxity at the PIP, allowing extensor forces to dominate and hyperextend the PIP. Simultaneously, the DIP flexes. Boutonniere deformity = PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension. Mechanism: central slip of the extensor tendon ruptures over the PIP joint, allowing the PIP to \"buttonhole\" through the two lateral bands. The lateral bands slip volarly and now flex the PIP while hyperextending the DIP. Mallet finger = DIP terminal extensor tendon rupture = DIP flexion only. Ulnar deviation = MCP joint collateral ligament erosion. These deformities are caused by tendon rupture and ligament laxity secondary to chronic synovitis rather than by direct joint destruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Swan-neck = PIP hyper-EXT + DIP FLEX. Boutonniere = PIP FLEX + DIP hyper-EXT. Mnemonic: swan-neck looks like a swan (neck extended). Boutonniere = button in a hole (PIP flexed through lateral bands). Ulnar deviation = MCP involvement. COMLEX: all these deformities can be assessed on physical exam; knowing the mechanism helps predict which tendons/ligaments are affected and plan surgical vs. conservative intervention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 4 — Describe the major antibody classes"},{"id":"sdl37_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between the non-specific antibodies (seen in those with and without RA) and more specific antibodies which may drive disease persistence.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with symmetric polyarthritis and 90-minute morning stiffness has a positive RF at titer 1:80. Her physician explains that a positive RF alone does not confirm RA. Which additional antibody test would most increase diagnostic specificity for RA in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Antinuclear antibody (ANA) at a titer exceeding 1:320, which has 30% sensitivity in RA and poor specificity, being present in Sjogren syndrome, SLE, scleroderma, and 5% of healthy controls regardless of joint disease","B":"Anti-Smith (anti-Sm) antibody, which is highly specific for SLE at greater than 99% specificity and would instead suggest reclassification as SLE-associated arthritis rather than primary seropositive RA","C":"ACPA (anti-citrullinated protein antibody / anti-CCP), which has greater than 95% specificity for RA and, when positive in combination with RF, strongly supports RA diagnosis and predicts more erosive disease course","D":"Anti-Ro (SSA) antibody, which is found in 70% of Sjogren syndrome patients and 25% of SLE patients, and when present alongside RF would suggest a Sjogren-RA overlap syndrome rather than primary RA","E":"Anti-Jo-1 antibody, which is an antisynthetase antibody associated with inflammatory myopathy and interstitial lung disease, and is not a diagnostic marker for RA regardless of the presence of joint symptoms"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When RF is positive but the diagnosis remains uncertain, ACPA (anti-CCP) is the next test to order because of its high specificity (>95%) for RA. A positive ACPA alongside RF strongly supports RA diagnosis and indicates seropositive disease, which tends to be more erosive, have more extra-articular manifestations, and be less likely to remit spontaneously. ANA is non-specific (positive in many autoimmune conditions and healthy individuals). Anti-Sm is SLE-specific. Anti-Ro is Sjogren/SLE-associated. Anti-Jo-1 is myositis-associated. None of these other antibodies increase diagnostic certainty for RA — they would instead suggest alternative or overlapping autoimmune diagnoses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RF positive + ACPA positive = highest diagnostic certainty for RA. RF alone = non-specific (SASHES). ACPA alone = >95% specific. Combined = greater confidence. Anti-CCP is the clinical ACPA assay. Other antibodies that do NOT confirm RA: ANA (non-specific), anti-dsDNA (SLE), anti-Sm (SLE), anti-Ro/La (Sjogren/SLE), anti-Jo-1 (myositis). COMLEX: order ACPA before starting DMARD therapy; ACPA status influences prognosis, treatment intensity, and patient counseling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response"},{"id":"sdl37_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between the non-specific antibodies (seen in those with and without RA) and more specific antibodies which may drive disease persistence.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman is evaluated for bilateral knee pain. RF is positive at 1:40. She has no morning stiffness greater than 15 minutes, no synovial thickening, and no periarticular erosions on radiography. She has no history of other autoimmune disease or chronic infection. Which of the following best explains the positive RF in this patient without underlying RA?","choices":{"A":"Healthy aging, as RF is detectable in approximately 5-25% of adults over age 65 without any underlying inflammatory joint disease, reflecting age-related polyclonal B cell activation and decreased immune tolerance","B":"Osteoarthritis, in which mechanical cartilage degradation generates citrullinated fibrin fragments that stimulate RF-producing B cells in synovial tissue, causing secondary RF elevation during osteoarthritic joint flares","C":"Fibromyalgia, in which central sensitization of pain processing pathways upregulates B cell activation markers and drives polyclonal IgM production including RF through a neuro-immune cross-talk mechanism","D":"Vitamin D deficiency, in which reduced 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D levels specifically impair Treg function in synovial tissue, allowing polyclonal IgM B cell activation and low-titer RF production","E":"Hypothyroidism, in which reduced thyroid hormone levels directly activate follicular B cells to produce IgM autoantibodies including RF through a thyroid hormone receptor-mediated B cell differentiation pathway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"RF prevalence increases with age and can be detected in 5-25% of adults over age 65 without any underlying inflammatory disease. This reflects age-related polyclonal B cell activation (declining tolerance mechanisms, accumulation of memory B cells generating low-affinity IgM antibodies), and the general principle that many autoantibodies increase in prevalence in the elderly. A low-titer RF (1:40) in an elderly patient without clinical signs of synovitis is most likely an age-related finding rather than indicative of RA. OA does not stimulate citrullination or RF production through the mechanisms described. RF is not associated with fibromyalgia, vitamin D deficiency, or hypothyroidism as primary causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low-titer RF in elderly = likely age-related (5-25% prevalence), not diagnostic of RA. High-titer RF (>1:160) in the right clinical context is more specific. Always combine RF with clinical assessment: morning stiffness >60 min, symmetric synovitis, elevated ESR/CRP, periarticular erosions on X-ray. COMLEX: differentiate RA from osteoarthritis in elderly patients using the ACPA test and clinical examination rather than RF alone; OA does not elevate CRP significantly and has no periarticular erosions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response"},{"id":"sdl37_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between the non-specific antibodies (seen in those with and without RA) and more specific antibodies which may drive disease persistence.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old ACPA-positive RA patient with high disease activity continues to have progressive joint erosions despite anti-TNF therapy. ACPA titers remain consistently elevated. The rheumatologist explains that ACPA may not just be a biomarker but may actively perpetuate synovial inflammation. Which mechanism best explains how ACPA may contribute to disease persistence in RA?","choices":{"A":"ACPA directly activates the NLRP3 inflammasome in synovial macrophages by binding to crystalline citrullinated fibrinogen deposits, triggering caspase-1 activation and IL-1beta secretion independent of immune complex formation","B":"ACPA binds FcgammaRIIB on regulatory B cells, delivering an inhibitory signal that prevents IL-10 secretion and eliminates regulatory B cell suppression of pathogenic Th17 cells in the synovial germinal centers","C":"ACPA activates complement exclusively via the alternative pathway by binding citrullinated histone H3 on neutrophil extracellular traps, generating C5a that recruits neutrophils into the joint in a B cell-independent manner","D":"ACPA directly drives osteoclast differentiation by binding RANK on osteoclast precursors and mimicking RANKL signaling, inducing periarticular bone erosion entirely independent of T cell RANKL expression or macrophage involvement","E":"ACPA, in combination with T cells reactive to citrullinated proteins, may contribute to disease severity and persistence by activating macrophages and complement via Fc-gamma receptor engagement and immune complex formation, perpetuating the synovial inflammatory state"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ACPA is not merely a passive biomarker — growing evidence supports a pathogenic role. ACPA IgG engages FcgammaR on macrophages, neutrophils, and dendritic cells, activating them to produce TNF, IL-1, and IL-6. ACPA also fixes complement via the classical pathway (C1q binds aggregated IgG Fc), generating C3a and C5a that recruit and activate further leukocytes. When combined with T cell responses to citrullinated proteins, this creates a self-sustaining inflammatory loop. The persistence of ACPA despite TNF blockade (which does not deplete B cells or plasma cells) explains why anti-TNF therapy controls but does not cure RA. Rituximab (anti-CD20, depletes B cells and ACPA-producing plasma cells) is effective in ACPA-positive patients resistant to anti-TNF.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACPA pathogenicity: FcgammaR engagement on macrophages + complement fixation + T cell synergy = persistent synovial inflammation. Rituximab (anti-CD20) depletes B cells and reduces ACPA titers; effective in ACPA-positive anti-TNF failures. ACPA titer correlates with erosive disease activity. COMLEX: monitoring ACPA levels can guide therapy intensity; persistently high ACPA despite clinical improvement suggests incomplete suppression of the underlying pathogenic B cell clone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response"}]},{"sdlNumber":38,"title":"Infectious Arthritis & Lyme Disease","questions":[{"id":"sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following accounts for the majority of cases of septic arthritis in native joints?","choices":{"A":"Direct inoculation of bacteria into the joint during an intra-articular corticosteroid injection performed under sterile technique","B":"Contiguous spread from adjacent cortical bone infection through the periosteal membrane into the overlying joint cavity","C":"Hematogenous seeding of the synovial space during a concurrent bacteremic episode originating from a distant infectious source","D":"Retrograde dissemination via lymphatic channels from a regional soft-tissue abscess ascending into the adjacent joint","E":"Transcapsular migration of organisms from a septic adjacent bursa through the joint capsule into the intra-articular space"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hematogenous seeding accounts for approximately 80% of septic arthritis cases in native joints. The synovial membrane lacks a limiting basement membrane, allowing circulating organisms to directly enter the joint space during bacteremia. Direct inoculation (injections, trauma, arthroscopy) and contiguous spread from adjacent osteomyelitis account for the remaining 20%. Pre-existing joint disease increases susceptibility to hematogenous seeding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic vignette — sudden monoarticular hot swollen joint in a patient with recent bacteremia or distant infection (UTI, skin infection, endocarditis). COMLEX: Joint aspiration is always the next step; fluid with >50,000 WBC/mm3 and >90% PMNs strongly suggests septic arthritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Septic joint fluid is the most extreme class of inflammatory effusion; hematogenous seeding explains why synovial fluid analysis is essential"},{"id":"sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which anatomic characteristic of the synovial membrane makes the joint space particularly susceptible to hematogenous bacterial seeding during bacteremia?","choices":{"A":"Absence of a limiting basement membrane in the synovial lining allows circulating organisms to enter the joint space directly from blood vessels","B":"Dense capillary network with fenestrated endothelium that provides unfiltered vascular access to the intra-articular synovial compartment","C":"High concentration of glycosaminoglycans within synovial fluid that creates an osmotic gradient drawing bacteremic blood into the joint","D":"Presence of toll-like receptor-rich macrophages that actively phagocytose and transport bacteria across the synovial membrane barrier","E":"Lack of lymphatic drainage within the synovial space creating a stagnant fluid environment that promotes sustained bacterial growth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The synovial membrane lacks a limiting basement membrane, unlike most other tissues. This structural absence means that bacteria circulating in the bloodstream can pass directly from synovial capillaries into the joint space without the mechanical barrier that basement membranes normally provide. Organisms with propensity to adhere to synovial tissues, especially Staphylococcus aureus, exploit this vulnerability preferentially during bacteremia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This anatomic fact explains why joints with pre-existing synovitis (RA, gout, OA) are disproportionately seeded during bacteremia — their inflamed synovium has increased vascularity and permeability. COMLEX: RA patients on biologics face compounded risk: pre-existing synovitis plus immunosuppression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Septic joint fluid is the most extreme class of inflammatory effusion; hematogenous seeding explains why synovial fluid analysis is essential"},{"id":"sdl38_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Involvement of which joint in septic arthritis should most specifically prompt consideration of injection drug use as a contributing route of infection?","choices":{"A":"The knee joint, because its large synovial surface area makes it the most frequent anatomic target during high-level bacteremic episodes","B":"The sternoclavicular joint, as axial joint involvement is characteristically associated with injection drug use and is disproportionately represented in this population","C":"The hip joint, because its deep anatomic location creates difficulty with early clinical recognition and facilitates prolonged undetected bacteremia","D":"The wrist joint, because small joint involvement is a typical feature of disseminated gonococcal infection in sexually active individuals","E":"The ankle joint, because its proximity to the lower extremity injection sites used for intravenous drug administration increases direct inoculation risk"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Axial joint involvement including the sternoclavicular, sacroiliac, and acromioclavicular joints is strongly associated with injection drug use (IDU). In the general population, the knee is the most commonly affected joint. IDU is associated with gram-negative organisms such as Pseudomonas and Serratia in addition to S. aureus. The sternoclavicular joint in particular raises strong clinical suspicion for IDU as the route of infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sternoclavicular joint + septic arthritis = injection drug use until proven otherwise. Also remember pubic symphysis and facet joints. COMLEX: PWID with septic arthritis of axial joints often have concurrent endocarditis; always perform blood cultures and auscultate the heart.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Septic joint fluid is the most extreme class of inflammatory effusion; hematogenous seeding explains why synovial fluid analysis is essential"},{"id":"sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the more common distribution of causative organisms in infectious arthritis as distributed by age, gender, and immune status.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Staphylococcus aureus has become the most common cause of bacterial arthritis across all age groups in the United States primarily due to which change in the epidemiologic landscape?","choices":{"A":"Increased prevalence of S. aureus skin colonization secondary to widespread use of broad-spectrum antibiotics selecting for resistant strains","B":"Rising rates of diabetes mellitus and immunosuppressive therapy that specifically predispose patients to Staphylococcal joint infection","C":"Effective vaccination programs dramatically reduced the incidence of Haemophilus influenzae arthritis in children, allowing S. aureus to predominate across all age groups","D":"Emergence of community-acquired MRSA strains with enhanced virulence factors enabling preferential colonization of synovial tissue","E":"Shift in prescribing patterns leading to S. aureus replacement of streptococcal species through ecological niche displacement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prior to widespread H. influenzae vaccination, H. influenzae was the leading cause of septic arthritis in children under 2 years. Effective immunization virtually eliminated H. influenzae arthritis in this age group, and S. aureus has since become the most common cause across all age groups in the United States. S. aureus causes approximately 58% of cases in some series, with MRSA comprising up to 13% of those.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the organism-by-population grid: Neonates = Group B Strep + E. coli; all ages = S. aureus; sexually active young adults = N. gonorrhoeae; sickle cell = Salmonella; IDU = Pseudomonas/gram-negative; complement MAC deficiency (C5-C9) = Neisseria. COMLEX: S. aureus remains #1 in adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Synovial fluid laboratory testing including WBC count, Gram stain, and culture is the definitive diagnostic step for identifying the causative organism"},{"id":"sdl38_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the more common distribution of causative organisms in infectious arthritis as distributed by age, gender, and immune status.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the neonatal period, which combination of pathogens is most commonly responsible for septic arthritis, and through which primary mechanism do they typically access the joint space?","choices":{"A":"Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pneumoniae, entering the joint directly via hematogenous seeding during systemic bacteremia","B":"Group B Streptococcus and Escherichia coli, entering the joint via contiguous spread from underlying epiphyseal osteomyelitis in the neonate","C":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Haemophilus influenzae, transmitted perinatally and gaining joint access via bacteremia from mucosal surfaces","D":"Salmonella species and gram-negative bacilli, spreading to the joint from bowel translocation during episodes of neonatal feeding stress","E":"Coagulase-negative staphylococci and Klebsiella pneumoniae, introduced via direct inoculation from neonatal intravenous catheter sites"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In neonates, Group B Streptococcus and E. coli are the dominant organisms. Unlike in older patients where hematogenous spread predominates, neonatal septic arthritis frequently occurs via contiguous extension from epiphyseal osteomyelitis. In neonates, metaphyseal blood vessels penetrate the growth plate, allowing infection to spread from bone to joint. This anatomic relationship resolves with skeletal maturation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neonatal osteomyelitis often coexists with septic arthritis because metaphyseal vessels cross the growth plate. After the growth plate forms, this pathway closes. COMLEX: GBS is the most important organism to cover empirically in a neonate with a swollen joint.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Synovial fluid laboratory testing including WBC count, Gram stain, and culture is the definitive diagnostic step for identifying the causative organism"},{"id":"sdl38_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the more common distribution of causative organisms in infectious arthritis as distributed by age, gender, and immune status.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 18-year-old male presents with recurrent disseminated gonococcal infection involving the knee joint. Testing reveals a terminal complement pathway deficiency. Which component deficiency most specifically explains his increased susceptibility to this organism?","choices":{"A":"Deficiency of C1q, which impairs classical pathway initiation via antibody-antigen complexes deposited on the bacterial surface","B":"Deficiency of C3, which eliminates opsonization of the organism and prevents neutrophil phagocytosis within the inflamed synovium","C":"Deficiency of C4b-binding protein, which allows unregulated activation of the amplification loop at the C3b convertase stage","D":"Deficiency of terminal complement components C5 through C9, which prevents membrane attack complex formation and direct lysis of the Gram-negative Neisseria outer membrane","E":"Deficiency of mannan-binding lectin, which impairs the lectin pathway required for innate clearance of encapsulated cocci"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Terminal complement components C5-C9 form the membrane attack complex (MAC), which lyses Gram-negative bacteria by creating pores in their outer membranes. Neisseria gonorrhoeae and N. meningitidis are uniquely dependent on MAC-mediated lysis because they can resist opsonophagocytosis. Patients with MAC deficiency (C5-C9) are specifically susceptible to recurrent disseminated Neisseria infections. This is distinct from the general susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria caused by C3 deficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MAC deficiency (C5-C9) = recurrent Neisseria infections (gonorrhoeae and meningitidis). C3 deficiency = susceptibility to ALL encapsulated bacteria. C1q deficiency = impaired apoptotic debris clearance, SLE predisposition. COMLEX: Any patient with recurrent disseminated Neisseria should be tested for complement deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Synovial fluid laboratory testing including WBC count, Gram stain, and culture is the definitive diagnostic step for identifying the causative organism"},{"id":"sdl38_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which organism causes Lyme disease, and through which primary vector is it transmitted to humans in the United States?","choices":{"A":"Borrelia recurrentis, a spirochete transmitted by the human body louse Pediculus humanus corporis during episodes of close human contact","B":"Borrelia hermsii, a spirochete transmitted by soft-bodied Ornithodoros ticks during nighttime blood meals in endemic western forest regions","C":"Borrelia burgdorferi, a spirochete transmitted by Ixodes deer ticks of the ricinus complex, predominantly in New England, the mid-Atlantic states, and upper Midwest","D":"Borrelia mayonii, a spirochete transmitted by the American dog tick Dermacentor variabilis during prolonged attachment in grassland areas","E":"Rickettsia rickettsii, a gram-negative obligate intracellular pathogen transmitted by the wood tick Dermacentor andersoni in the Rocky Mountains"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lyme disease is caused by Borrelia burgdorferi, a spirochete transmitted by Ixodes deer ticks (Ixodes scapularis in the Northeast/Midwest, Ixodes pacificus in the West). It is the most common arthropod-borne disease in the United States, endemic to New England, mid-Atlantic states, and upper Midwest. While Borrelia mayonii exists in the upper Midwest, B. burgdorferi sensu stricto is the dominant species in the US. Borrelia recurrentis and hermsii cause relapsing fever, not Lyme disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Borrelia burgdorferi + Ixodes tick = Lyme disease. The three phases: erythema migrans (early localized) → carditis/Bell palsy/meningitis (early disseminated) → arthritis (late). COMLEX: Lyme is the most common vector-borne illness in North America; know the geographic distribution.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Antibiotic-refractory Lyme arthritis represents a classic example of autoimmunity triggered by molecular mimicry following an infectious event"},{"id":"sdl38_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old hiker from Connecticut presents with a 3-month history of intermittent swelling of the left knee that recurs after aspiration. He denies fever. Which of the following best characterizes the articular manifestation of late Lyme disease?","choices":{"A":"Monoarthritis of the knee is the most common presentation, with large joint effusions that frequently recur after aspiration and attacks lasting weeks to months","B":"Symmetric small joint polyarthritis of the hands and feet is the most distinctive articular pattern of late disseminated Borrelia infection","C":"Migratory polyarthritis affecting more than five joints simultaneously is the defining late-stage articular manifestation of untreated Lyme","D":"Axial arthritis with sacroiliitis and enthesitis is the predominant joint pattern in untreated late-stage Borrelia burgdorferi infection","E":"Cricoarytenoid joint involvement producing hoarseness and dysphagia is the classic articular manifestation of chronic Lyme disease"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Late Lyme arthritis characteristically presents as monoarthritis of the knee — the most commonly affected joint — with large, recurrent effusions. Attacks are intermittent and last weeks to months. Unlike RA, small joint involvement of the hands and feet is uncommon. The arthritis is not particularly painful relative to the degree of swelling. Without treatment, up to 80% of untreated patients develop Lyme arthritis, but with antibiotics, arthritis occurs in less than 10% of infected individuals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Late Lyme arthritis = intermittent monoarthritis of the knee in a patient from an endemic region. Large effusion, not particularly painful. COMLEX: Lyme arthritis large joints in descending order: knee > shoulder > ankle > elbow > TMJ > wrist. Always fewer than 5 joints affected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Antibiotic-refractory Lyme arthritis represents a classic example of autoimmunity triggered by molecular mimicry following an infectious event"},{"id":"sdl38_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with confirmed Lyme arthritis completes two months of oral doxycycline followed by one month of intravenous ceftriaxone. Joint swelling persists, and synovial fluid PCR is negative for Borrelia DNA. Which mechanism best explains the persistence of arthritis in this antibiotic-refractory case?","choices":{"A":"Development of doxycycline-resistant Borrelia strains through upregulation of efflux pump mechanisms during prolonged antibiotic exposure","B":"Biofilm formation by residual Borrelia within the synovial pannus creating a protected sanctuary that prevents antibiotic penetration","C":"T cell-mediated delayed hypersensitivity reaction to persistent dead Borrelia antigens trapped within the synovial membrane matrix","D":"Immune complex deposition of circulating anti-Borrelia IgG antibodies within synovial microvessels triggering Type III hypersensitivity","E":"Cross-reactive autoimmune response between Borrelia outer surface protein A and an unknown self-antigen in the synovium perpetuating inflammation after organism clearance"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Antibiotic-refractory (postinfectious) Lyme arthritis occurs when joint inflammation persists despite complete antibiotic treatment and PCR-negative synovial fluid. The proposed mechanism is molecular mimicry: immune responses primed against Borrelia outer surface protein A (OspA) cross-react with an unknown synovial self-antigen, sustaining inflammation in the absence of viable organisms. This is essentially an autoimmune process triggered by infection. HLA-DR4 individuals may be at higher risk for this complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antibiotic-refractory Lyme arthritis = autoimmune mechanism (molecular mimicry with OspA), not antibiotic resistance. Negative PCR confirms no live organisms. COMLEX: This is a classic example of infection-triggered autoimmunity; the infection is the initial trigger but the ongoing pathology is autoimmune.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Antibiotic-refractory Lyme arthritis represents a classic example of autoimmunity triggered by molecular mimicry following an infectious event"},{"id":"sdl38_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes and known chronic osteomyelitis of the distal femur presents with 3 days of acute right knee pain, swelling, and fever. Knee aspiration yields 80,000 WBC/mm3 with 95% neutrophils. X-ray shows metaphyseal lucency within the capsular reflection of the knee. Which mechanism best explains the development of his septic arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Hematogenous seeding of the knee joint during a bacteremic episode originating from a concurrent urinary tract infection with gram-negative rods","B":"Direct inoculation of bacteria into the knee joint during a recent intra-articular corticosteroid injection administered for osteoarthritis","C":"Contiguous extension of metaphyseal osteomyelitis directly into the joint space via the intracapsular metaphysis of the distal femur","D":"Retrograde spread via lymphatics from a pedal soft-tissue abscess ascending along the femoral lymphatic channels to the joint","E":"Transcapsular migration of organisms from an adjacent septic prepatellar bursitis through the anterior joint capsule into the joint space"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The knee, hip, shoulder, and elbow are joints in which the metaphysis lies within the capsular reflection. When osteomyelitis involves an intracapsular metaphysis, pus can break through the bone cortex and discharge directly into the joint space — this is contiguous spread. The X-ray showing metaphyseal lucency within the capsular reflection is the key finding. This mechanism is distinct from hematogenous seeding, where bacteremia seeds the synovium directly rather than spreading from adjacent bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Joints with intracapsular metaphysis (knee, hip, shoulder, elbow) are at risk for septic arthritis secondary to adjacent osteomyelitis. COMLEX: Diabetic patients with chronic osteomyelitis can develop septic arthritis from contiguous spread; always image the bone when septic arthritis is found near a known osteomyelitis site.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected"},{"id":"sdl38_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with a 20-year history of rheumatoid arthritis managed with methotrexate and adalimumab presents with a single acutely swollen, hot, and painful right wrist disproportionate to her chronic bilateral joint disease. Synovial fluid reveals 95,000 WBC/mm3 with 98% neutrophils, and Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in clusters. Which combination of risk factors most directly explains why her joint was susceptible to hematogenous seeding?","choices":{"A":"Her postmenopausal status and vitamin D deficiency, which impair synoviocyte barrier function and reduce mucosal protection against bacteremic organisms","B":"Her use of methotrexate selectively reducing neutrophil production and eliminating the primary cellular defense against gram-positive organisms","C":"Her chronic synovitis and prior intra-articular injections reducing the bactericidal capacity of synovial fluid and promoting biofilm formation","D":"Her advanced age and female sex reducing complement-mediated clearance of encapsulated gram-positive organisms during bacteremia","E":"Her pre-existing synovitis increasing vascular permeability and her use of immunosuppressive therapy both independently increasing susceptibility to hematogenous seeding"},"correct":"E","explanation":"RA patients face dual risk for hematogenous septic arthritis: (1) pre-existing synovitis increases local blood flow and vascular permeability, making the joint a preferred site of bacterial localization during bacteremia; and (2) immunosuppressive medications including biologic DMARDs impair the immune response needed to clear bacteremia before joint seeding occurs. The gram-positive cocci in clusters indicate S. aureus, the most common organism in adult septic arthritis. The key is recognizing a disproportionately inflamed joint in a chronic arthritis patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In an RA patient, a single dramatically inflamed joint should prompt immediate aspiration for septic arthritis — never assume it is a flare without ruling out infection. COMLEX: Anti-TNF biologics substantially increase infection risk; superimposed septic arthritis on RA carries high morbidity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected"},{"id":"sdl38_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with a history of intravenous heroin use presents with two weeks of fever and right-sided chest pain. Physical examination reveals tenderness, erythema, and warmth over the right sternoclavicular joint. Blood cultures grow Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Which feature of this presentation is most characteristic of septic arthritis in injection drug users compared with the general population?","choices":{"A":"Involvement of an axial joint such as the sternoclavicular joint, which is disproportionately affected in injection drug users compared with the general adult population","B":"Polymicrobial bacteremia with gram-negative rod predominance caused by simultaneous seeding from contaminated needle inoculation sites","C":"Bilateral joint involvement from simultaneous hematogenous seeding during a high-level bacteremic episode from the injection site","D":"Sparing of large peripheral joints such as the knee and hip, which are typically protected by their depth of soft tissue coverage","E":"Development of septic arthritis in the absence of fever, because injection drug users have blunted systemic inflammatory responses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Injection drug users (IDU/PWID) characteristically develop septic arthritis in axial joints — sternoclavicular, sacroiliac, acromioclavicular, and facet joints — which are rarely affected in the general population. This reflects hematogenous seeding from venous access sites with organisms that prefer these anatomical locations. Gram-negative bacilli including Pseudomonas and Serratia are more common in IDU-related septic arthritis, though S. aureus (including MRSA) remains most common overall. The sternoclavicular joint involvement in a young person with IDU history is a classic board vignette.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sternoclavicular + sacroiliac joint septic arthritis = injection drug use. Cover gram-negative organisms (Pseudomonas) empirically in IDU patients. COMLEX: Always consider concurrent endocarditis when IDU patients have bacteremia and joint involvement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Describe the method by which joints become infected"},{"id":"sdl38_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the more common distribution of causative organisms in infectious arthritis as distributed by age, gender, and immune status.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old man with sickle cell disease presents with fever, right hip pain, and inability to bear weight. Blood and synovial fluid cultures are sent. Which organism should be specifically targeted in the empiric antibiotic regimen given his underlying hematologic condition?","choices":{"A":"Staphylococcus aureus alone, which causes the majority of bacterial arthritis in all age groups regardless of underlying comorbid conditions","B":"Streptococcus pneumoniae, which is the dominant pathogen in asplenic patients due to functional asplenia impairing opsonophagocytosis","C":"Haemophilus influenzae, which causes a disproportionate share of joint infections in patients with impaired opsonization of encapsulated bacteria","D":"Salmonella species, which cause a disproportionately high proportion of septic arthritis and osteomyelitis in patients with sickle cell disease","E":"Gram-negative enteric bacilli, which seed the joint preferentially via bacteremia from ischemic bowel during vasoocclusive crises"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sickle cell disease creates areas of bone and joint ischemia from vascular occlusion, which serve as a nidus for Salmonella infection. While S. aureus remains the overall most common cause of septic arthritis in sickle cell patients, Salmonella is uniquely overrepresented in this population compared to any other patient group. The impaired splenic function from autosplenectomy also contributes to susceptibility to encapsulated organisms. Coverage for both S. aureus and Salmonella is warranted empirically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sickle cell disease + osteomyelitis or septic arthritis = think Salmonella first. The ischemic bone provides a nidus for Salmonella bacteremia to localize. COMLEX: Sickle cell patients also have functional asplenia predisposing to encapsulated organisms, but Salmonella is the most tested organism for joint/bone infections specifically.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — Causative organism distribution by age, gender, and immune status"},{"id":"sdl38_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the more common distribution of causative organisms in infectious arthritis as distributed by age, gender, and immune status.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old sexually active woman presents with fever, migratory polyarthritis involving the right knee and left ankle, and painless pustular skin lesions on her distal extremities. Joint aspiration yields no organisms on Gram stain. Cervical cultures confirm the diagnosis. Which feature of this patient population primarily explains the increased susceptibility to dissemination of this pathogen compared with infected males?","choices":{"A":"Female hormonal cycling that directly suppresses complement activation during the luteal phase and permits gonococcal bacteremia","B":"Higher mucus viscosity in female genitourinary secretions that reduces local innate immune clearance of urogenital gonococci","C":"Greater membrane attack complex consumption during menstruation that transiently lowers complement-mediated gonococcal lysis","D":"Cervical anatomy that limits physical examination recognition of primary infection and delays presentation to medical care","E":"Greater likelihood of asymptomatic primary infection in women, which allows prolonged untreated bacteremia and dissemination before diagnosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Disseminated gonococcal infection (DGI) is more common in women than men largely because female urogenital gonorrhea is frequently asymptomatic. The absence of symptoms allows infection to persist longer without treatment, increasing the risk of bacteremia and dissemination to joints and skin. DGI typically presents with the triad of migratory polyarthritis, tenosynovitis, and petechial or pustular skin lesions. Gram stain of joint fluid is usually negative because the organism is present in low numbers; cultures of urogenital sites are more reliable.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DGI triad = migratory polyarthritis + tenosynovitis + skin lesions (petechial/pustular). Gram stain of joint fluid is often negative. Culture the urethra/cervix, pharynx, and rectum. COMLEX: Women, MSM, and C5-C9 complement deficient patients are at highest risk for DGI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — Causative organism distribution by age, gender, and immune status"},{"id":"sdl38_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the more common distribution of causative organisms in infectious arthritis as distributed by age, gender, and immune status.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia receiving induction chemotherapy develops fever and an acutely swollen left knee. Synovial fluid cultures grow Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Which patient characteristic best explains the shift toward gram-negative organism causation compared with immunocompetent adults?","choices":{"A":"Advanced age causing impaired neutrophil chemotaxis and reduced synovial phagocytic capacity specifically against extracellular gram-negative pathogens","B":"Underlying immunosuppression impairing complement-mediated opsonization and neutrophil function required for clearance of gram-negative bacteria during bacteremia","C":"Mucosal barrier disruption from chemotherapy-induced mucositis providing a direct portal of entry for Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia","D":"Corticosteroid premedication reducing TNF-alpha-mediated macrophage activation that normally clears gram-negative organisms from the bloodstream","E":"Leukopenia from chemotherapy selectively eliminating NK cells responsible for extracellular bactericidal activity against gram-negative rods"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gram-negative organisms including Pseudomonas, Serratia, and enteric bacilli predominate in immunocompromised patients, older adults, and injection drug users compared with the general adult population. Profound immunosuppression from induction chemotherapy impairs complement activation, neutrophil function, and clearance of gram-negative bacteremia, allowing organisms to seed joints. In immunocompetent adults, S. aureus dominates, but as immune function deteriorates, gram-negative coverage becomes increasingly important empirically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Immunocompromised + septic arthritis = cover gram-negative organisms including Pseudomonas. This includes chemotherapy patients, HIV with low CD4, and transplant recipients. COMLEX: The empiric antibiotic choice changes based on the patient context; always tailor coverage to host factors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — Causative organism distribution by age, gender, and immune status"},{"id":"sdl38_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman from coastal New England presents with 2 months of intermittent left knee swelling. She recalls a bull's-eye rash on her thigh 6 months ago that resolved spontaneously without treatment. Knee aspiration shows 25,000 WBC/mm3 with 70% neutrophils. Which diagnostic approach is most appropriate for confirming Lyme arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Synovial fluid PCR for Borrelia burgdorferi DNA alone, as this test provides superior sensitivity compared with serology at the late articular stage","B":"ELISA for IgM antibodies against Borrelia, as the IgM response predominates during late articular manifestations of Lyme disease","C":"Synovial biopsy with silver stain to detect spirochetes directly within the synovial tissue for definitive histologic confirmation","D":"Serum IgG antibody serology for Borrelia burgdorferi using a two-tier testing approach as the primary diagnostic method for late Lyme disease","E":"Empiric antibiotic treatment without confirmatory testing, as the characteristic history of erythema migrans is sufficient for clinical diagnosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"All patients with Lyme arthritis will have positive serum IgG antibodies for B. burgdorferi. Two-tier serologic testing (ELISA followed by Western blot) is the standard diagnostic approach. By the time arthritis develops (late Lyme disease), IgG — not IgM — predominates. PCR of synovial fluid has approximately 70% sensitivity and should supplement, not replace, serology. A positive IgM alone in late disease likely represents a false positive. The history of untreated erythema migrans 6 months prior followed by knee arthritis is classic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Late Lyme = IgG positive serology (two-tier). PCR of synovial fluid is ~70% sensitive and supplements serology. IgM alone in late disease = likely false positive. COMLEX: Erythema migrans is the most specific finding in early Lyme; in late disease, serology is required because EM is no longer present.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease"},{"id":"sdl38_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old man from New England presents with 8 weeks of left knee swelling. Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP are negative. Synovial biopsy reveals lymphocytic infiltration resembling rheumatoid arthritis, but the pathologist notes a unique vascular finding. Which specific vascular change in the synovial biopsy most specifically supports Lyme arthritis over rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Obliterative endarteritis with concentric onion-skin thickening of small arteriolar walls within the synovial tissue, characteristic of Borrelia-associated synovitis","B":"Endothelial cell activation with ICAM-1 upregulation and leukocyte rolling in synovial postcapillary venules consistent with any inflammatory arthritis","C":"Fibrinoid necrosis of medium-sized synovial vessels consistent with necrotizing vasculitis driven by immune complex deposition in the vessel walls","D":"Angiogenesis with abundant new vessel formation characteristic of synovial pannus growth driven by VEGF from activated fibroblasts in RA","E":"Granulomatous inflammation around small vessels with multinucleated giant cells consistent with tuberculosis-related mycobacterial synovitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The histopathology of Lyme synovitis can closely mimic RA with synoviocyte hyperplasia, fibrin deposition, and mononuclear infiltrates. The distinguishing feature is obliterative endarteritis — onion-skin thickening of arteriolar walls — which is characteristic of Borrelia synovitis and not a feature of RA. The infiltrate in Lyme synovitis is predominantly CD4+ T cells. This morphologic feature reflects the vascular tropism of Borrelia and helps distinguish it from autoimmune joint disease when serologic data are ambiguous.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lyme synovitis histology = mimics RA, but the distinguishing feature is obliterative endarteritis (onion-skin arteriolar thickening). COMLEX: In severe Lyme arthritis histology may be indistinguishable from RA; clinical context and serology remain essential for diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease"},{"id":"sdl38_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman from the mid-Atlantic states completes 2 months of oral doxycycline then 1 month of intravenous ceftriaxone for confirmed Lyme arthritis. Six weeks after finishing antibiotics, she returns with persistent knee swelling. Synovial fluid PCR is negative for Borrelia DNA. IgG serology remains strongly positive. Which statement most accurately describes the proposed pathogenic mechanism for her persistent arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Reinfection with Borrelia burgdorferi from a second tick bite, as persistently elevated IgG titers indicate ongoing active infection requiring retreatment","B":"Autoimmune perpetuation by cross-reactive T cells and antibodies targeting a synovial self-antigen after initial immune priming by Borrelia outer surface protein A","C":"Antibiotic treatment failure from selection of a doxycycline-resistant Borrelia clone with altered outer membrane lipoprotein expression","D":"Immune complex deposition of circulating anti-Borrelia IgG antibodies in the synovial microvasculature triggering Type III hypersensitivity vasculitis","E":"Reactivation of dormant intra-synovial Borrelia cysts that evade antibiotic penetration by adopting a cyst-protected metabolic resting state"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Antibiotic-refractory (postinfectious) Lyme arthritis is characterized by persistent joint inflammation despite complete antibiotic treatment, negative PCR for Borrelia DNA, and persistently positive IgG serology (which is expected and does not indicate active infection). The mechanism is autoimmune: molecular mimicry between Borrelia OspA epitopes and synovial self-antigens maintains inflammation after pathogen clearance. Retreatment with additional antibiotics is not beneficial because the organism is no longer present. This condition is treated with anti-inflammatory therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antibiotic-refractory Lyme = autoimmune mechanism (OspA molecular mimicry), negative PCR, positive IgG (this is expected). Do not retreat with antibiotics — treat the autoimmune component. COMLEX: Persistent IgG antibodies after Lyme treatment are NOT a sign of active infection; they can persist for years after successful treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — Define the causative agent and mechanism of infection in Lyme disease"}]},{"sdlNumber":39,"title":"Polymyalgia Rheumatica & Fibromyalgia","questions":[{"id":"sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which demographic characteristic is most consistently associated with polymyalgia rheumatica and should raise clinical suspicion in a patient presenting with new-onset proximal muscle aching and morning stiffness?","choices":{"A":"Female sex under 40 years with northern European ancestry and a personal or family history of autoimmune thyroid disease","B":"Age greater than 50 years, with the peak incidence occurring between 70 and 80 years; PMR is almost exclusively a disease of older adults","C":"Male sex over 60 years with a personal history of giant cell arteritis or recent temporal artery biopsy procedure","D":"Postmenopausal women with low bone mineral density and concurrent osteoporosis requiring bisphosphonate therapy","E":"Individuals of Asian or African American descent presenting with acute-onset bilateral shoulder girdle aching and stiffness"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PMR is almost exclusively a disease of adults over 50, with the peak incidence between 70 and 80 years. The prevalence increases progressively with advancing age. It does not occur in younger adults. It is more common in females (2-3:1 ratio) and in people of northern European descent, but the age criterion over 50 is the most fundamental epidemiologic requirement for the diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Any patient under 50 presenting with proximal aching should not be labeled PMR — consider other diagnoses. COMLEX: PMR is the second most common systemic inflammatory rheumatic disease in adults after RA; lifetime risk ~2.4% in females.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 2 — PMR and giant cell arteritis share the same vulnerable patient population: adults over 50 of northern European descent"},{"id":"sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The annual incidence of polymyalgia rheumatica is substantially higher in Scandinavian countries and among people of northern European descent. Which factor most likely explains this geographic and ethnic variation in disease incidence?","choices":{"A":"Genetic predisposition associated with specific HLA-DRB1 alleles that are more prevalent in northern European populations, contributing to elevated disease susceptibility","B":"Environmental exposure to colder temperatures in Scandinavian climates that directly triggers periarticular bursitis and tenosynovitis in aging joints","C":"Higher dietary intake of omega-6 fatty acids characteristic of traditional Scandinavian cuisine that promotes systemic inflammatory responses","D":"Greater physician awareness and systematic screening practices in Scandinavian healthcare systems leading to higher diagnostic capture rates","E":"Increased prevalence of giant cell arteritis in northern European climates that drives secondary polymyalgia rheumatica through shared pathophysiology"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The geographic clustering of PMR in Scandinavian countries and people of northern European descent reflects genetic susceptibility, particularly HLA-DRB1 alleles. The same HLA-DRB1 hypervariable region polymorphism associated with PMR and GCA is more prevalent in northern European populations. Incidence in Norway is 113/100,000/year while Italy reports only 13/100,000/year for those over 50 — a 9-fold difference that cannot be explained by climate or diet alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR has the highest incidence in Scandinavian and northern European descent populations; Olmsted County, MN (predominantly Scandinavian) incidence is 63.9/100,000/year. COMLEX: HLA-DRB1 is the primary genetic risk factor; this same gene locus also confers risk for GCA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 2 — PMR and giant cell arteritis share the same vulnerable patient population: adults over 50 of northern European descent"},{"id":"sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Polymyalgia rheumatica is described as the second most common systemic inflammatory rheumatic disease in adults after rheumatoid arthritis. Which additional epidemiologic feature is most characteristic of its distribution in the population?","choices":{"A":"Equal prevalence between males and females, with a lifetime risk exceeding 10 percent in adults over the age of 70 years","B":"Strong male predominance in individuals over 80 years of age due to declining estrogen-mediated immune regulation effects","C":"Female predominance with a female to male ratio of approximately 2 to 3 to 1, and a lifetime risk of approximately 2.43 percent in females and 1.66 percent in males","D":"Exclusive occurrence in adults over 70 years of age with no documented cases reported in the 50 to 69 year age range","E":"Similar prevalence across racial and ethnic groups with no significant differences in incidence rates documented between populations worldwide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PMR affects females 2-3 times more often than males, consistent with most autoimmune and inflammatory rheumatic diseases. The lifetime risk is 2.43% for females and 1.66% for males. It is less common — but not absent — in Asian, African American, and Latin American populations. PMR is notably second only to RA in prevalence among systemic inflammatory rheumatic diseases in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR demographics: female predominance (2-3:1), age >50, northern European descent. Lifetime risk ~2.4% females, ~1.7% males. COMLEX: PMR is relatively common — physicians should recognize the classic presentation of acute-onset bilateral proximal stiffness in an older woman.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 2 — PMR and giant cell arteritis share the same vulnerable patient population: adults over 50 of northern European descent"},{"id":"sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Approximately what percentage of patients diagnosed with giant cell arteritis also have polymyalgia rheumatica at some point during the course of their disease?","choices":{"A":"Less than 5 percent, as the two conditions share the same vascular inflammatory pathway but manifest in entirely different tissue compartments","B":"Approximately 20 percent, representing the subset with large-vessel GCA who also develop periarticular bursitis and tenosynovitis","C":"Approximately 30 percent, which reflects the proportion with positive temporal artery biopsy and concurrent proximal shoulder aching","D":"Approximately 50 percent, as PMR and GCA frequently co-occur and may represent different manifestations of a shared underlying disease spectrum","E":"Greater than 70 percent, because the cytokine environment in GCA invariably causes periarticular bursitis through IL-6-driven inflammation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PMR occurs in approximately 50% of patients with GCA, making it one of the most common associated findings. Conversely, roughly 5-30% of patients with PMR will develop GCA at some point. PMR is 2-3 times more common than GCA overall. The two conditions share overlapping immunopathology — HLA-DRB1 alleles, Th17/Treg imbalance, elevated IL-6 — and may represent different points along a continuum of the same disease process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR + GCA association: ~50% of GCA patients have PMR. ~5-30% of PMR patients develop GCA. PMR is 2-3x more common than GCA. COMLEX: Any PMR patient who develops new headache, jaw claudication, or visual symptoms needs urgent evaluation for GCA — blindness is a preventable complication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — The Th17/Treg imbalance and elevated IL-6 driving PMR-GCA overlap are the same immune mechanisms perpetuating tissue damage in giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"When comparing the immunopathology of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis, which finding is characteristic of GCA but is NOT typically seen in polymyalgia rheumatica?","choices":{"A":"Elevated serum IL-6 levels driving constitutional symptoms including fever, fatigue, anorexia, and elevated acute phase reactants","B":"Increased circulating Th17 cells and decreased regulatory T cells reflecting an imbalanced adaptive immune response","C":"Prominent interferon-gamma producing T cells that drive macrophage activation and granuloma formation within the arterial wall","D":"HLA-DRB1 polymorphism in the hypervariable region that maps to the antigen-binding cleft of the HLA-DR molecules","E":"Subclinical vascular inflammation detectable by PET scan showing fluorodeoxyglucose uptake in major arterial vessels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both PMR and GCA share elevated IL-6, Th17/Treg imbalance, and the HLA-DRB1 polymorphism. The key distinguishing feature is that GCA is characterized by prominent IFN-gamma-producing CD4+ T cells that activate macrophages, leading to granulomatous inflammation within the arterial wall. In PMR, IFN-gamma-producing T cells are NOT prominent — this is a critical distinction. Evidence of subclinical arterial inflammation with IL-1 and IL-6 is present in PMR temporal arteries, but the IFN-gamma-driven granulomas are specific to GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA has IFN-gamma-producing T cells driving granulomatous arteritis; PMR does NOT have prominent IFN-gamma T cells despite sharing IL-6 elevation and Th17 increases. COMLEX: This mechanistic difference explains why GCA causes arterial wall destruction and ischemic complications while PMR primarily affects periarticular structures.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — The Th17/Treg imbalance and elevated IL-6 driving PMR-GCA overlap are the same immune mechanisms perpetuating tissue damage in giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman is diagnosed with polymyalgia rheumatica based on clinical and laboratory criteria. She has no headache, jaw claudication, or visual changes. Based on published data, approximately what proportion of PMR patients without clinical features of GCA have subclinical large-vessel vasculitis detectable on imaging?","choices":{"A":"Less than 5 percent, as subclinical GCA is extremely rare in the absence of constitutional symptoms pointing toward arterial involvement","B":"Approximately 10 percent, representing those with subclinical carotid artery involvement who later develop temporal artery symptoms","C":"Approximately 15 percent, limited to the subset with very high ESR values above 100 mm per hour at time of diagnosis","D":"Approximately 35 percent, as MRI routinely shows subclavian artery wall edema in the majority of newly diagnosed PMR patients","E":"Approximately 23 percent, based on meta-analysis demonstrating subclinical GCA in roughly one-quarter of patients with polymyalgia rheumatica"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A meta-analysis of 566 patients with PMR found subclinical GCA in approximately 23% of cases. On PET scan, increased FDG vascular uptake compatible with vasculitis was reported in approximately one-third of consecutive PMR patients, predominantly in the subclavian arteries (but less intense than in clinical GCA). Despite this subclinical vascular inflammation, patients with \"pure\" PMR rarely develop severe ischemic complications such as blindness unless clinical GCA features emerge. Close monitoring throughout the disease course is warranted.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ~23% of PMR patients have subclinical GCA on imaging (meta-analysis). Clinical GCA features (jaw claudication, visual changes, headache) should always be assessed at each visit. COMLEX: PET scanning for subclinical GCA in PMR is a research tool; temporal artery biopsy is not required in PMR without GCA symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — The Th17/Treg imbalance and elevated IL-6 driving PMR-GCA overlap are the same immune mechanisms perpetuating tissue damage in giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Polymyalgia rheumatica and giant cell arteritis share a specific HLA association that differs from that found in rheumatoid arthritis. Which HLA allele and structural region are associated with both PMR and GCA?","choices":{"A":"HLA-B27 at the peptide-binding groove, which is shared with ankylosing spondylitis and other seronegative spondyloarthropathies","B":"HLA-DR4 shared epitope at positions 70 to 74 of the DRbeta chain, identical to the epitope responsible for rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis","C":"HLA-DQ6.02 at the antigen-presenting cleft, which is the same protective allele that reduces risk for type 1 diabetes mellitus","D":"HLA-DRB1 with a sequence polymorphism in the hypervariable region that maps to the antigen-binding cleft of the HLA-DR molecule","E":"HLA-DR2 at the complement receptor binding site, linking PMR pathogenesis to impaired clearance of apoptotic cell debris in aged tissue"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PMR and GCA share an HLA-DRB1 sequence polymorphism in the hypervariable region that maps to the antigen-binding cleft. This is distinct from the RA shared epitope at positions 70-74 of the DRbeta chain — patients with GCA and PMR share the DRB1 polymorphism, but it is NOT found in patients with RA. This different HLA allele suggests that different antigens are being presented in PMR/GCA versus RA, despite all three conditions being HLA-DR4 related. HLA-B27 is associated with spondyloarthropathies, not PMR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR/GCA HLA = HLA-DRB1 hypervariable region polymorphism (antigen-binding cleft); RA HLA = shared epitope positions 70-74. Same HLA-DR4 broad allele group but DIFFERENT specific polymorphisms. COMLEX: The distinction between the PMR/GCA HLA association and the RA shared epitope is a classic exam discriminator.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA-DR4 in PMR maps to the same Class II antigen-binding cleft role covered in SDL 36 for HLA alleles in autoimmune disease"},{"id":"sdl39_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokine is primarily responsible for the constitutional manifestations of polymyalgia rheumatica, and what evidence supports this role?","choices":{"A":"IL-6, which is elevated in peripheral blood of both PMR and GCA patients and is thought to drive constitutional symptoms including fever, fatigue, and elevated acute phase reactants","B":"IL-17, which is produced by the elevated Th17 cells in PMR and directly drives periarticular bursitis and tenosynovitis at the shoulder and hip","C":"IFN-gamma, which is secreted by activated CD4+ T cells in PMR and mediates macrophage activation within the periarticular bursae and tendons","D":"TNF-alpha, which is the dominant proinflammatory cytokine in PMR and explains the dramatic response to tocilizumab in refractory disease","E":"IL-1 beta, which is exclusively elevated in the temporal arteries of PMR patients and primarily drives the systemic fever and acute phase response"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-6 is the primary cytokine driving constitutional symptoms in both PMR and GCA. It stimulates hepatocytes to produce acute phase proteins (CRP, fibrinogen, SAA), causes fever through hypothalamic prostaglandin production, and promotes fatigue and anorexia. Elevated serum IL-6 is a consistent finding in active PMR and GCA. IL-6 receptor blockade with tocilizumab is effective in GCA and is being studied in PMR, providing therapeutic confirmation of its central role. Th17 cells are increased but IL-17 is not the primary mediator of constitutional symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-6 drives the constitutional manifestations of PMR and GCA (fever, fatigue, elevated CRP/ESR). Tocilizumab (IL-6 receptor blocker) is FDA-approved for GCA. COMLEX: The Th17/Treg imbalance is shared between PMR and GCA; Th17 cells are increased and Tregs are decreased in both conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA-DR4 in PMR maps to the same Class II antigen-binding cleft role covered in SDL 36 for HLA alleles in autoimmune disease"},{"id":"sdl39_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which T cell subset imbalance is found in both polymyalgia rheumatica and giant cell arteritis, suggesting a shared underlying immunopathological mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Increased CD8+ cytotoxic T cells with decreased CD4+ helper T cells, reflecting a shift toward direct cytolytic tissue destruction mechanisms","B":"Decreased Th1 cells with increased Th2 cells, resulting in an exaggerated humoral antibody response and elevated autoantibody production","C":"Increased Th17 cells and decreased regulatory T cells, which promotes uncontrolled pro-inflammatory signaling and impairs peripheral immune tolerance","D":"Increased memory B cells with decreased naive B cells, reflecting chronic antigen stimulation and class-switched autoantibody production","E":"Decreased natural killer T cells with increased double-negative T cells, disrupting lipid antigen presentation in inflamed vessel walls"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both PMR and GCA share a characteristic T cell imbalance: Th17 cells are increased (promoting IL-17-driven inflammation) and regulatory T cells (Tregs) are decreased (impairing peripheral tolerance). This shared immunopathology supports the concept that PMR and GCA may represent a disease spectrum rather than completely distinct conditions. IL-6 itself suppresses Treg differentiation while promoting Th17 development, creating a self-amplifying inflammatory loop in both conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR and GCA = Th17 increased + Treg decreased (shared immune abnormality). The key distinguishing feature of GCA over PMR is the additional presence of IFN-gamma producing T cells driving granulomatous arteritis. COMLEX: This Th17/Treg imbalance is a recurrent theme in autoimmune diseases including RA and SLE.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA-DR4 in PMR maps to the same Class II antigen-binding cleft role covered in SDL 36 for HLA alleles in autoimmune disease"},{"id":"sdl39_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Fibromyalgia is best classified as which type of pain condition, and what is the primary neurological mechanism responsible for its characteristic symptomatology?","choices":{"A":"A nociceptive pain condition driven by ongoing peripheral tissue damage in muscles and tendons that produces referred pain to distant body regions","B":"A neuropathic pain condition caused by small fiber polyneuropathy affecting intraepidermal nerve fibers throughout affected body regions","C":"An inflammatory pain condition mediated by synovial macrophage activation and elevated TNF-alpha in the periarticular soft tissue compartments","D":"A psychosomatic pain condition without any demonstrable neurobiological substrate, explained entirely by psychiatric comorbidity and somatization","E":"A nociplastic pain condition characterized by central sensitization, causing amplified neural signaling within the central nervous system in the absence of peripheral tissue damage"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fibromyalgia is the prototypic nociplastic pain disorder. Central sensitization — amplification of neural signaling within the CNS — results in pain hypersensitivity, temporal summation, and cross-modal sensory sensitization (light, sound, smell also become aversive). There is no peripheral tissue inflammation or structural damage. Muscle biopsies are histopathologically normal. Brain imaging studies using fMRI and PET demonstrate abnormal pain processing and altered neurotransmitter function (reduced serotonin and dopamine, elevated substance P in CSF). The disorder is neurophysiologic, not inflammatory or structural.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia = nociplastic pain (central sensitization). NOT inflammatory — normal ESR, CRP, muscle biopsy. NOT neuropathic — no nerve conduction abnormalities. COMLEX: Central sensitization explains why fibromyalgia patients are sensitive to multiple stimuli including light, sound, and temperature, not just mechanical pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 1 — Fibromyalgia diagnosis relies entirely on clinical criteria because standard laboratory tests have poor sensitivity and specificity for nociplastic pain"},{"id":"sdl39_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with fibromyalgia asks why her muscle biopsy was normal despite severe diffuse muscle pain. Which statement most accurately explains the histopathological findings expected in fibromyalgia?","choices":{"A":"Fibromyalgia causes subtle perivascular lymphocytic infiltrates in muscle tissue that are too focal for routine biopsy sampling to reliably detect","B":"Muscle tissue in fibromyalgia is histopathologically normal because the disorder reflects central pain sensitization, not peripheral tissue inflammation or structural damage","C":"Mild type II muscle fiber atrophy from deconditioning is the expected biopsy finding, reflecting disuse effects rather than inflammatory damage","D":"Scattered ragged red fibers visible on Gomori trichrome stain indicate underlying mitochondrial dysfunction as the primary pathogenic mechanism","E":"Patchy endomysial fibrosis is the characteristic biopsy finding that distinguishes fibromyalgia from inflammatory myopathies on histology"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Controlled studies have found no evidence of significant pathologic or biochemical muscle abnormalities in fibromyalgia. The muscle is histopathologically normal. The term \"fibrositis\" used historically implied soft-tissue inflammation, but this was disproven by controlled comparisons. The severe pain in fibromyalgia is a central sensitization phenomenon — the peripheral tissues are normal, but the CNS processing of pain signals is dysregulated. This is why anti-inflammatory treatments do not effectively address the underlying mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia = normal muscle biopsy. This distinguishes it from inflammatory myopathies (elevated CK, abnormal biopsy) and from PMR (periarticular bursitis on imaging). COMLEX: Normal muscle histology combined with normal CK and normal inflammatory markers is the classic laboratory and pathology profile of fibromyalgia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 1 — Fibromyalgia diagnosis relies entirely on clinical criteria because standard laboratory tests have poor sensitivity and specificity for nociplastic pain"},{"id":"sdl39_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following has been most strongly associated as a potential environmental trigger or aggravating factor for fibromyalgia symptom onset?","choices":{"A":"Acute sun exposure triggering mast cell degranulation in skin, releasing histamine and substance P into peripheral sensory nerve endings","B":"High-sodium dietary intake causing fluid retention that increases interstitial pressure on peripheral pain receptors in muscle fasciae","C":"Estrogen fluctuations during the menstrual cycle that alter serotonin receptor density in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord","D":"Physical or emotional trauma including PTSD, certain viral infections, and Lyme disease have been associated with triggering or worsening fibromyalgia symptoms","E":"Occupational repetitive strain creating microtrauma at entheses that overwhelms local tissue repair mechanisms in susceptible individuals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"There is no single causative event in fibromyalgia. However, physical and emotional trauma (including PTSD), infections (particularly certain viruses and Lyme disease), and psychosocial stressors are recognized as potential triggers or aggravating factors. \"Long COVID\" and post-treatment Lyme disease syndrome both produce symptoms strikingly similar to fibromyalgia, suggesting that post-infectious central sensitization may be a shared mechanism. Sleep disturbances and pre-existing mood disorders also independently increase fibromyalgia risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia triggers include trauma (physical/emotional), PTSD, viral infections, and Lyme disease. \"Long COVID\" symptoms overlap heavily with fibromyalgia. COMLEX: There is no single cause; fibromyalgia is a multifactorial condition with genetic predisposition and environmental triggers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 1 — Fibromyalgia diagnosis relies entirely on clinical criteria because standard laboratory tests have poor sensitivity and specificity for nociplastic pain"},{"id":"sdl39_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents with diffuse musculoskeletal pain, fatigue, disturbed sleep, and cognitive difficulties for 18 months. Physical examination reveals widespread soft tissue tenderness. Which laboratory finding is most consistent with a diagnosis of fibromyalgia?","choices":{"A":"Mildly elevated ESR of 28 mm per hour with a normal CRP, suggesting a low-grade inflammatory process contributing to her diffuse pain","B":"Normal CBC, normal ESR, normal CRP, and no autoantibodies on serologic testing, consistent with a nociplastic pain condition without peripheral inflammation","C":"Slightly elevated ANA at a titer of 1 to 40 with a homogeneous pattern, which is commonly found in fibromyalgia patients without lupus","D":"Elevated CK of 350 units per liter suggesting underlying mild inflammatory myopathy as the primary cause of her diffuse muscle pain","E":"Decreased serum serotonin level measurable on routine chemistry panels providing objective biochemical confirmation of central sensitization"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The hallmark laboratory feature of fibromyalgia is that ALL routine laboratory tests are normal: CBC, ESR, CRP, CMP, CK, ANA, RF, and anti-CCP. This completely normal laboratory profile is both characteristic of fibromyalgia and diagnostically important because it helps exclude inflammatory conditions. There is no commercially available laboratory test that confirms fibromyalgia. Serum serotonin is not measured on routine panels and its utility in diagnosis is unproven. A low ANA titer can be found in healthy older adults and does not indicate fibromyalgia or SLE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia = normal labs across the board (ESR, CRP, CK, ANA, CBC all normal). The diagnosis is entirely clinical. COMLEX: Normal labs in a patient with diffuse pain, fatigue, sleep disturbance, and cognitive symptoms points strongly to fibromyalgia over inflammatory conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — The normal CBC, ESR, CRP, and CMP in fibromyalgia directly contrast with the elevated acute phase reactants expected in inflammatory rheumatic disease"},{"id":"sdl39_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The 2016 American College of Rheumatology diagnostic criteria for fibromyalgia incorporate which two scoring components to quantify symptom burden and establish the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Tender point count of at least 11 of 18 standardized points and a pain severity score based on visual analog scale ratings over the past week","B":"Duration of symptoms exceeding 6 months and a positive response to low-dose naltrexone as a confirmatory therapeutic diagnostic trial","C":"Sleep study confirmation of non-restorative sleep pattern and a documented anxiety or depression comorbidity score above threshold","D":"Exclusion of inflammatory arthritis by negative RF and ANA combined with a positive fibromyalgia symptom checklist screening score","E":"Widespread Pain Index score and Symptom Severity Scale score, which together must meet threshold criteria to diagnose fibromyalgia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The 2010/2016 ACR criteria replaced the older 1990 tender point count criteria. The updated criteria use: (1) Widespread Pain Index (WPI), which counts painful body areas from a list of 19 regions, and (2) Symptom Severity Scale (SSS), which scores fatigue, waking unrefreshed, and cognitive symptoms. The older 1990 criteria required 11 of 18 tender points — these are no longer required for diagnosis. The updated criteria allow diagnosis without physical examination tender point testing and better capture the full symptom complex of fibromyalgia including cognitive and sleep symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 2016 ACR fibromyalgia criteria = WPI (Widespread Pain Index) + SSS (Symptom Severity Scale). The old 1990 tender point criteria (11/18 points) are no longer required. COMLEX: Fibromyalgia diagnosis is clinical — no laboratory confirmation required or available.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — The normal CBC, ESR, CRP, and CMP in fibromyalgia directly contrast with the elevated acute phase reactants expected in inflammatory rheumatic disease"},{"id":"sdl39_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman presents with bilateral shoulder and hip girdle aching and morning stiffness of 6 weeks duration. ESR is 8 mm per hour and CRP is within normal limits. Rheumatoid factor is negative. Which diagnosis is best supported by the completely normal inflammatory markers in this clinical context?","choices":{"A":"Polymyalgia rheumatica with atypically normal labs, which occurs in approximately 5 to 20 percent of confirmed PMR patients at diagnosis","B":"Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis, because late-onset RA presenting with proximal stiffness can occasionally have entirely normal acute phase reactants","C":"Giant cell arteritis restricted to the aorta, where temporal artery inflammation may be absent and peripheral inflammatory markers can be normal","D":"Fibromyalgia, because a nociplastic pain condition produces widespread aching and stiffness without elevating ESR, CRP, or hematocrit","E":"Hypothyroid myopathy, as thyroid dysfunction commonly produces proximal aching and stiffness with normal inflammatory markers and negative RF"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A completely normal ESR and CRP effectively excludes active PMR — in two studies, 99% of patients with PMR at diagnosis had elevated CRP, and a normal CRP is said to effectively rule out PMR. When a patient has proximal aching and morning stiffness but completely normal inflammatory markers, fibromyalgia becomes the much more likely diagnosis. While hypothyroid myopathy can present similarly, thyroid function tests should be ordered; normal labs overall support fibromyalgia. PMR with normal labs occurs in only 1.2% of clinically diagnosed cases by one study.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal CRP effectively excludes PMR; abnormal CRP is nearly universal in active PMR. Normal labs with proximal aching = think fibromyalgia first, then hypothyroid. COMLEX: This is the classic PMR vs fibromyalgia discriminator on exams — the ESR and CRP result is the key differentiating laboratory finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — The normal CBC, ESR, CRP, and CMP in fibromyalgia directly contrast with the elevated acute phase reactants expected in inflammatory rheumatic disease"},{"id":"sdl39_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which single laboratory finding most reliably distinguishes polymyalgia rheumatica from fibromyalgia in a patient presenting with bilateral proximal aching and morning stiffness?","choices":{"A":"Positive anti-CCP antibodies, which are elevated in PMR but absent in fibromyalgia due to a lack of citrullinated protein immune reactivity","B":"Elevated serum substance P, which is measurable as a marker of central sensitization in fibromyalgia but is consistently normal in PMR","C":"Elevated CRP or ESR, which is nearly universally present in active PMR but is consistently normal in fibromyalgia across all disease stages","D":"Low serum IL-6 in fibromyalgia, because nociplastic pain dysregulates cytokine production downward rather than causing elevation","E":"Elevated serum creatine kinase in PMR, which reflects periarticular muscle injury from adjacent bursitis and tenosynovitis activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The acute phase reactants — ESR and CRP — are the single most important distinguishing laboratory tests. PMR produces markedly elevated ESR and CRP (99% of PMR patients have elevated CRP at diagnosis), while fibromyalgia produces completely normal ESR and CRP. Anti-CCP and RF are negative in BOTH conditions (PMR is seronegative). CK is normal in both. Substance P is elevated in fibromyalgia but is not routinely measured. IL-6 is actually elevated in PMR, not low. The ESR/CRP is the practical, clinically available discriminating test.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR = elevated ESR/CRP. Fibromyalgia = normal ESR/CRP. Both = negative RF and ANA. COMLEX: This is the most commonly tested distinction between the two conditions; always check acute phase reactants in proximal aching patients over 50.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — PMR produces a characteristic autoantibody-negative, elevated acute phase reactant profile that distinguishes it from fibromyalgia on laboratory testing"},{"id":"sdl39_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Patient 1, a 68-year-old woman, reports bilateral shoulder and hip stiffness that developed almost overnight 3 weeks ago and prevents her from dressing herself. Patient 2, a 55-year-old woman, reports diffuse aching and stiffness that has been present for 4 years and gradually worsened following a stressful divorce. Which feature of the symptom timeline most reliably distinguishes PMR from fibromyalgia in these two presentations?","choices":{"A":"PMR characteristically presents with acute or subacute onset of recent discrete symptoms, whereas fibromyalgia symptoms are typically longstanding and of gradual or insidious onset","B":"The severity of morning stiffness distinguishes the conditions, with fibromyalgia consistently causing more severe and prolonged morning stiffness than PMR","C":"Geographic distribution of stiffness distinguishes the conditions, with fibromyalgia restricted to the shoulder girdle while PMR causes truly widespread diffuse pain","D":"Response to acetaminophen within 24 hours distinguishes PMR from fibromyalgia, as PMR responds to analgesics while fibromyalgia is analgesic-resistant","E":"Age of onset reliably distinguishes the conditions since fibromyalgia never occurs in patients over 60 and PMR never presents before the age of 70"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The onset pattern is a key clinical discriminator. PMR is characterized by recent, acute to subacute onset — often startlingly abrupt, seeming to occur almost overnight. A story of longstanding aching does not suggest PMR. Fibromyalgia, by contrast, typically involves symptoms of longstanding duration that develop gradually, often in association with psychosocial stressors or trigger events. Morning stiffness in PMR is invariable and severe (lasting into late morning), while in fibromyalgia it is more variable. Age of onset is helpful (PMR requires >50) but fibromyalgia can also occur in patients over 50.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR onset = acute/subacute (sometimes overnight). Fibromyalgia onset = chronic, gradual, longstanding. COMLEX: The history \"started 3 weeks ago suddenly\" favors PMR; \"has been going on for years\" favors fibromyalgia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — PMR produces a characteristic autoantibody-negative, elevated acute phase reactant profile that distinguishes it from fibromyalgia on laboratory testing"},{"id":"sdl39_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which characteristic of the response to treatment most sharply distinguishes polymyalgia rheumatica from fibromyalgia, and is itself used as a diagnostic criterion in some PMR classification systems?","choices":{"A":"Fibromyalgia responds dramatically to low-dose prednisone at 10 to 20 mg daily, while PMR requires high-dose steroid therapy at 40 to 60 mg daily","B":"PMR responds completely to hydroxychloroquine within 4 to 6 weeks, while fibromyalgia is entirely unresponsive to any antimalarial therapy","C":"Both conditions respond similarly to duloxetine and pregabalin, but only fibromyalgia responds additionally to serotonin reuptake inhibitors","D":"Fibromyalgia responds to intra-articular corticosteroid shoulder injections, while PMR requires systemic anti-inflammatory therapy for control","E":"PMR responds dramatically to low-dose oral prednisone at 10 to 20 mg daily within days to a week, while fibromyalgia does not respond to corticosteroids"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A dramatic, rapid response to low-dose corticosteroids — typically prednisone 10-20 mg daily with near-complete symptom relief within 48-72 hours — is so characteristic of PMR that it is incorporated as a supportive criterion in some classification systems. Fibromyalgia does not respond to corticosteroids because the underlying mechanism is central sensitization rather than peripheral inflammation. Fibromyalgia responds better to SNRIs (duloxetine), alpha-2-delta ligands (pregabalin, gabapentin), TCAs, aerobic exercise, and CBT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR + rapid dramatic response to prednisone 10-20 mg = confirmatory. Fibromyalgia does NOT respond to prednisone. COMLEX: OMM, aerobic exercise, and SNRIs are more appropriate for fibromyalgia than corticosteroids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — PMR produces a characteristic autoantibody-negative, elevated acute phase reactant profile that distinguishes it from fibromyalgia on laboratory testing"},{"id":"sdl39_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman of Swedish descent presents with a 3-week history of bilateral shoulder aching and stiffness worst in the morning that prevents her from dressing herself. She denies headache, jaw pain, or visual symptoms. ESR is 87 mm/hour and CRP is 42 mg/L. She has no prior joint disease. Which epidemiologic feature of this presentation is most characteristic of the expected population affected by polymyalgia rheumatica?","choices":{"A":"Age over 50 with northern European ancestry, consistent with the demographic group in which PMR incidence is highest globally","B":"ESR greater than 80 mm per hour combined with northern European descent, which together constitute the specific combination required for PMR diagnosis","C":"Acute onset of bilateral shoulder pain as the presenting manifestation, which is pathognomonic for PMR and cannot occur in fibromyalgia","D":"Female sex over 70 with northern European ancestry, representing the peak reproductive-to-aging transition window for autoimmune onset","E":"Absence of prior joint disease with markedly elevated acute phase reactants, representing the classic pre-PMR laboratory baseline state"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The most characteristic epidemiologic features of this presentation are age over 50 (she is 76) and northern European (Swedish) ancestry, the two most strongly associated demographic risk factors for PMR. Peak incidence is between 70 and 80 years. PMR incidence is highest in Scandinavian countries and in people of Scandinavian descent living elsewhere (e.g., Olmsted County, MN). The elevated ESR/CRP supports the diagnosis but is not specific to any demographic. The acute onset and bilateral proximal pattern are clinical rather than epidemiologic features.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR = age >50 + northern European descent + acute proximal girdle stiffness + elevated ESR/CRP. COMLEX: The demographic triad (older adult, female, northern European) combined with acute proximal stiffness and elevated acute phase reactants is the classic PMR presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica"},{"id":"sdl39_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with polymyalgia rheumatica asks his rheumatologist how common his disease is compared with other rheumatic diseases. His wife has rheumatoid arthritis and he wonders how the two diseases compare in prevalence. Which statement most accurately describes the epidemiologic relationship between PMR and RA in the adult population?","choices":{"A":"PMR and RA have equivalent prevalence rates in adults over 50, since both diseases share the HLA-DR4 risk allele as a primary susceptibility factor","B":"PMR is substantially rarer than RA, affecting fewer than 0.1 percent of adults over 50 due to its restricted northern European geographic distribution","C":"PMR affects primarily males over 65 while RA primarily affects females, with no meaningful overlap in the affected population demographics","D":"PMR is more common than RA in adults over 70, since advancing age progressively increases PMR incidence while RA incidence reaches a plateau","E":"PMR is the second most common systemic inflammatory rheumatic disease in adults after RA, with a lifetime risk of approximately 2.43 percent in females and 1.66 percent in males"},"correct":"E","explanation":"PMR is the second most common systemic inflammatory rheumatic disease in adults, after rheumatoid arthritis. The lifetime risk is approximately 2.43% in females and 1.66% in males. RA has a higher overall lifetime prevalence (~1% of the population across all ages), but PMR is notably common in the older adult population. Both conditions have female predominance, both are associated with HLA-DR4, and both can be seronegative, making the differential diagnosis clinically important.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR = 2nd most common systemic inflammatory rheumatic disease after RA in adults. Both have female predominance and HLA-DR4 association. COMLEX: Late-onset RA can mimic PMR; joint erosions on imaging help distinguish RA, and anti-CCP positivity supports RA over PMR.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica"},{"id":"sdl39_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A population study at Olmsted County, Minnesota documents an unusually high PMR incidence of 63.9 per 100,000 per year compared with southern European populations. The researchers attribute this to the ethnic composition of the Olmsted County population. Which ancestry predominates in this population and confers the elevated disease risk?","choices":{"A":"Celtic Irish ancestry, due to disproportionate carriage of HLA-DRB1 alleles associated with inflammatory rheumatic conditions in the Irish population","B":"German ancestry, which is overrepresented in the upper Midwest of the United States and is associated with the globally highest rates of PMR","C":"Finnish ancestry, because the unique Finnish population bottleneck enriched for rare HLA haplotypes specifically predisposing to PMR onset","D":"Scandinavian ancestry, as PMR incidence is highest in Scandinavian countries and in people of northern European descent, and Olmsted County has a predominantly Scandinavian-descent population","E":"British ancestry, because England has the highest documented population-based PMR incidence per 100,000 in all published epidemiologic studies"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Olmsted County, Minnesota is frequently cited in PMR epidemiology because its population is predominantly of Scandinavian descent. Norway has the highest reported PMR incidence in Europe at 113 per 100,000 per year for the over-50 population, while Italy reports only 13 per 100,000. Olmsted County (63.9 per 100,000) falls between these values, consistent with its partial Scandinavian genetic background. This real-world data underscores the strong genetic component of PMR risk linked to HLA-DRB1 alleles prevalent in Scandinavian populations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Olmsted County = high PMR incidence due to Scandinavian-descent population. Norway (113/100,000) vs Italy (13/100,000) illustrates the 9-fold geographic variation. COMLEX: Geographic clustering confirms genetic rather than purely environmental causation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 1 — Recognize the epidemiology of polymyalgia rheumatica"},{"id":"sdl39_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman is diagnosed with PMR. She has no headache, jaw claudication, or visual changes. A research PET scan shows increased FDG uptake at the subclavian arteries bilaterally. Which statement best characterizes the significance of this finding in the context of PMR management?","choices":{"A":"The PET scan confirms active temporal artery involvement requiring immediate escalation to high-dose prednisone to prevent irreversible vision loss","B":"Positive PET scan indicates imminent conversion to clinical GCA within 3 months, warranting prophylactic temporal artery biopsy immediately","C":"FDG uptake in the subclavian arteries is specific for Takayasu arteritis and changes the diagnosis from PMR to Takayasu disease","D":"The finding rules out PMR because subclavian artery FDG uptake is exclusively observed in giant cell arteritis with large-vessel predominance","E":"Subclinical vascular inflammation is present in approximately one-third of PMR patients on PET scan and its long-term clinical prognostic significance is still being defined"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Approximately one-third of consecutive PMR patients show increased FDG vascular uptake on PET scan, predominantly in the subclavian arteries. The intensity is less than that observed in clinical GCA. The clinical significance of this subclinical vascular inflammation is still being defined — patients with \"pure\" PMR rarely develop severe ischemic complications (blindness, stroke) despite this finding. However, close monitoring throughout the disease course is recommended since clinical GCA features can develop during follow-up. This finding does not change the initial treatment strategy for PMR without clinical GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ~1/3 of PMR patients have subclinical vascular inflammation on PET scan. This does NOT require high-dose steroids unless clinical GCA features are present. COMLEX: PET for subclinical GCA in PMR is a research tool; the clinical management threshold is the presence of GCA symptoms (jaw claudication, headache, visual changes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher measuring peripheral blood cytokine levels in patients with PMR and GCA separately finds an overlapping elevation in both conditions. Which cytokine finding would be most expected to be elevated in BOTH PMR and GCA compared with healthy controls?","choices":{"A":"IFN-gamma, which is markedly elevated in PMR but absent in GCA, explaining why PMR does not cause arterial wall granuloma formation","B":"IL-6, which is elevated in peripheral blood of both PMR and GCA patients and is responsible for the constitutional symptoms shared by both conditions","C":"IL-17, which is selectively elevated in GCA but not PMR, reflecting the exclusively large-vessel tropism of Th17-mediated vascular inflammation","D":"TNF-alpha, which is the dominant cytokine in both conditions and explains why anti-TNF therapy is effective first-line treatment in refractory PMR","E":"IL-1 beta alone, which is elevated exclusively in GCA while remaining normal in PMR, accounting for the absence of fever in uncomplicated PMR"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IL-6 is the cytokine most consistently elevated in both PMR and GCA. It drives the constitutional symptoms (fever, fatigue, anorexia, weight loss) and the acute phase response (elevated ESR, CRP, fibrinogen, hepcidin-driven anemia). IFN-gamma is characteristically elevated in GCA (driving granuloma formation) but NOT prominently elevated in PMR — this is the key mechanistic distinction. IL-17 is elevated in both (via the shared Th17 increase), not selective for GCA. Anti-TNF therapy is NOT effective for PMR or GCA; IL-6 receptor blockade (tocilizumab) is effective.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-6 elevated in both PMR and GCA (shared). IFN-gamma elevated in GCA but NOT PMR (distinguishing). Tocilizumab targets IL-6 receptor and is FDA-approved for GCA. COMLEX: Anti-TNF drugs (used in RA) are not effective for PMR or GCA; prednisone remains the primary treatment for PMR.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 73-year-old woman with PMR diagnosed 8 months ago is maintained on prednisone 8 mg daily with good symptom control. She now presents with new-onset temporal headaches and jaw pain with chewing. Which statement most accurately describes the documented risk of GCA development in PMR patients during their disease course?","choices":{"A":"Clinical GCA develops in fewer than 1 percent of PMR patients, and new temporal headache on prednisone is most likely tension-type or steroid-related","B":"Clinical GCA develops in approximately 10 percent of PMR patients within the first year, primarily those with very high initial ESR above 100 mm per hour","C":"Clinical GCA develops in approximately 5 to 30 percent of PMR patients at some point during the disease course, requiring prompt evaluation with temporal artery biopsy","D":"Clinical GCA develops in all PMR patients who are undertreated with prednisone doses below 20 mg daily for longer than 6 months duration","E":"Clinical GCA develops preferentially in HLA-DR4 positive PMR patients with the shared epitope at positions 70 to 74 of the DRbeta chain"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The development of clinical GCA in PMR patients ranges from 5-30% across reported series. New-onset jaw claudication (jaw pain with chewing) and temporal headache in a PMR patient are classic warning signs of GCA and require urgent evaluation — temporal artery biopsy, ophthalmology referral, and possible escalation to high-dose prednisone (40-60 mg/day) to prevent irreversible visual loss. Late-onset GCA can occur even in patients already on low-dose prednisone for PMR if the dose is insufficient to suppress vascular inflammation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR patient develops jaw claudication + headache = URGENT evaluation for GCA. Jaw claudication (jaw pain with chewing/talking that resolves with rest) is the most specific GCA symptom. COMLEX: Temporal artery biopsy should be performed promptly; high-dose steroids should not be delayed while awaiting biopsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Discuss the overlap of polymyalgia rheumatica with giant cell arteritis"},{"id":"sdl39_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with bilateral shoulder and hip girdle stiffness, ESR of 72 mm/hour, and negative RF and anti-CCP is found to be HLA-DRB1 positive. Which statement best distinguishes the role of HLA-DRB1 in polymyalgia rheumatica compared with its role in rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"The identical shared epitope at positions 70 to 74 of the DRbeta chain is the risk allele for both PMR and RA, confirming a common antigen-driven pathogenic pathway","B":"HLA-DRB1 confers risk for PMR only through Class I MHC antigen presentation to CD8+ T cells, unlike RA where Class II molecules present to CD4+ T cells","C":"PMR is associated with HLA-DRB1 alleles encoding a short antigen-binding cleft, while RA is associated with alleles encoding a wider and deeper cleft","D":"HLA-DRB1 in PMR acts as a complement regulatory molecule, while in RA it functions primarily as an antigen-presenting molecule for citrullinated proteins","E":"PMR and GCA share an HLA-DRB1 hypervariable region polymorphism that maps to the antigen-binding cleft and is distinct from the RA-associated shared epitope at positions 70 to 74"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both PMR/GCA and RA involve HLA-DR4 alleles, but the specific polymorphisms differ. In RA, the \"shared epitope\" at positions 70-74 of the DRbeta1 chain presents citrullinated peptides and drives pathogenic T cell responses. In PMR and GCA, the associated polymorphism is in the hypervariable region of HLA-DRB1 that maps to the antigen-binding cleft — importantly, this specific polymorphism is NOT found in RA patients. This distinction suggests that different antigens drive PMR/GCA versus RA despite both involving HLA-DR4 class II molecules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RA HLA = shared epitope (positions 70-74 DRbeta). PMR/GCA HLA = HLA-DRB1 hypervariable region (antigen-binding cleft) — different polymorphism, different antigen. COMLEX: The HLA distinction between PMR and RA also explains why anti-CCP antibodies are positive in RA but negative in PMR.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — Immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen in polymyalgia rheumatica"},{"id":"sdl39_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of immunologic findings in peripheral blood would be most consistent with a diagnosis of polymyalgia rheumatica in a patient presenting with proximal aching and stiffness?","choices":{"A":"Elevated IgG anti-CCP antibodies, increased CD8+ cytotoxic T cells, and decreased NK cells consistent with an autoantibody-driven arthritis","B":"Positive ANA with anti-dsDNA, decreased complement C3 and C4, and peripheral B cell lymphopenia consistent with immune complex consumption","C":"Increased circulating plasmablasts, elevated IgG4 levels, and IL-10 overproduction consistent with IgG4-related inflammatory disease","D":"Increased circulating Th17 cells, decreased regulatory T cells, and elevated serum IL-6 without prominent IFN-gamma predominance","E":"Elevated serum IL-4 and IL-13 with increased eosinophil count and total IgE levels reflecting a Th2-skewed inflammatory response pattern"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The immunologic profile of PMR includes: elevated IL-6 (driving constitutional symptoms and acute phase response), increased Th17 cells (promoting pro-inflammatory IL-17 signaling), and decreased regulatory T cells (impairing peripheral tolerance). Importantly, IFN-gamma-producing T cells are NOT prominent in PMR (unlike GCA). Anti-CCP and ANA are negative. There is no complement consumption. IgG4-related disease has a different cytokine profile (IL-10, IL-4, IL-13) and involves IgG4 deposition in tissues. The absence of IFN-gamma prominence helps distinguish PMR immunopathology from GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR immune profile = elevated IL-6 + Th17 up + Treg down, no IFN-gamma. GCA adds IFN-gamma driving granulomas. Anti-CCP and ANA are negative in both. COMLEX: The shared Th17/Treg imbalance between PMR and GCA provides a mechanistic rationale for why they frequently co-occur.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — Immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen in polymyalgia rheumatica"},{"id":"sdl39_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen associated with polymyalgia rheumatica.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with PMR reports severe bilateral hip and thigh aching. His rheumatologist explains that despite the name, the muscles themselves are not the primary target of inflammation. Which pathologic finding would most accurately characterize the tissue responsible for the proximal pain in PMR?","choices":{"A":"Endomysial inflammation with CD4+ T cell infiltrates surrounding necrotic muscle fibers in the proximal hip musculature on muscle biopsy","B":"Periarticular bursitis and tenosynovitis affecting subdeltoid, subacromial, iliopsoas, and trochanteric bursae as demonstrated by ultrasound, MRI, and PET imaging","C":"Synovial proliferation with pannus formation eroding the glenohumeral joint cartilage in a pattern identical to rheumatoid arthritis","D":"Myofascial trigger points with taut bands and local twitch response in the proximal muscle groups consistent with fibromyalgia overlap syndrome","E":"Necrotizing granulomas within arteriolar walls of proximal muscles, explaining an ischemic mechanism of pain in untreated PMR"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Despite the name \"polymyalgia\" (many muscle pains), the muscles in PMR are histopathologically normal. Modern imaging (ultrasound, MRI, PET-CT) has demonstrated that the proximal pain arises from periarticular structures — specifically bilateral subdeltoid/subacromial bursitis and biceps tenosynovitis at the shoulders, and iliopsoas bursitis, trochanteric bursitis, and hamstring tendinitis at the hips. Bilateral subdeltoid/subacromial bursitis is an imaging hallmark of PMR. The proximal aching represents referred pain from the inflamed bursae and tendons, not from the muscles themselves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR = periarticular bursitis/tenosynovitis (NOT muscle inflammation). Hallmark imaging finding = bilateral subdeltoid/subacromial bursitis on ultrasound. COMLEX: This explains why PMR pain is described as \"aching around the shoulders and hips\" rather than true muscle weakness — there is no myopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — Immunologic abnormalities and HLA antigen in polymyalgia rheumatica"},{"id":"sdl39_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with fibromyalgia reports that light touch is painful, bright lights are uncomfortable, loud sounds bother her, and her pain worsens with any physical activity. Which neurophysiological mechanism best explains this pattern of generalized sensory hypersensitivity?","choices":{"A":"Central sensitization with amplified neural signaling in the CNS causing pain hypersensitivity and cross-modal sensory sensitization extending beyond the musculoskeletal system","B":"Small fiber polyneuropathy reducing the pain threshold of peripheral nociceptors throughout the body surface and skeletal muscle compartments","C":"Elevated peripheral substance P causing direct sensitization of C-fiber nociceptors in skeletal muscle and periarticular fascial planes","D":"Mast cell activation syndrome triggering widespread histamine release that simultaneously sensitizes peripheral pain receptors throughout the body","E":"Widespread autoimmune small vessel vasculitis causing ischemia of peripheral sensory nerves and dorsal root ganglia throughout the body"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Central sensitization is the key mechanism in fibromyalgia. It involves amplification of neural signaling within the CNS, producing pain hypersensitivity (reduced pain threshold), temporal summation (wind-up), and cross-modal sensory sensitization — explaining why these patients are hypersensitive not only to mechanical stimuli but also to light, sound, smell, and temperature. fMRI studies demonstrate greater neuronal activity in pain-processing brain regions in fibromyalgia patients compared with controls for the same stimulus. The term \"nociplastic pain\" captures this altered central processing in the absence of peripheral damage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central sensitization = amplified CNS pain processing. Cross-modal sensitization (light, sound, smell) is a hallmark of fibromyalgia that does not occur in PMR or peripheral inflammatory arthritis. COMLEX: Central sensitization persists even when inflammatory arthritis is well-controlled, explaining why comorbid fibromyalgia in RA patients does not improve with biologics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with fibromyalgia reports that her mother and sister also have the diagnosis. She asks about the genetic component. Which statement most accurately reflects current understanding of the genetic contribution to fibromyalgia?","choices":{"A":"A single high-penetrance gene mutation in the sodium channel gene SCN9A is responsible for all familial fibromyalgia in affected family members","B":"Multiple polymorphisms in COMT, serotonin transporter, and opioid receptor genes contribute to susceptibility, with a sibling recurrence risk ratio of approximately 13.6","C":"Fibromyalgia is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with 90 percent penetrance, explaining the high familial clustering observed","D":"A single HLA allele confers 80 percent of genetic risk, identical to the Class II MHC mechanism in other chronic pain autoimmune conditions","E":"Fibromyalgia has no genetic component, and familial clustering reflects purely shared environmental stressors and learned pain behaviors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fibromyalgia has a significant genetic component without a single causative gene. Key genetic associations include: COMT gene polymorphisms (catecholamine metabolism, pain sensitivity), serotonin transporter (5-HTT) polymorphisms (comorbid pain and depression), and opioid/dopamine receptor polymorphisms. The sibling recurrence risk ratio is approximately 13.6, comparable to many recognized genetic diseases. A large GWAS identified 26 risk loci with heritability confined to brain and neural cell types. However, no single gene is responsible — fibromyalgia is polygenic with environmental gene-environment interactions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia is polygenic — COMT, 5-HTT, opioid receptor polymorphisms. Sibling RR = 13.6 (significant but not Mendelian). COMLEX: The genetic overlap with IBS and chronic fatigue syndrome supports a shared neurobiological substrate for nociplastic conditions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with fibromyalgia also has irritable bowel syndrome and a long history of migraines and chronic fatigue. She asks if these conditions are related to her fibromyalgia. Which statement most accurately characterizes the relationship between fibromyalgia and these co-occurring conditions?","choices":{"A":"The co-occurrence reflects a shared autoimmune mechanism in which ANA-positive patients develop multi-organ nociplastic inflammation simultaneously","B":"Fibromyalgia causes these conditions secondarily through dysautonomia-mediated visceral hypersensitivity and reduced cortisol reactivity","C":"This cluster represents a distinct autoimmune syndrome driven by anti-neuronal antibodies that target dorsal root ganglion neurons","D":"The IBS and migraines are medication side effects from serotonergic drugs commonly used to treat fibromyalgia-associated depression","E":"Fibromyalgia commonly co-occurs with IBS, chronic fatigue syndrome, and headache disorders as part of a broader spectrum of nociplastic pain and central sensitization"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fibromyalgia frequently co-occurs with IBS, myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome (ME/CFS), headache disorders, and other chronic overlapping conditions. These represent chronic primary pain and nociplastic conditions that share the underlying mechanism of central sensitization. Swedish national registry data showed fibromyalgia genetic risk overlaps with IBS and CFS but differs from classic autoimmune diseases. Notably, comorbid fibromyalgia persists in up to 40% of patients with rheumatic disease despite effective immunosuppression, confirming the distinct central neurologic mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia comorbidities include IBS, ME/CFS, migraine, and PTSD — all nociplastic/central sensitization disorders. Immunosuppression does not treat fibromyalgia even when comorbid with RA. COMLEX: Recognizing fibromyalgia overlap with inflammatory disease prevents over-treatment with immunosuppressants for symptoms that are nociplastic in origin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 4 — Define the characteristics of fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with fibromyalgia asks why her laboratory tests keep coming back normal despite her debilitating pain. Which explanation most accurately describes why laboratory tests are normal in fibromyalgia?","choices":{"A":"Fibromyalgia is a disorder of central pain processing without peripheral inflammation, so routine inflammatory markers including ESR, CRP, and CBC are expected to be normal","B":"Fibromyalgia is associated with low-grade inflammation that falls below the detection threshold of standard clinical laboratory assay sensitivity","C":"Standard ESR and CRP tests fail to detect the microglial activation and neuroinflammation that is specific to nociplastic pain conditions","D":"Fibromyalgia suppresses the acute phase response via hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis dysregulation, actively lowering inflammatory markers","E":"Patients with fibromyalgia have peripheral immune tolerance that prevents upregulation of inflammatory cytokines even during symptomatic flares"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Laboratory tests are normal in fibromyalgia because there is no peripheral inflammation, tissue damage, autoantibody production, or immune activation in the musculoskeletal system. The pathology is entirely within the CNS (altered pain processing, neurotransmitter dysregulation). ESR, CRP, CBC, CK, ANA, RF, and metabolic panel are all expected to be normal. This is not a limitation of test sensitivity — there genuinely is nothing peripheral to detect. This fundamental difference from inflammatory conditions is both diagnostically useful and explanatory for why anti-inflammatory treatments do not address the underlying mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal labs in fibromyalgia = expected finding, not a missed diagnosis. The pathology is central, not peripheral. COMLEX: An extensive workup chasing abnormal labs in fibromyalgia is unnecessary and can reinforce illness behavior; a confident clinical diagnosis based on criteria is appropriate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with chronic widespread pain, fatigue, and sleep disturbance has a positive ANA at 1:80 with a speckled pattern. RF and anti-CCP are negative. ESR and CRP are normal. Her physician is considering fibromyalgia. Which statement most accurately reflects the significance of the positive ANA in this context?","choices":{"A":"A positive ANA at any titer rules out fibromyalgia, as autoantibody production requires an inflammatory milieu incompatible with nociplastic pain","B":"A low titer positive ANA can be found in healthy individuals and does not confirm an autoimmune disease in the absence of supporting clinical or laboratory features","C":"The speckled ANA pattern is the specific pattern associated with fibromyalgia, distinguishing it from the homogeneous pattern seen in SLE","D":"Any positive ANA requires immunosuppressive therapy before attributing symptoms to fibromyalgia, to avoid missing subclinical lupus nephritis","E":"A positive ANA combined with normal ESR and CRP confirms mixed connective tissue disease as the underlying diagnosis in this patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Low-titer ANA (1:40 to 1:160) is found in up to 10% of healthy individuals, especially older adults. A positive ANA requires clinical context: lupus criteria, complement levels, anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith, anti-Sm/RNP, and organ manifestations. In this patient with normal ESR, CRP, and no other features of autoimmune disease, the positive ANA is an incidental finding and does not diagnose SLE, MCTD, or any other autoimmune disease. Fibromyalgia is characterized by negative autoantibodies, but the occasional low-titer ANA in a fibromyalgia patient does not change the diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low titer ANA (1:40-1:160) = not diagnostic of autoimmune disease; requires clinical correlation. Fibromyalgia = negative ANA (expected), but a low-titer incidental ANA does not exclude fibromyalgia. COMLEX: Avoid over-interpreting low ANA titers; the clinical picture drives the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman with diffuse muscle pain and perceived weakness receives laboratory testing: CK 38 units/L (normal), ESR 11 mm/hour, CRP 2.1 mg/L, CBC within normal limits, ANA negative. Which interpretation of this result pattern is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"The normal CK excludes statin-induced myopathy but the normal ESR and CRP are insufficient to distinguish fibromyalgia from early PMR in this age group","B":"The pattern is nonspecific and cannot differentiate fibromyalgia from seronegative RA without dedicated imaging of the peripheral joints","C":"The completely normal laboratory profile is consistent with fibromyalgia, which does not cause elevations in CK, inflammatory markers, or any CBC abnormalities","D":"Normal CK with normal inflammatory markers strongly points toward hypothyroidism as the most likely etiology requiring urgent thyroid function tests","E":"The combination of normal labs with diffuse pain requires muscle biopsy to exclude inclusion body myositis before confirming fibromyalgia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A completely normal CK, ESR, CRP, CBC, and negative ANA are the expected and characteristic laboratory findings in fibromyalgia. Normal CK excludes inflammatory myopathies (dermatomyositis, polymyositis) and statin-induced myopathy. Normal ESR and CRP exclude PMR and other inflammatory conditions. Normal CBC excludes anemia of chronic disease. Negative ANA makes lupus and MCTD unlikely. At age 39, PMR is essentially excluded (requires >50). This pattern supports fibromyalgia — the diagnosis is clinical and laboratory testing serves primarily to exclude other conditions. Muscle biopsy is not warranted with a normal CK.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibromyalgia workup strategy = rule out inflammatory/metabolic/autoimmune causes with labs (all normal). Normal CK + normal ESR/CRP + negative ANA = fibromyalgia profile. COMLEX: Muscle biopsy is only warranted if CK is elevated suggesting inflammatory myopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 5 — Discuss the laboratory abnormalities consistent with fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman presents with bilateral shoulder aching, hip girdle stiffness, and morning stiffness lasting until noon. ESR is 68 mm/hour and CRP is elevated. She is started on prednisone 15 mg daily. Within 72 hours, she reports near-complete resolution of all symptoms. What does this rapid, dramatic response to low-dose prednisone most strongly suggest?","choices":{"A":"Fibromyalgia with a placebo-mediated central sensitization reversal, since any effective intervention with strong expectation reduces nociplastic pain","B":"Giant cell arteritis without cranial ischemic complications, as uncomplicated GCA without visual symptoms responds to prednisone 15 mg before dose escalation","C":"Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis, as early RA can show complete symptomatic remission on low-dose corticosteroids before DMARD therapy initiation","D":"Polymyalgia rheumatica, because a dramatic response to low-dose prednisone of 10 to 20 mg daily within days is a characteristic feature that supports the PMR diagnosis","E":"Reactive arthritis with a post-infectious etiology, as post-infectious arthritis regularly resolves within 48 to 72 hours of systemic corticosteroid initiation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A dramatic and rapid response to low-dose prednisone (10-20 mg/day) is so characteristic of PMR that it is incorporated as a supportive diagnostic criterion in some classification systems. Near-complete relief within 48-72 hours strongly supports PMR. Fibromyalgia does not respond to corticosteroids — any apparent response is a placebo effect and would not be dramatic or sustained. GCA typically requires higher doses (40-60 mg/day) for cranial symptoms. RA may improve somewhat on low-dose steroids but not with the dramatic rapidity seen in PMR. This treatment response test is clinically useful when the diagnosis is uncertain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR = dramatic near-complete response to prednisone 15-20 mg/day within days. This response is nearly diagnostic. Lack of response should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis. COMLEX: \"The test of prednisone\" is a clinical pearl for PMR — if a suspected PMR patient does not respond dramatically to low-dose prednisone, reconsider the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Patient 1 is a 72-year-old woman whose bilateral shoulder and hip stiffness began acutely 4 weeks ago and prevents her from raising her arms above her head. Patient 2 is a 46-year-old woman whose diffuse aching and fatigue have been present for 5 years following a stressful divorce. Which pairing of diagnosis with supporting features is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Patient 1 is most likely PMR based on acute onset over age 50 with proximal girdle limitation; Patient 2 is most likely fibromyalgia based on chronic onset with a psychosocial trigger below age 50","B":"Patient 1 is most likely fibromyalgia because severe functional limitation preventing shoulder abduction is characteristic of central sensitization","C":"Patient 2 is most likely PMR because female sex and emotional stress are dominant risk factors for new-onset polymyalgia rheumatica","D":"Both patients most likely have fibromyalgia because morning stiffness is a universal feature of central sensitization disorders at any age","E":"Both patients require identical workup including temporal artery biopsy before differentiating between PMR and fibromyalgia diagnoses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Patient 1: age over 50 + acute onset (4 weeks) + proximal girdle involvement with functional limitation = PMR. Elevated acute phase reactants would confirm. Patient 2: age under 50 + chronic onset (5 years) + psychosocial trigger (divorce) + widespread symptoms = fibromyalgia. PMR requires age >50 by definition. Fibromyalgia can occur at any age and frequently follows stressful events. Temporal artery biopsy is only indicated when clinical GCA features (jaw claudication, headache, visual symptoms) are present — it is not part of the standard PMR or fibromyalgia workup.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Age >50 + acute proximal stiffness + elevated markers = PMR. Age variable + chronic widespread symptoms + normal labs + psychosocial trigger = fibromyalgia. COMLEX: Temporal artery biopsy is NOT indicated in uncomplicated PMR without clinical GCA features.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia"},{"id":"sdl39_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which comparison between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia is most accurate when evaluating a patient with bilateral proximal aching and morning stiffness?","choices":{"A":"Both conditions produce elevated ESR and CRP, but PMR additionally causes positive ANA and RF while fibromyalgia lacks any autoantibodies","B":"Fibromyalgia is associated with bilateral subacromial bursitis on ultrasound, while PMR produces diffuse soft tissue tenderness without any bursitis","C":"PMR produces elevated acute phase reactants and periarticular bursitis on imaging, while fibromyalgia produces normal labs, normal imaging, and normal muscle histology","D":"PMR occurs predominantly in patients under 50 with a chronic indolent course, while fibromyalgia predominantly affects older patients with acute onset","E":"Both conditions respond equally to the same treatment regimen, with low-dose prednisone 10 mg daily producing equivalent symptom relief in both"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The key distinguishing features: PMR = elevated ESR/CRP (nearly universal), negative RF/ANA, periarticular bursitis/tenosynovitis on imaging, normal muscle biopsy, dramatic prednisone response, age >50, acute onset. Fibromyalgia = normal ESR/CRP, normal imaging, normal muscle biopsy, no prednisone response, any age (often <50), chronic onset. Both have negative RF and ANA — this is a trap in answer choices. PMR does NOT cause positive ANA or RF. Bilateral subacromial bursitis is pathognomonic for PMR on imaging, not for fibromyalgia. Prednisone works dramatically in PMR and not at all in fibromyalgia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Master the PMR vs fibromyalgia comparison table: age, onset, labs (ESR/CRP), imaging (bursitis), muscle histology, prednisone response. Both: negative RF, negative ANA. Key difference: elevated vs normal ESR/CRP. COMLEX: This is high-yield for COMLEX clinical presentations; the complete picture of both conditions is frequently tested.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 6 — Contrast the differences between polymyalgia rheumatica and fibromyalgia"}]},{"sdlNumber":40,"title":"Vasculitides: Giant Cell, Takayasu & Polyarteritis Nodosa","questions":[{"id":"sdl40_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring in arteries affected by giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with new-onset temporal headache undergoes temporal artery biopsy. Pathology shows granulomatous inflammation centered on the internal elastic membrane with multinucleated giant cells and elastic lamina fragmentation. Which of the following best describes the predominant inflammatory cell infiltrate in this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophils and eosinophils forming microabscesses within the media, with sparse macrophage involvement at the elastic lamina","B":"B lymphocytes and plasma cells arranged in a follicular pattern within the adventitia, with rare macrophage infiltration","C":"CD4+ T cells and macrophages, with CD4+ cells outnumbering CD8+ T cells throughout the granulomatous infiltrate","D":"Natural killer cells and mast cells releasing histamine at the internal elastic lamina, with minimal T cell contribution","E":"Neutrophils forming palisading rows around a central zone of necrosis, with eosinophils at the periphery of the lesion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"GCA is a T-cell-mediated granulomatous vasculitis. The predominant infiltrate is CD4+ T cells and macrophages, with CD4 outnumbering CD8 cells. Macrophages fuse to form the characteristic multinucleated giant cells. Neutrophils and eosinophils are not the dominant cell type; those patterns are more typical of ANCA-associated or eosinophilic vasculitides. B cells in a follicular pattern are characteristic of lymphomatoid or rheumatoid vessel disease, not GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic histology = granulomatous inflammation centered on internal elastic membrane; CD4 > CD8 T cells; giant cells in ~75% of adequate biopsies. COMLEX: GCA is a Type IV hypersensitivity reaction; contrast with Type III (immune complex) seen in PAN.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Granulomatous vasculitis in GCA exemplifies Type IV cell-mediated hypersensitivity directed at vessel wall antigens."},{"id":"sdl40_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring in arteries affected by giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the spatial distribution of inflammatory lesions along the temporal artery in giant cell arteritis and its implication for diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Lesions are patchy and skip along the vessel length, producing skip areas that can yield false-negative biopsy results; accurate diagnosis requires a biopsy greater than 1 cm in length","B":"Lesions are continuous and circumferential, uniformly thickening the vessel from lumen to adventitia throughout its entire length","C":"Lesions are concentrated exclusively at vessel branch points and spare the straight segments of the superficial temporal artery","D":"Lesions begin only in the adventitia and spread inward to the media over months, so early biopsies show only adventitial changes","E":"Lesions selectively target the vasa vasorum while sparing the main vessel wall, producing a pattern of outer wall ischemia only"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pathologic changes of GCA are notoriously patchy along the length of any affected vessel. This discontinuous distribution — known as skip lesions — means that even an adequate biopsy can miss active disease. For this reason, a biopsy longer than 1 cm is recommended. A negative biopsy does not exclude the diagnosis. The other descriptions would imply uniform or anatomically predictable disease, which does not characterize GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA biopsy must be >1 cm due to skip lesions; negative biopsy does not exclude diagnosis. COMLEX: Do not delay steroids for biopsy if ocular symptoms are present — vision loss can be sudden and permanent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Granulomatous vasculitis in GCA exemplifies Type IV cell-mediated hypersensitivity directed at vessel wall antigens."},{"id":"sdl40_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring in arteries affected by giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A temporal artery biopsy shows medial attenuation with scarring, adventitial fibrosis, intimal thickening, and fragmentation of greater than 30% of the internal elastic lamina circumference. Active granulomatous inflammation and giant cells are absent. Which of the following best identifies this histologic phase of giant cell arteritis?","choices":{"A":"Active acute phase of GCA, in which neutrophilic infiltration of the internal elastic lamina is the dominant finding","B":"Active granulomatous phase of GCA, in which dense CD4+ T cell infiltration and giant cell formation are expected","C":"Pre-disease susceptibility state in which the vessel wall expresses the target antigen without triggering immune activation","D":"Concurrent superinfection phase, in which neutrophils have replaced the granulomatous response following bacterial invasion","E":"Healed phase of GCA, in which active granulomatous inflammation has resolved and been replaced by chronic fibrotic scarring with residual elastic lamina destruction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The description matches healed GCA. Active disease shows granulomatous inflammation with giant cells, CD4+ T cells, and macrophages centered on the internal elastic membrane. Healed lesions show medial attenuation and scarring, intimal thickening, fragmentation of greater than 30% of the internal elastic lamina circumference, and adventitial fibrosis — but no active granulomatous infiltrate. Recognizing the healed phase is important because it can be mistaken for other causes of fibrous arterial thickening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Healed GCA = medial scarring + intimal thickening + elastic lamina fragmentation >30% circumference; no active granulomas. COMLEX: Residual intimal thickening in healed GCA can cause persistent ischemia even after inflammation resolves.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Granulomatous vasculitis in GCA exemplifies Type IV cell-mediated hypersensitivity directed at vessel wall antigens."},{"id":"sdl40_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Define the patient population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following patient profiles most accurately defines the population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis?","choices":{"A":"Children under age 10 with recurrent fever and cervical lymphadenopathy of East Asian descent, in whom Kawasaki disease is the predominant vasculitis","B":"Women aged 15 to 30 with Asian or South American ancestry and diminished upper extremity pulses, in whom Takayasu arteritis is the predominant diagnosis","C":"Men over age 40 with HBsAg seropositivity and a history of necrotizing skin ulcers, in whom PAN from hepatitis B is the predominant vasculitis","D":"Adults over age 50, predominantly female, with northern European or Scandinavian ancestry, in whom GCA is the most common form of vasculitis","E":"Young adults of any sex with HLA-B27 positivity and inflammatory back pain, in whom spondyloarthritis-associated vasculitis is the predominant diagnosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GCA is rare before age 50 and peaks in incidence in the 70s. It is the most common form of vasculitis in elderly adults in the United States and Europe. Female sex and northern European or Scandinavian ancestry are well-established risk factors, with Norway having among the highest reported incidences. The other profiles describe the demographics of Kawasaki disease, Takayasu arteritis, PAN, and spondyloarthritis-associated vasculitis, respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA = age >50, female, Scandinavian/northern European; most common vasculitis in the elderly. COMLEX: GCA is associated with PMR; assess for shoulder/hip girdle aching in any GCA patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — The same elderly northern European population vulnerable to GCA carries a 5-30% lifetime risk of developing PMR."},{"id":"sdl40_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Define the patient population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 73-year-old woman develops sudden monocular vision loss. Her ESR is 112 mm/hr and she reports weeks of jaw pain with chewing and scalp tenderness. Which vessel is most directly responsible for the vision loss in giant cell arteritis, and what is the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"The subclavian artery, in which upper extremity ischemia causes a subclavian steal syndrome that reflexively reduces retinal perfusion","B":"The posterior communicating artery, in which granulomatous compression of the third cranial nerve produces visual field defects","C":"The ophthalmic artery, in which granulomatous vasculitis causes ischemia of the optic nerve head, producing anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","D":"The central retinal vein, in which venous stasis from markedly elevated ESR causes venous occlusion retinopathy","E":"The basilar artery, in which posterior circulation ischemia affects the occipital cortex and produces cortical blindness"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When giant cell arteritis involves the temporal arteries, the ophthalmic and vertebral arteries are also likely involved. Granulomatous vasculitis of the ophthalmic artery reduces perfusion to the optic nerve head, causing anterior ischemic optic neuropathy and sudden, often permanent monocular vision loss. This is the most feared complication of GCA and mandates urgent corticosteroid treatment. The other vessels do not explain monocular vision loss in this context.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ophthalmic artery involvement in GCA causes anterior ischemic optic neuropathy; start steroids IMMEDIATELY to prevent blindness. COMLEX: Jaw claudication and scalp tenderness are pathognomonic for GCA; do not wait for biopsy before treating if ocular symptoms are present.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — The same elderly northern European population vulnerable to GCA carries a 5-30% lifetime risk of developing PMR."},{"id":"sdl40_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Define the patient population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies the most common form of vasculitis among elderly adults in the United States and Europe?","choices":{"A":"Polyarteritis nodosa, which is the most common vasculitis in elderly adults due to its predilection for renal and visceral vessel involvement","B":"Takayasu arteritis, which is the most common vasculitis in elderly adults because of its global distribution and age-independent incidence","C":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which is the most common vasculitis in elderly adults due to its association with ANCA and renal failure","D":"Microscopic polyangiitis, which is the most common vasculitis in elderly adults because ANCA-associated disease peaks in the sixth and seventh decades","E":"Giant cell arteritis, which is the most common large vessel vasculitis in individuals over age 50 and is rare before that age"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Giant cell arteritis is explicitly described as the most common form of vasculitis among elderly adults in the United States and Europe. It is rare before age 50 and its incidence increases with age. The other vasculitides — PAN, Takayasu, GPA, and MPA — each have different demographic peaks and are not the most common vasculitis in elderly populations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA = most common vasculitis in elderly adults in the US and Europe; Takayasu = most common large vessel vasculitis in patients under 50. COMLEX: PMR is associated with GCA in up to 50% of GCA patients; bilateral shoulder girdle aching in an elderly woman warrants screening for GCA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — The same elderly northern European population vulnerable to GCA carries a 5-30% lifetime risk of developing PMR."},{"id":"sdl40_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the immune response felt to perpetuate the tissue damage seen in giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the initiating step in the immune pathogenesis of giant cell arteritis?","choices":{"A":"B cells in the media produce anti-endothelial cell antibodies that activate complement, causing fibrinoid necrosis and secondary T cell recruitment","B":"CD4+ T cells are activated in the adventitia by dendritic cells that recognize an uncharacterized vessel wall antigen, triggering the granulomatous inflammatory cascade","C":"Neutrophils degranulate within the vasa vasorum, releasing elastase that fragments the internal elastic lamina and attracts CD4+ T cells secondarily","D":"Mast cells in the adventitia release histamine that increases vascular permeability and drives the initial recruitment of Th1 and Th17 cells","E":"Eosinophils infiltrate the media in response to IL-5 released by Th2 cells activated by vessel wall antigens presented by adventitial macrophages"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GCA is initiated in the adventitia. Dendritic cells residing in the adventitia present an uncharacterized vessel wall antigen to CD4+ T cells entering via the vasa vasorum. This activates CD4+ T cells and initiates the granulomatous cascade. Anti-endothelial antibodies play a contributing but not initiating role. Neutrophil elastase, mast cell histamine, and eosinophil-IL-5 pathways are not the primary drivers of GCA pathogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA immune cascade initiates in the adventitia via CD4+ T cells activated by adventitial dendritic cells; vessel wall antigen is uncharacterized. COMLEX: T-cell-mediated (Type IV) hypersensitivity; excellent steroid response confirms immune etiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — CD4+ Th1 cells releasing IFN-gamma and Th17 cells releasing IL-17 are the key cytokine drivers of GCA tissue damage."},{"id":"sdl40_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the immune response felt to perpetuate the tissue damage seen in giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In giant cell arteritis, Th1 CD4+ cells produce IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages within the vessel media. Which of the following best describes the downstream consequences of macrophage activation in this setting?","choices":{"A":"Macrophage-derived IL-4 suppresses local inflammation and shifts the immune response toward Th2-mediated tissue healing and fibroblast activation","B":"Macrophages release IL-10 to downregulate Th17 cells and prevent progressive granuloma formation at the internal elastic lamina","C":"Activated macrophages fuse into multinucleated giant cells, produce reactive oxygen species, and secrete matrix metalloproteinases that destroy the internal elastic lamina","D":"Macrophages present antigen to CD8+ cytotoxic T cells, which directly kill smooth muscle cells lining the media of the temporal artery","E":"Macrophage-derived TGF-beta drives fibrous scarring of the adventitia while simultaneously inhibiting further CD4+ T cell entry via the vasa vasorum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IFN-gamma from Th1 cells activates macrophages, which are the central effector cells in GCA. Activated macrophages fuse to form multinucleated giant cells, produce reactive oxygen species that cause oxidative tissue damage, and secrete matrix metalloproteinases that degrade the internal elastic lamina. This leads to the characteristic elastic lamina fragmentation seen on biopsy. IL-4, IL-10, and TGF-beta would suppress rather than drive the active inflammatory phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IFN-gamma from Th1 activates macrophages that form giant cells and destroy elastic lamina via reactive oxygen species and MMPs. COMLEX: Macrophage-mediated elastic lamina destruction explains the intimal thickening and luminal narrowing that can cause ischemic complications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — CD4+ Th1 cells releasing IFN-gamma and Th17 cells releasing IL-17 are the key cytokine drivers of GCA tissue damage."},{"id":"sdl40_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the immune response felt to perpetuate the tissue damage seen in giant cell arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Giant cell arteritis shows an excellent therapeutic response to corticosteroids. Which of the following cytokine profiles most accurately represents the immune pathways that corticosteroids target in GCA?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 and IL-13 from Th2 cells, which drive eosinophil recruitment and mast cell degranulation at the vessel wall in an allergic-type response","B":"IL-5 and IL-9 from innate lymphoid cells, which drive eosinophilic granulomatous inflammation of the aorta and branch vessels","C":"TGF-beta and IL-10 from regulatory T cells, which drive autoantibody production against the internal elastic lamina and mediate fibrosis","D":"IFN-gamma from Th1 cells and IL-17 from Th17 cells, which together drive granulomatous macrophage activation and progressive vessel wall destruction","E":"IL-2 from CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and perforin-mediated killing of endothelial cells lining the temporal and ophthalmic arteries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GCA involves two parallel pathways: Th1 cells producing IFN-gamma activate macrophages and drive granuloma formation, while Th17 cells producing IL-17 amplify vessel wall inflammation. Corticosteroids suppress both cytokine pathways and the downstream macrophage activation, explaining their dramatic efficacy. The other cytokine profiles describe Th2, innate lymphoid cell, regulatory T cell, and CD8 cytotoxic mechanisms, none of which are the primary drivers of GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA = Th1 IFN-gamma + Th17 IL-17 as key cytokines; corticosteroids target both pathways. COMLEX: Anti-TNF therapies and IL-6 receptor antagonists (tocilizumab) are used as steroid-sparing agents in GCA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — CD4+ Th1 cells releasing IFN-gamma and Th17 cells releasing IL-17 are the key cytokine drivers of GCA tissue damage."},{"id":"sdl40_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the demographics and pathology of Takayasu arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies the key demographic distinction used to differentiate Takayasu arteritis from giant cell arteritis when their histology is identical?","choices":{"A":"Takayasu arteritis is diagnosed in patients under age 50, predominantly young women, while giant cell arteritis is diagnosed in adults over age 50","B":"Takayasu arteritis is ANCA-positive and targets medium vessels exclusively, while giant cell arteritis is ANCA-negative and targets only large vessels","C":"Takayasu arteritis is confined to Japanese and East Asian populations by definition, while giant cell arteritis is an exclusively northern European disease","D":"Takayasu arteritis targets the pulmonary circulation exclusively, while giant cell arteritis targets only the temporal and ophthalmic arteries","E":"Takayasu arteritis is caused by HBsAg immune complex deposition in vessel walls, while giant cell arteritis is a T-cell-mediated granulomatous process"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Takayasu arteritis and GCA share the same granulomatous histology and are distinguished primarily by patient age: Takayasu is diagnosed in patients under 50, while GCA is diagnosed in those over 50. Both are T-cell-mediated and ANCA-negative. Takayasu has a global distribution, not confined to Japan. Takayasu targets the aortic arch and great vessels, not the pulmonary circulation, and is not caused by HBsAg immune complexes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The only distinguishing factor between GCA and Takayasu is age: under 50 = Takayasu, over 50 = GCA; histology is identical in both. COMLEX: Takayasu = pulseless disease; absent upper extremity pulses in a young woman is the classic presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Takayasu arteritis represents a T-cell-mediated autoimmune vasculitis where the arterial wall serves as the target antigen."},{"id":"sdl40_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the demographics and pathology of Takayasu arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman presents with reduced blood pressure and diminished pulses in both upper extremities, fatigue, and visual disturbances. Angiography shows narrowing of the aortic arch and brachiocephalic arteries. Biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation with giant cells histologically indistinguishable from giant cell arteritis. Which of the following best describes the pathologic mechanism responsible for her weakened pulses?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrosis of the vasa vasorum causes intramural hematoma that compresses the vessel lumen from the outside","B":"ANCA-mediated neutrophil activation destroys the media of the subclavian arteries, causing aneurysm formation and reduced distal flow","C":"Immune complex deposition in the intima activates complement and causes segmental thrombotic occlusion of the subclavian arteries","D":"Transmural fibrous thickening with intimal hyperplasia causes marked luminal narrowing of the great vessels arising from the aortic arch, reducing perfusion to the upper extremities","E":"Eosinophilic infiltration of the adventitia triggers recurrent vasospasm of the subclavian arteries in cold environments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In Takayasu arteritis, granulomatous inflammation causes transmural fibrous thickening with intimal hyperplasia, leading to marked narrowing or obliteration of the great vessel lumens. This structural stenosis — not vasospasm, thrombosis, or aneurysm — explains the reduced blood pressure and absent pulses in the upper extremities. This is why Takayasu is also called pulseless disease. ANCA is not present, and immune complex deposition characterizes PAN, not Takayasu.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Takayasu = intimal hyperplasia and fibrous thickening narrows aortic arch branch vessels, causing absent pulses in upper extremities. COMLEX: Claudication in legs if distal aorta involved; pulmonary hypertension if pulmonary artery involved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Takayasu arteritis represents a T-cell-mediated autoimmune vasculitis where the arterial wall serves as the target antigen."},{"id":"sdl40_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the demographics and pathology of Takayasu arteritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with known Takayasu arteritis develops new-onset hypertension with a blood pressure of 162/96 mmHg. Imaging confirms bilateral renal artery stenosis. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of her hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Immune complex deposition in the glomerular mesangium activates complement, causing membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis and secondary volume-dependent hypertension","B":"Granulomatous inflammation and intimal hyperplasia narrow the renal artery lumen, reducing renal perfusion and activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","C":"ANCA-mediated necrotizing glomerulonephritis destroys renal parenchyma, reducing GFR and causing hypertension through fluid retention","D":"Fibrinoid necrosis of the afferent arterioles mimicking PAN causes microinfarcts that reduce functional renal mass and increase renin secretion","E":"Aortic root dilation from Takayasu causes aortic valve regurgitation with volume overload, producing high-output systolic hypertension"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Renal artery involvement occurs in roughly half of Takayasu arteritis patients. Granulomatous inflammation and intimal hyperplasia narrow the renal artery lumen, causing renovascular ischemia. Reduced renal perfusion pressure activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, leading to systemic hypertension. This is distinct from glomerulonephritis (ANCA-associated diseases) or immune complex nephritis, neither of which is the mechanism in Takayasu.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renal artery involvement in Takayasu causes renovascular hypertension in ~50% of patients via RAAS activation. COMLEX: Blood pressure measurement in both arms is important in Takayasu; asymmetric readings indicate subclavian stenosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Takayasu arteritis represents a T-cell-mediated autoimmune vasculitis where the arterial wall serves as the target antigen."},{"id":"sdl40_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the potential organ involvement, pathology, and possible lab findings in polyarteritis nodosa.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Polyarteritis nodosa is a systemic vasculitis of small to medium muscular arteries. Which of the following vascular beds is characteristically spared in PAN, regardless of disease severity?","choices":{"A":"The renal arteries, which are classically spared in PAN, with hypertension attributed solely to mesenteric inflammation in this disease","B":"The mesenteric arteries, which are not involved in PAN because small muscular arteries in the GI tract are protected from immune complex deposition","C":"The pulmonary circulation, which is characteristically spared by PAN regardless of disease severity or extent of systemic involvement","D":"The coronary arteries, which are never affected in PAN despite involvement of other medium muscular arteries throughout the body","E":"The hepatic arteries, which are spared in all forms of PAN including hepatitis B-associated disease due to portal circulation protection"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A hallmark feature of PAN is that it spares the pulmonary circulation. In contrast, it typically affects renal and visceral vessels — including mesenteric, hepatic, and coronary arteries — in descending order of frequency. The sparing of pulmonary vessels distinguishes PAN from microscopic polyangiitis, which does involve pulmonary capillaries. Renal involvement is actually the most common and most morbid manifestation of PAN.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAN spares the pulmonary circulation; this is the classic board distinction from MPA and GPA. COMLEX: Viscerosomatic reflexes from renal and mesenteric ischemia may be an osteopathic examination topic in PAN.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — HBsAg immune complex deposition in PAN vessel walls exemplifies Type III hypersensitivity causing fibrinoid necrosis."},{"id":"sdl40_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the potential organ involvement, pathology, and possible lab findings in polyarteritis nodosa.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the pathologic hallmark of polyarteritis nodosa on biopsy or autopsy?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse, global necrotizing inflammation involving all vessel segments uniformly, with sparing of the adventitia and internal elastic lamina","B":"Granulomatous inflammation with multinucleated giant cells centered on the internal elastic membrane with elastic lamina fragmentation","C":"ANCA-mediated leukocytoclastic vasculitis involving capillaries and venules, with sparse immune deposits on immunofluorescence","D":"Segmental transmural necrotizing inflammation with fibrinoid necrosis of small to medium muscular arteries, often with superimposed aneurysm formation or luminal thrombosis","E":"Eosinophil-rich necrotizing vasculitis of small to medium vessels associated with asthma and marked peripheral blood eosinophilia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PAN is characterized by segmental transmural necrotizing inflammation of small to medium muscular arteries, with fibrinoid necrosis, often accompanied by aneurysm formation and thrombosis. Lesions are typically segmental and show a predilection for branch points. The granulomatous giant cell pattern describes GCA/Takayasu. ANCA-associated leukocytoclastic vasculitis describes MPA or GPA. Eosinophil-rich inflammation with asthma describes eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAN = segmental transmural fibrinoid necrosis + aneurysm at branch points; all lesion stages (acute and chronic) coexist simultaneously. COMLEX: Microaneurysms on mesenteric or renal angiography are diagnostic in the appropriate clinical context.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — HBsAg immune complex deposition in PAN vessel walls exemplifies Type III hypersensitivity causing fibrinoid necrosis."},{"id":"sdl40_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the potential organ involvement, pathology, and possible lab findings in polyarteritis nodosa.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"One-third of patients with polyarteritis nodosa have an associated viral infection that contributes to pathogenesis through immune complex deposition in vessel walls. Which of the following correctly identifies this virus and the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Hepatitis C virus, which triggers cryoglobulin-mediated mixed cryoglobulinemic vasculitis through immune complex deposition in small vessels","B":"Hepatitis B virus, in which HBsAg-anti-HBsAg immune complexes deposit in affected vessel walls and trigger complement-mediated inflammation and fibrinoid necrosis","C":"HIV, which causes immune complex-mediated vasculitis via gp41 antigen-antibody complex deposition in medium-sized muscular arteries","D":"Cytomegalovirus, which activates endothelial TLR2 signaling and drives neutrophilic vessel wall destruction independent of immune complex deposition","E":"Epstein-Barr virus, which promotes molecular mimicry against medium vessel smooth muscle antigens through latent LMP1 protein expression"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Approximately one-third of PAN patients have chronic hepatitis B infection. HBsAg-anti-HBsAg immune complexes deposit in affected vessel walls, triggering complement activation and the inflammatory cascade that leads to necrotizing vasculitis. This is a classic example of Type III immune complex-mediated hypersensitivity. Hepatitis C is associated with cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, not classic PAN. The other viruses are not established causes of PAN.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HBsAg in ~33% of PAN patients; immune complex (Type III) mechanism; no ANCAs in PAN. COMLEX: Test for HBsAg in any PAN patient; antiviral therapy reduces disease burden in hepatitis B-associated PAN.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — HBsAg immune complex deposition in PAN vessel walls exemplifies Type III hypersensitivity causing fibrinoid necrosis."},{"id":"sdl40_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring in arteries affected by giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman presents with a 3-week history of severe right temporal headache and jaw pain. She has bilateral shoulder aching worse in the morning, a tender palpable right temporal artery, and an ESR of 98 mm/hr. Right temporal artery biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation centered on the internal elastic membrane with multinucleated giant cells and CD4+ T cells outnumbering CD8+ T cells. A left temporal artery biopsy performed 3 weeks earlier by another provider was read as normal. Which of the following best explains the discrepancy between the two biopsy results?","choices":{"A":"The left temporal artery biopsy was too superficial and sampled only adventitial fat without including the vessel wall itself","B":"Giant cell arteritis selectively spares the left temporal artery in all cases because inflammatory lesions are always right-sided and unilateral","C":"The lesions of GCA are notoriously patchy along the vessel, producing skip areas that result in false-negative biopsy results even from the same arterial territory","D":"The left biopsy represented healed end-stage GCA with complete fibrotic replacement, which lacks active granulomas and requires immunostaining to detect","E":"The CD4/CD8 ratio difference between the two sides confirms active disease on the right and a post-traumatic reaction masquerading as GCA on the left"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The pathologic changes of GCA are notoriously patchy along the length of any affected vessel. Even when disease is present, skip areas between inflamed segments can yield negative biopsy results. This explains why a biopsy longer than 1 cm is recommended and why a negative biopsy does not exclude the diagnosis. GCA is not exclusively unilateral, and the healed phase still shows residual elastic lamina changes — not a completely normal biopsy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patchy skip lesions explain false-negative temporal artery biopsies; negative biopsy does not rule out GCA. COMLEX: Clinical diagnosis still possible with classic features (jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, elevated ESR) even with negative biopsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl40_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring in arteries affected by giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with biopsy-confirmed giant cell arteritis develops sudden monocular vision loss in his right eye. Ophthalmologic examination reveals pallor and edema of the right optic disc consistent with anterior ischemic optic neuropathy. High-dose prednisone is started immediately. Which of the following correctly identifies the vessel directly responsible for his vision loss and the mechanism of injury?","choices":{"A":"The central retinal vein is occluded by the inflammatory process, causing venous stasis retinopathy with optic disc edema and flame-shaped hemorrhages","B":"The posterior communicating artery is compressed by a granulomatous mass, producing a third nerve palsy with ptosis and visual field loss","C":"The carotid artery is obstructed by intimal thickening that reduces global cerebral perfusion, secondarily impairing retinal blood flow","D":"The optic tract within the cranium is invaded by granulomatous inflammation extending from an adjacent inflamed vessel, causing a homonymous hemianopia","E":"The ophthalmic artery is involved by granulomatous vasculitis, reducing perfusion to the optic nerve head and causing anterior ischemic optic neuropathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When GCA is present in the temporal arteries, the ophthalmic and vertebral arteries are likely also involved. Granulomatous vasculitis of the ophthalmic artery reduces perfusion to the optic nerve head, causing anterior ischemic optic neuropathy — the most feared complication of GCA. This presents as sudden monocular vision loss with optic disc pallor and edema. Venous occlusion, carotid occlusion, and optic tract invasion are not the mechanism in GCA-related vision loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA vision loss = ophthalmic artery involvement causing anterior ischemic optic neuropathy; optic disc pallor and edema are hallmarks. COMLEX: Ocular symptoms in GCA are an emergency; steroid treatment must not be delayed for biopsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl40_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring in arteries affected by giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with biopsy-confirmed GCA has an ESR of 115 mm/hr and CRP of 42 mg/L. She asks whether her disease involves only the temporal arteries or whether other vessels are at risk. Which of the following most accurately describes the vascular distribution of GCA beyond the temporal arteries?","choices":{"A":"The vertebral and ophthalmic arteries are likely involved when temporal artery disease is present, and the aorta can also be affected in a variant called giant cell aortitis","B":"GCA is anatomically confined to the external carotid territory and does not involve the ophthalmic, vertebral, or internal carotid arteries","C":"GCA spreads in a predictable rostrocaudal pattern from the temporal artery to the subclavian artery and then the aorta over months to years","D":"Only vessels expressing a specific surface glycoprotein unique to scalp and facial arteries are susceptible to GCA, limiting spread beyond the head","E":"The internal carotid artery is always involved in GCA while the vertebral and ophthalmic arteries are characteristically protected from granulomatous inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"GCA is classified as a large vessel vasculitis and can involve vessels beyond the temporal artery. When GCA is present in the temporal arteries, the vertebral and ophthalmic arteries are likely also involved. The aorta can also be affected, a variant termed giant cell aortitis. Medium and even small vessels may be affected as well. The temporal arteries are biopsied because of accessibility, not because they are uniquely vulnerable. Spread does not follow a predictable anatomical sequence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA is not limited to temporal arteries; vertebral, ophthalmic, and aorta all at risk. Giant cell aortitis = aortic involvement by GCA. COMLEX: Screen for aortic aneurysm on long-term follow-up in GCA patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl40_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Define the patient population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman of Norwegian descent presents with 6 weeks of bilateral hip and shoulder girdle aching, over 1 hour of morning stiffness, scalp pain when brushing her hair, and new temporal headache. Her ESR is 88 mm/hr. High-dose prednisone produces dramatic symptom relief within 48 hours. An urgent ophthalmology consult is arranged before starting steroids. Which of the following best identifies the rationale for the urgent ophthalmic assessment?","choices":{"A":"Prednisone raises intraocular pressure and can precipitate acute angle-closure glaucoma, requiring prophylactic laser iridotomy before treatment","B":"The combination of elevated ESR and Scandinavian ancestry increases the likelihood of ANCA-positive microscopic polyangiitis with glomerulonephritis affecting the retinal vessels","C":"Corticosteroids are contraindicated in GCA and the consult is needed to explore alternative anti-cytokine biologic therapies before committing to treatment","D":"Fundoscopic examination is required to confirm the GCA diagnosis by identifying granulomatous inflammation in the retinal arterioles before steroid initiation","E":"Ophthalmic artery involvement in GCA can lead to sudden and permanent vision loss, and treatment with steroids must begin before this complication occurs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ophthalmic artery involvement in GCA can cause sudden and permanent blindness. This is the most feared complication of GCA, and affected persons must be diagnosed and treated promptly. Ophthalmology is consulted to assess for early ophthalmic involvement and to ensure prompt treatment is initiated. Steroids are not contraindicated in GCA; they are the treatment. Prednisone-induced glaucoma is a long-term concern, not an acute pre-treatment barrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA + ocular symptoms = ophthalmologic emergency; start high-dose steroids immediately, do not wait for biopsy. COMLEX: Giant cell arteritis is the most common cause of acute vision loss in patients over age 50 in the US.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Overlap of Polymyalgia Rheumatica with Giant Cell Arteritis"},{"id":"sdl40_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Define the patient population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old man presents with a 4-week history of headache and jaw pain when chewing. Physical examination reveals a tender, indurated left temporal artery and an ESR of 105 mm/hr. Temporal artery biopsy confirms GCA. His physician wants to counsel him about the incidence of this disease in different populations. Which population carries the highest background incidence of giant cell arteritis?","choices":{"A":"Young women of East Asian descent living in Japan, where Takayasu arteritis and GCA share an overlapping peak incidence in the fifth decade","B":"Elderly adults of northern European or Scandinavian ancestry, in whom GCA incidence is markedly higher than in other ethnic populations","C":"Middle-aged African American men with HLA-B27 positivity and a prior history of uveitis and inflammatory back pain","D":"Young adult men of any ancestry with HBsAg seropositivity and prior hepatitis B virus infection in childhood","E":"Postmenopausal women with HLA-DR4 positivity of any ethnic ancestry, where HLA allele alone predicts GCA incidence"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GCA shows a strong geographic and ethnic predilection for northern European and Scandinavian populations. Norway has among the highest reported incidences worldwide. Although HLA class II haplotypes are associated with GCA susceptibility, ethnicity remains the most clinically useful epidemiologic predictor. East Asian populations are more affected by Takayasu arteritis; African Americans have lower GCA incidence; HBsAg seropositivity is associated with PAN, not GCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA highest incidence in Scandinavian/northern European populations; Norway has highest documented rates. COMLEX: Ethnic predilection for GCA reflects both genetic and environmental factors; Olmsted County MN (Scandinavian descent) data support this.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Overlap of Polymyalgia Rheumatica with Giant Cell Arteritis"},{"id":"sdl40_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Define the patient population most vulnerable to giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with newly diagnosed GCA has bilateral shoulder girdle aching and jaw claudication. Her rheumatologist notes she likely has an overlap syndrome involving a second condition. Which of the following correctly describes the epidemiologic relationship between giant cell arteritis and polymyalgia rheumatica?","choices":{"A":"GCA and PMR are caused by identical mechanisms and represent the same disease at different clinical stages; all PMR eventually evolves into GCA if untreated","B":"PMR is found in fewer than 5% of all patients with GCA; the two conditions rarely coexist and should be evaluated as independent diagnoses","C":"PMR is present in a substantial subset of GCA patients, and a smaller but significant minority of PMR patients will develop clinically apparent GCA over time","D":"GCA evolves universally from PMR in all patients if PMR is left untreated with corticosteroids for more than 3 years from symptom onset","E":"GCA and PMR are causally unrelated diseases that coincidentally share HLA-DR4 as a risk allele, explaining their apparent but spurious co-occurrence"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PMR and GCA are closely linked. A substantial proportion of GCA patients have concurrent PMR symptoms, and a minority — estimated at 5 to 30% — of PMR patients will eventually develop clinically apparent GCA. They share HLA class II haplotypes, cytokine profiles, and a response to corticosteroids. However, not all PMR evolves into GCA, and they are considered distinct but overlapping conditions rather than the same disease at different stages.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PMR and GCA overlap: ~50% of GCA patients have PMR; 5-30% of PMR patients develop GCA. COMLEX: Treat all PMR with corticosteroids to prevent GCA; monitor for new headache, jaw claudication, or visual symptoms in PMR patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Overlap of Polymyalgia Rheumatica with Giant Cell Arteritis"},{"id":"sdl40_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the immune response felt to perpetuate the tissue damage seen in giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman with biopsy-confirmed GCA is started on prednisone. Her physician explains that the T-cell-mediated immune response in GCA involves two distinct CD4+ T cell cytokine pathways operating in parallel. Which of the following best describes both pathways and their effector mechanisms in GCA pathogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Th1 cells produce IL-4 to activate eosinophils in the adventitia, while Th2 cells produce IFN-gamma to stimulate endothelial proliferation in the intima","B":"Th2 cells produce IL-13 to drive adventitial fibrosis, while Th17 cells produce IL-22 to stimulate endothelial cell proliferation and intimal thickening","C":"Regulatory T cells produce TGF-beta to drive giant cell formation at the elastic lamina, while Th1 cells produce IL-10 to suppress macrophage activation","D":"Th1 cells produce IL-17 to recruit neutrophils to the elastic lamina, while CD8+ cytotoxic T cells produce IFN-gamma to activate macrophages in the media","E":"Th1 cells produce IFN-gamma to activate macrophages and drive multinucleated giant cell formation, while Th17 cells produce IL-17 to amplify local inflammation and progressive vessel wall injury"},"correct":"E","explanation":"GCA involves two parallel T helper cell pathways. Th1 cells produce IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages, drives granuloma formation, and leads to multinucleated giant cell formation and elastic lamina destruction. Th17 cells produce IL-17, which amplifies local vascular inflammation. Both pathways contribute to progressive vessel wall injury. IL-4 and IL-13 are Th2 cytokines not central to GCA; TGF-beta drives fibrosis rather than giant cell formation; IL-10 is anti-inflammatory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA Th1/Th17 dual pathway: IFN-gamma from Th1 drives macrophage activation; IL-17 from Th17 amplifies inflammation. COMLEX: Corticosteroids suppress both pathways; tocilizumab (anti-IL-6 receptor) is an approved steroid-sparing agent for GCA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — Immune Cells Producing Cytokines and Specific Cytokines Released"},{"id":"sdl40_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the immune response felt to perpetuate the tissue damage seen in giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher investigating GCA pathogenesis finds that the earliest histologic changes in affected temporal arteries are located in the adventitia, adjacent to the vasa vasorum, rather than in the intima or lumen. Which of the following most accurately explains why immune activation begins in this anatomic location?","choices":{"A":"Dendritic cells residing in the adventitia present vessel wall antigens to CD4+ T cells entering via the vasa vasorum, initiating the granulomatous inflammatory cascade from the outside of the vessel inward","B":"Luminal neutrophils first degrade the endothelial glycocalyx, allowing CD4+ T cells to enter the media before migrating outward to the adventitia over days","C":"The internal elastic lamina acts as a physical barrier preventing T cells from entering the adventitia, so all immune activation must begin in the intima","D":"Plasma cells in the adventitia produce anti-endothelial antibodies that activate complement, triggering secondary CD4+ T cell recruitment to the adventitia","E":"Mast cells in the adventitia release CXCL5, which recruits neutrophils into the vasa vasorum; these neutrophils then activate CD4+ T cells through antigen presentation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In GCA, immune activation begins in the adventitia. Dendritic cells residing in the adventitia process and present vessel wall antigens to CD4+ T cells that enter via the vasa vasorum. Activated CD4+ T cells then differentiate into Th1 and Th17 effectors and recruit macrophages, initiating granulomatous inflammation that spreads inward toward the media and internal elastic lamina. This outside-in mechanism explains why the earliest inflammatory changes are adventitial.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GCA immune activation is outside-in: adventitial dendritic cells activate CD4+ T cells entering via vasa vasorum. COMLEX: The adventitial initiation site explains why vasa vasorum are involved early and why the elastic lamina is a late target.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — Immune Cells Producing Cytokines and Specific Cytokines Released"},{"id":"sdl40_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the immune response felt to perpetuate the tissue damage seen in giant cell arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with GCA is being treated with high-dose prednisone. Her rheumatologist considers adding an IL-6 receptor antagonist (tocilizumab) to enable steroid dose reduction. Which of the following best explains the rationale for targeting the IL-6 pathway in the immunopathogenesis of GCA?","choices":{"A":"IL-6 produced exclusively by CD8+ cytotoxic T cells is the primary cytokine responsible for elastic lamina fragmentation in active GCA","B":"IL-6 directly activates mast cells in the adventitia to release histamine, causing vasodilation that promotes macrophage and T cell entry into vessel walls","C":"IL-6 is uniquely produced by multinucleated giant cells themselves and is solely responsible for the granulomatous morphology seen in GCA lesions","D":"IL-6 is an important proinflammatory cytokine in GCA that drives the systemic acute phase response, contributes to Th17 cell differentiation, and promotes ongoing vascular inflammation","E":"IL-6 acts as the sole activation signal for all macrophages in GCA; blocking it completely abrogates the granulomatous response in all patients"},"correct":"D","explanation":"IL-6 is a pleiotropic cytokine that plays an important role in GCA. It drives the systemic acute phase response, which explains the markedly elevated ESR and CRP seen in active GCA. IL-6 also promotes Th17 cell differentiation, linking it to the IL-17-mediated pathway of vascular inflammation. Tocilizumab (anti-IL-6 receptor) is approved for GCA as a steroid-sparing agent. IL-6 is not produced exclusively by giant cells or CD8 T cells, and it does not act as the sole macrophage activator.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-6 in GCA drives ESR/CRP elevation and Th17 differentiation; tocilizumab is FDA-approved as steroid-sparing in GCA. COMLEX: Markedly elevated ESR in GCA reflects IL-6-driven acute phase response; ESR can be used to monitor disease activity and treatment response.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — Immune Cells Producing Cytokines and Specific Cytokines Released"},{"id":"sdl40_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the demographics and pathology of Takayasu arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman from South Korea presents with a 3-month history of fatigue, fever, and reduced blood pressure in both arms. Angiography reveals severe narrowing of the brachiocephalic, carotid, and subclavian arteries arising from the aortic arch. Biopsy of the carotid artery shows granulomatous inflammation with giant cells histologically indistinguishable from giant cell arteritis. Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Giant cell arteritis presenting atypically in a young Asian woman due to rare early-onset disease associated with HLA-DR4 susceptibility alleles","B":"Takayasu arteritis, a granulomatous large vessel vasculitis occurring in patients under age 50 that preferentially involves the aortic arch and great vessels and causes diminished upper extremity pulses","C":"Polyarteritis nodosa causing necrotizing vasculitis of medium-sized arteries including the carotid and subclavian vessels with fibrinoid necrosis","D":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis causing upper respiratory granulomas and medium vessel vasculitis with ANCA positivity and glomerulonephritis","E":"Kawasaki disease presenting with coronary artery aneurysms and great vessel involvement in a young adult of East Asian descent with fever"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This presentation is classic for Takayasu arteritis: a young woman of Asian descent with reduced upper extremity blood pressure and pulses, aortic arch and great vessel involvement on angiography, and granulomatous histology. Age under 50 with aortic arch involvement distinguishes Takayasu from GCA. PAN causes segmental fibrinoid necrosis without granulomas, is ANCA-negative, and does not involve large vessels. GPA causes upper respiratory granulomas and is ANCA-positive. Kawasaki typically affects children under age 5.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Takayasu = young woman + pulseless + aortic arch stenosis + granulomatous histology identical to GCA. COMLEX: Pulseless disease; absent radial pulse in a woman under 40 is the classic board scenario for Takayasu.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl40_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the demographics and pathology of Takayasu arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with known Takayasu arteritis presents for routine follow-up. Her blood pressure is 165/98 mmHg in the right arm; no pulse is detectable in the left arm. Imaging confirms bilateral renal artery stenosis as well as left subclavian occlusion. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiologic mechanism responsible for her hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Granulomatous inflammation and intimal hyperplasia narrow the renal artery lumen, reducing renal perfusion pressure and activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to produce systemic hypertension","B":"Immune complex deposition in the glomerular mesangium activates complement and causes membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis with secondary volume-dependent hypertension","C":"ANCA-mediated pauci-immune necrotizing glomerulonephritis destroys renal parenchyma, reducing GFR and causing hypertension through sodium and water retention","D":"Fibrinoid necrosis of the afferent arterioles resembling PAN causes microinfarcts that reduce functional renal mass and increase renin secretion nonspecifically","E":"Aortic root dilation from Takayasu causes aortic valve regurgitation, producing high-output volume overload hypertension with a wide pulse pressure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Renal artery involvement occurs in roughly half of Takayasu arteritis patients. The granulomatous inflammation and intimal hyperplasia that characterize Takayasu narrow the renal artery lumen. This reduces renal perfusion pressure, activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) and producing renovascular hypertension. This is a major cause of morbidity in Takayasu. Immune complex nephritis, ANCA-associated GN, and aortic regurgitation are not the mechanism in Takayasu-related hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renovascular hypertension in Takayasu = renal artery stenosis activating RAAS; occurs in ~50% of patients. COMLEX: Measure blood pressure in both arms in suspected Takayasu; unequal readings indicate subclavian or aortic arch asymmetric involvement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl40_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the demographics and pathology of Takayasu arteritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pathologist describes the histologic progression of Takayasu arteritis from earliest to most advanced in the same vessel wall over time. Which of the following correctly sequences the histologic progression of Takayasu arteritis?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrosis of the media → intimal hyperplasia → adventitial mononuclear infiltration → granuloma formation at the elastic lamina","B":"Intimal hyperplasia → medial granuloma formation → adventitial fibrosis → complete luminal obliteration without prior mononuclear infiltration","C":"Giant cell formation at the intima-media junction → medial necrosis → spread to adventitial mononuclear infiltrates → collagen deposition","D":"Luminal thrombosis → subendothelial fibrin deposition → medial inflammation → periadventitial granulomas as the final stage","E":"Adventitial mononuclear infiltrates with vasa vasorum cuffing → intense mononuclear inflammation in the media → granulomatous inflammation with giant cells and medial necrosis → collagenous scarring of all three vessel wall layers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Takayasu arteritis progresses histologically in a characteristic outside-in pattern. The earliest changes are adventitial mononuclear infiltrates with perivascular cuffing of the vasa vasorum. This progresses to intense mononuclear inflammation in the media, followed by granulomatous inflammation with giant cells and patchy medial necrosis. The end-stage is collagenous scarring of all three vessel layers. This outside-in progression parallels the pathogenesis of GCA, where the adventitia is also the initiating site.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Takayasu histology progresses from adventitial infiltrates to medial granulomas to collagenous scarring; histologically identical to GCA at any stage. COMLEX: End-stage fibrotic scarring of all three layers causes the rigid, narrowed vessel responsible for pulseless disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl40_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the potential organ involvement, pathology, and possible lab findings in polyarteritis nodosa.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old man presents with a 2-month history of new-onset hypertension, diffuse abdominal pain with bloody stools, diffuse myalgias, and foot drop. Laboratory studies show neutrophilia, elevated ESR, and positive HBsAg serology. ANCA testing is negative. Mesenteric angiography demonstrates multiple microaneurysms. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis presenting with systemic vasculitis, GI involvement, and ANCA-positive glomerulonephritis affecting small and medium vessels","B":"Polyarteritis nodosa associated with hepatitis B infection, in which HBsAg immune complexes deposit in small to medium muscular arteries causing necrotizing vasculitis with characteristic microaneurysms","C":"Microscopic polyangiitis with pANCA positivity causing small vessel necrotizing glomerulonephritis and pulmonary capillaritis with GI involvement","D":"Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis presenting with GI vasculitis, peripheral eosinophilia, and asthma as the primary triad of findings","E":"Takayasu arteritis involving the mesenteric and renal arteries in a young adult with co-existent hepatitis B-associated aortitis and peripheral neuropathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This presentation is classic for PAN: a young adult man with hypertension, GI ischemia, myalgias, mononeuritis multiplex, positive HBsAg, ANCA negativity, and mesenteric microaneurysms on angiography. One-third of PAN patients have chronic hepatitis B, with HBsAg immune complexes driving the vasculitis. Microaneurysms on angiography are a classic diagnostic finding. GPA and MPA are ANCA-positive. Eosinophilic GPA requires asthma and eosinophilia. Takayasu affects large vessels and does not cause microaneurysms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAN = HBsAg in 33% + ANCA negative + microaneurysms on angiography + spares lungs + renal/GI/peripheral nerve involvement. COMLEX: Mononeuritis multiplex in PAN reflects ischemic injury to peripheral motor nerves from necrotizing vasculitis of vasa nervorum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl40_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the potential organ involvement, pathology, and possible lab findings in polyarteritis nodosa.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pathologist reviews a renal biopsy from a patient with suspected PAN. Multiple sections reveal vessels at different stages of activity simultaneously: some arteries show active fibrinoid necrosis with neutrophilic infiltrate, others show only fibrotic wall thickening with chronic mononuclear cells, and still others appear unaffected. Which of the following correctly interprets this mixed-stage histologic pattern?","choices":{"A":"The coexistence of active and healed lesions indicates that the patient has two simultaneous vascular diseases operating at different rates in the same kidney","B":"The uninvolved vessels confirm that only the main renal artery trunk is affected in PAN; the interlobular arteries are always spared in this disease","C":"The active fibrinoid necrosis in some vessels and fibrosis in others represents alternating cycles of bacterial infection and host-mediated healing responses","D":"PAN characteristically shows all stages of activity simultaneously in the same tissue, reflecting continuous and recurrent vascular injury rather than a single synchronous acute episode","E":"The mixed-stage pattern is found exclusively in hepatitis B-associated PAN and does not occur in ANCA-negative idiopathic forms of polyarteritis nodosa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A hallmark of PAN is that all stages of vascular lesion activity — from active fibrinoid necrosis and neutrophilic infiltration to healed fibrous thickening — coexist simultaneously in different vessels or even within the same vessel. This mixed-stage pattern reflects ongoing and recurrent injury rather than a single synchronized acute event. It is characteristic of PAN in both HBsAg-associated and idiopathic forms, and it helps distinguish PAN from conditions that cause uniform acute injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAN hallmark = simultaneous coexistence of active, healing, and healed lesions in the same tissue; indicates ongoing injury. COMLEX: Mixed-stage PAN lesions are best identified on renal or nerve biopsy; mesenteric angiography showing microaneurysms is an alternative diagnostic approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl40_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the potential organ involvement, pathology, and possible lab findings in polyarteritis nodosa.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with polyarteritis nodosa. Her physician discusses prognosis and initiates immunosuppressive therapy. Which of the following correctly identifies the primary organ system responsible for mortality in PAN and the approximate 5-year survival achieved with immunosuppression?","choices":{"A":"Renal artery involvement leading to rapidly progressive hypertension and renal failure is the most common cause of mortality; immunosuppression provides up to 80% 5-year survival","B":"Pulmonary artery involvement causing pulmonary hemorrhage is the leading cause of death in PAN; oxygen therapy and anticoagulation provide approximately 60% 5-year survival","C":"Coronary artery involvement alone is responsible for all PAN-related mortality; stenting of affected coronary segments achieves 95% 5-year survival in treated patients","D":"ANCA-mediated crescentic glomerulonephritis is the dominant cause of renal death in PAN; plasma exchange combined with steroids provides 90% 5-year survival","E":"Mesenteric artery involvement causing bowel infarction accounts for all PAN mortality; surgical resection of affected bowel segments achieves 70% 5-year survival"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Renal involvement is a major cause of mortality in PAN, with rapidly accelerating hypertension from renal artery disease being a defining clinical feature. The GI, peripheral nervous system, and coronary vasculature are also affected but renal disease drives most mortality. Untreated, PAN is typically fatal. With immunosuppression, 5-year survival reaches approximately 80%. PAN characteristically spares the pulmonary circulation, making pulmonary hemorrhage an unlikely direct cause of death. ANCA is not associated with PAN.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAN prognosis: untreated = typically fatal; immunosuppression achieves ~80% 5-year survival; renal involvement is major mortality driver. COMLEX: PAN requires both steroids and cyclophosphamide in severe cases; screen and treat underlying hepatitis B to reduce disease burden.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"}]},{"sdlNumber":41,"title":"Systemic Sclerosis & Sjögren's Syndrome","questions":[{"id":"sdl41_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies the three interrelated pathogenic processes that together produce the characteristic features of systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial infection, neutrophilic abscess formation in the dermis, and Type I collagen degradation by matrix metalloproteinases","B":"Type II complement-mediated hypersensitivity, endothelial lysis, and granulomatous replacement of dermal collagen by macrophages","C":"B-cell immune complex deposition in vessel walls, fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles, and consequent fibroblast inhibition","D":"Mast cell degranulation with eosinophil recruitment, dermis destruction by major basic protein, and TGF-alpha-driven re-epithelialization","E":"Autoimmune inflammation, widespread microvascular damage, and progressive interstitial and perivascular fibrosis in the skin and multiple organs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Systemic sclerosis results from three interrelated processes: (1) autoimmune inflammation driven by CD4+ T cells and humoral immunity, (2) widespread microvascular damage affecting small blood vessels, and (3) progressive fibrosis of the skin and viscera. These three processes are interdependent — immune activation drives vascular injury, and both together trigger the fibrogenic response. The other answer choices describe mechanisms of bacterial infection, hypersensitivity vasculitis, immune complex disease, and eosinophilic syndromes, none of which are the primary pathogenic triad in systemic sclerosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc pathogenic triad = autoimmunity + microvascular damage + fibrosis; all three operate simultaneously. COMLEX: Systemic sclerosis is a systemic disease — skin is most visible but GI, lung, kidney, and heart are frequently involved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Systemic sclerosis autoimmunity involves CD4+ Th2 cells releasing TGF-beta and IL-13 that drive fibroblast collagen overproduction in a Type IV hypersensitivity pattern."},{"id":"sdl41_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which CD4+ T helper cell subtype has been isolated from the skin of patients with systemic sclerosis and is considered responsible for driving the fibrogenic cytokine response?","choices":{"A":"Th2 cells, which produce TGF-beta and IL-13 that stimulate fibroblasts to transcribe collagen and fibronectin genes throughout the dermis","B":"Th1 cells, which produce IFN-gamma that activates macrophages to deposit compact collagen bundles throughout the dermis","C":"Th17 cells, which produce IL-17 that triggers fibroblast hyperproliferation and elastin degradation in the deep reticular dermis","D":"Th22 cells, which produce IL-22 that directly activates dermal fibroblasts to deposit Type IV collagen in the basement membrane zone","E":"Regulatory T cells, which produce TGF-beta to suppress local inflammation while paradoxically driving collagen overproduction in fibroblasts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Th2 cells have been isolated from the skin of systemic sclerosis patients. These cells produce TGF-beta and IL-13, both of which stimulate fibroblasts to transcribe genes encoding collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins such as fibronectin. Th1-derived IFN-gamma would typically suppress fibrosis rather than promote it. Th17 and Th22 cells play primary roles in other autoimmune conditions. Regulatory T cells produce TGF-beta in an anti-inflammatory context but are not the dominant effector cells accumulating in SSc skin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc fibrosis driven by Th2 cytokines TGF-beta and IL-13 stimulating fibroblast collagen gene transcription. COMLEX: TGF-beta is the master fibrogenic cytokine; it also drives perivascular fibrosis from platelet release during endothelial injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Systemic sclerosis autoimmunity involves CD4+ Th2 cells releasing TGF-beta and IL-13 that drive fibroblast collagen overproduction in a Type IV hypersensitivity pattern."},{"id":"sdl41_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following autoantibodies is highly specific for systemic sclerosis and is associated with diffuse disease, pulmonary fibrosis, and peripheral vascular disease?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SSA (Ro), which is the most specific serologic marker for diffuse systemic sclerosis with aggressive visceral involvement","B":"Anti-double-stranded DNA antibody, which is elevated in systemic sclerosis during flares of scleroderma renal crisis","C":"Anti-centromere antibody, which is specifically associated with diffuse systemic sclerosis with early and rapid visceral involvement","D":"Anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl 70), detected in 10-20% of patients with diffuse systemic sclerosis, and associated with pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral vascular disease","E":"Anti-U1-RNP antibody, which is highly specific for systemic sclerosis when found in isolation without features of mixed connective tissue disease"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl 70) is highly specific for systemic sclerosis. It is detected in 10-20% of patients with diffuse SSc and correlates with pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral vascular disease. Anti-centromere antibody, not anti-Scl 70, is associated with limited disease and the CREST syndrome. Anti-SSA/Ro is the serologic marker for Sjogren syndrome. Anti-dsDNA is specific for SLE. Anti-U1-RNP characterizes mixed connective tissue disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-Scl 70 = diffuse SSc + pulmonary fibrosis; anti-centromere = limited SSc + CREST; both are highly specific for SSc. COMLEX: The presence of anti-Scl 70 indicates higher risk for pulmonary and vascular complications requiring closer monitoring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Systemic sclerosis autoimmunity involves CD4+ Th2 cells releasing TGF-beta and IL-13 that drive fibroblast collagen overproduction in a Type IV hypersensitivity pattern."},{"id":"sdl41_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes which cause these changes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the typical distribution and progression of skin involvement in diffuse systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse sclerotic atrophy beginning in the fingers and distal upper extremities, then extending proximally to involve the upper arms, shoulders, neck, and face","B":"Diffuse inflammatory plaques beginning on the trunk and proximal extremities, then progressing distally to the fingertips over months","C":"Patchy erythematous rash following a dermatomal distribution on the face and scalp, with sparing of the fingers and distal extremities","D":"Symmetric scaling plaques concentrated on extensor surfaces of the elbows and knees, sparing the face and distal fingers entirely","E":"Violaceous papules at the knuckles and proximal nail folds progressing to scalp alopecia and periorbital edema over time"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In diffuse systemic sclerosis, sclerotic atrophy begins distally in the fingers and moves proximally up the upper extremities to the upper arms, shoulders, neck, and face. This distal-to-proximal pattern is characteristic of SSc and helps distinguish it from other fibrosing conditions. The description of inflammatory plaques on the trunk is not typical of SSc. Scaling extensor plaques suggest psoriasis. Violaceous knuckle papules with periorbital edema suggest dermatomyositis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc skin distribution = distal to proximal; starts in fingers, extends toward trunk. COMLEX: Sclerodactyly (skin tightening over fingers) + Raynaud in any patient warrants SSc workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TGF-beta and IL-13 produced in systemic sclerosis skin directly stimulate fibroblast collagen gene transcription, representing the effector cytokine function that drives dermal fibrosis."},{"id":"sdl41_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes which cause these changes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A skin biopsy from a patient with advanced systemic sclerosis would be expected to show which of the following histologic findings?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophilic infiltration of the dermis with microabscess formation and enzymatic destruction of dermal collagen bundles","B":"Eosinophilic infiltration of the deep dermis with sclerosing lipogranulomas and hypertrophic collagen bundles in a fasciitis pattern","C":"Intense perivascular lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate with flame figures and focal necrosis of dermal appendages in a rosette pattern","D":"Lichenoid lymphocytic infiltration at the dermal-epidermal junction with basal cell destruction and colloid body formation","E":"Marked increase of compact collagen in the dermis, thinning of the epidermis, loss of rete pegs, atrophy of dermal appendages, and hyaline thickening of arterioles and capillary walls"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Advanced SSc skin biopsy shows extensive deposition of compact collagen in the dermis with thinning of the epidermis, loss of rete pegs, atrophy of dermal appendages including hair follicles and sebaceous glands, and hyaline thickening of arteriole and capillary walls. The epidermis is thinned, not thickened. Neutrophilic microabscesses suggest acute infection or Sweet syndrome. Eosinophilic fasciitis is a separate entity. Lichenoid infiltrate with basal destruction suggests lichen planus or lupus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc biopsy = compact collagen deposits + thinned epidermis + lost rete pegs + atrophied appendages + thickened vessel walls. COMLEX: Loss of dermal appendages on biopsy means permanent loss of sweat glands and hair follicles in affected skin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TGF-beta and IL-13 produced in systemic sclerosis skin directly stimulate fibroblast collagen gene transcription, representing the effector cytokine function that drives dermal fibrosis."},{"id":"sdl41_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes which cause these changes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In patients with the CREST variant of systemic sclerosis, what does the C in the CREST acronym represent, and where do these lesions most commonly occur?","choices":{"A":"Calreticulin deposits within the nucleus of epidermal keratinocytes that cause rete peg loss and thinning of the epidermis","B":"Focal and diffuse subcutaneous calcifications that develop particularly in CREST syndrome patients, visible as firm nodules beneath the skin at pressure points","C":"Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition in the synovium of interphalangeal joints causing chondrocalcinosis and an inflammatory arthropathy","D":"Calcification of the internal elastic lamina of digital arteries causing critical limb ischemia and digital gangrene","E":"Hydroxyapatite crystal accumulation within the tendon sheaths of the flexor tendons of the hand causing tenosynovitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the CREST acronym, C stands for calcinosis — focal and diffuse subcutaneous calcifications that develop particularly in patients with limited systemic sclerosis and the CREST syndrome. These calcifications are composed of calcium hydroxyapatite and occur in soft tissues, especially at pressure points. They are visible on imaging and can be palpated as firm nodules. The other options describe calreticulin deposits, CPPD disease, vessel wall calcification, and tendon sheath disease, none of which represent the C in CREST.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CREST = Calcinosis, Raynaud, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, Telangiectasia; associated with anti-centromere antibody and limited SSc. COMLEX: CREST patients have a more benign course than diffuse SSc; visceral involvement occurs late if at all.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TGF-beta and IL-13 produced in systemic sclerosis skin directly stimulate fibroblast collagen gene transcription, representing the effector cytokine function that drives dermal fibrosis."},{"id":"sdl41_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathology of vasculopathy and the mechanism of organ damage occurring in Raynaud's phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Raynaud phenomenon is present in virtually all patients with systemic sclerosis and may precede other symptoms. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiologic mechanism of Raynaud phenomenon in SSc?","choices":{"A":"IgG immune complex deposition in digital vessel walls activates complement and triggers neutrophilic vasculitis that causes episodic luminal occlusion","B":"Anti-endothelial cell antibodies directly lyse endothelial cells, causing structural vessel collapse and obliterative endarteritis of digital arteries","C":"Mast cell degranulation in the adventitia of digital arteries releases histamine, causing episodic vasodilation alternating with reactive vasoconstriction","D":"Repeated endothelial injury triggers platelet aggregation and release of PDGF and TGF-beta, leading to perivascular fibrosis and adrenergic receptor upregulation on vascular smooth muscle, causing episodic vasoconstriction","E":"Th1-mediated macrophage activation releases reactive oxygen species that oxidize nitric oxide in digital arteries, causing irreversible capillary bed obliteration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In SSc, repeated cycles of endothelial injury are followed by platelet aggregation. Activated platelets release PDGF and TGF-beta, which trigger perivascular fibrosis. Vascular smooth muscle cells also show increased expression of adrenergic receptors, making them hyperresponsive to vasoconstrictive stimuli. This combination of structural narrowing and functional hyperreactivity produces the episodic vasoconstriction of Raynaud phenomenon. Immune complex vasculitis and macrophage-mediated destruction are not the primary mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Raynaud in SSc = endothelial injury cycle → platelet PDGF/TGF-beta release → perivascular fibrosis + adrenergic receptor upregulation → vasoconstriction. COMLEX: Raynaud is often the first symptom of SSc; present in virtually all patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 53 → Obj 1 — Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis arises from microvascular endothelial injury and adrenergic receptor upregulation in smooth muscle, the same vasospastic mechanism studied in isolated Raynaud."},{"id":"sdl41_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathology of vasculopathy and the mechanism of organ damage occurring in Raynaud's phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Scleroderma renal crisis involves a characteristic pattern of vascular injury restricted to a specific caliber of renal vessel. Which of the following correctly identifies the vessel size affected and the histologic change in scleroderma renal disease?","choices":{"A":"Interlobular arteries of 150 to 500 micrometers diameter show intimal thickening from deposition of mucinous or finely collagenous material with concentric intimal cell proliferation","B":"Glomerular capillaries show subendothelial immune complex deposits with complement activation causing membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis with red cell casts","C":"Large renal arteries show granulomatous inflammation with giant cells and intimal hyperplasia similar to the renovascular lesion of Takayasu arteritis","D":"Small renal veins develop fibrin thrombi and venous congestion causing cortical necrosis from venous outflow obstruction in the medullary rays","E":"Arterioles throughout the cortex show eosinophilic infiltration with fibrinoid necrosis identical in distribution to ANCA-associated pauci-immune vasculitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In scleroderma renal disease, the characteristic vascular changes are found in interlobular arteries measuring 150-500 micrometers in diameter. These vessels show intimal thickening from deposition of mucinous or finely collagenous material (histochemically positive for glycoprotein and acid mucopolysaccharides) with concentric intimal cell proliferation. There are no specific glomerular changes. In malignant hypertension from scleroderma renal crisis, fibrinoid necrosis, thrombosis, and infarction are superimposed on these pre-existing vascular changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Scleroderma renal crisis = interlobular arteries 150-500 micrometers with mucinous intimal thickening; malignant hypertension adds fibrinoid necrosis; accounts for ~50% of SSc deaths historically. COMLEX: Treat scleroderma renal crisis with ACE inhibitors; they are the only therapy shown to improve outcomes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 53 → Obj 1 — Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis arises from microvascular endothelial injury and adrenergic receptor upregulation in smooth muscle, the same vasospastic mechanism studied in isolated Raynaud."},{"id":"sdl41_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathology of vasculopathy and the mechanism of organ damage occurring in Raynaud's phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Pulmonary arterial hypertension is a major complication of systemic sclerosis. Which of the following best describes the initiating mechanism of pulmonary hypertension in this disease?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SSA antibodies deposit in pulmonary vessel walls, directly activating complement and causing fibrinoid necrosis of pulmonary arterioles","B":"Granulomatous inflammation centered on the elastic lamina of pulmonary arteries mirrors the GCA mechanism and narrows the pulmonary arterial lumen","C":"Pulmonary vascular endothelial dysfunction causes vasospasm of pulmonary vessels, representing a Raynaud-like phenomenon in the pulmonary circulation that drives elevated pulmonary artery pressures","D":"Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage from leukocytoclastic vasculitis of pulmonary capillaries causes reactive pulmonary arterial muscularization","E":"Immune complex deposition in pulmonary arterioles triggers Type III hypersensitivity fibrinoid necrosis of the entire pulmonary vascular bed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pulmonary vascular endothelial dysfunction in SSc causes vasospasm of pulmonary vessels, which is considered the initiating pathogenic event in pulmonary arterial hypertension in this disease. This is conceptually analogous to Raynaud phenomenon, where endothelial injury and dysfunction of the systemic microvasculature causes episodic vasospasm. Over time, structural remodeling adds to the functional vasospasm, producing sustained pulmonary hypertension. SSc is not an ANCA-associated or immune complex disease in the pulmonary circulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc pulmonary HTN = endothelial dysfunction causing vasospasm in the pulmonary circulation; pulmonary involvement now exceeds renal disease as the leading cause of SSc death. COMLEX: Screen all SSc patients annually for pulmonary hypertension with echocardiography and PFTs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 53 → Obj 1 — Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis arises from microvascular endothelial injury and adrenergic receptor upregulation in smooth muscle, the same vasospastic mechanism studied in isolated Raynaud."},{"id":"sdl41_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the underlying pathologic cause of Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the pathologic mechanism by which Sjögren syndrome produces keratoconjunctivitis sicca and xerostomia?","choices":{"A":"Immune complex deposition in the ductal basement membrane of lacrimal and salivary glands triggers complement-mediated acinar cell necrosis and ductal obliteration","B":"CD4+ helper T cells and B cells including plasma cells infiltrate the lacrimal and salivary glands, causing immunologically mediated destruction of these exocrine glands and reducing secretion","C":"Autoantibodies against the M3 muscarinic receptor block acetylcholine-mediated secretion by acinar cells, reducing tear and saliva production without causing gland destruction","D":"Granulomatous inflammation centered on the salivary duct epithelium causes ductal obstruction and secondary acinar atrophy through backpressure injury","E":"NK cell-mediated cytotoxicity against ductal epithelial cells expressing the SS-A antigen destroys the duct lining and prevents secretion of tears and saliva"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sjögren syndrome results from lymphocytic infiltration of the lacrimal and salivary glands. The infiltrate contains predominantly activated CD4+ helper T cells and B cells including plasma cells. This immunologically mediated destruction progressively impairs secretory function, causing dry eyes and dry mouth. Immune complex deposition is not the primary mechanism. Anti-M3 muscarinic receptor antibodies may contribute in some patients but are not the primary lesion. Granulomatous and NK cell mechanisms are not characteristic of Sjögren syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren = CD4+ T cell and B cell infiltration of lacrimal and salivary glands causing exocrine destruction; dry eyes and dry mouth are the hallmark symptoms. COMLEX: Sjögren is the most common autoimmune exocrinopathy; female-to-male ratio is markedly skewed toward women.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Sjögren syndrome exemplifies autoimmune exocrine gland destruction driven by self-antigen-specific CD4+ T cells that escaped central tolerance."},{"id":"sdl41_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the underlying pathologic cause of Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the earliest histologic finding in the salivary glands of a patient with Sjögren syndrome, before disease has reached an advanced stage?","choices":{"A":"Dense germinal center formation throughout the gland parenchyma with obliteration of acinar structures and fibrotic replacement of secretory tissue","B":"Acinar atrophy with fat replacement and scattered residual lymphocytes without active inflammatory infiltrate indicating late-stage burnout","C":"Periductal and perivascular lymphocytic infiltration as the initial histologic lesion, before progression to extensive glandular inflammation and germinal center formation","D":"Granulomatous inflammation with multinucleated giant cells surrounding the ductal epithelium in a noncaseating sarcoid-like pattern","E":"Diffuse neutrophilic infiltration of the acini with microabscess formation and suppurative ductal destruction as the acute-phase reaction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The earliest histologic finding in Sjögren syndrome-affected salivary glands is periductal and perivascular lymphocytic infiltration. This progresses over time to extensive lymphocytic infiltration with lymphoid follicle and germinal center formation, ductal epithelial hyperplasia, acinar atrophy, fibrosis, and eventual fat replacement. Dense germinal centers and acinar atrophy with fat replacement represent advanced stages, not early disease. Granulomatous and neutrophilic patterns do not characterize Sjögren syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren earliest histology = periductal/perivascular lymphocytic infiltration; advanced = germinal centers, acinar atrophy, fibrosis, fat replacement. COMLEX: Minor salivary gland biopsy (lower lip) is the standard diagnostic procedure; requires focus score of at least 1 lymphocyte aggregate per 4 mm squared.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Sjögren syndrome exemplifies autoimmune exocrine gland destruction driven by self-antigen-specific CD4+ T cells that escaped central tolerance."},{"id":"sdl41_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the underlying pathologic cause of Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with Sjögren syndrome develops a painless enlarging parotid gland mass over several months. Biopsy reveals a monoclonal B-cell population with marginal zone morphology. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which Sjögren syndrome predisposes to this malignant complication?","choices":{"A":"Chronic CD8+ T cell cytotoxicity against ductal epithelium releases neoantigens that drive sustained NK cell activation toward lymphomagenesis in the gland","B":"Anti-SSB antibodies bind to a B-cell surface receptor and constitutively activate the BCR signaling pathway, bypassing normal proliferation checkpoints","C":"Viral integration into the salivary gland genome by HTLV or HIV triggers direct oncogenic transformation of ductal epithelial cells into B-cell lymphoma","D":"Ongoing polyclonal B-cell stimulation in the chronically inflamed gland allows individual clones to acquire somatic mutations and gain a proliferative advantage, producing a marginal zone lymphoma","E":"Hypergammaglobulinemia from sustained polyclonal B-cell activation overwhelms somatic hypermutation proofreading, activating BCL-2 and c-Myc"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 5% of Sjögren syndrome patients develop B-cell lymphoma, an incidence 40-fold higher than the general population. The mechanism is prolonged polyclonal B-cell stimulation within the chronically inflamed gland. Over time, individual B-cell clones acquire somatic mutations that confer a proliferative advantage, eventually producing a monoclonal marginal zone lymphoma — the same mechanism that drives lymphoma in other chronic B-cell stimulation states such as Hashimoto thyroiditis. HTLV, HIV, and hepatitis C are associated with Sjögren-like disease but are not established causes of the lymphoma complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren syndrome is associated with a 40-fold increased risk of marginal zone B-cell lymphoma arising from chronic polyclonal B-cell stimulation. COMLEX: Parotid mass in a Sjögren patient warrants biopsy to exclude lymphoma; emergence of a dominant clone signals malignant transformation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Sjögren syndrome exemplifies autoimmune exocrine gland destruction driven by self-antigen-specific CD4+ T cells that escaped central tolerance."},{"id":"sdl41_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the typical antibody profile of patients with Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following autoantibodies are considered the most important serologic markers of Sjögren syndrome and can be detected in up to 90% of patients using sensitive assay techniques?","choices":{"A":"Anti-double-stranded DNA and anti-Smith antibodies, which are highly specific for SLE and elevated in secondary Sjögren syndrome","B":"Anti-centromere and anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl 70), which together are specific for Sjögren syndrome complicated by concurrent scleroderma","C":"Anti-SS-A (Ro) and anti-SS-B (La), two ribonucleoprotein autoantibodies that are considered the diagnostic serologic markers of Sjögren syndrome","D":"Anti-Jo-1 and anti-Mi-2, which are the primary serologic markers of Sjögren syndrome when complicated by inflammatory myopathy","E":"ANCA in a perinuclear pattern, which is elevated in Sjögren syndrome when it is associated with glomerulonephritis and systemic vasculitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anti-SS-A (Ro) and anti-SS-B (La) are ribonucleoprotein autoantibodies that serve as the major serologic markers of Sjögren syndrome. They are detectable in up to 90% of patients using sensitive techniques. Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith are markers for SLE. Anti-centromere and anti-Scl 70 are markers for systemic sclerosis. Anti-Jo-1 and anti-Mi-2 are markers for inflammatory myopathies. ANCA is associated with vasculitides, not Sjögren syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren serologic markers = anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La); detectable in up to 90% by sensitive techniques. COMLEX: Anti-SSA/SSB can cross the placenta and cause neonatal lupus and congenital heart block; test pregnant women with these antibodies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are diagnostic serologic markers for Sjögren syndrome within the spectrum of autoantibodies used in rheumatic disease evaluation."},{"id":"sdl41_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the typical antibody profile of patients with Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Approximately what percentage of patients with Sjögren syndrome are rheumatoid factor positive, even in the absence of concurrent rheumatoid arthritis, and what is the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Fewer than 10%, because RF is produced predominantly by synovial plasma cells in RA and does not arise from exocrine gland inflammation","B":"Approximately 75%, because chronic B-cell activation and polyclonal B-cell stimulation in Sjögren syndrome commonly produce RF, which is an antibody reactive with self-IgG","C":"Approximately 20%, because only the subset of Sjögren patients with secondary disease and concurrent RA produce detectable rheumatoid factor","D":"Over 95%, because RF production is obligatory in any autoimmune exocrinopathy driven by CD4+ T cells and plasma cell infiltration of salivary glands","E":"Approximately 40%, because RF elevation requires glomerulonephritis-associated immune complex deposition to sustain the B-cell activation needed for RF production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About 75% of Sjögren syndrome patients are rheumatoid factor positive, even without concurrent rheumatoid arthritis. RF is an antibody reactive with self-IgG, and its production reflects the chronic polyclonal B-cell activation that is a hallmark of Sjögren syndrome. This high RF prevalence underscores that RF is not specific for RA. The polyclonal B-cell stimulation in Sjögren syndrome also drives ANA positivity, hypergammaglobulinemia, and ultimately the increased risk of B-cell lymphoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RF positive in ~75% of Sjögren patients regardless of RA; RF is not RA-specific. COMLEX: High RF in Sjögren reflects chronic polyclonal B-cell activation — the same process that drives the 40-fold increased lymphoma risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are diagnostic serologic markers for Sjögren syndrome within the spectrum of autoantibodies used in rheumatic disease evaluation."},{"id":"sdl41_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the typical antibody profile of patients with Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"High-titer anti-SSA (Ro) antibodies in a Sjögren syndrome patient correlate specifically with which of the following clinical manifestations?","choices":{"A":"Accelerated development of marginal zone lymphoma through direct BCR crosslinking by the Ro ribonucleoprotein antigen complexed with anti-SSA","B":"Early and severe salivary gland enlargement, because anti-SSA directly targets the M3 acetylcholine receptor on the surface of salivary acinar cells","C":"Glomerulonephritis through immune complex deposition in the glomerular basement membrane, similar to anti-SSA-mediated lupus nephritis","D":"Accelerated destruction of the corneal epithelium through complement-mediated lysis of lacrimal acinar cells bearing the SSA antigen","E":"Extraglandular disease manifestations including synovitis, diffuse pulmonary fibrosis, and peripheral neuropathy, which are more common in patients with high-titer anti-SSA"},"correct":"E","explanation":"High-titer anti-SSA (Ro) antibodies in Sjögren syndrome are specifically associated with extraglandular disease manifestations, including synovitis, diffuse pulmonary fibrosis, and peripheral neuropathy. These are seen in approximately one-third of Sjögren patients, and their presence correlates with elevated anti-SSA. In contrast, glomerular lesions are extremely rare in Sjögren syndrome (unlike SLE). Anti-SSA does not directly accelerate lymphomagenesis or cause gland destruction through receptor blockade.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High-titer anti-SSA in Sjögren = extraglandular manifestations: synovitis, pulmonary fibrosis, peripheral neuropathy. COMLEX: Anti-SSA also causes neonatal lupus and congenital complete heart block in offspring of mothers with high-titer anti-SSA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are diagnostic serologic markers for Sjögren syndrome within the spectrum of autoantibodies used in rheumatic disease evaluation."},{"id":"sdl41_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman presents with progressive skin tightening of her hands and forearms over 18 months. Skin biopsy shows extensive collagen deposition in the dermis with sparse perivascular CD4+ T cell infiltrates and virtual absence of hair follicles. ANA is positive with a nucleolar pattern; anti-Scl 70 is positive. Pulmonary function tests reveal a reduced DLCO. Which of the following best describes the sequence of pathogenic events that produced her dermal fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Scl 70 antibodies directly crosslink collagen fibrils in the dermis, causing progressive physical stiffening of the skin without any cellular involvement","B":"CD4+ Th2 cells accumulate in the skin and release TGF-beta and IL-13, which stimulate fibroblasts to transcribe collagen and fibronectin genes, causing progressive dermal fibrosis","C":"Anti-centromere antibodies bind to centromere proteins on dermal fibroblasts, constitutively activating them to produce excess collagen independent of T cell help","D":"Mast cells in the dermis release PDGF and TGF-alpha in response to vascular injury, directly stimulating collagen gene expression without T cell involvement","E":"CD8+ cytotoxic T cells kill dermal fibroblasts, triggering a wound-repair response in which surviving fibroblasts deposit collagen under TGF-beta1 stimulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The pathogenesis of SSc skin fibrosis involves CD4+ Th2 cells accumulating in the skin and releasing fibrogenic cytokines — primarily TGF-beta and IL-13. These cytokines stimulate fibroblasts to transcribe genes encoding collagen (especially Type I and III) and fibronectin, leading to progressive dermal fibrosis. The inflammatory infiltrate is typically sparse despite extensive fibrosis, which explains why the biopsy shows few T cells but abundant compact collagen. Anti-Scl 70 antibodies are diagnostic markers but do not directly crosslink or cause fibrosis through structural mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc fibrosis sequence — Th2 cells release TGF-beta + IL-13 → fibroblast collagen gene transcription → progressive dermal fibrosis. COMLEX: Anti-Scl 70 identifies patients at highest risk for pulmonary fibrosis; monitor with annual PFTs and HRCT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl41_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis has a strongly positive anti-Scl 70 (anti-topoisomerase I) antibody. Her rheumatologist compares her prognosis with that of a patient who is anti-centromere antibody positive. Which of the following correctly contrasts the clinical associations of these two autoantibodies?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Scl 70 is associated with limited scleroderma confined to the fingers and face, while anti-centromere predicts diffuse disease with rapid visceral involvement","B":"Anti-Scl 70 is found in the majority of all systemic sclerosis patients and is non-specific, while anti-centromere is rare and highly specific for the CREST subset","C":"Anti-centromere is associated with diffuse systemic sclerosis and pulmonary fibrosis, while anti-Scl 70 is specific for limited CREST syndrome","D":"Anti-Scl 70 and anti-centromere are equally common in diffuse systemic sclerosis and carry identical prognostic implications for visceral involvement","E":"Anti-Scl 70 is associated with diffuse systemic sclerosis, pulmonary fibrosis, and peripheral vascular disease, while anti-centromere is associated with limited disease and CREST syndrome with a more benign course"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Anti-Scl 70 (anti-topoisomerase I) is detected in 10-20% of patients with diffuse systemic sclerosis and is associated with pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral vascular disease — indicating a more severe disease subtype. Anti-centromere antibody is found in 20-30% of patients who tend to have limited scleroderma with CREST syndrome. These patients have relatively limited skin involvement confined to fingers, forearms, and face, and visceral involvement occurs late if at all, conferring a more benign course. The antibodies are not interchangeable prognostically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-Scl 70 = diffuse SSc + pulmonary fibrosis; anti-centromere = limited SSc + CREST = better prognosis. COMLEX: Never confuse these two antibodies on boards; the mnemonic is Scl 70 = Severe (diffuse), Centromere = Confined (limited/CREST).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl41_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis has a positive ANA in a nucleolar staining pattern and negative anti-Scl 70 and anti-centromere antibodies. She asks about the significance of her ANA test. Which of the following most accurately characterizes the ANA findings in systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Virtually all patients with systemic sclerosis have ANAs; specific antibodies such as anti-Scl 70 and anti-centromere provide diagnostic and prognostic information beyond ANA positivity alone","B":"ANA positivity in a nucleolar pattern is specific for systemic sclerosis and indicates that visceral organ involvement has already begun at the time of first detection","C":"ANAs are absent in systemic sclerosis and their presence in this patient indicates a co-existing diagnosis of SLE or mixed connective tissue disease","D":"The nucleolar ANA pattern is caused exclusively by anti-SSA and anti-SSB antibodies and predicts the rapid development of Sjogren overlap syndrome","E":"ANA positivity is found in fewer than 30% of systemic sclerosis patients and its detection is a late marker of severe diffuse visceral involvement"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Virtually all systemic sclerosis patients have positive ANAs. The role of these ANAs in disease pathogenesis is unclear, but they provide diagnostic and prognostic information. Specific ANAs carry distinct implications: anti-Scl 70 indicates diffuse disease with pulmonary fibrosis risk, while anti-centromere indicates limited disease/CREST. A nucleolar ANA pattern is common in SSc but is not specific for it, nor does it exclusively indicate anti-SSA/anti-SSB. The absence of specific antibodies does not reduce the likelihood of SSc in a clinically compatible case.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ANAs are found in virtually all SSc patients; specific antibodies (anti-Scl 70, anti-centromere) provide clinically actionable subtype and prognostic information. COMLEX: High-titer nucleolar ANA pattern should prompt specific SSc antibody testing even if anti-Scl 70 and anti-centromere are negative.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl41_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes which cause these changes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis has progressive difficulty opening her mouth fully, tapered clawlike finger deformities, and multiple small ulcerations at her fingertips. Skin biopsy confirms extensive dermal fibrosis. Which of the following best explains why the fingertip ulcerations develop in this clinical setting?","choices":{"A":"Anti-endothelial cell antibodies cause leukocytoclastic vasculitis in the dermal capillaries of the fingertips, producing immune-mediated digital infarction","B":"Subcutaneous calcification from CREST syndrome erodes through the thinned skin surface at digital tips, creating sterile ulcerations from calcium hydroxyapatite deposition","C":"Progressive loss of blood supply from vascular obliteration caused by repeated endothelial injury cycles and perivascular fibrosis leads to cutaneous ischemia and ulceration at the digital tips","D":"Collagen deposition compresses dermal lymphatics in the fingertips, causing lymphedema and secondary skin breakdown from impaired wound healing","E":"Dense dermal fibrosis physically abrades the overlying thinned epidermis during hand movement, producing pressure-induced frictional ulcerations at sites of stress"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fingertip ulcerations in systemic sclerosis result from progressive vascular disease. Repeated cycles of endothelial injury and platelet aggregation, followed by PDGF/TGF-beta release, lead to perivascular fibrosis and eventual obliteration of the microvascular bed in the digital tips. This ischemia causes cutaneous ulcerations and, in severe cases, autoamputation of terminal phalanges. Leukocytoclastic vasculitis, calcinosis erosion, and lymphedema are not the primary mechanism of digital ulceration in SSc.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Digital ulcers in SSc = ischemia from microvascular obliteration; severe disease leads to autoamputation of terminal phalanges. COMLEX: Nailfold capillaroscopy shows capillary loop distortion and obliteration early in SSc — a useful clinical tool.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl41_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes which cause these changes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with long-standing systemic sclerosis develops progressive dysphagia to solid foods and then liquids. Esophageal manometry reveals markedly reduced lower esophageal sphincter pressure with absent peristalsis in the lower two-thirds of the esophagus. Which of the following best explains the pathologic basis for her esophageal dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Progressive atrophy and collagenous fibrous replacement of the muscularis in the lower two-thirds of the esophagus impairs peristaltic motility and reduces lower esophageal sphincter pressure","B":"CD4+ T cell infiltration of the myenteric plexus causes autoimmune destruction of Auerbach ganglia, producing a pattern identical to primary achalasia","C":"Anti-centromere antibodies bind to smooth muscle contractile proteins in the esophageal wall, directly inhibiting peristaltic contractions through receptor blockade","D":"Fibrinoid necrosis of esophageal submucosal arterioles from malignant hypertension causes ischemic smooth muscle loss and aperistalsis throughout the esophagus","E":"Granulomatous inflammation of the esophageal submucosa causes concentric stricture formation, producing obstructive dysphagia without motility loss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The GI tract is affected in approximately 90% of SSc patients. The esophagus is the most severely affected segment. Progressive atrophy and collagenous fibrous replacement of the muscularis causes loss of peristaltic function in the lower two-thirds and reduces lower esophageal sphincter pressure. This leads to gastroesophageal reflux, Barrett esophagus risk, and eventually rubber-hose-like inflexibility of the esophageal wall. The mechanism is fibrosis of smooth muscle, not ganglion cell destruction (as in achalasia) or antibody receptor blockade.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc esophageal disease = collagenous replacement of muscularis causes aperistalsis and low LES pressure; gastroesophageal reflux in most patients. COMLEX: SSc is the most common systemic disease causing aperistalsis; management includes proton pump inhibitors and prokinetics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl41_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes which cause these changes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman has skin tightening confined to her fingers and forearms that has progressed slowly over 5 years. She also has telangiectasias, calcinosis cutis at her fingertips, and episodic digital color changes with cold exposure. Her anti-centromere antibody is positive. Which of the following best characterizes her disease subset and expected clinical course?","choices":{"A":"She has diffuse scleroderma with rapid visceral progression; anti-centromere positivity indicates imminent scleroderma renal crisis and early pulmonary fibrosis","B":"She has limited scleroderma with CREST syndrome; skin involvement is confined to the fingers, forearms, and face, with a relatively benign course and late or absent visceral involvement","C":"She has the overlap syndrome of systemic sclerosis and SLE; anti-centromere is a specific marker for this overlap and indicates risk of lupus nephritis","D":"She has eosinophilic fasciitis, a variant of systemic sclerosis with anti-centromere positivity that predominantly affects the fascia rather than the dermis","E":"She has the diffuse form of systemic sclerosis presenting with a deceptively limited initial skin distribution; anti-centromere indicates visceral disease will not occur"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has limited systemic sclerosis with CREST syndrome: Calcinosis, Raynaud, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, and Telangiectasia. Anti-centromere antibody is found in 20-30% of SSc patients and is characteristically associated with this limited subset. Skin involvement in CREST is confined to the fingers, forearms, and face. Visceral involvement, including esophageal disease, pulmonary hypertension, and biliary cirrhosis, may occur late or not at all. The course is relatively benign compared with diffuse SSc, and scleroderma renal crisis is uncommon in this subset.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-centromere = CREST syndrome = limited SSc = better prognosis. COMLEX: CREST patients still need monitoring for pulmonary hypertension and biliary cirrhosis, which are late complications specific to this subset.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl41_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathology of vasculopathy and the mechanism of organ damage occurring in Raynaud's phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis develops acute-onset hypertension with blood pressure 182/114 mmHg, rising serum creatinine from 0.9 to 3.1 mg/dL over 4 days, and microangiopathic hemolytic anemia. She is diagnosed with scleroderma renal crisis. Which of the following best describes the vascular mechanism responsible for this acute deterioration?","choices":{"A":"In the setting of malignant hypertension, the pre-existing interlobular artery intimal thickening undergoes superimposed fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles with thrombosis and infarction, causing rapidly progressive renal failure","B":"Anti-Scl 70 antibodies deposit in the glomerular basement membrane and activate complement, causing diffuse crescentic glomerulonephritis with fibrinoid necrosis of glomeruli","C":"CD4+ T cells infiltrate the renal cortex and medulla and release TGF-beta, driving acute tubulointerstitial nephritis with eosinophilic tubular casts","D":"Renal artery granulomatous inflammation causes segmental fibrous thickening of the main renal artery lumen, identical to the renovascular lesion of Takayasu arteritis","E":"Immune complex deposition in mesangial cells activates complement, causing mesangioproliferative glomerulonephritis with red cell casts and proteinuria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Scleroderma renal crisis occurs when accelerating or malignant hypertension develops on the background of pre-existing SSc vascular disease. The interlobular arteries (150-500 micrometers) already have SSc-related intimal thickening. When malignant hypertension supervenes, these vessels develop superimposed fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles, thrombosis, and cortical infarction, causing rapidly progressive renal failure. There are no specific glomerular changes in SSc; crescentic GN and immune complex GN are not the mechanism. ACE inhibitors are the treatment of choice for scleroderma renal crisis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Scleroderma renal crisis = pre-existing arteriolar changes + malignant hypertension = fibrinoid necrosis + thrombosis + infarction = rapidly progressive renal failure. COMLEX: Treat immediately with ACE inhibitors; do not withhold due to rising creatinine — ACE inhibitors are life-saving in this setting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 53 Obj 1 — Pathogenesis of Raynaud Phenomenon"},{"id":"sdl41_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathology of vasculopathy and the mechanism of organ damage occurring in Raynaud's phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis and Raynaud phenomenon asks why her fingers turn white, then blue, then red during cold exposure. Her nailfold capillaroscopy shows distorted and obliterated capillary loops. Which of the following most accurately describes the microvascular sequence producing a Raynaud episode in systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Cold exposure triggers anti-endothelial antibody complement activation, causing transient capillary occlusion followed by reactive hyperemia as complement becomes depleted","B":"Histamine release from perivascular mast cells in the cold induces initial vasodilation, then reflex vasoconstriction, then secondary vasodilation during rewarming","C":"Cold exposure causes PDGF-mediated smooth muscle contraction followed by passive filling of the dilated capillary bed, then reactive vasodilation from prostaglandin E2 release","D":"Cold triggers episodic vasoconstriction of arteries and arterioles from adrenergic receptor upregulation on vascular smooth muscle, causing pallor and cyanosis, followed by reactive hyperemia on rewarming","E":"Cold directly induces TGF-beta-mediated endothelial cell apoptosis, releasing von Willebrand factor that causes transient thrombotic microangiopathy in digital capillaries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Raynaud phenomenon in SSc is caused by episodic vasoconstriction of digital arteries and arterioles. In SSc, repeated endothelial injury has led to perivascular fibrosis and upregulation of adrenergic receptors on vascular smooth muscle. Cold exposure, which is a normal physiologic trigger for adrenergic vasoconstriction, produces an exaggerated vasoconstrictive response. This causes pallor from cutoff of blood flow, then cyanosis from deoxygenated stagnant blood, then reactive erythema from hyperemia on rewarming. The underlying structural vessel abnormality in SSc makes the episodes more severe and prolonged than in primary Raynaud.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Raynaud triple color sequence = white (vasospasm/pallor) → blue (cyanosis from stagnant blood) → red (reactive hyperemia); adrenergic receptor upregulation from SSc vascular disease amplifies cold-triggered vasoconstriction. COMLEX: Primary Raynaud has no structural disease; secondary Raynaud (as in SSc) has nailfold capillaroscopy abnormalities.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 53 Obj 1 — Pathogenesis of Raynaud Phenomenon"},{"id":"sdl41_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathology of vasculopathy and the mechanism of organ damage occurring in Raynaud's phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis develops progressive exertional dyspnea. Echocardiography shows elevated right heart pressures, and right heart catheterization confirms a mean pulmonary artery pressure of 38 mmHg. High-resolution CT shows no interstitial fibrosis. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of her isolated pulmonary arterial hypertension in systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Anti-centromere antibodies deposit in pulmonary arteriolar walls, triggering Type II complement-mediated lysis of pulmonary endothelium and reactive smooth muscle hypertrophy","B":"Granulomatous inflammation of pulmonary arterial walls from a GCA-like mechanism causes progressive luminal narrowing of pulmonary arteries and pulmonary hypertension","C":"TGF-beta-driven alveolar fibrosis obliterates pulmonary capillaries, increasing pulmonary vascular resistance through mechanical compression of the capillary bed","D":"Anti-Scl 70 antibodies form immune complexes in the pulmonary circulation, triggering Type III hypersensitivity and fibrinoid necrosis of pulmonary arterioles","E":"Pulmonary vascular endothelial dysfunction causes vasospasm of pulmonary vessels, representing a Raynaud-like phenomenon in the pulmonary circulation that drives elevated pulmonary artery pressures"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In SSc, pulmonary vascular endothelial dysfunction causes vasospasm of pulmonary vessels. This is conceptually a Raynaud-like phenomenon in the pulmonary circulation — the same mechanism of repeated endothelial injury, perivascular fibrosis, and smooth muscle adrenergic hyperreactivity that operates in the systemic circulation also affects pulmonary vessels. The absence of interstitial fibrosis in this case distinguishes isolated pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) from pulmonary hypertension secondary to pulmonary fibrosis. Pulmonary disease has now become the leading cause of SSc-related death.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc PAH = endothelial dysfunction causing Raynaud-like vasospasm in pulmonary circulation; pulmonary disease is now the #1 cause of SSc death. COMLEX: Screen SSc patients with annual echo and PFTs; treat PAH with phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors, endothelin receptor antagonists, or prostacyclins.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 53 Obj 1 — Pathogenesis of Raynaud Phenomenon"},{"id":"sdl41_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the underlying pathologic cause of Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman presents with 6 months of dry eyes, dry mouth, and bilateral parotid gland enlargement. Salivary gland biopsy shows periductal lymphocytic infiltration with germinal center formation. Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are both positive. She has no other autoimmune disease. Which of the following best describes the pathogenesis of her condition?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SSA and anti-SSB antibodies directly block M3 muscarinic acetylcholine receptors on acinar cells, inhibiting parasympathetic secretion without causing structural gland destruction","B":"Aberrant CD4+ T cell and B cell activation, possibly triggered by viral infection of glandular tissue releasing self-antigens, leads to lymphocytic infiltration and immunologically mediated destruction of exocrine glands","C":"Immune complex deposition of anti-SSA and anti-SSB antibody complexes in ductal basement membranes activates complement, causing ductal necrosis and progressive obstruction","D":"Th17-driven IL-17 production causes neutrophilic invasion of salivary ducts, destroying ductal epithelium and blocking secretion by structural obliteration of the ductal system","E":"NK cell-mediated cytotoxicity against ductal cells expressing Ro and La antigens causes periductal scarring and secondary acinar atrophy by interrupting glandular drainage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The pathogenesis of Sjögren syndrome involves aberrant activation of both CD4+ T cells and B cells. The initiating trigger may be viral infection of glandular tissue, which causes cell death, releases self-antigens, and activates autoreactive lymphocytes that escaped tolerance. These lymphocytes infiltrate the lacrimal and salivary glands, causing progressive destruction. Anti-SSA and anti-SSB antibodies are serologic markers, but their direct pathogenic role is unclear and they do not function primarily by receptor blockade or immune complex deposition in the gland.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren pathogenesis = aberrant T cell and B cell activation, possible viral trigger, leading to exocrine gland lymphocytic destruction. COMLEX: Primary Sjögren = sicca syndrome alone; secondary Sjögren = sicca + another autoimmune disease (RA most common).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl41_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the underlying pathologic cause of Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman with Sjögren syndrome and persistent bilateral parotid swelling undergoes repeat salivary gland biopsy after 7 years of disease. The biopsy reveals a dominant monoclonal B-cell population with marginal zone morphology. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which Sjögren syndrome carries a markedly elevated risk of this complication?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SSA antibodies bind to the BCR co-receptor CD21 on gland-infiltrating B cells, constitutively activating the NFkB pathway and bypassing normal apoptotic checkpoints","B":"Ductal epithelial hyperplasia mechanically prevents B-cell egress from the gland, trapping proliferating B cells and allowing clonal outgrowth without immune surveillance","C":"Prolonged polyclonal B-cell stimulation in the chronically inflamed gland allows individual B-cell clones to accumulate somatic mutations and gain a proliferative advantage, producing a marginal zone lymphoma","D":"Viral integration by HTLV-1 into B cells that have infiltrated salivary glands directly transforms them through LMP1 oncogene activation causing malignant proliferation","E":"Hypergammaglobulinemia from chronic B-cell activation overwhelms somatic hypermutation proofreading, causing point mutations that constitutively activate BCL-2 and c-Myc"},"correct":"C","explanation":"About 5% of Sjögren syndrome patients develop B-cell lymphoma — a 40-fold increased risk. The mechanism is chronic polyclonal B-cell stimulation within the inflamed gland. Under sustained antigen stimulation, individual B-cell clones acquire somatic mutations that confer a proliferative advantage, eventually producing a monoclonal marginal zone lymphoma. Emergence of a dominant B-cell clone on biopsy signals this transformation. The same mechanism drives lymphoma in Hashimoto thyroiditis. HTLV associations are with Sjögren-like disease, not established as a direct lymphoma-causing mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren lymphoma risk = 40-fold elevated; marginal zone B-cell lymphoma from chronic polyclonal B-cell stimulation. COMLEX: Salivary gland enlargement in Sjögren that is new, firm, or rapid-onset warrants biopsy to exclude lymphoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl41_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the underlying pathologic cause of Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with primary Sjögren syndrome develops non-anion gap metabolic acidosis, glucosuria with normal serum glucose, and phosphaturia. Urinalysis shows no casts or significant proteinuria. Which of the following best explains this pattern of renal involvement in Sjögren syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SSA antibodies cause immune complex glomerulonephritis with subepithelial deposits, heavy proteinuria, and nephrotic syndrome in primary Sjögren syndrome","B":"Anti-SSB antibodies deposit in glomerular capillary walls, causing membranous nephropathy and subsequent secondary tubular dysfunction from nephrotic-range proteinuria","C":"Granulomatous interstitial nephritis caused by salivary gland-reactive T cells destroys glomerular podocytes, producing mixed nephrotic-nephritic syndrome","D":"Lymphocytic infiltration of the renal tubular interstitium causes tubulointerstitial nephritis, with tubular dysfunction including renal tubular acidosis, glucosuria, and phosphaturia","E":"Fibrinoid necrosis of renal arterioles from ANCA-mediated vasculitis causes ischemic tubular injury and non-anion gap acidosis without glomerular involvement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glomerular lesions are extremely rare in Sjögren syndrome, unlike SLE. Instead, the dominant renal manifestation is tubulointerstitial nephritis caused by lymphocytic infiltration of the renal tubular interstitium. This produces tubular dysfunction including renal tubular acidosis (type I or II), glucosuria, phosphaturia, and uricosuria — a Fanconi syndrome-like pattern. The absence of casts and significant proteinuria points to tubular rather than glomerular disease. ANCA vasculitis and immune complex GN are not features of primary Sjögren syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren renal disease = tubulointerstitial nephritis, NOT glomerulonephritis; tubular dysfunction includes RTA, glucosuria, phosphaturia. COMLEX: Contrast with SLE, where glomerulonephritis is the dominant renal lesion; Sjögren spares glomeruli.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl41_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the typical antibody profile of patients with Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman presents with dry eyes, dry mouth, and recurrent parotid swelling. Her ANA is positive by immunofluorescence. Specific antibody testing is ordered. Which of the following serologic findings would be most consistent with a diagnosis of primary Sjögren syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Anti-dsDNA positive at high titer, anti-Smith positive, complement C3 and C4 decreased, with anti-SSA and anti-SSB both negative","B":"Anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl 70) positive, anti-centromere negative, anti-SSA negative, and RF negative throughout the course of disease","C":"Anti-SSA (Ro) positive, anti-SSB (La) positive, RF positive in approximately 75% of patients, and ANA positive in 50-80% by immunofluorescence","D":"Anti-Jo-1 positive, anti-Mi-2 negative, anti-SSA weakly positive, with elevated CK and aldolase consistent with inflammatory myopathy overlap","E":"ANCA positive in a perinuclear pattern, anti-SSA negative, anti-SSB negative, and cryoglobulin positive indicating small vessel vasculitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The classic serologic profile of primary Sjögren syndrome includes positive anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La) in up to 90% of patients by sensitive techniques, RF positive in approximately 75% reflecting chronic B-cell activation, and ANA positive in 50-80% by immunofluorescence. Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith with low complement indicate SLE. Anti-Scl 70 indicates diffuse systemic sclerosis. Anti-Jo-1 indicates inflammatory myopathy. Perinuclear ANCA with cryoglobulin indicates vasculitis. None of these alternative profiles characterize primary Sjögren syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren serologic profile = anti-SSA + anti-SSB (up to 90%) + RF (75%) + ANA (50-80%). COMLEX: RF positivity in Sjögren without RA reflects the same polyclonal B-cell activation driving lymphoma risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — Autoantibodies in Rheumatic Disease Associated with Specific Conditions"},{"id":"sdl41_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the typical antibody profile of patients with Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman with Sjögren syndrome has a high titer of anti-SSA antibodies and is found to have interstitial lung disease and mild symmetric sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy. Her oral and ocular dryness symptoms are mild. Which of the following correctly explains the association between her high-titer anti-SSA and these extraglandular findings?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SSA antibodies form cryoglobulins that deposit in pulmonary capillary walls and peripheral nerve vasa nervorum, causing Type III complement-mediated injury","B":"High-titer anti-SSA triggers NK cell activation against peripheral nerves expressing the Ro antigen, causing direct cytotoxic demyelination of sensorimotor fibers","C":"Anti-SSA binds to voltage-gated calcium channels on peripheral nerve axons, causing calcium-dependent conduction block and progressive axonal degeneration","D":"Anti-SSA antibodies activate the classical complement pathway in the pulmonary interstitium, causing alveolar wall destruction identical to anti-GBM disease","E":"High-titer anti-SSA antibodies in Sjögren syndrome are specifically associated with extraglandular disease manifestations, including synovitis, diffuse pulmonary fibrosis, and peripheral neuropathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"High-titer anti-SSA (Ro) antibodies are specifically associated with extraglandular manifestations of Sjögren syndrome. Extraglandular disease is seen in approximately one-third of Sjögren patients and includes synovitis, diffuse pulmonary fibrosis, and peripheral neuropathy. Patients with extraglandular disease and high-titer anti-SSA may have relatively mild sicca symptoms while their systemic involvement is more prominent. The mechanism of extraglandular injury is not fully established, but it is not cryoglobulin-mediated, NK-cell-mediated, or classical complement-mediated in the same way as described in the distractors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High-titer anti-SSA in Sjögren = extraglandular disease (synovitis, lung fibrosis, peripheral neuropathy). COMLEX: Anti-SSA in pregnant women with Sjögren is associated with neonatal lupus and congenital complete heart block — a distinct clinical scenario from extraglandular adult disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — Autoantibodies in Rheumatic Disease Associated with Specific Conditions"},{"id":"sdl41_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the typical antibody profile of patients with Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with Sjögren syndrome is counseled that anti-SSA and anti-SSB antibodies are not entirely specific for her condition. Which of the following best explains this diagnostic limitation of these serologic markers?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are also found in all patients with systemic sclerosis and are used as required diagnostic criteria for the CREST variant of SSc","B":"Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are identical in molecular target to anti-Smith antibodies, which are also elevated in both SLE and Sjögren syndrome and cannot distinguish between the two","C":"Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are detectable in all autoimmune diseases because they target ubiquitous intracellular RNA-binding proteins expressed in every cell type of the body","D":"Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are also present in a smaller percentage of SLE patients, meaning they are considered diagnostic serologic markers for Sjögren syndrome but cannot be used in isolation to exclude other autoimmune diagnoses","E":"Anti-SSA is found exclusively in secondary Sjögren syndrome associated with RA, while anti-SSB is found exclusively in primary Sjögren syndrome without concurrent autoimmune disease"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La) antibodies are the most important serologic markers of Sjögren syndrome but are not entirely specific. They are also present in a smaller percentage of SLE patients. Therefore, their presence alone does not exclude SLE or confirm Sjögren syndrome. The full clinical picture, including the sicca syndrome, salivary gland biopsy findings, and exclusion of SLE, is required to make a definitive Sjögren diagnosis. Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are not markers of SSc, are not identical to anti-Smith, and are not present in all autoimmune conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-SSA and anti-SSB = best serologic markers for Sjögren but also seen in a subset of SLE patients; use clinical context and biopsy for definitive diagnosis. COMLEX: The combination of positive anti-SSA/anti-SSB with characteristic biopsy findings (focus score at least 1) meets classification criteria for Sjögren syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — Autoantibodies in Rheumatic Disease Associated with Specific Conditions"}]},{"sdlNumber":42,"title":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus","questions":[{"id":"sdl42_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the genetic, immunologic, and environmental contributions to the predisposition and possible causes of systemic lupus erythematosus.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which genetic factor in SLE most directly impairs clearance of apoptotic nuclear debris, thereby sustaining the autoantigen burden that drives anti-dsDNA production?","choices":{"A":"Inherited deficiency of early complement components such as C1q, C2, or C4, which are required for opsonization and phagocytic removal of apoptotic bodies and immune complexes","B":"HLA-DQ allelic variation linked to anti-Sm and antiphospholipid antibody production via altered peptide presentation in thymic selection","C":"Genome-wide association loci encoding proteins involved in lymphocyte signaling that lower the threshold for autoreactive B-cell activation","D":"Defective peripheral tolerance checkpoints for autoreactive CD4+ helper T cells that escape deletion in lymph node germinal centers","E":"Overexpression of TLR7 and TLR9 on plasmacytoid dendritic cells that amplifies downstream interferon responses to self nucleic acids"},"correct":"A","explanation":"C1q normally opsonizes apoptotic bodies for phagocytic clearance; its deficiency allows nuclear autoantigens to persist, engage TLRs on B cells and DCs, and sustain anti-DNA production. HLA-DQ variation influences autoantibody specificity but not clearance. Non-MHC GWAS loci affect signaling thresholds but are not clearance mechanisms. Peripheral T-cell tolerance defects drive amplification downstream. TLR overexpression amplifies responses that depend on antigen persistence already established by complement deficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complement deficiency (C1q, C2, C4) is the classic genetic predisposition to SLE; C1q knockout mice develop lupus-like disease. COMLEX: C4A null allele is a key genetic risk factor — know the complement-clearance mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — SLE exemplifies autoimmune failure of self-tolerance driven by genetic complement deficiencies, TLR hyperactivation by self nucleic acids, and type I IFN amplification of autoreactive lymphocyte responses."},{"id":"sdl42_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the genetic, immunologic, and environmental contributions to the predisposition and possible causes of systemic lupus erythematosus.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which environmental trigger exacerbates SLE by simultaneously inducing cellular apoptosis, modifying nuclear DNA to enhance TLR recognition, and stimulating keratinocytes to release pro-inflammatory IL-1?","choices":{"A":"Chronic intracellular bacterial infections that produce molecular mimics of nuclear antigens and trigger polyclonal B-cell activation via superantigen-like mechanisms","B":"Viral triggers including Epstein-Barr virus that activate latent autoreactive B cells through molecular mimicry of Sm antigen ribonucleoprotein complexes","C":"Estrogen and other sex hormones that promote B-cell survival and activation in female patients of reproductive age, explaining the 9:1 female-to-male ratio","D":"Exposure to ultraviolet light, which drives keratinocyte apoptosis releasing nuclear autoantigens, alters DNA to enhance TLR7 and TLR9 recognition, and stimulates keratinocytes to secrete IL-1 promoting inflammation","E":"Drug exposures including hydralazine and procainamide that induce histone hypomethylation and interfere with central tolerance by altering thymic antigen presentation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"UV light exacerbates SLE through three mechanisms: inducing apoptosis in skin cells that releases nuclear autoantigens, altering DNA in ways that enhance TLR recognition by innate immune sensors, and stimulating keratinocytes to produce IL-1, which amplifies local inflammation. Bacterial molecular mimicry is not a named SLE trigger. EBV is proposed but the UV triad is the testable mechanism. Sex hormones contribute to sex bias but are not environmental exacerbants per se. Drug-induced lupus from hydralazine and procainamide involves histone modification, not the UV pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UV light is the classic SLE environmental trigger — exacerbates via apoptosis, TLR-modified DNA, and IL-1. Malar rash worsens with sun exposure. COMLEX: Counsel SLE patients on sun avoidance and sunscreen use.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — SLE exemplifies autoimmune failure of self-tolerance driven by genetic complement deficiencies, TLR hyperactivation by self nucleic acids, and type I IFN amplification of autoreactive lymphocyte responses."},{"id":"sdl42_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the genetic, immunologic, and environmental contributions to the predisposition and possible causes of systemic lupus erythematosus.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"TLR stimulation by self nucleic acids contributes to SLE pathogenesis primarily by activating which two cell populations to perpetuate a cycle of autoantibody production and type I interferon secretion?","choices":{"A":"NK cells and mast cells, which release cytotoxic granules and histamine upon TLR stimulation by self DNA, producing widespread tissue injury in SLE target organs","B":"B cells specific for nuclear antigens and plasmacytoid dendritic cells: B cells receive TLR7 and TLR9 co-stimulation from internalized nucleic acids driving autoantibody production, while pDCs activated by TLRs produce IFN-alpha that amplifies the autoreactive response","C":"CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and plasma cells, which mediate direct cellular killing of self-reactive targets and secrete high-affinity autoantibodies respectively via TLR-independent pathways","D":"Neutrophils and tissue macrophages, which phagocytose apoptotic debris via TLR4 engagement and present nuclear antigens on MHC class II to stimulate naive autoreactive T cells","E":"Regulatory T cells and follicular helper T cells, which lose suppressive control via TLR9 engagement and then co-stimulate autoreactive B cells in germinal centers via CD40L"},"correct":"B","explanation":"B cells specific for nuclear antigens internalize immune complexes through surface immunoglobulin, and the nucleic acid components engage TLR7 and TLR9 to provide a second activating signal driving autoantibody production. Simultaneously, plasmacytoid DCs activated by self nucleic acids via TLRs produce IFN-alpha that further activates B cells and autoreactive T cells, creating an amplification loop. NK cells and mast cells are not the central TLR-driven players in SLE. CD8+ T cells contribute but are not the primary TLR responders. Neutrophils and macrophages clear debris but B cells and pDCs are the critical amplifiers. Treg loss is downstream of TLR-driven activation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The type I IFN signature (high serum IFN-alpha) is a biomarker of SLE disease activity; correlates with severity. TLR7 and TLR9 on B cells and pDCs are activated by self RNA and DNA from apoptotic nuclear debris. COMLEX: TLR dysregulation bridges innate and adaptive autoimmunity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — SLE exemplifies autoimmune failure of self-tolerance driven by genetic complement deficiencies, TLR hyperactivation by self nucleic acids, and type I IFN amplification of autoreactive lymphocyte responses."},{"id":"sdl42_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the type of hypersensitivity responsible for lupus renal disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The renal disease in SLE results from which pathogenic mechanism, and what laboratory finding at the time of a flare directly reflects this mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Type II cytotoxic antibodies that bind fixed glomerular basement membrane collagen antigens and activate complement locally, producing linear IgG deposits and fibrinoid necrosis on biopsy","B":"Type IV delayed hypersensitivity in which CD4+ T cells infiltrate the glomerulus and release IFN-gamma, causing tubulointerstitial nephritis without antibody involvement or complement deposition","C":"Type I IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation in glomerular capillaries releasing vasoactive mediators that produce transient proteinuria without structural glomerular damage","D":"Direct CD8+ T-cell cytotoxicity against renal tubular epithelial cells that express aberrant MHC class I, causing tubular injury and cast nephropathy without glomerular involvement","E":"Type III immune complex hypersensitivity: DNA-anti-dsDNA complexes deposit in glomeruli, fix complement via the classical pathway, and drive proliferative glomerulonephritis, reflected by hypocomplementemia from complement consumption during active disease"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lupus nephritis is the canonical Type III hypersensitivity example. DNA-anti-DNA complexes circulate, deposit in the glomerular mesangium and capillary walls, and fix complement via C1q, consuming C3 and C4 and producing hypocomplementemia during active flares. Rising anti-dsDNA and falling C3 and C4 together are the classic flare markers. Type II anti-GBM disease produces linear IF deposits, not granular. Type IV is cell-mediated without complement deposition. Type I is IgE-mast cell mediated. CD8+ tubular cytotoxicity is not the primary mechanism of lupus nephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lupus nephritis = Type III hypersensitivity = granular IF pattern + hypocomplementemia + anti-dsDNA. Class IV (diffuse proliferative) is the most severe and common nephritis class. COMLEX: Low C3/C4 during SLE flare is the clinical pearl for immune complex-mediated disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Lupus nephritis is the canonical Type III hypersensitivity lesion: DNA–anti-DNA immune complexes deposit in glomeruli, fix complement, and drive mesangial and endothelial injury."},{"id":"sdl42_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the type of hypersensitivity responsible for lupus renal disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Renal biopsy from an SLE patient with proteinuria and RBC casts shows granular deposits of IgG and C3 on immunofluorescence. Which of the following best explains the granular rather than linear deposit pattern?","choices":{"A":"Antibodies bind directly and continuously to fixed glomerular basement membrane antigens along the entire GBM, which creates the granular appearance due to uneven antigen density across the membrane","B":"Circulating immune complexes trap nonuniformly along the glomerulus, producing discontinuous granular deposits consistent with Type III immune complex hypersensitivity rather than antibody binding to a fixed uniform antigen","C":"IgE antibodies attach to Fc epsilon receptors on mesangial cells and activate embedded mast cells that release granule contents, forming discrete granular deposits visible on immunofluorescence","D":"CD4+ T cells infiltrate the glomerulus and deposit cytokine-laden granules at random intervals along the capillary wall, visible as discontinuous granular deposits on immunofluorescence","E":"ANCA-directed antibodies against myeloperoxidase deposit in capillary loops at sites of neutrophil degranulation, producing a scattered granular staining pattern on direct immunofluorescence"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Granular IF staining indicates immune complex deposition (Type III). Complexes form in circulation or in situ and trap at irregular intervals along the glomerular capillary wall and mesangium, creating a discontinuous granular pattern. This is the hallmark of lupus nephritis. In contrast, anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture, Type II) produces linear staining because antibodies bind uniformly along the entire GBM surface of the NC1 domain of type IV collagen. IgE does not produce glomerular IF deposits. T cells produce no visible IF pattern. ANCA-associated GN is characteristically pauci-immune with negative or near-negative IF staining.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Granular IF = immune complex (Type III); Linear IF = anti-GBM (Type II). This distinction is a classic board question. COMLEX: Lupus nephritis Class IV shows full-house granular IF with IgG, IgM, IgA, C3, and C1q.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Lupus nephritis is the canonical Type III hypersensitivity lesion: DNA–anti-DNA immune complexes deposit in glomeruli, fix complement, and drive mesangial and endothelial injury."},{"id":"sdl42_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the type of hypersensitivity responsible for lupus renal disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best distinguishes lupus nephritis from anti-GBM disease in terms of the underlying immunopathologic mechanism and its laboratory reflection?","choices":{"A":"Lupus nephritis involves circulating immune complex deposition producing a granular IF pattern with hypocomplementemia, while anti-GBM disease involves antibodies to a fixed NC1 structural antigen producing linear IF with normal or minimally reduced complement levels","B":"Lupus nephritis is caused by Type IV hypersensitivity with glomerular CD4+ T cell infiltrates, while anti-GBM disease is a Type III immune complex disorder with granular subendothelial deposits on EM","C":"Lupus nephritis causes nephrotic syndrome exclusively via podocyte injury from subepithelial immune complexes, while anti-GBM disease causes nephritic syndrome via direct complement-mediated GBM destruction","D":"Lupus nephritis requires inherited complement deficiency to initiate glomerulonephritis, while anti-GBM disease develops only in patients with normal complement and HLA-DR2 or DR15 genotype","E":"Lupus nephritis is driven by anti-Sm antibodies depositing within the GBM matrix, while anti-GBM disease is driven by anti-dsDNA antibodies binding podocyte surface proteins"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lupus nephritis (Type III) results from circulating DNA-anti-DNA immune complexes depositing in the glomerulus, causing granular IF staining and complement consumption (low C3, C4). Anti-GBM disease (Type II, Goodpasture) results from antibodies directed against the fixed alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen in the GBM, producing linear IF staining and typically normal complement levels because the antigen is structural and not circulating to consume complement. The other options reverse or misattribute the hypersensitivity types and antibody specificities involved in each disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Goodpasture = linear IgG + normal complement + anti-GBM antibody + HLA-DR2/DR15 association. Lupus nephritis = granular IF + low complement + anti-dsDNA. COMLEX: Both can cause rapidly progressive (crescentic) GN but by different mechanisms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Lupus nephritis is the canonical Type III hypersensitivity lesion: DNA–anti-DNA immune complexes deposit in glomeruli, fix complement, and drive mesangial and endothelial injury."},{"id":"sdl42_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the type of hypersensitivity responsible for blood cell destruction in SLE (RBC, WBC, Platelets).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An SLE patient develops anemia with a positive direct Coombs test and elevated indirect bilirubin. Which mechanism best accounts for the reduction in circulating RBCs?","choices":{"A":"Type III immune complex deposition on red cell membranes activates the terminal complement pathway, driving osmotic intravascular lysis of erythrocytes in the systemic circulation","B":"Type IV delayed hypersensitivity in which macrophage-derived cytokines such as TNF-alpha suppress erythropoiesis in the bone marrow, reducing red cell production without peripheral destruction","C":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation in the bone marrow sinusoids releases histamine that directly damages erythrocyte precursors, reducing reticulocyte output in a Type I reaction","D":"Antiphospholipid antibodies binding red cell membrane phospholipids trigger platelet aggregation and mechanical fragmentation of erythrocytes in narrowed microvasculature","E":"Type II antibody-mediated hypersensitivity: autoantibodies coat red cell surface antigens, promoting opsonization and extravascular phagocytic destruction of RBCs by splenic macrophages bearing Fc receptors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia in SLE is a Type II (antibody-mediated) cytotoxic hypersensitivity reaction. Warm-reactive IgG autoantibodies coat red cell surface antigens; these antibody-coated cells are recognized by Fc receptors on splenic macrophages and destroyed by extravascular phagocytosis, producing spherocytes, elevated indirect bilirubin, low haptoglobin, and a positive direct Coombs test. Type III immune complexes cause glomerulonephritis and vasculitis, not primarily hemolysis. Type IV is cell-mediated. IgE and mast cells mediate Type I allergy. Antiphospholipid antibodies cause thrombosis via different mechanisms, not mechanical fragmentation hemolysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Warm AIHA = IgG opsonization = positive direct Coombs = extravascular hemolysis = spherocytes, no schistocytes. SLE is a leading cause of warm AIHA. COMLEX: Direct Coombs positive = antibody on RBC surface; indirect Coombs = free antibody in serum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Autoantibodies against RBCs, WBCs, and platelets in SLE drive Type II cytotoxic destruction via opsonization and Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis in the spleen."},{"id":"sdl42_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the type of hypersensitivity responsible for blood cell destruction in SLE (RBC, WBC, Platelets).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"SLE-associated thrombocytopenia is classified under which hypersensitivity type, and what is the primary mechanism of platelet destruction?","choices":{"A":"Type II hypersensitivity: antiplatelet IgG autoantibodies opsonize platelets for phagocytic destruction by the mononuclear phagocyte system, predominantly via Fc receptor-mediated clearance in the spleen","B":"Type III hypersensitivity: circulating immune complexes deposit on platelet membranes and activate the terminal complement pathway, causing osmotic lysis of platelets in the peripheral blood","C":"Type I hypersensitivity: IgE antibodies bound to platelet surface Fc epsilon receptors trigger degranulation and platelet aggregation via cross-linking of adjacent receptor-antibody complexes","D":"Type IV hypersensitivity: sensitized CD8+ T cells release perforin and granzyme B to directly kill platelets that aberrantly upregulate MHC class I presentation of self peptides","E":"Mixed Type II and Type III: antiphospholipid antibodies simultaneously opsonize platelets and form immune complexes that activate the classical complement pathway on the platelet surface"},"correct":"A","explanation":"SLE-associated thrombocytopenia is Type II (antibody-mediated) cytotoxic hypersensitivity, analogous to immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP). Autoantibodies against platelet surface glycoproteins (particularly GPIIb/IIIa) coat platelets, which are then phagocytosed by macrophages in the spleen via Fc receptors. This is the same mechanism as RBC destruction in autoimmune hemolytic anemia, applied to platelets. Type III involves immune complex deposition in vessel walls or glomeruli, not the primary platelet destruction mechanism. Type I requires IgE and mast cells. Type IV is cell-mediated without antibody opsonization. Antiphospholipid antibodies can cause thrombocytopenia but through a somewhat different mechanism than direct opsonization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLE causes thrombocytopenia (Type II) AND can cause antiphospholipid syndrome (separate mechanism of platelet consumption via thrombosis). Know which is which. Bone marrow biopsy in immune thrombocytopenia shows megakaryocyte hyperplasia. COMLEX: Splenomegaly in SLE reflects hyperactive splenic phagocytosis of opsonized blood cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Autoantibodies against RBCs, WBCs, and platelets in SLE drive Type II cytotoxic destruction via opsonization and Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis in the spleen."},{"id":"sdl42_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the type of hypersensitivity responsible for blood cell destruction in SLE (RBC, WBC, Platelets).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An SLE patient has a positive direct Coombs test and hemoglobin of 7.8 g/dL. Her peripheral smear shows spherocytes but no schistocytes. Which finding most confirms that the hemolysis is extravascular rather than intravascular or microangiopathic?","choices":{"A":"Reduced serum C3 and C4 consistent with complement consumption from immune complex deposition directly on red cell membranes driving classical pathway-mediated lysis within the circulation","B":"Elevated indirect bilirubin and undetectable serum haptoglobin consistent with extravascular hemolysis: antibody-coated RBCs are phagocytosed by splenic macrophages releasing hemoglobin catabolized to bilirubin, while haptoglobin is consumed by any free hemoglobin released during partial phagocytosis","C":"Positive antiphospholipid antibodies with prolonged PTT confirming that phospholipid-reactive antibodies are primarily responsible for coating and lysing red cell membranes","D":"Thrombocytopenia and leukopenia on CBC confirming immune complex deposition targeting all hematopoietic cell lines simultaneously via a Type III mechanism","E":"Normal serum complement with elevated anti-dsDNA titers confirming that anti-dsDNA antibodies cross-react with red cell surface antigens and mediate complement-independent lysis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Extravascular hemolysis (Type II AIHA) produces elevated indirect bilirubin from macrophage catabolism of phagocytosed RBCs and low haptoglobin from binding free hemoglobin released during partial phagocytosis. Spherocytes form when macrophages partially remove RBC membrane during incomplete phagocytosis. Schistocytes are absent because there is no mechanical shear force fragmenting cells. Reduced complement is characteristic of active SLE (Type III) glomerulonephritis, not the mechanism of hemolysis. Antiphospholipid antibodies cause thrombosis, not primary hemolysis. Pancytopenia with low complement suggests global immune activation, not confirmation of extravascular hemolysis mechanism. Normal complement with anti-dsDNA does not diagnose the hemolysis mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Warm AIHA lab triad = elevated indirect bilirubin + low haptoglobin + positive direct Coombs. No schistocytes = extravascular process. COMLEX: Contrast with cold agglutinin disease (IgM, complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis, complement fixation, hemoglobinuria).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Autoantibodies against RBCs, WBCs, and platelets in SLE drive Type II cytotoxic destruction via opsonization and Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis in the spleen."},{"id":"sdl42_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the antibodies and clinical manifestations in lupus patients with hypercoagulable outcomes (DVT, arterial thrombosis, recurrent spontaneous miscarriages, focal cerebral or ocular ischemia).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old female with SLE has two unexplained DVTs and three first-trimester miscarriages. Her PTT is prolonged but her PT is normal. Antiphospholipid antibodies are positive. Which best explains the paradox of in vitro anticoagulation but in vivo thrombosis?","choices":{"A":"Anticardiolipin antibodies activate factor XII of the intrinsic pathway in vivo but inhibit the same factor in vitro because phospholipid micelles in the PTT reagent sequester factor XII away from the antibody","B":"The prolonged PTT reflects true systemic anticoagulation that prevents venous thrombosis but paradoxically allows arterial thrombosis through platelet-dependent pathways independent of the coagulation cascade","C":"Antiphospholipid antibodies compete with coagulation factors for phospholipid surfaces in vitro, prolonging PTT, but in vivo they bind beta2-glycoprotein I and other plasma proteins, impairing natural anticoagulant pathways and activating endothelial cells and platelets to create a net hypercoagulable state","D":"Lupus anticoagulant antibodies form immune complexes with factor VIII in the circulation causing acquired hemophilia A, while simultaneously triggering compensatory platelet hyperaggregation in vivo","E":"IgM anticardiolipin antibodies cross-react with von Willebrand factor, prolonging PTT by displacing vWF from the phospholipid surface while stimulating endothelial vWF release and platelet adhesion in vessels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The antiphospholipid syndrome paradox arises from different phospholipid environments in vitro vs in vivo. In vitro PTT assays use exogenous phospholipid reagents; antiphospholipid antibodies compete with clotting factors for these surfaces, slowing clot formation and prolonging PTT. In vivo, these antibodies bind beta2-glycoprotein I and other proteins (protein C, protein S, annexin V, prothrombin), impairing natural anticoagulant pathways and directly activating endothelial cells (upregulating tissue factor and adhesion molecules) and platelets, producing a prothrombotic state. Factor XII is not the relevant target. True systemic anticoagulation does not selectively prevent venous but allow arterial thrombosis. Factor VIII immune complex formation is acquired hemophilia, a different disorder. vWF is not the antiphospholipid target.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antiphospholipid syndrome = thrombosis + pregnancy loss + positive lupus anticoagulant or anticardiolipin antibodies. Prolonged PTT in lupus is from lupus anticoagulant, NOT a bleeding risk. COMLEX: APS is the leading cause of hypercoagulability in young SLE women.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — Antiphospholipid antibodies including anticardiolipin, anti-beta2-GPI, and lupus anticoagulant define secondary antiphospholipid syndrome and produce a paradoxical hypercoagulable state despite prolonging PTT in vitro."},{"id":"sdl42_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the antibodies and clinical manifestations in lupus patients with hypercoagulable outcomes (DVT, arterial thrombosis, recurrent spontaneous miscarriages, focal cerebral or ocular ischemia).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which serologic finding, when detected on two or more occasions at least 12 weeks apart in an SLE patient with recurrent miscarriages, is most specific for diagnosing secondary antiphospholipid syndrome?","choices":{"A":"High-titer anti-dsDNA with simultaneously reduced C3 and C4, confirming active SLE-driven immune complex production that secondarily creates a hypercoagulable state via complement-mediated endothelial activation","B":"Lupus anticoagulant detected by clotting-based assays or medium-to-high titer IgG or IgM anticardiolipin antibodies confirmed on at least two separate occasions 12 weeks apart","C":"ANA titer of 1:640 or higher with homogeneous pattern on HEp-2 immunofluorescence in the context of recurrent first-trimester miscarriages and DVT history","D":"Anti-Smith antibody detected on two consecutive visits with concurrent thrombocytopenia below 100,000 per microliter confirming SLE-driven platelet consumption","E":"False-positive VDRL confirmed by a subsequent positive fluorescent treponemal antibody absorption test in a young female with DVT and pregnancy loss history"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Antiphospholipid syndrome is defined serologically by persistent positivity on at least two occasions at least 12 weeks apart for: lupus anticoagulant (clotting-based assay), medium-to-high titer IgG or IgM anticardiolipin antibodies, or anti-beta2-glycoprotein I antibodies. The 12-week persistence requirement excludes transient positivity from infections or medications. Anti-dsDNA with hypocomplementemia defines SLE flare activity but not APS specifically. ANA titer characterizes SLE, not APS. Anti-Sm is specific for SLE but does not diagnose APS. A positive FTA-ABS would confirm true syphilis and rule out the false-positive seen in APS patients (APS patients have positive VDRL but negative FTA-ABS).","boardPrep":"Step 1: APS criteria require: clinical event (thrombosis or pregnancy morbidity) + persistent laboratory positivity (12+ weeks apart). The three lab criteria are lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin IgG or IgM, or anti-beta2-GPI. COMLEX: Recurrent spontaneous miscarriage in a young woman should prompt APS workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — Antiphospholipid antibodies including anticardiolipin, anti-beta2-GPI, and lupus anticoagulant define secondary antiphospholipid syndrome and produce a paradoxical hypercoagulable state despite prolonging PTT in vitro."},{"id":"sdl42_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the antibodies and clinical manifestations in lupus patients with hypercoagulable outcomes (DVT, arterial thrombosis, recurrent spontaneous miscarriages, focal cerebral or ocular ischemia).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with SLE and antiphospholipid antibodies presents with focal cerebral ischemia and no cardiac source of embolism on workup. Which mechanism most directly accounts for this complication?","choices":{"A":"Antiphospholipid antibodies impair natural anticoagulant proteins including protein C, protein S, and annexin V while activating endothelial cells to upregulate tissue factor, promoting arterial thrombus formation that occludes or embolizes to cerebral vessels","B":"Anti-dsDNA antibodies cross-react with neuronal NMDA receptors and cause direct excitotoxic neuronal death in cortical neurons, producing ischemic-appearing lesions without true vascular occlusion","C":"Type III immune complex deposition in cerebral arteriolar walls activates complement-driven fibrinoid necrosis, destroying the vessel wall and producing ischemia via structural vessel disintegration","D":"Noninflammatory intimal proliferation driven by immune complex endothelial injury gradually narrows cerebral small vessel lumens over years, producing watershed zone infarctions without acute thrombosis","E":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis vegetations on the mitral or aortic valve shed sterile fibrin-platelet microemboli that travel to cerebral arterioles and produce occlusive embolic strokes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Arterial thrombosis causing focal cerebral ischemia in SLE with antiphospholipid antibodies is most directly explained by antiphospholipid syndrome: these antibodies bind beta2-GPI and interfere with the function of anticoagulant proteins (protein C, S, annexin V), while also directly activating endothelial cells to express tissue factor and adhesion molecules and activating platelets, producing arterial thromboembolism. Anti-NMDA receptor antibodies may contribute to neuropsychiatric manifestations via direct neurotoxicity rather than ischemia. Type III immune complex fibrinoid necrosis is SLE vasculitis, not the APS thrombosis mechanism. Noninflammatory intimal proliferation is a morphologic SLE finding but not the primary explanation here given the APS context. Libman-Sacks can embolize but the specific APS antibody profile points to thrombotic APS as the mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The SLE-APS triad for focal neurologic deficits: young female + antiphospholipid antibodies + arterial thrombosis without cardiac source. COMLEX: APS causes ocular ischemia (amaurosis fugax), cerebral ischemia, and DVT-PE in the same patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — Antiphospholipid antibodies including anticardiolipin, anti-beta2-GPI, and lupus anticoagulant define secondary antiphospholipid syndrome and produce a paradoxical hypercoagulable state despite prolonging PTT in vitro."},{"id":"sdl42_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations which may be found in patients with SLE.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old female meets 6 ACR criteria for SLE including nonerosive synovitis. Which feature of her joint disease most definitively distinguishes SLE arthritis from rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"The presence of anti-CCP antibodies at low titer in her serum, which confirms early seropositive rheumatoid arthritis coexisting with SLE as a recognized overlap connective tissue syndrome","B":"The absence of rheumatoid factor in her serum, which confirms that no B-cell autoimmune activation is occurring in the joint synovium and therefore excludes rheumatoid pathology","C":"The nonerosive pattern of synovitis in SLE: despite persistent inflammation and effusions, SLE arthritis characteristically does not erode articular cartilage or subchondral bone, contrasting with the destructive pannus formation seen in rheumatoid arthritis","D":"The symmetric small joint distribution involving wrists and MCPs, which is pathognomonic for SLE arthritis and does not occur in rheumatoid arthritis which characteristically spares the MCPs","E":"The presence of anti-dsDNA antibodies that cross-react with cartilage surface proteoglycans and produce inflammation without the destructive enzymatic cascade activated by RF-containing immune complexes in RA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SLE arthritis is specifically defined as nonerosive synovitis in the ACR classification criteria. Unlike RA, where synovial pannus invades and erodes cartilage and bone over time, SLE arthritis produces tenderness, swelling, and effusions without radiographic erosions. This nonerosive characteristic is clinically and radiographically distinguishable. Anti-CCP antibodies are highly specific for RA and are rarely present in SLE. Rheumatoid factor can be positive in up to 25% of SLE patients, so its absence does not definitively exclude RA overlap. Symmetric small joint involvement occurs in both RA and SLE. Anti-dsDNA antibodies are not known to cross-react with cartilage proteoglycans.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLE arthritis = nonerosive; RA = erosive pannus. On board radiographs, SLE joints look normal or show subluxation (Jaccoud arthropathy) without erosions. COMLEX: SLE joint disease causes Jaccoud arthropathy due to ligamentous laxity, not cartilage destruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — The broad clinical spectrum of SLE including hypocomplementemia during flares, cytopenias on CBC, and multi-organ manifestations reflects the systemic impact of immune complex deposition and autoantibody production."},{"id":"sdl42_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations which may be found in patients with SLE.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of laboratory findings is most characteristic of an active SLE flare with renal involvement?","choices":{"A":"Elevated ESR, elevated CRP, elevated serum C3 and C4, and high-titer RF confirming systemic inflammation with intact complement and an additional rheumatoid disease component","B":"Positive ANA with fine speckled pattern, normal complement levels, normal CBC, and trace proteinuria confirming low-grade serologically active SLE without clinical end-organ manifestations","C":"High-titer anti-Sm antibody, normal renal function, normal urinalysis, and only lymphopenia on CBC confirming serologic disease activity without current end-organ involvement","D":"High-titer anti-dsDNA antibodies, low C3 and C4, hematuria with RBC casts on urinalysis, and thrombocytopenia together confirm active lupus nephritis with immune complex-driven complement consumption and Type II platelet autoantibodies","E":"Positive lupus anticoagulant, prolonged PTT, elevated serum creatinine, and anti-centromere antibody positivity confirming antiphospholipid nephropathy rather than immune complex glomerulonephritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Active lupus nephritis produces a classic constellation: rising anti-dsDNA titer (most reliable flare activity marker), hypocomplementemia with low C3 and C4 from complement consumption, active urinary sediment with hematuria and RBC casts confirming glomerulonephritis, and thrombocytopenia from concurrent Type II autoantibody-mediated platelet destruction. CRP is characteristically low in SLE flares (unlike bacterial infection where CRP rises); elevated CRP in SLE should prompt workup for infection. Fine speckled ANA with normal complement is nonspecific. Anti-Sm is specific for SLE but does not correlate with disease activity. Anti-centromere is associated with limited systemic sclerosis, not SLE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLE flare markers = rising anti-dsDNA + falling complement (C3, C4). CRP is usually low or normal in SLE flares; high CRP suggests superimposed infection. COMLEX: Monitor SLE with serial anti-dsDNA, C3, C4, and urinalysis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — The broad clinical spectrum of SLE including hypocomplementemia during flares, cytopenias on CBC, and multi-organ manifestations reflects the systemic impact of immune complex deposition and autoantibody production."},{"id":"sdl42_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations which may be found in patients with SLE.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis in SLE is best distinguished from infective endocarditis and rheumatic heart disease by which morphologic characteristic?","choices":{"A":"Libman-Sacks vegetations are confined exclusively to the lines of closure of valve leaflets, identical in distribution to rheumatic vegetations but distinguished by their larger size and irregular surface texture","B":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis produces large friable vegetations on a single valve that shed emboli to the systemic circulation more frequently than infective endocarditis due to their loose fibrin-platelet composition","C":"Libman-Sacks vegetations are detectable only on transesophageal echocardiography and are composed of calcified fibrin that distinguishes them from the non-calcified plaques of rheumatic endocarditis","D":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis involves exclusively right-sided heart valves reflecting immune complex deposition preferentially in low-pressure chambers and is the only form of endocarditis affecting the tricuspid valve","E":"Libman-Sacks vegetations are small (1 to 3 mm) nonbacterial warty deposits that can occur on either surface of any heart valve leaflet, distinguishing them from the larger vegetations of infective endocarditis and the closure-line deposits of rheumatic disease"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis is characterized by 1 to 3 mm warty nonbacterial deposits that can appear on either surface (atrial or ventricular) of the leaflets of any heart valve. This distinguishes it from infective endocarditis (much larger, typically single-surface vegetations, with signs of systemic infection) and from rheumatic endocarditis (small beads limited to the lines of closure of valve leaflets). Libman-Sacks is not confined to closure lines. Vegetations are not consistently large and friable. They are visible on standard echocardiography and are not calcified. They can affect any valve, not only right-sided valves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Libman-Sacks = nonbacterial verrucous endocarditis = small vegetations on either leaflet surface of any valve = associated with SLE and antiphospholipid syndrome. COMLEX: Negative blood cultures + valvular vegetations in SLE patient = Libman-Sacks, not infective endocarditis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — The broad clinical spectrum of SLE including hypocomplementemia during flares, cytopenias on CBC, and multi-organ manifestations reflects the systemic impact of immune complex deposition and autoantibody production."},{"id":"sdl42_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the genetic, immunologic, and environmental contributions to the predisposition and possible causes of systemic lupus erythematosus.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman presents with 4 months of fatigue, low-grade fevers, wrist and knee swelling, and a facial rash that worsens with sun exposure. Her mother has Hashimoto thyroiditis. Labs: ANA 1:1280 homogeneous, anti-dsDNA positive, C3 low, lymphopenia 950 cells/mm3, serum IFN-alpha markedly elevated. The high IFN-alpha most likely reflects activation of which upstream pathway?","choices":{"A":"CD8+ cytotoxic T cells lysing self-reactive plasma cells and releasing granule contents that secondarily stimulate plasmacytoid DCs to produce IFN-alpha in a cytotoxicity-triggered innate response","B":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation at dermoepidermal junctions releasing vasoactive mediators that secondarily activate plasmacytoid dendritic cells to produce type I interferons","C":"TLR stimulation of plasmacytoid dendritic cells by self nucleic acids in apoptotic debris and immune complexes, driving IFN-alpha production that amplifies B-cell autoantibody responses and promotes further apoptosis in a self-sustaining cycle","D":"NK cell recognition of MHC class I-deficient autoreactive B cells in lymph nodes that releases IFN-gamma cross-activating plasmacytoid DCs to switch from IFN-gamma to IFN-alpha production","E":"Complement-mediated opsonization of nuclear debris by C1q that directs apoptotic material to plasmacytoid DCs, which then produce IFN-alpha in a strictly complement-dependent manner"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The type I IFN signature in SLE is primarily driven by plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs) bearing TLR7 and TLR9. When nuclear DNA and RNA in immune complexes or apoptotic debris engage these TLRs, pDCs produce large amounts of IFN-alpha. This IFN-alpha further activates B cells, promotes autoreactive T-cell responses, and induces apoptosis in other cells, creating a self-amplifying loop that correlates with SLE disease activity. CD8+ T cells are not the initiating trigger for pDC IFN-alpha production. IgE-mast cell degranulation is Type I allergy. NK cell IFN-gamma does not cross-activate pDCs to produce IFN-alpha. C1q deficiency promotes SLE but C1q-dependent opsonization is not the trigger for IFN-alpha production.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type I IFN signature is a biomarker of SLE activity. TLR7 recognizes single-stranded RNA; TLR9 recognizes unmethylated CpG DNA — both can be engaged by self nucleic acids in SLE. COMLEX: Belimumab targets BAFF/BLyS; anifrolumab targets IFNAR (type I IFN receptor) for refractory SLE.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl42_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the genetic, immunologic, and environmental contributions to the predisposition and possible causes of systemic lupus erythematosus.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with SLE has worsening symptoms after 6 months off medications. She works as a lifeguard and notes UV-triggered flares. Her twin sister was recently diagnosed with SLE. Genetic testing reveals HLA-DQ alleles associated with anti-Sm production and a heterozygous C4 null allele. Which statement best characterizes the role of the C4 deficiency in her SLE susceptibility?","choices":{"A":"C4 deficiency prevents activation of the classical complement pathway, resulting in unopposed alternative pathway activation that drives chronic low-grade vessel inflammation mimicking type III vasculitis","B":"C4 deficiency causes impaired opsonization of encapsulated bacteria, leading to recurrent pyogenic infections whose molecular mimics trigger anti-dsDNA production via cross-reactive epitopes with nuclear antigens","C":"C4 deficiency reduces total serum immunoglobulin production because complement-dependent B-cell co-receptor signaling through CD21-CR2 is impaired in peripheral lymph nodes during germinal center reactions","D":"C4 deficiency increases TLR7 and TLR9 surface expression on autoreactive B cells by impairing intracellular trafficking of complement-receptor signaling complexes, lowering the threshold for nuclear antigen-driven activation","E":"C4 deficiency impairs clearance of immune complexes and apoptotic debris via complement receptors CR1 and CR2, allowing nuclear autoantigens to persist and sustain autoreactive B-cell activation and anti-dsDNA production"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inherited C4 deficiency (particularly the C4A null allele) promotes SLE via two related mechanisms: impaired clearance of circulating immune complexes through complement receptors (CR1, CR2), allowing deposits to accumulate in tissues, and impaired phagocytic clearance of apoptotic cells (normally requiring C1q and downstream complement activation to opsonize apoptotic debris). The resulting accumulation of nuclear autoantigen sustains B-cell autoreactivity and anti-dsDNA production. C4 deficiency does not preferentially activate the alternative pathway. Recurrent bacterial infection from isolated C4 deficiency is mild because the alternative and lectin pathways remain intact. C4 does not regulate total immunoglobulin synthesis. TLR expression is not regulated by C4-dependent complement receptor signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Early complement deficiencies (C1q, C4, C2) predispose to SLE by impairing immune complex and apoptotic debris clearance. C4A null allele is the most common genetic complement risk factor for SLE. COMLEX: Recurrent Neisseria infections point to terminal complement deficiency (C5-C9), not C4 deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl42_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the genetic, immunologic, and environmental contributions to the predisposition and possible causes of systemic lupus erythematosus.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old male with drug-induced lupus from procainamide has anti-histone antibodies but no anti-dsDNA or anti-Sm. Symptoms resolve 6 weeks after stopping the drug. Compared to idiopathic SLE, drug-induced lupus is least likely to cause which feature, and why?","choices":{"A":"Arthritis and arthralgias are unlikely in drug-induced lupus because procainamide-modified histones selectively activate T cells with a skin-homing phenotype rather than joint-specific synovial T cells","B":"Photosensitivity is absent in drug-induced lupus because anti-histone antibodies do not undergo class switching to IgE, which is required for UV-triggered mast cell degranulation in dermal vasculature","C":"Thrombocytopenia is rare in drug-induced lupus because anti-histone antibodies cannot cross-react with platelet surface glycoproteins in the manner that anti-dsDNA antibodies opsonize platelets","D":"Severe nephritis and CNS disease are uncommon in drug-induced lupus because anti-histone immune complexes are less nephritogenic than anti-dsDNA complexes and resolve when the drug is withdrawn, eliminating the sustained antigenic drive required for progressive glomerular damage","E":"Hypocomplementemia does not occur in drug-induced lupus because histone-reactive immune complexes are too large to pass through glomerular fenestrations and therefore do not activate classical pathway complement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Drug-induced lupus (procainamide, hydralazine, D-penicillamine) is characterized by anti-histone antibodies and typically lacks the highly pathogenic anti-dsDNA antibodies of idiopathic SLE. Anti-histone complexes are less complement-fixing and less persistently produced; when the causative drug is withdrawn, antigen disappears and immune complex formation resolves, explaining the mild and self-limited course without severe nephritis or CNS disease. Arthritis is common in drug-induced lupus. Photosensitivity can occur through complement-independent mechanisms. Thrombocytopenia is not specifically prevented by anti-histone antibody specificity. Hypocomplementemia can occur in drug-induced lupus, though it is milder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Drug-induced lupus mnemonic = HIPP (Hydralazine, Isoniazid, Procainamide, Phenytoin) = anti-histone antibodies = no nephritis = resolves with drug discontinuation. COMLEX: First step in drug-induced lupus management is discontinuing the offending drug.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl42_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the type of hypersensitivity responsible for lupus renal disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with SLE has edema, hypertension, creatinine 2.1 mg/dL, urine protein 3.8 g/24 hours, RBC casts, C3 11 mg/dL (normal 88-201), C4 4 mg/dL (normal 16-47), and anti-dsDNA 1:640. Biopsy shows diffuse proliferative GN with subendothelial deposits on EM. Which IF finding is most consistent with this diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Granular deposits of IgG, IgM, IgA, C3, and C1q (full-house pattern) along the glomerular capillary loops and mesangium, consistent with immune complex deposition from the multiple autoantibody specificities characteristic of SLE nephritis class IV","B":"Linear IgG deposits along the glomerular basement membrane with no C3 or IgM, consistent with antibodies directed against the NC1 domain of type IV collagen in anti-GBM disease","C":"Negative immunofluorescence with segmental fibrinoid necrosis and crescents on light microscopy, consistent with pauci-immune glomerulonephritis from ANCA-associated vasculitis","D":"Coarse granular IgG and C3 deposits limited to the subepithelial surface of the outer GBM with no mesangial deposits, consistent with membranous nephropathy from anti-PLA2R autoantibodies","E":"Mesangial IgA deposits with C3 and rare IgG in a mesangial-predominant pattern without capillary loop deposits, consistent with IgA nephropathy from mucosal antigen deposition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lupus nephritis class III or IV shows the full-house immunofluorescence pattern: IgG, IgM, IgA, C3, and C1q all positive in the mesangium and along capillary loops in a granular pattern. This full-house positivity is essentially pathognomonic for lupus nephritis and reflects the broad autoantibody repertoire in SLE targeting multiple nuclear antigens. Linear IgG is the pattern of anti-GBM disease. Pauci-immune negative IF defines ANCA-associated GN. Subepithelial granular IgG with anti-PLA2R characterizes idiopathic membranous nephropathy. Mesangial IgA with C3 is IgA nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Full-house IF (IgG+IgM+IgA+C3+C1q) is pathognomonic for lupus nephritis. Class IV (diffuse proliferative) = most severe, most common symptomatic class, wire-loop lesions on LM, subendothelial deposits on EM. COMLEX: Lupus nephritis is the most common cause of death in young women with SLE.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl42_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the type of hypersensitivity responsible for lupus renal disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with class IV lupus nephritis treated for 8 months shows normalizing C3 and C4 and falling anti-dsDNA titer from 1:640 to 1:40. Which mechanism best explains why complement levels serve as a surrogate marker of lupus nephritis activity?","choices":{"A":"Active lupus nephritis exclusively activates the alternative complement pathway, depleting circulating C3 while leaving C4 normal because C4 is only consumed by the classical and lectin pathways","B":"The lectin pathway is activated in lupus nephritis via mannose-binding lectin binding to glomerular glycoproteins, consuming C4 specifically while leaving C3 relatively spared compared to classical pathway activation","C":"High levels of anti-dsDNA antibodies block the C3 convertase of the classical pathway by competing for C3b binding sites, reducing C3 levels via a competitive exclusion mechanism independent of actual complex deposition","D":"Antiphospholipid antibodies in SLE directly inhibit C1q from binding immune complexes in the glomerulus, consuming C4 before C3 and leaving C3 disproportionately low relative to C4 during active nephritis","E":"DNA-anti-DNA immune complexes activate the classical complement pathway via C1q binding, consuming C4 first then C3 sequentially, so hypocomplementemia during flares reflects ongoing classical pathway activation by circulating immune complexes that exceeds hepatic complement synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lupus nephritis complement consumption follows the classical pathway: DNA-anti-DNA complexes are recognized by C1q, which cleaves C4 and C2 to form C3 convertase, then cleaves C3. During active disease, immune complex formation and classical pathway activation exceed hepatic complement synthesis, producing hypocomplementemia with low C3 and C4. As treatment reduces immune complex burden, complement levels recover. This is why serial C3 and C4 levels serve as activity markers alongside anti-dsDNA titer. The alternative pathway does not explain C4 depletion. The lectin pathway is not the primary driver of lupus nephritis complement consumption. Anti-dsDNA does not block C3 convertase. Antiphospholipid antibodies do not inhibit C1q.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classical pathway sequence = C1q-C1r-C1s cleaves C4 and C2 then C3. Lupus nephritis consumes both C4 and C3 (classical). Membranoproliferative GN type II consumes C3 but not C4 (alternative pathway C3 nephritic factor). COMLEX: Rising C3 and C4 = effective immunosuppressive therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl42_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the type of hypersensitivity responsible for lupus renal disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old SLE patient's biopsy shows wire-loop thickening of glomerular capillary loops on light microscopy, full-house IF staining, and massive subendothelial immune complex deposits on EM. Which of the following best explains the wire-loop lesion?","choices":{"A":"Antiphospholipid antibodies binding endothelial cell phospholipid membranes cause endothelial swelling that compresses the capillary lumen and creates a wire-loop appearance on silver staining","B":"Fibrinoid necrosis of capillary loops driven by macrophage-mediated Type IV granulomatous inflammation deposits collagen along the GBM, producing the thickened wire-loop appearance on PAS staining","C":"Massive subendothelial immune complex deposits between the endothelial cell and the GBM expand the capillary wall outward, producing a thickened rigid loop visible on light microscopy as the wire-loop lesion characteristic of Class IV lupus nephritis","D":"Mesangial cell hyperplasia and matrix expansion extending into capillary loops creates a double-contour pattern on silver staining that is commonly misidentified as the wire-loop lesion in clinical practice","E":"Complement membrane attack complex (C5b-9) insertion into the GBM causes focal splitting of the lamina densa and creates a wire-loop appearance on periodic acid-Schiff staining in severe Class IV disease"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The wire-loop lesion is the classic light microscopy finding in Class IV (diffuse proliferative) lupus nephritis. It results from massive subendothelial immune complex deposits composed of DNA-anti-DNA complexes and complement that accumulate between the endothelial cell and the glomerular basement membrane. These large deposits push the capillary wall outward, creating the characteristic thick rigid loop appearance on PAS or silver staining. It is not produced by antiphospholipid antibodies on endothelial surfaces, granulomatous fibrinoid necrosis, mesangial expansion (which causes double-contour in MPGN), or membrane attack complex insertion into the GBM.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Wire-loop lesion = Class IV lupus nephritis = massive subendothelial deposits = EM shows electron-dense subendothelial deposits. Contrast with Class V (membranous lupus) which shows subepithelial deposits and spike-and-dome pattern. COMLEX: Class IV has worst prognosis and requires aggressive treatment with mycophenolate mofetil or cyclophosphamide.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl42_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the type of hypersensitivity responsible for blood cell destruction in SLE (RBC, WBC, Platelets).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old female with SLE has hemoglobin 7.8 g/dL, elevated reticulocyte count of 4.2%, positive direct Coombs for IgG, indirect bilirubin 3.1 mg/dL, and undetectable haptoglobin. Her peripheral smear shows spherocytes but no schistocytes. Which best explains the absence of schistocytes?","choices":{"A":"The SLE autoantibodies target red cell ABO surface antigens rather than structural membrane proteins, causing osmotic lysis in the plasma without mechanical fragmentation or passage through splenic cords","B":"The hemolysis is predominantly extravascular: antibody-coated RBCs are phagocytosed by splenic macrophages during partial engulfment that remodels the cell into a spherocyte without fragmenting it, as opposed to microangiopathic processes that physically shear cells in narrowed or fibrin-lined vessels","C":"The positive direct Coombs reflects cold agglutinins of the IgM class that agglutinate RBCs at peripheral temperatures but dissociate at body temperature without causing membrane fragmentation","D":"Complement activation via membrane attack complex inserts into RBC membranes and lyses them osmotically in the systemic circulation, which produces spherocytic ghosts rather than fragmented schistocyte forms","E":"The elevated reticulocyte count reflects vigorous compensatory erythropoiesis that maintains RBC deformability, preventing fragmentation because reticulocytes are more flexible than mature RBCs when traversing the microvasculature"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Warm AIHA produces extravascular hemolysis: IgG autoantibodies coat RBCs, which are then partially engulfed by splenic macrophages via Fc receptors. During partial phagocytosis, pieces of the RBC membrane are removed, reducing surface area relative to volume and converting the biconcave disc to a spherocyte. Since this process occurs in the spleen rather than in vessels with mechanical shear forces (fibrin strands in TTP or HUS, prosthetic valve turbulence), there is no fragmentation and no schistocytes. ABO antigens are not the primary targets of warm AIHA. Cold agglutinins are IgM, not IgG, and produce a different Coombs pattern. MAC-driven osmotic lysis occurs in cold AIHA, not warm IgG AIHA. Reticulocyte flexibility does not prevent fragmentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schistocytes = microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (TTP, HUS, DIC, valve hemolysis). Spherocytes without schistocytes = immune (warm AIHA) or hereditary spherocytosis. Distinguish by Coombs test. COMLEX: In SLE, look for both warm AIHA (Type II) and thrombocytopenia (Type II) simultaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl42_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the type of hypersensitivity responsible for blood cell destruction in SLE (RBC, WBC, Platelets).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old female with new SLE has a platelet count of 48,000 per microliter, normal PT and PTT, and bone marrow biopsy showing megakaryocyte hyperplasia. Anti-platelet IgG is detected. High-dose corticosteroids improve her count to 112,000 within 3 weeks. Which mechanism best explains the corticosteroid response?","choices":{"A":"Corticosteroids directly stimulate thrombopoietin synthesis in the liver, increasing platelet production by megakaryocytes in the bone marrow to compensate for ongoing peripheral immune-mediated destruction","B":"Corticosteroids block TLR7 and TLR9 signaling on B cells, suppressing the nuclear antigen-driven co-stimulatory pathway that specifically produces anti-platelet glycoprotein autoantibodies in SLE","C":"Corticosteroids activate splenic regulatory T cells to produce IL-10, which directly inhibits autoreactive B-cell clones producing antiplatelet antibodies via STAT3-dependent suppression","D":"Corticosteroids downregulate Fc receptor expression on splenic macrophages, decreasing phagocytic destruction of antibody-coated platelets, while broadly suppressing B-cell autoantibody production to reduce the ongoing opsonization of platelets","E":"Corticosteroids stabilize platelet phospholipid membranes by intercalating into the bilayer, preventing complement-dependent MAC formation and osmotic platelet lysis by antiplatelet antibodies"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Corticosteroids improve immune thrombocytopenia via two complementary mechanisms: reducing Fc receptor expression on splenic macrophages (decreasing recognition and phagocytosis of antibody-coated platelets) and broadly suppressing B-cell activity and autoantibody production over time. Megakaryocyte hyperplasia confirms that platelet production is intact and peripheral destruction is the mechanism, making the splenic Fc receptor effect the most immediate therapeutic target. Corticosteroids do not stimulate thrombopoietin. TLR blockade is not the primary steroid mechanism. Treg-IL-10 activation may contribute secondarily but is not the primary splenic mechanism explaining rapid response. Corticosteroids do not intercalate into platelet membranes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Corticosteroids work in ITP and SLE thrombocytopenia by reducing splenic Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis. For refractory cases: IVIG (blocks Fc receptors acutely), splenectomy, rituximab, or thrombopoietin receptor agonists. COMLEX: Megakaryocyte hyperplasia on bone marrow biopsy = increased production trying to compensate for peripheral destruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl42_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the type of hypersensitivity responsible for blood cell destruction in SLE (RBC, WBC, Platelets).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old female with SLE has WBC 2,800 per microliter with 750 lymphocytes per microliter. Anti-lymphocyte antibodies are detected. Her physician explains this is the same hypersensitivity mechanism as her prior autoimmune hemolytic anemia. Which mechanism best accounts for the lymphopenia?","choices":{"A":"Type III immune complex deposition in bone marrow sinusoids suppresses hematopoietic stem cell proliferation, reducing lymphocyte output and producing lymphopenia without peripheral destruction of mature lymphocytes","B":"Type IV delayed hypersensitivity: sensitized CD8+ T cells recognize autologous lymphocytes expressing aberrant MHC class II presentation of self peptides and destroy them via perforin-granzyme cytotoxic pathways","C":"Type II antibody-mediated cytotoxicity: anti-lymphocyte IgG autoantibodies opsonize circulating lymphocytes for Fc receptor-mediated phagocytic destruction by mononuclear phagocytes in the spleen and liver, the same mechanism as antibody-opsonized RBC destruction in autoimmune hemolytic anemia","D":"Type I hypersensitivity: IgE anti-lymphocyte antibodies bind Fc epsilon receptors on lymphocytes and trigger complement-dependent osmotic lysis of the antibody-bound cells in peripheral blood","E":"Direct retroviral cytopathic effect by an endogenous retroviral element activated by SLE immune dysregulation that preferentially infects and kills T cells in the peripheral blood without antibody involvement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anti-lymphocyte antibodies in SLE are IgG autoantibodies that opsonize circulating T and B lymphocytes for Fc receptor-mediated phagocytic destruction in the spleen and liver — the exact same Type II antibody-mediated mechanism as autoimmune hemolytic anemia, applied to a different cellular target. The ACR criteria include leukopenia (less than 4,000 per mm3) and lymphopenia (less than 1,500 per mm3) as hematologic manifestations of SLE. Type III immune complex bone marrow suppression does not explain peripheral lymphocyte destruction. Type IV CD8+ cytotoxicity requires antigen-specific recognition, not the nonspecific anti-lymphocyte antibody mechanism. IgE is not the isotype of SLE autoantibodies. Endogenous retroviral elements are proposed SLE triggers but are not the mechanism of ongoing lymphopenia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLE hematologic criteria include anemia (warm AIHA), thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, and lymphopenia, all via Type II antibody-mediated mechanisms. COMLEX: SLE patients are immunocompromised from both the disease itself (lymphopenia, complement depletion) and from immunosuppressive treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl42_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the antibodies and clinical manifestations in lupus patients with hypercoagulable outcomes (DVT, arterial thrombosis, recurrent spontaneous miscarriages, focal cerebral or ocular ischemia).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old female with SLE has three prior miscarriages, two DVTs, and amaurosis fugax. PTT is 68 seconds, PT is normal. Anticardiolipin IgG is positive on two occasions 14 weeks apart. Her rheumatologist orders anti-beta2-GPI testing. Why is anti-beta2-GPI the most pathogenically relevant antibody target in this syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Beta2-GPI is a lipid transfer protein that delivers phospholipids to platelet membranes; its inhibition by autoantibodies directly destabilizes platelet lipid bilayers and causes thrombocytopenia as the primary APS manifestation","B":"Beta2-GPI is a cofactor for thrombomodulin on endothelial cells; anti-beta2-GPI antibodies block thrombomodulin function, preventing thrombin-mediated protein C activation and creating acquired activated protein C resistance","C":"Beta2-GPI is the dominant complement-fixing antigen in SLE; anti-beta2-GPI immune complexes deposit in vessel walls and activate the classical complement pathway, mimicking Type III vasculitis to cause arterial stenosis","D":"Beta2-GPI binds phospholipid membranes and normally promotes anticoagulation; anti-beta2-GPI antibodies interfere with this anticoagulant function, inhibit annexin V, and directly activate endothelial cells and platelets through Fc and apoER2 receptors to create a prothrombotic state","E":"Beta2-GPI is expressed on trophoblast surfaces during early pregnancy; anti-beta2-GPI directly activates the trophoblast intrinsic apoptosis pathway via caspase-9, causing placental insufficiency entirely independent of uteroplacental thrombosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Beta2-glycoprotein I (apolipoprotein H) is a plasma protein that binds phospholipid membranes and has anticoagulant properties, including displacing prothrombin from phospholipid surfaces and supporting annexin V anticoagulant shield formation. Anti-beta2-GPI antibodies interfere with these anticoagulant functions and also directly activate endothelial cells (via TLR4 and apoER2 receptors) to upregulate tissue factor and adhesion molecules, and activate platelets, producing a net prothrombotic state. This explains the PTT prolongation in vitro (antibodies compete with clotting factors for phospholipid surfaces) combined with thrombosis in vivo. Beta2-GPI is not a lipid transfer protein. It does not function through thrombomodulin. It is not the primary complement-fixing antigen. Trophoblast apoptosis may occur but uteroplacental thrombosis is the primary mechanism of pregnancy loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Beta2-GPI = the true antigenic target in APS. Anticardiolipin antibodies bind cardiolipin-beta2-GPI complexes. Anti-beta2-GPI is the most specific test for true APS. COMLEX: APS = most common acquired cause of hypercoagulability; treat with anticoagulation (warfarin for thrombotic APS, heparin during pregnancy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — Autoantibodies in Rheumatic Disease Associated with Specific Conditions"},{"id":"sdl42_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the antibodies and clinical manifestations in lupus patients with hypercoagulable outcomes (DVT, arterial thrombosis, recurrent spontaneous miscarriages, focal cerebral or ocular ischemia).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old female with SLE and secondary APS is 10 weeks pregnant with three prior first-trimester losses. She is started on low-molecular-weight heparin. Which best explains why heparin is preferred over warfarin during early pregnancy?","choices":{"A":"Heparin enhances anti-beta2-GPI antibody clearance through the reticuloendothelial system, reducing antiphospholipid antibody titers more effectively than warfarin during the critical first-trimester implantation period","B":"Warfarin crosses the placenta and is teratogenic in the first trimester, causing fetal warfarin syndrome with nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses, and intrauterine growth restriction, while heparin does not cross the placenta due to its large molecular size and is safe for the fetus","C":"Warfarin is ineffective in antiphospholipid syndrome because it inhibits protein C and protein S, which are already functionally blocked by antiphospholipid antibodies, making the combined anticoagulant effect paradoxically procoagulant","D":"Heparin has direct anti-complement activity at the C3 level that neutralizes complement-mediated trophoblast destruction independent of its anticoagulant mechanism, providing protection that warfarin cannot replicate","E":"Heparin activates tissue plasminogen activator at the uteroplacental interface, dissolving fibrin microthrombi in spiral arteries before they progress to complete vessel occlusion and placental infarction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Heparin is the anticoagulant of choice during pregnancy, especially the first trimester, because it is a large, negatively charged molecule that does not cross the placental barrier and is therefore not teratogenic. Warfarin crosses the placenta freely and during weeks 6-12 can inhibit vitamin K-dependent gamma-carboxylation of proteins involved in bone and cartilage development, causing fetal warfarin syndrome: nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses (chondrodysplasia punctata), and intrauterine growth restriction. After the first trimester, warfarin can be used with monitoring. Heparin does not reduce antiphospholipid antibody titers. Warfarin inhibiting protein C and S is a transient concern at initiation but is not the reason for preferring heparin in pregnancy. Anti-complement effects of heparin are not the primary clinical rationale. tPA activation is not the mechanism of heparin action.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LMWH is safe in pregnancy (does not cross placenta); warfarin is teratogenic in first trimester (weeks 6-12). For APS in pregnancy: LMWH + low-dose aspirin throughout pregnancy. COMLEX: Recurrent pregnancy loss workup includes antiphospholipid antibody panel as first-line serologic testing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — Autoantibodies in Rheumatic Disease Associated with Specific Conditions"},{"id":"sdl42_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the antibodies and clinical manifestations in lupus patients with hypercoagulable outcomes (DVT, arterial thrombosis, recurrent spontaneous miscarriages, focal cerebral or ocular ischemia).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old female with SLE has a positive lupus anticoagulant screen and a reactive VDRL. A confirmatory FTA-ABS test returns negative. She has no history of thrombosis or STIs. Which best explains the false-positive VDRL in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Antiphospholipid antibodies in SLE cross-react with cardiolipin in the VDRL test antigen, producing a false-positive nontreponemal result, while the FTA-ABS is negative because SLE antibodies do not bind intact Treponema pallidum antigens used in the confirmatory test","B":"The lupus anticoagulant cross-reacts with recombinant Treponema pallidum proteins used in both the VDRL and FTA-ABS assays, making both tests reactive in all patients with confirmed antiphospholipid syndrome","C":"The VDRL detects IgG against treponemal lipoprotein; anti-beta2-GPI antibodies bind the lipoprotein carrier in the VDRL antigen, producing a false positive that is confirmed as specific by the FTA-ABS cross-reactivity","D":"Anti-dsDNA antibodies in SLE cross-react with VDRL cardiolipin because both antigens share a negatively charged phosphate backbone, producing false-positive VDRL but negative FTA-ABS confirming no actual syphilis","E":"The prolonged PTT from lupus anticoagulant physically interferes with the flocculation reaction in the VDRL assay, producing a false-positive result that normalizes when serum is diluted to correct for the PTT abnormality"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The VDRL and RPR use a cardiolipin-cholesterol-lecithin antigen to detect reagin antibodies as a screen for syphilis. In SLE and APS, antiphospholipid antibodies (particularly anticardiolipin antibodies) cross-react with the cardiolipin in the VDRL antigen, producing a false-positive nontreponemal test. The FTA-ABS uses actual Treponema pallidum organisms as antigen; SLE and APS antibodies do not bind these treponemal antigens, so the FTA-ABS is negative, confirming the VDRL positivity is false (syphilis is excluded). A negative FTA-ABS with positive VDRL is the classic APS serologic profile. Lupus anticoagulant does not cross-react with treponemal proteins in FTA-ABS. Anti-beta2-GPI binds phospholipid complexes, not treponemal lipoproteins. Anti-dsDNA cross-reactivity with VDRL via phosphate backbone is not the mechanism. PTT does not interfere with flocculation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: APS/SLE false-positive VDRL: positive nontreponemal (VDRL/RPR) + negative treponemal (FTA-ABS/TPPA) = false positive due to anticardiolipin antibodies. True syphilis: both positive. COMLEX: False-positive VDRL is an ACR classification criterion for SLE.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — Autoantibodies in Rheumatic Disease Associated with Specific Conditions"},{"id":"sdl42_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations which may be found in patients with SLE.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old African American female presents with fatigue, alopecia, oral ulcers, malar rash, bilateral pleuritis, confusion, WBC 2,400, hemoglobin 9.2 g/dL, platelets 88,000, proteinuria, hematuria with casts, and ANA 1:2560. She meets 11 ACR criteria. Which best characterizes why SLE presents so heterogeneously across patients?","choices":{"A":"SLE exclusively involves organs that express double-stranded DNA on their cell surfaces, and the breadth of involvement reflects the ubiquity of dsDNA as a plasma membrane antigen across all body systems","B":"SLE follows a predictable sequential pattern of organ involvement proceeding from skin to joints to kidney to CNS, with new systems affected only after prior systems have responded to initial treatment","C":"SLE causes multisystem involvement primarily because anti-Sm antibodies bind RNA splicing machinery in all actively transcribing cells, globally inhibiting protein synthesis in tissues with the highest mRNA turnover","D":"SLE organ involvement is limited to structures expressing Ro/SS-A and La/SS-B antigens, which explains why the same organs affected in Sjogren syndrome are affected in SLE with autoantibody overlap","E":"SLE causes multisystem disease because immune complex deposition in small vessels, autoantibody binding to diverse cellular targets, and complement activation can affect virtually any organ, with individual presentations varying based on which autoantibodies are present, the patient genetic background, and environmental exposures"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SLE is the prototypic multisystem autoimmune disease because its pathogenic mechanisms — immune complex deposition in small vessels (Type III), autoantibody binding to diverse cell surface and intracellular antigens (Type II), and complement activation — can affect virtually any organ or tissue. The specific organs involved in a given patient depend on their autoantibody repertoire (anti-dsDNA correlates with nephritis, antiphospholipid antibodies with thrombosis, anti-Ro with neonatal lupus), genetic background (HLA alleles, complement gene polymorphisms), and environmental exposures (UV light). This inherent mechanistic and genetic heterogeneity makes SLE both the great imitator and highly variable in presentation. dsDNA is not expressed on cell surfaces. Organ involvement does not follow a fixed sequence. Anti-Sm does not inhibit global protein synthesis. Ro/SS-A and La/SS-B positivity is not required for organ involvement in SLE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLE = great imitator; must be in the differential for any young woman with multisystem disease. 95-100% ANA positive (sensitive but not specific); anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm are specific for SLE. COMLEX: SLE is more prevalent and severe in African American, Hispanic, and Asian women than in European ancestry women.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Commonly Used Laboratory Tests to Aid Evaluation of Rheumatic Disease"},{"id":"sdl42_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations which may be found in patients with SLE.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old SLE patient on hydroxychloroquine has her anti-dsDNA titer rise from 1:40 to 1:640 and C3 fall from 97 to 52 mg/dL over 3 months, but remains asymptomatic with normal urinalysis. Two months later she returns with proteinuria, edema, and hematuria. Which trend best predicted the renal flare?","choices":{"A":"The rise in total ANA titer from 1:320 to 1:640 directly reflects the degree of complement activation and accurately predicts the magnitude of glomerular immune complex deposition during flares","B":"The reduction in serum C3 alone is the most sensitive single serologic predictor of SLE nephritis because complement depletion always precedes glomerular injury by exactly 8 to 12 weeks in Type III hypersensitivity","C":"Rising anti-dsDNA titer combined with falling C3 and C4 together reflect increasing immune complex formation and classical pathway activation and are validated as serologic predictors of impending lupus nephritis flare before clinical symptoms develop","D":"The normalization of hemoglobin and platelet count over the same period indicates that Type II autoantibody activity is decreasing, signaling a shift to predominant Type III renal disease activity in SLE","E":"The rise in ESR with simultaneously normal CRP over the same 3-month period is characteristic of SLE flare and specifically predicts renal involvement when ESR exceeds 60 mm per hour"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The combination of rising anti-dsDNA titer and falling complement levels (both C3 and C4) is the validated serologic predictor of impending lupus nephritis flare. Rising anti-dsDNA reflects increasing autoantibody production; falling complement reflects consumption of the classical pathway by DNA-anti-DNA immune complexes. Together they indicate increasing immune complex burden before overt clinical renal disease develops, providing a window for treatment intensification. ANA titer does not directly reflect complement activation or flare severity. C3 alone is less specific than the combination with rising anti-dsDNA. Changes in hemoglobin and platelets (Type II manifestations) do not specifically predict Type III renal flare. Elevated ESR with normal CRP is a general SLE pattern but does not specifically predict nephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Monitor SLE activity with serial anti-dsDNA + C3 + C4 + urinalysis. Rising anti-dsDNA + falling complement = treat before overt nephritis. CRP is useful for distinguishing SLE flare (low CRP) from superimposed infection (high CRP). COMLEX: Hydroxychloroquine reduces flare frequency and decreases risk of progression to nephritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Commonly Used Laboratory Tests to Aid Evaluation of Rheumatic Disease"},{"id":"sdl42_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations which may be found in patients with SLE.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 33-year-old female with SLE has echocardiographic vegetations on both the atrial and ventricular surfaces of the mitral valve with negative blood cultures after 5 days. Libman-Sacks endocarditis is diagnosed. Compared to infective endocarditis, Libman-Sacks carries which specific risk related to SLE pathogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Libman-Sacks vegetations are more highly embologenic than infective endocarditis vegetations because they contain more lipid-rich foam cells and no bacterial matrix to hold the vegetation cohesively to the valve surface","B":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis invariably progresses to severe valvular stenosis requiring surgical replacement within 5 years because immune complex deposits in the vegetation undergo progressive dystrophic calcification and leaflet fusion","C":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis causes accelerated coronary atherosclerosis specifically by shedding immune complex microemboli into coronary ostia that initiate endothelial injury and lipid deposition in the coronary artery intima","D":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis is associated with secondary antiphospholipid syndrome, so its presence in an SLE patient marks a coexisting hypercoagulable risk from antiphospholipid antibodies that can cause systemic thromboembolism including stroke and retinal ischemia","E":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis causes persistent sterile febrile illness indistinguishable from infective endocarditis, and the prolonged antibiotic courses given before the correct diagnosis suppress normal flora and worsen SLE immune dysregulation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis is nonbacterial verrucous endocarditis strongly associated with antiphospholipid syndrome in SLE. The vegetations can embolize to the brain, retina, kidney, and extremities, and their presence in an SLE patient should prompt antiphospholipid antibody testing, as they represent a clinical manifestation of secondary APS. The association with APS is the SLE-specific risk that distinguishes Libman-Sacks from other nonbacterial endocarditis. Libman-Sacks vegetations are not proven more embologenic than infective endocarditis. Most cases do not progress to severe valvular stenosis requiring surgery. Accelerated coronary atherosclerosis in SLE is multifactorial (corticosteroids, immune complex endothelial damage, traditional risk factors) and is not primarily caused by Libman-Sacks microemboli. Antibiotics are not given for Libman-Sacks endocarditis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Libman-Sacks = SLE + APS association. Small vegetations on either valve surface + negative cultures + SLE history. COMLEX: Libman-Sacks vegetations are sterile and do not require antibiotics; treat the underlying APS and SLE. Echocardiogram surveillance is recommended in APS patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Commonly Used Laboratory Tests to Aid Evaluation of Rheumatic Disease"}]},{"sdlNumber":43,"title":"Inflammatory Myopathies","questions":[{"id":"sdl43_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the types of inflammatory myopathy with or without skin involvement.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly pairs an inflammatory myopathy with its distinguishing skin involvement and characteristic muscle biopsy pattern?","choices":{"A":"Polymyositis — heliotrope rash and Gottron papules over the knuckles with perifascicular atrophy on biopsy caused by CD8+ T cells invading the perimysium and complement-fixing the capillary bed","B":"Dermatomyositis — pathognomonic heliotrope rash and Gottron papules with perifascicular atrophy on biopsy, driven by CD4+ T cell perimysial inflammation and complement-mediated vasculopathy of intramuscular capillaries","C":"Inclusion body myositis — mechanic's hands and calcinosis cutis with rimmed vacuoles and amyloid deposits on biopsy in elderly patients with symmetric proximal leg and distal arm weakness","D":"Antisynthetase syndrome — heliotrope rash with anti-Jo-1 antibodies driving perimysial endomysial CD8+ T cell infiltrates and perifascicular atrophy in patients with concomitant interstitial lung disease","E":"Immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy — Gottron papules with prominent myofiber necrosis and anti-SRP antibodies specifically in patients with prior statin exposure and normal skin on non-knuckle surfaces"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dermatomyositis (DM) is the only inflammatory myopathy with pathognomonic skin findings: the heliotrope rash (lilac discoloration of the upper eyelids) and Gottron papules (erythematous papules over the extensor surfaces of MCP and IP joints). The muscle biopsy shows perifascicular atrophy resulting from CD4+ T cell-driven perimysial inflammation and complement (C5b-9)-mediated capillary dropout, which preferentially damages myofibers at the edge of fascicles. Polymyositis has no skin involvement and shows CD8+ endomysial infiltrates. Inclusion body myositis has no skin findings and shows rimmed vacuoles. Antisynthetase syndrome features mechanic's hands, not heliotrope rash, and has a different biopsy pattern. IMNM has no skin involvement and shows pauci-inflammatory necrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dermatomyositis = heliotrope rash + Gottron papules + perifascicular atrophy. The only inflammatory myopathy with skin changes. COMLEX: Both heliotrope rash and Gottron papules are pathognomonic for DM — know both on sight.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Dermatomyositis (CD4+ perimysial vasculopathy, perifascicular atrophy, skin involvement), polymyositis/antisynthetase (CD8+ endomysial T cells, no skin), and inclusion body myositis (CD8+ + rimmed vacuoles, asymmetric distal-proximal) represent distinct T-cell-mediated inflammatory myopathies."},{"id":"sdl43_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the types of inflammatory myopathy with or without skin involvement.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old female presents with 4 months of symmetric proximal weakness, no skin changes, elevated CK, and muscle biopsy showing CD8+ T cells invading endomysial connective tissue with diffuse necrotic and regenerating myofibers scattered throughout fascicles. Which inflammatory myopathy best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Dermatomyositis presenting without skin manifestations, in which the CD8+ T cell endomysial pattern represents a variant with normal IFN signaling and absent perifascicular atrophy due to preserved capillary density","B":"Inclusion body myositis, characterized by asymmetric distal-predominant weakness with rimmed vacuoles, CD8+ T cell invasion of individual myofibers, and amyloid deposits in patients over 50 years","C":"Polymyositis (now often reclassified as antisynthetase syndrome or overlap myositis), characterized by CD8+ endomysial T cell infiltrates, diffuse scattered myofiber necrosis and regeneration throughout fascicles, and no skin manifestations","D":"Immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, characterized by a pauci-inflammatory biopsy with predominantly necrotic and regenerating fibers without significant T cell infiltrate and positive anti-HMGCR or anti-SRP antibodies","E":"Juvenile dermatomyositis, characterized by symmetric proximal weakness with calcinosis cutis, GI vasculitis, and perifascicular atrophy in a pediatric patient under age 18 with anti-NXP-2 antibodies"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Polymyositis (and its modern reclassifications including antisynthetase syndrome) presents with symmetric proximal weakness without skin involvement. The biopsy shows CD8+ cytotoxic T cells invading the endomysium (the connective tissue surrounding individual myofibers) with diffuse myofiber necrosis and regeneration scattered throughout fascicles — unlike dermatomyositis which preferentially damages the perifascicular region. The absence of skin findings and the CD8+ endomysial pattern distinguish it from DM. Inclusion body myositis causes asymmetric weakness and shows rimmed vacuoles. IMNM has minimal inflammatory infiltrate on biopsy. Juvenile DM presents with skin findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Polymyositis = CD8+ endomysial infiltrates + diffuse random necrosis + no skin. DM = CD4+ perimysial + perifascicular atrophy + skin. IBM = CD8+ + rimmed vacuoles + asymmetric distal predominant. COMLEX: Most patients formerly called polymyositis are now reclassified into antisynthetase syndrome, IMNM, or IBM.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Dermatomyositis (CD4+ perimysial vasculopathy, perifascicular atrophy, skin involvement), polymyositis/antisynthetase (CD8+ endomysial T cells, no skin), and inclusion body myositis (CD8+ + rimmed vacuoles, asymmetric distal-proximal) represent distinct T-cell-mediated inflammatory myopathies."},{"id":"sdl43_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the types of inflammatory myopathy with or without skin involvement.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which features best distinguish inclusion body myositis from dermatomyositis and polymyositis in terms of patient demographics, pattern of weakness, and biopsy findings?","choices":{"A":"Inclusion body myositis typically affects patients over 50 (most common inflammatory myopathy over age 65), causes asymmetric weakness preferentially in quadriceps and finger flexors, and shows rimmed vacuoles with amyloid deposits and CD8+ T cell invasion on biopsy, in contrast to the symmetric proximal-only weakness of dermatomyositis and polymyositis","B":"Inclusion body myositis affects children and young adults, causes symmetric proximal weakness with rapid progression over weeks, and shows perifascicular atrophy with CD4+ T cell perimysial infiltrates and C5b-9 capillary deposits","C":"Inclusion body myositis is the only inflammatory myopathy that responds consistently to corticosteroids, with dramatic improvement in CK and muscle strength within 6 weeks of treatment, distinguishing it from steroid-resistant dermatomyositis","D":"Inclusion body myositis causes symmetric proximal limb weakness indistinguishable from polymyositis but is distinguished solely by calcinosis cutis and GI vasculitis in older patients and the absence of any positive autoimmune antibodies","E":"Inclusion body myositis only affects bulbar muscles producing dysphagia and dysarthria in isolation, without any limb muscle weakness, distinguishing it clearly from the limb-predominant weakness of dermatomyositis and polymyositis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sporadic inclusion body myositis (sIBM) is the most common inflammatory myopathy in patients over 65 years and is unique in its pattern: asymmetric weakness predominantly in the quadriceps (causing falls) and distal upper extremity muscles, especially the finger flexors (causing difficulty with grip and pinch). Dysphagia is also common. Muscle biopsy shows CD8+ T cells invading individual myofibers, rimmed vacuoles (containing lysosomal aggregates and neurodegenerative proteins like beta-amyloid and TDP-43), and amyloid deposits. Importantly, sIBM typically responds poorly to corticosteroids and immunosuppressive drugs, unlike dermatomyositis and polymyositis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBM = age >50 + asymmetric quadriceps + finger flexors weak + rimmed vacuoles + steroid-resistant. DM and PM = symmetric proximal + steroid-responsive. COMLEX: IBM is the most common inflammatory myopathy in patients over 65 and is the one that does NOT respond to steroids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Dermatomyositis (CD4+ perimysial vasculopathy, perifascicular atrophy, skin involvement), polymyositis/antisynthetase (CD8+ endomysial T cells, no skin), and inclusion body myositis (CD8+ + rimmed vacuoles, asymmetric distal-proximal) represent distinct T-cell-mediated inflammatory myopathies."},{"id":"sdl43_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the multi-system involvement causing significant morbidity which can occur in dermatomyositis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of extra-muscular manifestations defines the multi-system morbidity of dermatomyositis and mandates specific surveillance in all adult patients at diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Polyarthritis, uveitis, and hepatosplenomegaly driven by granulomatous perimysial inflammation, occurring in over 50% of dermatomyositis patients regardless of antibody subtype and mandating liver biopsy at diagnosis","B":"Glomerulonephritis, CNS vasculitis, and Libman-Sacks endocarditis from immune complex deposition in dermatomyositis, occurring in a pattern indistinguishable from secondary antiphospholipid syndrome in SLE","C":"Peripheral neuropathy, mononeuritis multiplex, and autonomic dysfunction from anti-Jo-1-directed complement-mediated Schwann cell destruction in dermatomyositis patients with antisynthetase overlap","D":"Interstitial lung disease, myocarditis, dysphagia from esophageal muscle involvement, and a 20-30% risk of occult malignancy requiring cancer screening (mammography, colonoscopy, CT chest, gynecologic evaluation) in all adult dermatomyositis patients","E":"Renal amyloidosis, splenomegaly, and adrenal insufficiency from chronic IL-6 and SAA-driven secondary amyloidosis in patients with longstanding dermatomyositis who have not received adequate immunosuppression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dermatomyositis has important multi-system manifestations. The 20-30% risk of occult malignancy (breast, lung, GI, ovarian, pancreatic, bladder) mandates comprehensive cancer screening in all adult patients at diagnosis. ILD can occur in any DM subtype and is particularly aggressive with anti-MDA5 antibodies. Myocarditis causes arrhythmias and cardiomyopathy. Dysphagia results from pharyngeal and esophageal muscle involvement in approximately one-third of patients. Granulomatous inflammation is not a feature of DM. Lupus-type renal and CNS manifestations are not characteristic of DM. Peripheral neuropathy and amyloidosis are not primary multi-system features of DM.","boardPrep":"Step 1: All adult DM patients need cancer screening — especially breast, ovarian, lung, GI. Anti-TIF1-gamma = highest cancer risk over age 38. COMLEX: Dermatomyositis is a paraneoplastic syndrome in 20-30% of cases; skin and muscle improve when the cancer is treated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — Type I IFN signaling and CD4+ T-cell cytokine release in dermatomyositis drive multi-system morbidity including ILD, myocarditis, dysphagia, and a 20-30% malignancy risk requiring cancer surveillance in all adult patients."},{"id":"sdl43_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the multi-system involvement causing significant morbidity which can occur in dermatomyositis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which antibody-clinical phenotype pairing in dermatomyositis is CORRECT?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Mi-2 antibodies are associated with rapidly progressive ILD and minimal muscle disease, with the poorest prognosis of all dermatomyositis subtypes regardless of age at diagnosis or geographic ancestry","B":"Anti-TIF1-gamma antibodies are associated with prominent skin involvement, dysphagia, and a significant association with occult malignancy in dermatomyositis patients over 38 years of age","C":"Anti-NXP-2 antibodies are associated with the highest malignancy risk among all DM antibody subtypes and produce the most severe myositis with prominent high CK and perifascicular necrosis on biopsy","D":"Anti-MDA5 antibodies are associated with high CK, severe myositis, periungual telangiectasias, and a uniformly favorable prognosis with first-line corticosteroid treatment in all patient populations","E":"Anti-SRP antibodies are associated with dermatomyositis-specific skin changes including calcinosis cutis, GI vasculitis, and childhood-onset disease with excellent treatment response to hydroxychloroquine alone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anti-TIF1-gamma (transcriptional intermediary factor 1 gamma) antibodies define a dermatomyositis subtype with prominent skin involvement, dysphagia, and importantly a significant association with underlying malignancy in patients over 38 years — the highest cancer-associated DM subtype. Anti-Mi-2 is associated with severe myositis and high CK, but not primarily ILD or minimal muscle disease. Anti-NXP-2 is associated with calcinosis cutis and muscle involvement, with moderate cancer risk. Anti-MDA5 is associated with clinically amyopathic DM, rapidly progressive ILD, and poor prognosis — not favorable outcomes. Anti-SRP is an IMNM antibody, not a DM-specific antibody.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DM antibody pearls — anti-TIF1-gamma = cancer risk (age >38); anti-MDA5 = ILD, little muscle; anti-Mi-2 = classic DM, high CK; anti-NXP-2 = calcinosis in juvenile DM. COMLEX: Anti-SRP and anti-HMGCR are IMNM antibodies, not DM-specific.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — Type I IFN signaling and CD4+ T-cell cytokine release in dermatomyositis drive multi-system morbidity including ILD, myocarditis, dysphagia, and a 20-30% malignancy risk requiring cancer surveillance in all adult patients."},{"id":"sdl43_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the multi-system involvement causing significant morbidity which can occur in dermatomyositis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old male with dermatomyositis develops abdominal pain, bloody stools, and bowel wall thickening on imaging. Which mechanism best explains this gastrointestinal complication unique to juvenile dermatomyositis?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Jo-1-mediated smooth muscle autoimmunity causing progressive intestinal hypomotility, bacterial overgrowth, and malabsorption in pediatric dermatomyositis patients with the antisynthetase overlap phenotype","B":"Anti-NXP-2-mediated calcinosis cutis extending into the bowel wall submucosa, causing mechanical obstruction of the intestinal lumen and mucosal erosion with secondary infection in juvenile dermatomyositis","C":"Immune complex deposition in mesenteric arterioles triggering Type III hypersensitivity vasculitis of the bowel wall in juvenile dermatomyositis, identical to the renal vasculitis pattern seen in childhood-onset lupus nephritis","D":"CD8+ T cell invasion of gastrointestinal mucosal enterocytes producing an IBD-like pattern of transmural inflammation in response to anti-Mi-2 antibody cross-reactivity with intestinal nucleosome proteins","E":"Vasculitis of the gastrointestinal blood supply driven by CD4+ T cell-mediated and complement-mediated vascular injury, causing ischemic symptoms ranging from abdominal pain and GI bleeding to bowel perforation in juvenile dermatomyositis patients"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Juvenile dermatomyositis (JDM) is the only form of inflammatory myopathy that characteristically involves GI vasculitis. The same vascular mechanism driving perifascicular muscle atrophy (CD4+ T cells and complement-mediated capillary damage) extends to the GI vasculature, causing ischemic injury to the bowel wall. This can manifest as abdominal pain, GI bleeding, or, in severe cases, bowel perforation. This is a distinguishing feature of JDM compared to adult DM. Anti-Jo-1 smooth muscle targeting is not the mechanism. Calcinosis does not obstruct the bowel lumen. Type III immune complex GI vasculitis characterizes HSP (IgA vasculitis), not DM. CD8+ enterocyte invasion is IBM and polymyositis pattern, not DM.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Juvenile DM features beyond adult DM: calcinosis cutis (especially anti-NXP-2), GI vasculitis with bowel perforation risk. COMLEX: All DM patients should have esophageal evaluation for dysphagia; juvenile DM additionally needs GI vasculitis monitoring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — Type I IFN signaling and CD4+ T-cell cytokine release in dermatomyositis drive multi-system morbidity including ILD, myocarditis, dysphagia, and a 20-30% malignancy risk requiring cancer surveillance in all adult patients."},{"id":"sdl43_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the medication most often associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy as well as the antibody-driven mechanism of damage.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which medication class is most commonly associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, and what best distinguishes IMNM from direct drug myotoxicity?","choices":{"A":"Statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors) are most associated with IMNM; IMNM is distinguished by persistence or worsening of weakness after drug discontinuation, positive anti-HMGCR or anti-SRP antibodies, biopsy showing prominent necrosis with minimal T cell infiltrate, and requirement for immunosuppressive therapy rather than supportive care alone","B":"Fibrates are most associated with IMNM; fibrate-induced IMNM is distinguished by elevated lipoprotein lipase activity, systemic hypertriglyceridemia, and massive rhabdomyolysis that resolves completely within days of drug discontinuation without immunosuppressive treatment","C":"Hydroxychloroquine is most associated with IMNM, driving immune-mediated muscle damage by activating plasmacytoid dendritic cells via TLR7 and TLR9 to produce IFN-alpha that directly attacks myofibers expressing HMG-CoA reductase","D":"Corticosteroids are most associated with IMNM; high-dose corticosteroids paradoxically trigger anti-HMGCR antibody production via TSH receptor-mediated HMG-CoA suppression in skeletal muscle","E":"Antiretrovirals such as zidovudine are most associated with IMNM via mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma inhibition, distinguished from direct toxicity by the presence of anti-SRP antibodies and concurrent ILD requiring additional immunosuppression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Statins are the medication class most associated with IMNM. Direct statin myotoxicity resolves with drug discontinuation, while IMNM is an antibody-driven disease that persists or worsens after stopping the statin, requires immunosuppressive therapy, and shows positive anti-HMGCR or anti-SRP antibodies. Biopsy in IMNM shows prominent necrotic and regenerating myofibers with a paucity of inflammatory infiltrate — distinct from the robust CD8+ infiltrate in polymyositis. Note: anti-HMGCR IMNM also occurs in statin-naive patients, indicating that the antibody can arise independently of statin exposure. Fibrates, hydroxychloroquine, steroids, and zidovudine are not the primary statin-associated IMNM class.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Statins cause both direct myotoxicity (resolves with discontinuation, no antibody) and IMNM (anti-HMGCR antibody, persists after stopping, requires immunosuppression). Key distinction on boards. COMLEX: Risk factors for direct statin toxicity: age >65, renal failure, hepatic failure, hypothyroidism, high-dose statin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 3 — Statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors) are the medication class most associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, driven by anti-HMGCR or anti-SRP antibodies causing myofiber necrosis that persists after drug discontinuation and requires immunosuppressive therapy."},{"id":"sdl43_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the medication most often associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy as well as the antibody-driven mechanism of damage.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In IMNM associated with anti-HMGCR antibodies and prior statin use, which of the following best describes the antibody-driven mechanism of myofiber damage?","choices":{"A":"Anti-HMGCR antibodies form circulating immune complexes that deposit in the endomysium, activate the classical complement pathway, and trigger MAC-mediated osmotic lysis of myofibers in a Type III pattern analogous to lupus nephritis","B":"Anti-HMGCR antibodies bind HMG-CoA reductase expressed on the surface of myofibers (upregulated by statin exposure), activate complement and recruit macrophages and NK cells to mediate necrosis with minimal T cell involvement, producing prominent necrotic and regenerating myofibers with few inflammatory infiltrates on biopsy","C":"Anti-HMGCR antibodies block intracellular cholesterol synthesis in myofibers, causing sarcolemmal membrane instability from cholesterol depletion that leads to spontaneous myofiber rupture and the characteristic rimmed vacuoles on modified Gomori trichrome stain","D":"Anti-HMGCR antibodies cross-react with cardiac troponin I, causing both skeletal myopathy and severe myocarditis that specifically distinguishes anti-HMGCR IMNM from anti-SRP IMNM which has exclusively skeletal muscle involvement","E":"Anti-HMGCR antibodies activate Toll-like receptors on satellite cells, permanently suppressing myofiber regeneration and causing progressive proximal weakness despite statin discontinuation and high-dose corticosteroid therapy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In IMNM, statins upregulate HMG-CoA reductase (HMGCR) expression on the surface of myofibers as a compensatory response to enzymatic blockade. Anti-HMGCR autoantibodies then bind these surface-expressed HMGCR molecules and mediate myofiber destruction through complement activation and recruitment of NK cells and macrophages — primarily a Type II antibody-mediated process rather than T cell-mediated Type IV. This explains the characteristic biopsy finding of prominent necrotic and regenerating myofibers with a paucity of T cell infiltrate (pauci-inflammatory necrosis), which contrasts sharply with polymyositis and dermatomyositis. Anti-HMGCR does not form deposited immune complexes like lupus nephritis. Cholesterol depletion does not cause rimmed vacuoles. Cross-reactivity with troponin I is not the established mechanism. Satellite cell TLR activation is not the pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IMNM biopsy = prominent necrosis + minimal inflammation. Statin increases HMGCR on myofiber surface → anti-HMGCR binds → complement + macrophage mediated necrosis. Contrast with DM (CD4+ perimysial) and PM (CD8+ endomysial). COMLEX: Anti-HMGCR and anti-SRP are the two IMNM antibodies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 3 — Statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors) are the medication class most associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, driven by anti-HMGCR or anti-SRP antibodies causing myofiber necrosis that persists after drug discontinuation and requires immunosuppressive therapy."},{"id":"sdl43_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the medication most often associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy as well as the antibody-driven mechanism of damage.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"How does anti-SRP antibody-associated IMNM clinically differ from anti-HMGCR antibody-associated IMNM?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SRP patients more often have pathognomonic skin changes including Gottron papules and heliotrope rash, while anti-HMGCR patients have no skin involvement and are definitively associated with prior statin exposure in 100% of cases","B":"Anti-SRP patients have exclusively childhood-onset IMNM with calcinosis cutis and GI vasculitis, while anti-HMGCR patients have adult-onset IMNM occurring only in statin-naive patients without any relevant drug history","C":"Anti-SRP patients typically have more severe disease with more frequent dysphagia and cardiac involvement (arrhythmias, cardiomyopathy), while anti-HMGCR patients may or may not have prior statin exposure and generally follow a less severe clinical course with a higher rate of treatment response","D":"Anti-SRP patients achieve complete remission with statin discontinuation alone without immunosuppressive therapy, while anti-HMGCR patients require indefinite high-dose corticosteroids plus cyclophosphamide for disease control","E":"Anti-SRP patients present with lower CK levels and a more indolent clinical course than anti-HMGCR patients, who characteristically present with acute rhabdomyolysis and oliguric renal failure requiring emergent plasmapheresis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anti-SRP (signal recognition particle) IMNM is generally more severe than anti-HMGCR IMNM. Anti-SRP patients more frequently develop dysphagia from pharyngeal muscle involvement and cardiac involvement including arrhythmias and myocarditis, contributing to greater overall morbidity. Anti-SRP IMNM also tends to present with higher CK levels and more rapid weakness progression. Anti-HMGCR IMNM can occur with or without prior statin exposure and generally has a milder course with better treatment response. Neither subtype has dermatomyositis-specific skin findings. Anti-SRP is not exclusive to children. Statin discontinuation alone is insufficient for either subtype (both require immunosuppression). Anti-SRP patients do not have lower CK levels than anti-HMGCR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-SRP = more severe, dysphagia, cardiac involvement. Anti-HMGCR = statin-associated (but not always), less severe. Both require immunosuppression. COMLEX: Both IMNM subtypes show pauci-inflammatory necrosis on biopsy — the low T cell infiltrate distinguishes IMNM from polymyositis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 3 — Statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors) are the medication class most associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, driven by anti-HMGCR or anti-SRP antibodies causing myofiber necrosis that persists after drug discontinuation and requires immunosuppressive therapy."},{"id":"sdl43_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the myopathies associated with thyroid dysfunction.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 45-year-old female with proximal weakness, muscle cramping, slowed reflexes, weight gain, and cold intolerance has CK of 1,800 U/L and TSH of 45 mIU/L. Which muscle biopsy finding is most consistent with hypothyroid myopathy?","choices":{"A":"Myofiber atrophy, increased subsarcolemmal nuclei abnormally positioned internally, glycogen aggregates, and occasional mucopolysaccharide deposition in connective tissue, reflecting metabolic changes from thyroid hormone deficiency affecting muscle energy metabolism","B":"Perifascicular atrophy with CD4+ T cell perimysial infiltrates and C5b-9 complement deposition in capillaries, consistent with an autoimmune mechanism triggered by hypothyroidism-induced upregulation of complement proteins in skeletal muscle","C":"Rimmed vacuoles with CD8+ T cell endomysial invasion and amyloid deposits on Congo red stain, indicating that hypothyroidism accelerates the protein aggregation pathways of sporadic inclusion body myositis in genetically susceptible older patients","D":"Prominent pauci-inflammatory myofiber necrosis with positive anti-HMGCR antibodies, indicating that hypothyroidism promotes IMNM by upregulating HMG-CoA reductase expression on myofiber surfaces in the absence of statin exposure","E":"Nemaline rods on modified Gomori trichrome staining representing alpha-actinin Z-band disruption from hypothyroid-mediated sarcomeric reorganization of type I oxidative fibers in chronically hypothyroid skeletal muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypothyroid myopathy results from thyroid hormone deficiency impairing muscle metabolism. The histologic changes include myofiber atrophy, abnormally positioned (internal) nuclei, glycogen aggregates from impaired glycogenolysis, and occasional mucopolysaccharide (glycosaminoglycan) deposition in connective tissue. These metabolic changes are reversible with thyroid hormone replacement. There is no autoimmune inflammatory infiltrate (distinguishing it from DM, PM), no rimmed vacuoles (distinguishing it from IBM), no pauci-inflammatory anti-HMGCR necrosis (distinguishing it from IMNM), and no nemaline rods (a congenital myopathy finding).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothyroid myopathy = elevated CK + slowed deep tendon reflexes + cramping + proximal weakness. Reverts with levothyroxine. Hypothyroidism also increases statin myotoxicity risk. COMLEX: Check TSH in any patient with unexplained proximal weakness and elevated CK before pursuing inflammatory myopathy workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — Thyroid dysfunction causes myopathy detectable on CK and EMG: hypothyroid myopathy produces elevated CK, slowed reflexes, glycogen aggregates, and cramping; thyrotoxic myopathy produces proximal weakness with normal or low CK and brisk reflexes in the setting of hypermetabolism."},{"id":"sdl43_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the myopathies associated with thyroid dysfunction.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"How does thyrotoxic myopathy differ from hypothyroid myopathy in clinical presentation, CK levels, and reflex examination?","choices":{"A":"Thyrotoxic myopathy presents as asymmetric distal limb weakness with elevated CK and hyperreflexia, while hypothyroid myopathy causes symmetric proximal weakness with normal CK and mildly slowed but otherwise normal deep tendon reflexes","B":"Thyrotoxic myopathy presents as a rapidly progressive bulbar palsy with markedly elevated CK and absent deep tendon reflexes, while hypothyroid myopathy causes predominantly respiratory muscle weakness with normal CK and preserved reflexes throughout the course","C":"Thyrotoxic myopathy presents as episodic paralysis triggered by carbohydrate intake with dramatically elevated CK between episodes, while hypothyroid myopathy causes persistent proximal weakness with normal CK and hyperreflexia from thyroid hormone-mediated sodium channel upregulation","D":"Thyrotoxic myopathy presents as an acute inflammatory myositis with elevated anti-TIF1-gamma antibodies and positive ANA at high titer, while hypothyroid myopathy presents with anti-HMGCR antibodies and pauci-inflammatory necrosis on biopsy","E":"Thyrotoxic myopathy presents as proximal muscle weakness with normal or low CK and hyperreflexia (brisk reflexes) in the context of hyperthyroidism signs, while hypothyroid myopathy causes proximal weakness with markedly elevated CK, slowed movement and reflexes, and muscle cramping"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Thyrotoxic myopathy most commonly presents as subacute or chronic proximal muscle weakness in the setting of hyperthyroidism (tachycardia, heat intolerance, weight loss, exophthalmos). CK is characteristically normal or low because the hypermetabolic state accelerates muscle protein breakdown without causing necrosis as the primary mechanism. Reflexes are brisk. Ophthalmoplegia (exophthalmic ophthalmoplegia) with eyelid swelling and diplopia may also occur. Hypothyroid myopathy presents with elevated CK (sometimes markedly), slowed movement, slow relaxation of deep tendon reflexes (the classic \"hung-up\" reflex), and cramping from reduced myosin ATPase activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thyrotoxic = proximal weakness + normal/low CK + hyperreflexia + hyperthyroid signs. Hypothyroid = proximal weakness + elevated CK + slowed \"hung-up\" reflexes + cramping. Both improve with treating the underlying thyroid disorder. COMLEX: Hung-up reflexes = hypothyroidism hallmark; brisk reflexes in muscle weakness = consider hyperthyroidism or upper motor neuron disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — Thyroid dysfunction causes myopathy detectable on CK and EMG: hypothyroid myopathy produces elevated CK, slowed reflexes, glycogen aggregates, and cramping; thyrotoxic myopathy produces proximal weakness with normal or low CK and brisk reflexes in the setting of hypermetabolism."},{"id":"sdl43_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the myopathies associated with thyroid dysfunction.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which endocrine condition is listed as a specific risk factor for direct statin myotoxicity (as opposed to statin-induced IMNM), and what is the pharmacologic basis for this increased risk?","choices":{"A":"Hyperthyroidism, because elevated thyroid hormone increases CYP3A4 enzyme activity that metabolizes lipophilic statins more rapidly, producing toxic hydroxylated statin intermediates that accumulate preferentially in skeletal muscle cells","B":"Hypothyroidism, because thyroid hormone deficiency reduces hepatic clearance of statins by impairing CYP450-mediated statin metabolism, elevating plasma statin levels, while simultaneously causing independent metabolic muscle vulnerability that synergizes with statin-induced mitochondrial toxicity","C":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus, because hyperglycemia upregulates HMG-CoA reductase expression on myofiber surfaces via AGE-receptor signaling, creating metabolic vulnerability to statin-induced mitochondrial coenzyme Q10 depletion","D":"Primary hyperparathyroidism, because elevated PTH directly disrupts myofiber membrane calcium homeostasis and increases susceptibility to statin-mediated mitochondrial complex I inhibition via calpain-dependent proteolysis","E":"Adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease), because cortisol deficiency impairs satellite cell recovery from statin-mediated mitochondrial damage by blocking glucocorticoid receptor-mediated upregulation of mitochondrial biogenesis genes in regenerating myofibers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hypothyroidism is a documented risk factor for direct statin myotoxicity for two reasons. First, thyroid hormone is required for normal hepatic CYP450 enzyme activity; its deficiency reduces statin metabolism and clearance, elevating circulating statin plasma levels and increasing exposure of muscle to toxic concentrations. Second, hypothyroidism independently causes metabolic myopathy through impaired ATP synthesis and myosin ATPase activity, making muscle cells more vulnerable to additional insults like statin-induced mitochondrial dysfunction. The combination produces synergistic risk for rhabdomyolysis. Hyperthyroidism increases, not decreases, CYP enzyme activity. Type 2 diabetes, hyperparathyroidism, and adrenal insufficiency are not established statin myotoxicity risk factors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Statin myotoxicity risk factors = high statin dose + age >65 + renal failure + hepatic failure + HYPOTHYROIDISM. Check TSH before attributing weakness to statin alone. COMLEX: A hypothyroid patient started on a statin who develops weakness should have the statin held and thyroid function optimized.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — Thyroid dysfunction causes myopathy detectable on CK and EMG: hypothyroid myopathy produces elevated CK, slowed reflexes, glycogen aggregates, and cramping; thyrotoxic myopathy produces proximal weakness with normal or low CK and brisk reflexes in the setting of hypermetabolism."},{"id":"sdl43_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the types of inflammatory myopathy with or without skin involvement.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 64-year-old white male presents with 18 months of slowly progressive weakness — he falls frequently from his knees giving out and struggles to button his shirt. Examination reveals asymmetric quadriceps and finger flexor weakness with relatively preserved shoulder strength. CK is 450 U/L. Biopsy shows CD8+ T cells invading myofibers, rimmed vacuoles on modified Gomori trichrome, and amyloid deposits on Congo red staining. Which diagnosis best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Dermatomyositis presenting as amyopathic dermatomyositis without skin manifestations, in which rimmed vacuoles represent IFN-induced lysosomal failure in perifascicular myofibers that have lost capillary perfusion from complement-mediated dropout","B":"Polymyositis presenting atypically with asymmetric weakness and an unusual biopsy pattern from concurrent statin-induced direct toxicity superimposed on the classic CD8+ endomysial T cell infiltrate causing focal rimmed vacuoles","C":"Antisynthetase syndrome presenting with isolated limb involvement, where rimmed vacuoles represent RNA granule aggregates resulting from anti-aminoacyl tRNA synthetase immune complex deposition in the myoplasm","D":"Sporadic inclusion body myositis: age over 50, asymmetric weakness with quadriceps (proximal leg) and finger flexor (distal arm) predominance, slow progression, modestly elevated CK, and biopsy showing rimmed vacuoles with amyloid deposits and CD8+ T cell invasion","E":"Immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy in a statin-naive older patient, where rimmed vacuoles represent lysosomal accumulation of undegraded HMGCR protein rather than classical neurodegenerative protein aggregates"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is the classic sporadic IBM presentation: white male over 50 (most common inflammatory myopathy over 65), asymmetric weakness targeting quadriceps (causing knee buckling and falls) and finger flexors (causing grip weakness and difficulty buttoning), slow progression, relatively mild CK elevation (reflecting smoldering disease), and the pathognomonic biopsy triad of rimmed vacuoles containing lysosomal aggregates and neurodegenerative proteins (beta-amyloid, TDP-43, ubiquitin), amyloid deposits detectable by Congo red stain, and CD8+ T cell invasion of individual myofibers. IBM responds poorly to corticosteroids — a clinical clue. Dermatomyositis has skin findings and perifascicular atrophy. Polymyositis shows symmetric proximal weakness. Antisynthetase has ILD and mechanic's hands. IMNM shows pauci-inflammatory necrosis without rimmed vacuoles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBM = the inflammatory myopathy that mimics ALS in distal hand weakness, does not respond to steroids, and has rimmed vacuoles. Amyloid deposits in IBM distinguish it from PM and DM. COMLEX: Dysphagia in IBM is common and can cause aspiration pneumonia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl43_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the types of inflammatory myopathy with or without skin involvement.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old female presents with proximal weakness, mechanic's hands (hyperkeratotic fissuring on the radial aspects of fingers), arthritis, and progressive dyspnea. HRCT shows bilateral ground-glass opacities. CK is 1,800 U/L. Anti-Jo-1 antibodies are positive. Biopsy shows perifascicular atrophy and perifascicular necrosis. Which disease does this constellation represent, and what extra-muscular feature most determines prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Antisynthetase syndrome (Jo-1 syndrome): the triad of myositis with proximal weakness, interstitial lung disease, and arthritis along with mechanic's hands is defining, and ILD is the extra-muscular complication that most significantly determines prognosis in anti-Jo-1-positive patients","B":"Dermatomyositis with anti-MDA5 antibodies presenting as clinically amyopathic dermatomyositis, where ILD is less rapidly progressive than classic anti-MDA5 DM and mechanic's hands represent a DM-specific skin manifestation distinct from Gottron papules","C":"Polymyositis with antisynthetase overlap, in which the ILD reflects aspiration pneumonitis from esophageal muscle weakness rather than primary autoimmune lung injury driven by anti-Jo-1 antibody-mediated tRNA synthetase damage in pneumocytes","D":"Systemic sclerosis with overlap myositis, in which anti-Jo-1 antibodies cross-react with pulmonary fibroblast tRNA synthetase, driving progressive ILD independently of the myositis component through direct fibroblast activation","E":"Sjogren syndrome with overlap inflammatory myopathy and anti-Ro antibody-mediated lung disease, where mechanic's hands represent a secondary sicca-related fissuring keratoderma from decreased sweat gland secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Antisynthetase syndrome is defined by antibodies against aminoacyl tRNA synthetases, most commonly anti-Jo-1 (anti-histidyl tRNA synthetase). The full syndrome includes myositis, interstitial lung disease, arthritis, mechanic's hands (hyperkeratotic fissuring on radial fingers and ulnar thumb), Raynaud phenomenon, and fever. ILD is the major determinant of morbidity and mortality in antisynthetase syndrome. Biopsy may show perifascicular atrophy similar to DM or random necrosis. Anti-MDA5 DM presents without mechanic's hands as a defining feature. Aspiration pneumonitis is not the mechanism of ILD in antisynthetase syndrome. Systemic sclerosis has a distinct antibody profile (anti-Scl-70, anti-centromere). Sjogren lacks mechanic's hands.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antisynthetase syndrome = anti-Jo-1 + myositis + ILD + arthritis + mechanic's hands. ILD determines prognosis. COMLEX: Anti-Jo-1 is the most common antisynthetase antibody; others include anti-PL-7, anti-PL-12, anti-EJ.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl43_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the types of inflammatory myopathy with or without skin involvement.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old female has proximal weakness, heliotrope rash, Gottron papules, and calcium deposits over her elbows and buttocks. Biopsy shows perifascicular atrophy and C5b-9 in capillaries. Compared to adult dermatomyositis, which features specifically characterize juvenile dermatomyositis?","choices":{"A":"Juvenile DM has a lower risk of malignancy than adult DM but a significantly higher risk of ILD, making pulmonary function testing and lung surveillance the priority over cancer screening in all pediatric dermatomyositis patients","B":"Juvenile DM is enriched for anti-NXP-2 antibodies and more commonly develops calcinosis cutis (dystrophic calcium deposits in superficial soft tissues), and can develop GI vasculitis causing abdominal pain, bleeding, or perforation — features less characteristic of adult DM","C":"Juvenile DM always responds completely to corticosteroids as monotherapy without needing steroid-sparing agents, making its long-term prognosis uniformly favorable compared to the more treatment-refractory adult dermatomyositis subtype","D":"Juvenile DM produces perimysial rather than perifascicular atrophy on biopsy and lacks C5b-9 capillary deposition, histopathologically distinguishing it from adult DM in which complement deposition is the defining mechanistic feature","E":"Juvenile DM primarily affects boys over age 10 with anti-TIF1-gamma antibodies and carries the same malignancy risk as adult DM over 38, requiring identical cancer screening from age 10 onward regardless of sex or antibody status"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Juvenile DM differs from adult DM in several important ways. First, malignancy is uncommon in juvenile DM (unlike adult DM where 20-30% have occult cancer). Second, juvenile DM is enriched for anti-NXP-2 antibodies, which are associated with calcinosis cutis — dystrophic calcium deposits in superficial soft tissues including skin, subcutaneous tissue, and muscle fasciae. Third, juvenile DM can cause GI vasculitis (the same complement-mediated capillary injury that damages muscle and skin extends to GI vasculature), ranging from abdominal pain and bleeding to bowel perforation — a potentially life-threatening complication rarely seen in adult DM. The biopsy findings (perifascicular atrophy, C5b-9 deposition) are similar to adult DM.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Juvenile DM hallmarks = calcinosis cutis + GI vasculitis (perforation risk) + anti-NXP-2 enrichment + low malignancy risk. COMLEX: All DM features occur on a spectrum — JDM adds the calcinosis and vasculitis dimensions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl43_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the multi-system involvement causing significant morbidity which can occur in dermatomyositis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old female with dermatomyositis (anti-TIF1-gamma positive) has proximal weakness, Gottron papules, heliotrope rash, dysphagia, and unintentional 6-kg weight loss over 4 months. What is the most critical next step in her management given her antibody profile?","choices":{"A":"High-resolution CT chest to evaluate for interstitial lung disease, which is the most common and most dangerous extra-muscular complication specifically associated with anti-TIF1-gamma antibodies in all adult DM patients over 38 years","B":"Electromyography and nerve conduction studies to evaluate for concurrent peripheral neuropathy, which occurs in over 40% of anti-TIF1-gamma dermatomyositis patients via complement-mediated Schwann cell injury","C":"Echocardiography to evaluate for Libman-Sacks endocarditis, which develops in up to 20% of anti-TIF1-gamma dermatomyositis patients who simultaneously fulfill serologic criteria for secondary antiphospholipid syndrome","D":"Brain MRI with gadolinium to evaluate for CNS vasculitis, which specifically occurs in anti-TIF1-gamma dermatomyositis through complement-mediated cerebral small vessel injury causing infarcts in watershed zones","E":"Age-appropriate cancer screening including mammography, colonoscopy, CT chest/abdomen/pelvis, and gynecologic evaluation, because anti-TIF1-gamma antibodies carry a significant association with occult malignancy in dermatomyositis patients over 38 years"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Anti-TIF1-gamma antibodies are the DM-specific antibody most strongly associated with malignancy. In patients over 38 years with anti-TIF1-gamma DM, comprehensive cancer screening is the most critical management step: mammography, colonoscopy, CT chest/abdomen/pelvis, CA-125 for ovarian malignancy, and gynecologic evaluation. The unintentional weight loss in this patient further heightens malignancy concern. ILD occurs in DM but is more characteristically associated with anti-MDA5 and anti-Jo-1, not anti-TIF1-gamma. Peripheral neuropathy at 40% is not supported by evidence. Libman-Sacks endocarditis is an SLE/APS finding. CNS vasculitis from anti-TIF1-gamma is not established.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-TIF1-gamma = cancer red flag in DM, especially over 38 years. Screen for breast, lung, GI, ovarian, pancreatic, bladder cancers. Treating the malignancy can improve DM. COMLEX: DM is a paraneoplastic syndrome in ~25% of adult cases; malignancy may precede, coincide with, or follow DM diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl43_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the multi-system involvement causing significant morbidity which can occur in dermatomyositis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old female with dermatomyositis develops progressive exertional dyspnea and a dry cough 6 months after her myopathy diagnosis. HRCT shows bilateral basilar ground-glass opacities. PFTs show a restrictive pattern with reduced DLCO. Anti-MDA5 antibodies are detected. Which statement best characterizes the ILD in this clinical context?","choices":{"A":"The ILD in anti-MDA5 DM is caused solely by aspiration pneumonitis from pharyngeal and esophageal muscle weakness, making swallowing rehabilitation and head-of-bed elevation the primary interventions rather than immunosuppression","B":"The ILD in anti-MDA5 DM is driven by anti-Jo-1 antibodies that invariably coexist with anti-MDA5 in the antisynthetase overlap, producing the same clinical and radiographic ILD phenotype as anti-Jo-1-positive antisynthetase syndrome","C":"The ILD in anti-MDA5 DM is reversible with corticosteroid monotherapy within 4 to 6 weeks because MDA5 is an intracellular target not accessible to circulating antibodies once membrane integrity is restored by immunosuppression","D":"ILD associated with anti-MDA5 antibodies can follow a rapidly progressive course with poor prognosis in a subset of patients, distinct from the relatively mild or absent muscle involvement (clinically amyopathic DM) that anti-MDA5 patients often display","E":"The ILD in anti-MDA5 DM develops exclusively in patients who smoke and is produced by IFN-alpha-mediated fibroblast activation independent of the T cell-mediated vasculopathy that causes perifascicular atrophy in DM muscle"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anti-MDA5 (melanoma differentiation-associated protein 5) is an IFN-induced cytosolic viral RNA sensor. Anti-MDA5 DM is characterized by clinically amyopathic or mild muscle disease combined with a high risk of rapidly progressive ILD (RP-ILD), which can be life-threatening. Some anti-MDA5 patients have a more indolent ILD course with skin manifestations and joint involvement, while others develop fulminant RP-ILD with poor prognosis even with aggressive immunosuppression. This paradox — minimal myositis but severe pulmonary involvement — is a clinical pearl. The ILD is not from aspiration. Anti-Jo-1 and anti-MDA5 are distinct antibodies defining different DM subtypes. Corticosteroid monotherapy is often insufficient for anti-MDA5 ILD. Smoking is not required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-MDA5 = amyopathic DM + rapidly progressive ILD = worst prognosis subset of DM. Remember the inverse: least muscle disease = most lung disease risk. COMLEX: Anti-MDA5 DM patients need pulmonologist co-management from diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl43_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the multi-system involvement causing significant morbidity which can occur in dermatomyositis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old male with dermatomyositis develops exertional dyspnea and resting tachycardia. ECG shows PR prolongation and nonspecific ST changes. Echo reveals LVEF of 40% with global hypokinesis. Troponin-I is elevated. Which mechanism best explains the cardiac complication?","choices":{"A":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis from antiphospholipid antibody-mediated fibrin-platelet valve vegetation formation that subsequently embolizes to the coronary arteries causing myocardial infarction and diffuse global hypokinesis in DM patients","B":"Pericarditis from immune complex deposition in the pericardium identical to SLE serositis, in which anti-dsDNA antibodies that coexist with DM cross-react with double-stranded DNA in pericardial mesothelial cells","C":"Myocarditis from CD4+ T cell and IFN-driven inflammatory injury to cardiac myocytes, analogous to the perimysial vasculopathy and IFN signaling that damage skeletal muscle in dermatomyositis, producing arrhythmias and cardiomyopathy","D":"Coronary artery aneurysm formation from complement-mediated vasculitis of the epicardial coronary vessels, identical in mechanism to the coronary artery dilation seen in pediatric Kawasaki disease inflammatory vasculitis","E":"Pulmonary arterial hypertension from anti-MDA5-driven pulmonary endothelial Raynaud-like vasospasm producing right heart strain that reduces LVEF through interventricular septal flattening and paradoxical motion on echo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Myocarditis is a recognized extra-muscular manifestation of dermatomyositis, occurring through the same pathogenic mechanisms that damage skeletal muscle: CD4+ T cell-mediated and IFN-driven inflammatory injury to myocytes, causing global myocardial dysfunction. This produces cardiomyopathy (reduced LVEF, global hypokinesis), arrhythmias (conduction block, PR prolongation), and elevated troponin. The clinical picture is that of inflammatory cardiomyopathy, not coronary artery disease. Libman-Sacks endocarditis and anti-dsDNA are SLE findings. Coronary aneurysms are Kawasaki disease. PAH with right heart strain would cause RV dilation and right-axis deviation, not global LV hypokinesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DM multi-system = ILD + myocarditis + malignancy + dysphagia. Myocarditis in DM produces the same inflammatory cardiomyopathy phenotype as viral myocarditis. COMLEX: Screen DM patients with ECG and echo at baseline; elevated troponin in DM may reflect myocarditis, not coronary disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl43_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the medication most often associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy as well as the antibody-driven mechanism of damage.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old male on atorvastatin 80 mg for 3 years develops progressive proximal weakness over 4 months. He stops the statin but weakness continues to worsen over the next 2 months. CK is 8,400 U/L. Biopsy shows abundant necrotic and regenerating myofibers with a paucity of inflammatory infiltrate. Anti-HMGCR antibodies are positive. Which finding best distinguishes this as IMNM rather than direct statin myotoxicity?","choices":{"A":"The CK of 8,400 U/L distinguishes IMNM from direct toxicity because direct statin myotoxicity invariably produces CK below 1,000 U/L and values above this threshold are pathognomonic for immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy in all statin users","B":"The patient age of 58 years distinguishes IMNM from direct statin toxicity because direct statin myotoxicity only occurs in patients under 50 years, and all statin-associated weakness in patients over 50 is immune-mediated by definition","C":"The biopsy showing predominantly necrotic fibers without regenerating fibers distinguishes IMNM, because direct statin toxicity produces regenerating fibers as the dominant biopsy finding and IMNM completely suppresses satellite cell activation","D":"The high-dose atorvastatin 80 mg regimen distinguishes IMNM from direct toxicity because immune-mediated IMNM only occurs with high-intensity statins while direct myotoxicity is exclusive to low-dose statin regimens by pharmacologic definition","E":"Persistence and progression of weakness after statin discontinuation combined with positive anti-HMGCR antibodies and biopsy showing necrotizing myopathy with minimal T cell infiltrate, requiring immunosuppressive rather than just supportive treatment to achieve remission"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The defining distinguishing feature of IMNM versus direct statin myotoxicity is the behavior after drug discontinuation: direct toxicity improves within weeks of stopping the statin, while IMNM persists or worsens because the antibody-mediated process is now self-perpetuating regardless of continued drug exposure. Anti-HMGCR positivity confirms immune-mediated pathology requiring immunosuppression (corticosteroids plus steroid-sparing agents). The pauci-inflammatory biopsy (necrosis without T cells) is the histologic signature of IMNM. CK cutoffs do not absolutely distinguish the two. Age is not a defining criterion. Both IMNM and direct toxicity produce necrosis and regeneration. Dose is not the sole determinant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key IMNM vs direct statin toxicity: IMNM continues or worsens after stopping statin; toxicity resolves. Anti-HMGCR confirms IMNM. Pauci-inflammatory biopsy = IMNM. COMLEX: Never restart a statin in confirmed anti-HMGCR IMNM without rheumatology guidance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — Most Common Adverse Effects by Class of Treatment"},{"id":"sdl43_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the medication most often associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy as well as the antibody-driven mechanism of damage.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old statin-naive woman develops subacute progressive proximal weakness with CK of 12,000 U/L. Biopsy shows randomly distributed necrotic and regenerating myofibers with minimal inflammatory infiltrate. Anti-SRP antibodies are detected. She later develops dysphagia and a new cardiac arrhythmia. Which best explains why anti-SRP IMNM is considered more clinically severe than anti-HMGCR IMNM?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SRP antibodies directly inhibit the signal recognition particle function in cardiomyocytes, preventing co-translational membrane insertion of sarcomeric proteins and causing a unique dilated cardiomyopathy that is not seen with anti-HMGCR antibodies under any circumstances","B":"Anti-SRP antibodies bind HMG-CoA reductase expressed on muscle cell surfaces via a different complement pathway than anti-HMGCR, causing faster and more complete membrane attack complex-mediated lysis of both skeletal and cardiac myofibers simultaneously","C":"Anti-SRP patients are exclusively statin-naive, meaning their disease has no removable drug trigger, so they invariably require higher doses of immunosuppressives than anti-HMGCR patients who achieve partial remission from statin discontinuation alone","D":"Anti-SRP patients more frequently develop dysphagia and cardiac involvement including arrhythmias and myocarditis compared to anti-HMGCR patients, contributing to greater overall morbidity, and generally present with more severe myopathy including higher CK levels and more rapid weakness progression","E":"Anti-SRP IMNM is the only IMNM subtype that responds to high-dose corticosteroids alone without steroid-sparing agents, and the greater apparent severity reflects a high initial steroid dose requirement that tapers completely over 18 months"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anti-SRP (signal recognition particle) IMNM is generally more severe than anti-HMGCR IMNM. SRP is a ribonucleoprotein complex involved in co-translational protein targeting to the ER membrane; anti-SRP antibodies are associated with more severe proximal myopathy (higher CK), more frequent dysphagia from pharyngeal muscle involvement, and cardiac involvement including myocarditis and cardiomyopathy. Anti-HMGCR IMNM is associated with prior statin exposure (but not exclusively) and tends toward a milder course. Both subtypes require immunosuppressive therapy — neither responds to statin discontinuation alone. Anti-SRP does not function through HMGCR binding. Being statin-naive does not determine immunosuppressive dose requirements. Both subtypes are steroid-refractory and require steroid-sparing agents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-SRP IMNM = more severe, cardiac and pharyngeal involvement, statin-naive patients. Anti-HMGCR = statin-associated (but not required), less severe, better treatment response. COMLEX: Both need immunosuppression — the antibody subtype guides prognosis counseling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — Most Common Adverse Effects by Class of Treatment"},{"id":"sdl43_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the medication most often associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy as well as the antibody-driven mechanism of damage.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old female with anti-HMGCR IMNM is told she must restart a statin for secondary prevention after a recent MI. Which best explains why anti-HMGCR IMNM can develop in statin-naive patients, complicating the simple causal assumption?","choices":{"A":"Statin-naive patients develop anti-HMGCR IMNM because statins contaminate livestock feed at sub-therapeutic concentrations, exposing individuals to cumulative statin doses that prime autoreactive B cells for HMGCR-targeted responses over decades","B":"Statin-naive patients with anti-HMGCR IMNM have a genetic HMGCR variant that serves as a neoantigen, triggering anti-HMGCR antibody production via molecular mimicry with microbial cell wall sterol biosynthesis enzymes","C":"HMG-CoA reductase expression is upregulated in regenerating muscle fibers regardless of statin exposure, suggesting that any cause of muscle injury triggering regeneration can lead to HMGCR antigen presentation and anti-HMGCR antibody production in genetically susceptible individuals, independent of statin exposure","D":"Anti-HMGCR antibodies in statin-naive patients arise from cross-reactivity between HMGCR and complement protein C1q, triggered by early complement deficiency that unmasks HMGCR as a neoplastic self-antigen in regenerating muscle","E":"Statin-naive anti-HMGCR IMNM is always a paraneoplastic process, arising exclusively when tumor cells upregulate HMG-CoA reductase for cholesterol biosynthesis and generate immune responses that cross-react with normal myofiber HMGCR expression"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The occurrence of anti-HMGCR IMNM in statin-naive patients reveals that HMGCR is expressed physiologically in regenerating muscle fibers (as part of the cholesterol synthesis required for membrane assembly during regeneration), independent of pharmacologic HMG-CoA inhibition. Any trigger of muscle injury and regeneration — including other causes of myopathy, mechanical injury, or viral infection — can upregulate HMGCR in regenerating myofibers, potentially presenting this antigen to the immune system and triggering anti-HMGCR antibody production in genetically susceptible individuals. This also explains why re-exposing a patient with anti-HMGCR IMNM to a statin is dangerous — it amplifies HMGCR expression on myofibers. Food contamination is not evidence-based. A genetic HMGCR neoantigen has not been identified. C1q cross-reactivity is speculative. Not all statin-naive cases are paraneoplastic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-HMGCR IMNM can occur without statin exposure because regenerating myofibers normally upregulate HMGCR. This distinguishes IMNM as a true autoimmune disease, not purely drug-induced. COMLEX: Do not restart statins in confirmed anti-HMGCR IMNM patients; alternative lipid-lowering agents (ezetimibe, PCSK9 inhibitors) are preferred.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — Most Common Adverse Effects by Class of Treatment"},{"id":"sdl43_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the myopathies associated with thyroid dysfunction.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 38-year-old female presents with 5 months of proximal weakness, difficulty rising from chairs, weight gain, constipation, cold intolerance, and prolonged relaxation of deep tendon reflexes. CK is 1,100 U/L. TSH is 68 mIU/L with low free T4. Her weakness and CK normalize over 3 months on levothyroxine. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains her myopathy?","choices":{"A":"Hypothyroidism impairs myosin ATPase activity and oxidative phosphorylation in skeletal muscle, producing a metabolic myopathy characterized by myofiber atrophy, internally positioned nuclei, glycogen aggregates, and mucopolysaccharide deposition in connective tissue, all reversible with thyroid hormone replacement","B":"Hypothyroidism triggers anti-HMGCR antibody production through TSH receptor-mediated complement activation on myofiber surfaces, producing pauci-inflammatory necrosis identical to statin-induced IMNM that resolves when thyroid hormone normalizes TSH","C":"Hypothyroidism activates perimysial CD4+ T cells via thyroid hormone deficiency-induced aberrant MHC class II upregulation on myofiber surfaces, producing perifascicular atrophy that reverses with hormone replacement and mirrors the dermatomyositis mechanism","D":"Hypothyroid myopathy results from autoimmune attack on muscle by thyroid peroxidase antibodies (anti-TPO) that cross-react with myosin heavy chain epitopes, producing a Type II antibody-mediated cytotoxic myopathy indistinguishable from AIHA in its immunopathology","E":"Hypothyroidism depletes mitochondrial coenzyme Q10 via TSH-mediated inhibition of mitochondrial biogenesis factors, producing a mitochondrial myopathy with ragged-red fibers on modified Gomori trichrome that is indistinguishable from zidovudine-induced mitochondrial myopathy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypothyroid myopathy is a metabolic myopathy resulting from thyroid hormone deficiency. Thyroid hormone is critical for normal myosin ATPase function, oxidative phosphorylation, and glycogenolysis. Its deficiency reduces muscle energy metabolism, producing the characteristic clinical picture: proximal weakness, elevated CK (from metabolic myofiber stress), slowed deep tendon reflex relaxation (the hung-up reflex, from slowed actin-myosin dissociation), muscle cramping, and cold intolerance. Biopsy shows non-inflammatory changes: myofiber atrophy, internal nuclei, glycogen accumulation, and mucopolysaccharide deposition in connective tissue. These changes and the elevated CK are completely reversible with levothyroxine. This is NOT an immune-mediated process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothyroid myopathy = metabolic, not immune. Elevated CK + hung-up reflexes + cramping + proximal weakness = check TSH. Resolves with levothyroxine. Statin toxicity risk is also increased in hypothyroid patients. COMLEX: Order TSH before muscle biopsy in any patient with unexplained proximal weakness and elevated CK.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Commonly Used Laboratory Tests to Aid Evaluation of Rheumatic Disease"},{"id":"sdl43_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the myopathies associated with thyroid dysfunction.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 28-year-old female with Graves disease presents with progressive proximal weakness, palpitations, heat intolerance, and 5-kg weight loss. CK is 85 U/L (normal). TSH is undetectable with markedly elevated free T4. EMG shows a myopathic pattern. Which best distinguishes thyrotoxic myopathy from hypothyroid myopathy in terms of mechanism and key laboratory finding?","choices":{"A":"Thyrotoxic myopathy is caused by anti-TSH receptor antibody cross-reaction with muscle nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, producing a myasthenic syndrome with fatigable weakness and a normal CK in all patients, while hypothyroid myopathy causes a true myopathy with markedly elevated CK only in severe disease","B":"Thyrotoxic myopathy causes hypokalemic periodic paralysis from beta-adrenergic-driven potassium entry into cells, producing episodic weakness with normal inter-ictal CK, while hypothyroid myopathy causes constant proximal weakness with elevated CK only in the most severe myxedema cases","C":"Thyrotoxic myopathy produces a myotonic pattern on EMG with repetitive discharges from thyroid hormone-mediated voltage-gated sodium channel upregulation, while hypothyroid myopathy produces a purely denervating EMG pattern from nerve root compression by glycosaminoglycan epidural deposits","D":"Thyrotoxic myopathy produces proximal weakness with normal or low CK and excess catabolism from thyroid hormone-driven hypermetabolism, while hypothyroid myopathy produces proximal weakness with markedly elevated CK, slowed myosin ATPase activity, and metabolic accumulation of glycogen and mucopolysaccharides","E":"Thyrotoxic myopathy is caused by complement-mediated destruction of myofibers by anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies that cross-react with sarcolemmal enzymes, while hypothyroid myopathy is a purely metabolic disorder with no autoimmune component and no antibody involvement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Thyrotoxic myopathy most commonly presents as subacute proximal weakness from hypermetabolic catabolic effects of excess thyroid hormone on skeletal muscle — muscle protein is broken down faster than it can be synthesized. Because this is primarily a catabolic rather than a necrotic process, CK is normal or low (no significant cell lysis). Reflexes are brisk from thyroid hormone-mediated neural hyperexcitability. Exophthalmic ophthalmoplegia (eyelid swelling, conjunctival edema, diplopia) may accompany the condition. In contrast, hypothyroid myopathy produces metabolic myofiber stress with elevated CK, slowed myosin ATPase dissociation (hung-up reflexes), glycogen accumulation, and cramping. Anti-TSH receptor antibodies cause Graves ophthalmopathy, not nicotinic receptor myasthenia. Hypokalemic periodic paralysis is a separate thyrotoxic complication distinct from thyrotoxic myopathy. Myotonic EMG is not characteristic of thyrotoxicosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thyrotoxic myopathy = catabolism = proximal weakness + normal/low CK + brisk reflexes + hyperthyroid signs (tachycardia, heat intolerance, weight loss, exophthalmos). COMLEX: Exophthalmic ophthalmoplegia (diplopia, periorbital edema) may accompany thyrotoxic myopathy in Graves disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Commonly Used Laboratory Tests to Aid Evaluation of Rheumatic Disease"},{"id":"sdl43_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the myopathies associated with thyroid dysfunction.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 50-year-old male with uncontrolled hypothyroidism is started on simvastatin 40 mg. Six weeks later he presents with severe proximal pain and weakness. CK is 18,000 U/L. Which mechanism best explains the synergistic risk for severe muscle injury in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Hypothyroidism depletes myofiber coenzyme Q10 via TSH-mediated inhibition of mevalonate pathway enzymes, and statins block the same pathway, resulting in additive mitochondrial respiratory chain dysfunction that produces rhabdomyolysis through complex I and III inhibition","B":"Hypothyroid myopathy upregulates HMG-CoA reductase on myofiber surfaces via TSH receptor activation, and statins invariably trigger anti-HMGCR antibody production in all hypothyroid patients started on statins, converting direct toxicity to immune-mediated IMNM","C":"Hypothyroidism reduces hepatic CYP3A4-mediated statin metabolism, increasing circulating statin plasma levels, while simultaneously producing metabolic muscle vulnerability from impaired oxidative phosphorylation and myosin ATPase activity, creating synergistic risk for statin-induced rhabdomyolysis","D":"Hypothyroidism activates TLR7 and TLR9 on muscle satellite cells via myxedematous cellular debris release, triggering IFN-alpha production that directly enhances statin-mediated myofiber apoptosis through caspase-3 activation in a type I interferon-dependent pathway","E":"Hypothyroidism and statins both independently deplete sarcolemmal phosphatidylcholine, causing membrane fragility that makes myofibers susceptible to calcium influx-mediated proteolysis during normal daily contraction-relaxation cycles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The synergy between hypothyroidism and statin-induced myotoxicity occurs through two mechanisms. First, thyroid hormone normally maintains hepatic CYP3A4 activity; hypothyroidism reduces this enzyme's function, impairing first-pass statin metabolism and elevating plasma statin concentrations — increasing muscle exposure to toxic statin levels. Second, hypothyroidism independently produces metabolic myopathy (impaired oxidative phosphorylation, reduced myosin ATPase, glycogen accumulation), which makes muscle cells more vulnerable to any additional metabolic insult. Together these mechanisms produce markedly synergistic risk for rhabdomyolysis. Coenzyme Q10 depletion is proposed but not the primary established mechanism. Anti-HMGCR IMNM does not develop in all hypothyroid statin users. TLR/IFN-alpha is not established in this context. Phosphatidylcholine depletion is not the mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothyroidism + statin = synergistic rhabdomyolysis risk. Always check TSH before starting statins, and optimize thyroid function first. COMLEX: The combination is one of the highest-yield drug-disease interactions for causing severe myopathy — appears on boards frequently.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Commonly Used Laboratory Tests to Aid Evaluation of Rheumatic Disease"}]},{"sdlNumber":44,"title":"Rheumatologic Laboratory Testing","questions":[{"id":"sdl44_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concept of sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio in the use of laboratory testing for rheumatic diseases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following statements correctly defines the sensitivity of a diagnostic test?","choices":{"A":"The proportion of patients with disease who test positive, representing the true positive rate of the test","B":"The proportion of patients without disease who test negative, representing the true negative rate of the test","C":"The probability that a positive result correctly identifies a patient who truly has the disease in question","D":"The ratio of true positives to the sum of true positives and false positives for a given diagnostic test","E":"The overall proportion of correct results from a test, including both true positives and true negatives combined"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sensitivity measures how well a test detects disease: it equals true positives divided by all patients with the disease. The answer describing true negatives over all without disease defines specificity. Positive predictive value is true positives over all positive results. Accuracy is total correct results over all tested. Sensitivity is critical for ruling out disease; a highly sensitive test rarely misses cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sensitivity = TP / TP + FN; a sensitive test rules OUT disease when negative (SnNout). COMLEX: High-sensitivity tests are preferred when missing a diagnosis causes serious harm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio govern the interpretation of all rheumatologic laboratory tests; pretest probability and disease prevalence determine whether a positive result meaningfully shifts post-test odds in clinical decision making."},{"id":"sdl44_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concept of sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio in the use of laboratory testing for rheumatic diseases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A screening test for rheumatoid arthritis has a sensitivity of 60% and a specificity of 80% in a studied population. Which of the following values most closely represents the positive likelihood ratio of this test?","choices":{"A":"0.5, consistent with a test that performs no better than random chance in this patient population setting","B":"8.0, consistent with good diagnostic performance and a meaningful shift in the probability of disease","C":"12.0, consistent with excellent diagnostic performance and a large definitive shift in the post-test probability","D":"3.0, consistent with fair to poor diagnostic performance by standard likelihood ratio interpretation guidelines","E":"0.2, consistent with the negative likelihood ratio value, which reflects performance when the test result is negative"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The positive likelihood ratio equals sensitivity divided by one minus specificity. With 60% sensitivity and 80% specificity, LR+ = 0.60 / (1 - 0.80) = 0.60 / 0.20 = 3.0. A LR+ below 5 indicates fair to poor performance. LR+ of 5 to 10 indicates good performance, and LR+ of 10 or greater indicates excellent performance. The LR of 0.2 represents the negative likelihood ratio: (1 - 0.60) / 0.80 = 0.50 for this test, not 0.2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LR+ = Sensitivity / (1 - Specificity). LR+ greater than 10 = excellent; 5 to 10 = good; below 5 = fair to poor. COMLEX: These formulas appear on COMLEX-1 in biostatistics clinical vignettes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio govern the interpretation of all rheumatologic laboratory tests; pretest probability and disease prevalence determine whether a positive result meaningfully shifts post-test odds in clinical decision making."},{"id":"sdl44_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concept of sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio in the use of laboratory testing for rheumatic diseases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following statements about likelihood ratios correctly characterizes a test with excellent diagnostic performance?","choices":{"A":"The positive likelihood ratio is at least 5 and the negative likelihood ratio is 0.1 to 0.2, indicating good performance overall","B":"The positive likelihood ratio is at least 3 and the negative likelihood ratio is 0.2 to 0.5, indicating fair to poor performance","C":"The positive likelihood ratio is at least 10 and the negative likelihood ratio is less than 0.1, indicating excellent performance","D":"The positive likelihood ratio equals 1.0, indicating that a positive result produces no change in the pretest probability","E":"The positive likelihood ratio is greater than 2 and the negative likelihood ratio is below 0.5, indicating moderate performance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Excellent test performance is defined by a positive LR of 10 or greater combined with a negative LR below 0.1. A positive LR of 5 to 10 and negative LR of 0.1 to 0.2 represents good performance. Values below these thresholds represent fair to poor performance. A LR+ of 1.0 means the test result does not change the pre-test probability at all, making it diagnostically worthless.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize LR thresholds: LR+ more than 10 and LR minus less than 0.1 is excellent. COMLEX: Likelihood ratios are independent of disease prevalence, unlike predictive values.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio govern the interpretation of all rheumatologic laboratory tests; pretest probability and disease prevalence determine whether a positive result meaningfully shifts post-test odds in clinical decision making."},{"id":"sdl44_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the commonly used laboratory tests to aid in evaluation of patients with rheumatic disease (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the difference in response kinetics between erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein during inflammatory rheumatic disease?","choices":{"A":"CRP is the primary marker of bone marrow function and is superior to ESR for monitoring drug-induced cytopenias during DMARD therapy","B":"ESR changes slowly in response to both increases and decreases in inflammation, whereas CRP rises and falls more rapidly as disease activity fluctuates","C":"ESR is produced directly by hepatocytes in response to IL-6, whereas CRP is determined by red blood cell aggregation caused by plasma proteins","D":"CRP values are physiologically higher in women than in men and increase with age and during pregnancy, while ESR remains constant across groups","E":"ESR is preferred over CRP for detecting infection in patients receiving IL-6 receptor antagonists because it is unaffected by cytokine pathway inhibition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CRP is directly synthesized by hepatocytes under IL-6 stimulation and peaks and troughs rapidly with disease activity changes. ESR reflects erythrocyte aggregation driven by fibrinogen and immunoglobulins and is much slower to change. It is ESR that is physiologically higher in women, older adults, and pregnant patients. CRP may be falsely normal in patients on IL-6 receptor blockers such as tocilizumab even during active infection, making ESR or procalcitonin the safer marker in that setting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CRP is the better real-time inflammatory marker; ESR lags. Both are elevated in PMR and GCA. COMLEX: ESR elevation in PMR greater than 50 mm per hour is a classic clinical presentation trigger.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — IL-6 is the primary hepatic stimulus for CRP synthesis and fibrinogen production, linking the pro-inflammatory cytokine milieu of RA and other rheumatic diseases to the acute-phase reactants used to monitor disease activity and drug toxicity."},{"id":"sdl44_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the commonly used laboratory tests to aid in evaluation of patients with rheumatic disease (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with a 15-year history of seropositive rheumatoid arthritis presents with recurrent bacterial infections. CBC shows a white blood cell count of 2,100 per microliter with an absolute neutrophil count of 800. Physical examination reveals firm splenomegaly without lymphadenopathy. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Drug-induced bone marrow suppression from disease-modifying antirheumatic drug therapy, causing global cytopenias requiring immediate dose reduction","B":"Systemic lupus erythematosus, which can cause neutropenia and splenomegaly through anti-neutrophil antibodies and immune complex deposition","C":"Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which characteristically produces marked eosinophilia rather than neutropenia in affected patients","D":"Large granular lymphocyte syndrome unrelated to rheumatoid arthritis, presenting with isolated neutropenia and splenomegaly without autoantibodies","E":"Felty syndrome, defined by the triad of seropositive rheumatoid arthritis, neutropenia, and splenomegaly in a single patient"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Felty syndrome is the classic triad of long-standing seropositive RA, neutropenia, and splenomegaly. Neutropenia in RA in the absence of splenomegaly raises concern for drug toxicity. Neutropenia plus eosinophilia in another clinical context suggests Churg-Strauss. Large granular lymphocyte syndrome can mimic Felty but lacks the RA background. Drug toxicity typically causes pancytopenia rather than isolated neutropenia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Felty syndrome = seropositive RA plus neutropenia plus splenomegaly; neutropenia predisposes to recurrent skin and pulmonary infections. COMLEX: Associated with high RF titers and HLA-DR4.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — IL-6 is the primary hepatic stimulus for CRP synthesis and fibrinogen production, linking the pro-inflammatory cytokine milieu of RA and other rheumatic diseases to the acute-phase reactants used to monitor disease activity and drug toxicity."},{"id":"sdl44_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the commonly used laboratory tests to aid in evaluation of patients with rheumatic disease (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient receiving tocilizumab for rheumatoid arthritis develops a temperature of 38.9 degrees Celsius, tachycardia, and purulent sputum, but the CRP level returns at 0.4 mg per liter. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this discrepancy?","choices":{"A":"Tocilizumab depletes circulating B lymphocytes, reducing immunoglobulin production and thereby blunting all inflammatory marker production","B":"Tocilizumab directly neutralizes tumor necrosis factor-alpha, the primary cytokine responsible for CRP induction during bacterial infection","C":"Tocilizumab blocks the IL-6 receptor on hepatocytes, suppressing CRP synthesis even in the presence of active bacterial infection","D":"CRP is physiologically lower in all patients on long-term immunosuppression because chronic therapy suppresses baseline hepatic acute-phase responses","E":"Tocilizumab increases IL-10 production, which selectively counteracts IL-6 at the receptor level without affecting ESR or serum ferritin levels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IL-6 is the primary driver of hepatic CRP synthesis. Tocilizumab blocks the IL-6 receptor, preventing CRP production regardless of the infectious stimulus. This creates a dangerous clinical gap where CRP cannot be relied upon to detect infection, and clinicians must depend on clinical examination, procalcitonin, and imaging. Tocilizumab does not deplete B cells or block TNF-alpha; rituximab depletes B cells and adalimumab blocks TNF-alpha.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-6 drives CRP; tocilizumab blocks this. CRP may be normal during infection in patients on IL-6 inhibitors. COMLEX: A common RA management clinical question involving monitoring for infectious complications on biologics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — IL-6 is the primary hepatic stimulus for CRP synthesis and fibrinogen production, linking the pro-inflammatory cytokine milieu of RA and other rheumatic diseases to the acute-phase reactants used to monitor disease activity and drug toxicity."},{"id":"sdl44_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the autoantibodies in rheumatic disease as they associate with specific conditions. Understand the antibodies which are specific, non-specific, and those that are sensitive for diagnosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following rheumatic diseases has the highest reported sensitivity for antinuclear antibody detection by indirect immunofluorescence on HEp-2 cells?","choices":{"A":"Mixed connective tissue disease, with antinuclear antibody positivity approaching 100% because high-titer anti-U1 RNP is a diagnostic prerequisite","B":"Systemic lupus erythematosus, with antinuclear antibody positivity of 95 to 100% depending on the specific assay and patient population","C":"Drug-induced lupus erythematosus, with antinuclear antibody positivity of approximately 90% and predominantly anti-histone antibody specificity","D":"Sjögren syndrome, with antinuclear antibody positivity of 40 to 70% and predominantly anti-Ro and anti-La antibody specificity on testing","E":"Pauciarticular juvenile chronic arthritis, with antinuclear antibody positivity of approximately 70% in the pauciarticular subtype specifically"},"correct":"A","explanation":"According to published reference data, mixed connective tissue disease has a 100% sensitivity for ANA because high-titer anti-U1 RNP antibodies, a prerequisite for diagnosis, invariably produce a positive ANA result. SLE approaches 95 to 100%, drug-induced lupus approximately 90%, and pauciarticular JCA approximately 70%. Sjögren syndrome has the lowest sensitivity among these at 40 to 70%. Knowing the hierarchy of ANA sensitivity by disease is high-yield for USMLE question stems.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ANA sensitivity hierarchy: MCTD 100% then SLE 95-100% then drug-induced lupus 90%. ANA is a screening tool, not diagnostic. COMLEX: A positive ANA requires reflex testing for anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm, anti-Ro, anti-La.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Autoantibodies arise from failed central and peripheral self-tolerance; their specificity for individual diseases reflects unique antigen presentations in each condition, making antibody-disease correlation a core rheumatologic diagnostic skill."},{"id":"sdl44_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the autoantibodies in rheumatic disease as they associate with specific conditions. Understand the antibodies which are specific, non-specific, and those that are sensitive for diagnosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following statements about anti-Smith antibodies best describes their diagnostic utility in systemic lupus erythematosus?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Sm antibodies are the most sensitive serologic marker for SLE, detectable in over 90% of patients during active disease flares and remission","B":"Anti-Sm antibodies are highly specific for SLE but have low sensitivity of only 10 to 40%, so a negative result is unhelpful for exclusion of the diagnosis","C":"Anti-Sm antibodies are the defining serologic prerequisite for diagnosing mixed connective tissue disease when present at high titers","D":"Anti-Sm antibodies are associated with subacute cutaneous lupus, photosensitivity, and congenital heart block through transplacental transfer","E":"Anti-Sm antibodies produce a centromere staining pattern on HEp-2 cells and are specifically associated with limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anti-Sm antibodies target the spliceosome and produce a coarse speckled ANA pattern. They are found almost exclusively in SLE, making them highly specific, but they are present in only 10 to 40% of SLE patients, limiting their sensitivity. The prerequisite antibody for MCTD is anti-U1 RNP, not anti-Sm. Anti-Ro and anti-La are associated with subacute cutaneous lupus and neonatal lupus. Anticentromere antibodies are specific to limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-Sm = very specific for SLE, low sensitivity; positive result is strong evidence for SLE but a negative does not exclude it. COMLEX: Included in EULAR/ACR SLE classification criteria as a domain item.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Autoantibodies arise from failed central and peripheral self-tolerance; their specificity for individual diseases reflects unique antigen presentations in each condition, making antibody-disease correlation a core rheumatologic diagnostic skill."},{"id":"sdl44_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the autoantibodies in rheumatic disease as they associate with specific conditions. Understand the antibodies which are specific, non-specific, and those that are sensitive for diagnosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following clinical phenotypes is most strongly associated with anti-Scl-70 antibodies in patients with systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis with isolated pulmonary arterial hypertension and anticentromere antibody co-positivity in the same patient","B":"Mixed connective tissue disease with overlapping features of systemic sclerosis and lupus driven by high-titer anti-U1 RNP antibodies","C":"Primary Sjögren syndrome with predominant glandular involvement, dry eyes, dry mouth, and anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies as driving markers","D":"Diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis with significant interstitial lung disease involving pulmonary parenchymal fibrosis","E":"Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis presenting with asthma, eosinophilia, and perinuclear ANCA as the primary serologic finding"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anti-Scl-70 targets topoisomerase I and is associated with diffuse cutaneous SSc and pulmonary parenchymal interstitial lung disease. By contrast, anticentromere antibodies are associated with limited SSc and pulmonary arterial hypertension. These two antibodies rarely occur in the same patient. Anti-Scl-70 and anticentromere represent distinct clinical endotypes of SSc. Limited SSc is defined as skin involvement distal to the elbows and knees, while diffuse SSc extends proximal to these landmarks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-Scl-70 equals diffuse SSc plus ILD; anticentromere equals limited SSc plus PAH. These two antibodies rarely co-occur in the same patient. COMLEX: Limited SSc features CREST syndrome: Calcinosis, Raynaud, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, Telangiectasias.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Autoantibodies arise from failed central and peripheral self-tolerance; their specificity for individual diseases reflects unique antigen presentations in each condition, making antibody-disease correlation a core rheumatologic diagnostic skill."},{"id":"sdl44_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concept of sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio in the use of laboratory testing for rheumatic diseases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with fatigue and diffuse arthralgias for four months is referred to rheumatology after a positive antinuclear antibody test at a titer of 1:80. The prevalence of SLE in the general population is approximately 1 in 2,000, and 1 in 20 healthy individuals test positive for ANA at this titer. She has no rash, no serositis, and no renal symptoms. Which of the following best explains why this positive ANA result has a low positive predictive value in this clinical scenario?","choices":{"A":"ANA testing by HEp-2 immunofluorescence is poorly standardized across laboratory platforms, making all positive results unreliable and clinically uninterpretable","B":"Low disease prevalence means that even a highly sensitive test will generate substantially more false positives than true positives in an unselected population","C":"A titer of 1:80 falls below the diagnostic threshold of 1:320 required for antinuclear antibodies to carry any clinical significance in autoimmune evaluation","D":"Positive predictive value is artificially elevated when tests are ordered in referral populations, so this result actually represents a stronger diagnostic signal than it appears","E":"Antinuclear antibody sensitivity for SLE is below 50%, making it an inappropriate first-line screening test and causing frequent false-positive results"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PPV depends on both test performance and disease prevalence. When SLE has a prevalence of 1 in 2,000 but 1 in 20 healthy individuals test ANA positive, the vast majority of positive results will be false positives. This is a fundamental Bayesian principle: the lower the pretest probability, the lower the PPV even for a highly sensitive test. ANA sensitivity for SLE is 95 to 100%, so it is a valid screening tool, but its low specificity in general populations dilutes PPV significantly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PPV decreases when prevalence falls, even if sensitivity and specificity are constant. ANA is a screening test, not diagnostic. COMLEX: A positive ANA in a patient with low pretest probability for SLE requires reflex testing and clinical correlation, not immediate diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic Laboratory Testing for Joint Effusions"},{"id":"sdl44_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concept of sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio in the use of laboratory testing for rheumatic diseases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with inflammatory low back pain, anterior uveitis, and restricted lumbar flexion is tested for HLA-B27. The test has a sensitivity of 90% for ankylosing spondylitis and a specificity of 94% in non-diseased controls in this population. Which of the following correctly calculates the positive likelihood ratio for this test?","choices":{"A":"LR+ equals 0.90 multiplied by 0.94, yielding a value of 0.85, which represents the combined probability of correct classification across all results","B":"LR+ equals 1 minus 0.90 divided by 0.94, yielding a value of 0.11, which actually represents the negative likelihood ratio for this test","C":"LR+ equals 0.94 divided by 1 minus 0.90, yielding a value of 9.4, which overestimates performance by inverting sensitivity and specificity","D":"LR+ equals 0.90 plus 0.94 divided by 2, yielding 0.92, which represents the arithmetic mean of sensitivity and specificity rather than LR+","E":"LR+ equals 0.90 divided by 1 minus 0.94, yielding a value of 15.0, indicating excellent diagnostic performance by standard thresholds"},"correct":"E","explanation":"LR+ = sensitivity / (1 - specificity) = 0.90 / (1 - 0.94) = 0.90 / 0.06 = 15.0, indicating excellent performance. LR minus = (1 - sensitivity) / specificity = 0.10 / 0.94 = 0.11, indicating excellent negative performance as well. Multiplying sensitivity by specificity is not a valid formula for any standard diagnostic test metric. An LR+ of 15 means that a positive HLA-B27 result shifts the odds of ankylosing spondylitis 15-fold, making it clinically very meaningful in the appropriate setting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LR+ formula and interpretation are directly tested. HLA-B27 is positive in 90% of ankylosing spondylitis patients and 6% of the general population, making it a useful but not definitive test. COMLEX: HLA-B27 is associated with the seronegative spondyloarthropathies, which are a COMLEX Clinical Presentation category.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic Laboratory Testing for Joint Effusions"},{"id":"sdl44_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concept of sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio in the use of laboratory testing for rheumatic diseases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A rheumatology fellow applies an anti-dsDNA assay with 95% specificity and 70% sensitivity to two different populations: a high-risk SLE referral clinic where SLE prevalence is 20%, and a general primary care population where SLE prevalence is 0.1%. Which of the following best predicts how test performance will differ between these two settings?","choices":{"A":"The positive predictive value will be substantially higher in the SLE referral clinic, even though sensitivity and specificity remain identical across both populations","B":"The sensitivity and specificity of the anti-dsDNA assay will increase in the higher-prevalence SLE clinic because more true positives are present to anchor both values","C":"The positive predictive value will be identical in both populations because it is calculated from the same sensitivity and specificity in both settings","D":"The likelihood ratio will increase in the SLE referral clinic, making it the most appropriate statistic for comparing test utility across populations","E":"The negative predictive value will be substantially lower in the primary care population because fewer patients without disease are available for calculation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sensitivity and specificity are intrinsic properties of the test and do not change with prevalence. However, PPV increases as prevalence increases because there are proportionally more true positives relative to false positives. In a population with 20% SLE prevalence, a positive anti-dsDNA result is highly likely to represent true disease. In a 0.1% prevalence population, most positive results are false positives. Likelihood ratios are also prevalence-independent and would remain constant across both settings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sensitivity and specificity are stable across populations; PPV and NPV change with prevalence. This is one of the most tested biostatistics concepts on USMLE Step 1. COMLEX: Applied to rheumatologic testing in the context of appropriate test ordering and clinical reasoning.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic Laboratory Testing for Joint Effusions"},{"id":"sdl44_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the commonly used laboratory tests to aid in evaluation of patients with rheumatic disease (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with known SLE presents with facial edema, hematuria, and 3+ proteinuria. Laboratory evaluation reveals anti-dsDNA titer of 1:640, C3 of 45 mg per dL, C4 of 5 mg per dL, and creatinine of 1.9 mg per dL, consistent with active lupus nephritis. With successful immunosuppressive treatment of the acute flare, which of the following laboratory changes would most reliably indicate improvement?","choices":{"A":"Rising rheumatoid factor titers with normalization of complement levels, indicating immune reconstitution independent of SLE disease activity","B":"Falling ESR with persistently elevated CRP, reflecting resolution of the acute-phase response despite ongoing nephritis at the cellular level","C":"Normalization of ANA titer from 1:640 to negative, since ANA levels track directly and predictably with SLE disease activity during nephritis flares","D":"Rising C3 and C4 with falling anti-dsDNA titers, reflecting decreased immune complex deposition and reduced complement pathway consumption","E":"Rising anti-Sm titers alongside decreasing anti-dsDNA, indicating a shift toward a less nephrotoxic autoantibody specificity as nephritis resolves"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In active lupus nephritis, DNA-anti-dsDNA immune complexes deposit in the glomeruli, activate complement, and consume C3 and C4. Successful treatment reduces immune complex formation, allowing complement levels to normalize and anti-dsDNA titers to fall. ANA titers do not reliably track with disease activity; ANA can remain positive even in remission. Anti-Sm titers do not correlate with lupus nephritis activity. RF is not a marker of SLE disease activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Active lupus nephritis = elevated anti-dsDNA plus low C3 and C4 plus active urinary sediment. Normalization of complement with falling anti-dsDNA tracks treatment response. COMLEX: Lupus nephritis is a Type III hypersensitivity injury from immune complex deposition in glomeruli.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory Cytokines Involved in Rheumatoid Inflammation"},{"id":"sdl44_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the commonly used laboratory tests to aid in evaluation of patients with rheumatic disease (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with seropositive rheumatoid arthritis is started on methotrexate 15 mg weekly along with folic acid supplementation. He returns for follow-up after 8 weeks of therapy. Which of the following laboratory tests is most essential to monitor at regular intervals throughout this treatment course?","choices":{"A":"Serum uric acid levels and synovial fluid crystal analysis, to detect methotrexate-induced hyperuricemia and drug-related crystal arthropathy","B":"Complete blood count and hepatic aminotransferases, with dose reduction required if AST or ALT rises above three times the upper limit of normal","C":"Complement C3 and C4 levels alongside anti-dsDNA titers, to exclude the development of methotrexate-induced lupus erythematosus during therapy","D":"Serum ferritin and S100 protein levels as markers of macrophage activation syndrome, which can be triggered by methotrexate in susceptible patients","E":"Antinuclear antibody and anti-histone antibody testing, to detect methotrexate-induced autoimmunity and drug-induced lupus in this patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Methotrexate requires monitoring for hepatotoxicity, bone marrow suppression, and renal function because impaired renal clearance increases methotrexate accumulation and toxicity. AST and ALT should be monitored, and the drug should be stopped if levels exceed three times the upper limit of normal. CBC monitors for macrocytosis and cytopenias from folate antagonism. Methotrexate does not cause crystal arthropathy or complement depletion. Drug-induced lupus from methotrexate is extremely rare and anti-histone testing is not standard monitoring.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Methotrexate monitoring: CBC for cytopenias plus liver function tests for hepatotoxicity plus creatinine for renal clearance. Folic acid supplementation reduces mucosal and hematologic toxicity without reducing efficacy. COMLEX: Methotrexate toxicity is worsened by NSAIDs, alcohol, and renal impairment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory Cytokines Involved in Rheumatoid Inflammation"},{"id":"sdl44_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the commonly used laboratory tests to aid in evaluation of patients with rheumatic disease (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with long-standing seropositive rheumatoid arthritis presents for routine follow-up. CBC shows hemoglobin of 10.1 g per dL with a mean corpuscular volume of 83 fL. Iron studies show low serum iron, low transferrin saturation, and markedly elevated ferritin. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"NSAID-induced gastrointestinal bleeding causes true iron deficiency anemia, which presents with low serum iron, low ferritin, and elevated total iron-binding capacity","B":"Methotrexate-induced folate deficiency causes macrocytic anemia with megaloblastic changes on peripheral smear and elevated mean corpuscular volume","C":"Chronic inflammation-driven hepcidin excess impairs macrophage iron release and reduces intestinal iron absorption, producing functional iron-restricted erythropoiesis","D":"Antierythrocyte antibodies in rheumatoid arthritis cause hemolytic anemia with low haptoglobin, elevated indirect bilirubin, and positive direct Coombs test","E":"Renal amyloidosis from long-standing RA reduces erythropoietin production, causing normocytic anemia with low reticulocyte count and normal iron stores"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anemia of chronic inflammation involves IL-6-driven hepcidin production by the liver. Hepcidin binds and degrades ferroportin, trapping iron inside macrophages and enterocytes and reducing circulating iron available for erythropoiesis. This produces a normocytic or mildly microcytic anemia with low serum iron, low transferrin saturation, but elevated ferritin because iron is sequestered, not depleted. True iron deficiency anemia would show low ferritin and elevated TIBC. Folate deficiency from methotrexate produces macrocytic anemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anemia of chronic disease features low serum iron plus low TIBC plus elevated ferritin, distinguishing it from iron deficiency where ferritin is low and TIBC is high. COMLEX: Hepcidin is the master iron regulator; chronic inflammation elevates it via IL-6.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory Cytokines Involved in Rheumatoid Inflammation"},{"id":"sdl44_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the autoantibodies in rheumatic disease as they associate with specific conditions. Understand the antibodies which are specific, non-specific, and those that are sensitive for diagnosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with a 10-year history of Raynaud phenomenon, skin tightening limited to the hands and face, and telangiectasias is found to have anticentromere antibodies on ANA reflex testing. Which of the following disease associations and complication risks is most consistent with this antibody profile?","choices":{"A":"Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis with elevated risk of isolated pulmonary arterial hypertension rather than pulmonary parenchymal fibrosis","B":"Diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis with severe interstitial lung disease and likely co-positivity of anti-Scl-70 antibodies in the same patient","C":"Primary Sjögren syndrome with predominant glandular lymphocytic infiltration, dry eyes, dry mouth, and anti-Ro and anti-La as the primary antibodies","D":"Mixed connective tissue disease with overlapping features of sclerosis, lupus, and myositis driven by high-titer anti-U1 RNP antibodies on ELISA","E":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis with upper respiratory destruction, saddle-nose deformity, and proteinase-3 ANCA as the primary serologic marker"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anticentromere antibodies are strongly associated with limited cutaneous SSc, characterized by skin involvement distal to elbows and knees, Raynaud phenomenon, telangiectasias, and a high risk of isolated pulmonary arterial hypertension. In contrast, anti-Scl-70 antibodies target topoisomerase I and are associated with diffuse SSc and interstitial pulmonary fibrosis. These two antibodies rarely occur in the same patient and represent distinct SSc endotypes with different organ complication profiles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anticentromere = limited SSc plus PAH risk; anti-Scl-70 = diffuse SSc plus ILD risk. These two antibody types rarely overlap. COMLEX: Limited SSc features the CREST syndrome constellation and a better prognosis than diffuse SSc.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl44_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the autoantibodies in rheumatic disease as they associate with specific conditions. Understand the antibodies which are specific, non-specific, and those that are sensitive for diagnosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman presents with her third first-trimester pregnancy loss. Coagulation studies show a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time that does not correct on a 1:1 mixing study with normal plasma. Anticardiolipin IgG antibodies are markedly elevated and have been confirmed on repeat testing 12 weeks apart. Which of the following best explains the paradox of a prolonged clotting assay with a clinically hypercoagulable phenotype?","choices":{"A":"Antiphospholipid antibodies directly activate intrinsic coagulation cascade factors, causing both laboratory clotting factor consumption and clinical arterial thrombosis","B":"Factor VIII deficiency co-existing with antiphospholipid syndrome causes a prolonged aPTT that corrects on mixing study and produces bleeding rather than thrombosis","C":"Lupus anticoagulant antibodies block phospholipid-dependent coagulation complexes in vitro, prolonging laboratory assays while promoting thrombin generation and clotting in vivo","D":"Antiphospholipid antibodies activate complement on platelet surfaces, simultaneously causing thrombocytopenia and prolonged clotting times through platelet depletion","E":"Lupus anticoagulant blocks thrombomodulin on endothelial cells, reducing protein C activation and causing both laboratory assay prolongation and in vivo thrombosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lupus anticoagulant antibodies bind to phospholipid-protein complexes in the test tube, blocking the assembly of the prothrombinase complex and prolonging phospholipid-dependent clotting assays such as the aPTT. However, in vivo these antibodies promote a prothrombotic state by interfering with anticoagulant mechanisms. The failure to correct on a 1:1 mixing study distinguishes lupus anticoagulant from factor deficiencies, which would correct. Antiphospholipid syndrome diagnoses require confirmed positive testing on two occasions at least 12 weeks apart.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lupus anticoagulant paradox: prolonged aPTT in vitro plus thrombosis in vivo. Does not correct on mixing study. COMLEX: Antiphospholipid syndrome triad: thrombosis plus pregnancy loss plus positive antiphospholipid antibodies on two occasions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl44_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the autoantibodies in rheumatic disease as they associate with specific conditions. Understand the antibodies which are specific, non-specific, and those that are sensitive for diagnosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man presents with saddle-nose deformity, chronic sinusitis with bloody nasal discharge, hemoptysis, and hematuria with red blood cell casts on urinalysis. ANCA testing shows a coarse granular fluorescence in the cytoplasm of ethanol-fixed neutrophils, which is confirmed by ELISA targeting a specific serine protease. Which of the following combinations of ANCA pattern and target antigen is most consistent with this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Perinuclear ANCA pattern directed against myeloperoxidase, characteristic of microscopic polyangiitis and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","B":"Perinuclear ANCA pattern directed against proteinase-3, seen in a subset of granulomatosis with polyangiitis patients when formalin-fixed cells are used","C":"Cytoplasmic ANCA pattern directed against myeloperoxidase, associated with drug-induced vasculitis and atypical ANCA-associated disease presentations","D":"Perinuclear ANCA pattern directed against lactoferrin, a nonspecific finding associated with inflammatory bowel disease rather than systemic vasculitis","E":"Cytoplasmic ANCA pattern directed against proteinase-3, characteristic of granulomatosis with polyangiitis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis is characterized by c-ANCA targeting proteinase-3. The cytoplasmic fluorescence pattern results from the diffuse distribution of PR3 in neutrophil cytoplasm. Microscopic polyangiitis and eosinophilic GPA are associated with p-ANCA targeting myeloperoxidase. The saddle-nose deformity, upper and lower respiratory tract involvement, and glomerulonephritis with RBC casts are the classic clinical triad of GPA. ANCA testing guidelines now recommend direct ELISA for MPO and PR3 as the primary method rather than IIF screening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: c-ANCA plus anti-PR3 equals GPA; p-ANCA plus anti-MPO equals MPA or eGPA. GPA classically involves upper respiratory tract, lungs, and kidneys. COMLEX: ANCA-associated vasculitides cause pauci-immune glomerulonephritis with few immune deposits on immunofluorescence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"}]},{"sdlNumber":45,"title":"DMARDs: Conventional & Biologic","questions":[{"id":"sdl45_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the conventional synthetic DMARDs from the biological DMARDs.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following agents is correctly classified as a conventional synthetic DMARD?","choices":{"A":"Adalimumab, a fully human IgG1 monoclonal antibody produced by recombinant DNA technology that neutralizes both soluble and membrane-bound TNF-alpha","B":"Methotrexate, a chemically synthesized small-molecule anti-metabolite administered orally or subcutaneously that inhibits folate-dependent enzymes","C":"Abatacept, a soluble CTLA-4 fusion protein produced by recombinant DNA technology that blocks CD28-mediated costimulation of T lymphocytes","D":"Tocilizumab, a humanized monoclonal antibody produced by recombinant technology that blocks both soluble and membrane-bound IL-6 receptors","E":"Rituximab, a chimeric anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody produced by recombinant technology that depletes mature B cells through multiple cytotoxic mechanisms"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Conventional synthetic DMARDs are chemically synthesized small molecules, including methotrexate, leflunomide, hydroxychloroquine, sulfasalazine, azathioprine, mycophenolate mofetil, and cyclophosphamide. Biological DMARDs are large proteins produced by recombinant DNA technology and include TNF inhibitors, IL-6 receptor antagonists, abatacept, rituximab, and belimumab. JAK inhibitors are targeted synthetic DMARDs, a distinct subclass of small-molecule agents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: csDMARDs = small molecules; bDMARDs = recombinant proteins. Methotrexate is the anchor csDMARD for RA. COMLEX: First-line RA treatment uses methotrexate plus hydroxychloroquine plus sulfasalazine as triple therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — T and B cell activation mechanisms underpin the targets of biological DMARDs: abatacept blocks CD28 costimulation, rituximab depletes CD20-positive B cells, and belimumab blocks BLyS-dependent B cell survival, while conventional synthetic DMARDs use small-molecule chemistry to broadly suppress proliferating immune cells."},{"id":"sdl45_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the conventional synthetic DMARDs from the biological DMARDs.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the fundamental distinction between biological DMARDs and conventional synthetic DMARDs?","choices":{"A":"Biological DMARDs are large-molecule proteins produced by recombinant DNA technology that target specific immune mediators, whereas conventional synthetic DMARDs are chemically synthesized small molecules","B":"Biological DMARDs are exclusively given intravenously and always require infusion center administration, while conventional synthetic DMARDs are always taken as daily oral tablets","C":"Biological DMARDs have shorter half-lives than conventional synthetic DMARDs and require more frequent daily dosing to maintain adequate therapeutic levels","D":"Biological DMARDs act broadly across all inflammatory pathways without selectivity, while conventional synthetic DMARDs each target only a single cytokine or receptor","E":"Conventional synthetic DMARDs require mandatory tuberculosis screening before initiation, while biological DMARDs carry no risk of infectious reactivation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The defining distinction is method of production and molecular size. Biological DMARDs are recombinant proteins targeting specific immune molecules such as TNF-alpha, IL-6 receptors, CD20, CD28, or BLyS. Conventional synthetic DMARDs are small chemically synthesized molecules with broader metabolic mechanisms. Both classes require TB screening. Many biologics are available subcutaneously as well as intravenously.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bDMARDs target specific immune proteins; csDMARDs are synthetic small molecules. All classes require TB and hepatitis B screening before initiation. COMLEX: Biosimilar bDMARDs have equivalent efficacy and toxicity to original biologics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — T and B cell activation mechanisms underpin the targets of biological DMARDs: abatacept blocks CD28 costimulation, rituximab depletes CD20-positive B cells, and belimumab blocks BLyS-dependent B cell survival, while conventional synthetic DMARDs use small-molecule chemistry to broadly suppress proliferating immune cells."},{"id":"sdl45_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the conventional synthetic DMARDs from the biological DMARDs.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Tofacitinib is approved for rheumatoid arthritis and was initially marketed alongside biological agents. It is correctly classified as which type of DMARD?","choices":{"A":"A conventional synthetic DMARD equivalent in classification to methotrexate and leflunomide, because it is not produced by recombinant DNA technology","B":"A biosimilar DMARD approved as a lower-cost alternative to abatacept, sharing its mechanism of T cell costimulation blockade at reduced production cost","C":"A targeted synthetic DMARD, because it is a chemically synthesized small molecule that selectively inhibits JAK kinases rather than a recombinant protein","D":"A biological original DMARD produced by recombinant DNA technology, because it was the first agent approved specifically targeting the JAK-STAT pathway","E":"A hybrid DMARD occupying a distinct regulatory class between conventional synthetic and biological agents defined by its intracellular enzymatic mechanism"},"correct":"C","explanation":"JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, baricitinib, upadacitinib) are targeted synthetic DMARDs because they are small molecules synthesized chemically, not recombinant proteins. Despite targeting specific intracellular kinases, they are chemically rather than biologically derived. This places them in their own tsDMARD category distinct from both csDMARDs and bDMARDs. They are not biosimilars, which are approved copies of existing biologic reference products.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tsDMARDs = small-molecule targeted agents; JAK inhibitors are the main tsDMARD class in rheumatology. COMLEX: JAK inhibitors carry FDA black box warnings for VTE, MACE, and malignancy not shared with all other DMARD classes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — T and B cell activation mechanisms underpin the targets of biological DMARDs: abatacept blocks CD28 costimulation, rituximab depletes CD20-positive B cells, and belimumab blocks BLyS-dependent B cell survival, while conventional synthetic DMARDs use small-molecule chemistry to broadly suppress proliferating immune cells."},{"id":"sdl45_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional synthetic DMARDs: TNF-alpha inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, Janus Kinase inhibitors, T cell activation inhibitors, Interferon blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, B cell inhibitors, IL-23 inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors, antimalarials, IL-1 inhibitors, mycophenolate mofetil, anti-metabolites.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary mechanism of action of low-dose methotrexate in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of dihydrofolate reductase as the primary mechanism at rheumatic doses, leading to reduced tetrahydrofolate and impaired nucleotide synthesis","B":"Inhibition of inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase, selectively blocking guanosine synthesis and preferentially suppressing T and B lymphocyte proliferation","C":"Inhibition of dihydroorotate dehydrogenase, reducing pyrimidine synthesis and arresting proliferating lymphocytes in the G1 phase of the cell cycle","D":"Inhibition of AICAR transformylase leading to extracellular AMP accumulation, which suppresses neutrophil, macrophage, dendritic cell, and lymphocyte function broadly","E":"Inhibition of calcineurin phosphatase, preventing NFAT nuclear translocation and selectively reducing IL-2, IL-3, IL-4, and TNF-alpha gene transcription"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At low rheumatic doses, methotrexate primarily inhibits AICAR transformylase and thymidylate synthetase, leading to extracellular adenosine accumulation. Adenosine is broadly anti-inflammatory, suppressing neutrophil chemotaxis and macrophage, dendritic cell, and lymphocyte function. The dihydrofolate reductase mechanism is relatively minor at these doses and is more relevant to oncologic uses. Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase is inhibited by leflunomide; IMPDH is inhibited by mycophenolate; calcineurin is inhibited by cyclosporine and tacrolimus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MTX at low rheumatic doses works via AICAR/adenosine accumulation, not primarily DHFR. Folic acid supplementation reduces toxicity with minimal efficacy loss. COMLEX: MTX is contraindicated in pregnancy; use teratogenicity counseling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — Cytokine functions define the molecular rationale for biologic DMARD targets: TNF-alpha drives synovial inflammation in RA, IL-6 stimulates acute-phase proteins and osteoclastogenesis, IL-17 promotes neutrophil recruitment in spondyloarthritis, and IL-23 sustains Th17 differentiation in psoriatic disease."},{"id":"sdl45_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional synthetic DMARDs: TNF-alpha inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, Janus Kinase inhibitors, T cell activation inhibitors, Interferon blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, B cell inhibitors, IL-23 inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors, antimalarials, IL-1 inhibitors, mycophenolate mofetil, anti-metabolites.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the mechanism by which mycophenolate mofetil exerts its immunosuppressive effects?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of AICAR transformylase, leading to extracellular AMP accumulation that broadly suppresses neutrophil chemotaxis and macrophage activation","B":"Inhibition of dihydroorotate dehydrogenase via the active metabolite A77 1726, blocking pyrimidine synthesis and arresting lymphocytes in G1 phase","C":"Inhibition of inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase, blocking guanosine nucleotide synthesis and selectively suppressing T and B lymphocyte proliferation","D":"Inhibition of calcineurin phosphatase activity, preventing NFAT nuclear translocation and reducing transcription of IL-2 and other T cell survival cytokines","E":"Blockade of CD28-mediated T cell costimulation by competitively binding CD80 and CD86 on antigen-presenting cells to prevent full T cell activation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mycophenolate mofetil is a prodrug converted to mycophenolic acid, which inhibits IMPDH. This selectively blocks de novo guanosine synthesis, which T and B lymphocytes depend on because they lack the salvage pathway used by other cells. Downstream, MMF also inhibits leukocyte adhesion by reducing E-selectin, P-selectin, and ICAM expression. AICAR transformylase is the methotrexate target. Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase is the leflunomide target. Calcineurin is blocked by cyclosporine and tacrolimus. CD28 blockade is abatacept.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MMF = IMPDH inhibitor; selectively suppresses lymphocytes. Used for lupus nephritis and SSc ILD. COMLEX: MMF is contraindicated in pregnancy; switch to azathioprine for pregnant SLE patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — Cytokine functions define the molecular rationale for biologic DMARD targets: TNF-alpha drives synovial inflammation in RA, IL-6 stimulates acute-phase proteins and osteoclastogenesis, IL-17 promotes neutrophil recruitment in spondyloarthritis, and IL-23 sustains Th17 differentiation in psoriatic disease."},{"id":"sdl45_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional synthetic DMARDs: TNF-alpha inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, Janus Kinase inhibitors, T cell activation inhibitors, Interferon blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, B cell inhibitors, IL-23 inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors, antimalarials, IL-1 inhibitors, mycophenolate mofetil, anti-metabolites.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the mechanism of action of leflunomide in rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of dihydroorotate dehydrogenase via its active metabolite A77 1726, blocking pyrimidine de novo synthesis and arresting activated lymphocytes in G1 phase","B":"Inhibition of AICAR transformylase leading to extracellular AMP accumulation that broadly suppresses innate and adaptive immune cell proliferation and function","C":"Inhibition of inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase, depleting guanosine nucleotides and preferentially blocking T and B lymphocyte de novo nucleotide synthesis","D":"Inhibition of calcineurin phosphatase, blocking NFAT nuclear translocation and selectively reducing transcription of IL-2 and pro-inflammatory cytokine genes","E":"Inhibition of CD20 on B cell surfaces, triggering complement-dependent cytotoxicity and antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity to deplete B lymphocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leflunomide is a prodrug rapidly converted to its active metabolite A77 1726 in the intestines and plasma. A77 1726 inhibits dihydroorotate dehydrogenase, the rate-limiting enzyme in pyrimidine synthesis, arresting proliferating cells including lymphocytes in G1. It also decreases TNF-alpha-dependent NF-kB activation. Leflunomide has approximately equal efficacy to methotrexate in RA. The other mechanisms described belong to methotrexate, mycophenolate, calcineurin inhibitors, and rituximab respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leflunomide inhibits dihydroorotate dehydrogenase and equals MTX efficacy in RA. Active metabolite A77 1726 has a half-life of 19 days. COMLEX: Teratogenic; washout with cholestyramine required if pregnancy is planned.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — Cytokine functions define the molecular rationale for biologic DMARD targets: TNF-alpha drives synovial inflammation in RA, IL-6 stimulates acute-phase proteins and osteoclastogenesis, IL-17 promotes neutrophil recruitment in spondyloarthritis, and IL-23 sustains Th17 differentiation in psoriatic disease."},{"id":"sdl45_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the most common adverse effects by class of treatment.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following adverse effects is covered by the FDA black box warning that applies to all three approved JAK inhibitors used in rheumatic diseases?","choices":{"A":"Reactivation of latent tuberculosis as the primary concern, requiring monthly chest radiograph monitoring and isoniazid prophylaxis in all patients","B":"Increased risk of serious infections, venous thromboembolism, major adverse cardiovascular events, and malignancy based on postmarketing safety data","C":"Hepatitis B reactivation causing fulminant hepatic failure, requiring serologic screening and prophylactic antiviral therapy before treatment initiation","D":"Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy from JC virus reactivation, particularly in patients with long-term use and low CD4 lymphocyte counts","E":"Irreversible retinopathy causing central scotoma and color vision loss, requiring annual ophthalmologic examination with spectral-domain OCT imaging"},"correct":"B","explanation":"All JAK inhibitors carry an FDA black box warning for serious infections, VTE including DVT and PE, MACE, and malignancy. This originated from a large postmarketing study of tofacitinib that showed dose-dependent cardiovascular and thrombotic events. Herpes zoster reactivation is a notable infection risk for JAK inhibitors. TB screening is required before initiation, but TB reactivation is not the primary black box concern as it is for TNF inhibitors. Hepatitis B reactivation is the signature risk of rituximab. PML is associated with natalizumab. Retinopathy is the signature toxicity of hydroxychloroquine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: JAK inhibitor black box = VTE plus MACE plus malignancy plus serious infections. Herpes zoster risk is distinctively elevated versus other DMARD classes. COMLEX: JAK inhibitors require lipid monitoring due to dose-dependent LDL elevation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Adverse effects of DMARDs directly reflect disrupted immune tolerance mechanisms: TNF inhibitors reactivate latent TB by impairing granuloma maintenance, rituximab reactivates hepatitis B by depleting B cell immunity, and JAK inhibitors increase herpes zoster risk by impairing cytokine-dependent viral surveillance."},{"id":"sdl45_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the most common adverse effects by class of treatment.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the most serious long-term adverse effect of hydroxychloroquine used in rheumatic diseases?","choices":{"A":"Nephrotoxicity with progressive glomerular filtration rate decline, requiring regular serum creatinine monitoring and dose adjustment in renal impairment","B":"Hepatotoxicity with elevation of aminotransferases above three times normal, requiring dose reduction and consideration of drug discontinuation","C":"Retinopathy causing irreversible parafoveal photoreceptor damage, occurring in less than 1% of patients at recommended doses within the first five years of use","D":"Bone marrow suppression causing neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, and macrocytic anemia requiring complete blood count monitoring at each clinical visit","E":"Pulmonary toxicity causing progressive interstitial pneumonitis, presenting with dyspnea, dry cough, and bilateral ground-glass infiltrates on chest CT"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hydroxychloroquine retinopathy is dose-dependent and typically occurs at doses exceeding 5 mg per kg per day. At recommended doses of 5 mg per kg per day or less, retinopathy risk is below 1% within 5 years and below 2% within 10 years, increasing thereafter. Retinopathy is irreversible, so annual ophthalmologic screening with automated visual fields and spectral-domain OCT is recommended. HCQ does not cause nephrotoxicity, hepatotoxicity at standard doses, or pulmonary toxicity. Bone marrow suppression is associated with methotrexate and azathioprine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HCQ retinopathy is dose-dependent and irreversible; screen annually with visual fields and OCT. Safer in pregnancy than most other DMARDs. COMLEX: HCQ also improves lipid profiles and reduces cardiovascular risk in SLE and RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Adverse effects of DMARDs directly reflect disrupted immune tolerance mechanisms: TNF inhibitors reactivate latent TB by impairing granuloma maintenance, rituximab reactivates hepatitis B by depleting B cell immunity, and JAK inhibitors increase herpes zoster risk by impairing cytokine-dependent viral surveillance."},{"id":"sdl45_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the most common adverse effects by class of treatment.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best summarizes the major class adverse effects shared by all five approved TNF-alpha inhibitors used in rheumatic diseases?","choices":{"A":"Venous thromboembolism, major adverse cardiovascular events, dose-dependent hyperlipidemia, and herpes zoster reactivation as the primary shared class risks","B":"Hepatitis B reactivation without risk of tuberculosis reactivation, progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy, and infusion-related hypersensitivity reactions","C":"Retinopathy causing parafoveal visual loss, drug-induced lupus with anti-histone antibodies, and reversible male infertility during active therapy","D":"Reactivation of latent tuberculosis and deep fungal infections, worsening or new-onset congestive heart failure, demyelinating disease, and lupus-like syndrome","E":"Injection-site reactions in up to 40% of treated patients, depression and suicidality, and gastrointestinal perforations in patients with prior diverticulitis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"TNF-alpha inhibitors share a class adverse effect profile including serious infections with particular risk of latent TB reactivation and deep fungal infections, worsening of CHF at moderate to severe grades, new or worsening demyelinating disease, possible lymphoma risk, headache, rash, and drug-induced lupus-like syndrome. VTE and MACE are the signature black box concern for JAK inhibitors, not TNF inhibitors. Hepatitis B reactivation without TB risk is characteristic of rituximab. Retinopathy is associated with hydroxychloroquine. Injection site reactions in 40% and depression are associated with IL-1 inhibitors and belimumab respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF inhibitor class risks: TB reactivation plus fungal infections plus CHF worsening plus demyelination plus lupus-like syndrome. Screen all patients for latent TB before initiation. COMLEX: Contraindicated in moderate to severe CHF, active demyelinating disease, and active serious infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Adverse effects of DMARDs directly reflect disrupted immune tolerance mechanisms: TNF inhibitors reactivate latent TB by impairing granuloma maintenance, rituximab reactivates hepatitis B by depleting B cell immunity, and JAK inhibitors increase herpes zoster risk by impairing cytokine-dependent viral surveillance."},{"id":"sdl45_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the conventional synthetic DMARDs from the biological DMARDs.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with recently diagnosed seropositive rheumatoid arthritis has active synovitis in multiple small joints, morning stiffness lasting 90 minutes, elevated CRP, and elevated anti-CCP titer. She has no prior DMARD exposure. Which of the following represents the most appropriate first-line disease-modifying treatment for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Methotrexate, the preferred first-line csDMARD for RA with proven efficacy in reducing synovitis, inhibiting joint erosion, and enabling future biologic escalation if needed","B":"Adalimumab, a TNF-alpha inhibitor biologic that is the most appropriate initial choice in newly diagnosed seropositive RA before any csDMARD trial","C":"Abatacept, a T cell costimulation blocker indicated as first-line monotherapy in seropositive RA due to its superior mechanism in ACPA-positive patients","D":"Rituximab, a B cell depleting biologic recommended as initial therapy in seropositive RA due to the pathogenic role of anti-CCP antibodies in this disease","E":"Tocilizumab, an IL-6 receptor antagonist preferred over methotrexate as initial therapy due to its rapid and superior normalization of inflammatory markers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Methotrexate is the anchor csDMARD for RA and is the preferred first-line agent per ACR guidelines. It can be used as monotherapy or as the backbone for combination therapy with other csDMARDs or biologics. Biologic DMARDs such as TNF inhibitors, abatacept, rituximab, and IL-6 inhibitors are indicated for patients with inadequate response to methotrexate, not as first-line therapy. Triple csDMARD therapy with methotrexate, hydroxychloroquine, and sulfasalazine can match biologic efficacy in some patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MTX is the first-line csDMARD for RA. Biologics are added or substituted when csDMARD response is inadequate. COMLEX: 2015 ACR guidelines strongly recommend combination traditional DMARDs or addition of biologics for moderate-to-high disease activity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Mechanisms Involved in Activating T Cells and B Cells"},{"id":"sdl45_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the conventional synthetic DMARDs from the biological DMARDs.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with seropositive RA on methotrexate 20 mg weekly has persistent synovitis. His rheumatologist adds abatacept. The patient asks how this medication works differently from methotrexate. Which of the following correctly describes abatacept's mechanism of immunosuppression?","choices":{"A":"Abatacept is a fully human IgG1 antibody that binds both soluble and membrane-bound TNF-alpha, preventing engagement with its p55 and p75 membrane receptors","B":"Abatacept binds the IL-6 receptor on hepatocytes and synovial cells, blocking downstream JAK-STAT3 signaling and reducing acute-phase protein production","C":"Abatacept is a CTLA-4-Ig fusion protein that binds CD80 and CD86 on antigen-presenting cells, blocking the CD28 costimulatory signal required for full T cell activation","D":"Abatacept is an anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody that depletes mature circulating B cells through complement-dependent cytotoxicity and direct apoptosis induction","E":"Abatacept selectively inhibits JAK1 and JAK2 kinases intracellularly, blocking cytokine receptor signaling and reducing pro-inflammatory mediator transcription"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Abatacept is a soluble fusion protein composed of the Fc region of human IgG1 fused to the extracellular domain of CTLA-4. CTLA-4 binds CD80 and CD86 on antigen-presenting cells with higher affinity than CD28, blocking the costimulatory signal that T cells require for full activation alongside TCR engagement. Without costimulation, T cells become anergic rather than activated. This mechanism is entirely different from TNF inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, anti-CD20 antibodies, or JAK inhibitors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Abatacept = CTLA-4-Ig = blocks CD28 costimulation = T cell anergy. This is the T cell activation inhibitor class in the DMARD classification. COMLEX: Abatacept has slightly lower TB reactivation risk than TNF inhibitors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Mechanisms Involved in Activating T Cells and B Cells"},{"id":"sdl45_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the conventional synthetic DMARDs from the biological DMARDs.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with SLE and persistent active disease on hydroxychloroquine and mycophenolate mofetil is started on belimumab after two major flares in the past year. Which of the following correctly identifies belimumab's target and mechanism of action?","choices":{"A":"Belimumab binds the IL-6 receptor on B cells, blocking STAT3 activation and suppressing B cell differentiation into antibody-secreting plasma cells","B":"Belimumab is a CTLA-4 fusion protein that blocks CD28 costimulation on autoreactive T helper cells, preventing their activation by self-antigen-loaded APCs","C":"Belimumab blocks the type I interferon receptor on plasmacytoid dendritic cells, reducing IFN-alpha-driven upregulation of toll-like receptor pathways","D":"Belimumab is an anti-CD20 antibody that selectively depletes mature circulating B cells while preserving naive and transitional B cell populations","E":"Belimumab is a fully human IgG1 antibody that inhibits soluble B-lymphocyte stimulator protein, reducing B cell survival, maturation, and autoantibody production in SLE"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Belimumab is a fully human IgG1 monoclonal antibody that selectively inhibits soluble B-lymphocyte stimulator protein, also called BLyS or BAFF. BLyS promotes B cell survival, differentiation, and class switching. By blocking BLyS, belimumab reduces B cell counts, lowers autoantibody titers, and decreases complement consumption in SLE. It is approved for SLE and active lupus nephritis in patients on background standard-of-care therapy. It should not be combined with other biologics or cyclophosphamide.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Belimumab targets BLyS/BAFF, a B cell survival factor; approved for SLE. Depression and suicidality are notable adverse effects requiring monitoring. COMLEX: Belimumab is approved for patients aged 5 and older with active SLE on standard-of-care therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Mechanisms Involved in Activating T Cells and B Cells"},{"id":"sdl45_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional synthetic DMARDs: TNF-alpha inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, Janus Kinase inhibitors, T cell activation inhibitors, Interferon blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, B cell inhibitors, IL-23 inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors, antimalarials, IL-1 inhibitors, mycophenolate mofetil, anti-metabolites.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with granulomatosis with polyangiitis refractory to cyclophosphamide is started on rituximab combined with glucocorticoids. His rheumatologist explains how this agent works. Which of the following correctly describes rituximab's mechanism of action?","choices":{"A":"Rituximab is a fully human antibody that inhibits B-lymphocyte stimulator protein, blocking B cell survival signals and reducing autoantibody production in vasculitis","B":"Rituximab is a chimeric anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody that depletes mature B cells through complement-dependent cytotoxicity, antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity, and direct apoptosis","C":"Rituximab inhibits IMPDH in activated B cells, depleting guanosine nucleotides and selectively blocking B cell clonal expansion without depleting resting B cells","D":"Rituximab is a bispecific antibody targeting both CD20 on B cells and CD3 on T cells, enabling T cell-mediated killing of autoreactive B lymphocytes","E":"Rituximab binds the IL-6 receptor on activated B cells, preventing downstream STAT3 signaling and blocking B cell differentiation into antibody-secreting plasma cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rituximab is a chimeric monoclonal antibody targeting CD20, a transmembrane phosphoprotein expressed on mature B cells but absent on stem cells and plasma cells. Binding triggers rapid depletion of peripheral and synovial B cells through complement-dependent cytotoxicity, ADCC via NK cells, and direct apoptosis. Depletion of synovial tissue B cells correlates with clinical response in RA. RTX is approved for RA with methotrexate and for GPA and MPA with glucocorticoids. Anti-BLyS is belimumab; IMPDH is MMF; bispecific CD20/CD3 antibodies are a separate oncology agent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rituximab = anti-CD20 = B cell depletion via complement plus ADCC plus apoptosis. CD20 is absent on plasma cells so autoantibody production may not fully normalize. COMLEX: Indicated for RA after TNF inhibitor failure and for GPA and MPA in combination with glucocorticoids.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines and Their Roles in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl45_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional synthetic DMARDs: TNF-alpha inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, Janus Kinase inhibitors, T cell activation inhibitors, Interferon blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, B cell inhibitors, IL-23 inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors, antimalarials, IL-1 inhibitors, mycophenolate mofetil, anti-metabolites.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman with active RA on methotrexate is started on tocilizumab. Six weeks later she develops fever, productive cough, and tachycardia. Her CRP returns at 0.3 mg per liter. Which of the following best explains why CRP is within normal limits despite this active respiratory infection?","choices":{"A":"Tocilizumab depletes circulating B cells, reducing immunoglobulin production and thereby suppressing acute-phase reactant synthesis as a secondary effect","B":"Tocilizumab blocks TNF-alpha, removing the primary upstream cytokine responsible for inducing IL-6 secretion and downstream hepatic CRP production","C":"Methotrexate inhibits AICAR transformylase in hepatocytes, leading to AMP accumulation that directly suppresses all acute-phase protein synthesis","D":"Tocilizumab blocks the IL-6 receptor on hepatocytes, preventing IL-6-driven CRP synthesis regardless of the presence or severity of active infection","E":"Tocilizumab selectively inhibits JAK1 kinase intracellularly, blocking the signaling cascade that drives all cytokine-induced acute-phase protein production"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tocilizumab is an IL-6 receptor antagonist that blocks both soluble and membrane-bound IL-6 receptors. Because IL-6 is the primary driver of hepatic CRP synthesis, its blockade causes CRP to remain low or undetectable even during active infection. This creates a clinical blind spot where CRP cannot be relied upon to monitor for infection. Clinicians must rely on fever, clinical examination, procalcitonin, and imaging to detect infection in patients on IL-6 inhibitors. Sarilumab has the same limitation. Tocilizumab does not deplete B cells, block TNF-alpha, or inhibit JAK kinases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-6 inhibitors suppress CRP independently of infection status, masking infection detection. Use procalcitonin and clinical assessment. COMLEX: Tocilizumab is also approved for giant cell arteritis, cytokine release syndrome, and severe COVID-19.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines and Their Roles in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl45_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional synthetic DMARDs: TNF-alpha inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, Janus Kinase inhibitors, T cell activation inhibitors, Interferon blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, B cell inhibitors, IL-23 inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors, antimalarials, IL-1 inhibitors, mycophenolate mofetil, anti-metabolites.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with moderate psoriatic arthritis and psoriatic skin plaques develops prominent diarrhea and nausea shortly after starting a new oral small-molecule agent. His medication increases intracellular cAMP levels in immune cells and inhibits production of TNF-alpha, IL-8, IL-12, IL-17, and IL-23. Which of the following agents and mechanism is most consistent with this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Baricitinib, a JAK1 and JAK2 inhibitor whose most prominent adverse effects include infection risk, VTE, and dose-dependent hyperlipidemia rather than early GI symptoms","B":"Leflunomide, a dihydroorotate dehydrogenase inhibitor that can cause diarrhea but which does not work through cAMP elevation or PDE4 inhibition","C":"Tofacitinib, a JAK1 and JAK3 inhibitor whose primary adverse effects include infection risk, VTE, and MACE rather than prominent nausea and diarrhea","D":"Upadacitinib, a selective JAK1 inhibitor whose most prominent adverse effects include serious infections, herpes zoster, and elevated creatine kinase levels","E":"Apremilast, a PDE4 inhibitor that prevents cAMP degradation in immune cells, reducing transcription of multiple pro-inflammatory cytokines through PKA activation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Apremilast is an oral PDE4 inhibitor that increases intracellular cAMP by blocking its degradation. Elevated cAMP activates protein kinase A, which reduces transcription of multiple pro-inflammatory cytokines including TNF-alpha, IL-8, IL-12, IL-17, IL-23, IFN-gamma, and others. The most common adverse effects are GI: diarrhea (predominant), nausea, upper abdominal pain, vomiting, and upper respiratory infections. GI symptoms typically improve over the first month. It is approved for psoriatic arthritis, plaque psoriasis, and oral ulcers in Behcet disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Apremilast = PDE4 inhibitor = elevated cAMP = reduced cytokine production. GI side effects are the most common. COMLEX: Apremilast is a small oral molecule approved for psoriatic arthritis without requiring TB screening, unlike biologics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines and Their Roles in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl45_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the most common adverse effects by class of treatment.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with RA refractory to methotrexate and two TNF inhibitors is being considered for rituximab. Her rheumatologist reviews the unique infectious risk profile of rituximab compared to TNF-alpha inhibitors. Which of the following best describes this distinction?","choices":{"A":"Rituximab carries the same risk of latent tuberculosis reactivation as TNF inhibitors, requiring identical screening, prophylaxis, and ongoing chest radiograph monitoring","B":"Rituximab is associated with hepatitis B reactivation but, unlike TNF inhibitors, has not been associated with reactivation of latent tuberculosis or increased lymphoma risk","C":"Rituximab causes progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy from JC virus reactivation as its primary infectious complication, replacing the TB risk seen with TNF inhibitors","D":"Rituximab is associated with herpes zoster reactivation as its most distinctive infectious risk, analogous to the pattern seen with JAK inhibitor use in RA","E":"Rituximab causes deep endemic fungal infections and histoplasmosis as its primary risk, replacing the bacterial infection pattern typical of TNF inhibitor therapy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rituximab is uniquely associated with hepatitis B reactivation because it depletes CD20-positive B cells including memory B cells that harbor latent HBV. Hepatitis B surface antigen and core antibody screening is required before initiation, and prophylactic antiviral therapy should be given. In contrast to TNF inhibitors, rituximab has not been associated with reactivation of latent TB or de novo lymphoma. PML from JC virus has been reported but is extremely rare. Herpes zoster is the signature infectious risk of JAK inhibitors, not rituximab.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rituximab = hepatitis B reactivation risk, not TB. TNF inhibitors = TB reactivation risk. Both require screening before initiation. COMLEX: Screen for HBsAg and anti-HBc before rituximab; treat positive patients with prophylactic antivirals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl45_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the most common adverse effects by class of treatment.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with RA on methotrexate 20 mg weekly develops painful oral ulcers, mild hair thinning, and fatigue over three weeks. CBC shows hemoglobin of 10.6 g per dL with elevated mean corpuscular volume. Which of the following best explains the mechanism underlying these toxicities?","choices":{"A":"Methotrexate inhibits folate-dependent enzymes, impairing nucleotide synthesis and reducing proliferation of rapidly dividing mucosal epithelial, hematopoietic, and follicular cells","B":"Methotrexate accumulates in hepatocytes and inhibits mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase, causing direct hepatocyte necrosis and secondary hematopoietic suppression","C":"Methotrexate blocks pyrimidine synthesis through dihydroorotate dehydrogenase inhibition, causing mucosal cell atrophy and megaloblastic changes in bone marrow","D":"Methotrexate inhibits calcineurin in mucosal epithelial cells, reducing IL-2 production and causing immune-mediated apoptosis of rapidly regenerating surface cells","E":"Methotrexate depletes guanosine nucleotides through IMPDH inhibition, blocking DNA synthesis specifically in lymphocytes, follicular cells, and enterocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Methotrexate inhibits thymidylate synthetase and AICAR transformylase, ultimately reducing folate-dependent nucleotide synthesis. Rapidly dividing cells including mucosal epithelium, hematopoietic precursors, and hair follicles are most vulnerable, producing stomatitis, macrocytic anemia, leukopenia, and alopecia. Supplemental folic acid reduces these toxicities by providing substrate that bypasses the inhibited enzymes, with a modest 10 to 18% reduction in efficacy. Leucovorin is used for acute toxicity rescue. MTX is contraindicated in pregnancy because of these antiproliferative effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MTX toxicities reflect antifolate effects on dividing cells: stomatitis, alopecia, macrocytic anemia, leukopenia. Folic acid supplementation reduces but does not eliminate toxicity. COMLEX: Leucovorin rescues acute MTX toxicity; folic acid is daily prophylaxis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl45_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the most common adverse effects by class of treatment.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis and peripheral joint involvement is started on sulfasalazine. He and his partner are planning to conceive within the next year. Which of the following correctly describes the most important reproductive consideration for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Sulfasalazine is teratogenic in both sexes and must be discontinued at least six months before any conception attempt by either the male or female partner","B":"Sulfasalazine irreversibly reduces testosterone production by inhibiting Leydig cell steroidogenesis, causing permanent hypogonadism after prolonged use","C":"Sulfasalazine impairs female fertility primarily by inducing anovulation and luteal phase dysfunction, requiring hormonal supplementation throughout therapy","D":"Sulfasalazine is the safest available DMARD in pregnancy for both sexes with no reproductive effects, making it the preferred agent in patients planning to conceive","E":"Sulfasalazine causes reversible male infertility through impaired sperm motility and morphology that resolves after the drug is discontinued"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sulfasalazine causes reversible oligospermia and impaired sperm motility and morphology in men, thought to be due to the sulfapyridine moiety. This effect resolves within months of discontinuation. Sulfasalazine does not affect female fertility and is relatively safe in pregnancy. It does not cause irreversible hypogonadism or testosterone suppression. This reversibility is clinically important because the patient can discontinue the drug when actively attempting conception and resume therapy afterward if needed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sulfasalazine causes reversible male infertility; safe in female patients and pregnancy. Does not cause irreversible testosterone suppression. COMLEX: Sulfasalazine is effective in RA, reactive arthritis, and peripheral joint disease in ankylosing spondylitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"}]},{"sdlNumber":46,"title":"Spondyloarthritis Pathogenesis","questions":[{"id":"sdl46_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the genetic contribution to disease in spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which HLA allele is most strongly associated with ankylosing spondylitis, and in approximately what proportion of AS patients is this allele detected?","choices":{"A":"HLA-DR4 is the primary susceptibility allele for ankylosing spondylitis and is found in approximately 70-80% of affected patients, conferring an odds ratio similar to its association with rheumatoid arthritis.","B":"HLA-B27 is the primary susceptibility allele for ankylosing spondylitis and is present in approximately 85-95% of affected patients, conferring an odds ratio of 100-200 for disease development.","C":"HLA-DR3 is the primary susceptibility allele for ankylosing spondylitis and is expressed in approximately 50-60% of affected patients, particularly in those with associated IBD or uveitis complications.","D":"HLA-B51 is the primary susceptibility allele for ankylosing spondylitis and is identified in approximately 65-75% of affected patients, particularly those with sacroiliac joint involvement and aortitis.","E":"HLA-DQ2 is the primary susceptibility allele for ankylosing spondylitis and is detected in approximately 55-65% of affected patients, conferring risk through MHC class II-mediated CD4+ T cell activation."},"correct":"B","explanation":"HLA-B27 is the dominant genetic risk factor for ankylosing spondylitis (AS), present in 85-95% of AS patients versus approximately 8% of the general population, yielding an odds ratio of 100-200. HLA-DR4 is associated with rheumatoid arthritis, not AS. HLA-B51 is associated with Behcet disease. HLA-DQ2 is associated with celiac disease and type 1 diabetes. HLA-DR3 is associated with SLE and Sjogren syndrome. The seronegative spondyloarthropathies (AS, PsA, reactive arthritis, IBD-associated SpA) all share HLA-B27 association.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HLA-B27 is the classic board association for AS — memorize odds ratio 100-200 and prevalence in AS (85-95%). COMLEX: Seronegative spondyloarthropathy triad: HLA-B27 + RF-negative + axial/sacroiliac involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA alleles such as HLA-B27 are among the strongest MHC-I susceptibility alleles known, conferring genetic risk for spondyloarthritis through antigen-presentation and misfolding mechanisms."},{"id":"sdl46_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the genetic contribution to disease in spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which non-MHC gene polymorphism most significantly alters Th17 cell differentiation and has been identified as a shared risk locus across ankylosing spondylitis, psoriasis, and inflammatory bowel disease?","choices":{"A":"NOD2 polymorphisms directly reduce intestinal innate immune receptor function through impaired NLR-mediated pathogen sensing, producing gut dysbiosis and bacterial translocation that seeds entheseal inflammation in all three conditions.","B":"ERAP1 polymorphisms reduce aminopeptidase efficiency in peptide trimming for MHC class I loading, increasing misfolded HLA-B27 complex formation in antigen-presenting cells and driving T cell-mediated inflammation in all three conditions.","C":"IL23R polymorphisms alter receptor signaling that governs Th17 cell differentiation, linking shared genetic susceptibility across ankylosing spondylitis, psoriasis, and inflammatory bowel disease through the IL-23/Th17 axis.","D":"TNFRSF1A polymorphisms increase TNF receptor sensitivity on synoviocytes and intestinal epithelium, amplifying synovial inflammation and mucosal injury through enhanced TNF-alpha signaling in all three associated conditions.","E":"CARD15 polymorphisms impair NLR-mediated pathogen recognition in macrophages, directly promoting entheseal seeding from gut-derived bacteria and mucosal immune dysregulation shared across all three disease states."},"correct":"C","explanation":"IL23R polymorphisms are among the most consistently replicated non-MHC risk loci across the spondyloarthritis spectrum, psoriasis, and IBD because the IL-23/Th17 axis is central to all three conditions. IL23R variants alter signaling intensity on Th17 precursor cells, modifying the Th17/Treg balance. ERAP1 is also a SpA risk gene but acts through MHC-I peptide trimming specifically in HLA-B27 contexts. NOD2 mutations are primarily associated with Crohn disease but not the full SpA spectrum. TNFRSF1A mutations cause periodic fever syndromes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-MHC SpA risk genes — IL23R (Th17 axis), ERAP1 (peptide trimming for HLA-B27). COMLEX: The gut-joint axis in IBD-associated SpA is mediated through shared IL-23/IL-17 pathway genetics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA alleles such as HLA-B27 are among the strongest MHC-I susceptibility alleles known, conferring genetic risk for spondyloarthritis through antigen-presentation and misfolding mechanisms."},{"id":"sdl46_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the genetic contribution to disease in spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The arthritogenic peptide hypothesis is one proposed mechanism by which HLA-B27 contributes to spondyloarthritis pathogenesis. Which statement best describes this hypothesis?","choices":{"A":"HLA-B27 heavy chain homodimerization on cell surfaces activates NK cells and innate lymphoid cells through KIR3DL2 receptor binding, independent of any microbial antigen presentation or CD8+ T cell activation.","B":"HLA-B27 misfolding in the endoplasmic reticulum triggers the unfolded protein response, activating IRE1-alpha and NF-kB to induce IL-23 production that drives downstream Th17 differentiation independent of peptide presentation.","C":"HLA-B27 overexpression on the cell surface increases antigen presentation to regulatory T cells, reducing peripheral tolerance through exhaustion of the Treg compartment and enabling autoimmune entheseal inflammation.","D":"HLA-B27 allelic polymorphisms cause structural changes in the peptide-binding groove that prevent loading of microbial antigens entirely, triggering autoreactive T cell responses against empty MHC complexes on joint synoviocytes.","E":"HLA-B27 presents self-peptides or microbially cross-reactive peptides to autoreactive CD8+ T cells that then target joint tissue due to molecular mimicry between foreign antigens and self-peptides displayed on joint cells."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Three competing but not mutually exclusive hypotheses explain HLA-B27 pathogenicity: (1) Arthritogenic peptide: HLA-B27 presents cross-reactive peptides activating autoreactive CD8+ T cells via molecular mimicry. (2) Misfolding/UPR: HLA-B27 heavy chains misfold in the ER, activating UPR and IL-23 production. (3) Free heavy chain: HLA-B27 homodimers on the cell surface activate innate immune cells via KIR receptors. The arthritogenic peptide hypothesis is supported by evidence of bacterial triggers (Klebsiella, Chlamydia) sharing sequence homology with HLA-B27 or self-peptides it presents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know all three HLA-B27 disease mechanisms: arthritogenic peptide (molecular mimicry), UPR/misfolding (IL-23 induction), free heavy chain (innate activation). COMLEX: Seronegative SpA distinguishes from RA by RF negativity and HLA-B27 positivity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA alleles such as HLA-B27 are among the strongest MHC-I susceptibility alleles known, conferring genetic risk for spondyloarthritis through antigen-presentation and misfolding mechanisms."},{"id":"sdl46_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proinflammatory mediators associated with the inflammatory response in spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokine is the primary upstream driver of Th17 cell differentiation at entheseal sites and represents the key initiating signal in the IL-23/IL-17 axis governing spondyloarthritis inflammation?","choices":{"A":"IL-23 is produced by dendritic cells and macrophages and serves as the critical differentiation and survival signal for Th17 cells, initiating the IL-23/IL-17 inflammatory cascade at entheseal and synovial sites in spondyloarthritis.","B":"IL-6 serves as the primary driver of Th17 differentiation in spondyloarthritis by acting directly on naive T cells without TGF-beta co-stimulation, initiating the SpA inflammatory cascade exclusively at entheseal fibrocartilage insertion sites.","C":"IL-12 is the upstream Th17-polarizing cytokine in spondyloarthritis, activating STAT4 signaling on innate lymphoid cells and promoting IL-17 production through an IFN-gamma-independent pathway at sites of entheseal inflammation.","D":"TNF-alpha serves as the primary initiating cytokine of the IL-23/IL-17 axis by activating dendritic cells to produce IL-17 directly, bypassing the Th17 differentiation step at inflamed joint surfaces in spondyloarthritis.","E":"IFN-gamma drives Th17 differentiation from naive CD4+ T cells in spondyloarthritis, representing the key polarizing cytokine that initiates the downstream IL-17 response at sites of entheseal and sacroiliac joint inflammation."},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-23 (produced by dendritic cells and macrophages) is the master upstream regulator of Th17 differentiation and survival, acting through STAT3 to promote IL-17A and IL-17F production. IL-23/IL-17 axis is the dominant pathway in SpA entheseal inflammation. IL-6 combined with TGF-beta can also drive Th17 differentiation but is not the primary entheseal initiator. IL-12 drives Th1/IFN-gamma responses, not Th17. IFN-gamma actually inhibits Th17 differentiation. This axis is the target of ustekinumab (anti-p40, blocks IL-12 and IL-23), guselkumab (anti-IL-23 p19), secukinumab, and ixekizumab (anti-IL-17A).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-23 drives Th17; IL-17A drives entheseal inflammation. IL-12 drives Th1 not Th17. COMLEX: Th17 = joint inflammation; therapeutics target IL-23 (upstream) or IL-17A (downstream).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — Th17 cells are the primary producers of IL-17 in spondyloarthritis; dendritic cells and macrophages producing IL-23 are the upstream drivers of Th17 differentiation at entheseal sites."},{"id":"sdl46_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proinflammatory mediators associated with the inflammatory response in spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"IL-17A drives entheseal inflammation in spondyloarthritis through which primary downstream mechanism at sites of tendon and ligament insertion?","choices":{"A":"IL-17A directly activates osteoclasts through RANK-RANKL upregulation to produce progressive bone erosion exclusively at entheseal insertion sites, independent of secondary cytokine amplification by stromal cells.","B":"IL-17A cross-links complement components C3 and C5 at entheseal surfaces, generating membrane attack complexes that directly destroy fibrocartilage matrix proteins and produce the acute entheseal inflammatory response.","C":"IL-17A activates mast cell degranulation at entheseal sites through high-affinity IL-17RA binding, releasing preformed histamine and prostaglandin D2 granules that produce the primary acute inflammatory response.","D":"IL-17A binds directly to entheseal chondrocytes through a distinct receptor isoform, triggering caspase-3-mediated apoptosis and releasing DAMPs that initiate the sterile inflammatory cascade at fibrocartilage insertion zones.","E":"IL-17A stimulates resident fibroblasts and osteoblasts at entheseal insertion sites to produce TNF-alpha, IL-6, and neutrophil-attracting chemokines such as CXCL1 and CXCL5 that amplify and sustain local inflammation."},"correct":"E","explanation":"IL-17A acts on stromal cells (fibroblasts, osteoblasts, epithelial cells) at entheseal sites, inducing production of TNF-alpha, IL-6, CXCL1, CXCL5, and G-CSF/GM-CSF that recruit and activate neutrophils, amplifying the inflammatory response. This explains the dense neutrophilic infiltrate characteristic of enthesitis. IL-17A does eventually contribute to osteoclast activation and bone erosion, but this is secondary to the stromal cell response. The primary IL-17A receptor complex is IL-17RA/IL-17RC expressed on fibroblasts and epithelial cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-17A acts on stromal cells to produce secondary cytokines (TNF, IL-6) and neutrophil chemoattractants. Target of secukinumab and ixekizumab. COMLEX: Enthesitis (tendon/ligament insertion inflammation) is the hallmark of SpA distinguishing it from RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — Th17 cells are the primary producers of IL-17 in spondyloarthritis; dendritic cells and macrophages producing IL-23 are the upstream drivers of Th17 differentiation at entheseal sites."},{"id":"sdl46_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proinflammatory mediators associated with the inflammatory response in spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"TNF-alpha plays a central role in spondyloarthritis inflammation. Which statement best characterizes its contribution to structural joint damage in ankylosing spondylitis?","choices":{"A":"TNF-alpha primarily drives new bone formation at entheses by directly activating osteoblasts through the Wnt signaling pathway, producing the syndesmophyte formation and bamboo spine radiographic appearance in advanced AS.","B":"TNF-alpha acts exclusively through TNFR2 receptors on synoviocytes to produce cartilage degradation without contributing to systemic inflammatory markers such as CRP or ESR elevation in active spondyloarthritis.","C":"TNF-alpha directly activates Th17 differentiation from naive CD4+ T cells by upregulating IL-23 receptor expression, creating a positive feedback loop that sustains IL-17 production and entheseal inflammation in spondyloarthritis.","D":"TNF-alpha activates osteoclasts and promotes synoviocyte hyperplasia, driving bone erosion and cartilage degradation at the joint surface while also stimulating hepatic acute-phase reactants that sustain systemic inflammation.","E":"TNF-alpha functions exclusively as a systemic inflammatory mediator in spondyloarthritis with no direct structural role at the enthesis, serving only to maintain the febrile response and constitutional symptoms during flares."},"correct":"D","explanation":"TNF-alpha drives structural joint damage through osteoclast activation (RANK-RANKL upregulation), synoviocyte/pannus hyperplasia, and matrix metalloproteinase induction causing cartilage degradation. It also triggers hepatic CRP and fibrinogen synthesis. Paradoxically, TNF-alpha suppresses the Wnt pathway and BMP pathway, so it actually INHIBITS new bone formation — explaining why TNF inhibitor therapy does not reliably halt radiographic progression of syndesmophytes (new bone forms as the TNF suppression is relieved). This TNF-Wnt paradox is a classic board concept.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF inhibitors improve symptoms and inflammatory markers in AS but do not reliably halt syndesmophyte formation. COMLEX: TNF-Wnt paradox — TNF inhibits Wnt, so removing TNF may disinhibit new bone formation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — Th17 cells are the primary producers of IL-17 in spondyloarthritis; dendritic cells and macrophages producing IL-23 are the upstream drivers of Th17 differentiation at entheseal sites."},{"id":"sdl46_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the major elements in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Spondyloarthritis is classified as a seronegative arthropathy. Which laboratory finding defines this seronegativity and distinguishes SpA from rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Absence of anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies defines the seronegative classification of spondyloarthritis and is the most specific laboratory feature distinguishing SpA from rheumatoid arthritis on autoantibody panels.","B":"Absence of antinuclear antibodies on immunofluorescence assay defines the seronegative classification of spondyloarthritis, as ANA positivity is the hallmark of all seropositive inflammatory arthritides including RA.","C":"Absence of anti-double-stranded DNA antibodies in spondyloarthritis serum defines its seronegative classification, distinguishing it from the immune complex-mediated articular inflammation characteristic of rheumatoid disease.","D":"Absence of complement consumption markers C3 and C4 in spondyloarthritis serum defines its seronegative pattern, as RA consumes complement through synovial immune complex deposition while SpA does not activate the complement cascade.","E":"Absence of rheumatoid factor (RF) defines the seronegative classification of spondyloarthritis, distinguishing the entire SpA spectrum (AS, PsA, reactive arthritis, IBD-associated SpA) from seropositive rheumatoid arthritis."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Seronegative arthropathy specifically refers to the absence of rheumatoid factor (RF) in the spondyloarthritis spectrum (AS, PsA, reactive arthritis, IBD-associated SpA, undifferentiated SpA). RF is an IgM antibody directed against the Fc region of IgG and is the defining autoantibody of seropositive RA. Anti-CCP is also negative in SpA but is not what defines the seronegative classification historically. ANA and anti-dsDNA are not part of RA or SpA classification. The seronegative classification reflects the different immune pathogenesis (MHC class I/CD8+/Th17 in SpA versus MHC class II/CD4+/Th1/RF in RA).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Seronegative = RF negative. SpA triad: HLA-B27+, RF-, sacroiliac involvement. COMLEX: Distinguish seropositive RA (RF+, anti-CCP+, symmetric small joints, pannus) from seronegative SpA (RF-, HLA-B27+, axial/entheseal).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — TNF-alpha inhibitors and anti-IL-17A biologics (secukinumab, ixekizumab) directly target the proinflammatory mediators that drive the major pathogenic elements of spondyloarthritis."},{"id":"sdl46_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the major elements in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which feature of inflammatory back pain in ankylosing spondylitis most reliably distinguishes it from mechanical low back pain and reflects the underlying cytokine-driven pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"Inflammatory back pain in AS is provoked by rotational movements and weight-bearing exercise, reflecting sacroiliac joint capsule destruction with asymmetric articular erosion causing mechanical instability at the pelvic girdle level.","B":"Inflammatory back pain in AS radiates into both lower extremities in a dermatomal distribution, reflecting bilateral nerve root compression at L4-L5 levels from inflammatory osteophyte formation at the posterior vertebral elements.","C":"Inflammatory back pain in AS improves with physical activity and worsens with prolonged rest and inactivity, reflecting cytokine-driven entheseal inflammation that responds to movement-induced synovial fluid circulation and anti-inflammatory signaling.","D":"Inflammatory back pain in AS is associated with exquisite point tenderness over individual lumbar vertebral bodies in the midline, reflecting vertebral periostitis from direct hematogenous bacterial seeding at the discovertebral junction.","E":"Inflammatory back pain in AS is exclusively nocturnal and fully resolves upon waking and standing, reflecting circadian TNF-alpha surges during overnight recumbency that are completely suppressed by daytime upright posture."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inflammatory back pain (IBP) criteria include: onset before age 45, insidious onset, improvement with exercise (not rest), no improvement with rest, pain at night with improvement on getting up. The classic mnemonic is \"stiffness improves with movement, worsens with rest\" — the opposite of mechanical back pain. Morning stiffness lasting >1 hour is characteristic. This pattern reflects the cytokine-driven inflammation where activity mobilizes synovial fluid and may reduce local cytokine concentrations. IBP is a key diagnostic criterion in the ASAS classification of axial SpA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBP = improves with exercise, worsens with rest, morning stiffness >1 hour, onset <45 years. COMLEX: IBP vs. mechanical: mechanical worsens with activity, improves with rest. Opposite for IBP.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — TNF-alpha inhibitors and anti-IL-17A biologics (secukinumab, ixekizumab) directly target the proinflammatory mediators that drive the major pathogenic elements of spondyloarthritis."},{"id":"sdl46_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the major elements in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which extraarticular manifestation is most strongly associated with HLA-B27 positivity across the spondyloarthritis spectrum and occurs in approximately 25-40% of ankylosing spondylitis patients during their disease course?","choices":{"A":"Psoriasis involving the scalp and extensor surfaces is the most characteristic extraarticular manifestation of HLA-B27-positive AS, occurring in approximately 30% of patients and preceding joint disease by several years on average.","B":"Acute anterior uveitis is the most common extraarticular manifestation of spondyloarthritis, presenting as painful unilateral red eye with photophobia and blurred vision, occurring in 25-40% of AS patients and strongly associated with HLA-B27.","C":"Pulmonary apical fibrosis is the most strongly HLA-B27-associated extraarticular feature of AS, occurring in approximately 25-35% of patients with long-standing disease and producing a restrictive pattern on pulmonary function testing.","D":"Aortic regurgitation secondary to aortitis at the aortic root is the most common extraarticular manifestation of HLA-B27-positive spondyloarthritis, present in 30-40% of AS patients early in the disease course.","E":"Secondary amyloidosis affecting renal glomeruli is the most frequent extraarticular complication associated with HLA-B27 positivity in long-standing AS, occurring in 25-40% of patients with persistent active disease."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acute anterior uveitis (AAU) is the most common extraarticular manifestation of spondyloarthritis, present in 25-40% of AS patients. It is typically unilateral, non-granulomatous, and alternating between eyes over time. It presents with sudden pain, redness, photophobia, and blurred vision. HLA-B27 positivity is found in ~50% of all acute anterior uveitis cases. Aortitis/AR occurs in <10% of long-standing AS. Pulmonary apical fibrosis is rare (<1%). Psoriasis is a feature of PsA, not primarily AS. AAU can be the presenting feature of SpA before joint symptoms appear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acute anterior uveitis = most common extraarticular SpA manifestation; unilateral, alternating, painful. HLA-B27 in 50% of AAU cases. COMLEX: Classic triad of reactive arthritis = conjunctivitis + urethritis + arthritis (not uveitis — distinguish from AS-associated AAU).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — TNF-alpha inhibitors and anti-IL-17A biologics (secukinumab, ixekizumab) directly target the proinflammatory mediators that drive the major pathogenic elements of spondyloarthritis."},{"id":"sdl46_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the genetic contribution to disease in spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis and HLA-B27 positivity undergoes genetic testing that also identifies a co-inherited ERAP1 polymorphism. Researchers propose this combination amplifies disease susceptibility through an additive mechanism. Which function of ERAP1 best explains why its polymorphism interacts with HLA-B27 to increase spondyloarthritis risk?","choices":{"A":"ERAP1 trims peptides in the endoplasmic reticulum to optimal length for HLA class I loading; ERAP1 variants alter the peptide repertoire presented on HLA-B27, increasing the display of arthritogenic peptide sequences that activate autoreactive CD8+ T cells.","B":"ERAP1 cleaves the HLA-B27 signal peptide during post-translational processing, and variants impair this cleavage step, causing increased HLA-B27 surface expression density and proportionally enhanced autoreactive T cell activation at joint surfaces.","C":"ERAP1 phosphorylates the cytoplasmic tail of HLA-B27 after peptide loading is complete, and variants impair this phosphorylation, preventing recycling of loaded HLA-B27 complexes from the ER to the cell surface for CD8+ T cell presentation.","D":"ERAP1 catalyzes the final trimming step of antigens loaded onto HLA-DR molecules for MHC class II presentation, and variants alter the CD4+ Th17 helper response that amplifies downstream IL-17 production in spondyloarthritis.","E":"ERAP1 degrades misfolded HLA-B27 homodimers in the endoplasmic reticulum lumen, and ERAP1 variants reduce this degradation efficiency, causing accumulation of homodimers on the cell surface that constitutively activate NK cells via KIR receptors."},"correct":"A","explanation":"ERAP1 (Endoplasmic Reticulum Aminopeptidase 1) trims peptides in the ER to 8-10 amino acid length optimal for HLA class I loading. ERAP1 polymorphisms alter its peptide-trimming specificity and efficiency, changing which peptides are ultimately displayed on HLA-B27. When combined with disease-associated HLA-B27, ERAP1 variants increase presentation of arthritogenic peptides. Importantly, ERAP1 risk variants only confer SpA risk in HLA-B27-positive individuals, consistent with epistatic interaction. ERAP1 does not act on MHC class II, does not phosphorylate HLA, and does not degrade homodimers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ERAP1 + HLA-B27 epistatic interaction — ERAP1 risk only in HLA-B27+ background. ERAP1 trims peptides for MHC-I loading. COMLEX: Non-MHC SpA genetics: IL23R (Th17) and ERAP1 (peptide trimming) are the two highest-yield non-HLA risk genes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases."},{"id":"sdl46_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the genetic contribution to disease in spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with a 4-year history of inflammatory low back pain is found to be HLA-B27 positive. Analysis of his synovial tissue macrophages reveals increased GRP78 expression, IRE1-alpha activation, and XBP-1 mRNA splicing. Which mechanism best explains these findings in the context of HLA-B27-associated spondyloarthritis?","choices":{"A":"These findings reflect heightened antigen presentation activity in macrophages, where upregulated GRP78 chaperone promotes HLA-B27 peptide-loading efficiency and XBP-1 drives MHC-I transcription to enhance TCR engagement by autoreactive CD8+ T cells.","B":"These findings reflect complement-mediated activation downstream of HLA-B27 surface homodimerization, where IRE1-alpha acts as a complement convertase and spliced XBP-1 promotes MAC assembly proteins at the synovial macrophage surface.","C":"These findings reflect increased Th17 differentiation signaling in synovial macrophages, where GRP78 acts as the IL-23 receptor co-activator and XBP-1 splicing drives IL-17A gene transcription independent of upstream UPR activation.","D":"These findings reflect endoplasmic reticulum stress from HLA-B27 heavy chain misfolding; GRP78 and IRE1-alpha activation represent the unfolded protein response (UPR), and XBP-1 splicing by IRE1-alpha promotes IL-23 transcription, driving downstream Th17 inflammation.","E":"These findings reflect mitochondrial dysfunction in HLA-B27-expressing macrophages, where GRP78 and IRE1-alpha redirect oxidative phosphorylation complexes toward the IL-23 synthesis pathway through direct protein-protein interaction."},"correct":"D","explanation":"HLA-B27 heavy chains have an intrinsic tendency to misfold in the ER due to their unique beta-57 conformation. Misfolded HLA-B27 triggers the unfolded protein response (UPR): GRP78 binds misfolded proteins and dissociates from IRE1-alpha, activating it; IRE1-alpha splices XBP-1 mRNA, and the resulting transcription factor promotes IL-23 production. This IL-23 then drives Th17 differentiation and IL-17 production. The UPR hypothesis explains how HLA-B27 can drive inflammation even without peptide presentation to T cells, providing a T cell-independent innate immune pathway to SpA inflammation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UPR hypothesis: HLA-B27 misfolds → ER stress → GRP78/IRE1-alpha/XBP-1 → IL-23 → Th17 → IL-17. Three HLA-B27 mechanisms = arthritogenic peptide, UPR/misfolding, free heavy chain. COMLEX: ER stress and UPR in autoimmune disease is a recurring theme across multiple rheumatic conditions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases."},{"id":"sdl46_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the genetic contribution to disease in spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with Crohn disease develops insidious low back pain and bilateral sacroiliitis on MRI. She tests HLA-B27 negative. Genetic testing identifies an IL23R polymorphism also carried by her HLA-B27-negative affected sibling. Through which mechanism do IL23R variants contribute to spondyloarthritis susceptibility independent of HLA-B27?","choices":{"A":"IL23R polymorphisms alter HLA-B27 surface expression levels on antigen-presenting cells, creating a functionally equivalent MHC class I antigen-presentation defect that drives CD8+ cytotoxic arthritis even in genetically HLA-B27-negative individuals.","B":"IL23R polymorphisms alter Th17 cell differentiation and survival by modifying receptor signaling intensity in response to IL-23, shifting the Th17/Treg balance toward pathogenic tissue inflammation in gut and joint tissue independent of HLA-B27 presentation.","C":"IL23R polymorphisms reduce IL-12 receptor signaling competition on Th1 cells, shifting the immune balance from Th1-mediated IFN-gamma responses toward Th17-predominant inflammation in gut-associated lymphoid tissue and entheseal sites.","D":"IL23R polymorphisms constitutively activate NF-kB in intestinal epithelial cells entirely independent of T cell signaling, directly producing the mucosal inflammation and bacterial translocation that seeds entheseal and sacroiliac joint inflammation.","E":"IL23R polymorphisms impair regulatory T cell FoxP3 expression by reducing IL-23-mediated Treg plasticity signaling, eliminating peripheral tolerance mechanisms that normally suppress self-reactive B cells targeting sacroiliac joint proteoglycans."},"correct":"B","explanation":"IL23R is the receptor for IL-23, the master upstream cytokine driving Th17 differentiation and the IL-17 inflammatory axis. IL23R polymorphisms that affect receptor signaling efficiency alter the Th17/Treg balance — with risk alleles promoting excess Th17 activity in both gut mucosa and joint tissue. This explains the shared genetic basis of IBD-associated SpA, AS, and psoriasis even in HLA-B27-negative patients. The gut-joint axis in IBD-associated SpA operates through intestinal dendritic cells producing IL-23 that drives Th17 cells which then traffic to joint tissue via shared adhesion molecules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL23R risk alleles drive Th17 excess independent of HLA-B27, explaining IBD-SpA overlap in HLA-B27-negative patients. COMLEX: IBD-associated SpA: 5-20% of IBD patients develop SpA; sacroiliitis may be asymptomatic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases."},{"id":"sdl46_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proinflammatory mediators associated with the inflammatory response in spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis and inadequate response to two TNF-alpha inhibitors is started on secukinumab, with significant improvement in BASDAI score and CRP at 16 weeks. What is the precise mechanism of action of this agent that accounts for its efficacy following TNF inhibitor failure?","choices":{"A":"Secukinumab is a monoclonal antibody that selectively neutralizes IL-17A, blocking its binding to the IL-17RA/IL-17RC receptor complex on fibroblasts and osteoblasts at entheseal sites and thereby suppressing downstream CXCL chemokine and secondary cytokine production.","B":"Secukinumab is a Janus kinase 1 and 3 inhibitor that blocks cytokine receptor signaling downstream of IL-6 and IL-23, preventing STAT3-mediated Th17 activation and IL-17 transcription at entheseal and synovial sites in TNF-refractory disease.","C":"Secukinumab is a monoclonal antibody targeting the p40 subunit shared by IL-12 and IL-23, simultaneously blocking both Th1-mediated and Th17-mediated inflammatory pathways in patients who have failed TNF inhibitor therapy.","D":"Secukinumab binds and neutralizes IL-23 by targeting its p19 subunit, preventing upstream Th17 cell differentiation and IL-17 production at sites of entheseal inflammation through blockade of the most proximal cytokine in the IL-23/Th17 axis.","E":"Secukinumab is a recombinant IL-17 receptor-Fc fusion protein that acts as a soluble decoy receptor, sequestring circulating IL-17A and IL-17F with equal affinity and preventing both cytokines from binding their cellular receptors at joints."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Secukinumab (and ixekizumab) are anti-IL-17A monoclonal antibodies. They bind IL-17A and block its interaction with the IL-17RA/IL-17RC heterodimeric receptor on stromal cells. This is distinct from: ustekinumab (anti-p40, blocks IL-12 and IL-23), guselkumab/risankizumab (anti-IL-23 p19), tofacitinib/upadacitinib (JAK inhibitors). Secukinumab is approved for AS, PsA, and plaque psoriasis. Bimekizumab targets both IL-17A and IL-17F. Anti-IL-17A efficacy in TNF-refractory SpA is well-established since IL-17A operates downstream of TNF and through independent entheseal pathways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secukinumab/ixekizumab = anti-IL-17A. Ustekinumab = anti-p40 (IL-12/23). JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib) = downstream of cytokine receptors. COMLEX: Treatment ladder: NSAIDs → TNF inhibitors → anti-IL-17A or anti-IL-23 for refractory SpA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — Immune cells producing cytokines and specific cytokines each cell type releases."},{"id":"sdl46_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proinflammatory mediators associated with the inflammatory response in spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with 15-year history of ankylosing spondylitis has a spine radiograph showing bridging syndesmophytes and bamboo spine. His rheumatologist notes evidence of both bone erosion at discovertebral junctions and extensive new bone formation. Which statement best explains the paradox of simultaneous bone erosion and new bone formation in AS?","choices":{"A":"In AS, TNF-alpha drives osteoclast activation exclusively at cortical bone surfaces while Wnt pathway activation simultaneously protects cancellous vertebral bone from erosion, producing cortical destruction with central bone preservation and the bamboo spine appearance.","B":"In AS, the synovial membrane upregulates DKK-1 during early disease to suppress syndesmophyte formation, then downregulates DKK-1 in late disease to permit ankylosis, producing the alternating erosive and ankylosing phases seen on serial radiographs.","C":"In AS, IL-17A promotes osteoclastogenesis exclusively at sacroiliac joint surfaces while simultaneously driving osteoblast activation at lumbar vertebral joints, explaining the anatomical divergence between sacroiliac erosion and vertebral syndesmophyte formation.","D":"In AS, TNF-alpha initially drives erosion and simultaneously inhibits Wnt-mediated osteoblast activation, preventing new bone formation; once TNF-mediated inflammation subsides (spontaneously or with treatment), the underlying BMP and Wnt pathways become disinhibited and drive pathological syndesmophyte formation at previously eroded sites.","E":"In AS, complement-mediated fibrocartilage destruction at entheses releases BMP-2 and BMP-4 at sites of prior erosion, producing dystrophic calcification rather than true enchondral new bone formation that could progress to bridging syndesmophytes."},"correct":"D","explanation":"The TNF-Wnt paradox in AS: TNF-alpha drives osteoclast activation and bone erosion while simultaneously suppressing Wnt signaling (via DKK-1 upregulation) which inhibits osteoblast-mediated new bone formation. When TNF is suppressed (spontaneously or with anti-TNF therapy), DKK-1 levels fall, disinhibiting Wnt and BMP pathways that drive syndesmophyte formation at previously eroded entheses. This explains why anti-TNF therapy improves symptoms but does not reliably halt radiographic progression. BMPs (bone morphogenetic proteins) are potent drivers of the anabolic phase leading to bamboo spine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF-Wnt paradox = TNF inhibits Wnt/new bone, so TNF inhibitors may paradoxically allow more syndesmophyte formation. BMP and Wnt drive the anabolic bone formation phase. COMLEX: Bamboo spine = bridging syndesmophytes at ALL disc levels on lateral lumbar spine X-ray; pathognomonic for advanced AS.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — Immune cells producing cytokines and specific cytokines each cell type releases."},{"id":"sdl46_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proinflammatory mediators associated with the inflammatory response in spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with psoriatic arthritis and dactylitis of the right second finger has elevated serum IL-17A and dense neutrophilic infiltration of the tendon sheath on biopsy. Which cytokine produced by Th17 cells directly recruits neutrophils while also inducing stromal cell CXCL chemokine production to sustain the dactylitic tendon sheath inflammatory cycle?","choices":{"A":"IL-21 is the primary Th17-derived cytokine that directly activates neutrophil NADPH oxidase at tendon sheaths and induces fibroblast IL-8 production, making it the dominant amplification signal in dactylitic tendon sheath inflammation in PsA.","B":"IL-22 acts upstream of the neutrophilic tendon sheath response by activating resident fibroblasts to produce defensins and antimicrobial peptides that recruit circulating neutrophils to the site of dactylitic inflammation independent of IL-17A.","C":"IL-17A directly promotes neutrophil survival and recruitment while inducing resident fibroblasts to produce CXCL1, CXCL5, and GM-CSF, creating an autocrine amplification loop that sustains the neutrophilic infiltrate in dactylitic tendon sheath inflammation.","D":"IL-17F acts upstream of neutrophilic inflammation by binding the IL-17RA/RC receptor complex with approximately 10-fold higher affinity than IL-17A, making it the quantitatively dominant neutrophil-recruiting cytokine at dactylitic tendon sheath sites.","E":"IL-23 directly activates tissue-resident neutrophils at the tendon sheath through a distinct receptor independent of IL-17A, serving as the dominant neutrophilic amplification signal in PsA dactylitis rather than acting through Th17 cell intermediaries."},"correct":"C","explanation":"IL-17A (not IL-17F, not IL-21, not IL-22) is the primary Th17 effector cytokine driving neutrophilic entheseal and tendon sheath inflammation. IL-17A binds IL-17RA/IL-17RC on stromal fibroblasts, inducing CXCL1, CXCL5 (GRO-alpha/ENA-78), and GM-CSF that recruit and prolong neutrophil survival. IL-17A also directly activates neutrophil respiratory burst. This creates a self-sustaining loop. IL-22 (also from Th17 cells) acts on epithelial barrier maintenance and keratinocytes (important in psoriasis). IL-21 amplifies B cell differentiation. The dense neutrophilic synovitis in SpA differs from the predominantly mononuclear infiltrate in RA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-17A = neutrophil recruiter via CXCL chemokines + direct activation. IL-22 = keratinocyte/epithelial barrier. IL-21 = B cell differentiation. COMLEX: Dactylitis (sausage digit) = diffuse inflammation of entire finger including tendon sheath + joints; characteristic of PsA and reactive arthritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — Immune cells producing cytokines and specific cytokines each cell type releases."},{"id":"sdl46_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the major elements in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old sexually active man presents 3 weeks after treatment for urethral discharge with bilateral knee swelling, unilateral eye redness with photophobia, and dysuria. He is HLA-B27 positive. Synovial fluid cultures are sterile despite visible clouding. Which organism most commonly triggers this clinical syndrome in sexually active young adults in the United States?","choices":{"A":"Chlamydia trachomatis is the most common trigger of reactive arthritis in sexually active adults in the United States, producing the urethritis component of the classic triad and initiating a post-infectious sterile immune response targeting synovial tissue.","B":"Shigella flexneri is the most common trigger of reactive arthritis in sexually active adults in the United States, producing gastrointestinal colitis that seeds joint synovium through bacterial translocation across the inflamed intestinal epithelium.","C":"Salmonella typhimurium is the most common trigger of reactive arthritis in sexually active adults in the United States, entering joint synovium through blood-borne spread following mucosal breach of the inflamed intestinal epithelial barrier.","D":"Yersinia enterocolitica is the most common trigger of reactive arthritis in sexually active adults in the United States, producing terminal ileitis with Peyer patch involvement that initiates a gut-associated immune response seeding the sacroiliac joint.","E":"Campylobacter jejuni is the most common trigger of reactive arthritis in sexually active adults in the United States, producing acute enteritis that generates cross-reactive synovial antibodies targeting collagen type II at weight-bearing joint surfaces."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reactive arthritis (formerly Reiter syndrome) follows infection with specific organisms: sexually acquired (Chlamydia trachomatis, most common in the US), or enteric (Shigella, Salmonella, Yersinia, Campylobacter — more common in endemic regions and after foodborne outbreaks). The classic triad is \"urethritis + conjunctivitis + arthritis\" (cannot see, cannot pee, cannot climb a tree). Synovial fluid is sterile (post-infectious immune-mediated). HLA-B27 positivity increases risk and severity. The condition is typically self-limited (3-5 months) though can become chronic. C. trachomatis is more common in the US while enteric organisms predominate in developing countries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reactive arthritis triad: urethritis + conjunctivitis + arthritis. Sexually acquired = Chlamydia trachomatis (US). Enteric = Shigella, Salmonella, Yersinia, Campylobacter. HLA-B27 predisposes. COMLEX: \"Cannot see, cannot pee, cannot climb a tree\" — classic mnemonic for Reiter triad.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — Classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional DMARDs."},{"id":"sdl46_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the major elements in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with a 5-year history of Crohn disease develops bilateral sacroiliitis confirmed on MRI and insidious onset inflammatory back pain. She tests HLA-B27 negative. Colonoscopy shows active ileitis with granulomatous inflammation. Which mechanism best explains the gut-joint axis driving IBD-associated spondyloarthritis in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal barrier breach in Crohn disease allows direct bacterial seeding of sacroiliac joints via the portal venous system and Batson vertebral venous plexus, producing low-grade infectious sacroiliitis that mimics the radiographic appearance of inflammatory SpA.","B":"Activated Th1 cells from the inflamed Crohn mucosa home to sacroiliac joint synovium by following IL-8 gradients from activated synoviocytes, where IFN-gamma drives a CD4+ T cell-mediated entheseal inflammation pattern characteristic of IBD-associated SpA.","C":"Intestinal dendritic cells activated by dysbiotic microbiota in the inflamed IBD mucosa produce IL-23, driving Th17 differentiation in mesenteric lymph nodes; these Th17 cells then traffic to joint tissue via shared mucosal addressin expression on joint endothelium.","D":"Complement activation in the inflamed Crohn mucosa generates circulating C5a and IgG immune complexes that deposit in sacroiliac joint synovium, producing a Type III hypersensitivity-driven sacroiliitis with complement consumption and low C3/C4 levels.","E":"B cells activated by gut luminal bacterial antigens in Crohn disease produce cross-reactive IgG antibodies targeting sacroiliac joint proteoglycans, creating immune complex-mediated entheseal inflammation with RF-positive serology in IBD-SpA patients."},"correct":"C","explanation":"The gut-joint axis in IBD-associated SpA operates through: (1) dysbiotic gut microbiota → mucosal dendritic cell IL-23 production → Th17 differentiation in mesenteric lymph nodes → joint trafficking via alpha4-beta7 integrin and MAdCAM-1 (shared mucosal addressins expressed on both gut and joint endothelium). This explains why vedolizumab (anti-alpha4-beta7), which blocks gut-specific trafficking, can improve gut disease but may not control joint disease as well. Sacroiliac involvement in IBD-SpA correlates with gut disease activity independently of HLA-B27 status. This is not a Type III hypersensitivity or infectious process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gut-joint axis: IL-23 from gut mucosa → Th17 → joint via shared mucosal addressins. IBD-SpA can be HLA-B27 negative. COMLEX: IBD-associated SpA: sacroiliitis often correlates with gut flares. Vedolizumab treats gut but may not fully control joint manifestations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — Classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional DMARDs."},{"id":"sdl46_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the major elements in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher proposes that some cases of HLA-B27-associated ankylosing spondylitis may be initiated by prior Klebsiella pneumoniae exposure. Through which mechanism does the molecular mimicry hypothesis explain this proposed bacterial trigger for joint inflammation in genetically susceptible individuals?","choices":{"A":"Klebsiella pneumoniae lipopolysaccharide directly binds HLA-B27 on synoviocytes, activating TLR4-mediated NF-kB signaling that triggers direct IL-17 production from innate immune synoviocytes and initiates the early entheseal inflammatory cascade in susceptible individuals.","B":"Klebsiella nitrogenase shares a peptide sequence with HLA-B27 or joint self-peptides; antibodies and T cells generated against this bacterial epitope cross-react with HLA-B27 or self-peptides presented on joint stromal cells, initiating the autoimmune entheseal inflammation of AS.","C":"Klebsiella pneumoniae directly invades the intestinal mucosa of HLA-B27-positive individuals exclusively, generating reactive oxygen species that modify collagen type II to create immunogenic neoantigens that drive autoimmune sacroiliac joint destruction.","D":"Klebsiella pneumoniae produces a urease enzyme that alkalinizes the intestinal lumen, selectively promoting growth of an IL-23-activating microbiome that produces bacteriocins capable of directly triggering synovial macrophage NLRP3 inflammasome activation.","E":"Klebsiella pullulanase contains a structural domain that mimics the IL-23 receptor extracellular portion, generating cross-reactive antibodies that constitutively activate IL-23 receptor signaling and drive uncontrolled Th17 expansion in HLA-B27-positive hosts."},"correct":"B","explanation":"The molecular mimicry hypothesis for AS proposes that Klebsiella pneumoniae nitrogenase reductase shares an amino acid sequence (QTDRED) with HLA-B27 and with collagenase, and another sequence (TKGNR) is shared with the HLA-B27 molecule itself. Immune responses to Klebsiella generate cross-reactive T cells and antibodies that target synovial and skeletal tissue in HLA-B27-positive individuals. This is supported by elevated anti-Klebsiella antibodies in active AS and increased Klebsiella colonization rates in active disease. Molecular mimicry is a broad mechanism in autoimmunity where pathogen-derived epitopes resemble self-antigens, linking infection to autoimmune initiation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Molecular mimicry in SpA — Klebsiella nitrogenase shares sequence homology with HLA-B27; supports arthritogenic peptide hypothesis. COMLEX: Molecular mimicry is a key concept across rheumatic diseases — also applies to reactive arthritis (Chlamydia cross-reaction with self-antigens in HLA-B27+ hosts).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — Classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional DMARDs."}]},{"sdlNumber":47,"title":"Marfan Syndrome & Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome","questions":[{"id":"sdl47_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Marfan's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which gene mutation underlies Marfan syndrome, and how do the resulting protein defects primarily contribute to connective tissue instability across multiple organ systems?","choices":{"A":"Mutations in FBN2 (chromosome 5q23) reduce fibrillin-2 production in elastic microfibrils, destabilizing connective tissue networks exclusively during embryonic development and producing arachnodactyly without cardiovascular involvement.","B":"Mutations in COL1A1 (chromosome 17q21) reduce type I collagen synthesis, weakening load-bearing connective tissues in the skeleton, eyes, and cardiovascular system through structural scaffold loss without TGF-beta dysregulation.","C":"Mutations in COL3A1 (chromosome 2q32) reduce type III collagen synthesis in vascular walls, producing aortic dilation and dissection along with joint laxity through impaired vascular matrix structural support and increased wall compliance.","D":"Mutations in TGFBR2 (chromosome 3p24) increase TGF-beta receptor signaling constitutively in all connective tissues, producing the full Marfan phenotype through overactivation of fibrotic and matrix metalloproteinase-mediated degradation pathways.","E":"Mutations in FBN1 (chromosome 15q21) reduce fibrillin-1 scaffolding in extracellular microfibrils and dysregulate TGF-beta bioavailability, together weakening microfibril networks in the skeleton, eyes, and cardiovascular system."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Marfan syndrome is caused by heterozygous FBN1 mutations on chromosome 15q21. Fibrillin-1 serves two roles: (1) structural scaffold for ECM microfibrils providing tensile strength to connective tissues in bone, eye, and aorta; (2) sequestration of latent TGF-beta complexes in the ECM. FBN1 mutations cause both structural scaffold loss AND excess TGF-beta release. FBN2 mutations cause congenital contractural arachnodactyly (CCA), not classic Marfan. COL1A1 mutations cause OI/EDS. COL3A1 mutations cause vascular EDS. TGFBR2 mutations cause Loeys-Dietz syndrome (similar to Marfan).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Marfan = FBN1 (chr 15q21), fibrillin-1. Dual mechanism: structural loss + TGF-beta excess. Loeys-Dietz = TGFBR1/2 mutations (more aggressive aortic disease). COMLEX: Autosomal dominant, 75% familial, 25% de novo. Screen first-degree relatives.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Vascular remodeling from TGF-beta dysregulation in Marfan syndrome parallels the vasculopathy driving Raynaud phenomenon and scleroderma renal crisis in systemic sclerosis."},{"id":"sdl47_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Marfan's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Fibrillin-1 normally sequesters latent TGF-beta complexes in the extracellular matrix. How does loss-of-function FBN1 mutation lead to excess TGF-beta bioavailability, and what is the primary consequence of this dysregulation in the aortic wall?","choices":{"A":"Reduced fibrillin-1 releases sequestered TGF-beta from extracellular matrix storage sites, producing elevated free TGF-beta that drives smooth muscle cell apoptosis and cystic medial necrosis in the aortic wall, predisposing to aneurysm and dissection.","B":"Reduced fibrillin-1 prevents glycoprotein cross-linking in the extracellular matrix, exposing TGF-beta binding sites on collagen fibers that then constitutively activate adventitial fibroblasts through paracrine signaling and producing progressive aortic fibrosis.","C":"Reduced fibrillin-1 increases cleavage of latent TGF-beta binding proteins by matrix metalloproteinases, producing truncated TGF-beta precursors that paradoxically activate both pro-apoptotic and pro-survival pathways in aortic smooth muscle cells.","D":"Reduced fibrillin-1 promotes alternative splicing of TGF-beta receptor transcripts, creating constitutively active receptor isoforms that no longer require ligand binding to activate downstream SMAD2/3 signaling in aortic smooth muscle cells.","E":"Reduced fibrillin-1 activates plasminogen-to-plasmin conversion in aortic connective tissue, producing unregulated TGF-beta precursor proteolysis and releasing active TGF-beta independently of normal latent complex activation mechanisms."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fibrillin-1 normally sequesters latent TGF-beta by binding the large latent TGF-beta binding protein (LTBP) in the ECM. When fibrillin-1 is reduced, latent TGF-beta complexes are released from ECM storage, increasing bioavailable TGF-beta. In the aortic media, excess TGF-beta drives smooth muscle cell apoptosis, matrix metalloproteinase activation, and replacement of smooth muscle cells with pools of myxoid ground substance — the histologic lesion of cystic medial necrosis. This weakens the tunica media, predisposing to aortic root aneurysm at the sinuses of Valsalva, which can progress to Type A aortic dissection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FBN1 reduced → TGF-beta released from ECM → aortic smooth muscle apoptosis → cystic medial necrosis → aortic root aneurysm → Type A dissection. COMLEX: Losartan (AT1 receptor blocker) reduces TGF-beta signaling and slows aortic root dilation in Marfan syndrome clinical trials.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Vascular remodeling from TGF-beta dysregulation in Marfan syndrome parallels the vasculopathy driving Raynaud phenomenon and scleroderma renal crisis in systemic sclerosis."},{"id":"sdl47_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Marfan's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Most FBN1 mutations in Marfan syndrome are missense mutations that produce a dominant negative effect rather than simple haploinsufficiency. Which molecular mechanism best explains dominant negative pathogenicity in fibrillin-1 disease?","choices":{"A":"Dominant negative FBN1 mutations insert premature stop codons that produce truncated fibrillin-1 proteins, which are secreted into the ECM and competitively displace full-length fibrillin-1 from microfibril assembly sites through competitive receptor binding.","B":"Dominant negative FBN1 missense mutations reduce fibrillin-1 gene transcription through gain-of-function promoter modification effects, producing haploinsufficiency that accounts for 85-90% of Marfan cases rather than the minority claimed in older literature.","C":"Dominant negative FBN1 mutations produce fibrillin-1 lacking the RGD integrin-binding motif, preventing smooth muscle cell attachment to microfibrils and causing aortic media apoptosis through anoikis-mediated cell death at the integrin-fibrillin interface.","D":"Dominant negative FBN1 missense mutations produce structurally abnormal fibrillin-1 that co-assembles with normal subunits in forming microfibrils, disrupting the entire polymer assembly and amplifying functional damage beyond what simple halving of normal protein production would cause.","E":"Dominant negative FBN1 mutations delete the calcium-binding EGF-like domains of fibrillin-1, preventing calcium-dependent protein folding and thereby inactivating all fibrillin-1 subunits including those encoded by the intact wild-type allele."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fibrillin-1 assembles into head-to-tail microfibrils with multiple subunits. When a missense FBN1 mutation produces structurally abnormal fibrillin-1, these abnormal subunits incorporate into the microfibril polymer alongside normal subunits. Because microfibrils are long-chain polymers, even a minority of abnormal subunits can disrupt the structural integrity of entire polymer chains — this is the dominant negative mechanism. In contrast, haploinsufficiency (simply reduced protein production) accounts for only ~10-15% of Marfan cases. Premature stop codons typically cause haploinsufficiency via nonsense-mediated decay rather than dominant negative effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dominant negative = abnormal protein incorporates into and disrupts the polymer; worse than simple haploinsufficiency. Haploinsufficiency (reduced gene dosage) = ~10-15% of Marfan. Most (~85-90%) = missense dominant negative. COMLEX: Distinguish haploinsufficiency from dominant negative — key concept in genetic disease mechanisms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Vascular remodeling from TGF-beta dysregulation in Marfan syndrome parallels the vasculopathy driving Raynaud phenomenon and scleroderma renal crisis in systemic sclerosis."},{"id":"sdl47_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the potential multi-organ phenotypes seen in Marfan's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old tall male with Marfan syndrome undergoes echocardiography showing mitral valve prolapse and aortic root dilation at the sinuses of Valsalva. He asks which cardiovascular complication represents the leading cause of premature death in Marfan syndrome.","choices":{"A":"Mitral valve prolapse with progressive mitral regurgitation leading to left ventricular volume overload and eventual systolic dysfunction represents the leading cause of premature cardiovascular death in Marfan syndrome patients.","B":"Pulmonary artery aneurysm and rupture due to fibrillin-1 deficiency in the pulmonary arterial media represents the most common lethal cardiovascular complication in Marfan syndrome, occurring before aortic disease in most patients.","C":"Spontaneous pneumothorax from bilateral apical pulmonary bleb rupture represents the leading cause of premature death in tall, thin Marfan syndrome patients and carries higher mortality risk than the cardiovascular complications.","D":"Progressive aortic regurgitation from annular dilation causing acute left ventricular failure represents the single most common immediate cause of death in Marfan syndrome, occurring before dissection in most untreated patients.","E":"Aortic dissection originating at the aortic root (Type A) due to cystic medial necrosis in the tunica media is the leading cause of premature death in Marfan syndrome, typically occurring at the aortic root where fibrillin-1-deficient media is most vulnerable."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aortic dissection (Type A, originating at the aortic root) is the leading cause of premature death in Marfan syndrome. Cystic medial necrosis weakens the aortic root at the sinuses of Valsalva, leading to progressive dilation and eventually dissection. Untreated, aortic dissection is fatal. Prophylactic aortic root replacement is recommended when the diameter reaches 4.5-5.0 cm. Mitral valve prolapse is common (40-50%) but rarely causes death. Aortic regurgitation is a consequence of aortic root dilation but not the primary cause of death. Spontaneous pneumothorax occurs but is not the leading cause of death.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leading cause of death in Marfan = Type A aortic dissection. Threshold for surgery = aortic root >4.5-5.0 cm. Beta-blockers slow dilation rate. COMLEX: Marfan screening: annual echo for aortic root diameter. Also screen first-degree family members.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 53 → Obj 2 — Secondary Raynaud phenomenon occurs in connective tissue diseases including Marfan syndrome, linking its multi-organ vascular phenotype to the broader spectrum of secondary vasomotor disorders."},{"id":"sdl47_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the potential multi-organ phenotypes seen in Marfan's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old female with Marfan syndrome is referred to ophthalmology. Slit-lamp examination reveals bilateral lens subluxation. In which direction does ectopia lentis characteristically displace in Marfan syndrome, and which structural element is primarily deficient?","choices":{"A":"The lens displaces inferiorly and nasally in Marfan syndrome due to zonular fiber weakness caused by fibrillin-1 deficiency, in contrast to homocystinuria where the lens also displaces inferiorly but from cystathionine accumulation in ciliary muscle.","B":"The lens displaces anteriorly into the anterior chamber in Marfan syndrome as fibrillin-1-deficient zonular fibers elongate under intraocular pressure, producing acute angle-closure glaucoma as the characteristic primary ocular emergency.","C":"The lens displaces laterally toward the temporal visual field in Marfan syndrome due to asymmetric zonular fiber weakness on the nasal side, reflecting the mosaic distribution of fibrillin-1 expression across the ciliary body circumference.","D":"The lens displaces superiorly and temporally in Marfan syndrome due to weakness of fibrillin-1-containing zonular fibers that suspend the lens from the ciliary body, contrasting with the inferior and nasal displacement seen in homocystinuria.","E":"The lens displaces posteriorly into the vitreous humor in Marfan syndrome as fibrillin-1-deficient posterior zonular fibers undergo progressive TGF-beta-mediated degeneration from chronically elevated TGF-beta in the vitreous compartment."},"correct":"D","explanation":"In Marfan syndrome, ectopia lentis (lens subluxation) is bilateral and displaces superiorly and temporally (upward and outward), reflecting weakness in the superior zonular fibers (suspensory ligaments of the lens) that contain fibrillin-1. In homocystinuria, lens dislocation is classically inferior and nasal (downward and inward) due to homocysteine-mediated damage to zonular fibers — the classic board comparison. Bilateral ectopia lentis in a young patient warrants evaluation for both Marfan syndrome and homocystinuria. The mnemonic: Marfan = Up and Out; Homocystinuria = Down and In.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Marfan ectopia lentis = bilateral, superior-temporal (up and out). Homocystinuria = inferior-nasal (down and in). COMLEX: Marfan vs homocystinuria: both tall + lens dislocation, but Marfan = normal intelligence + up/out; homocystinuria = intellectual disability + thrombosis + down/in.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 53 → Obj 2 — Secondary Raynaud phenomenon occurs in connective tissue diseases including Marfan syndrome, linking its multi-organ vascular phenotype to the broader spectrum of secondary vasomotor disorders."},{"id":"sdl47_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the potential multi-organ phenotypes seen in Marfan's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old male with Marfan syndrome has a thoracolumbar scoliosis of 38 degrees. His parents ask if this skeletal finding shares the same molecular basis as his concurrent aortic root dilation. Which statement correctly explains the shared molecular underpinning of both manifestations?","choices":{"A":"Both scoliosis and aortic dilation arise from TGF-beta-driven fibrosis of paraspinal muscles and aortic adventitia through independent secondary mutations that each occur against the background of the primary FBN1 haploinsufficiency.","B":"Both scoliosis and aortic dilation arise from the same FBN1 mutation that reduces fibrillin-1 in extracellular microfibrils of both the spinal ligaments and vertebral periosteum and the aortic tunica media, reflecting connective tissue-wide vulnerability from a single gene defect.","C":"Scoliosis in Marfan syndrome results from TGF-beta-mediated paraspinal myopathy that is independent of fibrillin-1 scaffold loss, whereas aortic dilation results exclusively from structural fibrillin-1 scaffold loss without TGF-beta involvement.","D":"Scoliosis and aortic root dilation arise from distinct secondary genes that co-segregate with FBN1 mutations in familial Marfan rather than from the primary FBN1 defect, which affects only ocular and cardiovascular connective tissues.","E":"Both complications arise from fibrillin-1-deficient microfibrils that activate the alternative complement pathway in connective tissue, generating C3a and C5a that recruit macrophages to degrade vertebral ligament collagen and aortic elastin."},"correct":"B","explanation":"FBN1 encodes fibrillin-1, a ubiquitous ECM microfibril component present in elastic and non-elastic connective tissues throughout the body. Because fibrillin-1 is present in periosteum, ligaments, and cartilage of the spine as well as in the aortic media, a single FBN1 mutation produces multi-system disease. The skeletal manifestations (scoliosis, pectus, arachnodactyly, tall stature) and cardiovascular manifestations (aortic root dilation, MVP) all arise from the same underlying fibrillin-1 microfibril weakness plus TGF-beta dysregulation — the dual mechanism operating in all affected tissues.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Marfan is a systemic connective tissue disorder — all phenotypes arise from the single FBN1 defect: skeletal + ocular + cardiovascular. COMLEX: Ghent nosology for Marfan diagnosis uses major and minor criteria across these three systems; aortic root Z-score + ectopia lentis are the cardinal features.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 53 → Obj 2 — Secondary Raynaud phenomenon occurs in connective tissue diseases including Marfan syndrome, linking its multi-organ vascular phenotype to the broader spectrum of secondary vasomotor disorders."},{"id":"sdl47_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the underlying defect contributing to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"All subtypes of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome share a common molecular theme in their pathogenesis. Which statement best characterizes the unifying defect underlying all EDS subtypes?","choices":{"A":"All EDS subtypes result from mutations in genes encoding fibronectin, laminin, or perlecan, the three principal basement membrane proteins that maintain structural integrity of skin, joints, and vascular tissue in connective tissues.","B":"All EDS subtypes result from mutations disrupting elastin fiber assembly, either through direct mutations in the elastin gene or through defects in lysyl oxidase enzymes that cross-link elastin precursors in dermal and vascular connective tissue.","C":"All EDS subtypes result from complement activation against extracellular matrix collagen components, producing autoantibody-mediated progressive destruction of collagen fibers in skin, joints, and blood vessel walls through immune complex deposition.","D":"All EDS subtypes result from defects in collagen synthesis, structure, or assembly, either through direct mutations in collagen structural genes or through mutations in enzymes and accessory proteins required for collagen post-translational processing and fibril formation.","E":"All EDS subtypes result from mutations disrupting fibrillin microfibril assembly and resulting in excessive TGF-beta bioavailability that drives matrix metalloproteinase-mediated collagen degradation in affected skin, joint, and vascular connective tissues."},"correct":"D","explanation":"The unifying molecular thread in all EDS subtypes is some form of collagen abnormality. The defects span three categories: (1) mutations in structural collagen genes (COL1A1/A2, COL3A1, COL5A1/A2, COL12A1); (2) mutations in enzymes for collagen post-translational modification (PLOD1 = lysyl hydroxylase; ADAMTS2 = procollagen N-peptidase); (3) mutations in accessory ECM proteins that regulate collagen assembly (TNXB = tenascin X). Only hypermobile EDS lacks an identified causative gene. FBN1/fibrillin mutations cause Marfan, not EDS. Elastin mutations cause cutis laxa. The 2017 classification recognizes 13 EDS subtypes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EDS common thread = collagen defect (synthesis, structure, or assembly). 13 subtypes in 2017 classification. COMLEX: Clinical features common to most EDS: hyperextensible skin, hypermobile joints, fragile skin with poor wound healing, and joint dislocation risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Joint hypermobility and recurrent effusions in EDS reflect the collagen structural defects that alter synovial joint architecture, placing EDS in the differential for class I non-inflammatory joint effusions."},{"id":"sdl47_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the underlying defect contributing to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Kyphoscoliotic EDS is caused by mutations in the PLOD1 gene and is the most common autosomal recessive EDS subtype. Through which mechanism do PLOD1 mutations impair collagen structural integrity?","choices":{"A":"PLOD1 mutations disrupt the signal peptide of procollagen alpha chains, preventing their entry into the endoplasmic reticulum and causing cytosolic procollagen accumulation that activates the UPR and cannot undergo the folding required for fibril formation.","B":"PLOD1 mutations reduce lysyl hydroxylase enzyme activity, impairing hydroxylation of lysine residues in newly synthesized collagen chains and thereby preventing the formation of hydroxylysine-derived intermolecular and intramolecular cross-links essential for collagen fibril tensile strength.","C":"PLOD1 mutations impair prolyl-4-hydroxylase enzyme activity, preventing hydroxyproline formation in the Gly-X-Pro collagen repeating sequence and destabilizing the triple helix at normal body temperature in all affected collagen types.","D":"PLOD1 mutations eliminate the collagen-specific chaperone HSP47 binding function, preventing proper triple-helix stabilization in the ER and causing collagen misfolding that triggers UPR and ER-associated degradation of the misfolded chains.","E":"PLOD1 mutations impair procollagen N-peptidase function, preventing removal of N-terminal propeptide domains from newly synthesized procollagen and leading to dermal procollagen accumulation that cannot assemble into normal extracellular fibrils."},"correct":"B","explanation":"PLOD1 encodes lysyl hydroxylase 1 (LH1), the enzyme that hydroxylates lysine residues at the Y position of Gly-X-Y repeats in collagen. Hydroxylysine serves as the substrate for glycosylation AND as the attachment point for intermolecular aldol cross-links (allysine-hydroxylysine cross-links = pyridinoline cross-links). Without hydroxylysine, collagen fibrils lack structural cross-links, reducing tensile strength. Diagnostic clue: reduced urinary hydroxylysylpyridinoline:lysylpyridinoline ratio. PLOD1 ≠ prolyl hydroxylase (P4H). PLOD1 ≠ procollagen peptidase. Hydroxyproline (from P4H) stabilizes triple helix; hydroxylysine (from LH) enables cross-links.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PLOD1 = lysyl hydroxylase → hydroxylysine → cross-links. Kyphoscoliotic EDS = autosomal recessive, PLOD1. Features: kyphoscoliosis, ocular fragility, hypermobility. COMLEX: Distinguish lysyl hydroxylase (cross-links, PLOD1) from prolyl hydroxylase (triple helix stability, requires vitamin C).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Joint hypermobility and recurrent effusions in EDS reflect the collagen structural defects that alter synovial joint architecture, placing EDS in the differential for class I non-inflammatory joint effusions."},{"id":"sdl47_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the underlying defect contributing to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Vascular EDS differs fundamentally from other EDS subtypes in its clinical presentation and underlying molecular defect. Which statement best contrasts the features of vascular EDS with classic EDS?","choices":{"A":"Vascular EDS results from COL3A1 mutations affecting type III collagen in vessel walls and intestines, causing life-threatening spontaneous arterial rupture and bowel perforation without the skin hyperextensibility that characterizes classic EDS.","B":"Vascular EDS results from COL5A1 mutations affecting type V collagen in large vessel media, causing aortic dissection and arterial aneurysm formation while also producing the characteristic skin hyperextensibility and fragility of classic EDS subtypes.","C":"Vascular EDS results from ADAMTS2 mutations impairing procollagen-N-peptidase function in vascular smooth muscle cells, causing defective procollagen conversion that specifically weakens arterial walls without significant joint or skin involvement.","D":"Vascular EDS results from PLOD1 mutations reducing lysyl hydroxylase activity selectively in vascular collagen, impairing fibril cross-linking in arteries and bowel walls while leaving dermal and joint collagen structurally and functionally intact.","E":"Vascular EDS results from TNXB mutations eliminating tenascin X function in large vessel adventitia, disrupting the fibrillin-collagen scaffold that normally prevents arterial wall dilation and aneurysm formation in medium and large vessels."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vascular EDS (COL3A1 mutations, type III collagen) is the most life-threatening EDS subtype. Type III collagen is the predominant collagen in vessel walls and intestinal walls. COL3A1 mutations cause spontaneous rupture of medium and large arteries (especially splenic, renal, iliac) and spontaneous bowel perforation, with median survival of 48 years. Unlike other EDS subtypes, SKIN IS NOT HYPEREXTENSIBLE because skin hyperextensibility requires type V collagen deficiency (classic EDS, COL5A1). Vascular EDS can be identified by characteristic facies (thin nose, prominent eyes, lobeless ears, thin lips). Diagnosis confirmed by COL3A1 sequencing and dermal fibroblast type III collagen studies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vascular EDS = COL3A1, type III collagen, spontaneous arterial/bowel rupture, NO skin hyperextensibility. Classic EDS = COL5A1, type V collagen, skin hyperextensibility + joint hypermobility. COMLEX: Most dangerous EDS subtype = vascular (bowel and arterial rupture). Advise against contact sports, heavy lifting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Joint hypermobility and recurrent effusions in EDS reflect the collagen structural defects that alter synovial joint architecture, placing EDS in the differential for class I non-inflammatory joint effusions."},{"id":"sdl47_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the variety of types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome as classified by phenotype.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A medical student reviewing the 2017 EDS classification notes that one subtype is the most commonly diagnosed form but lacks an identified causative gene. Which EDS subtype has no known causative gene, and what is its primary clinical feature?","choices":{"A":"Classical EDS is the most commonly diagnosed subtype with an unknown causative gene, presenting primarily with skin fragility, papyraceous scarring, and poor wound healing rather than joint hypermobility as the defining feature.","B":"Vascular EDS is the most common form with a currently unidentified genetic etiology, presenting primarily with spontaneous large-artery rupture and bowel perforation without significant skin or joint manifestations in most patients.","C":"Hypermobile EDS is the most commonly diagnosed subtype with an unidentified causative gene; it presents primarily with generalized joint hypermobility and musculoskeletal pain without significant skin fragility or life-threatening vascular or visceral complications.","D":"Kyphoscoliotic EDS is the most commonly diagnosed subtype with an unknown causative gene, presenting primarily with progressive scoliosis and ocular fragility without appreciable skin hyperextensibility or joint laxity features.","E":"Periodontal EDS is the most common subtype with an unidentified causative gene, presenting primarily with early-onset severe periodontitis and premature tooth loss without significant joint or skin manifestations."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypermobile EDS (hEDS) is the most prevalent EDS subtype and the ONLY subtype without an identified causative gene as of the 2017 classification. It presents with generalized joint hypermobility, chronic musculoskeletal pain, and often dysautonomia. Skin findings are mild (velvet texture, mild hyperextensibility) but skin is NOT fragile. Vascular and visceral complications are absent. Many patients also carry a diagnosis of postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS). Because no genetic test exists, diagnosis is clinical using the 2017 hEDS criteria. All other 12 EDS subtypes have identified genetic defects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypermobile EDS = most common EDS, no known gene, joint hypermobility + chronic pain, no skin fragility. All other subtypes have known genes. COMLEX: hEDS + POTS + mast cell activation syndrome = frequently co-occurring triad in clinical practice.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 2 — Skin changes in systemic sclerosis reflect collagen overproduction, whereas EDS subtypes reflect collagen structural or assembly defects; both conditions demonstrate tissue-specific collagen pathology."},{"id":"sdl47_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the variety of types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome as classified by phenotype.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman presents with marked skin hyperextensibility, skin that tears with minor trauma, and wide papyraceous scars from prior wounds. Genetic testing reveals a heterozygous COL5A1 mutation. Which EDS subtype does this represent, and what is its inheritance pattern?","choices":{"A":"This represents classical EDS caused by COL5A1 mutations affecting type V collagen, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern and characterized by skin hyperextensibility, skin fragility with poor wound healing, and joint hypermobility.","B":"This represents classical-like EDS caused by TNXB mutations affecting tenascin X, inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern and presenting identically to classical EDS with skin hyperextensibility, papyraceous scarring, and joint hypermobility.","C":"This represents dermatosparaxis EDS caused by ADAMTS2 mutations impairing procollagen-N-peptidase, inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern and producing the characteristic skin fragility and poor wound healing described in this patient.","D":"This represents arthrochalasia EDS caused by COL1A1 mutations affecting the pro-alpha-1 chain of type I collagen, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern and producing the skin fragility, hyperextensibility, and wound healing defects described.","E":"This represents cardiac-valvular EDS caused by homozygous COL1A2 mutations affecting the pro-alpha-2 chain of type I collagen, inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern and presenting with progressive skin and joint manifestations."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Classical EDS is caused by heterozygous COL5A1 (or rarely COL1A1) mutations, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. Type V collagen (alpha1, alpha2, alpha3 chains) is a fibrillar collagen that regulates type I collagen fibril diameter during fibrillogenesis. When type V collagen is deficient, type I collagen fibrils become abnormally large and irregular, reducing tensile strength. Classical EDS features: hyperextensible skin, velvety texture, papyraceous (cigarette paper) scars, joint hypermobility, molluscoid pseudotumors. Classical-like EDS (TNXB) is autosomal recessive with similar phenotype but no scarring. Dermatosparaxis EDS skin is extremely fragile with sagging and severe bruising.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classical EDS = COL5A1, AD, skin hyperextensibility + papyraceous scars + joint hypermobility. Classical-like = TNXB, AR, similar phenotype but no scars. COMLEX: Type V collagen regulates type I collagen fibril diameter — loss = large disorganized fibrils = weak skin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 2 — Skin changes in systemic sclerosis reflect collagen overproduction, whereas EDS subtypes reflect collagen structural or assembly defects; both conditions demonstrate tissue-specific collagen pathology."},{"id":"sdl47_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the variety of types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome as classified by phenotype.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"The arthrochalasia and dermatosparaxis EDS subtypes both involve defective conversion of procollagen to mature collagen but differ in their molecular mechanism and inheritance pattern. Which statement correctly distinguishes these two subtypes?","choices":{"A":"Arthrochalasia EDS results from recessive ADAMTS2 mutations impairing procollagen-N-peptidase enzyme activity, while dermatosparaxis EDS results from dominant COL1A1/COL1A2 mutations producing a procollagen substrate resistant to peptidase cleavage.","B":"Arthrochalasia EDS results from dominant COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutations producing procollagen chains that resist N-terminal peptidase cleavage, while dermatosparaxis EDS results from recessive ADAMTS2 mutations eliminating procollagen-N-peptidase enzyme activity itself.","C":"Arthrochalasia EDS results from recessive PLOD1 mutations impairing lysine hydroxylation that is required for N-terminal peptidase recognition of procollagen, while dermatosparaxis EDS results from dominant COL1A2 frameshift mutations causing enzyme deficiency.","D":"Arthrochalasia EDS results from recessive TNXB mutations disrupting tenascin X-mediated procollagen assembly at the cell surface, while dermatosparaxis EDS results from dominant ADAMTS2 gain-of-function mutations producing ectopic peptidase activity.","E":"Arthrochalasia EDS results from recessive COL5A1 mutations producing type V procollagen that resists C-terminal peptidase cleavage, while dermatosparaxis EDS results from dominant PLOD1 mutations that produce a gain-of-function lysyl hydroxylase."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Both subtypes share the final common pathway of defective procollagen N-terminal processing, but through different mechanisms. Arthrochalasia EDS: dominant mutations in COL1A1 or COL1A2 produce structurally abnormal pro-alpha chains that RESIST N-peptidase cleavage — the substrate is defective. Only 50% of chains are abnormal, but heterozygotes manifest disease because abnormal chains incorporate into triple helices. Dermatosparaxis EDS: recessive mutations in ADAMTS2 eliminate procollagen-N-peptidase ENZYME activity — the enzyme is absent. Since this is enzyme deficiency, both alleles must be non-functional (autosomal recessive). Both result in retention of N-propeptide domains and structurally deficient collagen fibrils.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arthrochalasia = dominant COL1A1/2 (resistant substrate). Dermatosparaxis = recessive ADAMTS2 (absent enzyme). Both = defective procollagen N-terminal cleavage. COMLEX: Enzyme deficiency diseases are usually recessive; structural protein diseases are often dominant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 2 — Skin changes in systemic sclerosis reflect collagen overproduction, whereas EDS subtypes reflect collagen structural or assembly defects; both conditions demonstrate tissue-specific collagen pathology."},{"id":"sdl47_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Marfan's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old male presents with tall stature, arm span exceeding height, arachnodactyly, high-arched palate, and pectus excavatum. Echocardiography shows aortic root dilation at 4.1 cm. Neither parent demonstrates Marfan features, and genetic testing confirms neither carries the identified FBN1 missense variant. Which statement best characterizes this patient's genetic presentation?","choices":{"A":"This patient's presentation reflects gonadal mosaicism in one parent, where a proportion of germline cells carry the FBN1 mutation below somatic cell detection threshold, producing the characteristic Marfan phenotype from partial germline transmission.","B":"This patient's de novo FBN1 mutation likely arose from mitotic recombination during early embryogenesis, producing segmental Marfan syndrome where only tissues derived from the affected cell lineage are phenotypically involved.","C":"Approximately 25% of Marfan syndrome cases arise from de novo FBN1 mutations not inherited from either parent; this patient's full phenotypic expression from a single mutant FBN1 allele reflects the autosomal dominant nature of the disease with complete penetrance.","D":"This patient's de novo presentation requires homozygous FBN1 mutations at both alleles for phenotypic expression, since de novo mutations arising during meiosis typically produce haploinsufficiency insufficient to cause disease in heterozygotes.","E":"This patient's de novo mutation originated from an epigenetic modification of the FBN1 promoter rather than a coding sequence change, producing an environmentally acquired phenotype with reduced heritability compared to germline missense mutations."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Marfan syndrome follows autosomal dominant inheritance. Approximately 75% of cases are inherited from an affected parent; ~25% arise from de novo FBN1 mutations that occurred in the germline of one unaffected parent. De novo mutations carry the same pathogenicity and follow the same autosomal dominant inheritance going forward — this patient has a 50% chance of transmitting the mutation to each child. Full penetrance means all carriers develop some degree of Marfan features, though expressivity (severity) is variable. Gonadal mosaicism is possible but would not be detectable by standard somatic tissue testing and is not the primary explanation for the ~25% de novo frequency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 75% inherited, 25% de novo in Marfan. De novo = new germline mutation, not inherited. Still AD with 50% transmission risk. COMLEX: Counsel all Marfan patients: 50% risk to each child. Recommend genetic testing of children and siblings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Pathology of vasculopathy and mechanism of organ damage in Raynaud phenomenon and scleroderma."},{"id":"sdl47_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Marfan's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher studies a haploinsufficient FBN1 mouse model with 50% fibrillin-1 reduction. The mice develop progressive aortic root dilation similar to human Marfan syndrome. When treated with a TGF-beta neutralizing antibody, dilation is significantly attenuated. What does this experiment support about the dual pathogenic mechanism of FBN1 mutations?","choices":{"A":"This experiment supports haploinsufficiency as the exclusive mechanism, confirming that structural scaffold loss alone drives the full cardiovascular phenotype and that TGF-beta antibody acts through an off-target effect on aortic smooth muscle contractility.","B":"This experiment supports the arthritogenic peptide hypothesis by showing that fibrillin-1 peptide fragments bind HLA molecules on aortic endothelium, and that TGF-beta antibody blocks TCR engagement with fibrillin-1 neoantigens at the aortic root.","C":"This experiment supports a TGF-beta-exclusive mechanism in fibrillin haploinsufficiency, implying that the structural scaffold loss is irrelevant to cardiovascular disease and that all Marfan manifestations are driven by TGF-beta signaling alone.","D":"This experiment supports that excess TGF-beta bioavailability from reduced fibrillin-1 sequestration is a primary disease driver, and that TGF-beta-mediated smooth muscle apoptosis and matrix remodeling contribute to aortic pathology alongside the direct structural scaffold loss.","E":"This experiment supports neomorphic gain-of-function as the mechanism in fibrillin haploinsufficiency, where the single remaining FBN1 allele upregulates TGF-beta synthesis through a compensatory feedback mechanism that is blocked by the antibody."},"correct":"D","explanation":"This classic experiment (Habashi et al., Science 2006) demonstrated that TGF-beta neutralization with a pan-TGF-beta antibody or with losartan (an AT1R antagonist that reduces TGF-beta signaling) attenuated aortic root dilation in the Marfan mouse model. This established that excess TGF-beta — not just structural scaffold loss — is a primary driver of Marfan aortic pathology. This has clinical translation: losartan is used adjunctively to beta-blockers in Marfan syndrome management to reduce aortic root dilation progression. The experiment does not refute the scaffold hypothesis but demonstrates that TGF-beta is independently pathogenic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Losartan (ARB) reduces TGF-beta signaling and slows aortic dilation in Marfan — addresses the TGF-beta arm of the dual mechanism. Beta-blockers address hemodynamic stress. COMLEX: Both beta-blockers and ARBs are used to slow aortic dilation in Marfan; surgery (aortic root replacement) when diameter >4.5-5.0 cm.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Pathology of vasculopathy and mechanism of organ damage in Raynaud phenomenon and scleroderma."},{"id":"sdl47_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Marfan's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with Marfan syndrome caused by a de novo FBN1 missense mutation asks about the risk of transmitting the condition to her children. Her partner does not carry FBN1 mutations. Which statement most accurately describes the inheritance risk for her offspring?","choices":{"A":"Because FBN1 mutations cause autosomal dominant Marfan syndrome with complete penetrance, this patient has a 50% probability of transmitting the mutation to each child, regardless of her partner's genotype, and all carriers can be expected to develop Marfan features.","B":"Because this patient's FBN1 mutation arose de novo, it carries a significantly lower recurrence risk in her offspring compared to inherited mutations, as de novo origins are associated with reduced germline transmission efficiency in subsequent generations.","C":"Because FBN1 missense mutations act through dominant negative mechanisms, the risk to offspring is 75% rather than 50%, since one mutant and one normal allele together produce sufficient abnormal fibrillin-1 polymer disruption in nearly all children.","D":"Because FBN1 mutations show variable expressivity and incomplete penetrance, the transmission risk to each child is 50% but only 30-40% of children who inherit the mutation will develop clinically significant Marfan syndrome requiring medical management.","E":"Because this mutation arose de novo in somatic cells during embryogenesis producing tissue mosaicism, the actual germline transmission risk is lower than 50% and depends on the proportion of germline cells carrying the mutation."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autosomal dominant inheritance with complete penetrance: once a person carries a disease-causing FBN1 allele, regardless of whether it arose de novo or was inherited, the transmission risk to each child is 50% (Mendelian segregation). De novo origin does NOT reduce transmission risk in subsequent generations — the mutation is fully heritable once established in the germline. Complete penetrance means all carriers manifest some features (though expressivity varies). Variable expressivity is present in Marfan but incomplete penetrance is not — all FBN1 mutation carriers develop some Marfan features. The dominant negative mechanism does not change the 50% transmission probability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AD inheritance = 50% risk per child regardless of de novo or inherited origin. De novo in generation 1 = fully heritable in generation 2. COMLEX: Marfan genetic counseling: 50% child risk, recommend prenatal/preimplantation genetic testing if desired.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Pathology of vasculopathy and mechanism of organ damage in Raynaud phenomenon and scleroderma."},{"id":"sdl47_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the potential multi-organ phenotypes seen in Marfan's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old man with known Marfan syndrome presents with sudden-onset tearing chest pain radiating to his back. BP is 160/90 in the right arm and 130/80 in the left arm. CT angiography confirms Type A aortic dissection originating at the sinuses of Valsalva. Which histopathologic finding in the aortic media accounts for the structural vulnerability in Marfan syndrome that led to this complication?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrosis of the intima with macrophage and lymphocyte infiltration producing cytokine-mediated destruction of the tunica media, resembling the giant cell arteritis pattern of transmural inflammatory vessel wall destruction.","B":"Transmural neutrophilic infiltration with microabscess formation in the aortic media reflecting hematogenous bacterial seeding, consistent with infective aortitis from transient bacteremia in fibrillin-1-deficient vascular tissue.","C":"Cystic medial necrosis with loss of elastic fibers and smooth muscle cells in the tunica media, replaced by pools of myxoid extracellular matrix material, reflects TGF-beta-driven smooth muscle apoptosis and matrix remodeling that weakens the aortic wall.","D":"Atherosclerotic plaque with lipid-laden foam cells and dystrophic calcification in the tunica intima producing mechanical weakening and stress concentration that predisposes to intimal tear and retrograde Type A dissection.","E":"Granulomatous inflammation with Langerhans-type giant cells and central necrosis in the aortic media, reflecting an autoimmune response to fibrillin-1 neoantigen exposed by TGF-beta-mediated matrix degradation in the aortic wall."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cystic medial necrosis (also called cystic medial degeneration) is the characteristic histopathologic lesion of Marfan aortic disease. It consists of smooth muscle cell loss (apoptosis driven by excess TGF-beta), elastic fiber fragmentation, and accumulation of myxoid/mucoid material in the media. This produces \"cysts\" of ground substance visible between elastic lamellae. The weakened media cannot withstand systemic pressure at the aortic root (highest wall stress location), resulting in aneurysm and dissection. This lesion is also seen in other connective tissue diseases and in aging hypertension, but is dramatically accelerated and severe in Marfan syndrome. Atherosclerosis is not the mechanism in young Marfan patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Marfan aorta histology = cystic medial necrosis (smooth muscle loss + elastic fragmentation + myxoid pools). Contrast with: GCA = granulomas in media; aortitis = inflammatory infiltrate; atherosclerosis = intimal plaque. COMLEX: Marfan aortic root is the highest-risk location; Type A dissection = surgical emergency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 53 Obj 2 — Primary vs secondary Raynaud and underlying diseases associated with secondary causes."},{"id":"sdl47_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the potential multi-organ phenotypes seen in Marfan's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl with Marfan syndrome has bilateral superiorly and temporally displaced lenses on slit-lamp examination. Her mother asks how this differs from the lens dislocation in a classmate recently diagnosed with homocystinuria. Which statement correctly differentiates the ocular findings and structural basis of these two conditions?","choices":{"A":"In Marfan syndrome the lens dislocates superiorly due to fibrillin-1-deficient zonules pulling upward under intraocular pressure, while in homocystinuria the lens also dislocates superiorly but due to cystathionine-induced ciliary muscle hypertrophy stretching the inferior zonules.","B":"In Marfan syndrome the lens dislocates anteriorly into the anterior chamber due to weakened posterior zonular fibers, while in homocystinuria the lens dislocates posteriorly into the vitreous due to abnormal zonule-retinal adhesion from homocysteine accumulation.","C":"Both Marfan syndrome and homocystinuria cause superior lens dislocation through identical fibrillin-1 disruption mechanisms, but homocystinuria additionally causes corneal thinning and retinal detachment through sulfhydryl cross-link disruption in corneal stroma.","D":"In Marfan syndrome the lens dislocates inferiorly due to fibrillin-1-deficient superior zonules having maximum weakness, while in homocystinuria the lens dislocates superiorly due to methionine-mediated inferior zonule hypertrophy pulling it upward.","E":"In Marfan syndrome the lens dislocates superiorly and temporally due to fibrillin-1-deficient zonular fiber weakness, while in homocystinuria the lens dislocates inferiorly and nasally due to homocysteine-mediated disruption and weakening of the inferior zonular fiber attachments."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ectopia lentis comparison: Marfan syndrome — lens displaces superiorly and temporally (up and out) because the superior zonular fibers containing fibrillin-1 are weakest. Homocystinuria — lens displaces inferiorly and nasally (down and in) because elevated homocysteine damages the sulfide bonds in zonular fibers, with preferential inferior fiber weakness. Both conditions cause bilateral lens subluxation in young patients, but can be distinguished by direction. Additional distinguishing features: Marfan = normal intelligence, no thrombosis, FBN1 mutation; Homocystinuria = intellectual disability, venous and arterial thrombosis, CBS mutation, responds to vitamin B6 in some cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Marfan lens = UP and OUT. Homocystinuria lens = DOWN and IN. Both = bilateral in young patients. COMLEX: Homocystinuria also has thromboembolic risk (stroke, DVT), mental retardation, and osteoporosis. Marfan does not.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 53 Obj 2 — Primary vs secondary Raynaud and underlying diseases associated with secondary causes."},{"id":"sdl47_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the potential multi-organ phenotypes seen in Marfan's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with Marfan syndrome has been on atenolol for 10 years. Her 8-year-old daughter, who has some Marfan features, is referred for cardiac evaluation. Echocardiography of the daughter reveals mitral valve prolapse with mild regurgitation and mild aortic root dilation. Which cardiac manifestation is most prevalent in pediatric Marfan syndrome, often preceding significant aortic root dilation?","choices":{"A":"Aortic regurgitation from progressive aortic annular dilation is the most common initial cardiac finding in pediatric Marfan syndrome, preceding the aortic root dilation that develops predominantly in late adolescence and early adulthood.","B":"Mitral valve prolapse is the most common cardiac manifestation in pediatric Marfan syndrome, present in 40-50% of patients, resulting from fibrillin-1-deficient myxomatous changes in the mitral valve leaflets and chordae tendineae.","C":"Pulmonary artery dilation is the most common initial cardiac finding in pediatric Marfan syndrome, reflecting fibrillin-1 deficiency in the pulmonary arterial media that appears radiographically before systemic arterial involvement.","D":"Tricuspid valve regurgitation from papillary muscle elongation is the most common early cardiac finding in pediatric Marfan syndrome, reflecting the disproportionate chordal growth in tall patients before annular dilation becomes evident.","E":"Ventricular septal defect from fibrillin-1 deficiency at the interventricular junction is the most common cardiac manifestation in pediatric Marfan syndrome, arising during cardiogenesis and identified on newborn echo screening."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mitral valve prolapse (MVP) is the most common cardiac finding in Marfan syndrome, present in approximately 40-50% of patients. Fibrillin-1 deficiency leads to myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve leaflets and chordae tendineae (accumulation of proteoglycans, collagen disorganization), causing leaflet billowing and prolapse into the left atrium during systole. MVP can cause mitral regurgitation and, rarely, is associated with sudden cardiac death from arrhythmia. Aortic root dilation (at sinuses of Valsalva) also develops but may be less severe and less commonly symptomatic in younger pediatric patients. Screening echocardiography should be performed every 6-12 months in children with Marfan syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MVP in Marfan = 40-50% prevalence, myxomatous valve degeneration. Aortic root dilation = more serious long-term. Both require echo monitoring. COMLEX: Annual echo in Marfan; beta-blockers initiated when aortic root begins to dilate or MVP causes symptomatic regurgitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 53 Obj 2 — Primary vs secondary Raynaud and underlying diseases associated with secondary causes."},{"id":"sdl47_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the underlying defect contributing to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman presents with progressive scoliosis, bilateral corneal thinning with ocular fragility, and marked joint hypermobility. Biochemical analysis shows a reduced hydroxylysylpyridinoline to lysylpyridinoline cross-link ratio in her urine. Genetic testing reveals a homozygous PLOD1 mutation. Which statement best explains the mechanism by which this enzyme deficiency impairs collagen structural integrity?","choices":{"A":"PLOD1 encodes prolyl-4-hydroxylase, and its deficiency prevents hydroxyproline formation in Gly-X-Pro collagen repeats, destabilizing the collagen triple helix at physiologic temperature in a vitamin C-dependent manner across all affected tissues.","B":"PLOD1 encodes procollagen N-peptidase, and its deficiency prevents removal of N-terminal propeptide domains from type I and III procollagen, producing procollagen aggregates in the extracellular space that cannot assemble into proper collagen fibrils.","C":"PLOD1 encodes lysyl hydroxylase 1, and its deficiency prevents hydroxylation of collagen lysine residues in the ER, impairing formation of the hydroxylysine-derived pyridinoline intermolecular cross-links that provide essential tensile strength to collagen fibrils.","D":"PLOD1 encodes lysyl oxidase, and its deficiency prevents oxidative deamination of lysine and hydroxylysine residues in mature extracellular collagen fibers, impairing the formation of allysine-derived cross-links that reinforce mature fibrils.","E":"PLOD1 encodes procollagen C-peptidase, and its deficiency prevents C-terminal propeptide cleavage from newly synthesized collagen chains, blocking the nucleation event that initiates ordered collagen monomer lateral assembly into fibrils."},"correct":"C","explanation":"PLOD1 encodes lysyl hydroxylase 1 (LH1), which hydroxylates specific lysine residues in collagen chains within the ER. The resulting hydroxylysine residues serve two functions: (1) sites for O-glycosylation; (2) substrates for lysyl oxidase to produce hydroxylysine-derived aldehyde cross-links (hydroxylysyl pyridinoline = HP cross-links). When PLOD1 is deficient, total HP cross-links fall and LP (lysyl pyridinoline) cross-links predominate — hence the reduced HP:LP ratio in urine. The result is structurally weak collagen fibrils with insufficient cross-links. LH1 ≠ prolyl hydroxylase (which uses vitamin C). LH1 ≠ lysyl oxidase (acts extracellularly on mature fibrils, different enzyme, different gene LOX).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PLOD1 = lysyl hydroxylase 1 (not lysyl oxidase, not prolyl hydroxylase). Kyphoscoliotic EDS key lab: reduced urinary HP:LP ratio. COMLEX: Prolyl hydroxylase requires vitamin C; lysyl hydroxylase (PLOD1) requires iron and alpha-ketoglutarate. Distinct enzymes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class."},{"id":"sdl47_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the underlying defect contributing to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy requires emergency surgery for spontaneous right colonic perforation. He has mild joint hypermobility but notably no skin hyperextensibility. The surgeon notes the bowel wall was strikingly thin and fragile. Genetic testing reveals a COL3A1 missense mutation. Which statement best explains why vascular EDS lacks the skin hyperextensibility that characterizes most other EDS subtypes?","choices":{"A":"Vascular EDS caused by COL3A1 mutations preferentially disrupts deep reticular dermal collagen rather than superficial papillary collagen, producing deep dermal rigidity rather than surface hyperextensibility from the structural collagen reorganization.","B":"Vascular EDS caused by COL3A1 mutations activates compensatory upregulation of type I collagen in dermal fibroblasts, which structurally replaces deficient type III collagen in the dermis and maintains normal skin biomechanical properties.","C":"Vascular EDS caused by COL3A1 mutations impairs collagen synthesis only in mesenchymal-derived vascular smooth muscle cells and intestinal submucosal fibroblasts but not in ectoderm-derived dermal fibroblasts due to lineage-specific promoter activity.","D":"Vascular EDS caused by COL3A1 mutations reduces type III collagen in skin but the normal skin extensibility in this subtype reflects compensatory hypertrophy of elastic fibers in the dermis that maintain normal skin recoil and extensibility properties.","E":"Vascular EDS caused by COL3A1 mutations affects type III collagen, which is critical in blood vessel walls and intestines but skin hyperextensibility in classic EDS depends primarily on type V collagen deficiency, not type III, explaining the absent skin finding in vascular EDS."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Skin hyperextensibility in EDS depends primarily on type V collagen (COL5A1/A2 mutations in classical EDS). Type V collagen regulates type I collagen fibril diameter in skin — without it, fibrils become abnormally large and loosely packed, producing hyperextensible skin. Type III collagen (COL3A1, deficient in vascular EDS) is the predominant structural collagen in blood vessel walls and intestinal walls, not the primary determinant of skin extensibility. Therefore, vascular EDS patients have structurally vulnerable vessels and bowel BUT normal skin extensibility. This is a critical clinical and board distinction — if a patient has EDS features with arterial/bowel rupture but NO skin hyperextensibility, vascular EDS (COL3A1) is the diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vascular EDS = COL3A1 (type III collagen in vessels/bowel), NO skin hyperextensibility. Classic EDS = COL5A1 (type V collagen in skin), YES skin hyperextensibility. COMLEX: Suspected vascular EDS = confirm with COL3A1 sequencing; avoid elective surgery and invasive procedures due to rupture risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class."},{"id":"sdl47_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the underlying defect contributing to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Dermatosparaxis EDS is caused by ADAMTS2 mutations and follows autosomal recessive inheritance, unlike most EDS subtypes. Which molecular explanation best accounts for why this specific EDS subtype follows a recessive rather than dominant inheritance pattern?","choices":{"A":"ADAMTS2 mutations produce a dominant negative procollagen-N-peptidase that dimerizes with and inactivates the wild-type enzyme, but residual activity from heterozygous cells is above the clinical threshold and only bilateral null alleles reduce activity below the critical level.","B":"ADAMTS2 mutations cause dermatosparaxis through gain-of-function enzyme activity that ectopically cleaves non-collagen ECM substrates, but the recessive pattern reflects dosage compensation in which heterozygotes produce sufficient normal ADAMTS2 to suppress the gain-of-function allele.","C":"ADAMTS2 mutations cause dermatosparaxis through epigenetic silencing of type I and type III collagen genes, but the recessive inheritance reflects the requirement for homozygous maternal and paternal ADAMTS2 allele loss to achieve complete biallelic collagen gene silencing.","D":"ADAMTS2 encodes procollagen-N-peptidase, an enzyme required for N-terminal propeptide cleavage; single heterozygous mutations reduce enzyme activity but leave sufficient residual activity for procollagen processing, while homozygous loss of both alleles causes complete inability to cleave procollagen N-propeptides.","E":"ADAMTS2 mutations cause dermatosparaxis exclusively through haploinsufficiency in skin fibroblasts but require below-threshold enzyme activity that is reached only when both alleles contribute null mutations due to tissue-specific dosage sensitivity."},"correct":"D","explanation":"The recessive inheritance of dermatosparaxis EDS follows classic enzyme deficiency genetics: one functional ADAMTS2 allele in heterozygotes produces sufficient procollagen-N-peptidase enzyme to process procollagen N-propeptides adequately (carriers are phenotypically normal). Only when both alleles carry null mutations is enzyme activity eliminated, preventing N-propeptide cleavage and producing dermatosparaxis. This contrasts with arthrochalasia EDS where COL1A1/A2 mutations produce a structurally abnormal substrate (the procollagen chain itself) that incorporates into triple helices and resists peptidase cleavage — this is dominant because structural protein abnormalities can exert dominant negative effects even in heterozygotes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enzyme deficiency = usually recessive (both alleles must be non-functional). Structural protein defect = usually dominant (one abnormal allele disrupts the polymer/complex). Dermatosparaxis: ADAMTS2 = enzyme, AR. Arthrochalasia: COL1A1 = structural protein, AD. COMLEX: Apply this principle across genetic diseases — distinguish enzyme deficiency (AR) from structural protein dominant negative (AD).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class."},{"id":"sdl47_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the variety of types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome as classified by phenotype.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A genetics resident reviews a pedigree showing three generations of autosomal dominant EDS. The proband has severe skin hyperextensibility and papyraceous scarring with a heterozygous COL5A1 mutation. A distant cousin has clinically similar features but a homozygous TNXB mutation. Which statement correctly explains why these genetically distinct mutations produce similar clinical presentations?","choices":{"A":"The COL5A1 and TNXB mutations produce identical clinical features because both genes encode identical molecular functions in type V collagen biosynthesis, with TNXB serving as the regulatory chaperone of the COL5A1 transcriptional activation complex.","B":"COL5A1 encodes structural type V collagen while TNXB encodes tenascin X, a large extracellular matrix glycoprotein that interacts with fibrillar collagens (types I, III, and V) to regulate fibril assembly; both gene defects disrupt type V collagen-mediated dermal architecture through different molecular entry points.","C":"The clinical similarity reflects phenotypic convergence in which any collagen-related gene mutation preferentially disrupts the COL5A1-rich papillary dermis regardless of the gene primarily affected, producing the same superficial skin phenotype across all genotypes.","D":"Both COL5A1 and TNXB encode enzymes required for the same N-hydroxylation step in type V collagen post-translational modification, producing equivalent collagen structural defects through convergent enzymatic dysfunction.","E":"Both mutations cause classical EDS through identical haploinsufficiency mechanisms, and the differing inheritance patterns reflect differences in gene copy number sensitivity between individuals rather than truly distinct molecular pathomechanisms."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Classic EDS (COL5A1 mutation) and classical-like EDS (TNXB mutation) produce similar clinical phenotypes through different but converging molecular mechanisms. Tenascin X (encoded by TNXB) is a large extracellular matrix glycoprotein that physically interacts with type I, III, and V collagens and regulates their lateral fibril assembly and fibril diameter. When tenascin X is absent (biallelic TNXB mutations — autosomal recessive), fibrillar collagen (especially type V-containing fibrils) fails to assemble normally, producing a phenotype similar to primary type V collagen deficiency. This illustrates how ECM accessory proteins that regulate collagen assembly can cause EDS subtypes even without direct collagen structural gene mutations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classical EDS = COL5A1 (AD). Classical-like EDS = TNXB (AR). Similar phenotype, different inheritance. Tenascin X regulates collagen fibril assembly. COMLEX: EDS subtype identification requires genetic testing to distinguish classical from classical-like and guide inheritance counseling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 2 — Typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes that cause them."},{"id":"sdl47_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the variety of types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome as classified by phenotype.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman presents with lifelong generalized joint hypermobility, chronic diffuse musculoskeletal pain, orthostatic intolerance, and palpitations. Her skin is mildly velvet-textured but not fragile. No pathogenic variants are found in COL5A1, COL3A1, or PLOD1. Physical therapy for joint stabilization is recommended. Which EDS subtype does this represent, and what is known about its genetic basis?","choices":{"A":"This represents hypermobile EDS, the most common EDS subtype in which the causative gene remains unidentified; joint hypermobility and musculoskeletal pain predominate while skin findings are mild, vascular complications are absent, and autonomic dysfunction frequently co-occurs.","B":"This represents the myopathic EDS subtype caused by COL12A1 mutations affecting collagen XII in myotendinous junctions, preferentially producing combined proximal muscle weakness and joint hypermobility without significant skin or vascular features.","C":"This represents the periodontal EDS subtype caused by C1r and C1s mutations in complement serine proteases that maintain connective tissue homeostasis, producing joint laxity and autonomic complications when both genes are simultaneously deficient.","D":"This represents spondylodysplastic EDS caused by B4GALT7 mutations impairing galactosyltransferase function in glycosaminoglycan biosynthesis, affecting proteoglycan hydration and producing joint hypermobility without fragile skin or vascular risk.","E":"This represents musculocontractural EDS caused by CHST14 mutations impairing dermatan sulfate biosynthesis in fibroblasts, producing the joint hypermobility, autonomic phenotype, and musculoskeletal pain described through proteoglycan-collagen matrix disruption."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypermobile EDS (hEDS) is by far the most common EDS subtype in clinical practice and the only one without an identified causative gene as of the 2017 International Classification. Diagnosis is clinical, using the 2017 hEDS criteria which require: (1) generalized joint hypermobility (Beighton score ≥5), (2) two of three features (musculoskeletal complications, systemic manifestations such as soft/velvety skin, mild hyperextensibility, and positive family history), (3) absence of unusual skin fragility, arterial/intestinal fragility, and exclusion of other heritable connective tissue disorders. Autonomic dysfunction (POTS) and mast cell activation syndrome frequently co-occur. No genetic test is available for hEDS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hEDS = most common EDS, no known gene, clinical diagnosis, joint hypermobility + pain + autonomic features, no skin fragility, no vascular risk. COMLEX: hEDS + POTS + dysautonomia = common clinical triad. Distinguish from hypermobility spectrum disorder (HSD) which does not meet full hEDS criteria.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 2 — Typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes that cause them."},{"id":"sdl47_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the variety of types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome as classified by phenotype.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A pathology student reviewing EDS genetics notes that arthrochalasia EDS results from dominant COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutations producing procollagen chains resistant to N-terminal peptidase cleavage. Through which molecular mechanism do these structural COL1A1 mutations produce a dominant phenotype despite affecting only 50% of pro-alpha-1(I) chains?","choices":{"A":"Mutant pro-alpha-1(I) chains from COL1A1 mutations are preferentially degraded in the ER, reducing the total pool of available type I collagen precursors; this haploinsufficiency produces inadequate total collagen quantity despite the presence of normal chains from the unaffected allele.","B":"Mutant pro-alpha-1(I) chains from COL1A1 mutations activate an autocrine TGF-beta feedback loop in fibroblasts that suppresses transcription from the wild-type COL1A1 allele, creating secondary loss of function in cells expressing the mutant chain.","C":"Mutant pro-alpha-1(I) chains that resist N-terminal cleavage co-assemble with normal chains in the type I collagen triple helix; because each type I collagen molecule requires two pro-alpha-1(I) chains, one mutant chain disrupts the majority of assembled collagen molecules through a dominant negative polymer effect.","D":"Mutant pro-alpha-1(I) chains that resist N-terminal cleavage contain a constitutively exposed collagen-binding domain that competitively inhibits wild-type procollagen from binding procollagen-N-peptidase in a trans-dominant manner across all fibroblasts.","E":"Mutant pro-alpha-1(I) chains from arthrochalasia COL1A1 mutations are secreted at twice the normal rate due to bypassed ER quality control, producing dosage imbalance between mutant and wild-type chains and overwhelming normal triple helix assembly."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Type I collagen is a heterotrimer: [alpha1(I)]2 alpha2(I) — two pro-alpha-1(I) chains and one pro-alpha-2(I) chain. Each collagen molecule contains two copies of the product of COL1A1. If 50% of pro-alpha-1(I) chains are abnormal (resist N-terminal cleavage), then random assembly produces: 25% molecules with 2 normal chains (unaffected), 50% with 1 abnormal and 1 normal chain (partially affected), and 25% with 2 abnormal chains (fully abnormal) = 75% of all type I collagen molecules contain at least one mutant chain. This dominant negative stoichiometric amplification explains why heterozygous structural collagen mutations produce dominant disease: the abnormal protein incorporated into the majority of polymer units disrupts most of the functional collagen produced.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type I collagen = [alpha1(I)]2 [alpha2(I)] — two COL1A1 products per molecule. 50% abnormal chains → 75% molecules contain at least one abnormal chain = dominant negative. COMLEX: Same principle applies in OI (osteogenesis imperfecta) type II from COL1A1/A2 mutations — dominant lethal because virtually all collagen is disrupted.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 2 — Typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes that cause them."}]},{"sdlNumber":53,"title":"Raynaud's Phenomenon","questions":[{"id":"sdl53_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Alpha-2C adrenoceptors on vascular smooth muscle cells play a central role in cold-induced vasoconstriction in Raynaud phenomenon. Which statement best describes the mechanism by which cold selectively activates these receptors?","choices":{"A":"At normal temperature, alpha-2C adrenoceptors are expressed constitutively at high density on the plasma membrane of digital arterioles; cold exposure increases their affinity for norepinephrine through a temperature-dependent conformational change in the receptor ligand-binding domain.","B":"At normal temperature, alpha-2C adrenoceptors are stored silently within the Golgi apparatus; cold exposure activates the Rho/Rho kinase signaling pathway, triggering receptor translocation to the plasma membrane and augmenting vasoconstriction in response to norepinephrine.","C":"At normal temperature, alpha-2C adrenoceptors are degraded by a ubiquitin-proteasome mechanism; cold exposure stabilizes these receptors by inhibiting their ubiquitination, producing a net increase in surface receptor expression on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells.","D":"At normal temperature, alpha-2C adrenoceptors are phosphorylated and internalized via beta-arrestin; cold exposure dephosphorylates these receptors and promotes their recycling to the plasma membrane through a protein kinase A-dependent mechanism.","E":"At normal temperature, alpha-2C adrenoceptors are sequestered by circulating catecholamines at baseline; cold exposure displaces bound norepinephrine from alpha-2C receptors through allosteric competition, creating a paradoxical increase in free receptor availability."},"correct":"B","explanation":"In cutaneous arterioles at normal temperature, alpha-2C adrenoceptors are stored silently in the Golgi apparatus and do not contribute to baseline vascular tone. Cold exposure triggers a rapid increase in reactive oxygen species in smooth muscle cells, activating the Rho/Rho kinase signaling pathway. This causes translocation of alpha-2C adrenoceptors from the Golgi to the plasma membrane, where they augment the contractile response to norepinephrine. This mechanism explains why alpha-2C receptors mediate cold-induced but not baseline vasoconstriction. Importantly, in primary Raynaud phenomenon, this pathway is exaggerated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alpha-2C adrenoceptors = cold-specific vasoconstriction via Golgi-to-membrane translocation through Rho/Rho kinase. Alpha-2A/B = baseline adrenergic tone. COMLEX: Rho kinase inhibitors are an emerging therapeutic target in Raynaud phenomenon and pulmonary arterial hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis reflects endothelial dysfunction with reduced nitric oxide and prostacyclin and elevated endothelin-1, directly linking RP pathogenesis to scleroderma vasculopathy."},{"id":"sdl53_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Arteriovenous anastomoses are the primary thermoregulatory vessels in glabrous skin. Which statement best describes their role in the pathogenesis of Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Arteriovenous anastomoses connect paired digital arteries and provide collateral flow between adjacent fingers; their constriction during cold exposure is a protective mechanism that preserves nutritional capillary flow and prevents the cyanosis characteristic of Raynaud phenomenon.","B":"Arteriovenous anastomoses are low-resistance conduits that connect cutaneous lymphatics to the deep venous plexus; their constriction during cold increases lymphatic pressure in the fingers, producing the edema and discoloration characteristic of severe Raynaud attacks.","C":"Arteriovenous anastomoses connect the superficial to the deep dermal plexus and are richly innervated by the sympathetic adrenergic system; their exaggerated constriction during cold exposure shunts blood away from digital arteries and cutaneous capillaries, producing the ischemic pallor of Raynaud phenomenon.","D":"Arteriovenous anastomoses function exclusively as baroreceptors that sense changes in digital perfusion pressure; their impaired mechanosensory function in primary Raynaud phenomenon reduces the normal vasodilatory reflex triggered by digital ischemia.","E":"Arteriovenous anastomoses are found predominantly in hairy non-glabrous skin of the forearm and trunk; their selective dysfunction in Raynaud phenomenon explains why episodes preferentially involve the non-glabrous skin of the dorsal hand rather than the palmar surface."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Arteriovenous anastomoses are low-resistance shunt vessels concentrated in glabrous skin of fingers, toes, palms, soles, ears, and nose. They connect the superficial arteriolar plexus to the deep venous plexus, bypassing nutritional capillaries. They are richly innervated by the sympathetic adrenergic system and serve as the primary thermoregulatory vessels. During cold exposure, sympathetic activation constricts AVAs, shunting blood to the deep plexus for heat conservation. In Raynaud phenomenon, this response is exaggerated, reducing total digital blood flow. In primary RP, nutritional capillary flow is largely preserved despite AVA constriction; in secondary RP, nutritional flow is also impaired.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AVAs = thermoregulatory shunt vessels in glabrous digital skin, richly sympathetically innervated. AVA constriction = reduced total digital flow but nutritional capillaries preserved in primary RP. COMLEX: AVA dysfunction is central to RP pathogenesis; found in fingers, toes, ears, nose, lips.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis reflects endothelial dysfunction with reduced nitric oxide and prostacyclin and elevated endothelin-1, directly linking RP pathogenesis to scleroderma vasculopathy."},{"id":"sdl53_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Endothelin-1 is a potent vasoconstrictor produced by endothelial cells. Which statement best characterizes the role of endothelin-1 in Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Endothelin-1 is constitutively released by endothelial cells at high levels in all forms of Raynaud phenomenon; it acts on ETA receptors on smooth muscle cells to produce the sustained digital vasospasm and is the primary mediator of both primary and secondary forms.","B":"Endothelin-1 acts as a vasodilator in the digital microcirculation by activating ETB receptors on endothelial cells to release nitric oxide; its deficiency in secondary Raynaud phenomenon removes this protective vasodilatory signal and promotes vasospasm.","C":"Endothelin-1 is the primary mediator of cold-induced vasoconstriction in primary Raynaud phenomenon; its release from digital endothelial cells is directly triggered by cold temperature via TRPM8 receptor activation on endothelial cell cilia.","D":"Endothelin-1 is not released under normal vascular conditions but is produced when endothelial damage or dysfunction occurs; elevated endothelin-1 contributes to the exaggerated vasoconstriction in secondary Raynaud phenomenon but is not a primary mediator in primary Raynaud phenomenon where endothelial function is preserved.","E":"Endothelin-1 competitively inhibits norepinephrine binding at alpha-2C adrenoceptors in normal conditions; its absence in Raynaud phenomenon removes this inhibitory mechanism and allows unrestrained norepinephrine-mediated vasoconstriction of digital arteries."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Endothelin-1 is a potent vasoconstrictor but is not released under normal conditions in primary Raynaud phenomenon, where endothelial function is generally preserved. It is elevated in secondary Raynaud phenomenon, especially systemic sclerosis, where endothelial injury and dysfunction are early and prominent features. In SSc, reduced nitric oxide and prostacyclin production combined with elevated endothelin-1 creates a pro-vasoconstrictive endothelial state. Endothelin-1 does act on both ETA receptors on smooth muscle cells to cause vasoconstriction AND ETB receptors on endothelial cells to release NO (vasodilation), making it a complex mediator.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary RP = preserved endothelial function, normal endothelin-1. Secondary RP (especially SSc) = endothelial dysfunction, elevated endothelin-1, reduced NO/prostacyclin. COMLEX: Bosentan (endothelin receptor antagonist) is used for SSc-related digital ulcers and pulmonary arterial hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis reflects endothelial dysfunction with reduced nitric oxide and prostacyclin and elevated endothelin-1, directly linking RP pathogenesis to scleroderma vasculopathy."},{"id":"sdl53_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary Raynaud's as well as underlying diseases associated with secondary causes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement best describes the features of primary Raynaud phenomenon that distinguish it from secondary Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon affects predominantly older males, presents with asymmetric extremity involvement that progressively worsens over time, and results from structural vascular changes including intimal fibrosis and luminal obliteration of digital arteries.","B":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon affects individuals over age 50 with equal sex distribution, presents with unilateral hand involvement that correlates with occupational cold exposure, and is characterized by endothelial dysfunction with elevated endothelin-1 and reduced nitric oxide.","C":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon affects 3-5% of the general population, predominantly young females, presents with symmetric extremity involvement without progression in severity, and results from intrinsic smooth muscle cell hyperreactivity without structural vascular changes in the arterial walls.","D":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon affects predominantly young males with a strong family history of connective tissue disease, presents with episodic toe involvement more commonly than finger involvement, and is distinguished by the presence of anti-centromere antibodies on serologic testing.","E":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon affects patients of any age with a prior history of digital ischemia, presents with bilateral asymmetric involvement that improves over time, and is characterized by structural intimal thickening of proximal palmar digital arteries at presentation."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon characteristics: 3-5% prevalence in the general population; predominantly young females; onset typically between age 15-30; symmetric bilateral involvement of fingers and sometimes toes; course is benign with stable or improving severity over time; no underlying disease; intrinsic smooth muscle hyperreactivity to cold/emotion; no structural arterial wall changes except in long-standing disease where mild intimal thickening can develop. Critical ischemia with ulceration or gangrene is rare. Autoantibody testing and nail fold capillaroscopy are normal in primary RP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary RP = young female, symmetric, benign course, no structural changes, no underlying disease. Secondary RP = asymmetric, progressive, underlying disease, structural changes, nutritional capillary involvement. COMLEX: Nailfold capillaroscopy: normal loops in primary RP; dilated/tortuous/avascular areas in secondary RP.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 1 — Secondary Raynaud phenomenon is the sentinel vascular manifestation of systemic sclerosis, preceding skin fibrosis and internal organ involvement in the majority of SSc patients."},{"id":"sdl53_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary Raynaud's as well as underlying diseases associated with secondary causes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which connective tissue disease is most commonly associated with secondary Raynaud phenomenon and in what percentage of affected patients does Raynaud phenomenon occur, often as the earliest clinical manifestation?","choices":{"A":"Systemic sclerosis is the connective tissue disease most strongly associated with Raynaud phenomenon, occurring in 90-95% of affected patients and frequently preceding skin fibrosis and internal organ involvement by months to years.","B":"Systemic lupus erythematosus is the connective tissue disease most strongly associated with Raynaud phenomenon, occurring in 90-95% of affected patients and representing the most common initial clinical manifestation that triggers autoantibody evaluation.","C":"Mixed connective tissue disease is the connective tissue disease most strongly associated with Raynaud phenomenon, occurring in 90-95% of affected patients because anti-U1 RNP antibodies directly activate alpha-2C adrenoceptors on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells.","D":"Rheumatoid arthritis is the connective tissue disease most strongly associated with Raynaud phenomenon, occurring in 90-95% of affected patients as a consequence of synovial pannus encasing and compressing digital artery walls during active joint inflammation.","E":"Sjogren syndrome is the connective tissue disease most strongly associated with Raynaud phenomenon, occurring in 90-95% of affected patients because aquaporin-3 deficiency in glandular and vascular tissue simultaneously impairs salivary secretion and digital vasodilation."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Systemic sclerosis has the strongest association with Raynaud phenomenon of all connective tissue diseases, with up to 90-95% of SSc patients developing RP. Raynaud phenomenon is often the very first symptom of SSc, preceding skin thickening by months to years. The mechanism in SSc involves endothelial dysfunction (reduced NO and prostacyclin, elevated endothelin-1), intimal fibrosis, alpha-2C adrenoceptor upregulation, and structural microangiopathy. SLE has RP in about 30-40% of patients. MCTD has RP in about 75-85%. Sjogren syndrome has RP in about 15-30%. Rheumatoid arthritis can cause RP but is not the most common association.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc + Raynaud phenomenon = 90-95% prevalence; often first symptom years before skin disease. SLE = 30-40%. MCTD = 75-85%. COMLEX: Any new Raynaud phenomenon in patient over 30 or with asymmetric/progressive features warrants evaluation for underlying connective tissue disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 1 — Secondary Raynaud phenomenon is the sentinel vascular manifestation of systemic sclerosis, preceding skin fibrosis and internal organ involvement in the majority of SSc patients."},{"id":"sdl53_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary Raynaud's as well as underlying diseases associated with secondary causes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which distinction in the effect on digital blood flow best differentiates primary from secondary Raynaud phenomenon and explains why critical digital ischemia and gangrene occur in secondary but not primary forms?","choices":{"A":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon, cold-induced constriction affects only the distal interphalangeal joint capillaries; in secondary Raynaud phenomenon, the proximal digital arteries are also involved, producing retrograde ischemia that propagates from the palm to the fingertips.","B":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon, cold-induced constriction is limited to the alpha-2A adrenoceptors on large digital arteries; in secondary Raynaud phenomenon, alpha-2C adrenoceptors on the nutritional capillaries are additionally activated, producing complete microvascular arrest.","C":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon, cold-induced constriction is fully reversed within 30 seconds of rewarming due to intact endothelial nitric oxide production; in secondary Raynaud phenomenon, structural vascular changes prevent nitric oxide release and trap the digits in permanent vasospasm.","D":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon, cold-induced constriction reduces arteriovenous anastomosis flow but nutritional capillary flow to tissue is largely preserved; in secondary Raynaud phenomenon, both arteriovenous anastomosis flow and nutritional capillary flow are reduced, producing critical ischemia and enabling tissue necrosis.","E":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon, cold-induced constriction affects all digital vessels symmetrically but is limited in magnitude by normal Rho kinase activity; in secondary Raynaud phenomenon, activated Rho kinase produces an amplitude of constriction 10-fold greater than in primary disease."},"correct":"D","explanation":"The key pathophysiologic distinction: In primary RP, exaggerated AVA constriction reduces total digital blood flow but nutritional capillary flow is maintained at most temperatures. Without nutritional capillary involvement, tissue oxygenation is preserved and critical ischemic events (digital ulcers, pitting scars, gangrene) are rare. In secondary RP, the underlying vascular disease (structural microangiopathy, endothelial dysfunction, intimal fibrosis in SSc) also compromises nutritional capillary flow, leaving digits vulnerable to critical ischemia. This is why digital pitting, ulceration, and gangrene are features of secondary RP but essentially absent in uncomplicated primary RP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary RP = preserved nutritional capillary flow = no gangrene/ulcers. Secondary RP = nutritional capillary also compromised = digital ulcers, gangrene, pitting scars possible. COMLEX: Digital ulcers in Raynaud = secondary RP until proven otherwise; evaluate for SSc with nailfold capillaroscopy and ANA/anti-Scl-70/anticentromere.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 1 — Secondary Raynaud phenomenon is the sentinel vascular manifestation of systemic sclerosis, preceding skin fibrosis and internal organ involvement in the majority of SSc patients."},{"id":"sdl53_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss triggers for Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"Cold exposure is the most common trigger of Raynaud phenomenon. Through which specific molecular mechanism does cold temperature initiate the exaggerated vasospastic response in affected patients?","choices":{"A":"Cold exposure directly depolarizes voltage-gated calcium channels on smooth muscle cell membranes, producing a sustained influx of extracellular calcium that triggers myosin light chain kinase phosphorylation and tonic contraction independent of adrenergic receptor activation.","B":"Cold exposure generates reactive oxygen species in digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells, activating the Rho/Rho kinase signaling pathway that triggers alpha-2C adrenoceptor translocation from the Golgi to the plasma membrane and augments calcium sensitivity of contractile proteins.","C":"Cold exposure inhibits endothelial nitric oxide synthase by reducing the availability of its cofactor tetrahydrobiopterin, producing endothelial uncoupling that generates superoxide instead of nitric oxide and precipitates vasospasm through oxidative stress.","D":"Cold exposure activates TRPM8 cold receptors on digital artery endothelial cells, triggering direct calcium influx into the endothelium that releases thromboxane A2 and serotonin from endothelial granules, which then activate smooth muscle vasoconstriction.","E":"Cold exposure directly activates the NLRP3 inflammasome in perivascular macrophages, releasing IL-1beta that acts on smooth muscle cells to upregulate endothelin-1 receptor expression and amplify endothelin-driven digital vasospasm in susceptible individuals."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cold triggers RP through the Rho/Rho kinase pathway: cold exposure causes a rapid increase in ROS in cutaneous smooth muscle cells, activating Rho/Rho kinase, which then (1) triggers alpha-2C adrenoceptor translocation from Golgi to plasma membrane and (2) augments calcium sensitivity of the contractile proteins (via myosin light chain phosphatase inhibition). This is distinct from direct calcium channel activation or endothelial mechanisms. TRPM8 is expressed on afferent sensory neurons to detect cold (not on endothelial cells). The ROS-Rho-alpha-2C pathway is a self-reinforcing cycle: ischemia from vasospasm generates more ROS, potentially perpetuating attacks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cold trigger mechanism: ROS → Rho/Rho kinase → alpha-2C translocation + augmented Ca sensitivity → vasoconstriction. TRPM8 = cold sensory receptor on afferent neurons, not the direct vasoconstriction trigger. COMLEX: Calcium channel blockers (nifedipine) are first-line treatment for RP, targeting the downstream calcium sensitivity component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 5 — Cold and emotional stress triggers Raynaud episodes in SLE patients, reflecting shared vascular reactivity abnormalities between primary RP and lupus-associated secondary Raynaud phenomenon."},{"id":"sdl53_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss triggers for Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"Several drug classes are known to trigger or exacerbate Raynaud phenomenon. Which statement correctly identifies a drug class and its mechanism for worsening digital vasospasm?","choices":{"A":"Calcium channel blockers of the dihydropyridine class trigger Raynaud phenomenon by directly activating alpha-2C adrenoceptors through an off-target receptor interaction that selectively increases cold-induced vasoconstriction without affecting resting vascular tone.","B":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors trigger Raynaud phenomenon by increasing bradykinin levels in digital arterioles, which paradoxically activates B1 receptors on smooth muscle cells to produce vasoconstriction rather than the normal vasodilatory response.","C":"Proton pump inhibitors trigger Raynaud phenomenon by reducing magnesium absorption, lowering plasma magnesium levels and causing vascular smooth muscle membrane depolarization that increases the amplitude of cold-induced digital artery vasoconstriction.","D":"HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors trigger Raynaud phenomenon by depleting mevalonate pathway intermediates required for Rho protein prenylation, paradoxically activating constitutive Rho/Rho kinase signaling in digital smooth muscle cells through loss of a feedback inhibitory mechanism.","E":"Calcitonin gene-related peptide antagonist monoclonal antibodies, used for migraine prevention, remove the vasodilatory counterbalance provided by sensory nerve CGRP release in the digital microcirculation, unmasking or worsening existing Raynaud phenomenon."},"correct":"E","explanation":"CGRP antagonist monoclonal antibodies (erenumab, fremanezumab, galcanezumab) are used for migraine prevention and are associated with new-onset or worsening Raynaud phenomenon. CGRP is a potent vasodilating neuropeptide released from sensory nerve fibers in digital skin; it normally counterbalances the sympathetic vasoconstriction. When CGRP signaling is blocked, this protective vasodilatory mechanism is removed, leaving alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction unopposed. Other drug triggers: beta-blockers (block compensatory beta-2-mediated vasodilation), ergotamines (direct vasoconstriction), bleomycin (vascular endothelial injury → secondary RP), interferon-alpha, cocaine, sympathomimetics. Calcium channel blockers (nifedipine, amlodipine) TREAT RP, they do not trigger it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CGRP antagonists (for migraine) → worsen/trigger RP by removing vasodilatory counterbalance. Beta-blockers → worsen RP by blocking compensatory beta-2 vasodilation. Bleomycin → secondary RP via vascular injury. COMLEX: First-line RP treatment = calcium channel blockers. Beta-blockers are contraindicated in RP.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 5 — Cold and emotional stress triggers Raynaud episodes in SLE patients, reflecting shared vascular reactivity abnormalities between primary RP and lupus-associated secondary Raynaud phenomenon."},{"id":"sdl53_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss triggers for Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"Emotional stress is a recognized non-cold trigger of Raynaud phenomenon. Through which pathway does psychological stress initiate digital vasospasm?","choices":{"A":"Emotional stress activates hypothalamic circuits, particularly the dorsomedial hypothalamus, releasing norepinephrine from sympathetic nerve terminals innervating digital arterioles and arteriovenous anastomoses, triggering alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction in susceptible individuals.","B":"Emotional stress activates the parasympathetic nervous system via the dorsal vagal nucleus, releasing acetylcholine from digital arteriolar nerve terminals that paradoxically activates muscarinic M3 receptors on smooth muscle cells rather than the expected vasodilatory response.","C":"Emotional stress directly activates circulating NK cells that release cytotoxic granule proteins into the digital microvasculature, producing endothelial injury and transient platelet aggregation that mechanically obstructs digital capillary flow during stress episodes.","D":"Emotional stress activates the renin-angiotensin system through amygdala-hypothalamic-pituitary axis signaling, releasing angiotensin II that directly activates AT2 receptors on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells to produce vasospasm during psychological stress.","E":"Emotional stress activates mast cells in digital skin through substance P release from central projections, producing histamine and prostaglandin D2 that paradoxically constrict digital arterioles through H2 receptor activation rather than producing the expected vasodilation."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Emotional stress activates the central nervous system, specifically hypothalamic circuits including the dorsomedial hypothalamus, which coordinates cutaneous vasoconstriction as part of the overall sympathetic response to perceived threat. This leads to increased sympathetic output, releasing norepinephrine from adrenergic nerve terminals that innervate digital arterioles and AVAs. In individuals with Raynaud phenomenon, the exaggerated alpha-2-adrenergic response amplifies the normal vasoconstriction to produce frank digital ischemia. Both cold-induced and emotionally-induced attacks share the same final common pathway of alpha-2 adrenoceptor-mediated smooth muscle contraction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two RP triggers: cold (most common) and emotional stress. Both trigger via sympathetic norepinephrine → alpha-2 adrenoceptor activation. COMLEX: Non-pharmacologic RP management includes cold avoidance, stress reduction, smoking cessation, avoiding sympathomimetic drugs and beta-blockers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 5 — Cold and emotional stress triggers Raynaud episodes in SLE patients, reflecting shared vascular reactivity abnormalities between primary RP and lupus-associated secondary Raynaud phenomenon."},{"id":"sdl53_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with episodic migraine is started on fremanezumab for migraine prevention. Six weeks later she reports new episodes of bilateral finger blanching followed by cyanosis and redness triggered by cold exposure. Her past medical history is otherwise unremarkable and ANA is negative. Which mechanism best explains this adverse effect?","choices":{"A":"Fremanezumab cross-reacts with alpha-2C adrenoceptors on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells due to structural homology between the CGRP receptor and the alpha-2C receptor extracellular domain, directly triggering cold-induced Golgi-to-membrane receptor translocation.","B":"Fremanezumab blocks calcitonin gene-related peptide, a vasodilating neuropeptide from sensory nerve fibers in digital skin that normally counteracts sympathetic vasoconstriction; its blockade removes this protective vasodilatory signal, unmasking latent Raynaud phenomenon in susceptible individuals.","C":"Fremanezumab reduces CGRP signaling in the hypothalamic thermoregulatory center, impairing central temperature sensing and causing the central nervous system to misinterpret neutral temperatures as cold, triggering inappropriate sympathetic vasoconstriction of digital vessels.","D":"Fremanezumab activates complement C3 via the classical pathway through its Fc region, producing circulating C3a anaphylatoxin that selectively acts on digital arteriolar mast cells to release histamine-driven vasoconstriction independent of adrenergic mechanisms.","E":"Fremanezumab inhibits prostacyclin production by endothelial cells through a CGRP-receptor-independent off-target effect on COX-2 in the digital microvascular endothelium, removing a key vasodilatory counterbalance against alpha-adrenergic constriction."},"correct":"B","explanation":"CGRP is a 37-amino-acid vasodilating neuropeptide released from sensory nerve fibers in the skin, including digital tissue. It normally provides a vasodilatory counterbalance to sympathetic noradrenergic vasoconstriction. Case series report that CGRP antagonist monoclonal antibodies used for migraine prevention are associated with onset or worsening of Raynaud phenomenon. This represents a pharmacologically predicted adverse effect given CGRP's role in maintaining basal digital vasodilatory tone. Evidence also shows reduced CGRP-immunoreactive nerve fibers in skin biopsies of primary RP patients, further implicating CGRP deficiency in RP pathogenesis. This patient has primary RP triggered by drug-induced CGRP blockade.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CGRP = vasodilatory sensory neuropeptide in digital skin. CGRP antagonist antibodies (for migraine) → worsen/trigger RP. Reduced CGRP fibers in primary RP skin biopsies also implicated in disease. COMLEX: Assess all RP patients for medication triggers including CGRP antagonists, beta-blockers, ergots, and bleomycin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Pathology of vasculopathy in Raynaud phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma."},{"id":"sdl53_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman with primary Raynaud phenomenon undergoes skin biopsy of a digital lesion. Immunohistochemical staining shows a marked reduction in calcitonin gene-related peptide-immunoreactive nerve fibers in the dermis compared to normal controls. Which mechanism best explains how this finding contributes to her exaggerated vasospastic attacks?","choices":{"A":"Reduced CGRP-containing nerve fibers impair the afferent sensory signaling that detects finger temperature, preventing the central thermoregulatory reflex from activating compensatory systemic warming responses that would normally abort the vasospastic episode.","B":"Reduced CGRP-containing nerve fibers decrease substance P co-release from nociceptive fibers, impairing the axon reflex vasodilation that normally recruits additional blood flow to inflamed tissue adjacent to ischemic digital arteries during vasospastic attacks.","C":"Reduced CGRP-containing nerve fibers decrease neuropeptide Y co-release from sympathetic terminals, removing the autocrine inhibition that normally limits norepinephrine release from digital adrenergic nerve endings and prevents excessive alpha-2C receptor activation.","D":"Reduced CGRP-containing nerve fibers impair the perivascular anti-inflammatory signal that normally prevents mast cell degranulation in digital skin, enabling histamine-driven platelet aggregation and thromboxane A2 release during cold-induced vasospastic episodes.","E":"Reduced sensory nerve CGRP-containing fibers remove a critical vasodilatory counterbalance in digital skin; without adequate CGRP release during cold exposure, the sympathetic alpha-2-adrenergic vasoconstriction is unopposed, producing exaggerated and prolonged digital vasospasm."},"correct":"E","explanation":"CGRP-containing sensory nerve fibers in digital skin release CGRP in response to tissue stimuli, providing vasodilatory tone that counterbalances sympathetic vasoconstriction. A reduction in these fibers removes this protective anti-vasospastic signal. Without adequate CGRP release, the alpha-2-adrenergic vasoconstriction from sympathetic nerve stimulation is not opposed by sensory nerve vasodilation. This contributes to the exaggerated and prolonged vasoconstriction of Raynaud attacks. This mechanism is supported by the clinical observation that migraine treatments blocking CGRP trigger Raynaud episodes. CGRP also has NO-releasing effects on endothelium, further contributing to its vasodilatory role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CGRP from sensory nerve fibers = vasodilatory counterbalance. Reduced CGRP fibers in primary RP skin = unopposed alpha-2 vasoconstriction. COMLEX: Both the sensory nervous system and the sympathetic nervous system regulate digital vascular tone in opposing directions; imbalance toward sympathetic predominance drives RP.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Pathology of vasculopathy in Raynaud phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma."},{"id":"sdl53_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old premenopausal woman has had primary Raynaud phenomenon since age 18. Her symptoms improve significantly after she reaches menopause at age 52. Which mechanism best explains the estrogen-related female predominance and the improvement of primary Raynaud phenomenon after menopause?","choices":{"A":"Estrogen upregulates alpha-2C adrenoceptor expression on cutaneous arteriolar smooth muscle cells, augmenting cold-induced vasoconstriction; when estrogen levels fall at menopause, alpha-2C receptor density decreases and cold-induced vasoconstriction is reduced, explaining both the female predominance and menopausal improvement.","B":"Estrogen increases sympathetic nerve fiber density in digital arteriolar smooth muscle, creating more adrenergic terminals per vessel and amplifying norepinephrine release during cold; menopause reduces nerve fiber density and attenuates the noradrenergic trigger of vasospastic attacks.","C":"Estrogen directly activates TRPM8 cold receptors on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells through a genomic receptor mechanism, increasing the cells sensitivity to cold temperatures; when estrogen declines at menopause, TRPM8 receptor sensitivity decreases and vasospastic attacks become less frequent.","D":"Estrogen suppresses endothelial nitric oxide synthase transcription in digital arteriolar endothelium, reducing the vasodilatory counterbalance against sympathetic vasoconstriction; menopause restores eNOS expression and increases nitric oxide production, reducing vasospastic episodes.","E":"Estrogen promotes mast cell accumulation in digital skin through recruitment signals from estrogen receptor-expressing perivascular stromal cells; at menopause, reduced mast cell density decreases histamine-driven platelet activation that amplifies alpha-adrenergic digital vasoconstriction."},"correct":"A","explanation":"In vitro studies demonstrate that estrogen upregulates alpha-2C adrenoceptor expression on cutaneous vessels through the Epac/JNK/AP-1 signaling pathway. Experimental estrogen administration increases alpha-2C adrenoceptor density, leading to potentiated cold-induced vasoconstriction in mouse tail arteries. Epidemiologically, primary RP is 3-9 times more common in premenopausal females than age-matched males, and symptoms frequently improve after menopause when estrogen levels fall. Unopposed estrogen use in postmenopausal women is also associated with increased RP risk in epidemiologic studies. This explains why RP is more prevalent in young women during their reproductive years.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Estrogen upregulates alpha-2C adrenoceptors → augments cold-induced vasoconstriction → explains female predominance and menopausal improvement of primary RP. COMLEX: Primary RP = young female, onset age 15-30, symmetric, benign, improves with age/menopause.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Pathology of vasculopathy in Raynaud phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma."},{"id":"sdl53_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary Raynaud's as well as underlying diseases associated with secondary causes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman presents with progressive bilateral finger color changes triggered by cold and asymmetric severity between her hands. Nailfold capillaroscopy shows dilated, distorted capillary loops, avascular areas, and giant capillaries. ANA is positive with anti-Scl-70 detected. Which statement best explains what the capillaroscopy findings indicate about her Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Dilated capillary loops with avascular areas on nailfold capillaroscopy indicate excessive arteriovenous anastomosis dilation from endothelial prostacyclin overproduction in primary Raynaud phenomenon, representing a compensatory response to recurrent vasospastic ischemia.","B":"Dilated capillary loops with avascular areas on nailfold capillaroscopy indicate complement-mediated destruction of the deep dermal venous plexus, producing the same pattern regardless of whether the Raynaud phenomenon is primary or secondary to connective tissue disease.","C":"Dilated, tortuous, and disrupted capillary loops with avascular areas on nailfold capillaroscopy indicate structural microangiopathy in the nutritional capillary bed, characteristic of secondary Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis and indicating compromised nutritional tissue perfusion.","D":"Dilated capillary loops with avascular areas on nailfold capillaroscopy represent a normal physiologic response to chronic repetitive cold exposure in any Raynaud patient and do not distinguish primary from secondary Raynaud phenomenon in clinical evaluation.","E":"Dilated capillary loops with avascular areas on nailfold capillaroscopy indicate excessive alpha-2C adrenoceptor density on nutritional capillary pericytes, producing a pattern specific to primary Raynaud phenomenon where the local defect is most pronounced in small nutritional capillaries."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nailfold capillaroscopy is the key test for distinguishing primary from secondary Raynaud phenomenon. Normal: regular, parallel capillary loops. Primary RP: normal capillaroscopy. Secondary RP (SSc pattern): dilated capillary loops, giant capillaries, avascular areas, hemorrhages, and disorganized architecture. These findings reflect structural microvascular damage with intimal fibrosis and capillary loss that characterizes SSc microangiopathy. Anti-Scl-70 confirms SSc. The avascular areas indicate capillary loss/dropout, meaning nutritional tissue flow is impaired even at rest, explaining the risk of digital ulceration and critical ischemia that distinguishes secondary RP from primary RP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nailfold capillaroscopy: normal = primary RP; abnormal (giant capillaries, avascular areas, dropout) = secondary RP, especially SSc. Giant capillaries are pathognomonic for SSc. COMLEX: Anti-Scl-70 = diffuse SSc + ILD; anticentromere = limited SSc + CREST + PAH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — Contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis."},{"id":"sdl53_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary Raynaud's as well as underlying diseases associated with secondary causes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with longstanding hypothyroidism and poor medication compliance presents with bilateral symmetric finger pallor and cyanosis triggered by modest cold exposure that developed over the past year. His thyroid-stimulating hormone is markedly elevated. Which mechanism best explains hypothyroidism as a cause of secondary Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Hypothyroidism directly upregulates TRPM8 cold receptor expression on digital arteriolar endothelial cells through thyroid hormone response element-mediated suppression, causing these vessels to respond to normal ambient temperatures as if exposed to extreme cold.","B":"Hypothyroidism elevates circulating thyroid-stimulating hormone levels, which acts directly on TSH receptors on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells to activate G-protein-mediated phospholipase C, increasing intracellular calcium and producing sustained vasospasm.","C":"Hypothyroidism decreases alpha-2C adrenoceptor expression through reduced thyroid hormone-mediated receptor gene transcription, producing a paradoxical state where cold stimulation induces massive receptor upregulation from a lower baseline, generating an exaggerated vasospastic response.","D":"Hypothyroidism reduces basal metabolic rate and cardiac output, decreasing peripheral tissue perfusion and blood flow; it also impairs vascular smooth muscle relaxation by reducing the sensitivity of adrenergic receptors to vasodilatory beta-adrenergic signals, leaving alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction relatively unopposed during cold exposure.","E":"Hypothyroidism increases plasma fibrinogen and blood viscosity through reduced hepatic fibrinogen clearance, producing sluggish digital capillary flow that slows local temperature equilibration and amplifies the duration of cold-induced vasoconstriction in affected fingers."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hypothyroidism causes secondary Raynaud phenomenon through multiple mechanisms: reduced cardiac output and peripheral perfusion at baseline; impaired vasodilatory responses (thyroid hormone normally sensitizes vessels to beta-adrenergic vasodilation); increased peripheral vascular resistance; and potential effects on sympathetic nervous system sensitivity. Elevated TSH does not directly act on digital vessels. Treatment of hypothyroidism often improves RP. Other endocrine causes of secondary RP include pheochromocytoma (excess catecholamines) and carcinoid syndrome. The important clinical point is that secondary RP may reveal an underlying treatable systemic condition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothyroidism → secondary RP via reduced cardiac output and impaired vasodilation. Screen any new secondary RP for thyroid disease with TSH. COMLEX: Secondary RP evaluation: ANA, anti-Scl-70, anticentromere, CBC, CMP, TSH, urinalysis, nailfold capillaroscopy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — Contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis."},{"id":"sdl53_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary Raynaud's as well as underlying diseases associated with secondary causes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old man presents with finger blanching and cyanosis involving predominantly the first two fingers of his right hand but sparing his left hand. He has no known connective tissue disease. His presentation is being evaluated for primary versus secondary Raynaud phenomenon. Which feature of his presentation most strongly suggests secondary rather than primary Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"The male sex of this patient strongly suggests secondary Raynaud phenomenon, as primary Raynaud phenomenon almost exclusively affects females and the occurrence in males should always prompt evaluation for an underlying systemic vascular disease.","B":"The involvement of only two fingers rather than all fingers of both hands strongly suggests secondary Raynaud phenomenon, as primary Raynaud phenomenon requires involvement of all ten digits to fulfill the diagnostic criteria established by international classification guidelines.","C":"The age of onset at 45 years rather than during adolescence strongly suggests secondary Raynaud phenomenon, as primary Raynaud phenomenon exclusively occurs before age 30 and any new-onset vasospastic disease in adults over 40 is by definition secondary.","D":"The occurrence of isolated finger blanching without the complete triphasic white-blue-red color change strongly suggests secondary Raynaud phenomenon, as primary Raynaud phenomenon requires all three color changes to be present on physical examination for diagnosis.","E":"The asymmetric distribution, with involvement confined to specific fingers of one hand rather than bilateral symmetric involvement, strongly suggests secondary Raynaud phenomenon from localized vascular disease affecting specific vessels rather than a systemic sympathetic hyperreactivity pattern."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Asymmetric involvement is a key clinical feature favoring secondary over primary Raynaud phenomenon. Primary RP typically causes symmetric bilateral involvement of both hands with similar digit distribution. Asymmetric or unilateral Raynaud suggests localized vascular pathology such as atherosclerosis, Buerger disease, thoracic outlet syndrome, emboli, or local injury to specific vessels. Male sex is a relative indicator for secondary RP (primary is more common in females, but males do get primary RP). Age over 40 at onset suggests secondary RP. The complete triphasic color change is not required for diagnosis of either form. Any of these features — asymmetry, male sex, older onset, progressive worsening — should prompt evaluation for secondary causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Red flags for secondary RP: asymmetric/unilateral involvement, age >30 at onset, male sex, progressive worsening, abnormal nailfold capillaroscopy, positive ANA or specific autoantibodies. COMLEX: Thoracic outlet syndrome can cause unilateral upper extremity RP from compression of the subclavian artery or brachial plexus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — Contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis."},{"id":"sdl53_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss triggers for Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 52-year-old male construction worker who has operated pneumatic jackhammers for 20 years presents with bilateral finger blanching and numbness triggered by cold, which has progressively worsened over the past 5 years. He denies joint symptoms, skin changes, or systemic symptoms. Which mechanism best explains his occupationally acquired Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Chronic repetitive vibration exposure causes direct structural damage to digital arteriolar walls and neural dysfunction in digital nerves, producing both a fixed vasculopathic component from vessel wall injury and abnormal vascular reactivity from impaired vasomotor nerve function.","B":"Chronic repetitive vibration exposure selectively activates TRPM8 cold receptors in digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells, producing permanent upregulation of these receptors that causes inappropriate cold perception at normal temperatures in occupationally exposed workers.","C":"Chronic repetitive vibration exposure causes repetitive micro-fractures of the periosteal attachments of digital flexor tendons, releasing bone morphogenetic protein-2 that stimulates digital arteriolar smooth muscle cell transdifferentiation into bone-forming cells and reduces vessel lumen diameter.","D":"Chronic repetitive vibration exposure activates the NLRP3 inflammasome in digital skin macrophages through mechanical DAMP signals, releasing IL-1beta that promotes alpha-2C adrenoceptor overexpression on smooth muscle cells through an NFkB-mediated transcriptional mechanism.","E":"Chronic repetitive vibration exposure produces supraphysiologic norepinephrine release from digital sympathetic nerve terminals, exhausting norepinephrine stores and causing paradoxical denervation hypersensitivity of alpha-2C adrenoceptors on smooth muscle cells."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vibration-associated Raynaud phenomenon, also called hand-arm vibration syndrome or vibration white finger, is an occupational secondary RP caused by long-term use of vibrating tools such as jackhammers, chainsaws, and grinding tools. The mechanism involves direct vascular injury to digital artery walls and neural dysfunction from mechanical trauma to digital nerves. Biomarkers of endothelial injury and dysfunction, inflammation, and neuronal injury are elevated in affected workers. This results in both structural vessel wall damage and abnormal vascular reactivity. The condition is progressive with continued exposure and may not fully reverse with job change. Recognition is important for occupational health.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vibration white finger = occupational secondary RP from pneumatic tools, chainsaws. Direct vessel wall and nerve injury. Progressive with continued exposure. COMLEX: Occupational history is essential in RP evaluation. Hand-arm vibration syndrome is a reportable occupational disease in some jurisdictions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 5 — Broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations in SLE patients."},{"id":"sdl53_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss triggers for Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman with hepatitis C virus infection and mixed cryoglobulinemia develops episodic finger blanching, cyanosis, and purpuric lesions that are triggered by cold exposure. Her symptoms are more severe and persistent than typical vasospasm. Which mechanism explains cryoglobulinemia as a cause of Raynaud-like digital ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Cryoglobulins act as autoantibodies directed against alpha-2C adrenoceptors on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells, activating these receptors at cold temperatures through a conformational change in the immunoglobulin Fc region that mimics norepinephrine binding.","B":"Cryoglobulins deposit in digital arteriolar walls as immune complexes that activate complement exclusively through the alternative pathway, producing membrane attack complex formation in smooth muscle cells and causing irreversible lysis of digital arteriolar smooth muscle.","C":"Cryoglobulins are immunoglobulins that reversibly precipitate in cold temperatures, physically obstructing digital microvascular lumens during cold exposure and activating complement and platelet aggregation, producing a vasoconstrictive-thrombotic combined mechanism of digital ischemia.","D":"Cryoglobulins bind directly to endothelial TRPM8 cold receptors on digital capillaries, acting as permanent cold receptor agonists that maintain TRPM8 in a constitutively active state even at normal ambient temperature and producing temperature-independent vasospasm.","E":"Cryoglobulins are released from activated mast cells during cold-induced degranulation in digital skin, providing an IgE-mediated triggering mechanism that produces histamine-driven digital arteriolar constriction rather than the classic adrenergic mechanism of primary Raynaud phenomenon."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cryoglobulins are immunoglobulins that reversibly precipitate at temperatures below 37°C (98.6°F). In cold conditions, cryoglobulins form insoluble aggregates that can physically obstruct digital microvascular lumens, triggering complement activation and platelet aggregation at the site of precipitation. This creates a unique combined vasoconstrictive-thrombotic mechanism distinct from primary RP. HCV-associated mixed cryoglobulinemia (type II) involves monoclonal IgM with rheumatoid factor activity complexed with polyclonal IgG. Clinical features include palpable purpura, arthralgias, neuropathy, and Raynaud-like symptoms. Treatment targets the underlying HCV infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cryoglobulinemia + cold = precipitated Ig → microvascular obstruction + complement activation. HCV = most common cause of mixed cryoglobulinemia. Triad: purpura + arthralgia + neuropathy. COMLEX: Secondary RP hematologic causes: cryoglobulinemia, polycythemia vera, paraproteinemia (increased blood viscosity).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 5 — Broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations in SLE patients."},{"id":"sdl53_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss triggers for Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with hypertension and primary Raynaud phenomenon has his antihypertensive regimen changed from amlodipine to atenolol by his cardiologist. Within three weeks his Raynaud episodes become significantly more frequent and severe. Which mechanism explains why beta-blockers worsen Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Atenolol crosses the blood-brain barrier and directly activates alpha-2C adrenoceptor transcription in the hypothalamus, increasing the central sympathetic output to digital arterioles and amplifying the cold-induced vasoconstriction of Raynaud phenomenon through a central mechanism.","B":"Beta-adrenergic receptor blockade prevents beta-2-mediated compensatory vasodilation in peripheral cutaneous vessels, leaving the alpha-adrenergic vasoconstrictive effect of norepinephrine unopposed during cold exposure and emotional stress, worsening the frequency and severity of vasospastic attacks.","C":"Atenolol causes peripheral edema by reducing cardiac output, increasing venous hydrostatic pressure in digital capillaries and displacing erythrocytes from the microcirculation into the interstitium, producing a mechanical reduction in digital capillary blood flow that mimics vasospasm.","D":"Atenolol reduces renal prostaglandin synthesis by blocking beta-1 receptors on juxtaglomerular cells, decreasing renin-mediated prostaglandin E2 production that would otherwise provide vasodilatory protection to digital arterioles during cold-induced vasospastic attacks.","E":"Atenolol directly activates TRPM8 cold receptors in digital arteriolar smooth muscle through an off-target ion channel mechanism, lowering the temperature threshold for cold-sensing and causing vasospastic attacks to be triggered by warmer ambient temperatures than before."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Beta-2 adrenergic receptors on vascular smooth muscle mediate vasodilation when activated by circulating epinephrine or local beta-adrenergic agonists. This beta-2-mediated vasodilation acts as a physiologic counterbalance to alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction. When beta-blockers are administered, this compensatory vasodilatory mechanism is blocked, leaving alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction unopposed. In patients with Raynaud phenomenon, this unmasking of alpha-adrenergic dominance worsens vasospastic attacks. Atenolol is a beta-1 selective blocker but still has some beta-2 blocking activity peripherally at higher doses. All beta-blockers should be avoided in patients with Raynaud phenomenon; calcium channel blockers such as amlodipine or nifedipine are preferred antihypertensives in these patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Beta-blockers worsen RP by blocking beta-2-mediated compensatory vasodilation, leaving alpha-adrenergic constriction unopposed. Contraindicated in RP. COMLEX: Preferred antihypertensives in RP: calcium channel blockers (nifedipine, amlodipine) which also treat RP. Avoid: beta-blockers, sympathomimetics, ergots.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 5 — Broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations in SLE patients."}]},{"sdlNumber":54,"title":"Bone Tumors: Benign vs Malignant","questions":[{"id":"sdl54_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between benign and malignant bone lesions and their location in bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which bone tumor is most commonly found in the metaphysis of long bones and characteristically contains a cartilage cap overlying a bony exostosis?","choices":{"A":"Osteochondroma, the most common benign bone tumor, arises at the metaphysis near the growth plate and is capped by hyaline cartilage","B":"Enchondroma, which arises in the medullary cavity of small tubular bones in the hands and feet and is not capped by external cartilage","C":"Chondroblastoma, which arises in the epiphysis of long bones in skeletally immature patients and does not project externally from cortical bone","D":"Giant cell tumor, which arises in the epiphysis of skeletally mature long bones and is not associated with a cartilage cap or cortical exostosis","E":"Ewing sarcoma, which arises in the diaphysis of long bones and presents as an aggressive lytic lesion rather than a cartilage-capped exostosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteochondroma is the most common benign bone tumor and arises at the metaphysis adjacent to the growth plate. It projects outward from the cortex as a bony stalk capped by hyaline cartilage. The cartilage cap is the key distinguishing feature; malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma is heralded by a cap thickening >2 cm. Enchondromas occur in medullary bone of small tubular bones. Chondroblastomas and giant cell tumors are epiphyseal. Ewing sarcoma is diaphyseal and aggressive.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteochondroma is THE most common benign bone tumor — metaphysis, cartilage cap, near growth plate. HME (hereditary multiple exostoses) caused by EXT1/EXT2 loss-of-function mutations; increased malignant transformation risk. COMLEX: Palpable painless mass near a joint in a teenager — think osteochondroma first.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 55 → Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and remodeling as the cellular context for tumor development and location"},{"id":"sdl54_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between benign and malignant bone lesions and their location in bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 15-year-old male presents with worsening knee pain and a palpable mass. X-ray shows a sunburst pattern and Codman triangle at the distal femur. Which of the following best describes the location rule for this tumor type?","choices":{"A":"Osteosarcoma characteristically arises in the epiphysis of long bones, explaining its association with the subchondral bone and articular surface","B":"Osteosarcoma characteristically arises in the diaphysis of long bones, where Haversian canal disruption provides the initial nidus for tumor growth","C":"Osteosarcoma characteristically arises in the vertebral posterior elements, which is why it frequently causes neurologic compromise at presentation","D":"Osteosarcoma characteristically arises in flat bones of the axial skeleton, particularly the pelvis, as the site of highest red marrow activity in adolescents","E":"Osteosarcoma characteristically arises in the metaphysis of long bones, with the distal femur and proximal tibia being the most common sites"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Osteosarcoma is the most common primary malignant bone tumor in adolescents and arises in the metaphysis of long bones — distal femur (most common), proximal tibia, and proximal humerus together account for the majority of cases. The metaphysis is the fastest-growing region of long bones, explaining the predilection. Periosteal elevation creates the Codman triangle, and tumor extension into soft tissue produces the sunburst pattern on X-ray. Ewing sarcoma is the diaphyseal malignancy in this age group. Chondrosarcoma affects the axial skeleton in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic board vignette — teenage male, knee pain, sunburst/Codman triangle, distal femur = osteosarcoma until proven otherwise. Bimodal distribution: peak 10-20 years (de novo) and >65 years (secondary to Paget's, radiation, bone infarct). COMLEX: Bone pain at night that is NOT relieved by aspirin in a teenager near the knee = osteosarcoma (contrast with osteoid osteoma, which IS aspirin-responsive).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 55 → Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and remodeling as the cellular context for tumor development and location"},{"id":"sdl54_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between benign and malignant bone lesions and their location in bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old man presents with dull aching pain in the pelvis. MRI reveals a lobulated intramedullary lesion with a chondroid matrix in the iliac bone. Biopsy shows atypical chondrocytes in lacunae without osteoid production. Which tumor type does this lesion most likely represent?","choices":{"A":"Osteosarcoma, which produces malignant osteoid as its defining histologic feature and primarily affects the metaphysis of long bones in adolescents","B":"Enchondroma, which is a benign cartilage tumor of the medullary cavity that most commonly affects the small tubular bones of the hands and feet","C":"Osteochondroma, which is a cartilage-capped exostosis arising at the metaphysis and is the most common benign bone tumor overall","D":"Chondrosarcoma, which preferentially involves the axial skeleton and pelvis in middle-aged to older adults and does not produce osteoid","E":"Ewing sarcoma, which involves the diaphysis of long bones and flat bones and histologically shows small round blue cells rather than atypical chondrocytes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chondrosarcoma is the second most common primary malignant bone tumor, following osteosarcoma. It preferentially arises in the axial skeleton — pelvis, shoulder girdle, and ribs — in adults aged 40-60 years, with a male predominance of approximately 2:1. Histologically, atypical chondrocytes in lacunae are the defining feature; no osteoid is produced. Chondrosarcoma is largely chemotherapy-resistant, making wide surgical resection the primary treatment. Osteosarcoma requires osteoid production for diagnosis. Enchondroma is its benign counterpart in small tubular bones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chondrosarcoma — adults, axial/pelvic location, chondroid matrix, atypical chondrocytes in lacunae, NO osteoid, chemotherapy resistant (surgery only). Second most common primary malignant bone tumor after osteosarcoma. Enchondroma → chondrosarcoma transformation risk is elevated in Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome (IDH1/IDH2 mutations). COMLEX: Pelvic bone lesion with chondroid matrix in a middle-aged male = chondrosarcoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 55 → Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and remodeling as the cellular context for tumor development and location"},{"id":"sdl54_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring with osteosarcomas.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following histologic features is required to establish a definitive diagnosis of osteosarcoma?","choices":{"A":"Production of lacelike osteoid directly by malignant stromal cells is the single required histologic criterion for diagnosing osteosarcoma","B":"Periosteal elevation forming a Codman triangle is the definitive histologic criterion, as it reflects tumor expansion through the cortex to the periosteum","C":"Presence of small round blue cells arranged in sheets with Homer Wright pseudorosettes distinguishes osteosarcoma from other malignant bone tumors","D":"Identification of atypical chondrocytes within lacunae surrounded by a chondroid matrix is the pathognomonic finding required for osteosarcoma diagnosis","E":"High mitotic index greater than ten mitoses per ten high-power fields in a spindle cell neoplasm arising in bone establishes the diagnosis of osteosarcoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteosarcoma is defined histologically by the production of lacelike osteoid by malignant mesenchymal cells. This is the single required criterion — without osteoid production, the tumor cannot be classified as osteosarcoma regardless of other findings. The cells show marked pleomorphism, hyperchromatic nuclei, and abnormal mitoses. Codman triangle is a radiographic finding, not histologic. Small round blue cells with pseudorosettes describe Ewing sarcoma. Atypical chondrocytes in lacunae describe chondrosarcoma. High mitotic index in a spindle cell tumor without osteoid production would suggest fibrosarcoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The most testable osteosarcoma histology point — malignant cells producing LACELIKE OSTEOID is the REQUIRED histologic finding. Pleomorphism and mitoses are expected but not defining. Codman triangle and sunburst are radiographic, not histologic. COMLEX: Bone biopsy showing atypical spindle cells producing pink amorphous material = osteoid = osteosarcoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 7 — Paget's disease of the bone as a differential for osteosarcoma in older patients"},{"id":"sdl54_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring with osteosarcomas.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl is newly diagnosed with osteosarcoma. Genetic testing reveals a germline mutation in the RB1 gene. Compared to patients with sporadic osteosarcoma, patients with germline RB1 mutations have which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Identical osteosarcoma risk to sporadic cases, because RB1 mutations only predispose to retinoblastoma and do not influence bone tumor formation","B":"Modestly elevated risk of about 5-fold compared to the general population, as RB1 loss alone is insufficient without additional oncogenic co-mutations","C":"Approximately 1000-fold increased lifetime risk of developing osteosarcoma compared to the general population due to loss of the RB1 tumor suppressor","D":"Reduced osteosarcoma risk compared to sporadic cases because germline mutations trigger p53-mediated apoptosis before malignant transformation occurs","E":"Elevated risk exclusively for the low-grade parosteal subtype of osteosarcoma rather than the conventional high-grade intramedullary variant"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Germline RB1 mutations cause hereditary retinoblastoma and confer approximately a 1000-fold increased risk of osteosarcoma compared to the general population. RB1 mutations are found in approximately 70% of sporadic osteosarcomas as somatic events, making Rb pathway disruption central to osteosarcoma pathogenesis. The mechanism involves loss of G1/S checkpoint control. Other genetic associations include TP53 (Li-Fraumeni syndrome), CDKN2A (p16/p14ARF), and MDM2/CDK4 amplification in low-grade osteosarcomas on chromosome 12q14. MYC amplification in approximately 50% of cases correlates with poor prognosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RB1 mutations — sporadic 70%, germline = 1000-fold osteosarcoma risk. TP53 mutations = Li-Fraumeni syndrome → osteosarcoma. MDM2/CDK4 amplification on chr 12q14 = low-grade osteosarcoma variant. MYC amplification ~50% = poor prognosis. COMLEX: Young patient with prior retinoblastoma develops bone pain near the knee in adolescence — screen for second primary osteosarcoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 7 — Paget's disease of the bone as a differential for osteosarcoma in older patients"},{"id":"sdl54_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring with osteosarcomas.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the standard first-line treatment approach for high-grade osteosarcoma of the extremity, and what is the approximate 5-year survival without distant metastasis?","choices":{"A":"Immediate amputation followed by adjuvant radiation therapy is the standard approach, achieving approximately 40% five-year survival regardless of tumor extent","B":"Radiation therapy alone as the primary local control modality achieves approximately 60% five-year survival because osteosarcoma is highly radiosensitive","C":"Surgery alone without chemotherapy is appropriate for metaphyseal tumors under 5 cm and achieves approximately 85% five-year survival in this selected group","D":"Neoadjuvant chemotherapy alone without surgery is preferred in adolescents to preserve growth plate function, achieving 50% five-year survival in non-metastatic cases","E":"Neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by limb-salvage surgery and adjuvant chemotherapy achieves approximately 70% five-year survival in non-metastatic disease"},"correct":"E","explanation":"High-grade osteosarcoma is treated with a trimodal approach: neoadjuvant chemotherapy (typically MAP — methotrexate, doxorubicin, cisplatin) followed by limb-salvage surgery, then adjuvant chemotherapy. This approach achieves approximately 65-70% five-year survival in non-metastatic disease, a dramatic improvement over the pre-chemotherapy era when amputation alone yielded <20% survival. With pulmonary metastases present at diagnosis, five-year survival drops below 20%. The response to neoadjuvant therapy (assessed histologically as percent tumor necrosis in the resected specimen) is the strongest prognostic factor. Osteosarcoma is relatively radioresistant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteosarcoma = neoadjuvant chemo (MAP protocol) + limb-salvage surgery + adjuvant chemo. 5-year survival: ~70% without metastasis, <20% with lung mets. Osteosarcoma is RADIORESISTANT (unlike Ewing sarcoma which is radiosensitive). Histologic response to neoadjuvant chemo = strongest prognostic factor. COMLEX: Lung is the primary metastatic site (hematogenous spread via cortical veins).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 7 — Paget's disease of the bone as a differential for osteosarcoma in older patients"},{"id":"sdl54_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between benign and malignant bone lesions and their location in bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old male presents with a 3-month history of dull aching in his right thigh that is worse at night. Vital signs are normal. Physical exam shows mild swelling over the mid-diaphysis of the femur. X-ray reveals a poorly defined lytic lesion with an \"onion-skin\" periosteal reaction and no Codman triangle. CBC shows leukocytosis and elevated ESR. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Osteosarcoma, which typically arises in the metaphysis of long bones in adolescents and produces a sunburst pattern and Codman triangle rather than an onion-skin reaction","B":"Osteomyelitis, which can mimic Ewing sarcoma radiographically but typically presents with fever, localized warmth, and positive blood cultures in a shorter symptomatic course","C":"Ewing sarcoma, which characteristically arises in the diaphysis of long bones and flat bones, primarily in patients aged 10-20 years, with a classic onion-skin periosteal reaction","D":"Chondrosarcoma, which arises in the axial skeleton of middle-aged adults and produces a chondroid matrix on imaging rather than a pure lytic diaphyseal lesion","E":"Giant cell tumor, which arises in the epiphysis of skeletally mature patients as a soap-bubble lytic lesion and does not produce a periosteal reaction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ewing sarcoma is a small round blue cell tumor that arises in the diaphysis of long bones and flat bones (pelvis, ribs, vertebrae) and peaks in patients aged 10-20 years. The onion-skin periosteal reaction reflects successive layers of reactive bone laid down as the tumor elevates the periosteum. Systemic features (fever, leukocytosis, elevated ESR) are common and can mimic osteomyelitis. The EWSR1-FLI1 translocation (t(11;22)) is pathognomonic. Osteosarcoma is metaphyseal with a sunburst/Codman pattern. Chondrosarcoma and GCT are adult tumors at different locations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ewing sarcoma — diaphysis, 10-20 years, onion-skin periosteal reaction, small round blue cells, t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1 fusion. Radiosensitive (unlike osteosarcoma). Key differential: osteomyelitis (can look identical on imaging — biopsy required). COMLEX: Flat bone or diaphyseal bone lesion in a teenager with systemic symptoms — Ewing sarcoma until proven otherwise.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and remodeling as the cellular context for tumor development"},{"id":"sdl54_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between benign and malignant bone lesions and their location in bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman undergoes X-ray for a sprained wrist and is incidentally found to have a well-defined radiolucent lesion in the proximal phalanx of her ring finger. There is no periosteal reaction and no cortical destruction. She is asymptomatic. Biopsy shows bland chondrocytes in a chondroid matrix with no atypia. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and its associated genetic finding?","choices":{"A":"Enchondroma with IDH1 or IDH2 somatic mutations, which arise in the medullary cavity of small tubular bones and are most often asymptomatic and incidentally discovered","B":"Osteochondroma with EXT1 or EXT2 mutations, which arises at the metaphysis as an external exostosis rather than as an intramedullary lesion of the phalanges","C":"Chondrosarcoma with IDH1 mutations, which is the malignant transformation of this lesion and should be diagnosed based on the presence of bland chondrocytes without atypia","D":"Chondroblastoma with H3F3B mutations, which is an epiphyseal lesion of skeletally immature patients rather than a medullary lesion of the small bones of the hand","E":"Osteoid osteoma with no known recurrent mutation, which arises in the cortex of long bones and characteristically produces severe nocturnal pain relieved by aspirin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enchondromas are benign cartilage tumors that arise in the medullary cavity, most commonly in the small tubular bones of the hands and feet, and are often incidentally discovered. They harbor IDH1 or IDH2 somatic mutations. Histologically, bland chondrocytes without atypia are seen in a chondroid matrix. Multiple enchondromas characterize Ollier disease; when combined with spindle cell hemangiomas, this constitutes Maffucci syndrome, both of which carry elevated risk of chondrosarcoma transformation. Osteochondromas are external cortical lesions at the metaphysis with EXT1/EXT2 mutations. Chondroblastoma is epiphyseal in skeletally immature patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enchondroma — medullary cavity, small bones of hand, IDH1/IDH2 mutations, bland chondrocytes, incidental finding. Ollier disease = multiple enchondromas. Maffucci syndrome = enchondromas + spindle cell hemangiomas (both IDH-mutant). Malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma is a risk in both syndromes. COMLEX: Incidental radiolucent lesion in a finger phalanx in a young adult = enchondroma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and remodeling as the cellular context for tumor development"},{"id":"sdl54_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between benign and malignant bone lesions and their location in bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old man presents with knee pain and swelling. X-ray shows a lytic, soap-bubble lesion in the distal femoral epiphysis that extends to the articular surface. There is no periosteal reaction. He is skeletally mature. Biopsy reveals osteoclast-like giant cells interspersed with mononuclear stromal cells. The mononuclear cells are RANKL-positive. Which of the following best describes this tumor's behavior?","choices":{"A":"Giant cell tumor of bone is universally benign with essentially zero malignant potential, and simple observation without surgical intervention is the standard of care for all cases","B":"Giant cell tumor of bone is locally aggressive and has a high local recurrence rate after curettage, with approximately 1-2% undergoing malignant transformation to a sarcoma","C":"Giant cell tumor of bone is best classified as a high-grade sarcoma at diagnosis because RANKL-positive mononuclear cells drive aggressive bone destruction from the outset","D":"Giant cell tumor of bone has a 40-50% rate of malignant transformation to osteosarcoma within 5 years, requiring neoadjuvant chemotherapy before any surgical planning","E":"Giant cell tumor of bone is a purely reactive lesion driven by inflammation and resolves spontaneously following NSAIDs and physiotherapy without surgical intervention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Giant cell tumor of bone is a locally aggressive benign tumor characterized by RANKL-expressing mononuclear stromal cells that recruit and activate osteoclast-like giant cells to drive bone destruction. It arises in the epiphysis of skeletally mature long bones, most commonly the distal femur, proximal tibia, and distal radius, and may extend to the articular surface. The soap-bubble appearance on X-ray is classic. Treatment is extended curettage with adjuvant therapy; local recurrence rate is 25-50% after simple curettage, reduced to 10-15% with adjuvant methods. Malignant transformation occurs in approximately 1-2%. Denosumab targets RANKL and is used for unresectable or recurrent disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Giant cell tumor — EPIPHYSIS, skeletally MATURE, soap-bubble lytic lesion, osteoclast-like giant cells, RANKL-positive stromal cells. Locally aggressive but benign (~1-2% malignant transformation). Denosumab (anti-RANKL) for unresectable disease. COMLEX: Knee pain + soap-bubble epiphyseal lytic lesion in a 25-35 year old = giant cell tumor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and remodeling as the cellular context for tumor development"},{"id":"sdl54_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring with osteosarcomas.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old male undergoes resection of a distal femoral osteosarcoma following neoadjuvant chemotherapy. Histologic analysis of the resected specimen shows 60% viable tumor and 40% necrosis. His treatment team discusses the significance of this finding. Which of the following best describes the prognostic implication of this result?","choices":{"A":"Greater than 50% tumor necrosis is the threshold for a good histologic response, and this patient's 40% necrosis exceeds the threshold required for favorable prognosis","B":"Histologic response to neoadjuvant chemotherapy is prognostically irrelevant because survival in osteosarcoma is determined exclusively by the presence or absence of lung metastases at diagnosis","C":"Less than 90% tumor necrosis following neoadjuvant chemotherapy indicates a poor histologic response and is associated with significantly worse overall survival","D":"Greater than 30% tumor necrosis is considered an excellent response because osteosarcoma is intrinsically chemoresistant, making any necrosis above this threshold clinically significant","E":"Tumor necrosis percentage is used only to guide radiation planning and has no direct bearing on chemotherapy regimen adjustment or overall prognosis in osteosarcoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The histologic response to neoadjuvant chemotherapy — quantified as the percentage of tumor necrosis in the resected specimen — is the single strongest prognostic factor in osteosarcoma. Good response is defined as greater than or equal to 90% necrosis (Huvos grade III/IV); poor response is less than 90% necrosis. This patient with 60% viable tumor has only 40% necrosis, indicating a poor histologic response and significantly worse overall survival compared to good responders. Poor responders are typically switched to alternative salvage chemotherapy regimens for the adjuvant phase, though improving outcomes with this strategy remains an active area of investigation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteosarcoma prognosis — histologic response to neoadjuvant chemo is THE strongest prognostic factor. Good response = at least 90% necrosis (Huvos III/IV). Poor response = under 90% necrosis = worse survival, consider salvage chemo regimen change. COMLEX: Post-chemo resection showing greater than 90% necrosis = favorable prognosis; less than 90% = poor prognosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 7 — Paget's disease of the bone as a differential for osteosarcoma in older patients"},{"id":"sdl54_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring with osteosarcomas.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with a 15-year history of Paget's disease of the bone presents with a sudden worsening of his previously well-controlled thigh pain. X-ray shows a new lytic destructive lesion arising within a known pagetic focus of the proximal femur, with cortical breakthrough and an aggressive periosteal reaction. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of this secondary malignancy?","choices":{"A":"Bisphosphonate therapy used to treat Paget's disease directly causes osteosarcoma by inhibiting osteoblast apoptosis and promoting malignant transformation over time","B":"Secondary osteosarcoma in Paget's disease is caused by measles virus reactivation within osteoclasts, which then infects adjacent osteoblastic progenitors and initiates malignant change","C":"The osteosclerotic phase of Paget's disease physically compresses medullary stromal cells, causing ischemic necrosis followed by malignant repair in a process analogous to post-radiation sarcoma","D":"Chronically disordered bone remodeling in Paget's disease drives genomic instability in osteoblastic precursors, predisposing to secondary osteosarcoma development within pagetic bone","E":"Elevated alkaline phosphatase in Paget's disease directly acts as a mutagen on osteoblast DNA, with the risk of secondary osteosarcoma proportional to the alkaline phosphatase level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secondary osteosarcoma arising in pagetic bone is the most feared complication of Paget's disease, occurring in approximately 1% of affected patients. The pathophysiology involves chronically disordered bone remodeling — rapid alternating cycles of osteoclast-driven resorption and haphazard osteoblastic repair — which creates a microenvironment of ongoing genomic instability in osteoblastic precursors. Secondary osteosarcoma in Paget's disease has an extremely poor prognosis, with five-year survival typically below 10%, compared to approximately 70% in primary osteosarcoma of adolescents. Other secondary osteosarcoma risk factors include prior radiation exposure and bone infarcts. Bisphosphonate therapy is protective, not causative.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary osteosarcoma in Paget's disease — 1% lifetime risk, bimodal age peak (second peak >65 years), extremely poor prognosis (<10% 5-year survival). Other secondary causes: prior radiation, bone infarcts. Alkaline phosphatase is elevated in Paget's but is not directly mutagenic. COMLEX: Elderly patient with Paget's and sudden worsening of bone pain = rule out secondary osteosarcoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 7 — Paget's disease of the bone as a differential for osteosarcoma in older patients"},{"id":"sdl54_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring with osteosarcomas.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with osteosarcoma of the proximal humerus undergoes staging CT. Imaging reveals two new pulmonary nodules consistent with metastatic disease. Which of the following best describes why pulmonary metastasis is specifically the predominant distant spread pattern in osteosarcoma?","choices":{"A":"Osteosarcoma cells spread exclusively through lymphatic channels that drain the periosteal tissue and converge on hilar lymph nodes before seeding the lung parenchyma","B":"Osteosarcoma cells preferentially invade cortical Haversian venous channels and enter the systemic circulation, with the pulmonary capillary bed serving as the first vascular filter encountered","C":"Osteosarcoma preferentially metastasizes to the lungs because CXCR4 chemokine receptor expression on tumor cells responds to CXCL12 gradients produced selectively by pulmonary endothelium","D":"Pulmonary metastasis in osteosarcoma results from retrograde venous flow through the inferior vena cava during Valsalva maneuvers, forcing tumor emboli into the pulmonary vasculature","E":"Osteosarcoma cells have a specific tropism for surfactant-producing type II pneumocytes, and this cell-to-cell adhesion interaction drives selective colonization of lung tissue over other organs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Osteosarcoma metastasizes predominantly via hematogenous spread. The tumor invades cortical venous channels — Haversian system venous outflow — and enters the systemic circulation. Because the venous drainage of the extremities flows through the systemic veins to the right heart and then to the pulmonary circulation, the lungs are the first capillary bed encountered and trap tumor emboli. This explains the characteristic early pulmonary metastasis pattern. At diagnosis, approximately 15-20% of patients have detectable lung metastases; occult micrometastases are present in most patients, which is why systemic chemotherapy is integral to treatment. Lymph node involvement is uncommon in osteosarcoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteosarcoma metastasizes HEMATOGENOUSLY — lungs first, because venous drainage of extremities passes through the pulmonary circulation. NOT lymphatic spread (unlike carcinomas). 15-20% have lung mets at diagnosis. Presence of lung mets at diagnosis = <20% 5-year survival. COMLEX: Staging CT chest is mandatory in all osteosarcoma patients at diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 7 — Paget's disease of the bone as a differential for osteosarcoma in older patients"}]},{"sdlNumber":55,"title":"Bone Homeostasis & Soft Tissue Tumors","questions":[{"id":"sdl55_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the homeostasis and remodeling of bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In bone remodeling, which molecular interaction directly initiates osteoclast precursor differentiation into mature osteoclasts?","choices":{"A":"OPG secreted by osteoblasts binds to RANK on osteoclast precursors, activating NF-kB signaling and driving osteoclastogenesis","B":"Sclerostin produced by osteocytes binds to LRP5 and LRP6 on osteoclast precursors, activating NF-kB signaling and initiating osteoclast differentiation","C":"M-CSF produced by osteoclasts binds to RANKL on osteoblast precursors, activating beta-catenin signaling and promoting new bone matrix synthesis","D":"Wnt proteins secreted by osteoprogenitor cells bind to RANK on osteoclast precursors, activating NF-kB signaling and driving osteoclastogenesis","E":"RANKL expressed on osteoblasts and marrow stromal cells binds to RANK on osteoclast precursors, activating NF-kB signaling and driving osteoclastogenesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"RANKL is expressed on osteoblasts and marrow stromal cells and binds to its receptor RANK on osteoclast precursors. This RANK signaling activates NF-kB, which is essential for osteoclast generation and survival. OPG is a decoy receptor also made by osteoblasts that binds RANKL and prevents it from interacting with RANK, thereby inhibiting osteoclastogenesis. Sclerostin inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin in osteoblasts. M-CSF from osteoblasts is also essential for osteoclast generation but acts through a separate receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RANKL on osteoblasts/stromal cells → RANK on osteoclast precursors → NF-kB → osteoclast differentiation. OPG = decoy receptor made by osteoblasts → blocks RANKL → inhibits osteoclastogenesis. Denosumab mimics OPG (anti-RANKL antibody). COMLEX: RANK/RANKL/OPG axis is the molecular target of several rheumatologic and osteoporosis therapies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG are the molecular levers of BMU homeostasis and the pharmacologic targets in metabolic bone disease"},{"id":"sdl55_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the homeostasis and remodeling of bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cell type produces sclerostin, and what is its effect on bone remodeling?","choices":{"A":"Osteocytes produce sclerostin, which inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin signaling in osteoblasts, thereby reducing bone formation","B":"Osteoclasts produce sclerostin, which inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin signaling in osteoblasts, thereby reducing bone formation","C":"Osteoblasts produce sclerostin, which activates RANKL expression on marrow stromal cells, thereby increasing osteoclast recruitment and bone resorption","D":"Marrow stromal cells produce sclerostin, which activates NF-kB in osteoclast precursors, thereby promoting osteoclast differentiation and bone turnover","E":"T lymphocytes produce sclerostin, which activates OPG expression on osteoblasts, thereby inhibiting osteoclastogenesis and promoting bone formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteocytes, the most abundant bone cells embedded within the mineralized matrix, produce sclerostin. Sclerostin binds to LRP5 and LRP6 co-receptors on osteoblasts and inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin signaling, reducing osteoblast activity and bone formation. Romosozumab, an anti-sclerostin monoclonal antibody, is approved for osteoporosis treatment and works by removing this inhibitory brake on Wnt signaling, allowing increased bone formation. Osteoblasts produce OPG and RANKL; osteoclasts are derived from monocyte precursors and produce bone resorption enzymes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sclerostin = osteocyte-derived Wnt inhibitor → reduces osteoblast activity. Romosozumab = anti-sclerostin antibody → removes Wnt inhibition → anabolic effect. LRP5 loss-of-function mutations = osteoporosis; LRP5 gain-of-function = high bone density. COMLEX: Sclerostin connects mechanical load sensing (osteocytes) to bone formation regulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG are the molecular levers of BMU homeostasis and the pharmacologic targets in metabolic bone disease"},{"id":"sdl55_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the homeostasis and remodeling of bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Peak bone mass is achieved in early adulthood. After the fourth decade, bone resorption slightly exceeds formation, resulting in an average skeletal mass decline of what percentage per year?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 5% skeletal mass is lost per year after the fourth decade, affecting women preferentially due to estrogen-independent osteoclast hyperactivity","B":"Approximately 0.1% skeletal mass is lost per year after the fourth decade, an amount insufficient to cause clinically significant osteoporosis without additional risk factors","C":"Approximately 2% skeletal mass is lost per year after the fourth decade in both sexes due to decreased vitamin D production in aged skin and reduced intestinal absorption","D":"Approximately 0.7% skeletal mass is lost per year after the fourth decade, affecting both sexes equally, independent of menopausal-related bone loss","E":"Approximately 10% skeletal mass is lost per year after the fourth decade, explaining why most adults develop fractures by the seventh decade without pharmacologic intervention"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Once maximal skeletal mass is attained in early adulthood, bone resorption slightly exceeds formation at a rate of approximately 0.7% per year. This age-related bone loss affects both sexes equally and contributes to senile osteoporosis, which is a low-turnover form driven by reduced osteoblast proliferative and biosynthetic capacity. This is distinct from postmenopausal osteoporosis, which is a high-turnover form driven by estrogen deficiency and can cause up to 2% cortical bone and 9% cancellous bone loss per year in the first decade after menopause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Age-related bone loss = 0.7%/year, both sexes, senile osteoporosis = LOW-turnover. Postmenopausal osteoporosis = HIGH-turnover, up to 2% cortical/9% cancellous/year in first decade. Peak bone mass factors: VDR polymorphisms, nutrition, physical activity, hormonal state. COMLEX: Resistance training > endurance exercise for building bone mass (load magnitude > load cycles).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG are the molecular levers of BMU homeostasis and the pharmacologic targets in metabolic bone disease"},{"id":"sdl55_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the conceptual difference between Simple karyotype and Complex karyotype sarcomas.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following sarcomas is best classified as a simple karyotype tumor, and what is its defining molecular feature?","choices":{"A":"Leiomyosarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by a single recurrent chromosomal translocation producing a chimeric fusion protein that drives constitutive growth factor signaling","B":"Undifferentiated soft tissue sarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by a limited number of recurrent chromosomal aberrations that can serve as diagnostic molecular markers","C":"Synovial sarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by a t(X;18) translocation producing SS18::SSX1 or SS18::SSX2 fusion proteins that dysregulate chromatin remodeling complexes","D":"Pleomorphic liposarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by MDM2 gene amplification at chromosome 12q13-q15 that drives proteasomal degradation of p53","E":"Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by a consistent t(2;13) translocation producing a PAX3::FOXO1 chimeric transcription factor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Simple karyotype sarcomas comprise approximately 20% of sarcomas and are characterized by euploid genomes with a single or limited number of recurrent chromosomal aberrations that are diagnostically specific. Synovial sarcoma carries t(X;18), producing SS18::SSX1, SS18::SSX2, or SS18::SSX4 fusion proteins that dysregulate chromatin remodeling. Other examples include Ewing sarcoma (EWSR1::FLI1), myxoid liposarcoma (FUS::DDIT3), and alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma (PAX3/7::FOXO1). These tumors are more common in younger patients with less pleomorphism. Leiomyosarcoma, undifferentiated soft tissue sarcoma, and pleomorphic liposarcoma are complex karyotype tumors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Simple karyotype (~20%) = euploid, single recurrent translocation, diagnostic marker, younger patients, less pleomorphic. Examples: Ewing t(11;22) EWSR1::FLI1, synovial sarcoma t(X;18) SS18::SSX, myxoid liposarcoma t(12;16) FUS::DDIT3, alveolar RMS PAX3::FOXO1. Complex karyotype (~80%) = aneuploid, multiple aberrations, adults, pleomorphic. COMLEX: Translocation-positive sarcoma in a teenager = simple karyotype.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 2 — Osteosarcoma exemplifies complex karyotype genomic instability with MDM2 and CDK4 amplification as parallel driver logic"},{"id":"sdl55_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the conceptual difference between Simple karyotype and Complex karyotype sarcomas.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has a retroperitoneal mass. Biopsy reveals pleomorphic, anaplastic cells with numerous chromosomal gains and losses on karyotype analysis and no consistent chromosomal translocation. This tumor is best classified as which category, and what examples share this genetic profile?","choices":{"A":"Simple karyotype sarcoma, characterized by a single diagnostically specific fusion gene that acts as an oncogenic chimeric protein and serves as a biomarker for targeted therapy","B":"Complex karyotype sarcoma, characterized by aneuploidy or polyploidy with multiple chromosomal aberrations reflecting underlying genomic instability; leiomyosarcoma and undifferentiated soft tissue sarcoma are prototypic examples","C":"Simple karyotype sarcoma, characterized by a single EWSR1 rearrangement that represents the most common recurrent translocation across sarcoma subtypes in middle-aged adults","D":"Hematologic malignancy with secondary bone marrow extension, as complex karyotype patterns are characteristic of leukemias and lymphomas rather than sarcomas of soft tissue","E":"Reactive fibroblastic proliferation with clonal cytogenetic changes, as aneuploidy in soft tissue lesions is pathognomonic of self-limited processes such as nodular fasciitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Complex karyotype sarcomas comprise approximately 80% of all sarcomas and are characterized by aneuploidy or polyploidy with multiple chromosomal gains and losses that reflect underlying genomic instability. Prototypic examples include leiomyosarcoma and undifferentiated soft tissue sarcoma, which are both more common in adults and tend to be histologically pleomorphic. Pleomorphic liposarcoma is another example. Because there is no single diagnostic fusion protein, targeted therapy based on molecular markers is not available for most complex karyotype sarcomas. Simple karyotype sarcomas are more common in younger patients and carry specific recurrent translocations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complex karyotype (~80%) = aneuploid/polyploid, multiple gains/losses, genomic instability, adults, pleomorphic, no targeted therapy based on fusion gene. Leiomyosarcoma and undifferentiated soft tissue sarcoma are classic examples. Also: pleomorphic liposarcoma. Nodular fasciitis = clonal but self-limited (MYH9::USP6). COMLEX: Pleomorphic sarcoma in an older adult without recurrent translocation = complex karyotype.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 2 — Osteosarcoma exemplifies complex karyotype genomic instability with MDM2 and CDK4 amplification as parallel driver logic"},{"id":"sdl55_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the conceptual difference between Simple karyotype and Complex karyotype sarcomas.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The FUS::DDIT3 fusion gene produced by t(12;16) translocation in myxoid liposarcoma encodes a chimeric DNA-binding protein. How does this oncoprotein drive tumorigenesis?","choices":{"A":"The FUS::DDIT3 fusion protein arrests adipocyte differentiation by interfering with normal transcriptional programming, causing proliferation of primitive undifferentiated mesenchymal cells","B":"The FUS::DDIT3 fusion protein constitutively activates the RAS/MAPK signaling cascade, driving cell cycle progression through unrestrained mitogenic signaling in committed adipocyte progenitors","C":"The FUS::DDIT3 fusion protein promotes proteasomal degradation of p53 by binding to MDM2, thereby eliminating the primary checkpoint that prevents malignant transformation","D":"The FUS::DDIT3 fusion protein activates NF-kB-dependent transcription of RANKL, creating an autocrine loop that sustains osteoclast-like resorptive activity within the tumor microenvironment","E":"The FUS::DDIT3 fusion protein amplifies the Wnt/beta-catenin pathway by inhibiting APC-mediated destruction complex activity, mimicking the mechanism seen in desmoid tumors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Myxoid liposarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by t(12;16) producing the FUS::DDIT3 fusion gene (older terminology: TLS::CHOP). The chimeric DNA-binding protein arrests adipocyte differentiation through uncertain mechanisms, leading to proliferation of primitive mesenchymal cells at various early stages of adipocytic differentiation. This represents a common oncogenic mechanism in simple karyotype sarcomas where fusion proteins disrupt normal differentiation programs. Histologically, myxoid liposarcoma shows abundant basophilic extracellular matrix, arborizing capillaries, and primitive cells resembling fetal fat. MDM2 amplification is the mechanism in well-differentiated liposarcoma, not myxoid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myxoid liposarcoma = t(12;16), FUS::DDIT3, arrests adipocyte differentiation, myxoid matrix with \"chicken wire\" capillaries, thigh/leg, 30s, intermediate prognosis, unusual bone mets. Well-differentiated liposarcoma = MDM2 amplification at chr 12q13-q15. Pleomorphic liposarcoma = complex karyotype. COMLEX: Myxoid liposarcoma is unique among sarcomas for its predilection to metastasize to bone and other soft tissues rather than lung.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 2 — Osteosarcoma exemplifies complex karyotype genomic instability with MDM2 and CDK4 amplification as parallel driver logic"},{"id":"sdl55_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between benign and malignant soft tissue tumors.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following soft tissue tumors is correctly matched with both its benign or malignant classification and its characteristic molecular or genetic finding?","choices":{"A":"Deep fibromatosis is a malignant mesenchymal tumor associated with FUS::DDIT3 fusion from t(12;16), conferring high rates of distant metastasis to the retroperitoneum and lungs","B":"Lipoma is a malignant adipose tumor associated with MDM2 amplification at chromosome 12q13-q15, causing p53 degradation and constitutive proliferation of mature adipocytes","C":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma is a benign skeletal muscle tumor associated with PAX3::FOXO1 fusion from t(2;13), found incidentally in the sinuses and genitourinary tract of children","D":"Leiomyosarcoma is a benign smooth muscle tumor associated with fumarate hydratase loss-of-function, which drives Krebs cycle disruption and metabolic transformation","E":"Nodular fasciitis is a benign clonal fibroblastic proliferation associated with a MYH9::USP6 fusion gene from t(17;22), which is self-limiting and may resolve spontaneously"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nodular fasciitis is a self-limited fibroblastic and myofibroblastic proliferation in young adults that harbors a t(17;22) translocation producing MYH9::USP6 fusion gene, establishing it as a clonal but self-limited neoplasm rather than a reactive process. It often regresses spontaneously. Deep fibromatosis is locally aggressive but does NOT metastasize and carries APC or CTNNB1 mutations. Lipoma is benign with HMGA2 rearrangements; MDM2 amplification defines well-differentiated liposarcoma. Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 is malignant. Leiomyosarcoma is malignant with complex karyotype; fumarate hydratase loss causes hereditary leiomyomas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nodular fasciitis = clonal (MYH9::USP6), self-limited, NOT reactive, young adults, forearm/chest/back, tissue culture pattern, mitoses without atypia, spontaneous regression. Deep fibromatosis = locally aggressive, NO mets, APC/CTNNB1, Gardner syndrome. COMLEX: Rapidly growing forearm mass in a 25-year-old with prior trauma history = nodular fasciitis (do not misdiagnose as sarcoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 1 — Benign versus malignant classification principles and bone location rules apply symmetrically to soft tissue tumors"},{"id":"sdl55_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between benign and malignant soft tissue tumors.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Liposarcoma is the most common sarcoma of adulthood. Which subtype is associated with MDM2 gene amplification and why does its location — extremity versus retroperitoneum — determine the clinical designation used?","choices":{"A":"Well-differentiated liposarcoma harbors MDM2 amplification at chromosome 12q13-q15; extremity tumors are called atypical lipomatous tumors due to their indolent behavior, while retroperitoneal tumors retain the liposarcoma designation due to recurrence and significant morbidity","B":"Myxoid liposarcoma harbors MDM2 amplification at chromosome 12q13-q15; extremity tumors are called atypical lipomatous tumors because MDM2 expression is suppressed in superficial locations by skin-derived factors","C":"Pleomorphic liposarcoma harbors MDM2 amplification at chromosome 12q13-q15; extremity tumors are called atypical lipomatous tumors because the complex karyotype is only expressed in deep retroperitoneal tumors","D":"Well-differentiated liposarcoma harbors FUS::DDIT3 from t(12;16); extremity tumors are called atypical lipomatous tumors due to indolent behavior, while retroperitoneal tumors retain the liposarcoma designation due to chemotherapy resistance","E":"Myxoid liposarcoma harbors FUS::DDIT3 from t(12;16); extremity tumors are labeled atypical lipomatous tumors because these arise from brown fat precursors that have a lower malignant potential than white fat precursors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Well-differentiated liposarcoma (WDLS) harbors amplification of the MDM2 gene at chromosome 12q13-q15. MDM2 is an oncoprotein that promotes p53 degradation. The same tumor is designated atypical lipomatous tumor (ALT) when it arises in the extremity, where complete surgical excision is feasible and behavior is indolent. In the retroperitoneum, where complete resection is often impossible, the WDLS designation is used because these tumors frequently recur locally and cause significant morbidity. This illustrates how tumor location influences not just treatment but nomenclature. Myxoid liposarcoma carries FUS::DDIT3 from t(12;16).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Well-differentiated liposarcoma = MDM2 amplification (12q13-q15), atypical spindle cells among mature adipocytes. Extremity = called ALT (indolent). Retroperitoneum = called WDLS (recurs, significant morbidity). Myxoid liposarcoma = FUS::DDIT3 t(12;16), chicken wire vessels, thigh, bone mets. Pleomorphic = complex karyotype, aggressive. COMLEX: Retroperitoneal adipose mass in 60-year-old = liposarcoma until proven otherwise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 1 — Benign versus malignant classification principles and bone location rules apply symmetrically to soft tissue tumors"},{"id":"sdl55_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between benign and malignant soft tissue tumors.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Rhabdomyosarcoma is the most common soft tissue sarcoma of childhood. The alveolar subtype carries PAX3::FOXO1 or PAX7::FOXO1 fusion genes. How do these fusion proteins drive malignant transformation?","choices":{"A":"PAX3 is a cell surface receptor for FGF; the PAX3::FOXO1 fusion protein constitutively activates RAS/MAPK signaling independently of ligand binding, driving unrestrained mitogenic proliferation","B":"PAX3 is a transcription factor that normally initiates skeletal muscle differentiation; the PAX3::FOXO1 chimeric protein interferes with normal differentiation, arresting cells in a proliferative undifferentiated state, similar to transcription factor fusions in acute leukemia","C":"PAX3 is a tumor suppressor; the PAX3::FOXO1 fusion protein acts as a dominant negative that silences PAX3 target genes through promoter hypermethylation, resulting in loss of cell cycle control","D":"PAX3 is a pro-apoptotic transcription factor; the PAX3::FOXO1 fusion protein sequesters p53 in the cytoplasm, preventing its nuclear translocation and enabling evasion of apoptotic checkpoints","E":"PAX3 is a chromatin remodeling enzyme; the PAX3::FOXO1 fusion protein dysregulates histone H3 methylation, creating bivalent chromatin domains that maintain the tumor in an epigenetically plastic state"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PAX3 is a transcription factor that initiates and regulates skeletal muscle differentiation. In alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma, the t(2;13) translocation produces a PAX3::FOXO1 chimeric fusion protein that interferes with normal myogenic differentiation, arresting cells in an undifferentiated, proliferative state. This is mechanistically analogous to transcription factor fusions in acute leukemias that block myeloid differentiation. The t(1;13) variant produces PAX7::FOXO1. Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma is more aggressive than the embryonal subtype. These tumors occur in sites that normally lack skeletal muscle (sinuses, genitourinary tract), underscoring that sarcomas arise from mesenchymal stem cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alveolar RMS = PAX3::FOXO1 (t(2;13)) or PAX7::FOXO1 (t(1;13)), 5-15 years, dyscohesive round cells in alveolar pattern, more aggressive. Embryonal RMS = RAS pathway, 1-5 years, genitourinary tract, best prognosis variant = botryoid. Pleomorphic RMS = adults, complex karyotype. All require immunohistochemistry for myogenin to confirm skeletal muscle lineage. COMLEX: Bladder/vaginal mass in a toddler = embryonal RMS (sarcoma botryoides).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 1 — Benign versus malignant classification principles and bone location rules apply symmetrically to soft tissue tumors"},{"id":"sdl55_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the homeostasis and remodeling of bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman taking long-term prednisone for lupus presents with back pain. DEXA scan shows T-score of -2.8 at the lumbar spine. Her provider explains that glucocorticoids contribute to bone loss through multiple mechanisms. Which of the following best describes the primary skeletal mechanism by which glucocorticoids cause osteoporosis?","choices":{"A":"Glucocorticoids directly activate osteoclast differentiation by binding to RANK receptors on osteoclast precursors, bypassing the need for osteoblast-derived RANKL as an intermediate signal","B":"Glucocorticoids cause osteoporosis exclusively by increasing urinary calcium excretion, resulting in secondary hyperparathyroidism that drives osteoclast-mediated bone resorption","C":"Glucocorticoids increase RANKL expression on osteoblasts and suppress OPG, thereby tilting the RANK-to-OPG ratio toward bone resorption while also directly inhibiting osteoblast function and new bone formation","D":"Glucocorticoids inhibit vitamin D activation in the kidney by suppressing 1-alpha hydroxylase, creating a functional vitamin D deficiency that reduces calcium absorption from the gut","E":"Glucocorticoids accelerate osteocyte apoptosis, releasing sclerostin that activates Wnt/beta-catenin in osteoclast precursors and causes paradoxical stimulation of bone resorption"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Glucocorticoids cause osteoporosis through multiple converging mechanisms. They increase RANKL expression on osteoblasts and decrease OPG production, increasing the RANK-to-OPG ratio and promoting osteoclastogenesis. Simultaneously, glucocorticoids directly suppress osteoblast proliferation and function, reducing bone matrix synthesis. They also antagonize vitamin D-stimulated intestinal calcium transport and stimulate renal calcium excretion, contributing to secondary hyperparathyroidism. Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis disproportionately affects cancellous bone of vertebral bodies and can cause fractures even at relatively preserved T-scores (fracture threshold is higher than in postmenopausal osteoporosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoids → increase RANKL, decrease OPG → osteoclastogenesis + directly inhibit osteoblasts. Affects cancellous bone (vertebrae) most. Fracture risk is higher at a given T-score than in postmenopausal osteoporosis. Bisphosphonates are first-line for glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis prevention. COMLEX: Any patient on chronic corticosteroids should be co-prescribed calcium, vitamin D, and a bisphosphonate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG as the molecular effectors linking hormonal and drug-induced bone loss"},{"id":"sdl55_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the homeostasis and remodeling of bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old male weightlifter has significantly higher bone density than age-matched sedentary controls. A 70-year-old man with paraplegia has severely reduced bone density in his lower extremities. Which cell type senses mechanical load and transduces signals to modulate osteoblast and osteoclast activity to explain both observations?","choices":{"A":"Osteoclasts are the primary mechanosensors; high mechanical load suppresses their bone resorption activity via direct cytoskeletal deformation, while immobilization removes this suppression and allows unchecked resorption","B":"Periosteal fibroblasts are the primary mechanosensors; they convert mechanical strain into prostaglandin synthesis that then activates osteoblast differentiation from marrow stromal cell precursors","C":"Osteoblasts are the primary mechanosensors; they directly increase matrix synthesis in response to compressive loads through integrin-mediated cytoskeletal tension without requiring any intermediary cell signaling","D":"Osteocytes are the mechanosensing cells of bone; they respond to mechanical load by modulating osteoblast and osteoclast activity through paracrine signals including sclerostin and RANKL, explaining both increased bone density with resistance training and disuse osteoporosis with immobilization","E":"Chondrocytes are the primary skeletal mechanosensors; they release nitric oxide in response to joint loading, which then diffuses into subchondral bone to regulate osteoblast and osteoclast activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Osteocytes — the most abundant bone cells, embedded within the mineralized matrix in lacunar spaces connected by canalicular networks — are the principal mechanosensors of bone. They respond to mechanical load by modulating sclerostin production (decreased under load, removing the Wnt inhibitory brake) and RANKL expression (adjusting osteoclast activity). Increased mechanical load (resistance training) reduces sclerostin → more Wnt/beta-catenin → more bone formation. Immobilization or microgravity increases sclerostin and promotes bone loss. The type of exercise matters: load magnitude (weight training) is more effective than load cycles (cycling) for increasing bone mass.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteocytes = mechanosensors. Mechanical load → decreased sclerostin → increased Wnt → bone formation. Immobilization → increased sclerostin → decreased bone formation + increased RANKL → bone loss. Resistance training > endurance exercise for bone health. COMLEX: Disuse osteoporosis (paralysis, bed rest, microgravity) is mediated by osteocyte mechanosensing → increased sclerostin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG as the molecular effectors linking mechanical and hormonal signals to bone cell activity"},{"id":"sdl55_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the homeostasis and remodeling of bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with multiple myeloma develops severe bone pain and lytic lesions throughout the axial skeleton. His oncologist explains that myeloma cells directly dysregulate the RANK/RANKL/OPG axis. Which of the following best explains the molecular mechanism by which multiple myeloma causes osteolytic bone destruction?","choices":{"A":"Myeloma cells directly differentiate into osteoclasts by expressing RANK on their surface, allowing them to perform autonomous bone resorption without requiring any osteoblast-derived RANKL signal","B":"Myeloma cells secrete factors that increase RANKL expression on marrow stromal cells and simultaneously suppress OPG production, markedly increasing the RANK-to-OPG ratio and driving osteoclast-mediated lytic bone destruction","C":"Myeloma cells suppress Wnt/beta-catenin signaling in osteoblasts through secretion of DKK-1, which directly activates RANK on osteoclast precursors and causes osteolysis independent of RANKL","D":"Myeloma cells secrete M-CSF that constitutively activates osteoclast formation, but the primary mechanism of bone destruction is direct tumor invasion rather than osteoclast-mediated resorption","E":"Myeloma cells activate the sclerostin pathway in osteocytes, which then suppresses both osteoblast formation and OPG production, resulting in secondary hyperparathyroidism that drives osteolysis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Multiple myeloma causes osteolytic bone disease primarily by dysregulating the RANKL/OPG axis. Myeloma cells and their stromal cell neighbors increase RANKL expression while suppressing OPG, dramatically increasing the RANK-to-OPG ratio and promoting osteoclastogenesis. Additionally, myeloma cells secrete DKK-1, which inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin signaling in osteoblasts, suppressing new bone formation and creating an uncoupling of resorption and formation (pure osteolysis without repair). Denosumab and bisphosphonates are used to mitigate myeloma bone disease by targeting the RANKL pathway. Myeloma bone lesions characteristically do not show increased uptake on bone scan because there is minimal osteoblastic activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myeloma bone disease = increased RANKL + decreased OPG + DKK-1 (inhibits Wnt/osteoblasts) = pure osteolysis with no repair. Bone scan NEGATIVE (no osteoblastic activity). Bisphosphonates/denosumab for bone protection. COMLEX: Lytic lesions + negative bone scan + plasma cell dyscrasia = multiple myeloma (contrast with metastatic prostate = sclerotic lesions, hot bone scan).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG as the molecular effectors of osteoclast activation in metabolic and neoplastic bone disease"},{"id":"sdl55_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the conceptual difference between Simple karyotype and Complex karyotype sarcomas.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy presents with a periorbital mass. MRI shows a lesion in the soft tissues adjacent to the orbit. Biopsy reveals uniform small round cells growing in an alveolar pattern, with fibrous septa creating aggregates and dyscohesive cells in the center. Cytogenetics shows t(2;13)(q35;q14). Which of the following best characterizes the prognosis and the molecular basis for this tumor's behavior relative to the embryonal subtype?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 has a more favorable prognosis than embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma because the translocation allows precise targeted therapy that completely eliminates the tumor stem cell population","B":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 has an identical prognosis to embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma because the RAS pathway activated in embryonal disease is equally aggressive as the PAX3::FOXO1 transcription factor","C":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 has a more favorable prognosis than embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma because the simple karyotype produces a more predictable tumor phenotype that is easier to stage and treat surgically","D":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 is more aggressive than embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma; the chimeric transcription factor drives a highly active gene expression program that promotes invasion and resistance to differentiation-inducing therapy","E":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 only occurs in adults, while embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma is the subtype seen in children under age 10, making the comparison between their prognoses clinically irrelevant for this patient"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 (t(2;13)) or PAX7::FOXO1 (t(1;13)) is more aggressive than embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma. The PAX3::FOXO1 fusion protein functions as a potent transcriptional activator that drives invasion, metastasis, and resistance to apoptosis, and is associated with a worse prognosis. Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma harbors RAS pathway mutations and has a better overall prognosis, with the botryoid variant having the most favorable outlook. Both subtypes occur in children and adolescents. Treatment involves surgery, chemotherapy, and often radiation. The presence of a specific fusion gene (simple karyotype) does not inherently confer favorable prognosis — it confers diagnostic specificity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alveolar RMS (PAX3::FOXO1) = WORSE prognosis than embryonal RMS (RAS mutations). Botryoid variant of embryonal = BEST prognosis among all subtypes. Pleomorphic RMS = adults = WORST prognosis. Both alveolar and embryonal occur in children. COMLEX: Head/neck or genitourinary soft tissue tumor in a child with round cells = rhabdomyosarcoma; t(2;13) = alveolar subtype = aggressive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 2 — Fusion protein-driven oncogenesis mirrors the simple karyotype mechanism in osteosarcoma driver mutations"},{"id":"sdl55_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the conceptual difference between Simple karyotype and Complex karyotype sarcomas.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Deep fibromatosis (desmoid tumor) harbors mutations in CTNNB1 or germline APC mutations. A 28-year-old woman with a large anterior abdominal wall mass is found to have a germline APC mutation. What syndrome does she have, and through what molecular mechanism do APC mutations lead to desmoid formation?","choices":{"A":"Gardner syndrome; loss of APC function prevents destruction of beta-catenin, leading to constitutive Wnt/beta-catenin signaling that drives fibroblast proliferation and desmoid tumor formation","B":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome; loss of APC function disrupts the spindle assembly checkpoint and causes chromosomal instability that drives fibroblast transformation into locally aggressive desmoid tumors","C":"Neurofibromatosis type 1; loss of APC function disrupts Ras GTPase activity by preventing NF1-mediated GAP regulation, leading to constitutive Ras/MAPK signaling in abdominal wall fibroblasts","D":"Lynch syndrome; loss of APC function impairs DNA mismatch repair in fibroblasts, creating microsatellite instability that accumulates secondary driver mutations leading to desmoid formation","E":"Weber-Osler-Rendu syndrome; loss of APC function disrupts TGF-beta receptor signaling in mesenchymal cells, causing unregulated fibroblastic proliferation in the mesentery and abdominal wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gardner syndrome is caused by germline APC mutations and is characterized by familial adenomatous polyposis (colon) plus extraintestinal manifestations including desmoid tumors, osteomas, and epidermoid cysts. APC is a key component of the beta-catenin destruction complex. Loss of APC prevents the phosphorylation and proteasomal degradation of beta-catenin, allowing it to accumulate and translocate to the nucleus, where it activates Wnt target genes that drive fibroblast proliferation. Sporadic desmoid tumors carry somatic CTNNB1 mutations that directly stabilize beta-catenin by preventing its phosphorylation. Both mutations converge on constitutive Wnt/beta-catenin activation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Desmoid tumor (deep fibromatosis) = locally aggressive, NO metastasis, APC or CTNNB1 mutations, Wnt/beta-catenin pathway. Gardner syndrome = germline APC + FAP + desmoids + osteomas + epidermoid cysts. Gamma-secretase inhibitors slow progression. COMLEX: Young woman with abdominal wall fibromatosis + colon polyps history = Gardner syndrome; check for germline APC.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 2 — Tumor suppressor gene loss as a parallel driver mechanism to fusion protein oncogenesis in soft tissue tumors"},{"id":"sdl55_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the conceptual difference between Simple karyotype and Complex karyotype sarcomas.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old man presents with a thigh mass. Biopsy reveals tight fascicles of uniform basophilic spindle cells with pseudoglandular structures. Molecular testing confirms SS18::SSX1 fusion. Which karyotype class does this tumor belong to, and where does it most commonly arise?","choices":{"A":"Synovial sarcoma is a complex karyotype tumor arising most commonly in the thigh and leg of older adults, defined by multiple chromosomal gains and losses rather than a single recurrent translocation","B":"Synovial sarcoma is a simple karyotype tumor arising most commonly within the synovial lining of large joints such as the knee, and it originates directly from synoviocytes within the joint capsule","C":"Synovial sarcoma is a simple karyotype tumor arising most commonly in the thigh and leg of young adults, defined by SS18::SSX fusion proteins that dysregulate chromatin remodeling complexes","D":"Synovial sarcoma is a complex karyotype tumor arising most commonly in the retroperitoneum of middle-aged adults, where its biphasic histology mimics adenocarcinoma with glandular differentiation","E":"Synovial sarcoma is a simple karyotype tumor arising most commonly in the head and neck region of children, where its SS18::SSX fusion protein mimics the mechanism of embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Synovial sarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by t(X;18), producing SS18::SSX1, SS18::SSX2, or SS18::SSX4 fusion proteins that dysregulate chromatin remodeling complexes. Despite the name, synovial sarcoma does NOT arise from synoviocytes; it is a tumor of uncertain histogenesis that most commonly arises in the soft tissues around large joints — particularly the thigh and leg near the knee — in adolescents and young adults aged 15-40 years. It can have a monophasic (spindle cell only) or biphasic (spindle cells plus pseudoglandular epithelioid structures) pattern. It is among the most common sarcomas in adolescents and young adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Synovial sarcoma = simple karyotype, t(X;18), SS18::SSX1, does NOT arise from synovium despite name, thigh/leg near knee, 15-40 years, biphasic (spindle + pseudoglandular) or monophasic. COMLEX: Soft tissue mass near a joint in a 25-year-old with biphasic histology = synovial sarcoma; NOT an inflammation of the joint.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 2 — Simple karyotype sarcomas share fusion protein-driven differentiation arrest with adolescent bone tumors"},{"id":"sdl55_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between benign and malignant soft tissue tumors.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman presents with a large, painful retroperitoneal mass causing abdominal fullness. CT shows a heterogeneous mass with areas of myxoid change. Biopsy shows eosinophilic spindle cells with blunt-ended hyperchromatic nuclei in interweaving fascicles, with brisk mitotic activity. Immunostains are positive for smooth muscle actin and desmin. Which of the following best characterizes this tumor's prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma has an excellent prognosis with surgery alone because smooth muscle tumors respond well to complete excision and almost never metastasize from the retroperitoneal location","B":"Retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma has an intermediate prognosis similar to well-differentiated liposarcoma because both tumors arise in the retroperitoneum and share similar molecular driver mechanisms","C":"Retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma is classified as a benign leiomyoma variant because the retroperitoneum is a protected anatomical compartment that inhibits malignant transformation of smooth muscle cells","D":"Retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma has a favorable prognosis when treated with neoadjuvant chemotherapy plus radiation, with 5-year survival rates comparable to extremity-based leiomyosarcomas","E":"Retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma is usually large and unresectable at presentation and is frequently fatal due to local extension and distant metastatic spread, particularly to the lungs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leiomyosarcoma is a malignant smooth muscle tumor most commonly arising in the deep soft tissues of the extremities and retroperitoneum of older adults, with a female predominance. Retroperitoneal leiomyosarcomas are large and bulky, causing abdominal symptoms, and are frequently unresectable at presentation. They are often fatal due to local extension and distant metastases, particularly to the lungs. In contrast, superficial or cutaneous leiomyosarcomas are small and have a good prognosis. The histologic features — eosinophilic spindle cells in fascicles, brisk mitoses, blunt-ended nuclei — are characteristic, and smooth muscle actin, desmin, and caldesmon on immunostaining confirm diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leiomyosarcoma — malignant smooth muscle, retroperitoneum/thigh, complex karyotype, older adults, female predominance. Retroperitoneal = large, unresectable, fatal. Superficial = small, good prognosis. IHC: smooth muscle actin, desmin, caldesmon. Contrast with leiomyoma (benign, uterus, FH gene mutation in hereditary syndrome = Reed syndrome). COMLEX: Retroperitoneal spindle cell sarcoma with smooth muscle markers in middle-aged woman = leiomyosarcoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 1 — Location-dependent behavior parallels bone tumor principles: axial/retroperitoneal = poor prognosis, extremity = better prognosis"},{"id":"sdl55_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between benign and malignant soft tissue tumors.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old man develops a rapidly enlarging mass on his forearm over 3 weeks. He recalls minor trauma at the site one month ago. Biopsy shows plump fibroblasts and myofibroblasts in a loose tissue-culture-like growth pattern with myxoid stroma, frequent mitoses but no atypical mitoses, and scattered lymphocytes. Molecular testing reveals MYH9::USP6 fusion. What is the most likely diagnosis and expected clinical course?","choices":{"A":"Myxofibrosarcoma — a low-grade malignant fibroblastic sarcoma with a predilection for rapid local recurrence after excision and eventual dedifferentiation to high-grade sarcoma in young adults","B":"Deep fibromatosis (desmoid tumor) — a locally aggressive fibroblastic proliferation driven by APC or CTNNB1 mutations, characterized by long fascicles of bland collagen-producing cells in musculoaponeurotic structures","C":"Nodular fasciitis — a clonal but self-limited fibroblastic proliferation that frequently regresses spontaneously and is cured by simple excision; mitoses without atypia and the USP6 fusion distinguish it from sarcoma","D":"Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma — a primitive mesenchymal tumor driven by RAS pathway activation that arises in soft tissues of young adults following traumatic seeding of mesenchymal stem cells","E":"Fibrosarcoma — a malignant spindle cell tumor with herringbone fascicles and high-grade nuclear atypia that commonly arises in the forearm of young adults following radiation or thermal injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nodular fasciitis is a self-limited clonal fibroblastic proliferation caused by acquired MYH9::USP6 fusion gene from t(17;22). It is no longer considered purely reactive — it is a clonal neoplasm that is nonetheless self-limited and frequently regresses spontaneously. Key distinguishing features from sarcoma: mitoses are frequent but atypical forms are absent, the growth pattern is loose and myxoid (tissue culture pattern), and it shows a maturation gradient (zonation) from hypercellular to fibrous. Prior trauma history is present in 10-50% of cases. It is cured by simple excision, and even without surgery, most lesions regress. The rapid growth rate can be alarming but should not prompt aggressive surgery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nodular fasciitis = clonal (MYH9::USP6 fusion), self-limited, frequently regresses, plump fibroblasts in myxoid stroma, frequent mitoses WITHOUT atypia, tissue culture pattern, zonation. Young adults, forearm/chest. NOT reactive (it is clonal). Cured by excision alone. COMLEX: Rapidly growing forearm mass in 20-30 year old with history of trauma = nodular fasciitis; high mitotic rate without atypia = benign.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 1 — Benign versus malignant classification requires synthesis of location, age, histology, and molecular findings across soft tissue and bone tumors"},{"id":"sdl55_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between benign and malignant soft tissue tumors.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman presents with a painless, soft, mobile subcutaneous mass on her back that has been present for years. Excision reveals a well-encapsulated tumor of mature adipocytes that is histologically identical to normal fat. No atypia, mitoses, or lipoblasts are seen. Molecular testing reveals chromosomal rearrangement involving 12q with dysregulation of HMGA2. What is this tumor and what is its relationship to liposarcoma?","choices":{"A":"Well-differentiated liposarcoma — histologically identical to a lipoma in early stages; the presence of HMGA2 rearrangement rather than MDM2 amplification indicates the transition from benign lipoma to malignant liposarcoma has already occurred","B":"Lipoma — the most common soft tissue tumor in adults; histologically identical to normal fat and cured by excision; it is distinguished from well-differentiated liposarcoma by the absence of atypical spindle cells and by HMGA2 rearrangement rather than MDM2 amplification","C":"Atypical lipomatous tumor — a well-differentiated liposarcoma of the extremity; HMGA2 rearrangement is interchangeable with MDM2 amplification as a diagnostic marker distinguishing it from conventional lipoma","D":"Angiolipoma — a benign adipose tumor with a prominent vascular component; chromosomal rearrangements at 12q with HMGA2 dysregulation are pathognomonic for this vascular variant of lipoma","E":"Hibernoma — a benign tumor of brown fat that mimics the molecular profile of conventional lipoma; HMGA2 rearrangements are characteristic and distinguish it from the white fat origin of conventional lipoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lipoma is the most common soft tissue tumor in adults and is a benign tumor of mature adipocytes. Most solitary lipomas harbor chromosomal rearrangements involving 12q that dysregulate HMGA2, a chromatin structure regulator. This is distinct from well-differentiated liposarcoma, which harbors MDM2 gene amplification at 12q13-q15. The critical histologic distinction is that lipoma consists of entirely normal-appearing mature adipocytes without atypical spindle cells, lipoblasts, or atypia. Well-differentiated liposarcoma contains scattered atypical spindle cells among mature adipocytes. HMGA2 rearrangement in lipoma does not confer malignant potential; these are benign, cured by excision, and do not transform to liposarcoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lipoma = most common soft tissue tumor adults, HMGA2 rearrangement (12q), mature adipocytes, NO atypia, cured by excision, NO malignant potential. Well-differentiated liposarcoma = MDM2 amplification, atypical spindle cells admixed. KEY DISTINCTION: HMGA2 rearrangement in lipoma vs MDM2 amplification in liposarcoma. COMLEX: Painless mobile subcutaneous adipose mass in middle-aged adult = lipoma; simple excision curative.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 1 — Benign and malignant tumors of mesenchymal origin require molecular markers to distinguish histologically similar lesions"}]},{"sdlNumber":56,"title":"Metabolic Bone Disease & Osteoporosis","questions":[{"id":"sdl56_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the factors that contribute to the achievement of peak bone mass.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which genetic factor has been most strongly linked to interindividual variation in peak bone mass through genome-wide association studies?","choices":{"A":"IGF-1 receptor gene polymorphisms that reduce growth hormone responsiveness in cortical bone osteoblasts during the pubertal growth spurt","B":"COL1A1 gene polymorphisms that reduce type I collagen cross-linking density and impair the structural quality of bone matrix during skeletal growth","C":"RUNX2 transcription factor polymorphisms that reduce osteoblast lineage commitment from mesenchymal progenitors throughout skeletal development","D":"Fibrillin-1 gene polymorphisms that alter TGF-beta sequestration in bone matrix and reduce growth factor availability for osteoblast-driven bone formation","E":"Vitamin D receptor gene polymorphisms that alter intestinal calcium absorption efficiency and osteoblast responsiveness to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The peak bone mass set point is determined by several genetic factors, of which polymorphisms in the vitamin D receptor gene are most strongly linked by genome-wide association studies. The VDR gene polymorphisms alter the efficiency of intestinal calcium absorption and the responsiveness of osteoblasts to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, both of which affect how much mineral is available for skeletal deposition. Other linked genes include RANK, RANKL, OPG, LRP5, and sclerostin. Non-genetic contributors include nutrition (calcium, vitamin D), physical activity (load magnitude and type), and hormonal status (sex steroids during puberty).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peak bone mass determinants = VDR polymorphisms, nutrition (Ca/vitamin D), physical activity, sex steroids. Achieved in early adulthood; declines ~0.7%/year thereafter. COMLEX: Weight-bearing mechanical load (tensile > compressive) is the strongest modifiable trigger for osteoblast activity via the osteocyte mechanosensory network.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 55 → Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and BMU remodeling cycles establish the skeletal set point that peak bone mass builds upon"},{"id":"sdl56_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the factors that contribute to the achievement of peak bone mass.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"During puberty, sex steroids contribute significantly to peak bone mass accrual. What is the primary mechanism by which estrogen promotes net bone formation during skeletal growth?","choices":{"A":"Estrogen directly suppresses PTH gene transcription in parathyroid chief cells, reducing calcium mobilization from cortical bone during the pubertal growth phase","B":"Estrogen reduces osteoclast lifespan by promoting osteoclast apoptosis while simultaneously enhancing osteoblast survival and activity, shifting the BMU balance toward net formation","C":"Estrogen stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity to increase circulating 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D levels and enhance intestinal calcium absorption for mineralization","D":"Estrogen inhibits sclerostin secretion from osteocytes, releasing the Wnt inhibitory brake on osteoblasts and driving periosteal bone apposition in long bones","E":"Estrogen upregulates calcitonin receptor expression on osteoclasts, amplifying calcitonin-mediated inhibition of acid secretion and preventing subperiosteal bone resorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Estrogen protects bone through multiple mechanisms. It directly reduces osteoclast lifespan by promoting osteoclast apoptosis and simultaneously increases OPG expression by osteoblasts, lowering the RANKL:OPG ratio and reducing osteoclastogenesis. Estrogen also promotes osteoblast survival. The net result is a shift toward bone formation during pubertal growth. Estrogen also closes the epiphyseal growth plates at the end of puberty — loss-of-function mutations in the estrogen receptor in males result in persistent growth plate activity and failure to close epiphyses, demonstrating the direct role of estrogen in both sexes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Estrogen → increases OPG, decreases osteoclast lifespan, promotes osteoblast survival. Estrogen also closes epiphyseal plates — aromatase-deficient males have unfused epiphyses and osteopenia. Postmenopausal estrogen loss = high-turnover osteoporosis (increased osteoclasts). COMLEX: Estrogen deficiency is a named risk factor for osteoporosis on COMLEX Clinical Presentation 8.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 55 → Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and BMU remodeling cycles establish the skeletal set point that peak bone mass builds upon"},{"id":"sdl56_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the factors that contribute to the achievement of peak bone mass.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"After peak bone mass is achieved in early adulthood, bone resorption slightly exceeds formation in both sexes. Approximately how much skeletal mass is lost per year during this age-related phase?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 5% of skeletal mass per year, a high-turnover process occurring predominantly in postmenopausal women that explains most osteoporotic fractures by age 60","B":"Approximately 0.05% of skeletal mass per year, a negligible rate of loss that has no clinically significant effect on fracture risk without additional secondary causes","C":"Approximately 2% of skeletal mass per year in women and 1% in men, reflecting sex-specific differences in osteoblast RANKL expression throughout adulthood","D":"Approximately 0.7% of skeletal mass per year, a low-turnover process affecting both sexes equally that reflects declining osteoblast proliferative and biosynthetic capacity with age","E":"Approximately 10% of skeletal mass per year, an accelerated loss that explains why the majority of untreated adults develop fractures by their seventh decade"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Once maximal skeletal mass is attained in early adulthood, bone resorption slightly exceeds formation at approximately 0.7% per year. This age-related loss affects both sexes equally and is categorized as senile osteoporosis — a low-turnover form driven by declining osteoblast proliferative and biosynthetic capacity with age. This is distinct from postmenopausal osteoporosis, which is a high-turnover form where estrogen deficiency drives osteoclast overactivation. In postmenopausal women, accelerated bone loss in the first decade after menopause can reach up to 2% of cortical bone and 9% of cancellous bone per year, far exceeding the 0.7% senile baseline.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Senile osteoporosis = low-turnover, both sexes, ~0.7%/year after 4th decade. Postmenopausal osteoporosis = high-turnover, estrogen deficiency, up to 9% cancellous/year in first decade. Both types increase fracture risk — vertebral compression, distal radius, and femoral neck are classic sites. COMLEX: Resistance training is the most effective lifestyle intervention for preserving bone mass in aging adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 55 → Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and BMU remodeling cycles establish the skeletal set point that peak bone mass builds upon"},{"id":"sdl56_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the physiologic factors leading to menopausal bone loss.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which molecular change in osteoblasts is the primary driver of increased osteoclastogenesis in postmenopausal women following estrogen withdrawal?","choices":{"A":"Decreased sclerostin production by osteocytes releases the Wnt inhibitory brake on osteoblasts, paradoxically stimulating osteoblast-derived RANKL secretion and osteoclast recruitment","B":"Decreased calcitonin secretion from parafollicular thyroid C cells removes the primary hormonal inhibition of osteoclast acid secretion and bone resorption","C":"Decreased OPG production by osteoblasts increases the RANKL-to-OPG ratio, allowing more RANKL to bind RANK on osteoclast precursors and amplify osteoclastogenesis","D":"Decreased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D synthesis from estrogen-dependent renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity impairs calcium absorption and drives secondary hyperparathyroidism","E":"Decreased M-CSF secretion by marrow stromal cells reduces osteoclast precursor survival and is replaced by IL-17-dependent osteoclastogenesis in estrogen deficiency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Estrogen maintains bone mass in part by stimulating OPG production in osteoblasts. OPG is a decoy receptor that binds RANKL and prevents it from interacting with its receptor RANK on osteoclast precursors. After menopause, estrogen deficiency reduces OPG production, increasing the RANKL:OPG ratio. The excess unbound RANKL then binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, activating NF-kB signaling and driving osteoclastogenesis. This results in a high-turnover state with increased osteoclast number and activity. Simultaneously, estrogen deficiency elevates pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha) that further amplify RANKL expression and osteoclast survival.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Menopause → estrogen → OPG → RANKL:OPG ratio → osteoclastogenesis. Also → IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha → further RANKL amplification. Result: high-turnover osteoporosis. Denosumab works by mimicking OPG — it is a RANKL-neutralizing antibody. COMLEX: Postmenopausal osteoporosis is a major COMLEX Clinical Presentation 8 condition; estrogen therapy is no longer first-line due to breast cancer and cardiovascular risks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha produced in estrogen deficiency directly amplify osteoclastogenesis via RANKL upregulation"},{"id":"sdl56_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the physiologic factors leading to menopausal bone loss.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which bone compartment undergoes the most rapid and pronounced loss in the first decade following menopause, and what is the approximate annual rate?","choices":{"A":"Cancellous bone, with its high surface-to-volume ratio providing extensive osteoclast access, loses up to 9% per year in the first decade after menopause","B":"Cortical bone of the femoral diaphysis, which provides the greatest total skeletal mass, loses up to 9% per year due to intracortical remodeling driven by PTH excess","C":"Subchondral bone in weight-bearing joints, which is most exposed to mechanical loading, loses up to 6% per year through estrogen-dependent osteoclast activation at joint surfaces","D":"Periosteal bone of the distal radius, which is metabolically active in younger women, loses up to 12% per year as periosteal osteoblast activity drops after estrogen withdrawal","E":"Endosteal cortical bone at the femoral neck, which is the most vascular skeletal region, loses up to 3% per year because of estrogen-dependent marrow vascularity changes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cancellous bone, also called trabecular or spongy bone, has a vastly higher surface-to-volume ratio than cortical bone, exposing far more bone surface to osteoclast-mediated resorption. In postmenopausal women, cancellous bone can lose up to 9% per year in the first decade after menopause, while cortical bone loses approximately 2% per year during this same accelerated phase. The cancellous-rich vertebral bodies and femoral neck are therefore the classic sites of postmenopausal fracture. After the first decade, the rate of loss decelerates as a new, lower steady state is established.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Postmenopausal bone loss — cancellous up to 9%/year, cortical up to 2%/year in the first decade. Classic fracture sites: vertebral compression, Colles fracture of distal radius, femoral neck fracture. DEXA T-score: -1.0 to -2.5 = osteopenia; -2.5 and below = osteoporosis. COMLEX: Vertebral compression fractures cause acute back pain, loss of height, and kyphosis — classic presentation on COMLEX clinical vignettes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha produced in estrogen deficiency directly amplify osteoclastogenesis via RANKL upregulation"},{"id":"sdl56_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the physiologic factors leading to menopausal bone loss.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the context of postmenopausal osteoporosis, which three pro-inflammatory cytokines are upregulated by estrogen withdrawal and directly amplify osteoclastogenesis and osteoclast survival?","choices":{"A":"IL-4, IL-10, and TGF-beta are elevated after menopause and stimulate osteoclast precursor proliferation through STAT6 and Smad3-dependent transcriptional activation","B":"IL-2, IFN-gamma, and TNF-beta are elevated in estrogen deficiency and drive osteoclast activation through T cell receptor signaling and MHC class I cross-presentation","C":"IL-8, IL-12, and IL-23 are upregulated by estrogen deficiency and recruit neutrophils and dendritic cells that amplify peritrabecular bone destruction at remodeling sites","D":"IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha are elevated after estrogen withdrawal and directly promote osteoclast precursor differentiation, prolong mature osteoclast survival, and amplify bone resorption","E":"IL-3, IL-5, and GM-CSF are produced by marrow stromal cells after estrogen withdrawal and drive mast cell degranulation and eosinophil recruitment near trabecular surfaces"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Estrogen deficiency after menopause leads to elevated levels of IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha. These cytokines directly promote osteoclastogenesis by increasing RANKL expression on osteoblasts and marrow stromal cells, and by prolonging osteoclast survival through anti-apoptotic signaling. IL-1 and TNF-alpha are particularly potent amplifiers of RANKL-driven osteoclast differentiation. This cytokine-driven amplification of the RANKL pathway explains why the postmenopausal period is characterized by high-turnover bone remodeling with markedly elevated markers such as urinary N-telopeptide and serum C-telopeptide.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Menopause cytokine triad = IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha. These same cytokines drive bone erosion in RA (periarticular erosions = RANKL amplification at synovial pannus). High-turnover markers: elevated urinary NTx, CTx, and serum bone-specific ALP. COMLEX: RANKL inhibitors like denosumab work both in osteoporosis and in cancer-related bone metastases by targeting the same pathway.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha produced in estrogen deficiency directly amplify osteoclastogenesis via RANKL upregulation"},{"id":"sdl56_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the function of RANK Ligand and OPG in bone metabolism.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"OPG is a key molecular regulator of osteoclastogenesis. On which cell type is OPG primarily produced, and what is its mechanism of action?","choices":{"A":"OPG is a membrane-bound co-receptor expressed on osteoclast precursors that competes with RANK for RANKL binding and reduces osteoclast activation when RANKL is limited","B":"OPG is a soluble decoy receptor secreted by osteoblasts that binds RANKL in the extracellular space, preventing RANKL from interacting with RANK on osteoclast precursors and inhibiting osteoclastogenesis","C":"OPG is a matrix metalloproteinase inhibitor secreted by marrow stromal cells that blocks collagen degradation during osteoclast-driven resorption lacunae formation","D":"OPG is a serine kinase receptor expressed on osteocytes that activates Wnt signaling downstream of LRP5 and promotes osteoblast progenitor differentiation from marrow stroma","E":"OPG is a cytokine receptor expressed on T lymphocytes that suppresses IL-6 and IL-1 release from macrophages and limits osteoclast-promoting inflammation in bone marrow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"OPG, or osteoprotegerin, is a soluble decoy receptor secreted primarily by osteoblasts and marrow stromal cells. It acts by binding RANKL in the extracellular space, preventing RANKL from engaging its functional receptor RANK on osteoclast precursors. Because OPG occupies RANKL without triggering any downstream signaling, it effectively neutralizes RANKL and inhibits osteoclastogenesis. The RANKL:OPG ratio is a key determinant of osteoclast number and activity. When OPG is high relative to RANKL, bone resorption is suppressed. When the ratio tips in favor of RANKL (such as in estrogen deficiency or inflammatory states), osteoclastogenesis increases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RANKL/RANK/OPG axis: RANKL on osteoblasts and stromal cells binds RANK on osteoclast precursors → NF-kB → osteoclastogenesis. OPG = decoy receptor, binds RANKL, blocks RANK interaction. OPG is the prototype for denosumab. Mutations in RANK, RANKL, OPG → severe disruption of bone metabolism. COMLEX: The RANKL-OPG axis is also active in lymph node development and immune regulation, making it a cross-system high-yield target.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in rheumatoid arthritis amplify RANKL expression on synoviocytes, driving periarticular bone erosion"},{"id":"sdl56_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the function of RANK Ligand and OPG in bone metabolism.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"When RANKL binds to RANK on osteoclast precursors, which intracellular signaling pathway is primarily activated to drive differentiation into mature osteoclasts?","choices":{"A":"NF-kB signaling is primarily activated by RANK-RANKL interaction and is essential for osteoclast generation, survival, and the maintenance of bone resorption capacity","B":"JAK-STAT signaling is activated by RANK-RANKL binding through associated JAK2 kinase, leading to STAT3-dependent transcription of osteoclast cathepsin K and acid pump genes","C":"Wnt/beta-catenin signaling is activated by RANK-RANKL binding through a RANK-LRP5 co-receptor complex that drives nuclear beta-catenin translocation in osteoclast precursors","D":"PI3K-AKT-mTOR signaling is the primary pathway activated by RANK-RANKL binding, promoting osteoclast precursor survival and protein synthesis through mTORC1 activation","E":"MAPK-ERK signaling is the primary pathway activated by RANK-RANKL binding, driving cyclin D1 expression and entry of osteoclast precursors into the cell cycle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"RANK belongs to the TNF receptor superfamily. Upon RANKL binding, RANK recruits TRAF adapter proteins (especially TRAF6) which activate NF-kB signaling. NF-kB translocates to the nucleus and drives transcription of genes essential for osteoclast differentiation, including NFATc1, the master regulator of osteoclastogenesis. NF-kB signaling is also required for osteoclast survival. This pathway is the reason why therapeutic RANKL inhibition with denosumab potently suppresses osteoclastogenesis — blocking RANKL prevents the RANK-TRAF6-NF-kB cascade from initiating. Wnt signaling and PI3K-AKT are important in osteoblast biology, not osteoclast differentiation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RANK-RANKL → TRAF6 → NF-kB → NFATc1 = master osteoclast transcription factor. NF-kB is also the target of bisphosphonate downstream effects on osteoclast apoptosis. Denosumab upstream blocks RANKL before it ever reaches RANK. COMLEX: NF-kB is a convergent pathway for both inflammatory cytokines and RANKL — explaining why RA (IL-1, TNF-alpha-driven) causes the same periarticular bone erosion pattern as primary osteoclast activation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in rheumatoid arthritis amplify RANKL expression on synoviocytes, driving periarticular bone erosion"},{"id":"sdl56_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the function of RANK Ligand and OPG in bone metabolism.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which drug directly mimics the function of OPG by binding RANKL in the extracellular space and preventing osteoclast activation?","choices":{"A":"Alendronate, a nitrogen-containing bisphosphonate that is internalized by osteoclasts during bone resorption and inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase to induce apoptosis","B":"Romosozumab, a monoclonal antibody directed against sclerostin that removes the Wnt inhibitory brake on osteoblasts and simultaneously suppresses osteoclast activity","C":"Teriparatide, recombinant PTH 1-34 that activates PTH-1 receptors on osteoblasts at intermittent doses to drive anabolic bone formation beyond resorption-coupled rates","D":"Denosumab, a human monoclonal antibody directed against RANKL, binds RANKL with high affinity and prevents it from interacting with RANK on osteoclast precursors","E":"Raloxifene, a selective estrogen receptor modulator that acts as an estrogen agonist in bone tissue and reduces osteoclast-mediated resorption through genomic ER signaling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Denosumab is a fully human IgG2 monoclonal antibody that binds RANKL with very high affinity and specificity, acting as an OPG mimetic. By neutralizing RANKL, denosumab prevents it from binding RANK on osteoclast precursors, thereby blocking osteoclastogenesis. Unlike bisphosphonates, denosumab does not integrate into the bone matrix and its effect is fully reversible upon discontinuation — stopping denosumab abruptly can cause rebound osteoclastogenesis and rapid bone loss. Given subcutaneously every 6 months, denosumab is approved for postmenopausal osteoporosis, glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis, and bone metastases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Denosumab = anti-RANKL monoclonal antibody, mimics OPG, given SC every 6 months. Critical: unlike bisphosphonates, must transition to antiresorptive when stopping denosumab or rebound bone loss and fractures occur. Also used in giant cell tumor of bone. COMLEX: Denosumab represents a biologic drug in the same conceptual class as biologic DMARDs — targeted neutralization of a specific ligand.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in rheumatoid arthritis amplify RANKL expression on synoviocytes, driving periarticular bone erosion"},{"id":"sdl56_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the process resulting in bone density occurring with hyperparathyroidism.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which histologic lesion is considered the hallmark of severe primary hyperparathyroidism in bone, and what accounts for the characteristic color of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Mosaic lamellar bone with irregular cement lines filling trabecular spaces and a patchwork of old and new bone matrix characterizes severe hyperparathyroid bone pathology","B":"Unmineralized osteoid seams wider than 12 microns accumulating on trabecular surfaces and staining orange-red on Goldner trichrome represent hyperparathyroid bone disease","C":"Cortical tunneling by osteoclasts forming intracortical canals that are later lined by fibrous tissue and hemosiderin deposits characterizes osteitis fibrosa of the diaphysis","D":"Subperiosteal erosions producing a lacy irregular cortical border on the radial surface of the middle phalanges represent the earliest radiographic sign of hyperparathyroid bone","E":"Brown tumors are osteoclast-filled bone cysts in which microfractures and hemorrhage cause hemosiderin deposition, imparting the characteristic brown discoloration"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brown tumors are the hallmark lesion of severe primary hyperparathyroidism. They are not true neoplasms but rather osteoclast-filled bone cysts that form as excess PTH drives intense cortical resorption. As osteoclasts enlarge these cavities, microfractures occur and secondary hemorrhage deposits hemosiderin within the lesion, giving it the characteristic brown color. Widespread development of brown tumors represents osteitis fibrosa cystica — the generalized severe form of hyperparathyroid bone disease. Modern diagnosis of primary hyperparathyroidism at early stages means osteitis fibrosa cystica is now rarely seen in clinical practice.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Brown tumor = osteoclast-filled cystic lesion with hemosiderin; brown color from hemosiderin. Generalized form = osteitis fibrosa cystica. Salt-and-pepper skull and subperiosteal resorption of radial middle phalanges are classic X-ray findings. Primary HPT: high Ca, high PTH, low PO4. COMLEX: Primary HPT classic triad = Stones (nephrolithiasis), Bones (bone pain, brown tumors), Groans (GI — constipation, nausea, peptic ulcers).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition is a recognized complication of primary hyperparathyroidism-driven metabolic dysregulation"},{"id":"sdl56_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the process resulting in bone density occurring with hyperparathyroidism.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Excess PTH in primary hyperparathyroidism drives osteoclastic resorption at a specific bone surface that produces the most pathognomonic radiographic finding. Which surface is this?","choices":{"A":"The subperiosteal surface of the radial aspect of the middle phalanges shows the most prominent osteoclast-driven resorption, producing a lacy irregular cortical border on plain radiograph","B":"The trabecular surfaces of the lumbar vertebral bodies show preferential osteoclast erosion, producing vertical corduroy striations due to selective horizontal trabecular resorption","C":"The endochondral growth plate of long bones shows the most prominent PTH-driven osteoclast activity, producing metaphyseal widening and flaring on plain films","D":"The articular cartilage of metacarpophalangeal joints undergoes osteoclast-mediated erosion, producing the joint space narrowing pattern that mimics rheumatoid arthritis","E":"The periosteal surface of the outer table of the skull shows preferential osteoclast activity, producing the ground glass pattern that overlaps with fibrous dysplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PTH-driven osteoclastic resorption most prominently occurs at the subperiosteal cortical bone surface, particularly the radial aspect of the middle phalanges of the hand. On plain radiograph, this appears as a lacy, irregular cortical border — a pathognomonic finding of hyperparathyroidism. Radiographically, the skull may show a salt-and-pepper pattern from diffuse osteoclastic activity. The classic clinical teaching is that subperiosteal resorption of the middle phalanges is the most specific radiographic finding for hyperparathyroidism among all bone diseases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTH effects on bone = increases osteoclast activity at subperiosteal surfaces; specifically radial aspect of middle phalanges on hand X-ray. Also skull = salt-and-pepper pattern. Biochemistry: primary HPT = high Ca, high PTH, low PO4, elevated ALP. COMLEX: PTH acts on bone through osteoblasts (which express PTH-1R) to upregulate RANKL, then osteoclasts respond — PTH has no direct receptor on osteoclasts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition is a recognized complication of primary hyperparathyroidism-driven metabolic dysregulation"},{"id":"sdl56_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the process resulting in bone density occurring with hyperparathyroidism.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which set of laboratory values best distinguishes primary hyperparathyroidism from secondary hyperparathyroidism caused by chronic renal failure?","choices":{"A":"Primary hyperparathyroidism shows elevated alkaline phosphatase with normal calcium, while secondary hyperparathyroidism shows elevated calcium and elevated alkaline phosphatase","B":"Primary hyperparathyroidism shows elevated PTH with low phosphate and normal calcium, while secondary shows low PTH with high phosphate and low calcium","C":"Primary hyperparathyroidism shows low 25-hydroxyvitamin D with elevated calcium, while secondary shows normal vitamin D with elevated calcium and elevated PTH","D":"Primary hyperparathyroidism shows elevated serum calcium with elevated PTH, while secondary hyperparathyroidism shows low serum calcium with elevated PTH driven by hypocalcemia","E":"Primary hyperparathyroidism shows elevated calcium with suppressed PTH, while secondary hyperparathyroidism shows elevated calcium with proportionally elevated PTH"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In primary hyperparathyroidism, an autonomous parathyroid adenoma secretes excess PTH independent of serum calcium levels, resulting in hypercalcemia with inappropriately elevated (or non-suppressed) PTH. In secondary hyperparathyroidism from chronic renal failure, the failing kidney cannot activate vitamin D or excrete phosphate; the resulting hypocalcemia is the stimulus for compensatory PTH secretion. Therefore, secondary hyperparathyroidism is characterized by low serum calcium with elevated PTH. Both forms produce elevated PTH and increased osteoclastic bone resorption, but the calcium level differentiates them and guides treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary HPT = high Ca + high PTH + low PO4. Secondary HPT from CKD = LOW Ca + high PTH + high PO4. PTH in primary = autonomous; in secondary = compensatory response to hypocalcemia. Both increase osteoclast activity. Tertiary HPT = CKD patient develops autonomous PTH secretion after prolonged secondary HPT → high Ca + very high PTH. COMLEX: CKD sequence: decreased 1,25-OH2D → low Ca → high PTH → secondary HPT → renal osteodystrophy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition is a recognized complication of primary hyperparathyroidism-driven metabolic dysregulation"},{"id":"sdl56_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — List the bone changes related to vitamin D deficiency.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the primary pathologic mechanism by which vitamin D deficiency causes osteomalacia in adults?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin D deficiency directly triggers osteoclast overactivation through RANKL upregulation on marrow stromal cells, causing high-turnover resorption of already-mineralized trabecular bone","B":"Vitamin D deficiency inhibits osteoblast differentiation from mesenchymal progenitors by blocking Wnt/beta-catenin signaling, reducing type I collagen synthesis and osteoid production","C":"Vitamin D deficiency induces osteocyte apoptosis by depriving them of anti-apoptotic 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D receptor signaling, releasing mineral-binding proteins into the lacunar space","D":"Vitamin D deficiency reduces synthesis of bone morphogenetic proteins in osteoblasts, preventing hydroxyapatite nucleation even when calcium and phosphate are present in normal concentrations","E":"Vitamin D deficiency impairs intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption, reducing their serum availability and preventing adequate mineralization of newly synthesized osteoid matrix"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vitamin D deficiency causes osteomalacia (in adults) and rickets (in children) primarily by impairing calcium and phosphate absorption from the intestine. 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is essential for inducing intestinal calcium transport proteins (calbindin and TRPV6). Without adequate calcium and phosphate in the serum, newly synthesized osteoid cannot undergo normal mineralization, leading to accumulation of unmineralized osteoid seams. On bone biopsy, widened osteoid seams greater than 12.5 micrometers are diagnostic. Normal bone mineralization depends on both adequate substrate (calcium and phosphate) and functional alkaline phosphatase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin D deficiency → impaired Ca and PO4 absorption → unmineralized osteoid accumulates → osteomalacia in adults, rickets in children. Labs: low Ca, low PO4, elevated PTH (secondary HPT), elevated ALP, low 25-OH vitamin D. Bone biopsy: widened osteoid seams on tetracycline labeling. COMLEX: Osteomalacia causes bone pain (from periosteal irritation by unmineralized osteoid) and proximal muscle weakness — a key clinical clue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Vitamin D deficiency impairs immune tolerance and is linked to autoimmune disease susceptibility through type III and IV hypersensitivity"},{"id":"sdl56_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — List the bone changes related to vitamin D deficiency.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In children with rickets, which radiographic finding at the growth plate best reflects failure of provisional cartilage calcification at the zone of hypertrophic chondrocytes?","choices":{"A":"Chalk-stick transverse fractures through the diaphysis of long bones reflecting brittle, disorganized woven bone replacing the normal lamellar structure in rickets","B":"Metaphyseal cupping and fraying with a widened, irregular radiolucent growth plate reflects the accumulation of uncalcified hypertrophic chondrocytes in vitamin D-deficient endochondral ossification","C":"Periosteal elevation along the femoral and tibial diaphyses reflecting subperiosteal hemorrhage from scurvy, which must be excluded in the differential diagnosis of rickets","D":"Vertical striations in the vertebral bodies reflecting the preferential cancellous bone resorption pattern in calcium-deficient rickets from inadequate dietary intake","E":"Dense metaphyseal bands reflecting calcium precipitation lines at the zone of provisional calcification that appear as lead lines in vitamin D-deficient growth plates"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In normal endochondral ossification, hypertrophic chondrocytes in the growth plate undergo apoptosis and their lacunae become calcified at the zone of provisional calcification, allowing vascular invasion and replacement by woven bone. In vitamin D deficiency, calcium and phosphate availability is reduced, preventing mineralization of this zone. Hypertrophic chondrocytes accumulate, the zone of provisional calcification widens and becomes irregular, and the metaphysis shows cupping and fraying on plain radiograph. Additional pediatric findings include bowing of weight-bearing legs, rachitic rosary from costochondral junction widening, and craniotabes from soft calvaria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rickets X-ray = metaphyseal cupping and fraying + widened irregular growth plate. Also: bow legs, rachitic rosary, craniotabes, frontal bossing. Vs. scurvy = subperiosteal hemorrhage and periosteal elevation, scorbutic zone (radiolucent metaphyseal line). COMLEX: Rickets from nutritional vitamin D deficiency has a different presentation from X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets, which involves FGF23 excess causing phosphate wasting — treatment includes phosphate supplementation plus calcitriol.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Vitamin D deficiency impairs immune tolerance and is linked to autoimmune disease susceptibility through type III and IV hypersensitivity"},{"id":"sdl56_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — List the bone changes related to vitamin D deficiency.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In addition to impaired bone mineralization, what secondary endocrine complication of vitamin D deficiency further accelerates bone loss in patients with osteomalacia?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin D deficiency triggers primary hyperthyroidism through downregulation of vitamin D receptor in thyroid follicular cells, increasing metabolic rate and bone resorption","B":"Vitamin D deficiency causes autonomous insulin hypersecretion from pancreatic beta cells, leading to reactive hypoglycemia that impairs osteoblast energy metabolism","C":"Hypocalcemia from impaired intestinal calcium absorption stimulates secondary hyperparathyroidism, which increases osteoclast-driven bone resorption beyond the primary mineralization defect","D":"Vitamin D deficiency directly stimulates FGF23 overproduction from osteocytes independent of phosphate levels, causing renal phosphate wasting and further depleting mineralization substrates","E":"Vitamin D deficiency activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, increasing distal tubular calcium excretion and creating a positive feedback loop of worsening hypocalcemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In vitamin D deficiency, reduced intestinal calcium absorption causes hypocalcemia, which is a potent stimulus for PTH secretion by the parathyroid glands. This secondary hyperparathyroidism adds a resorptive component to the bone disease of vitamin D deficiency: PTH drives osteoclast-mediated cortical and trabecular bone resorption on top of the failure of osteoid mineralization. Clinically, labs in osteomalacia show low calcium, low phosphate, elevated PTH, elevated ALP, and low 25-hydroxyvitamin D. Secondary hyperparathyroidism can partially restore serum calcium at the expense of skeletal mineral content.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin D deficiency → low Ca → secondary PTH elevation → bone resorption amplified on top of impaired mineralization. Key lab pattern: low Ca + low PO4 + HIGH PTH + HIGH ALP + low 25-OH-D. Contrast with primary HPT: HIGH Ca + HIGH PTH + low PO4 + HIGH ALP. Osteomalacia treatment = replete vitamin D + calcium. COMLEX: Proximal muscle weakness and bone pain are classic clinical clues for vitamin D deficiency/osteomalacia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Vitamin D deficiency impairs immune tolerance and is linked to autoimmune disease susceptibility through type III and IV hypersensitivity"},{"id":"sdl56_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the bone changes associated with chronic renal disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In chronic kidney disease, which biochemical step in vitamin D metabolism is impaired by the failing kidney, resulting in reduced active vitamin D levels?","choices":{"A":"The failing kidney loses 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity, preventing conversion of 25-hydroxyvitamin D to the active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D that is required for intestinal calcium absorption","B":"The failing kidney excessively excretes 25-hydroxyvitamin D-binding protein into the urine, depleting circulating vitamin D metabolite reservoir and reducing substrate availability","C":"The failing kidney accumulates uremic toxins that inhibit hepatic 25-hydroxylase activity, blocking the first step of vitamin D activation from cholecalciferol to calcidiol","D":"The failing kidney produces excess FGF23 independently of phosphate, driving 24-hydroxylase upregulation that catabolizes 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D before it can act on target tissues","E":"The failing kidney develops vascular calcification that obstructs tubular blood flow, selectively ischemic the proximal tubule cells responsible for vitamin D hydroxylation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vitamin D undergoes two sequential hydroxylation steps for full activation. The first (25-hydroxylation) occurs in the liver, producing 25-hydroxyvitamin D. The second (1-alpha-hydroxylation) occurs primarily in the proximal tubule of the kidney, producing the biologically active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. In chronic kidney disease, the progressive loss of functional proximal tubular mass impairs 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity. Without adequate 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, intestinal calcium absorption falls, serum calcium drops, and secondary hyperparathyroidism develops. This is why standard vitamin D supplements are insufficient in CKD — patients require exogenous active vitamin D analogs such as calcitriol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin D activation pathway: skin/diet → D3 → liver 25-hydroxylation → 25-OH-D → kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylation → 1,25-OH2-D (calcitriol, active form). CKD blocks step 2. Treatment with calcitriol bypasses the impaired kidney step. FGF23 (from bone) inhibits renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase — elevated in CKD as a compensatory but ultimately counterproductive response. COMLEX: CKD patients require calcitriol (active analog) not ergocalciferol (D2) or cholecalciferol (D3) because the kidney cannot hydroxylate them.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — Routine lab panels in chronic renal disease reveal hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and elevated PTH as markers of renal osteodystrophy"},{"id":"sdl56_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the bone changes associated with chronic renal disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In renal osteodystrophy, how does hyperphosphatemia from the failing kidney contribute to worsening secondary hyperparathyroidism?","choices":{"A":"Hyperphosphatemia directly stimulates parathyroid chief cell proliferation by binding a phosphate-sensing receptor that activates the calcium-sensing receptor signaling cascade","B":"Hyperphosphatemia inhibits calcitonin secretion from parafollicular thyroid C cells, removing the negative feedback signal that normally suppresses PTH gene transcription","C":"Hyperphosphatemia lowers ionized calcium through calcium-phosphate complexation and suppresses renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity, removing two key inhibitors of PTH secretion simultaneously","D":"Hyperphosphatemia causes phosphaturia-driven metabolic alkalosis in the proximal tubule that sensitizes parathyroid cell calcium-sensing receptors to small decreases in ionized calcium","E":"Hyperphosphatemia upregulates PTH gene transcription directly through a phosphate response element in the PTH promoter region that is activated by elevated serum phosphate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperphosphatemia from renal failure impairs PTH regulation through two main mechanisms. First, high phosphate complexes with calcium in the circulation, lowering free ionized calcium. The parathyroid calcium-sensing receptor detects this drop in ionized calcium and increases PTH secretion. Second, hyperphosphatemia (and the elevated FGF23 that follows) inhibits renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, further reducing 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D levels. Low 1,25-OH2D normally suppresses PTH synthesis at the gene level, so its absence removes this brake. The combined effect is persistent, driven secondary hyperparathyroidism that causes osteitis fibrosa cystica as part of renal osteodystrophy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CKD cascade — low GFR → high PO4 and low 1,25-OH2D → low Ca → high PTH. Also elevated FGF23 worsens 1-alpha-hydroxylase suppression. Manage with phosphate binders, dietary phosphate restriction, calcitriol, and calcimimetics (cinacalcet) to suppress PTH. COMLEX: Rugger jersey spine on plain film = dense osteosclerotic bands at vertebral endplates alternating with less dense central bone — classic renal osteodystrophy finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — Routine lab panels in chronic renal disease reveal hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and elevated PTH as markers of renal osteodystrophy"},{"id":"sdl56_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the bone changes associated with chronic renal disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Renal osteodystrophy encompasses multiple skeletal pathologies. Which of the following best describes the full spectrum of bone changes that can occur in this condition?","choices":{"A":"Renal osteodystrophy produces exclusively osteosclerotic changes throughout the skeleton from calcium phosphate precipitation into bone matrix driven by hyperphosphatemia","B":"Renal osteodystrophy produces only high-turnover osteoclastic changes identical to primary hyperparathyroidism, with no component of impaired mineralization or adynamic disease","C":"Renal osteodystrophy consists solely of avascular necrosis at the femoral head and distal femoral condyles from vascular calcification obstructing subchondral blood supply","D":"Renal osteodystrophy encompasses osteomalacia from impaired vitamin D activation, secondary hyperparathyroid bone changes, osteosclerosis of vertebral endplates, and adynamic bone disease with very low turnover","E":"Renal osteodystrophy causes periosteal new bone formation along long bone diaphyses driven by FGF23-mediated osteoblast activation independent of PTH or vitamin D changes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Renal osteodystrophy is a collective term for the skeletal changes occurring in chronic renal failure, reflecting multiple simultaneous pathologic processes. These include: (1) osteomalacia from reduced 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D and impaired mineralization; (2) secondary hyperparathyroidism causing osteitis fibrosa cystica with subperiosteal resorption; (3) osteosclerosis, particularly at vertebral endplates giving the rugger jersey spine appearance; and (4) adynamic bone disease, particularly in dialysis patients who are over-treated with vitamin D analogs causing PTH to fall too low, leaving bone with virtually no remodeling capacity and reduced ability to repair microdamage. Growth retardation is a fifth component in skeletally immature patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renal osteodystrophy = four-component disease: osteomalacia + osteitis fibrosa (secondary HPT) + osteosclerosis + adynamic bone. Rugger jersey spine on lateral plain film is pathognomonic. Adynamic bone from over-suppressed PTH is paradoxically associated with high fracture risk despite low turnover. COMLEX: Dialysis patients need careful PTH monitoring — target PTH ranges are 2-9x the upper limit of normal to maintain adequate bone turnover without causing osteitis fibrosa.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — Routine lab panels in chronic renal disease reveal hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and elevated PTH as markers of renal osteodystrophy"},{"id":"sdl56_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the abnormal pathophysiology resulting in Paget's disease of the bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the hallmark microscopic finding in Paget disease of bone, and which phase of the disease does it represent?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse osteoid accumulation with widened unmineralized seams on all trabecular surfaces and osteoblasts embedded in uncalcified matrix characterizes the active mixed phase of Paget disease","B":"Peritrabecular fibrosis with osteoclast-filled cysts containing hemosiderin deposits and reactive woven bone formation characterizes the lytic phase of Paget disease","C":"A mosaic pattern of woven and lamellar bone in chaotic juxtapositions separated by irregular cement lines is the hallmark of the mixed phase of Paget disease and persists into the burned-out sclerotic stage","D":"Dense fibrous replacement of marrow spaces by spindle cells with interspersed woven bone trabeculae and peripheral rimming osteoblasts characterizes the sclerotic phase of Paget disease","E":"Infiltration of marrow by atypical osteoblasts forming haphazard new woven bone in sheets with entrapped osteoclasts is the histologic hallmark of pagetic bone remodeling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The histologic hallmark of Paget disease is the mosaic pattern — a patchwork of woven and lamellar bone in chaotic juxtaposition, separated by irregular scalloped cement lines that represent the boundaries of successive waves of resorption and deposition. This pattern arises because pagetic osteoclasts resorb bone abnormally rapidly, and osteoblasts deposit new bone in a disorganized fashion, producing architecturally inferior woven bone rather than organized lamellar bone. The three phases are: (1) lytic — predominantly osteoclastic; (2) mixed — concurrent osteoclastic and osteoblastic; (3) sclerotic — burnt-out, dense but structurally weak. The mosaic cement-line pattern is present throughout phases 2 and 3.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paget disease hallmark = mosaic pattern of woven and lamellar bone with irregular cement lines. Three phases: lytic (osteoclastic), mixed (concurrent), sclerotic (burned out). ALP is markedly elevated in active disease (osteoblastic activity); serum Ca and PO4 are NORMAL (unlike hyperparathyroidism). Serum ALP is the best marker of disease activity. COMLEX: Paget disease increases blood flow through hyperemic pagetic bone — can cause high-output heart failure in polyostotic disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 1 — Paget disease of bone carries a low but real risk of secondary osteosarcoma, the most feared malignant complication of pagetic remodeling"},{"id":"sdl56_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the abnormal pathophysiology resulting in Paget's disease of the bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 75-year-old man with known Paget disease has markedly elevated alkaline phosphatase but normal serum calcium, phosphate, and PTH. Which best explains why calcium remains normal in active Paget disease despite intense bone remodeling?","choices":{"A":"In Paget disease, the parathyroid glands detect excess calcium released from resorbing pagetic bone and maximally suppress PTH, driving renal calcium excretion to maintain normal serum levels","B":"In Paget disease, osteoclastic resorption and osteoblastic deposition occur in a coupled fashion within focal pagetic bone, so calcium liberated from resorption is locally reused in new bone and serum calcium remains balanced","C":"In Paget disease, calcitonin is autonomously secreted from C cells in response to pagetic bone hypervascularity, continuously suppressing serum calcium toward normal ranges","D":"In Paget disease, the renal tubules dramatically increase calcium excretion as a homeostatic response to the large flux of calcium entering the circulation from pagetic resorption sites","E":"In Paget disease, osteoclast activity is inhibited by intracellular signaling defects in pagetic cells, so net resorption does not exceed formation despite elevated ALP and intense histologic activity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Paget disease is characterized by coupled but chaotic and accelerated remodeling within focal pagetic lesions. Osteoclastic resorption and osteoblastic new bone formation occur simultaneously within the same pagetic foci, so the calcium liberated from resorption is quickly taken up by osteoblasts in the formation phase. The net effect is that serum calcium, phosphate, and PTH remain normal — a key feature distinguishing Paget disease from primary hyperparathyroidism. The biochemical hallmark is an isolated markedly elevated ALP (bone fraction) reflecting the intense osteoblastic response. Immobilization of a patient with Paget disease can uncouple this balance and cause hypercalcemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paget disease labs = markedly elevated ALP (bone fraction), NORMAL Ca, NORMAL PO4, NORMAL PTH. Contrast with primary HPT: high Ca + high PTH. Immobilization in Paget disease can cause hypercalcemia by uncoupling resorption from formation. Treatment: bisphosphonates (reduce osteoclast activity). Also calcitonin in refractory cases. COMLEX: Paget disease skull involvement causes hearing loss from cochlear compression; hat size increase from skull enlargement is a classic clinical vignette detail.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 1 — Paget disease of bone carries a low but real risk of secondary osteosarcoma, the most feared malignant complication of pagetic remodeling"},{"id":"sdl56_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the abnormal pathophysiology resulting in Paget's disease of the bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which rare but serious neoplastic complication occurs in fewer than 1% of all patients with Paget disease but in 5 to 10% of those with severe polyostotic involvement?","choices":{"A":"Secondary osteosarcoma arising within pagetic bone represents the most feared malignant complication, occurring in 5 to 10 percent of patients with severe polyostotic Paget disease","B":"High-output cardiac failure from arteriovenous shunting within hyperemic polyostotic pagetic bone can progress to dilated cardiomyopathy and requires urgent antiresorptive treatment","C":"Basilar invagination from skull base pagetic remodeling compresses the brainstem and produces cranial nerve palsies and ataxia requiring surgical decompression","D":"Chalk-stick transverse fractures through pagetic long bones result in permanent nonunion due to structurally abnormal pagetic bone callus with deficient osteoblast bridging","E":"Secondary amyloidosis from systemic chronic inflammation in polyostotic Paget disease causes progressive nephrotic syndrome requiring early bone marrow biopsy for diagnosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Secondary osteosarcoma is the most feared and clinically important complication of Paget disease. While it occurs in fewer than 1% of all Paget disease patients overall, the rate rises to 5-10% in patients with severe polyostotic disease. The osteosarcoma typically arises within pagetic bone and carries a very poor prognosis — worse than primary osteosarcoma in younger patients. Clinically, it presents as sudden worsening of bone pain, rapid expansion of a known pagetic lesion, or an elevated ALP that escalates further. TP53 and RB gene inactivation are the most commonly identified drivers. High-output heart failure and fractures are also complications but are not neoplastic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paget disease complications: (1) Secondary osteosarcoma — less than 1% overall, 5-10% with severe polyostotic disease; worst prognosis of all osteosarcomas. (2) High-output CHF from AV shunting in hyperemic pagetic bone. (3) Chalk-stick transverse fractures. (4) Hearing loss from cochlear/ossicular involvement. (5) Basilar invagination. COMLEX: Sudden worsening pain or rapidly rising ALP in a known Paget disease patient = rule out sarcomatous transformation immediately.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 1 — Paget disease of bone carries a low but real risk of secondary osteosarcoma, the most feared malignant complication of pagetic remodeling"},{"id":"sdl56_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the classes and mechanisms of action in medications approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Bisphosphonates are the most widely prescribed class of drugs for osteoporosis. What is the mechanism by which nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates inhibit osteoclast function?","choices":{"A":"Bisphosphonates bind to RANK receptors on osteoclast precursors and competitively block RANKL from initiating differentiation, acting upstream of the NF-kB signaling pathway","B":"Bisphosphonates bind hydroxyapatite in bone and are internalized by osteoclasts during resorption, where they inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway, preventing prenylation of GTPases and inducing osteoclast apoptosis","C":"Bisphosphonates mimic calcitonin by binding to calcitonin receptors on osteoclasts and activating adenylyl cyclase, suppressing osteoclast acid secretion and ruffled border formation","D":"Bisphosphonates inhibit cathepsin K activity in osteoclast lysosomes after direct binding, preventing collagenolysis and dissolution of the organic component of bone matrix","E":"Bisphosphonates block PTH-1 receptor signaling in osteoblasts, reducing RANKL expression and indirectly decreasing osteoclast recruitment and activation at remodeling sites"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate, zoledronate) are pyrophosphate analogs that bind tightly to hydroxyapatite in bone mineral. When osteoclasts resorb pagetic or osteoporotic bone, they ingest bisphosphonate-laden mineral. Intracellularly, the bisphosphonate inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate cholesterol synthesis pathway, preventing the prenylation of small GTPases such as Rac, Rho, and Ras that are required for osteoclast cytoskeletal function and survival. The result is osteoclast apoptosis. Because bisphosphonates are tightly bound to bone mineral, they have a prolonged skeletal residence time, which is why drug holidays are possible after long-term use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bisphosphonates = pyrophosphate analogs; bind hydroxyapatite; internalized by osteoclasts; inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in mevalonate pathway; prevent GTPase prenylation; cause osteoclast apoptosis. Key adverse effects: esophagitis (must stay upright 30-60 min), osteonecrosis of jaw (ONJ), atypical subtrochanteric femur fractures. Zoledronate = IV, given once yearly. COMLEX: Bisphosphonates also used for Paget disease, hypercalcemia of malignancy, osteogenesis imperfecta, and bone metastases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — Denosumab mirrors biologic DMARD logic by using a monoclonal antibody to neutralize a specific cytokine ligand in the RANKL pathway"},{"id":"sdl56_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the classes and mechanisms of action in medications approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which osteoporosis medication uniquely combines both anabolic and antiresorptive mechanisms simultaneously, distinguishing it from bisphosphonates and teriparatide?","choices":{"A":"Denosumab, a monoclonal antibody against RANKL, inhibits osteoclastogenesis while upregulating OPG in osteoblasts through a secondary feedback loop that promotes bone formation","B":"Raloxifene, a selective estrogen receptor modulator, acts as an estrogen agonist in bone tissue to increase osteoblast activity while antagonizing estrogen in breast and uterine tissue","C":"Teriparatide, recombinant PTH 1-34, increases bone formation predominantly at intermittent doses and also has a secondary antiresorptive effect through direct calcitonin stimulation","D":"Alendronate, a nitrogen-containing bisphosphonate, inhibits osteoclast farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase while simultaneously stimulating osteoblast differentiation through BMP pathway activation","E":"Romosozumab, a monoclonal antibody against sclerostin, simultaneously removes the Wnt inhibitory brake to increase bone formation while also suppressing osteoclast activity, producing a dual anabolic and antiresorptive effect"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Romosozumab is a monoclonal antibody against sclerostin, the Wnt signaling inhibitor produced by osteocytes. By neutralizing sclerostin, romosozumab removes the inhibitory brake on osteoblasts, leading to markedly increased bone formation. Additionally, in the first months of treatment, romosozumab also suppresses osteoclast activity — a dual mechanism not shared by bisphosphonates (antiresorptive only) or teriparatide (primarily anabolic with a secondary resorption increase at high doses). Romosozumab is approved for a maximum of 12 months, after which transition to an antiresorptive agent (bisphosphonate or denosumab) is recommended. It carries an FDA boxed warning for increased cardiovascular risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Romosozumab = anti-sclerostin antibody → dual mechanism: anabolic (removes Wnt inhibition) + antiresorptive (suppresses osteoclasts). Approved for 1 year only, then switch to antiresorptive. FDA boxed warning: increased MI and stroke risk — avoid in patients with recent cardiovascular events. COMLEX: Romosozumab vs. teriparatide: both are for severe osteoporosis; romosozumab is preferred when cardiovascular risk is low, teriparatide when cardiovascular risk is elevated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — Denosumab mirrors biologic DMARD logic by using a monoclonal antibody to neutralize a specific cytokine ligand in the RANKL pathway"},{"id":"sdl56_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the classes and mechanisms of action in medications approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Teriparatide and abaloparatide are anabolic agents approved for osteoporosis. What is the maximum approved duration of use for each agent, and what drug class is recommended to follow this therapy?","choices":{"A":"Both teriparatide and abaloparatide are approved for one year of use due to osteosarcoma risk in rat studies, after which transition to calcitonin therapy is required to consolidate gains","B":"Teriparatide is approved for up to three years in glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis and five years in postmenopausal osteoporosis, with no recommended transition therapy required","C":"Both teriparatide and abaloparatide are each approved for a maximum of two years of use, and patients should be transitioned to an antiresorptive agent such as a bisphosphonate or denosumab to preserve the bone gains","D":"Teriparatide is approved for indefinite use as long as bone mineral density continues to increase on serial DEXA scans and serum calcium remains within the normal reference range","E":"Both teriparatide and abaloparatide are approved for a maximum of six months, reflecting the short anabolic window before osteoblast PTH receptor desensitization limits further response"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Teriparatide (recombinant PTH 1-34) and abaloparatide (a PTHrP 1-34 analog) are each approved for a maximum of two years of use. The two-year limit derives from rat studies showing osteosarcoma development with prolonged high-dose PTH-1R stimulation, though this has not been observed in human clinical trials. After completing teriparatide or abaloparatide, patients must be transitioned to an antiresorptive agent (bisphosphonate or denosumab) to preserve the bone mineral density gained. Without this transition, much of the gain is rapidly lost as osteoclast activity rebounds. Abaloparatide is given as a daily 80-mcg subcutaneous injection, while teriparatide is given as 20 mcg subcutaneously daily.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Teriparatide = PTH 1-34, 20 mcg SC daily, max 2 years. Abaloparatide = PTHrP analog, 80 mcg SC daily, max 2 years. Mechanism: intermittent PTH-1R stimulation → preferential osteoblast activation over osteoclast. Adverse effects: transient hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria, orthostatic hypotension after first dose. Both are contraindicated in Paget disease and prior bone radiation. COMLEX: Teriparatide is the only anabolic option for glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis where bisphosphonates have failed or are contraindicated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — Denosumab mirrors biologic DMARD logic by using a monoclonal antibody to neutralize a specific cytokine ligand in the RANKL pathway"},{"id":"sdl56_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the factors that contribute to the achievement of peak bone mass.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old female competitive runner presents with a tibial stress fracture. DEXA shows bone density 1.8 standard deviations below age-matched peers. She reports amenorrhea for 9 months and consumes fewer than 1200 kcal/day with minimal dairy intake. Her coach notes she trains 25 hours per week. Serum estradiol is undetectable and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 14 ng/mL. Which combination of physiologic deficits most directly accounts for her failure to accrue expected peak bone mass?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive tibial microtrauma from high-volume training stimulates RANKL overexpression at remodeling sites, causing localized osteoclast overshoot that exceeds osteoblast-driven periosteal repair capacity","B":"Elevated cortisol from chronic training-related stress suppresses IGF-1 receptor expression in osteoblasts, reducing insulin-like growth factor-mediated matrix synthesis in cortical bone diaphyses","C":"Physical activity-induced FGF23 excess from osteocyte mechanosensory overload promotes renal phosphate wasting that impairs hydroxyapatite deposition in newly formed woven bone","D":"Excessive mechanical loading of immature cortical bone directly inhibits sclerostin in osteocytes, paradoxically downregulating Wnt signaling and impairing stress-induced bone formation","E":"Estrogen deficiency from hypothalamic amenorrhea reduces OPG expression in osteoblasts, increasing osteoclastogenesis, while inadequate calcium intake and low vitamin D further impair osteoid mineralization"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient has the female athlete triad: energy deficiency, functional hypothalamic amenorrhea, and low bone density. Hypothalamic amenorrhea suppresses estrogen, which normally maintains bone by increasing OPG production in osteoblasts to keep the RANKL:OPG ratio low. Without estrogen, OPG falls, RANKL dominates, and osteoclastogenesis accelerates. Concurrently, her very low calcium intake and vitamin D deficiency reduce substrate availability for osteoid mineralization. Peak bone mass requires adequate calcium intake, vitamin D, physical activity (which is present), and sex steroids — she is deficient in the last three of these four pillars.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Female athlete triad = energy deficiency + functional hypothalamic amenorrhea + low BMD. Peak bone mass requires: VDR-mediated calcium absorption, adequate calcium intake, sex steroids, weight-bearing activity. Loss of any combination reduces accrual. COMLEX: The athlete triad has a fourth component in the updated framework — relative energy deficiency in sport. Stress fractures in young female athletes should prompt evaluation of menstrual status and nutritional intake.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and BMU remodeling: osteocyte mechanosensory network couples mechanical load to formation, but cannot compensate for estrogen and nutrient deficiency"},{"id":"sdl56_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the factors that contribute to the achievement of peak bone mass.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old man is referred after sustaining a fragility fracture of the distal radius from a minor fall. DEXA shows a T-score of -2.9 at the hip. He denies steroid use, malabsorption, or chronic illness. Serum calcium, phosphate, and PTH are all normal. 25-hydroxyvitamin D is mildly low at 22 ng/mL. His physician explains that the majority of his bone loss represents the age-related form of osteoporosis and that his peak bone mass, achieved at age 28, was likely normal. Which of the following best describes the cellular mechanism of his age-related bone loss?","choices":{"A":"Declining osteoblast proliferative and biosynthetic capacity with age produces a low-turnover imbalance in which resorption slightly exceeds formation at approximately 0.7% per year in both sexes","B":"Age-related parathyroid hyperplasia driven by declining calcium absorption causes sustained PTH elevation that drives high-turnover osteoclastic resorption in cortical bone diaphyses","C":"Progressive loss of estrogen production in aging males due to declining aromatase activity creates a postmenopausal-equivalent high-turnover estrogen-deficiency resorption state","D":"Accumulation of advanced glycation end products in osteoblast collagen with aging impairs the RANKL-to-OPG signaling ratio and indirectly amplifies osteoclast recruitment","E":"Age-related increase in sclerostin production by osteocytes blocks Wnt/beta-catenin signaling in osteoblasts, progressively suppressing bone formation independent of resorption rates"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Senile osteoporosis is the low-turnover form of age-related bone loss affecting both sexes equally at approximately 0.7% per year after the fourth decade. It is driven primarily by declining osteoblast renewal — aged bone marrow produces fewer osteoblast progenitors and existing osteoblasts have reduced biosynthetic capacity. This stands in contrast to postmenopausal osteoporosis, which is a high-turnover form driven by estrogen deficiency and elevated IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha. Normal calcium, phosphate, and PTH in this patient exclude secondary causes. Mild vitamin D insufficiency may contribute marginally but does not explain the bulk of loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Senile osteoporosis = low-turnover, ~0.7%/year, both sexes, declining osteoblast capacity. Postmenopausal = high-turnover, estrogen deficiency, elevated IL-1/IL-6/TNF-alpha, up to 9% cancellous/year. Both increase fracture risk at vertebral bodies, distal radius, and femoral neck. COMLEX: Differentiating senile from postmenopausal osteoporosis matters for treatment: both benefit from bisphosphonates, but anabolic therapy is especially indicated for severe low-turnover disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis: the balance between osteoblast-driven formation and osteoclast-driven resorption within BMUs determines whether bone mass is maintained or progressively lost"},{"id":"sdl56_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the factors that contribute to the achievement of peak bone mass.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old perimenopausal woman is found to have osteopenia on DEXA with a T-score of -1.8 at the lumbar spine. She has adequate weight-bearing activity, a BMI of 20, and consumes 650 mg of calcium daily. Genetic testing performed as part of a research study reveals she carries a vitamin D receptor gene polymorphism associated with reduced efficiency of calcium absorption at normal dietary intakes. Which intervention most directly compensates for the impact of this polymorphism on her skeleton?","choices":{"A":"Adding resistance exercise training three times per week activates the osteocyte mechanosensory network and upregulates Wnt/beta-catenin signaling to drive osteoblast-mediated periosteal apposition independent of calcium","B":"Initiating prophylactic bisphosphonate therapy preserves current bone density by inhibiting osteoclast farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase and preventing resorption that would further unmask the calcium deficit","C":"Starting a SERM such as raloxifene increases OPG expression in osteoblasts and compensates for the VDR polymorphism by reducing RANKL-driven osteoclastogenesis through estrogen receptor agonism","D":"Increasing dietary calcium intake to 1200 mg per day combined with vitamin D3 supplementation to maintain 25-hydroxyvitamin D above 30 ng/mL saturates absorption pathways to offset the reduced efficiency from the VDR polymorphism","E":"Prescribing calcitriol directly bypasses VDR binding requirements entirely and delivers active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D to intestinal cells through a non-genomic membrane receptor mechanism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"VDR gene polymorphisms reduce the efficiency of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D-mediated calcium transport across intestinal epithelium, meaning less calcium is absorbed per unit of oral intake. The compensatory strategy is to increase both the substrate (dietary calcium) and the signal (vitamin D to produce 1,25-OH2D) to saturate absorption even with reduced per-receptor efficiency. A goal of 1200 mg calcium daily and 25-OH-D above 30 ng/mL is the standard recommendation for perimenopausal women. Resistance exercise is important but addresses mechanical bone formation rather than calcium absorption. Bisphosphonates address resorption but do not correct the mineralization substrate deficit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VDR polymorphisms are the strongest genetic determinant of peak bone mass variation in genome-wide association studies. Calcium and vitamin D supplementation remain the foundational intervention for all osteoporosis prevention. Daily requirements: calcium 1200 mg/day postmenopausal; vitamin D to maintain 25-OH-D above 30 ng/mL. COMLEX: Low dairy intake, dark skin, limited sun exposure, and northern latitude are the main risk factors for vitamin D insufficiency — all reduce substrate for 1,25-OH2D production.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Cytokine functions: 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D modulates cytokine production in immune cells, connecting calcium metabolism to immune regulation beyond its skeletal role"},{"id":"sdl56_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the physiologic factors leading to menopausal bone loss.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman presents 4 years after her last menstrual period with vertebral compression fractures at T7 and T9, acute back pain, and 2 cm of height loss. DEXA shows a lumbar spine T-score of -3.4. Serum markers reveal markedly elevated urinary N-telopeptide, indicating a high bone turnover state. Serum calcium and PTH are normal. Which molecular change at the level of the osteoblast best explains the high bone turnover found in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Increased sclerostin secretion from osteocytes in estrogen-depleted bone blocks Wnt/beta-catenin signaling and shifts the BMU balance toward high-turnover resorption over formation","B":"Decreased OPG secretion by estrogen-deficient osteoblasts raises the RANKL-to-OPG ratio, allowing more free RANKL to bind RANK on osteoclast precursors and amplify osteoclastogenesis beyond normal remodeling levels","C":"Elevated PTH from estrogen-deficiency-driven hypocalcemia activates cortical tunneling resorption through RANKL upregulation at endosteal surfaces, increasing overall bone turnover markers","D":"Deficient calcitonin from estrogen-dependent parafollicular C cell hypofunction removes the primary hormonal inhibitory brake on osteoclast acid secretion and resorption activity","E":"Decreased FGF23 after menopause increases renal phosphate reabsorption, raising the calcium-phosphate product and paradoxically stimulating hydroxyapatite dissolution in trabecular bone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Estrogen normally maintains bone by stimulating OPG production in osteoblasts. OPG is the decoy receptor that binds RANKL and prevents it from activating RANK on osteoclast precursors. After menopause, the drop in estrogen reduces OPG expression, shifting the RANKL:OPG ratio in favor of RANKL. Unbound RANKL then engages RANK on osteoclast precursors, triggering NF-kB-driven osteoclastogenesis. This produces a high-turnover state reflected in elevated bone resorption markers such as urinary N-telopeptide and C-telopeptide. Pro-inflammatory cytokines IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha upregulated by estrogen withdrawal further amplify this process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Postmenopausal high-turnover osteoporosis: estrogen → OPG → free RANKL → NF-kB → osteoclastogenesis. Lab marker of high turnover = elevated urinary NTx or CTx (resorption markers) and elevated bone-specific ALP (formation marker). COMLEX: Urinary NTx is used to monitor response to antiresorptive therapy — a 30-50% reduction within 3 months confirms therapeutic effect of bisphosphonates or denosumab.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha upregulated by estrogen withdrawal directly amplify RANKL on osteoblasts and marrow stromal cells, accelerating osteoclastogenesis beyond the OPG-loss alone"},{"id":"sdl56_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the physiologic factors leading to menopausal bone loss.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with postmenopausal osteoporosis asks her physician why her bone density declined most rapidly between ages 51 and 57 following menopause at age 51, but seems to be declining more slowly now. She has been off hormone therapy since age 53. Her DEXA T-scores are -2.2 at the hip and -2.6 at the lumbar spine. Which of the following best characterizes the accelerated phase of postmenopausal bone loss?","choices":{"A":"Bone loss of 0.7% per year occurs uniformly and indefinitely in all postmenopausal women without pharmacologic intervention, identical in rate to the age-related senile osteoporosis seen in men","B":"Bone loss of 5% per year occurs uniformly over exactly 5 years before complete arrest, with no further decline after the initial acute estrogen-withdrawal-driven osteoclast activation","C":"Bone loss of 1% per year in cortical bone and 3% per year in cancellous bone continues indefinitely after menopause without deceleration regardless of the time elapsed since last menses","D":"Up to 2% of cortical bone and 9% of cancellous bone can be lost per year in the first decade after menopause, driven by estrogen-deficiency-induced high-turnover osteoclastogenesis that gradually decelerates as a new lower steady state is reached","E":"Bone loss of 3% per year occurs symmetrically in all skeletal compartments for a period of exactly 10 years, after which osteoblast adaptation completely restores the RANKL-to-OPG ratio"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The accelerated phase of postmenopausal bone loss occurs predominantly in the first decade after menopause, driven by high-turnover estrogen-deficiency osteoclastogenesis. Rates can reach up to 9% per year for cancellous bone and 2% per year for cortical bone — far exceeding the 0.7% per year of age-related senile osteoporosis. This accelerated phase gradually decelerates as a new, lower steady state is established and the acute cytokine-driven osteoclast amplification diminishes. The bones most affected are cancellous-rich sites — vertebral bodies and the femoral neck — explaining why vertebral compression fractures often appear within the first decade after menopause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Postmenopausal bone loss rates: cancellous up to 9%/year in first decade; cortical up to 2%/year. Compare to senile: 0.7%/year both sexes, low turnover. Classic fracture sites: T-spine and L-spine compression, Colles distal radius, femoral neck. COMLEX: Vertebral compression fractures cause acute mid-back pain, height loss, and progressive kyphosis — often the first presentation of undiagnosed postmenopausal osteoporosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 1 — SLE: estrogen-related immune dysregulation shares the pathway where sex hormone loss amplifies pro-inflammatory cytokine production and accelerates tissue destruction"},{"id":"sdl56_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the physiologic factors leading to menopausal bone loss.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman with osteoporosis and a 10-year history of stable breast cancer in remission wants to understand how estrogen protects bone. Her oncologist has restricted her from estrogen therapy. Her endocrinologist explains the direct cellular mechanism by which estrogen prevents postmenopausal bone loss. Which of the following best describes estrogen's direct protective action on bone cells?","choices":{"A":"Estrogen directly increases OPG expression in osteoblasts and promotes osteoclast apoptosis through Fas-FasL pathway activation, reducing osteoclast lifespan and creating a net antiresorptive state","B":"Estrogen directly activates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase to increase circulating 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, secondarily enhancing intestinal calcium absorption and reducing the need for PTH-driven bone mobilization","C":"Estrogen directly suppresses PTH gene transcription in parathyroid chief cells through estrogen response elements in the PTH promoter, reducing calcium mobilization from skeletal stores independently of osteoblasts","D":"Estrogen directly inhibits sclerostin promoter activity in osteocytes, releasing the Wnt inhibitory brake on osteoblasts and stimulating compensatory bone formation to offset age-related resorption","E":"Estrogen directly activates calcitonin receptor expression on osteoclasts and sensitizes them to circulating calcitonin, amplifying the inhibitory effect of calcitonin on acid secretion and ruffled border formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Estrogen has direct effects on bone cells through estrogen receptors expressed on osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes. Key direct actions include: increasing OPG production by osteoblasts to suppress the RANKL:OPG ratio, and promoting osteoclast apoptosis through activation of the Fas-FasL pathway (reducing osteoclast lifespan). Estrogen also reduces osteoblast apoptosis, increasing osteoblast survival. The net result is decreased osteoclast number and activity with maintained or enhanced osteoblast function. Evidence that these are direct effects comes from studies of aromatase-deficient men and men with estrogen receptor mutations, who develop severe osteoporosis and failure of epiphyseal closure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Estrogen direct bone effects: increases OPG in osteoblasts → less RANKL available → less osteoclastogenesis; promotes osteoclast apoptosis via Fas-FasL; reduces osteoblast apoptosis. Indirect: modulates IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha production. SERMs (raloxifene) replicate bone effects without uterine/breast stimulation. COMLEX: Aromatase-deficient males develop tall stature (no epiphyseal closure) and osteoporosis — proof that estrogen is essential for bone maintenance in both sexes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in RA drive the same RANKL amplification pathway; estrogen opposes this axis, explaining the relative bone protection in premenopausal women with RA"},{"id":"sdl56_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the function of RANK Ligand and OPG in bone metabolism.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with postmenopausal osteoporosis and a T-score of -3.1 at the hip has been taking denosumab every 6 months for 3 years with excellent bone density response. Due to a change in insurance coverage she abruptly discontinues treatment without transitioning to another agent. Six months later she sustains three new vertebral fractures and repeat DEXA shows rapid bone density loss. Which pharmacologic property of denosumab best explains this rebound phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Denosumab permanently depletes osteoclast precursors from the bone marrow during treatment, causing compensatory hyperproliferation of precursor pools when the drug is discontinued","B":"Denosumab activates OPG gene expression in osteoblasts during therapy, and discontinuation removes this stimulus so osteoblasts dramatically reduce OPG production below pre-treatment baseline levels","C":"Denosumab does not incorporate into the bone mineral matrix the way bisphosphonates do, so when it is cleared from the circulation the RANKL pathway is no longer blocked and osteoclastogenesis rapidly rebounds","D":"Denosumab has a half-life of less than 24 hours requiring daily dosing, so missing even a single scheduled dose allows rapid and complete recovery of RANKL-driven osteoclast activity","E":"Denosumab cross-reacts with sclerostin during therapy and its discontinuation simultaneously restores both RANKL activity and sclerostin-mediated Wnt inhibition, causing synergistic bone loss"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unlike bisphosphonates, which bind tightly to hydroxyapatite in bone mineral and remain in the skeleton for years, denosumab circulates as a monoclonal antibody and does not integrate into the bone matrix. Its antiresorptive effect depends entirely on sustained RANKL neutralization while the drug is present. Once denosumab is cleared (half-life approximately 25-28 days), RANKL is no longer blocked and osteoclastogenesis rebounds rapidly and fully. This rebound can be so severe that it causes multiple vertebral fractures within months. All patients stopping denosumab must be transitioned to a bisphosphonate or another antiresorptive to maintain the bone density gains.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Denosumab discontinuation = rebound osteoclastogenesis → rapid bone loss → multiple vertebral fractures. Mechanism: denosumab does NOT bind bone mineral; effect is fully reversible when drug is cleared. Contrast: bisphosphonates bind bone and retain activity for years after stopping (drug holiday possible). Critical: transition to bisphosphonate when stopping denosumab. COMLEX: This rebound phenomenon is a major COMLEX high-yield clinical pearl — abrupt denosumab discontinuation is potentially catastrophic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — Biologic DMARD discontinuation produces analogous disease flare and cytokine rebound when the targeted ligand or receptor is no longer neutralized by the antibody"},{"id":"sdl56_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the function of RANK Ligand and OPG in bone metabolism.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In a research model, transgenic mice engineered to overexpress OPG throughout their lifespan develop dense, heavy bones that fracture easily when subjected to mechanical testing, despite showing no abnormalities in calcium, phosphate, or PTH. Histology shows obliterated marrow spaces and absent osteoclasts. Which of the following best explains why excess OPG produces brittle bones rather than strong ones?","choices":{"A":"OPG overexpression activates Wnt/beta-catenin signaling excessively in osteoblasts, causing unlimited matrix deposition that becomes brittle due to inadequate cross-linking of rapidly produced collagen","B":"OPG overexpression inhibits calcitonin receptor signaling on osteoclasts through a competitive receptor binding mechanism, paradoxically increasing non-calcitonin-dependent osteoclast activity","C":"OPG overexpression drives FGF23 secretion from osteocytes by relieving RANKL-mediated suppression, causing phosphate wasting and defective hydroxyapatite stoichiometry in new bone","D":"OPG overexpression completely abolishes osteoclast formation by sequestering all available RANKL, preventing the resorption required to remodel disorganized woven bone into load-bearing lamellar architecture","E":"OPG overexpression causes osteoblast apoptosis through a RANK-reverse signaling mechanism that removes the formation component of remodeling while preserving residual osteoclast activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bone strength requires more than density — it requires normal microarchitecture. Osteoclast-driven resorption is not just destructive; it is necessary for replacing old, fatigued, or architecturally disorganized bone with new, well-organized lamellar bone through the bone remodeling unit (BMU) cycle. When OPG is overexpressed and RANKL is entirely sequestered, osteoclastogenesis is abolished and no resorption-based remodeling can occur. Bone accumulates as primary woven bone with disorganized collagen orientation and accumulated microdamage — structurally inferior and brittle despite being dense. This is the cellular basis of osteopetrosis, the genetic equivalent of excess OPG signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteopetrosis = excess bone density with brittle fractures, obliterated marrow (→ anemia, infections, cranial nerve palsies). Mechanism: absent osteoclast function (carbonic anhydrase II mutation, ClC-7 mutation). OPG overexpression = functional osteopetrosis. Key teaching: bone strength requires BOTH formation AND resorption for architectural renewal. COMLEX: Osteopetrosis causes \"marble bone disease\" — stone-hard but fragile bones, pancytopenia from marrow obliteration, hearing loss from cranial foraminal narrowing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis: osteoclast-driven resorption is an essential partner to osteoblast-driven formation; abolishing either half of the BMU cycle produces pathologic bone"},{"id":"sdl56_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the function of RANK Ligand and OPG in bone metabolism.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A research team develops a novel recombinant protein that selectively stimulates RANKL secretion from marrow stromal cells in mice while leaving OPG production unchanged. After 8 weeks of daily administration, microCT imaging and bone histomorphometry are performed. Which skeletal finding would most likely be observed?","choices":{"A":"Increased osteoclast number and activity driven by excess RANKL-RANK-NF-kB signaling would produce accelerated trabecular bone resorption, widened resorption lacunae, and reduced bone mineral density","B":"Increased Wnt/beta-catenin activation through RANKL reverse signaling on LRP5 and LRP6 co-receptors on osteoblasts would paradoxically stimulate new bone formation and increase bone mineral density","C":"Compensatory upregulation of OPG by osteoblasts in response to excess RANKL would neutralize the added RANKL and maintain a normal RANKL-to-OPG ratio without skeletal change","D":"Sclerostin suppression through RANKL feedback on osteocytes would remove the Wnt inhibitory brake on osteoblasts, shifting the net balance toward bone formation and increased density","E":"Neutral effect on bone density because RANKL alone is insufficient to drive osteoclastogenesis without concurrent M-CSF binding to c-Fms on osteoclast precursors in the same marrow niche"},"correct":"A","explanation":"RANKL binding to RANK on osteoclast precursors activates NF-kB, which drives transcription of NFATc1 — the master regulator of osteoclast differentiation. Increasing RANKL without increasing OPG raises the RANKL:OPG ratio and drives excess osteoclastogenesis. In 8 weeks, this would produce increased osteoclast number, deeper and more numerous resorption lacunae on trabecular surfaces, thinning of trabeculae, and reduced bone mineral density — an osteoporosis-like phenotype. This is the mechanism exploited by tumors that secrete RANKL (e.g., multiple myeloma causing lytic lesions) and by estrogen-deficient states that reduce OPG relative to RANKL.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Excess RANKL → excess osteoclastogenesis → bone resorption → osteoporosis/lytic lesions. Multiple myeloma plasma cells produce RANKL (and DKK-1 to inhibit Wnt), causing the lytic bone lesions characteristic of myeloma. Bisphosphonates and denosumab are used in myeloma bone disease for this reason. COMLEX: Malignancy-associated bone loss (metastases, myeloma) uses the same RANKL pathway as postmenopausal osteoporosis — denosumab is approved for both indications.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — RA synoviocytes and infiltrating T cells overexpress RANKL in response to TNF-alpha and IL-1, replicating the effect of excess RANKL on periarticular bone and causing erosive joint disease"},{"id":"sdl56_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the process resulting in bone density occurring with hyperparathyroidism.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman is referred for evaluation of hypercalcemia found on routine metabolic panel. Serum calcium is 11.6 mg/dL, PTH is 138 pg/mL, phosphate is 2.1 mg/dL, and 24-hour urinary calcium is markedly elevated. She reports nephrolithiasis twice in the past 3 years, intermittent bone pain, and constipation. Neck ultrasound reveals a 1.2 cm right inferior parathyroid adenoma. Which of the following best describes the cellular mechanism by which excess PTH drives bone density loss in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Excess PTH directly binds RANK receptors on osteoclast precursors through molecular mimicry with RANKL, bypassing osteoblast intermediaries and independently activating NF-kB-driven osteoclastogenesis","B":"Excess PTH directly inhibits alkaline phosphatase synthesis in osteoblasts, preventing phosphate liberation from osteoid and impairing mineralization of newly synthesized bone matrix","C":"Excess PTH activates the calcium-sensing receptor on osteoclasts, directly stimulating cathepsin K secretion and acid pump activity independent of RANKL or NF-kB pathway involvement","D":"Excess PTH stimulates RANKL expression on osteoblasts and marrow stromal cells while suppressing OPG, increasing the RANKL-to-OPG ratio and driving osteoclast recruitment and activity at subperiosteal and endosteal surfaces","E":"Excess PTH suppresses 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D production by inhibiting renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, secondarily reducing calcium availability for mineralization of newly synthesized osteoid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PTH exerts its skeletal effects through PTH-1 receptors on osteoblasts — osteoclasts do not express PTH-1 receptors. When PTH is chronically elevated, it stimulates osteoblasts to upregulate RANKL and suppress OPG production, increasing the RANKL:OPG ratio. The resulting osteoclastogenesis drives tunneling resorption most prominently at subperiosteal cortical surfaces and endosteal surfaces. Radiographically, subperiosteal resorption on the radial aspect of the middle phalanges is pathognomonic for hyperparathyroid bone disease. With chronic disease, osteoclastic excavation of cortical bone and peritrabecular fibrosis characterize osteitis fibrosa cystica.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTH acts on osteoblasts (not osteoclasts directly) → RANKL up, OPG down → osteoclastogenesis. Classic radiograph: subperiosteal resorption of radial middle phalanges. Salt-and-pepper skull. Brown tumors = osteoclast-filled cysts with hemosiderin. Lab: high Ca + high PTH + low PO4. Treatment: parathyroidectomy for symptomatic primary HPT. COMLEX: Bones-Stones-Groans-Psychic Moans = the classic tetrad of primary hyperparathyroidism presentation on COMLEX vignettes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease is a recognized complication of primary hyperparathyroidism and should be suspected when pseudogout occurs in a hypercalcemic patient"},{"id":"sdl56_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the process resulting in bone density occurring with hyperparathyroidism.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with longstanding untreated primary hyperparathyroidism presents for evaluation of a painless, expansile jaw mass that has been enlarging over 6 months. Plain radiograph shows a well-defined lytic lesion with a soap-bubble appearance in the mandible. Biopsy reveals numerous large multinucleated giant cells filling the lesion with surrounding hemosiderin-laden macrophages and fibrous stroma. Serum calcium is 12.4 mg/dL and PTH is 220 pg/mL. What is this lesion, and what accounts for its characteristic color?","choices":{"A":"This is a giant cell tumor of bone, a benign neoplasm of osteoclast-like giant cells that occurs at the epiphyses of long bones and coincidentally presents during unrelated hyperparathyroidism","B":"This is an aneurysmal bone cyst in which the brown discoloration reflects organizing hematoma from repetitive microvascular rupture in the cyst wall that is unrelated to PTH-driven bone disease","C":"This is a brown tumor of hyperparathyroidism, in which PTH-driven osteoclastic resorption creates cavities that undergo microfracture and secondary hemorrhage, with hemosiderin deposition from old blood products producing the characteristic brown discoloration","D":"This is a fibrous dysplasia lesion in which woven bone is replaced by fibrous stroma containing hemosiderin-laden macrophages from microhemorrhage in dysplastic marrow tissue","E":"This is a metastatic renal cell carcinoma, which is a known paraneoplastic cause of hypercalcemia that produces both elevated PTH-related peptide and hemorrhagic bone lesions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Brown tumors are not true neoplasms but rather osteoclast-rich bone resorption cavities created by PTH-driven osteoclastogenesis. As PTH stimulates osteoclasts to excavate cortical and trabecular bone, microfractures occur within the lesion. Secondary hemorrhage follows, and red blood cells are broken down by macrophages to hemosiderin — the iron-containing pigment that imparts the characteristic brown color. Histologically, brown tumors appear identical to giant cell tumors of bone (both contain multinucleated giant cells), so elevated serum calcium and PTH are essential to distinguish them. Brown tumor in the jaw (mandible or maxilla) is a classic board vignette for primary hyperparathyroidism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Brown tumor = not a neoplasm; multinucleated osteoclasts + hemosiderin + microfractures. Histologically identical to giant cell tumor of bone — elevated PTH and hypercalcemia are the distinguishing features. Generalized form = osteitis fibrosa cystica. Modern diagnosis at early stage means osteitis fibrosa cystica is rare now. COMLEX: Brown tumor can occur in the jaw, ribs, clavicle, and pelvis — not just long bones. Jaw mass in a hypercalcemic patient = primary HPT until proven otherwise.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 1 — Bone lesion classification: brown tumors must be distinguished from giant cell tumors and other benign-appearing lytic lesions using clinical context including serum calcium and PTH"},{"id":"sdl56_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the process resulting in bone density occurring with hyperparathyroidism.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with CKD stage 4 is found to have a PTH of 420 pg/mL, calcium of 8.0 mg/dL, and phosphate of 5.8 mg/dL on routine labs. Her nephrologist diagnoses secondary hyperparathyroidism and initiates phosphate binders and calcitriol. She asks how her bone disease differs from that of her friend who had a parathyroid adenoma removed last year. Which of the following best distinguishes the skeletal picture of her secondary hyperparathyroidism from her friend's primary hyperparathyroidism?","choices":{"A":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism causes exclusively osteosclerotic changes throughout the skeleton from calcium phosphate deposition, while primary hyperparathyroidism produces only osteolytic lesions","B":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD co-occurs with osteomalacia from impaired vitamin D activation and osteosclerosis producing the rugger jersey spine, creating a mixed picture not seen in primary hyperparathyroidism with its isolated PTH excess","C":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism produces a higher frequency of brown tumor formation and pathologic fractures than primary hyperparathyroidism because the PTH elevation is more extreme and sustained","D":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism is distinguished by autonomous parathyroid gland enlargement that does not respond to calcium supplementation, while primary hyperparathyroidism shows normal gland anatomy","E":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism causes preferential cortical bone loss at the femoral neck and distal radius, while primary hyperparathyroidism causes exclusively cancellous vertebral bone loss"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In secondary hyperparathyroidism from CKD, the bone disease is far more complex than primary hyperparathyroidism. In addition to PTH-driven osteoclastic bone changes (osteitis fibrosa cystica), the failing kidney also produces inadequate 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, causing concurrent osteomalacia from impaired mineralization. Hyperphosphatemia drives calcium-phosphate deposition in bone, producing areas of osteosclerosis — particularly at vertebral endplates (rugger jersey spine). Adynamic bone disease from over-suppressed PTH may also be present. Primary hyperparathyroidism presents only with excess osteoclastic activity in the setting of high calcium; the renal and vitamin D components of renal osteodystrophy are absent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renal osteodystrophy = mixed disease: osteitis fibrosa (secondary HPT) + osteomalacia (low 1,25-OH2D) + osteosclerosis (rugger jersey spine) + adynamic bone. Primary HPT = isolated PTH excess, high calcium, normal vitamin D, osteitis fibrosa only. Rugger jersey spine = dense sclerotic bands at vertebral endplates on lateral X-ray, pathognomonic for renal osteodystrophy. COMLEX: Cinacalcet (calcimimetic) activates calcium-sensing receptors on parathyroid cells to suppress PTH — used for secondary HPT in CKD on dialysis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Lab panel interpretation: the combination of low calcium, high phosphate, elevated PTH, low 1,25-OH2D, and elevated ALP distinguishes secondary HPT from primary and guides management"},{"id":"sdl56_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — List the bone changes related to vitamin D deficiency.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-year-old child from a northern climate who was exclusively breastfed without vitamin D supplementation is brought in with bowing of the legs, frontal bossing, and a beaded appearance along the rib-cartilage junctions. Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 8 ng/mL. Wrist X-ray shows widened, irregular radiolucent growth plates with cupping and fraying at the distal radius metaphysis. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which vitamin D deficiency produces the characteristic growth plate changes on this X-ray?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin D deficiency activates PTH-related protein signaling in growth plate chondrocytes, causing premature arrest in the proliferative zone and irregular thickening at the metaphyseal junction","B":"Vitamin D deficiency triggers secondary hyperparathyroidism that directly stimulates chondrocyte hyperproliferation in the resting zone of the growth plate through PTH-1 receptor activation","C":"Vitamin D deficiency reduces calcium and phosphate availability, preventing mineralization of the zone of provisional calcification, so hypertrophic chondrocytes accumulate and expand the growth plate instead of being replaced by woven bone","D":"Vitamin D deficiency activates FGF receptor 3 signaling in growth plate chondrocytes through a shared VDR-FGFR3 signaling complex, mimicking the growth plate widening seen in achondroplasia","E":"Vitamin D deficiency causes osteoclast-driven erosion of calcified cartilage in the primary spongiosa, destroying the metaphyseal scaffolding and producing the fraying and cupping appearance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In normal endochondral ossification, chondrocytes in the growth plate differentiate through resting, proliferative, and hypertrophic zones. Hypertrophic chondrocytes then undergo apoptosis and their lacunae are mineralized at the zone of provisional calcification, followed by vascular invasion and replacement with woven bone. Vitamin D deficiency impairs calcium and phosphate absorption, reducing their serum concentrations. Without adequate substrate, the zone of provisional calcification cannot mineralize, hypertrophic chondrocytes accumulate beyond their normal zone, and the growth plate widens radiographically with cupping and fraying at the metaphysis. Additional features include rachitic rosary (costochondral junction widening), bowing from soft bone, and craniotabes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rickets X-ray = widened irregular radiolucent growth plate + metaphyseal cupping and fraying + bowing. Mechanism: failure of zone of provisional calcification to mineralize → hypertrophic chondrocytes pile up. Rachitic rosary = beaded ribs from costochondral junction widening. Craniotabes = soft skull bones. Treatment: vitamin D3 + calcium. COMLEX: X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets = FGF23 excess causing phosphate wasting; different from nutritional rickets — requires phosphate supplementation + calcitriol, not just vitamin D.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Vitamin D has immunomodulatory roles connecting metabolic bone disease to hypersensitivity pathways; its deficiency is associated with autoimmune disease susceptibility beyond skeletal effects"},{"id":"sdl56_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — List the bone changes related to vitamin D deficiency.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a 20-year history of Crohn disease involving the terminal ileum has undergone two bowel resections totaling 80 cm. She presents with diffuse bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, and a waddling gait. Serum labs: calcium 7.8 mg/dL, phosphate 2.2 mg/dL, PTH 145 pg/mL, ALP 380 U/L, and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 9 ng/mL. Bone biopsy shows widened osteoid seams and reduced tetracycline double-label width. Which of the following best explains why this patient's bowel disease predisposed her to this bone disorder?","choices":{"A":"Resection of the terminal ileum disrupts the enterohepatic circulation of bile acids required for fat absorption, impairing absorption of fat-soluble vitamin D from both dietary sources and oral supplements","B":"Resection of the terminal ileum eliminates the primary site of active transcellular calcium transport that is mediated by calbindin-D9k, bypassing 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D-dependent intestinal calcium uptake","C":"Resection of the terminal ileum depletes intrinsic factor stores critical for cobalamin absorption, causing cobalamin deficiency that directly impairs osteoblast DNA synthesis and bone matrix production","D":"Resection of the terminal ileum allows bacterial overgrowth that produces urease, generating ammonia that directly inhibits 25-hydroxylase activity in hepatocytes and blocks vitamin D activation","E":"Resection of the terminal ileum causes chronic diarrhea that accelerates renal calcium excretion, creating negative calcium balance that depletes skeletal mineral stores through secondary PTH activation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vitamin D is a fat-soluble vitamin that requires micellar solubilization by bile acids for intestinal absorption. The terminal ileum is the primary site of bile acid reabsorption (enterohepatic circulation). After terminal ileum resection, bile acids are lost into the colon rather than being recycled, reducing the bile acid pool available for fat-soluble vitamin absorption. The result is malabsorption of vitamins D, A, E, and K. Chronic vitamin D malabsorption leads to osteomalacia — characterized by widened osteoid seams, low calcium, low phosphate, elevated PTH (secondary hyperparathyroidism), and elevated ALP. Tetracycline-double labeling showing reduced inter-label width confirms slow mineralization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption in ileal disease/resection = vitamins A, D, E, K. Vitamin D malabsorption → osteomalacia. Labs: low Ca + low PO4 + high PTH + high ALP + low 25-OH-D + widened osteoid seams on biopsy. Key clinical clue: proximal muscle weakness + bone pain = osteomalacia until proven otherwise. COMLEX: Crohn disease and celiac disease are the two most common GI causes of vitamin D malabsorption — both are tested as board triggers for metabolic bone disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Metabolic panel in osteomalacia: low Ca + low PO4 + elevated PTH + elevated ALP + low 25-OH-D forms a recognizable biochemical pattern distinguishable from osteoporosis (normal labs) and primary HPT (high Ca)"},{"id":"sdl56_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — List the bone changes related to vitamin D deficiency.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with biopsy-confirmed osteomalacia from nutritional vitamin D deficiency is started on high-dose vitamin D3 and calcium supplementation. Her baseline labs showed calcium 7.6 mg/dL, phosphate 2.4 mg/dL, PTH 188 pg/mL, and ALP 312 U/L. After 3 months of therapy, which set of laboratory changes best confirms a meaningful therapeutic response?","choices":{"A":"Serum ALP falling to undetectable levels within the first month confirms complete remineralization of all osteoid, and urinary calcium dropping to zero confirms that no further calcium supplementation is required","B":"Serum calcium rising toward normal with a fall in PTH toward normal range confirms that the secondary hyperparathyroidism is resolving, and eventual narrowing of osteoid seams on follow-up biopsy confirms remineralization","C":"Serum sclerostin falling to below-normal levels confirms that the Wnt signaling inhibitory brake has been completely removed and osteoblast-driven mineralization is proceeding at maximum capacity","D":"Serum phosphate rising above the upper limit of normal combined with urinary hydroxyproline increasing above baseline confirms that excess remineralization is occurring at an appropriate therapeutic rate","E":"Serum calcitonin rising above the upper reference limit confirms that osteoclast-mediated bone resorption has been fully suppressed and the anabolic mineralization phase is underway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In osteomalacia, vitamin D deficiency causes hypocalcemia, which drives secondary hyperparathyroidism (elevated PTH). As vitamin D is repleted and calcium absorption normalizes, serum calcium rises, and the PTH falls toward normal (secondary hyperparathyroidism resolves). This normalizing PTH is an early and reliable marker of response. ALP may transiently rise as remineralization accelerates (the hungry bone phenomenon) before eventually returning to normal. Confirmation of successful bone remineralization ultimately requires a follow-up bone biopsy showing narrowing of osteoid seams, though this is not routinely performed in clinical practice. Serum 25-OH-D rising above 30 ng/mL and normalization of calcium, phosphate, and PTH are the practical clinical endpoints.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Treatment response markers in osteomalacia: PTH falls as Ca normalizes; ALP may initially rise (hungry bone), then fall; serum 25-OH-D increases above 30 ng/mL. Hungry bone syndrome after parathyroid surgery = rapid calcium influx into newly mineralizing bone → hypocalcemia (the inverse scenario). COMLEX: In osteomalacia from nutritional vitamin D deficiency, calcium supplementation must accompany vitamin D or remineralization will fail even with adequate vitamin D levels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — PTH normalization after vitamin D repletion correlates with reduction in IL-6-driven osteoclastogenesis, connecting calcium homeostasis restoration to cytokine-mediated inflammatory pathway normalization"},{"id":"sdl56_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the bone changes associated with chronic renal disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with end-stage renal disease on hemodialysis for 9 years presents with worsening bone pain and two pathologic rib fractures. Labs: calcium 7.6 mg/dL, phosphate 6.8 mg/dL, PTH 1100 pg/mL, 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D undetectable. Lateral thoracic spine X-ray shows alternating dense and less dense horizontal bands across the vertebral bodies. Which of the following best explains this characteristic radiographic finding?","choices":{"A":"Calcium phosphate precipitation along vertebral endplates from hyperphosphatemia-driven metastatic calcification in the soft tissue adjacent to endplates produces the dense band pattern","B":"Alternating zones of osteolytic brown tumor formation and calcified fibrous tissue that develop along the vertebral endplates in renal osteodystrophy produce the banded pattern","C":"Dense periosteal new bone formation stimulated by FGF23-dependent osteoblast activation along the superior and inferior endplates creates the alternating density pattern on plain film","D":"Dense osteosclerosis at vertebral endplates driven by PTH-stimulated osteoblast hyperactivity contrasts with the less dense central cancellous bone, producing the alternating band pattern known as the rugger jersey spine","E":"Symmetric collapse of the superior and inferior endplates from osteomalacic bone softening alternating with preserved central cortical bone produces the banded appearance in uremic patients"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rugger jersey spine is a classic radiographic finding of renal osteodystrophy with secondary hyperparathyroidism. Markedly elevated PTH drives intense osteoblastic new bone formation preferentially at the vertebral endplates (subendplate regions), where the bone is most metabolically active. This creates zones of dense osteosclerosis at the endplates that contrast with the less dense central cancellous bone of the vertebral body. The resulting alternating dense-lucent-dense horizontal banding on lateral spine X-ray resembles the horizontal stripes of a rugby jersey. It is pathognomonic for secondary hyperparathyroidism in the context of chronic kidney disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rugger jersey spine = alternating dense endplates and lucent central vertebral bodies on lateral X-ray. Pathognomonic for secondary HPT in CKD. Mechanism: PTH-driven osteoblast hyperactivity at endplates produces subendplate sclerosis. COMLEX: Other classic renal osteodystrophy X-ray findings: subperiosteal resorption of radial phalanges, periarticular calcifications from calcium-phosphate deposits, and arterial calcifications.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Lab panel in ESRD: PTH above 500 pg/mL + low Ca + high PO4 + undetectable 1,25-OH2D = severe secondary HPT driving the osteosclerosis and osteitis fibrosa components of renal osteodystrophy"},{"id":"sdl56_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the bone changes associated with chronic renal disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with end-stage renal disease on peritoneal dialysis for 6 years has been aggressively managed with high-dose calcitriol and calcium-based phosphate binders. Her current PTH is 52 pg/mL. Bone biopsy shows markedly reduced osteoid thickness, absent double tetracycline label, and obliterated remodeling activity. Despite having a T-score of -1.4, she sustains a hip fracture from a minor fall. Which of the following best explains her elevated fracture risk despite relatively preserved bone density?","choices":{"A":"Her fracture risk is paradoxically low because absent osteoclast activity preserves bone mass and microarchitecture, and her hip fracture must reflect an unrelated traumatic mechanism","B":"Adynamic bone disease with absent remodeling prevents repair of accumulated microcrack damage, causing progressive mechanical brittleness that increases fracture risk independent of bone mineral density","C":"Her low PTH suppresses osteoblast-derived growth factors including TGF-beta and IGF-1 that are required for collagen maturation, causing a qualitative collagen defect that weakens bone independent of density","D":"Her fracture risk is entirely explained by osteomalacia from incomplete remineralization of osteoid, which softens bones and reduces their resistance to the compressive forces of daily activity","E":"Her fracture risk is driven by calcitriol toxicity causing hypervitaminosis D that activates osteoclasts autonomously through 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D receptor-independent mechanisms in bone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Adynamic bone disease is a low-turnover form of renal osteodystrophy in which bone remodeling is markedly suppressed — often from over-treatment with calcitriol and/or calcium-based binders that suppress PTH too aggressively (PTH below 100-150 pg/mL in dialysis patients). Without active osteoclast-driven resorption followed by osteoblast-driven renewal, microdamage from daily mechanical loading accumulates without repair. This leads to progressive brittleness and increased fracture risk despite relatively preserved bone mineral density on DEXA. Both extremes of bone turnover — too high (secondary HPT) and too low (adynamic) — increase fracture risk in CKD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Adynamic bone disease = absent remodeling in CKD, often from PTH over-suppression. Biopsy: absent osteoid, no tetracycline labeling. Paradox: preserved BMD on DEXA but elevated fracture risk from microdamage accumulation. Target PTH in dialysis: 2-9x upper limit of normal to maintain adequate but not excess bone turnover. COMLEX: PTH below 150 pg/mL in dialysis patients = adynamic bone disease risk. PTH above 600 pg/mL = high-turnover osteitis fibrosa risk. Both increase fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 1 — Bone architectural integrity requires active remodeling; adynamic bone disease and pagetic bone share the failure to renew microarchitecture despite opposite mechanisms of turnover dysregulation"},{"id":"sdl56_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the bone changes associated with chronic renal disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with CKD stage 4 secondary to IgA nephropathy has a GFR of 22 mL/min/1.73m². His labs show: calcium 8.1 mg/dL, phosphate 5.2 mg/dL, 25-hydroxyvitamin D 28 ng/mL, and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D 14 pg/mL (low). His nephrologist prescribes calcitriol rather than vitamin D3 supplementation despite a nearly normal 25-OH-D level. A medical student asks why standard vitamin D3 would be insufficient. Which of the following best answers the student's question?","choices":{"A":"Standard vitamin D3 is poorly absorbed from the GI tract in CKD patients because uremic toxins directly compete with vitamin D for binding to the intestinal vitamin D-binding protein complex","B":"Standard vitamin D3 is aggressively cleared by the stage 4 kidney through tubular secretion, reducing serum 25-OH-D faster than oral supplementation can replete it at standard therapeutic doses","C":"Standard vitamin D3 requires hepatic activation to 25-hydroxyvitamin D as the initial step, but this CYP2R1-dependent hepatic hydroxylation is impaired by the systemic inflammation of CKD","D":"Standard vitamin D3 is inactivated in the circulation by FGF23-stimulated CYP24A1 upregulation before it can reach the kidney, making renal 1-alpha-hydroxylation unavailable as a therapeutic target","E":"Standard vitamin D3 requires conversion to active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D by renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, a step the stage 4 kidney cannot perform adequately, so only the already-active calcitriol can restore 1,25-OH2D levels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vitamin D3 from the diet or skin must undergo two sequential activations: first, hepatic 25-hydroxylation to 25-OH-D (which is the storage and transport form, reflected by serum 25-OH-D levels); second, renal 1-alpha-hydroxylation to the biologically active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. In CKD stage 4, despite a nearly normal 25-OH-D level reflecting adequate hepatic activation, the failing kidney cannot perform the critical 1-alpha-hydroxylation step adequately. Therefore, standard vitamin D3 or D2 is insufficient because its final activation step is blocked. Calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D) bypasses the renal activation step entirely and directly restores active vitamin D signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin D activation: D3 → liver (25-hydroxylation) → 25-OH-D → kidney (1-alpha-hydroxylation) → 1,25-OH2D (calcitriol, active). CKD blocks step 2. Give calcitriol directly. Nearly normal 25-OH-D in CKD = hepatic step is fine; the renal step is the problem. FGF23 rises early in CKD and inhibits 1-alpha-hydroxylase before GFR falls severely — early CKD vitamin D deficiency precedes obvious renal impairment. COMLEX: Active vitamin D analogs in CKD: calcitriol, paricalcitol, doxercalciferol — all bypass the failed renal activation step.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Distinguishing 25-OH-D from 1,25-OH2-D levels in CKD is essential: normal 25-OH-D with low 1,25-OH2-D in CKD indicates intact hepatic activation but failed renal activation requiring calcitriol therapy"},{"id":"sdl56_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the abnormal pathophysiology resulting in Paget's disease of the bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 76-year-old man presents with a 4-year history of progressive skull enlargement requiring larger hat sizes, bilateral sensorineural hearing loss, and increasing frontal headaches. His ALP is 920 U/L while serum calcium, phosphate, and PTH are all normal. Skull X-ray shows a mixture of lytic areas and dense sclerotic patches described as a cotton wool appearance. Which phase of Paget disease does this presentation represent, and what is happening histologically at this stage?","choices":{"A":"This represents the lytic phase, in which osteoclasts are resorbing bone rapidly without any compensatory osteoblastic response, producing purely lucent areas on skull radiographs and low ALP","B":"This represents the burned-out sclerotic phase, in which all osteoclast activity has ceased and dense disorganized lamellar bone remains without active remodeling or biochemical marker elevation","C":"This represents the mixed phase, in which simultaneous osteoclastic resorption and osteoblastic new bone formation produce the chaotic mosaic cement-line pattern histologically and the cotton wool radiographic appearance","D":"This represents the malignant transformation phase, in which osteoclasts have undergone sarcomatous change and are now invading the skull base and producing the radiographic density changes","E":"This represents the inflammatory phase, in which viral inclusion bodies in pagetic osteoclasts trigger macrophage recruitment and granulomatous inflammation creating the mixed radiographic appearance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paget disease progresses through three phases: the lytic phase (predominantly osteoclastic, seen as a flame-shaped or wedge-shaped lucency on X-ray), the mixed phase (concurrent osteoclastic and osteoblastic activity, markedly elevated ALP, cotton wool appearance from patches of sclerosis against a background of residual lucency), and the sclerotic/burned-out phase (predominantly dense disorganized bone, mildly elevated ALP). This patient has markedly elevated ALP with the cotton wool appearance, indicating active mixed-phase disease. Skull involvement causes hearing loss from compression of the cochlea or ossicles, and the expanding skull creates the characteristic hat-size increase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paget disease phases: lytic (osteoclastic, flame-shaped lucency), mixed (cotton wool, markedly elevated ALP), sclerotic (burned out, dense bone, modest ALP). Labs: markedly elevated ALP; NORMAL Ca, PO4, PTH — key differentiator from HPT. Skull complications: hearing loss (cochlea/ossicle compression), hat size increase, cranial nerve palsies, high-output CHF. COMLEX: Treat active symptomatic Paget disease with bisphosphonates (zoledronate preferred) or calcitonin to reduce osteoclast activity and lower ALP.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 2 — Secondary osteosarcoma arising in Paget disease is driven by TP53 and RB inactivation in osteoblast lineage cells, the same molecular drivers as high-grade primary osteosarcoma"},{"id":"sdl56_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the abnormal pathophysiology resulting in Paget's disease of the bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with longstanding polyostotic Paget disease involving the femur, pelvis, and skull presents with sudden severe thigh pain after standing from a chair. X-ray shows a transverse fracture through the mid-diaphysis of the femur without significant trauma. The radiologist notes that the affected femur is diffusely thickened, bowed anteriorly, and shows the mixed sclerotic and lytic density changes of pagetic bone. Which of the following best explains why structurally thicker pagetic bone fractures with minimal force?","choices":{"A":"Pagetic bone is hypervascular from arteriovenous shunting, and the resulting increase in intraosseous hydraulic pressure generates fatigue stress on the cortex that produces spontaneous transverse fractures","B":"Pagetic bone is mechanically inferior despite increased bulk because the chaotic mosaic of woven and lamellar bone produces disorganized collagen fiber orientation with poor tensile strength and resistance to bending forces","C":"Pagetic bone develops stress-shielding from abnormal bowing geometry that concentrates compressive forces on the concave side, causing cortical disuse osteoporosis that predisposes to fracture at the convex surface","D":"Pagetic bone accumulates calcium phosphate deposits in haversian canals that block normal osteocyte canalicular signaling, preventing mechanosensory-driven microcrack repair and causing brittle fracture","E":"Pagetic bone develops subperiosteal hemorrhage from hypervascularity that creates stress risers in the cortex, propagating cracks transversely through the diaphysis under physiologic bending loads"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pagetic bone is dense but architecturally inferior. The hallmark of Paget disease — the mosaic pattern of alternating woven and lamellar bone separated by irregular cement lines — produces bone with disorganized collagen fiber orientation. Normal cortical bone derives its tensile and bending strength from precisely oriented Type I collagen fibers within osteons. Pagetic bone lacks this ordered architecture, making it brittle despite its bulk, much like disorganized concrete. The result is the chalk-stick transverse fracture, where the bone snaps cleanly across the diaphysis under bending or torsional loads that normal bone would easily withstand. The bowing of the femur in Paget disease further concentrates stress.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pagetic bone = dense but brittle from disorganized collagen architecture. Chalk-stick fracture = transverse diaphyseal fracture characteristic of pagetic long bones. Also: bowing of the tibia (saber shin) is classic for tibial involvement. Treatment: bisphosphonates reduce osteoclast activity, lower ALP, and may reduce fracture risk. Surgical fixation of pagetic fractures is challenging due to hard, bleeding bone. COMLEX: In polyostotic Paget, the combination of high-output heart failure (AV shunting), hearing loss, and chalk-stick fractures covers the major systemic complications to know.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 3 — Soft tissue sarcomas with complex karyotypes also arise in pre-existing bone lesions, sharing the principle that architectural disorganization of matrix predisposes to genomic instability and malignant transformation"},{"id":"sdl56_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the abnormal pathophysiology resulting in Paget's disease of the bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a 10-year history of polyostotic Paget disease involving the pelvis, femur, and skull has been intermittently treated with bisphosphonates. He presents with rapidly worsening right thigh pain over 6 weeks despite adequate analgesia. Repeat X-ray shows a new expansile lytic lesion within a known pagetic focus of the femur with cortical destruction. Biopsy reveals pleomorphic spindle cells producing irregular osteoid in a background of disorganized pagetic bone. Which molecular alteration is most commonly identified as the driver of malignant transformation in this clinical setting?","choices":{"A":"SQSTM1 gain-of-function mutation that initiated the original pagetic disease also directly activates MDM2 amplification and confers osteoblast resistance to apoptosis, driving sarcomatous transformation","B":"RANK gain-of-function mutation causing original Paget disease persistence also activates secondary NF-kB-independent survival signaling in osteoblast precursors, initiating high-grade sarcomatous change","C":"FBN1 loss-of-function mutation activating dysregulated TGF-beta release from pagetic bone matrix predisposes osteoprogenitor cells to epithelial-mesenchymal transition and sarcomatous dedifferentiation","D":"VEGF amplification within the hyperemic pagetic bone microenvironment drives osteoblast progenitor cells through a hypoxia-inducible factor pathway that induces oncogenic chromosomal rearrangements","E":"TP53 and RB1 gene inactivation through mutation or deletion in osteoblast lineage cells within chronically remodeling pagetic bone are the most commonly identified molecular drivers of secondary osteosarcoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Secondary osteosarcoma arising in Paget disease is histologically a high-grade osteosarcoma and carries a very poor prognosis — worse than primary osteosarcoma in younger patients. The most commonly identified molecular drivers are TP53 mutation or deletion and RB1 (retinoblastoma gene) inactivation, both of which are critical tumor suppressor genes. The chronic cycles of abnormal resorption and disorganized new bone formation in Paget disease create genomic instability in rapidly proliferating osteoblast lineage cells, increasing the probability of TP53 and RB1 mutations accumulating over time. MDM2 amplification is more characteristic of well-differentiated osteosarcoma and parosteal osteosarcoma, not secondary pagetic osteosarcoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary osteosarcoma in Paget disease = high-grade, TP53 and RB1 inactivation, very poor prognosis. Occurs in less than 1% overall but 5-10% of severe polyostotic disease. Clinical clue: sudden worsening pain, rapidly expanding lytic lesion on X-ray, rapidly rising ALP. COMLEX: Secondary osteosarcoma can occur in any bone affected by Paget disease, radiation, chronic osteomyelitis, or bone infarcts — all represent states of chronic abnormal bone remodeling and genomic stress.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 2 — Secondary osteosarcoma in Paget disease shares the complex karyotype genomic instability profile of high-grade primary osteosarcoma, driven by TP53 and RB inactivation in osteoblast lineage cells"},{"id":"sdl56_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the classes and mechanisms of action in medications approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 73-year-old woman with severe postmenopausal osteoporosis, T-score -3.8 at the hip, and three vertebral fractures in the past 2 years is started on teriparatide 20 mcg subcutaneously daily. Her physician explains that the drug works anabolically at this intermittent dose. She asks why the same PTH hormone that causes bone loss in hyperparathyroidism is now being given to build bone. Which of the following best explains the difference?","choices":{"A":"Intermittent PTH-1R stimulation from a daily subcutaneous dose preferentially drives osteoblast differentiation, proliferation, and survival, whereas the sustained PTH elevation of hyperparathyroidism chronically upregulates RANKL and drives predominantly osteoclastic bone resorption","B":"Intermittent dosing allows teriparatide to accumulate in bone mineral over time where it inhibits osteoclast H-ATPase, while in hyperparathyroidism the continuous PTH instead activates calcitonin secretion","C":"Intermittent dosing selectively phosphorylates sclerostin at a PTH-dependent site, permanently inactivating it and sustaining Wnt-driven bone formation independent of ongoing PTH-1R stimulation","D":"Intermittent dosing generates calcitonin co-secretion from parafollicular C cells that suppresses osteoclast activity between PTH pulses, while chronic hyperparathyroidism lacks this co-secretory protective response","E":"Intermittent dosing upregulates OPG expression in osteoblasts during the drug-free period, shifting the RANKL-to-OPG ratio toward antiresorption in the hours between once-daily doses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The anabolic vs. catabolic paradox of PTH is resolved by the concept of intermittent vs. continuous receptor stimulation. PTH-1R on osteoblasts signals through different downstream pathways depending on duration of activation. Brief daily peaks (as with subcutaneous teriparatide) preferentially activate osteoblast differentiation from progenitors, suppress osteoblast apoptosis, and drive sclerostin suppression — collectively increasing bone formation above resorption. Sustained PTH receptor stimulation (as in primary hyperparathyroidism) instead chronically upregulates RANKL on osteoblasts, driving dominant osteoclastogenesis and cortical bone loss. The net result of identical receptor but different signaling duration is opposite skeletal outcomes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Teriparatide intermittent = anabolic (osteoblast differentiation, sclerostin suppression, IGF-1). Continuous PTH = catabolic (RANKL upregulation, osteoclastogenesis). Max 2 years; follow with antiresorptive. SE: transient hypercalcemia, dizziness after first dose. Contraindications: Paget disease, prior bone radiation, unexplained elevated ALP, pediatric patients with open epiphyses. COMLEX: Teriparatide is the first-choice anabolic for severe glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis and for patients who have failed antiresorptive therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — Teriparatide as a recombinant peptide hormone illustrates the biologic drug principle: the target receptor, dose, and signaling duration determine whether the pharmacologic effect is anabolic or catabolic"},{"id":"sdl56_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the classes and mechanisms of action in medications approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with osteoporosis is prescribed once-weekly alendronate 70 mg and given standard discharge instructions to take it on an empty stomach in the morning. Two weeks later she calls reporting severe pain with swallowing and a burning sensation behind her sternum that started the day after her first dose. Endoscopy reveals an esophageal ulceration at the gastroesophageal junction. Which specific counseling point, if provided at initiation, most likely would have prevented this complication?","choices":{"A":"Instructing the patient to take alendronate with a full glass of milk would have buffered its acidic properties and neutralized any caustic effect on the esophageal mucosa before the tablet dissolved","B":"Instructing the patient to crush the alendronate tablet and dissolve it in warm water would have eliminated the solid tablet contact with the esophageal epithelium during transit","C":"Instructing the patient to take alendronate with antacids would have neutralized its local acidity and formed a mucosal protective layer against direct esophageal irritation","D":"Instructing the patient to take alendronate at bedtime rather than in the morning would have allowed gravity-independent transit and reduced the duration of esophageal exposure during sleep","E":"Instructing the patient to remain completely upright for at least 30 minutes after swallowing alendronate with a full glass of plain water would have allowed esophageal clearance and prevented prolonged mucosal contact"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bisphosphonate-induced esophageal injury results from direct prolonged contact of the tablet or its contents with the esophageal mucosa, causing chemical irritation and ulceration. The critical preventive measures are: (1) swallow with a full glass (8 oz) of plain water — dairy and antacids impair absorption and must be avoided; (2) take on an empty stomach first thing in the morning; (3) remain upright (sitting or standing) for at least 30-60 minutes after taking the dose to ensure esophageal clearance by gravity and prevent reflux of the drug back into the esophagus. Lying down or taking alendronate with insufficient water allows the tablet to sit in the esophagus or reflux back. Bedtime dosing would be the worst option as lying prone prevents clearance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bisphosphonate instructions: empty stomach, full glass of plain water, remain upright 30-60 minutes, no food for 30-60 minutes. SE: esophagitis and esophageal ulceration (must stay upright), osteonecrosis of jaw (especially with dental procedures), atypical subtrochanteric femur fracture (with long-term use). IV zoledronate avoids GI side effects. COMLEX: Osteonecrosis of the jaw with bisphosphonates is most common with IV formulations used for bone metastases at high doses — less common with oral doses for osteoporosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — Adverse effect profiles by class: GI toxicity is a major limitation of oral disease-modifying agents including bisphosphonates and NSAIDs; switching to parenteral formulations can mitigate this class effect"},{"id":"sdl56_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the classes and mechanisms of action in medications approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with postmenopausal osteoporosis and a T-score of -3.5 at the hip is referred for consideration of romosozumab therapy. Her endocrinologist reviews her history and notes she had a myocardial infarction 6 months ago, currently managed with dual antiplatelet therapy and a statin. She also has well-controlled hypertension. Before prescribing romosozumab, which of the following statements about its safety profile is most accurate and clinically important?","choices":{"A":"Romosozumab carries no cardiovascular contraindications and is the preferred first-line anabolic agent for all patients with severe osteoporosis regardless of prior cardiac or cerebrovascular history","B":"Romosozumab increases cardiovascular risk only in patients with uncontrolled hypertension, and her well-controlled blood pressure with medication means the cardiovascular boxed warning does not apply to her","C":"Romosozumab carries an FDA boxed warning for increased risk of myocardial infarction and stroke and should not be initiated within 12 months of a cardiovascular event, making her an unsuitable candidate at this time","D":"Romosozumab is safe to use in patients on dual antiplatelet therapy because the mechanism of cardiovascular risk involves arterial inflammation rather than platelet-mediated thrombus formation","E":"Romosozumab cardiovascular risk is limited to the first injection and is fully mitigated by pretreatment with aspirin and a loading dose of a P2Y12 inhibitor before initiation of therapy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Romosozumab received FDA approval in 2019 with a boxed warning for increased risk of myocardial infarction and stroke. In the pivotal ARCH trial, patients randomized to romosozumab had a higher rate of serious cardiovascular adverse events compared to alendronate. The FDA label states romosozumab should not be initiated in patients who have had an MI or stroke within the preceding year. This patient had her MI 6 months ago, placing her within the contraindicated window. Alternatives for her would include teriparatide or abaloparatide (anabolics without the cardiovascular warning) as initial therapy, with transition to an antiresorptive when complete. Romosozumab is approved for a maximum of 12 months of therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Romosozumab = anti-sclerostin antibody, dual anabolic and antiresorptive, approved 12 months only, then transition to antiresorptive. FDA boxed warning: increased MI and stroke risk — do NOT use within 12 months of cardiovascular event. Contrast with teriparatide and abaloparatide, which carry an osteosarcoma-in-rats warning but no cardiovascular boxed warning. COMLEX: For a patient with recent MI and severe osteoporosis, teriparatide or abaloparatide is the appropriate anabolic choice; defer romosozumab until cardiovascular risk window has passed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — FDA boxed warnings in biologic agents reflect class-specific serious adverse effects that require mandatory risk-benefit counseling — romosozumab cardiovascular risk parallels the infection risk warnings on TNF inhibitors"}]}]}]};
